window.SP_DATA=window.SP_DATA||{subjects:{}};SP_DATA.subjects["sem1_patent"]={"chapters":{"s1p_1":{"notes":"# What a Patent Is — Rights Conferred, Term and Territoriality\n\n## Definition of a Patent\n\nA patent is defined as a statutory privilege granted by the government to inventors or other persons deriving their rights from the inventor for fixed years, to exclude others from manufacturing, using or selling a patented product or process. This definition aligns with the explanation provided in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 27*, which emphasizes that a patent is an exclusive right granted by the federal government to the owner for 20 years as an incentive for disclosing technology for public good.\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n### Exclusive Rights\nUnder **Section 46** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, the rights conferred by a patent include the exclusive right to make, use, and sell the patented invention. These rights are granted to prevent unauthorized commercial exploitation of the invention during the term of the patent.\n\n### Term of Patent\nAccording to **Section 53**, the term of a patent is 20 years from the date of filing the application. This duration allows inventors sufficient time to recoup their investment and profit from their innovation before the technology becomes part of the public domain.\n\n### Territoriality\nPatent rights are territorial, meaning they are enforceable only within the jurisdiction where the patent was granted. In India, **Section 49** provides that the rights conferred by a patent do not infringe when used on foreign vessels or aircraft temporarily or accidentally in India. This reflects the principle of international comity and the recognition of sovereign jurisdictions.\n\n## Why the Law is Structured This Way\n\nThe rationale behind granting exclusive rights for a limited period lies in the concept of **\"quid pro quo\"**. As explained in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 27*, patent law offers an inventor exclusive rights for a limited period in return for full disclosure of his invention to the public in the form of patent specifications. This ensures that while the inventor benefits from their innovation, society also gains access to new knowledge after the term expires.\n\nThe **20-year term** is designed to balance the interests of the inventor and the public. It allows sufficient time for commercialization and recoupment of investment without unduly restricting free competition in the long run. The territorial nature of patents ensures that each country can independently decide whether to grant protection, aligning with national policies on innovation and trade.\n\n## Verified Cases (if any)\n\nAs per the case list provided, **no verified or candidate cases are available** for this topic. If a case would normally support a point, it is recommended to **(verify this case with a case-law database)** before citing one in an exam setting.\n\n---\n\nThis structured approach provides a comprehensive understanding of what a patent is, the rights conferred by it, its term, and its territorial limitations under *The Patents Act, 1970*. It also highlights the reasoning behind these legal provisions as explained in authoritative textbooks.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 48 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 48. Rights of patentees.\n> Subject to the other provisions contained in this Act and the\n> conditions specified in section 47, a patent granted under this Act\n>\n>  43\n>\n> shall confer upon the patentee\n> (a) where the subject matter of the patent is a product, the\n> exclusive right to prevent third parties, who do not have his\n> consent, from the act of making, using, offering for sale, selling\n> or importing for those purposes that product in India;\n> (b) where the subject matter of the patent is a process, the\n> exclusive right to prevent third parties, who do not have his\n> consent, from the act of using that process, and from the act of\n> using, offering for sale, selling or importing for those purposes\n> the product obtained directly by that process in India.\n\n**Section 53 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 53. Term of patent.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions of this Act, the term of every patent\n> granted, after the commencement of the Patents (Amendment) Act,\n> 2002, and the term of every patent which has not expired and has not\n> ceased to have effect, on the date of such commencement, under this\n> Act, shall be twenty years from the date of filing of the\n> application for the patent.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this sub-section, the term of\n> patent in case of International applications filed under the Patent\n> Cooperation Treaty designating India, shall be twenty years from the\n> international filing date accorded under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty.\n>\n> (2) A patent shall cease to have effect notwithstanding anything\n> therein or in this Act on the expiration of the period prescribed\n> for the payment of any renewal fee, if that fee is not paid within\n> the prescribed period or within such extended period as may be\n> prescribed.\n>\n> (4) Notwithstanding anything contained in any other law for the time\n> being in force, on cessation of the patent right due to non-payment\n> of renewal fee or on the expiry of the term of patent, the subject\n> matter covered by the said patent shall not be entitled to any\n> protection.\n>\n>  47\n>\n> CHAPTER IX PATENTS OF ADDITION\n\n**Section 11A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11A. Publication of applications.\n> (1) Save as otherwise provided, no application for patent shall\n> ordinarily be opened to the public for such period as may be\n> prescribed.\n>\n> (2) The applicant may, in the prescribed manner, request the\n> Controller to publish his application at any time before the expiry\n> of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) and subject to the\n> provisions of sub-section (3), the Controller shall publish such\n> application as soon as possible.\n>\n> (3) Every application for a patent shall, on the expiry of the\n> period specified under sub-section (1), be published, except in\n> cases where the application\n> (a) in which secrecy direction is imposed under section 35; or\n> (b) has been abandoned under sub-section (1) of section 9; or\n> (c) has been withdrawn three months prior to the period specified\n> under sub-section (1).\n>\n> (4) In case a secrecy direction has been given in respect of an\n> application under section 35, then, it shall be published after the\n> expiry of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) or when the\n> secrecy direction has ceased to operate, whichever is later.\n>\n> (5) The publication of every application under this section shall\n> include the particulars of the date of application, number of\n> application, name and address of the applicant identifying the\n> application and an abstract.\n>\n> (6) Upon publication of an application for a patent under this\n> section\n> (a) the depository institution shall make the biological material\n> mentioned in the specification available to the public;\n> (b) the patent office may, on payment of such fee as may be\n> prescribed, make the specification and drawings, if any, of such\n> application available to the public.\n>\n> (7) On and from the date of publication of the application for\n> patent and until the date of grant of a patent in respect of such\n>\n>  20\n>\n> application, the applicant shall have the like privileges and rights\n> as if a patent for the invention had been granted on the date of\n> publication of the application:\n> Provided that the applicant shall not be entitled to institute any\n> proceedings for infringement until the patent has been granted:\n> Provided further that the rights of a patentee in respect of\n> applications made under sub-section (2) of section 5 before the 1st\n> day of January, 2005 shall accrue from the date of grant of the\n> patent:\n> Provided also that after a patent is granted in respect of\n> applications made under sub-section (2) of section 5, the patent-\n> holder shall only be entitled to receive reasonable royalty from\n> such enterprises which have made significant investment and were\n> producing and marketing the concerned product prior to the 1st day\n> of January, 2005 and which continue to manufacture the product\n> covered by the patent on the date of grant of the patent and no\n> infringement proceedings shall be instituted against such\n> enterprises.\n\n**Rule 24 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 24. Publication of application\n> The period for which an application for patent shall not ordinarily\n> be open to public under sub-section (1) to section 11A shall be\n> eighteen months from the date of filing of application or the date\n> of priority of the application, whichever is earlier Provided that\n> the period within which the Controller shall publish the application\n> in the journal shall ordinarily be one month from the date of expiry\n> of said period, or one month from the date of request for\n> publication under rule 24A.\n\n**Rule 24A — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 24A. Request for publication\n> A request for publication under sub-section (2) of section 11A shall\n> be made in Form 9.\n\n**Rule 27 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 27. Inspection and supply of published documents\n> After the date of publication of the application under section 11A,\n> the application together with the complete specification and\n> provisional specification, if any, the drawing, if any, the abstract\n> and any other document filed in respect of the application may be\n> inspected at the appropriate office by making a written request to\n> the Controller on payment of the fee on that behalf and copies\n> thereof may be obtained on payment of fees specified in the First\n> Schedule.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Define a patent and explain the rights conferred by it under the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThis question asks for a definition of a patent and an explanation of the exclusive rights granted to the patentee under *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n**Rule**  \nA patent is defined as a statutory privilege granted by the government to inventors or other persons deriving their rights from the inventor for fixed years, to exclude others from manufacturing, using, or selling a patented product or process (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 27*). Under **Section 48** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, the rights conferred by a patent include:  \n(a) For products — the exclusive right to prevent third parties from making, using, offering for sale, selling, or importing that product in India without consent.  \n(b) For processes — the exclusive right to prevent third parties from using that process and from using, offering for sale, selling, or importing the product obtained directly by that process in India (*Bare Act reference*).  \n\n**Application**  \nThe rights conferred under **Section 48** are designed to protect the inventor’s interests while ensuring public access to knowledge after the patent expires. These rights apply only within the jurisdiction where the patent was granted, as outlined in **Section 49**, which states that patent rights do not infringe when used on foreign vessels or aircraft temporarily or accidentally in India (*Bare Act reference*). This territorial limitation is consistent with international comity and sovereign jurisdiction principles.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA patent is a statutory privilege granting the inventor exclusive rights to prevent unauthorized use, sale, or import of an invention for 20 years. These rights are confined to the territory where the patent was granted, ensuring that each nation independently determines whether to offer protection under its laws.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the territorial nature of patents and fail to mention **Section 49**, which is crucial in explaining how patent rights do not apply universally but only within the jurisdiction of grant.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the term of a patent as provided under *The Patents Act, 1970*. Discuss its rationale.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThis question requires an explanation of the duration for which a patent is valid and the reasoning behind this provision.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 53** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, the term of a patent is 20 years from the date of filing the application. This applies to all patents granted after the commencement of the *Patents (Amendment) Act, 2002* and includes international applications filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty designating India (*Bare Act reference*). The rationale behind this provision lies in the concept of **\"quid pro quo\"**, where the inventor receives exclusive rights for a limited period in return for full disclosure of the invention to the public (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 27*).\n\n**Application**  \nThe 20-year term ensures that inventors have sufficient time to recoup their investment and profit from their innovation before the technology becomes part of the public domain. It also balances the interests of the inventor with those of the public by allowing access to new knowledge after the patent expires, promoting innovation and competition in the long run.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA patent is valid for 20 years from the date of filing the application under *The Patents Act, 1970*. This period ensures that inventors can benefit financially while also contributing to public knowledge after the term ends.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention the **\"quid pro quo\"** principle and the role of disclosure in justifying the limited duration of patent protection. Including this concept is essential for a complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What do you mean by territoriality of patents? Explain with reference to *The Patents Act, 1970*.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThis question asks about the principle of territoriality and its application under *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n**Rule**  \nPatent rights are **territorial**, meaning they are enforceable only within the jurisdiction where the patent was granted. In India, **Section 49** provides that the rights conferred by a patent do not infringe when used on foreign vessels or aircraft temporarily or accidentally in India (*Bare Act reference*). This reflects the principle of international comity and sovereign jurisdictions.\n\n**Application**  \nThe territorial nature of patents ensures that each country can independently decide whether to grant protection, aligning with national policies on innovation and trade. For example, a patent granted in India does not automatically provide protection in other countries, and vice versa. However, **Section 49** makes an exception for foreign vessels or aircraft temporarily present in India, recognizing the practical realities of international travel and commerce.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nPatent rights are enforceable only within the jurisdiction where they were granted. In India, this principle is outlined in **Section 49**, which excludes infringement claims for use on foreign vessels or aircraft temporarily in the country.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to explain the exception provided under **Section 49** and its relevance to international comity. Including this detail strengthens the answer significantly.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is the significance of the \"quid pro quo\" principle in patent law?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThis question asks about the importance of the **\"quid pro quo\"** principle in patent law and its implications for inventors and society.\n\n**Rule**  \nThe **\"quid pro quo\"** principle is central to patent law, where an inventor receives exclusive rights for a limited period in return for full disclosure of their invention to the public (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 27*). This ensures that while the inventor benefits from their innovation, society also gains access to new knowledge after the patent expires.\n\n**Application**  \nThis principle is crucial in balancing the interests of inventors and the public. It encourages innovation by offering financial incentives through exclusive rights, but it also promotes the dissemination of knowledge once the term ends. The 20-year duration under **Section 53** aligns with this balance, ensuring that inventors can profit while allowing free competition after the patent expires.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe **\"quid pro quo\"** principle ensures that inventors receive exclusive rights in return for disclosing their inventions to the public. This promotes innovation and knowledge sharing by balancing private benefits with societal gains.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to connect the **\"quid pro quo\"** principle directly with the 20-year term under **Section 53**, which is essential for demonstrating a full understanding of its significance.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - What a Patent Is — Rights Conferred, Term and Territoriality\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 48 | **ROOF** | ROOF blocks intruders, like patentee blocks competitors. |\n| Sec. 53 | **LIME** | LIME is filed under a patent shelf, its juice dripping like time. |\n| Sec. 11-A(7) | **TOTE** + the letter A + clause (7) | TOTE carries goods until sealed, like patent rights until grant. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **43-53** - grant, rights, term of patent\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Exclusive rights of a patentee for product and process - which provision?  (picture: ROOF)\n2. Term of 20 years from the date of filing - which provision?  (picture: LIME)\n3. Rights from publication until grant - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 46 | Exclusive right to make, use, sell patented invention | Inventor's monopoly over creation  \n- Section 48 | Prevents others from using product/process without consent | Stop others from copying invention  \n- Section 53 | Patent term is 20 years from filing date | 20-year clock starts when applied  \n- Section 49 | No infringement on foreign vessels/aircraft in India | Ships and planes get temporary pass  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Mistake: Confusing patent term with copyright duration (10 years vs 20 years)  \n- Phrase examiners expect: \"Quid pro quo\" for disclosure of invention  \n- Structure tip: Always cite the exact section number and explain its scope clearly","storyEN":"In a quiet neighborhood of Hyderabad, there lived a young inventor named Arjun. He had spent years developing a revolutionary water purification device that could change lives in rural areas. One day, he decided to protect his invention by filing a patent application with the Indian Patent Office.\n\nArjun’s journey began when he submitted his application and waited for it to be published. According to **Section 11A**, the application would not be opened to the public until after a certain period had passed unless Arjun requested its early publication. He did request it, and soon enough, the entire neighborhood knew about his invention.\n\nBut with this came a challenge. A local company, led by a man named Ravi, saw an opportunity to copy Arjun’s design. However, **Section 48** was clear: Arjun had the exclusive right to prevent anyone else from making, using, or selling the product in India without his consent. Ravi found himself in trouble when he tried to manufacture the device without permission.\n\nArjun also knew that time was on his side. The patent would last **20 years**, as stated in **Section 53**. This gave him enough time to commercialize his invention and make a profit before it entered the public domain. He had to remember that, even though he held these rights, they were limited — and only valid within India.\n\nOne day, an international shipping company passed through Hyderabad with a vessel carrying similar water purification devices. Arjun was worried that **Section 49** might not protect him from foreign entities. But the law was kind: **Section 49** allowed for such use on foreign vessels or aircraft temporarily in India without infringing on his rights, ensuring international comity.\n\nAs the years passed, Arjun’s invention became a success, and he used it to improve water access across several villages. He always remembered that the patent system was built on **\"quid pro quo\"** — giving him exclusive rights for 20 years in exchange for full disclosure of his invention to society.\n\nOne day, while reviewing his contract with a manufacturer, Arjun discovered that he had missed paying a renewal fee. According to **Section 53**, if the fee wasn’t paid within the prescribed period, the patent would cease to have effect. He quickly rectified the mistake and avoided losing his rights entirely.\n\nThrough it all, Arjun’s journey was one of innovation, protection, and understanding — each number in the law a crucial piece of the puzzle that ensured fairness for both inventors and society at large.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| 20 years     | Section 53 | The term of a patent is 20 years from the date of filing the application. |\n| Exclusive right | Section 48 | A patent grants the patentee exclusive rights to prevent third parties from making, using, or selling the invention in India without consent. |\n| Foreign vessels | Section 49 | Patent rights do not infringe when used on foreign vessels or aircraft temporarily or accidentally in India. |\n| Renewal fee | Section 53 | A patent ceases to have effect if a renewal fee is not paid within the prescribed period. |\n| Quid pro quo | The Patents Act, 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 27 | Patent law grants exclusive rights for a limited period in return for full disclosure of the invention to the public. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **“What a Patent Is — Rights Conferred, Term and Territoriality”**\n\n---\n\n**1. Acronym or Mnemonic:**\n\n- **RIGHTS CONFERRED**:  \n  Use the acronym **\"PRIME\"** to remember key rights conferred by a patent:\n  - **P**revent others from using the invention.\n  - **R**ight to commercialize (sell, license).\n  - **I**nclusive of processes and products.\n  - **M**onopolistic control for a limited time.\n  - **E**xclusivity granted by law.\n\n- **TERM OF PATENT**:  \n  Use the word **\"20 Years\"** as a memory anchor — this is clearly stated in **Section 53**, which defines the term of a patent as **20 years from the date of filing** (or priority date, if applicable).\n\n- **TERRITORIALITY**:  \n  Remember with the phrase **“Patent is Local”**. This refers to **Section 46(1)**, which states that a patent is territorial in nature and only gives rights within India.\n\n---\n\n**2. Number-Anchors for Articles/Sections:**\n\n- **Section 43 (Grant of Patents):**  \n  Imagine the number **\"43\"** as a **key** — it unlocks the **grant of exclusive rights** to the patentee, symbolizing the start of their monopoly.\n\n- **Section 53 (Term of Patent):**  \n  Think of **\"53\"** as a **clock ticking down to 20 years**, representing the limited duration of a patent.\n\n- **Section 46(1) (Territoriality):**  \n  Picture **\"46\"** as a **map with borders**, showing that patents are only valid within India, not globally unless extended through other means like PCT.\n\n---\n\n**3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Patent Term vs. Patent Renewal:**  \n  *Confusion*: Students may confuse the term of a patent (20 years) with renewal requirements.  \n  *Differentiator*: The **term is fixed at 20 years**, while **renewal** refers to continuing the patent’s validity through fees, but only if it's still in force — this is not directly covered under Section 53.\n\n- **Exclusive Rights vs. Licensing:**  \n  *Confusion*: Students may think that having exclusive rights means they cannot license the patent.  \n  *Differentiator*: **Exclusive rights** allow the patentee to **monetize** through **licensing**, but they are not restricted from doing so — in fact, it’s a common and legal practice under Section 48.\n\n- **Territoriality vs. International Protection:**  \n  *Confusion*: Students may think that a patent grants global rights by default.  \n  *Differentiator*: A patent is **territorial**, meaning it only applies within India unless the owner seeks protection in other countries through mechanisms like the **PCT** (covered under Section 57), which is not part of the Patents Act, 1970 itself.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you quickly recall key concepts related to what a patent is, the rights it confers, its term, and its territorial nature — all based on the **Patents Act, 1970**.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The term of a patent is 20 years from the date of filing. This period is specified to provide inventors with a limited monopoly for their invention.","question":"What is the term of a patent under the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"answer":"A patentee has the exclusive right to prevent others from making, using, selling, or importing the patented product in India. This is outlined in Section 48(a) of the Act.","question":"What exclusive rights does a patentee have for a product?"},{"answer":"Yes, a patentee can enforce rights from the date of publication until grant. This is because the patent is effective from publication under Section 46(1).","question":"Can a patentee enforce rights before the patent is granted?"},{"answer":"Patents are territorial because they are valid only in the country where they are granted. Section 46(1) states that a patent has effect throughout India, but not beyond.","question":"Why are patents considered territorial?"},{"answer":"A patent is effective throughout India from the date of grant. Section 46(1) mandates that every patent has effect in the entire territory of India.","question":"What is the procedure for a patent's effect in India?"},{"answer":"The 20-year term incentivizes inventors to disclose technology publicly in exchange for exclusive rights. This is highlighted in the Patents Act, 1970, and legal cases like Raj Prakash vs. Mangat Ram Choudhary.","question":"What is the significance of the 20-year term for patents?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 53 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Term of patent”.","options":["Section 12","Section 18","Section 110","Section 53"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Term of patent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 53 is headed “Term of patent”.","options":["Priority dates of claims of a complete specification","Applications for restoration of lapsed patents","Other provisions of Act to apply to convention applications","Term of patent"],"question":"Section 53 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 11A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Publication of applications”.","options":["Section 18","Section 67","Section 11A","Section 28"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Publication of applications?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 11A is headed “Publication of applications”.","options":["Surrender of patents","Power of Controller to give directions to co-owners","Publication of applications","Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of"],"question":"Section 11A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 48 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Rights of patentees”.","options":["Section 38","Section 48","Section 11B","Section 154"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Rights of patentees?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 48 is headed “Rights of patentees”.","options":["Service of notices, etc. by post","Rights of patentees","Protection of security of India","No anticipation if circumstances are only as described in"],"question":"Section 48 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The term is twenty years from the date of filing of the application for the patent, as stated in Section 53(1).","options":["Twenty years from the date of filing of the application for the patent.","Twenty years from the date of grant of the patent.","Fifteen years from the date of filing of the application for the patent.","Twenty years from the date of publication of the application."],"question":"What is the term of a patent granted under the Patents Act, 1970, after the commencement of the Patents (Amendment) Act, 2002?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The patent ceases to have effect if the renewal fee is not paid within the prescribed period, as per Section 53(2).","options":["The patent ceases to have effect.","The patent is extended for an additional five years.","The patent is transferred to the government.","The patent becomes a patent of addition."],"question":"What happens to a patent if the renewal fee is not paid within the prescribed period?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Applications with a secrecy direction under Section 35 are excluded from publication until the direction ceases, as per Section 11A(3)(a).","options":["If a secrecy direction is imposed under Section 35.","If the applicant requests early publication.","If the application is published after the expiry of the period prescribed under sub-section (1).","If the application is withdrawn three months prior to the period specified under sub-section (1)."],"question":"Under which circumstance is an application for a patent NOT published under Section 11A(3)?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The publication includes the date of application, number, applicant's details, and an abstract, as specified in Section 11A(5).","options":["The date of application, application number, applicant's details, and an abstract.","The full specification, claims, and drawings of the invention.","The names of all inventors and their addresses.","The renewal fee payment history."],"question":"What is included in the publication of a patent application under Section 11A(5)?"}]},"s1p_2":{"notes":"# Definitions under Section 2 — Invention, Inventive Step, New Invention, Capable of Industrial Application  \n\nThese definitions are crucial for understanding patentability criteria under the *Patents Act, 1970*. They form the basis of what constitutes a valid invention and how it must be assessed in terms of novelty, non-obviousness, and industrial applicability. Below is a detailed breakdown of each term as defined by the Act and explained in authoritative textbooks.\n\n---\n\n## **1. Invention (Section 2(1)(j))**\n\n### **Definition:**\n> \"Invention\" means a new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application.\n\n### **Key Concepts:**\n- An *invention* must be either a **new product** or a **new process**.\n- It must involve an **inventive step**, meaning it is not obvious to someone skilled in the relevant field.\n- It must be **capable of being used in industry**, i.e., have practical application.\n\n### **Why the Law Is As It Is:**\nThe requirement for an invention to be \"new\" and to involve an \"inventive step\" ensures that only truly novel and non-obvious innovations are granted patent protection. This prevents trivial or obvious modifications from being monopolized, thereby promoting genuine technological progress (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 563).\n\n### **Relevant Case:**\n- *(Verify this case with a case-law database)*\n\n---\n\n## **2. Inventive Step (Section 2(1)(ja))**\n\n### **Definition:**\n> \"Inventive step\" means a feature of an invention that involves technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having economic significance or both and that makes the invention not obvious to a person skilled in the art.\n\n### **Key Concepts:**\n- An *inventive step* is a **technical advancement** over what was known before.\n- It may also have **economic significance**, such as reducing costs, increasing efficiency, or enabling new applications.\n- The invention must be **non-obvious** to someone with expertise in the field.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 2 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 2. Definitions and interpretation.\n> (1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal\n> representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee\n> of any person include references to the assignee of the legal\n> representative or assignee of that person;\n> (aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the\n> International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the\n> purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April,\n> 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;\n> (ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an\n> invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used\n> in an industry;\n> (b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs\n> and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;\n> (c) “convention application” means an application for a patent made\n> by virtue of section 135;\n> (d) “convention country\" means a country or a country which is\n> member of a group of countries or a union of countries or an Inter-\n> governmental organisation referred to as a convention country in\n> section 133;\n> (e) “district court” has the meaning assigned to that expression by\n> the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908);\n> (f) “exclusive licence” means a licence from a patentee which\n> confers on the licensee, or on the licensee and persons authorised\n> by him, to the exclusion of all other persons (including the\n>\n>  8\n>\n> patentee), any right in respect of the patented invention, and\n> “exclusive licensee” shall be construed accordingly;\n> (h) “Government undertaking” means any industrial undertaking\n> carried on\n> (i) by a department of the Government, or\n> (ii) by a corporation established by a Central, Provincial or State\n> Act, which is owned or controlled by the Government, or\n> (iii) by a Government company as defined in section 617 of the\n> Companies Act, 1956 (1 of\n> 1956), or\n> 1(iv) by an institution wholly or substantially financed by the\n> Government;\n> (i) “High Court\", in relation to a State or Union territory, means\n> the High Court having territorial jurisdiction in that State or\n> Union territory, as the case may be;\n> (ia) “international application” means an application for patent\n> made in accordance with the Patent Cooperation Treaty;\n> (j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an\n> inventive step and capable of industrial application;\n> (ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves\n> technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having\n> economic significance or both and that makes the invention not\n> obvious to a person skilled in the art;\n> (k) “legal representative” means a person who in law represents the\n> estate of a deceased person;\n> (l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not\n> been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the\n> country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of\n> patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject\n> matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part\n> of the state of the art;\n> (la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under\n> sub-section (3) of section 25;\n> (m) “patent” means a patent for any invention granted under this\n> Act;\n> (n) “patent agent” means a person for the time being registered\n> under this Act as a patent agent;\n> (o) “patented article” and “patented process” mean respectively an\n> article or process in respect of which a patent is in force;\n> … [truncated]\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Define and explain the term 'Invention' under Section 2(1)(j) of the Patents Act, 1970. Discuss its significance in determining patentability.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** The definition and legal significance of the term \"invention\" as per Section 2(1)(j), and how it influences the determination of whether an invention is patentable.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 2(1)(j)**, “Invention” means a new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application. The requirement for novelty and non-obviousness is essential to ensure that only truly innovative ideas are granted patent protection (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 563). This aligns with the principle that patents should promote genuine innovation rather than minor or obvious modifications.\n\n**Application:** In order for an invention to be patentable, it must meet three key criteria: **novelty**, **inventive step**, and **industrial applicability**. Novelty means that the invention is new and has not been disclosed in any prior art before the date of filing. Inventive step implies that the invention is not obvious to a person skilled in the relevant field, and industrial applicability requires that it can be used in some form of industry or manufacturing process.\n\nFor example, if an inventor develops a new type of battery with significantly higher energy efficiency than existing models, this would likely meet all three criteria. However, if the improvement is merely incremental—such as increasing the capacity by only 5% without any substantial technical advancement—it may fail to satisfy the inventive step requirement and thus not be considered patentable.\n\n**Conclusion:** The term “invention” under Section 2(1)(j) sets a high bar for patentability, ensuring that only truly novel and non-obvious innovations are protected. This promotes technological progress by granting exclusivity only to those who contribute meaningfully to the field of technology or industry.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the importance of industrial applicability in defining an invention. It is not enough for something to be new or inventive; it must also have practical, commercial use in some form of industry.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What does 'Inventive Step' mean under Section 2(1)(ja) of the Patents Act, 1970? How does this concept affect the grant of a patent?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** The legal definition of \"inventive step\" and its role in determining whether an invention is non-obvious and thus eligible for patent protection.\n\n**Rule:** **Section 2(1)(ja)** defines “Inventive Step” as a feature of an invention that involves technical advance compared to the existing knowledge or has economic significance, and which makes the invention not obvious to a person skilled in the art. This requirement ensures that only inventions with genuine innovation are granted patents (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 563).\n\n**Application:** The concept of inventive step is crucial because it prevents trivial or obvious modifications from being patented. For instance, if an inventor improves a known machine by replacing one part with another that performs the same function in the same way, this would not constitute an inventive step and thus would be rejected as patentable.\n\nHowever, if the improvement involves a new method of assembly that significantly enhances performance—such as reducing energy consumption or increasing durability—this could qualify as an inventive step. The determination is made by comparing the invention to prior art and assessing whether the feature in question was obvious to someone skilled in the field at the time of filing.\n\n**Conclusion:** The concept of “inventive step” under Section 2(1)(ja) ensures that only truly innovative features are protected, thereby promoting meaningful technological advancement. It acts as a filter against minor or obvious modifications that do not contribute significantly to the state of the art.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse \"inventive step\" with novelty. While both are important, they are distinct criteria: novelty concerns whether the invention is new, while inventive step concerns whether it is non-obvious and involves a technical or economic advance.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the meaning of 'Capable of Industrial Application' as defined under Section 2(1)(ac) of the Patents Act, 1970. How does this criterion influence patentability?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** The legal definition of \"capable of industrial application\" and its role in determining whether an invention can be patented.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 2(1)(ac)**, “Capable of Industrial Application” means that the invention is capable of being made or used in an industry. This criterion ensures that only inventions with practical utility are eligible for patent protection (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 563).\n\n**Application:** The requirement of industrial applicability ensures that the invention is not purely theoretical or abstract but has real-world use in some form of industry. For example, a new chemical compound may be novel and involve an inventive step, but if it cannot be manufactured or used in any practical application—such as for pharmaceuticals, agriculture, or manufacturing—it would fail this criterion.\n\nOn the other hand, an invention that can be produced and applied in an industrial process, such as a new method of producing synthetic fibers with improved strength and durability, would satisfy the requirement of being capable of industrial application.\n\n**Conclusion:** The concept of “capable of industrial application” ensures that only inventions with practical use are granted patents. This prevents the granting of patents for purely theoretical or academic ideas that do not contribute to industry or commerce.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to understand the distinction between a scientific discovery and an invention. A scientific discovery may be novel, but unless it can be applied in some form of industry, it cannot be patented.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Definitions under Section 2 — Invention, Inventive Step, New Invention, Capable of Industrial Application\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 2(1)(j) | **HEN** + clause (1)(j) | Hen clucks a new product, inventing steps for industrial use. |\n| Sec. 2(1)(ja) | **HEN** + clause (1)(ja) | Hen lays egg with technical advance, non-obvious to others. |\n| Sec. 2(1)(l) | **HEN** + clause (1)(l) | Hen discovers new invention, absolute novelty in its feathers. |\n| Sec. 2(1)(ac) | **HEN** + clause (1)(ac) | Hen's eggshell forms industrial application, sturdy and useful. |\n| Sec. 2(1)(ab) | **HEN** + clause (1)(ab) | Hen embodies invention's definition: novel, useful, industrial. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **2** - definitions\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Invention means a new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n2. Inventive step — technical advance or economic significance and non-obviousness - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n3. New invention and absolute novelty - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n4. Capable of industrial application - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n5. Definitions under Section 2 — Invention, Inventive Step, New Invention, Capable of Industrial Application - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 2(1)(j) | Invention is new product/process with inventive step and industrial use | New idea, not obvious, used in industry  \n- Section 2(1)(ja) | Inventive step involves technical advance or economic significance, non-obvious to experts | Not obvious, has value, technical jump  \n- Section 2(1)(l) | New invention is one not previously published or used anywhere globally | No prior use, no public knowledge  \n- Section 2(1)(ac) | Capable of industrial application means can be made or used in industry | Works in real life, practical use  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing \"inventive step\" with mere improvement – it must be non-obvious  \n- Examiners expect clear distinction between novelty and inventive step  \n- Always structure answers by citing the exact section, then define, explain, and give examples","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, in a quiet neighborhood near Charminar, lived a young woman named Anjali, who had recently passed her exams to become a patent agent. She was determined to make a name for herself and help local innovators protect their ideas.\n\nOne day, she received an urgent call from a small tech startup called \"InnovateTech\" located in Banjara Hills. Their founder, Arjun, had developed a new kind of solar-powered water purifier that could be used in rural areas with limited access to clean drinking water. He needed help filing for a patent.\n\nAnjali sat down with the documents and began reading through them carefully. She knew that before she could proceed with the application, she had to make sure that Arjun’s invention met all the criteria under **Section 2** of *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\nAs she read, she recalled her notes from her textbook — a thick, worn-out copy of *\"Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\"*. She opened it to **page 563**, where Dr. Sheetal Chopra explained the importance of defining what an invention is under **Section 2(1)(j)**.\n\nAccording to that section, an *invention* must be a new product or process involving an **inventive step** and **capable of industrial application**. Anjali made sure she understood each part clearly.\n\nShe thought about Arjun’s purifier — was it truly new? Yes, because no one had developed a water purifier that used solar energy in such an efficient way before. Was there an **inventive step** involved? That meant the idea wasn’t obvious to someone with knowledge of the field. She checked several existing patents and scientific papers and found that Arjun’s method was indeed non-obvious.\n\nThen came the part about **capable of industrial application**, which was defined in **Section 2(1)(ac)** as being able to be made or used in an industry. Arjun’s purifier clearly met this requirement — it could be manufactured, distributed, and used by millions of people across India.\n\nShe also had to consider the term “**inventive step**”, which was defined under **Section 2(1)(ja)** as a feature that involved technical advance or economic significance and made the invention non-obvious. Arjun’s design had both — it was more efficient than existing models, reducing energy costs significantly.\n\nNext, she looked at the definition of “**new invention**” in **Section 2(l)**, which meant any invention not previously published or used anywhere in the world before the patent application was filed. Anjali checked all available databases and found no prior disclosure — Arjun’s idea was indeed new.\n\nAs she worked through these definitions, Anjali remembered a rule from her textbook: **\"The law ensures that only truly novel and non-obvious innovations are granted patents to promote genuine technological progress.\"** This made sense. If the patent system allowed every small change or obvious improvement to be patented, it would stifle innovation rather than encourage it.\n\nShe also knew she had to be careful with her calculations — the number of documents she reviewed was **twenty-one**, and each one had to be checked for prior art. She also had to file the application before **Section 73** of the Act expired, which gave a time limit on how long an application could remain pending.\n\nAfter several days of preparation, Anjali submitted the patent application with confidence. Arjun was thrilled — his invention now had a chance to change lives across India.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 2(1)(j) | \"Invention\" | An invention must be a new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application. |\n| Section 2(1)(ac) | Capable of Industrial Application | An invention is capable of being made or used in an industry. |\n| Section 2(1)(ja) | Inventive Step | A feature that involves technical advance, economic significance, or both, making the invention non-obvious to a person skilled in the art. |\n| Section 2(l) | New Invention | Any invention not previously published or used anywhere before the patent application was filed. |\n| Section 73 | Time limit for filing | The time period within which an application must be processed and decided upon by the Controller General. |\n| Twenty-one documents | Prior art search | Anjali reviewed twenty-one documents to ensure Arjun’s invention was novel and non-obvious. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for Definitions under Section 2 — Patent Agent Examination**\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Invention (Section 2(1)(j))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic:** **N.I.P.S.**  \n  *New, Inventive, Practical, and Suitable* – This captures the essence of an invention being new, involving an inventive step, capable of industrial application, and suitable for use.\n\n- **Number-anchor (Section 2(1)(j)):**  \n  *Imagine \"2\" as a swan swimming in water. The swan represents the “invention” because it is a **new**, **innovative** creation that moves through the **industrial application** waters.*\n\n- **Confusion Pair:** Invention vs. Scientific Principle  \n  *An invention must be practical and useful, while a scientific principle is merely theoretical and not patentable (Section 2(1)(c)).*\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Inventive Step (Section 2(1)(ja))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic:** **T.E.A.M.**  \n  *Technical, Economic, Advantageous, and Mental* – This reflects that an inventive step involves a technical or economic advance not obvious to someone skilled in the art.\n\n- **Number-anchor (Section 2(1)(ja)):**  \n  *Think of \"2\" as a pair of glasses. The glasses help you see clearly, just like an inventive step helps you **see** something new and non-obvious.*\n\n- **Confusion Pair:** Inventive Step vs. Novelty  \n  *Novelty is about being new (not known before), while an inventive step is about not being obvious to someone skilled in the field.*\n\n---\n\n#### **3. New Invention (Section 2(1)(l))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic:** **N.E.W.**  \n  *Not, Elsewhere, or Widely* – This helps you remember that a new invention must not have been anticipated by any publication or used elsewhere before the filing date.\n\n- **Number-anchor (Section 2(1)(l)):**  \n  *Visualize \"2\" as a “no entry” sign. The sign means something is **new**, and nothing has come in before it.*\n\n- **Confusion Pair:** New Invention vs. Prior Art  \n  *A new invention must not be found in prior art, which includes all existing knowledge or use of the invention anywhere in the world.*\n\n---\n\n#### **4. Capable of Industrial Application (Section 2(1)(ac))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic:** **I.N.D.U.S.T.R.Y.**  \n  *Inventive, New, Done, Used, Suitable, Technically, Relevant, Yielding* – This highlights that the invention must be able to be made or used in an industry.\n\n- **Number-anchor (Section 2(1)(ac)):**  \n  *Think of \"2\" as a factory. The factory represents where inventions are made and used — hence, **capable of industrial application**.*\n\n- **Confusion Pair:** Capable of Industrial Application vs. Scientific Principle  \n  *A scientific principle is not capable of industrial application because it’s theoretical, while an invention must be practical and usable in industry.*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"An invention is a new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application. Section 2(1)(j) explicitly states this definition.","question":"What defines an invention under the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"answer":"An inventive step involves technical advance, economic significance, or both, making the invention non-obvious to a skilled person. Section 2(1)(ja) defines this requirement.","question":"What constitutes an inventive step according to Section 2(1)(ja)?"},{"answer":"It must be possible to make or use the invention in any kind of industry. This is a mandatory criterion under the definition of invention in Section 2(1)(j).","question":"What is required for an invention to be 'capable of industrial application'?"},{"answer":"A complete specification must be filed within 12 months of the provisional application. This is a procedural requirement under the Patents Act.","question":"How long does a patentee have to file a complete specification after a provisional application?"},{"answer":"Non-obviousness ensures the invention is not trivial to a skilled person, reflecting technical or economic advancement. This is central to the inventive step requirement.","question":"Why is non-obviousness critical for patentability?"},{"answer":"The invention cannot be patented as novelty is a prerequisite under Section 2(1)(j), requiring the invention to be new globally.","question":"What happens if an invention lacks absolute novelty?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Section 2","Section 136","Section 32","Section 87"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Definitions and interpretation?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 2 is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Meaning of use of invention for purposes of Government","Definitions and interpretation","General purposes for granting compulsory licences","Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of"],"question":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"It means the invention is capable of being made or used in an industry.","options":["The invention can be made or used in an industry","The invention is novel and non-obvious","The invention is useful for academic research","The invention is protected under the Budapest Treaty"],"question":"What does 'capable of industrial application' mean in relation to an invention under the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The Controller is defined as the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks.","options":["The Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks","The Chairman of the Opposition Board","The Director General of the Patent Office","The Registrar of Trademarks"],"question":"Who is referred to as the 'Controller' under the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"An invention is defined as a new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application.","options":["A new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application","A discovery of a natural substance","A mathematical method","A work of art"],"question":"Which of the following correctly defines an 'invention' under the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"An inventive step involves technical advance or economic significance and makes the invention non-obvious.","options":["A feature involving technical advance or economic significance, making the invention non-obvious","A feature that is novel and previously unpublished","A feature that is protected under the Budapest Treaty","A feature that is used in a Government undertaking"],"question":"What constitutes an 'inventive step' according to the Patents Act, 1970?"}]},"s1p_3":{"notes":"# Other Key Definitions — Patentee, Person Interested, Priority Date, Convention Country  \n\n## 1. **Patentee**  \n### Definition:  \nA patentee is a person who is granted a patent under the Patents Act, 1970. The term \"patentee\" includes not only the original applicant but also any subsequent assignees or legal representatives of the original applicant (Section 2(1)(vii)).  \n\n### Key Concepts:  \n- A patentee holds exclusive rights to use, sell, and license the invention for a period of twenty years from the date of filing the application.  \n- The term \"patentee\" may also include co-applicants or joint owners in cases where multiple parties are involved in the invention.  \n\n### Legal Rationale:  \nThe Patents Act defines the rights and responsibilities of a patentee to ensure that only those who have been granted legal protection by the Controller can enforce their exclusive rights (Section 2(1)(vii)). This helps prevent unauthorized use or exploitation of an invention, thereby encouraging innovation and investment in research and development.  \n\n### Textbook Explanation:  \nThe role of a patentee is central to the functioning of the patent system because it establishes legal ownership over the invention. The Act ensures that only the rightful owner can enforce the rights attached to a patent (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 89).  \n\n### Verified Cases:  \n(verify this case with a case-law database)  \n\n---\n\n## 2. **Person Interested**  \n### Definition:  \nA \"person interested\" is defined in Section 2(1)(xxii) of the Patents Act, 1970 as any person who has an interest in the grant or revocation of a patent. This includes opponents during opposition proceedings and other parties who may be affected by the outcome of such proceedings.  \n\n### Key Concepts:  \n- A \"person interested\" is not limited to the applicant or the opponent; it can also include competitors, researchers, or any individual or entity that may benefit from or be harmed by a patent.  \n- The term includes both natural and legal persons who have an interest in the subject matter of the patent.  \n\n### Legal Rationale:  \nThe inclusion of \"person interested\" in the Act ensures transparency and fairness in the patent system. It allows any party with a legitimate stake to challenge or support the grant of a patent, thereby preventing misuse or monopolization (Section 2(1)(xxii)).  \n\n### Textbook Explanation:  \nThe concept of “person interested” is crucial for maintaining the balance between the rights of the patentee and the public interest. It allows any party who may be affected by the outcome of a patent to participate in the process, ensuring that the grant or revocation of a patent is fair and just (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 121).  \n\n### Verified Cases:\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 2 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 2. Definitions and interpretation.\n> (1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal\n> representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee\n> of any person include references to the assignee of the legal\n> representative or assignee of that person;\n> (aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the\n> International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the\n> purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April,\n> 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;\n> (ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an\n> invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used\n> in an industry;\n> (b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs\n> and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;\n> (c) “convention application” means an application for a patent made\n> by virtue of section 135;\n> (d) “convention country\" means a country or a country which is\n> member of a group of countries or a union of countries or an Inter-\n> governmental organisation referred to as a convention country in\n> section 133;\n> (e) “district court” has the meaning assigned to that expression by\n> the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908);\n> (f) “exclusive licence” means a licence from a patentee which\n> confers on the licensee, or on the licensee and persons authorised\n> by him, to the exclusion of all other persons (including the\n>\n>  8\n>\n> patentee), any right in respect of the patented invention, and\n> “exclusive licensee” shall be construed accordingly;\n> (h) “Government undertaking” means any industrial undertaking\n> carried on\n> (i) by a department of the Government, or\n> (ii) by a corporation established by a Central, Provincial or State\n> Act, which is owned or controlled by the Government, or\n> (iii) by a Government company as defined in section 617 of the\n> Companies Act, 1956 (1 of\n> 1956), or\n> 1(iv) by an institution wholly or substantially financed by the\n> Government;\n> (i) “High Court\", in relation to a State or Union territory, means\n> the High Court having territorial jurisdiction in that State or\n> Union territory, as the case may be;\n> (ia) “international application” means an application for patent\n> made in accordance with the Patent Cooperation Treaty;\n> (j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an\n> inventive step and capable of industrial application;\n> (ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves\n> technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having\n> economic significance or both and that makes the invention not\n> obvious to a person skilled in the art;\n> (k) “legal representative” means a person who in law represents the\n> estate of a deceased person;\n> (l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not\n> been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the\n> country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of\n> patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject\n> matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part\n> of the state of the art;\n> (la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under\n> sub-section (3) of section 25;\n> (m) “patent” means a patent for any invention granted under this\n> Act;\n> (n) “patent agent” means a person for the time being registered\n> under this Act as a patent agent;\n> (o) “patented article” and “patented process” mean respectively an\n> article or process in respect of which a patent is in force;\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 11 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11. Priority dates of claims of a complete specification.\n> (1) There shall be a priority date for each claim of a complete\n> specification.\n>\n> (2) Where a complete specification is filed in pursuance of a single\n> application accompanied by\n> (a) a provisional specification; or\n> (b) a specification which is treated by virtue of a direction under\n> sub-section (3) of section 9 as a provisional specification,\n> and the claim is fairly based on the matter disclosed in the\n> specification referred to in clause (a) or clause (b), the priority\n>\n>  17\n>\n> date of that claim shall be the date of the filing of the relevant\n> specification.\n>\n> (3) Where the complete specification is filed or proceeded with in\n> pursuance of two or more applications accompanied by such\n> specifications as are mentioned in sub-section (2) and the claim is\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed\n> (a) in one of those specifications, the priority date of that claim\n> shall be the date of the filing of the application accompanied by\n> that specification;\n> (b) partly in one and partly in another, the priority date of that\n> claim shall be the date of the filing of the application accompanied\n> by the specification of the later date.\n>\n> (3A) Where a complete specification based on a previously filed\n> application in India has been filed within twelve months from the\n> date of that application and the claim is fairly based on the matter\n> disclosed in the previously filed application, the priority date of\n> that claim shall be the date of the previously filed application in\n> which the matter was first disclosed.\n>\n> (4) Where the complete specification has been filed in pursuance of\n> a further application made by virtue of sub-section (1) of section\n> 16 and the claim is fairly based on the matter disclosed in any of\n> the earlier specifications, provisional or complete, as the case may\n> be, the priority date of that claim shall be the date of the filing\n> of that specification in which the matter was first disclosed.\n>\n> (5) Where, under the foregoing provisions of this section, any claim\n> of a complete specification would, but for the provisions of this\n> sub-section, have two or more priority dates, the priority date of\n> that claim shall be the earlier or earliest of those dates.\n>\n> (6) In any case to which sub-sections (2), (3), 1(3A), (4) and (5)\n> do not apply, the priority date of a claim shall, subject to the\n> provisions of section 137, be the date of filing of the complete\n> specification.\n>\n> (7) The reference to the date of the filing of the application or of\n> the complete specification in this section shall, in cases where\n> there has been a post-dating under section 9 or section 17 or, as\n>\n>  18\n>\n> the case may be, ante-dating under section 16, be a reference to the\n> date as so post-dated or ante-dated.\n>\n> (8) A claim in a complete specification of a patent shall not be\n> invalid by reason only of\n> (a) the publication or use of the invention so far as claimed in\n> that claim on or after the priority date of such claim; or\n> (b) the grant of another patent which claims the invention, so far\n> as claimed in the first mentioned claim, in a claim of the same or a\n> later priority date.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER IV PUBLICATION AND EXAMINATION OF APPLICATIONS\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Define and explain the term \"Patentee\" under the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What is the legal definition of a patentee, and what rights does a patentee hold under the Patents Act?\n\n**Rule:** Section 2(1)(vii) of the Patents Act, 1970 defines \"patentee\" as a person who is granted a patent. It includes the original applicant, subsequent assignees, or legal representatives of the original applicant. The term may also include co-applicants or joint owners in cases involving multiple parties (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 89). A patentee holds exclusive rights to use, sell, and license the invention for twenty years from the date of filing the application.\n\n**Application:** The definition ensures that only those legally granted a patent can enforce their rights. This prevents unauthorized exploitation of inventions and encourages innovation by providing legal protection to inventors. For instance, if an inventor assigns their patent to another party, the assignee becomes the new patentee and holds all the exclusive rights attached to the invention.\n\n**Conclusion:** A patentee is defined in Section 2(1)(vii) as the grantee of a patent, including assignees and legal representatives. The patentee has exclusive rights over the invention for twenty years from filing, which helps protect innovation and investment in research and development.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to mention that the term \"patentee\" includes not only the original applicant but also subsequent assignees or legal representatives. This is a key point under Section 2(1)(vii).\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is meant by the term “Person Interested” in the Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** Who qualifies as a \"person interested\" and why is this concept important in patent law?\n\n**Rule:** Section 2(1)(xxii) of the Patents Act defines a \"person interested\" as any person who has an interest in the grant or revocation of a patent. This includes opponents during opposition proceedings, competitors, researchers, or any party that may be affected by the outcome (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 121). The inclusion of such persons ensures transparency and fairness in the patent process.\n\n**Application:** For example, if a company files for a patent on a new pharmaceutical drug, any competitor or research institution that may be affected by the grant or revocation of that patent is considered a \"person interested.\" They can participate in opposition proceedings to challenge the validity of the application. This prevents monopolization and ensures public interest is protected.\n\n**Conclusion:** A “person interested” under Section 2(1)(xxii) refers to any individual or entity with an interest in the grant or revocation of a patent. The concept is crucial for ensuring transparency, fairness, and preventing misuse of patents.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook that \"person interested\" includes not only opponents but also anyone who may be affected by the outcome of a patent application. This broader scope is essential to understanding the balance between patentee rights and public interest.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Define “Priority Date” under the Patents Act, 1970. Explain its significance in determining the novelty of an invention.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What is a \"priority date,\" and how does it affect the determination of whether an invention is novel?\n\n**Rule:** Section 11 of the Patents Act defines the priority date for each claim in a complete specification. It establishes that if a complete specification is filed based on a prior application, the priority date may be the date of the earlier application (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 17). The significance lies in determining whether an invention was novel at the time of filing.\n\n**Application:** Suppose an inventor files a provisional specification on January 1st and then files a complete specification on April 1st. Under Section 11(2), the priority date for each claim would be January 1st, not April 1st. This means that any prior publication or use of the invention after January 1st but before April 1st would not invalidate the patent, as it was already protected by the earlier filing.\n\n**Conclusion:** The \"priority date\" is crucial in determining novelty because it allows an inventor to claim protection from the date of their first application. This prevents others from using or publishing similar inventions between the first and later filings.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students confuse the priority date with the actual filing date of the complete specification, failing to recognize its importance in preserving novelty based on earlier applications.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is meant by “Convention Country” under the Patents Act, 1970? Explain its relevance in international patent filings.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** Define \"Convention Country\" and explain how it relates to international patent filings.\n\n**Rule:** Section 2(1)(d) defines a \"convention country\" as any country or group of countries that is a member of an international convention, such as the Paris Convention for the Protection of Industrial Property (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 8). This term is relevant in enabling patent applicants to claim priority based on their first filing in a convention country.\n\n**Application:** If an inventor files a patent application in India, they may then file applications in other \"convention countries\" within twelve months without losing the novelty of their invention. The Indian filing date becomes the priority date for those subsequent filings under international treaties like the Paris Convention.\n\n**Conclusion:** A “Convention Country” refers to any member state of an international convention that allows patent applicants to claim priority based on their first application. This facilitates global protection and ensures that inventors are not disadvantaged by delayed filings in other countries.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often neglect to mention that \"convention country\" is tied to specific international treaties, such as the Paris Convention. Understanding this link is essential for grasping the relevance of priority dates in an international context.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Other Key Definitions — Patentee, Person Interested, Priority Date, Convention Country\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 2(1)(p) | **HEN** + clause (1)(p) | HEN clucks over patent documents, pecking at legal claims |\n| Sec. 2(1)(t) | **HEN** + clause (1)(t) | Priority date: HEN scratches timeline, leaving feathers as deadlines |\n| Sec. 2(1)(w) | **HEN** + clause (1)(w) | HEN struts through convention country, crowing at application forms |\n| Sec. 2(1)(d) | **HEN** + clause (1)(d) | HEN pecks at inventor's desk, scattering true claims from false |\n| Sec. 11 | **TOTE** | TOTE bag bursts with convention country forms, spilling application dates |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **2** - definitions\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Patentee and person interested - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n2. Priority date and how it is determined - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n3. Convention country and convention application - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n4. True and first inventor - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n5. Other Key Definitions — Patentee, Person Interested, Priority Date, Convention Country - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 2(1)(vii) | Defines patentee as grant recipient and assignees | Legal ownership image  \n- Section 2(1)(xxii) | Person interested includes those affected by patent decisions | Courtroom with many people  \n- Section 11 | Sets priority dates for claims in specifications | Filing date clock ticking  \n- Section 2(1)(d) | Convention country is a member state or organization | Passport stamp on application  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing patentee with inventor – they are not always the same  \n- Use exact section numbers and definitions from the Patents Act, 1970  \n- Always link priority dates to claim specifics in specifications","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, in a quiet neighborhood near Charminar, lived a young man named Arjun. He was an aspiring patent agent who had recently joined a firm called \"Legal Lenses.\" His first big task was to prepare for the Patent Agent Examination, and he had been given a set of notes on key definitions from the Patents Act, 1970.\n\nOne morning, as Arjun sat in his small room with stacks of books around him, he received an unusual letter. It read: \"There are twenty-one lamps lit in the old city hall — each one representing a section of the Patents Act.\" Intrigued, he decided to go and investigate.\n\nAt the old city hall, he found that indeed, twenty-one lamps were glowing in a room labeled **\"Section 2(1)(vii)\"**. A sign next to it read: \"Here lies the definition of *patentee* — the person who holds exclusive rights over an invention for twenty years from the date of filing.\" Arjun realized that this was the section that defined who could claim ownership and protection under a patent.\n\nAs he moved on, he saw a group of people arguing in front of a door marked **\"Section 2(1)(xxii)\"**. A notice above it said: \"This is where *person interested* comes into play — anyone with a stake in the grant or revocation of a patent may enter.\" Arjun watched as an opponent, named Ravi, challenged the grant of a patent. He saw that even competitors and researchers could step in to ensure fairness.\n\nFurther down the hall, he came across a room filled with books and scrolls. A sign read: **\"Section 11 — Priority Dates\"**. Inside were people who had filed their applications under different dates. One man, named Dev, was arguing that his claim should be dated back to an earlier application he had made in India. Arjun learned that the priority date of a claim could be as early as twelve months before the current filing if it was based on a prior disclosure.\n\nAs he passed through the building, he noticed a room labeled **\"Section 73 — Controller\"**. There were seven sacks of grain stacked outside, and a note read: \"The *Controller* is the authority who oversees patents, designs, and trademarks.\" Arjun now understood that this was the body responsible for granting and managing patents.\n\nFinally, he reached a room marked **\"Convention Country\"**, where maps and treaties were displayed. A sign said: \"This section defines countries that are part of international agreements like the Budapest Treaty — places where inventions can be protected globally under the Patents Act.\"\n\nArjun returned home with new clarity. He had not only seen the numbers in action but also understood their significance.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Section 2(1)(vii) of the Patents Act, 1970 | A patentee is a person granted a patent and includes assignees or legal representatives. |\n| Seven sacks of grain | Section 73 of the Patents Act, 1970 | The Controller is the authority responsible for granting patents and overseeing related matters. |\n| Twenty-one lamps (again) | Section 2(1)(vii) of the Patents Act, 1970 | A patentee holds exclusive rights over an invention for twenty years from the date of filing. |\n| Room labeled \"Section 2(1)(xxii)\" | Section 2(1)(xxii) of the Patents Act, 1970 | A person interested is any individual or entity with a stake in the grant or revocation of a patent. |\n| Room labeled \"Section 11 — Priority Dates\" | Section 11 of the Patents Act, 1970 | The priority date for a claim can be based on an earlier application filed within twelve months if it is fairly supported by that disclosure. |\n| Room labeled \"Convention Country\" | Section 133 of the Patents Act, 1970 | A convention country refers to any member state or international group under which patent applications can be made and recognized globally. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for Key Definitions — Patentee, Person Interested, Priority Date, Convention Country**\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Patentee**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"P\" = *Possessor of the patent*.\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 2** (Definitions and interpretation) – Imagine a person holding a **key** (symbolizing ownership), with the number **2** on it, unlocking rights under the Act.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Patentee vs. Inventor*.  \n  → Patentee is the legal owner; inventor is the creator. Use this: \"Only the **P**atentee can sue for infringement.\"\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Person Interested**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"PI\" = *Person with a vested Interest*.\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 25** (Opposition to the patent) – Picture someone standing in front of a **door labeled “25”**, trying to block entry (interest in opposing a patent).\n- **Confusion pair**: *Person interested vs. Applicant*.  \n  → A person interested may not be an applicant; they have a stake in the outcome. Use this: \"An **applicant** applies, but a **person interested** is someone who cares.\"\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Priority Date**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"PD\" = *Prior Date*.\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 11A** (Publication of applications) – Visualize a **calendar with “11A” on it**, showing the earliest date that matters for novelty.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Priority date vs. Filing date*.  \n  → Priority date is based on an earlier application, not when you file now. Use this: \"The **priority date** is your **first** claim to the invention.\"\n\n---\n\n#### 4. **Convention Country**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"CC\" = *Country with a Convention*.\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 8** (Foreign applications) – Imagine a **world map labeled “8”**, showing countries that recognize international patent filings.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Convention country vs. PCT country*.  \n  → A convention country is under the Paris Convention, while PCT countries are part of the Patent Cooperation Treaty. Use this: \"PCT is for **international** applications; **convention** is for **regional** ones.\"","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The priority date is the earliest filing date in a convention country. It is determined by the first application filed in any convention country under the Paris Convention.","question":"What is the priority date and how is it determined?"},{"answer":"The true and first inventor's consent is required for public displays or publications to avoid anticipation. Their rights are protected under Section 31(3).","question":"What role does the true and first inventor play in anticipation?"},{"answer":"A convention country application is a patent application filed in a country party to the Paris Convention, claiming priority from an earlier application.","question":"What is a convention country application?"},{"answer":"Applicants must submit a declaration stating they are the true inventor or assignee and claim priority from a convention country application, as per the Patents Rules, 2003.","question":"What procedure applies to declarations for convention applications?"},{"answer":"Consent from the true and first inventor is required for public displays at exhibitions to prevent the invention from being deemed anticipated under Section 31(1)(a).","question":"Why is the true and first inventor’s consent important for exhibitions?"},{"answer":"If an invention is commercially used in India before the priority date, it may anticipate the claim under Section 31(2), unless for reasonable trial.","question":"What happens if an invention is used in India before the priority date?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Section 2","Section 159","Section 42","Section 58"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Definitions and interpretation?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 2 is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Offences by companies","Definitions and interpretation","Procedure for disposal of applications for restoration of lapsed","Determination of certain contracts"],"question":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 11 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Order of recording applications”.","options":["Rule 4","Rule 125","Rule 83","Rule 11"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Order of recording applications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 11 is headed “Order of recording applications”.","options":["Payment of fees","Order of recording applications","Form of reference to another specification","Processing of demands for international preliminary"],"question":"Rule 11 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 11 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Priority dates of claims of a complete specification”.","options":["Section 11","Section 107","Section 50","Section 137"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Priority dates of claims of a complete specification?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 11 is headed “Priority dates of claims of a complete specification”.","options":["Short title, extent and commencement","Priority dates of claims of a complete specification","Secrecy directions to be periodically reviewed","Exercise of discretionary powers by Controller"],"question":"Section 11 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"}]},"s1p_4":{"notes":"# **Novelty and Anticipation — What Destroys Newness**\n\n## **Definition**\nThe concept of *novelty* in patent law refers to the requirement that an invention must be new, meaning it has not been disclosed or used publicly anywhere in the world before the date of filing a patent application. If an invention is already known or used by others, it lacks novelty and cannot be patented.\n\n**Key Section:**\n- **Section 2(1)(x)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* defines \"invention\" as something that is new and involves an inventive step.\n- **Sections 29 to 34** of the *Patents Act, 1970* outline various forms of anticipation that can destroy novelty.\n\n## **Key Concepts**\n\n### **What Destroys Novelty (Anticipation)**\nThe following are recognized as destroying the novelty of an invention:\n\n1. **Prior Publication:** \n   - If the subject matter of the invention has been disclosed in any publication before the filing date, it is considered anticipated.\n   - **Section 29** states that disclosure in a prior publication destroys novelty.\n\n2. **Prior Communication to Government:**\n   - Any communication of the invention to the government before the application was filed can also be an anticipation.\n   - This is covered under **Section 30**.\n\n3. **Public Display, Use or Sale:**\n   - If the invention has been publicly displayed, used, or sold anywhere in the world before the filing date, it lacks novelty.\n   - **Section 31** discusses this form of anticipation.\n\n4. **Prior Use by Others:**\n   - Any use of the invention by someone else before the applicant filed the patent application is a ground for rejection on the basis of lack of novelty.\n   - This is covered under **Section 32**.\n\n5. **Secret Prior Art:**\n   - Even if a prior art document was not publicly available, but it had an earlier priority date and was published after the filing date, it can still destroy the novelty of the invention.\n   - Such documents are termed \"hidden or secret prior art\" and are addressed in **Section 33**.\n\n6. **Disclosure by Others:**\n   - If the invention has been disclosed to others before the application was filed (e.g., through a public demonstration, sale, etc.), it is considered anticipated.\n   - This falls under **Section 34**.\n\n### **Why the Law Is As It Is**\n\nThe law on novelty and anticipation exists to ensure that only truly new inventions are granted patent protection. The rationale behind this is:\n\n- To prevent multiple parties from being granted patents for the same invention, which would lead to confusion and hinder innovation.\n- To promote transparency in the field of inventions by ensuring that all prior knowledge is made public before granting a patent.\n\nAs explained on **page 57** of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, \"the subject matter of the invention should not have fallen within the public domain\" for it to be considered novel. The law ensures that only inventions which are truly new, as opposed to those already known or used, receive protection.\n\n### **The Role of Prior Art Search**\n\nA prior art search is essential before filing a patent application because:\n\n- It helps identify if the invention is already known or used in the public domain.\n- If an invention lacks novelty, it cannot be patented and the applicant may lose time and resources unnecessarily.\n- It also allows for modifications to the invention or the claims in the specification to avoid rejection on grounds of lack of novelty.\n\nAs stated on **page 33** of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, a case was analyzed where the court found that an invention was not novel because it was merely an application of an old method known for several decades. This highlights the importance of conducting a thorough prior art search.\n\n### **What Happens if Novelty Is Lacked**\n\nIf an invention is found to lack novelty, the patent may be revoked or rejected at any stage of the process:\n\n- During examination by the Controller (under **Section 15**).\n- During opposition proceedings (under **Section 25**).\n- Even after grant, if it is challenged on grounds of invalidity.\n\nAs explained in **page 61**, when a court evaluates the validity of a patent based on inventive step or novelty, it considers whether the prior art provides a clear teaching and roadmap to arrive at the invention. It also looks for evidence that human ingenuity was involved in the development of the invention.\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\nNovelty is one of the fundamental requirements for granting a patent under *The Patents Act, 1970*. The law ensures that only new inventions are protected by defining various forms of anticipation and requiring prior art searches. Understanding these concepts is crucial for anyone preparing to appear in the Patent Agent Examination.\n\n**Note:** No verified or candidate cases are available for this chapter. If a case would normally support a point, write \"(verify this case with a case-law database)\" instead.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 13 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 13. Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior\n> claim.\n> (1) The examiner to whom an application for a patent is referred\n> under section 12 shall make investigation for the purpose of\n> ascertaining whether the invention so far as claimed in any claim of\n> the complete specification\n> (a) has been anticipated by publication before the date of filing of\n> the applicant’s complete specification in any specification filed in\n> pursuance of an application for a patent made in India and dated on\n> or after the 1st day of January, 1912;\n> (b) is claimed in any claim of any other complete specification\n> published on or after the date of filing of the applicant’s complete\n> specification, being a specification filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent made in India and dated before or claiming\n> the priority date earlier than that date.\n>\n> (2) The examiner shall, in addition, make such investigation for the\n> purpose of ascertaining whether the invention, so far as claimed in\n> any claim of the complete specification, has been anticipated by\n> publication in India or elsewhere in any document other than those\n>\n>  22\n>\n> mentioned in sub-section (1) before the date of filing of the\n> applicant’s complete specification.\n>\n> (3) Where a complete specification is amended under the provisions\n> of this Act before the grant of a patent, the amended specification\n> shall be examined and investigated in like manner as the original\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) The examination and investigations required under section 12 and\n> this section shall not be deemed in any way to warrant the validity\n> of any patent, and no liability shall be incurred by the Central\n> Government or any officer thereof by reason of, or in connection\n> with, any such examination or investigation or any report or other\n> proceedings consequent thereon.\n\n**Section 29 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 29. Anticipation by previous publication.\n> (1) An invention claimed in a complete specification shall not be\n> deemed to have been anticipated by reason only that the invention\n> was published in a specification filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent made in India and dated before the 1st day\n> of January, 1912.\n>\n> (2) Subject as hereinafter provided, an invention claimed in a\n> complete specification shall not be deemed to have been anticipated\n> by reason only that the invention was published before the priority\n> date of the relevant claim of the specification, if the patentee or\n> the applicant for the patent proves\n> (a) that the matter published was obtained from him, or (where he is\n> not himself the true and first inventor) from any person from whom\n> he derives title, and was published without his consent or the\n> consent of any such person; and\n> (b) where the patentee or the applicant for the patent or any person\n> from whom he derives title learned of the publication before the\n> date of the application for the patent, or, in the case of a\n> convention application, before the date of the application for\n> protection in a convention country, that the application or the\n> application in the convention country, as the case may be, was made\n> as soon as reasonably practicable thereafter:\n> Provided that this sub-section shall not apply if the invention was\n> before the priority date of the claim commercially worked in India,\n> otherwise than for the purpose of reasonable trial, either by the\n> patentee or the applicant for the patent or any person from whom he\n> derives title or by any other person with the consent of the\n> patentee or the applicant for the patent or any person from whom he\n> derives title.\n>\n> (3) Where a complete specification is filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent made by a person being the true and first\n> inventor or deriving title from him, an invention claimed in that\n> specification shall not be deemed to have been anticipated by reason\n> only of any other application for a patent in respect of the same\n> invention made in contravention of the rights of that person, or by\n> reason only that after the date of filing of that other application\n> the invention was used or published, without the consent of that\n>\n>  35\n>\n> person, by the applicant in respect of that other application or by\n> any other person in consequence of any disclosure of any invention\n> by that applicant.\n\n**Section 30 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 30. Anticipation by previous communication to Government.\n> An invention claimed in a complete specification shall not be deemed\n> to have been anticipated by reason only of the communication of the\n> invention to the Government or to any person authorised by the\n> Government to investigate the invention or its merits, or of\n> anything done, in consequence of such a communication, for the\n> purpose of the investigation.\n\n**Section 31 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 31. Anticipation by public display, etc.\n> An invention claimed in a complete specification shall not be deemed\n> to have been anticipated by reason only of\n> (a) the display of the invention with the consent of the true and\n> first inventor or a person deriving title from him at an industrial\n> or other exhibition to which the provisions of this section have\n> been extended by the Central Government by notification in the\n> Official Gazette, or the use thereof with his consent for the\n> purpose of such an exhibition in the place where it is held; or\n> (b) the publication of any description of the invention in\n> consequence of the display or use of the invention at any such\n> exhibition as aforesaid; or\n> (c) the use of the invention, after it has been displayed or used at\n> any such exhibition as aforesaid and during the period of the\n> exhibition, by any person without the consent of the true and first\n> inventor or a person deriving title from him; or\n> (d) the description of the invention in a paper read by the true and\n> first inventor before a learned society or published with his\n> consent in the transactions of such a society,\n> if the application for the patent is made by the true and first\n> inventor or a person deriving title from him not later than twelve\n> months after the opening of the exhibition or the reading or\n> publication of the paper, as the case may be.\n\n**Section 32 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 32. Anticipation by public working.\n> An invention claimed in a complete specification shall not be deemed\n> to have been anticipated by reason only that at any time within one\n> year before the priority date of the relevant claim of the\n> specification, the invention was publicly worked in India\n>\n>  36\n>\n> (a) by the patentee or applicant for the patent or any person from\n> whom he derives title; or\n> (b) by any other person with the consent of the patentee or\n> applicant for the patent or any person from whom he derives title,\n> if the working was effected for the purpose of reasonable trial only\n> and if it was reasonably necessary, having regard to the nature of\n> the invention, that the working for that purpose should be effected\n> in public.\n\n**Section 33 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 33. Anticipation by use and publication after provisional\n> specification.\n> (1) Where a complete specification is filed or proceeded with in\n> pursuance of an application which was accompanied by a provisional\n> specification or where a complete specification filed along with an\n> application is treated by virtue of a direction under sub-section\n> (3) of section 9 as a provisional specification, then,\n> notwithstanding anything contained in this Act, the Controller shall\n> not refuse to grant the patent, and the patent shall not be revoked\n> or invalidated, by reason only that any matter described in the\n> provisional specification or in the specification treated as\n> aforesaid as a provisional specification was used in India or\n> published in India or elsewhere at any time after the date of the\n> filing of that specification.\n>\n> (2) Where a complete specification is filed in pursuance of a\n> convention application, then, notwithstanding anything contained in\n> this Act, the Controller shall not refuse to grant the patent, and\n> the patent shall not be revoked or invalidated, by reason only that\n> any matter disclosed in any application for protection in a\n> convention country upon which the convention application is founded\n> was used in India or published in India or elsewhere at any time\n> after the date of that application for protection.\n\n**Section 34 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 34. No anticipation if circumstances are only as described in\n> sections 29, 30, 31 and 32.\n> Notwithstanding anything contained in this Act, the Controller shall\n> not refuse to grant a patent, and a patent shall not be revoked or\n> invalidated by reason only of any circumstances which, by virtue of\n> section 29 or section 30 or section 31 or section 32, do not\n> constitute an anticipation of the invention claimed in the\n> specification.\n>\n>  37\n>\n> CHAPTER VII PROVISIONS FOR SECRECY OF CERTAIN INVENTIONS\n\n**Rule 29 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 29. Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming\n> (1) When it is found that the invention as far as claimed in any\n>\n>  38\n>\n> claim of the complete specification, is claimed in any claim of any\n> other specification falling within clause (b) of sub-section (1) of\n> section 13, the applicant shall be so informed and shall be afforded\n> an opportunity to amend his specification.\n>\n> (2) If the applicant’s specification is otherwise in order for grant\n> and an objection under clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 13\n> is outstanding, the Controller may postpone the grant of patent and\n> allow a period of two months for removing the objection.\n\n**Rule 29A — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 29A. Grace period\n> An application to avail the period specified under section 31 shall\n> be filed in Form 31, along with the fees specified in the First\n> Schedule.\n\n**Rule 28 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 28. Procedure in case of anticipation by prior publication\n> (1) If the Controller is satisfied after investigation under section\n> 13 that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete\n> specification has been published in any specification or other\n> document referred to in clause (a) of sub-section (1) or sub-section\n> (2) of the said section, the Controller shall communicate the gist\n> of specific objections and the basis thereof to the applicant and\n> the applicant shall be afforded an opportunity to amend his\n> specification.\n>\n> (2) If the applicant contests any of the objections communicated to\n> him by the Controller under sub-rule (1), or if he refiles his\n> specification along with his observations as to whether or not the\n> specification is to be amended, he shall be given an opportunity to\n> be heard on the matter if he so requests:\n> Provided that such a request shall be made on a date earlier than\n> ten days of the final date of the period preferred to under sub-\n> section (1) of section 21:\n> Provided further that a request for hearing may be allowed to be\n> filed within such a shorter period as the Controller may deem fit in\n> the circumstances of the case.\n>\n>  37\n>\n> (3) If the applicant requests for a hearing under sub-rule (2)\n> within a period of one month from the date of communication of the\n> gist of objections, or, the Controller, considers it desirable to do\n> so, whether or not the applicant has refiled his application, he\n> shall forthwith fix a date and time for hearing having regard to the\n> period remaining for putting the application in order or to the\n> other circumstances of the case.\n>\n> (4) The applicant shall be given ten days’ notice of any such\n> hearing or such shorter notice as appears to the Controller to be\n> reasonable in the circumstances of the case and the applicant shall,\n> as soon as possible, notify the Controller whether he will attend\n> the hearing.\n>\n> (5) After hearing the applicant, or without hearing if the applicant\n> has not attended or has notified that he does not desire to be\n> heard, the Controller may specify or permit such amendment of the\n> specification as he thinks fit to be made and may refuse to grant\n> the patent unless the amendment so specified or permitted is made\n> within such period as may be fixed.\n>\n> (6) The hearing may also be held through video-conferencing or\n> audio-visual communication devices: Provided that such hearing shall\n> be deemed to have taken place at the appropriate office.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purposes of this rule, the expression\n> communication device shall have the same meaning as assigned to it\n> in clause (ha) of sub-section (1) of section 2 of the Information\n> Technology Act, 2000 (21 of 2000).\n>\n> (7) In all cases of hearing, written submissions and the relevant\n> documents, if any, shall be filed within fifteen days from the date\n> of hearing.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the concept of novelty in patent law and explain how anticipation destroys newness.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** The question seeks an explanation of the legal definition of *novelty* under the Patents Act and how various forms of anticipation destroy it.\n\n**Rule:**  \n- **Section 2(1)(x)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* defines \"invention\" as something that is new and involves an inventive step.  \n- The concept of novelty requires that the invention has not been disclosed or used publicly anywhere in the world before the filing date (as stated on **page 57** of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).  \n- Anticipation can occur through:  \n  - **Prior publication**, governed by **Section 29**, which states that disclosure in a prior publication destroys novelty.  \n  - **Communication to the government** before filing, covered under **Section 30**.  \n  - **Public display, use or sale**, outlined in **Section 31**.  \n  - **Prior use by others**, as per **Section 32**.  \n  - **Secret prior art**, discussed in **Section 33**.  \n  - **Disclosure by others**, addressed in **Section 34**.  \n\nThe law ensures that only truly new inventions are granted patents, to prevent duplication and promote innovation (as explained on **page 57** of the textbook). The doctrine is also supported by the case *Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries*, (1979) 2 SCC 511, which emphasizes that a patent must disclose something more than an obvious improvement.\n\n**Application:**  \nThe law on novelty and anticipation exists to ensure that only truly new inventions are granted patents. If the subject matter of the invention has been disclosed in any publication before the filing date (as per **Section 29**), it is considered anticipated, thereby destroying its novelty. Similarly, if an invention was publicly displayed or used prior to the filing date (under **Section 31**), it lacks novelty and cannot be patented. Even if the prior art document is not publicly available but has a priority date earlier than the application, it can still destroy novelty under **Section 33**, which deals with \"hidden or secret prior art.\"  \n\nIn *Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries*, (1979) 2 SCC 511, the court emphasized that a patent must not merely be an obvious application of known methods; it must involve inventive step and contribute something new to the field.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nNovelty is essential for granting a patent under *The Patents Act, 1970*. Anticipation can occur through various forms such as prior publication, public display, or prior use by others. These provisions ensure that only truly novel inventions are protected and prevent multiple parties from being granted patents for the same invention.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse *novelty* with *inventive step*. It is crucial to remember that novelty refers solely to whether the invention was already known or used, while inventive step concerns whether the invention is non-obvious. A clear distinction between these two concepts is essential for a complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the various forms of anticipation under The Patents Act, 1970? Explain each briefly.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** The question asks for an enumeration and brief explanation of the different types of anticipation that can destroy novelty under *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n**Rule:**  \nAnticipation is defined as any prior disclosure or use that renders an invention non-novel. Under **Sections 29 to 34** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, the following forms of anticipation are recognized:  \n\n- **Prior publication**: Disclosure in a prior publication before the filing date destroys novelty (Section 29).  \n- **Communication to government**: Any communication to the government or an authorized person before filing is considered anticipation (Section 30).  \n- **Public display, use or sale**: Public display or use of the invention before the filing date leads to loss of novelty (Section 31).  \n- **Prior use by others**: If another party used the invention before the applicant filed for a patent, it is an anticipation (Section 32).  \n- **Secret prior art**: Even if not publicly available but with a priority date earlier than the application, it can destroy novelty (Section 33).  \n- **Disclosure by others**: Disclosure to third parties before filing also constitutes anticipation (Section 34).\n\n**Application:**  \nUnder **Section 29**, any invention disclosed in prior publications is considered anticipated. For example, if an inventor publishes a paper describing the same invention before filing a patent application, the invention lacks novelty. Similarly, under **Section 31**, public display of the invention at an exhibition or its use for that purpose also destroys novelty.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Novelty and Anticipation — What Destroys Newness\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 13 | **TOMB** | Tomb's door slams shut on prior publication. |\n| Sec. 29 | **KNOB** | Knob turns, revealing prior claims and public use. |\n| Sec. 30 | **MOUSE** | Mouse scurries across public display for trial. |\n| Sec. 31 | **MAT** | Mat absorbs spills during twelve-month grace. |\n| Sec. 32 | **MOON** | Moon glows without prior anticipation. |\n| Sec. 33 | **MUMMY** | Mummy's bandages hide novelty's destruction. |\n| Sec. 34 | **MOWER** | Mower's blade cuts through novelty's claim. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **12-24** - examination, objections, time for putting in order\n- **29-34** - anticipation\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries\n_(1979) 2 SCC 511_\n\n- **Hook** - Bishwanath's workshop improvement sparks obviousness debate.\n- **Held** - Novelty requires disclosure beyond obvious workshop improvement.\n- **Use in** - Patent novelty and obviousness questions.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Anticipation by prior publication - which provision?  (picture: TOMB)\n2. Anticipation by prior claiming and prior public use - which provision?  (picture: KNOB)\n3. Anticipation by public display and by use for reasonable trial - which provision?  (picture: MOUSE)\n4. Grace period of twelve months - which provision?  (picture: MAT)\n5. What is NOT anticipation - which provision?  (picture: MOON)\n6. Novelty and Anticipation — What Destroys Newness - which provision?  (picture: MUMMY)\n7. Novelty and Anticipation — What Destroys Newness - which provision?  (picture: MOWER)\n8. Which case decided: Novelty requires disclosure beyond obvious workshop improvement.\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 2(1)(x) | Defines invention as new and involving inventive step | New idea with creative leap  \n- Section 29 | Prior publication destroys novelty | Book page revealing secret  \n- Section 30 | Communication to government anticipates | Official notice of invention  \n- Section 31 | Public display, use or sale anticipates | Product on show at fair  \n- Section 32 | Public working within one year anticipates | Machine in factory before filing  \n- Section 33 | Use after provisional spec not an anticipation | Draft idea used later  \n- Section 34 | No anticipation if covered by earlier sections | Safe zone for new invention  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries (1979) | Patent must show more than obvious improvement | Workshop tweak not enough  \n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing anticipation with prior art — they are related but distinct  \n- Examiners expect clear references to specific sections of the Patents Act  \n- Always structure answers by first defining novelty, then listing forms of anticipation with section numbers","storyEN":"**Story: The Invention of the Golden Lamp in Hyderabad**\n\nIn a quiet corner of Hyderabad, near the Charminar, lived a young inventor named Ravi. He had spent years perfecting a new type of lamp that used solar energy more efficiently than any other on the market. His invention was simple but brilliant — it could store sunlight during the day and emit warm light for hours at night without needing batteries or wires.\n\nOne day, he decided to file a patent application with the Controller of Patents in Delhi. But before doing so, Ravi heard about a man named Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam, who had once been involved in a case where his invention was rejected because it wasn’t truly new — only an improvement that someone else had already made.\n\nRavi decided to visit the local library and do some research. He discovered that there were **29** books on prior inventions, each detailing various lamps that used solar energy. One of them even described a method similar to his own, though not as efficient. That number — **29** — stuck with him because it was the same as the section in the Patents Act that talks about anticipation by previous publication.\n\nHe also found out that there were **30** letters from inventors who had communicated their ideas directly to the government before filing patents, which could also destroy novelty. That made Ravi nervous — what if someone else had shared his idea with a government official?\n\nThen he came across a newspaper article showing an exhibition in Mumbai where **31** lamps were displayed publicly. One of them looked suspiciously like his design. He remembered that Section **31** says that public display or use of the invention before filing can destroy novelty.\n\nRavi was worried, but then he read about a man named Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam again — this time in detail. The case had been about an inventor whose lamp wasn’t novel because it was merely an improvement on something already known. This made Ravi realize that even if his idea was different, if someone else had done similar work before him, he might not be able to get a patent.\n\nDetermined not to let that happen, Ravi went back to the library and looked for **32** more documents — this time about public use of solar lamps in India. He found one from **1978**, which was just one year before his idea. That meant if he filed now, it might be too late under Section **32**, which says that using an invention publicly within a year of the priority date could destroy novelty.\n\nHe also checked for any **provisional specifications** — documents that inventors sometimes file to secure their rights while they work on more detailed versions. He found one from 1976, but it was not enough to anticipate his lamp because Section **33** says that provisional specs don’t count as anticipation if the complete spec is filed later.\n\nFinally, Ravi decided to speak with a patent agent who told him about Section **34**, which says that even if something seems like anticipation under other sections, it doesn't always destroy novelty — only if those circumstances are clearly defined in the law. That gave him some hope.\n\nWith all this information, he filed his application carefully and included a detailed prior art search to show that his invention was truly new, not just an improvement on what others had done before.\n\n---\n\n**ANCHOR KEY**\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| 29 books     | Section 29 | Anticipation by previous publication destroys novelty unless the inventor proves that the prior disclosure was without his consent or that it was not commercially used before the priority date. |\n| 30 letters   | Section 30 | Communication of the invention to the Government does not destroy novelty if done for the purpose of investigation. |\n| 31 lamps     | Section 31 | Public display, use, or sale of an invention in an exhibition or other public forum can destroy novelty unless the applicant files a patent within twelve months. |\n| 32 documents | Section 32 | Public working of an invention within one year before the priority date may not destroy novelty if it was for reasonable trial purposes and with consent. |\n| 33 provisional specs | Section 33 | Matters described in a provisional specification do not anticipate a complete specification filed later, even if used or published afterward. |\n| 34 sections  | Section 34 | Circumstances that might seem like anticipation under other sections (like 29–32) are not valid grounds for rejecting a patent unless they meet specific legal criteria. |\n| Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries, (1979) 2 SCC 511 | Case | A patent must disclose something more than an obvious improvement — it must be truly novel and not merely a workshop refinement that would be obvious to a skilled worker in the field. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **\"Novelty and Anticipation — What Destroys Newness\"**\n\n---\n\n**1. Acronym/Mnemonic: \"NOVELTY\"**\n- **N**ew (Section 29): The invention must not have been previously published.\n- **O**ld use (Section 30): Prior use by the public or Government.\n- **V**isible display (Section 31): Public exhibition or display of the invention.\n- **E**xisting knowledge (Sections 29–34): Includes prior art and hidden references.\n- **L**egal disclosure (Section 35): Disclosure to the Government under secrecy directions.\n- **T**echnical anticipation (Section 13): Anticipation by any means, including prior use or communication.\n- **Y**our own use (Section 47): Use by the Government for its own purposes.\n\n---\n\n**2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 29**: *Newspaper* — Think of a newspaper article that reveals the invention before filing. This is how novelty can be destroyed.\n- **Section 30**: *Old man* — Imagine an old man using the invention publicly, which shows prior use.\n- **Section 31**: *Exhibition hall* — Picture a public exhibition where the invention was displayed.\n- **Section 35**: *Secret file* — Visualize a secret document held by the Government that reveals the invention before filing.\n- **Section 47**: *Government factory* — Think of the Government using the invention for its own purposes, which is exempt from infringement.\n\n---\n\n**3. Confusion Pairs:**\n\n- **Novelty vs. Inventive Step (obviousness):**\n  - *Difference*: Novelty checks if the invention was known before; inventive step checks if it was obvious to a skilled person.\n  - *One-liner*: \"New is not enough — it must also be non-obvious.\"\n\n- **Prior Use vs. Prior Publication:**\n  - *Difference*: Prior use refers to actual application of the invention by someone else; prior publication refers to written disclosure in any medium.\n  - *One-liner*: \"Use is action, publication is writing.\"\n\n- **Hidden Prior Art vs. Public Prior Art:**\n  - *Difference*: Hidden prior art was not known publicly but may still invalidate a patent if it existed before filing; public prior art is widely available and clearly invalidates novelty.\n  - *One-liner*: \"Hidden can destroy, but only if it’s real — public is always clear.\"\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall the legal framework for **novelty and anticipation** in a structured, visual, and logical way.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Prior publication accessible to the public, like in libraries, destroys novelty if it enables working the invention without experimentation. Section 33 excludes use in India after filing.","question":"What destroys novelty by prior publication?"},{"answer":"Form 31 must be filed under Rule 29(A) to claim the 12-month grace period after public display at exhibitions or learned societies.","question":"What form must be filed to claim the grace period after public display?"},{"answer":"Secret knowledge isn't accessible to the public, so it doesn't destroy novelty, as seen in the cancer medicine example.","question":"Why isn't secret knowledge considered prior art?"},{"answer":"A 'new invention' has absolute novelty, meaning it wasn't used or published anywhere globally before the patent application's filing date.","question":"What defines a 'new invention' under the Patents Act?"},{"answer":"Public use in India for reasonable trials within 12 months before the priority date isn't anticipation under Section 32.","question":"When is public use in India not considered anticipation?"},{"answer":"Publications without the inventor's consent are not anticipation if the patent application is filed promptly after learning of the publication.","question":"What is not considered anticipation under Section 29?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 29 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming”.","options":["Rule 74A","Rule 29","Rule 94","Rule 12"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 29 is headed “Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming”.","options":["Scale of costs","Request under section 52(2)","Form of application for the issue of a duplicate patent","Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming"],"question":"Rule 29 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 30 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Anticipation by previous communication to Government”.","options":["Section 30","Section 31","Section 74","Section 109"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Anticipation by previous communication to Government?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 30 is headed “Anticipation by previous communication to Government”.","options":["General principles applicable to working of patented inventions","Order for licence to operate as a deed between parties","Anticipation by previous communication to Government","Appeals to High Court"],"question":"Section 30 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 31 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Anticipation by public display, etc”.","options":["Section 19","Section 122","Section 31","Section 58"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Anticipation by public display, etc?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 31 is headed “Anticipation by public display, etc”.","options":["Anticipation by public display, etc","Rights of co-owners of patents","Patent to bind Government","Determination of certain contracts"],"question":"Section 31 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 33 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Form of reference to another patent”.","options":["Rule 108","Rule 33","Rule 107","Rule 19L"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Form of reference to another patent?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 33 is headed “Form of reference to another patent”.","options":["Surrender of Patents","Presentation of assignment, etc., of patent to Controller","Form of reference to another patent","Manner of advertisement of the proposed correction of any"],"question":"Rule 33 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 29 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Anticipation by previous publication”.","options":["Section 107","Section 127","Section 29","Section 144"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Anticipation by previous publication?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 29 is headed “Anticipation by previous publication”.","options":["Anticipation by public working","Patent rights not infringed when used on foreign vessels, etc.,","Anticipation by previous publication","Termination of compulsory licence"],"question":"Section 29 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 13 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior”.","options":["Section 134","Section 125","Section 133","Section 13"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 13 is headed “Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior”.","options":["Priority dates of claims of a complete specification","Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior","Restriction on power of court to grant damages or account of","Determination of certain contracts"],"question":"Section 13 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"An invention claimed in a complete specification shall not be deemed to have been anticipated by reason only that the invention was published in a specification filed in pursuance of an application for a patent made in India and dated before the 1st day of January, 1912.","options":["If the invention was commercially worked in India before the priority date","If the invention was published in a specification dated on or after 1st January 1912","If the invention was published in a specification filed before 1st January 1912","If the invention was disclosed in a provisional specification"],"question":"According to Section 29(1) of the Patents Act, 1970, when is an invention not deemed to be anticipated by a publication in a specification filed in India before 1st January 1912?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"An invention claimed in a complete specification shall not be deemed to have been anticipated by reason only of the communication of the invention to the Government or to any person authorised by the Government to investigate the invention or its merits, or of anything done, in consequence of such a communication, for the purpose of the investigation.","options":["If the communication was made for the purpose of public display","If the communication was made to a person authorised by the Government to investigate the invention","If the invention was published in a convention country","If the invention was used in India within one year of the priority date"],"question":"Under Section 30 of the Patents Act, 1970, when is an invention not deemed to be anticipated by communication to the Government?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The display of the invention with the consent of the true and first inventor or a person deriving title from him at an industrial or other exhibition... shall not be deemed to have been anticipated.","options":["If the display was with the consent of the true and first inventor","If the invention was published in a convention country","If the application for the patent is made within six months of the exhibition","If the invention was used commercially before the exhibition"],"question":"According to Section 31(1) of the Patents Act, 1970, under what condition is an invention not deemed to be anticipated by public display at an exhibition?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The invention shall not be deemed to have been anticipated by reason only that at any time within one year before the priority date... the invention was publicly worked in India... if the working was effected for the purpose of reasonable trial only and if it was reasonably necessary...","options":["If the working was done for the purpose of reasonable trial and was reasonably necessary","If the working was done in a convention country","If the invention was disclosed in a provisional specification","If the application for the patent was made within twelve months of the exhibition"],"question":"Under Section 32 of the Patents Act, 1970, when is public working of an invention not considered anticipation?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Controller shall not refuse to grant a patent, and a patent shall not be revoked or invalidated by reason only of any circumstances which, by virtue of section 29 or section 30 or section 31 or section 32, do not constitute an anticipation of the invention claimed in the specification.","options":["They are considered valid grounds for revoking a patent","They do not constitute anticipation of the invention","They require the Controller to refuse the grant of a patent","They are deemed to be prior art for the purposes of examination"],"question":"According to Section 34 of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the effect of circumstances covered in Sections 29, 30, 31, and 32?"}]},"s1p_5":{"notes":"# Inventive Step and Obviousness — The Person Skilled in the Art\n\n## Definition of Inventive Step\n\nAn **inventive step** refers to a feature of an invention that involves either:\n\n- A **technical advance** compared to existing knowledge, or  \n- An **economic significance**, or both.  \n\nThis feature must make the invention **not obvious** to a person skilled in the art.\n\nThe legal definition is provided under **Section 2(1)(ja)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*:\n\n> “(ja) 'inventive step' means a feature of an invention that involves technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having economic significance or both and that makes the invention not obvious to a person skilled in the art.”\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n### 1. Person Skilled in the Art (PSIA)\n\nThe concept of **person skilled in the art** is central to determining whether an invention meets the inventive step requirement.\n\n- This individual is assumed to have:\n  - Ordinary knowledge and skill in the field.\n  - Access to all prior art.\n  - The ability to combine known techniques or solutions.\n\nIn the United States, this figure is often referred to as **PHOSITA** (Person Having Ordinary Skill In The Art). While not explicitly used in Indian law, the concept is implicitly applied under *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n- According to *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 61)*:\n  > “A person skilled in the art should be able to arrive at the invention following the prior arts. Such persons, in the USA, are called as PHOSITA i.e., the person having ordinary skill in the art.”\n\n### 2. Non-Obviousness\n\nAn invention is considered **non-obvious** if:\n\n- It is not an obvious modification or combination of known elements.\n- It involves a **technical advance**, or provides **economic significance**.\n\nAccording to *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 60)*:\n> “A patent application involves an inventive step if the proposed invention is not obvious to a person skilled in the art.”\n\n### 3. Hindsight and Prior Art\n\n- **Hindsight** must be eliminated when assessing inventive step.\n- The prior art should provide a **clear roadmap** or teaching that leads to the invention.\n\nAs explained in *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 61)*:\n> “Hindsight is to be eliminated as well: which means one should not first look into the invention and then accordingly find out the prior art and try to map it.”\n\n### 4. Technical Problem and Solution\n\n- The **object of the invention** must clearly state the problem in existing technology that the invention solves.\n- This helps establish whether the solution is non-obvious or inventive.\n\nAs stated in *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 157)*:\n> “The object of the invention is stated to establish the necessity for carrying out the invention. It addresses the issue of obviousness and renders the invention as inventive and non-obvious over the existing technology.”\n\n## Why the Law Is As It Is\n\n### Purpose of the Inventive Step Requirement\n\n- The **inventive step** requirement ensures that only truly novel and innovative inventions are granted patents.\n- This prevents the patenting of trivial or obvious modifications to existing knowledge.\n\nAccording to *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 60)*:\n> “It measures the technical achievement of an invention and serves to fulfill the objective of the advancement of science through the patent system.”\n\n### Importance in Patent Specifications\n\n- A clear description of the **state of art**, **object of the invention**, and **statement of the invention** is essential for demonstrating inventive step.\n\nAs explained in *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 157)*:\n> “The description of the prior art is very important for the Controller in judging the patentability of the invention.”\n\n### Role of Prior Art Search\n\n- A thorough **prior art search** helps determine whether an invention meets the criteria of novelty and inventive step.\n- It also aids in defining the scope of claim protection.\n\nAs noted in *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 437)*:\n> “Prior art searches help in identifying the closest prior arts, which helps in properly defining the scope of claim protection.”\n\n## Conclusion\n\n- The **inventive step** requirement under *The Patents Act, 1970* ensures that only truly innovative and non-obvious inventions are granted patents.\n- The concept of a **person skilled in the art** is central to evaluating whether an invention meets this criterion.\n- Proper documentation, including prior art analysis, object of the invention, and statement of the invention, plays a crucial role in demonstrating inventive step.\n\n> Note: No verified or candidate cases have been identified for this topic. If required for exam preparation, it is recommended to consult IndianKanoon or other case law databases for relevant judgments.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 2 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 2. Definitions and interpretation.\n> (1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal\n> representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee\n> of any person include references to the assignee of the legal\n> representative or assignee of that person;\n> (aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the\n> International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the\n> purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April,\n> 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;\n> (ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an\n> invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used\n> in an industry;\n> (b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs\n> and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;\n> (c) “convention application” means an application for a patent made\n> by virtue of section 135;\n> (d) “convention country\" means a country or a country which is\n> member of a group of countries or a union of countries or an Inter-\n> governmental organisation referred to as a convention country in\n> section 133;\n> (e) “district court” has the meaning assigned to that expression by\n> the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908);\n> (f) “exclusive licence” means a licence from a patentee which\n> confers on the licensee, or on the licensee and persons authorised\n> by him, to the exclusion of all other persons (including the\n>\n>  8\n>\n> patentee), any right in respect of the patented invention, and\n> “exclusive licensee” shall be construed accordingly;\n> (h) “Government undertaking” means any industrial undertaking\n> carried on\n> (i) by a department of the Government, or\n> (ii) by a corporation established by a Central, Provincial or State\n> Act, which is owned or controlled by the Government, or\n> (iii) by a Government company as defined in section 617 of the\n> Companies Act, 1956 (1 of\n> 1956), or\n> 1(iv) by an institution wholly or substantially financed by the\n> Government;\n> (i) “High Court\", in relation to a State or Union territory, means\n> the High Court having territorial jurisdiction in that State or\n> Union territory, as the case may be;\n> (ia) “international application” means an application for patent\n> made in accordance with the Patent Cooperation Treaty;\n> (j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an\n> inventive step and capable of industrial application;\n> (ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves\n> technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having\n> economic significance or both and that makes the invention not\n> obvious to a person skilled in the art;\n> (k) “legal representative” means a person who in law represents the\n> estate of a deceased person;\n> (l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not\n> been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the\n> country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of\n> patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject\n> matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part\n> of the state of the art;\n> (la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under\n> sub-section (3) of section 25;\n> (m) “patent” means a patent for any invention granted under this\n> Act;\n> (n) “patent agent” means a person for the time being registered\n> under this Act as a patent agent;\n> (o) “patented article” and “patented process” mean respectively an\n> article or process in respect of which a patent is in force;\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 64 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 64. Revocation of Power patents.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions contained in this Act, a patent\n> whether granted before or after the commencement of this Act, may,\n> be revoked on a petition of any person interested or of the Central\n> Government or on a counter-claim in a suit for infringement of the\n> patent by the High Court on any of the following grounds, that is to\n> say\n> (a) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification, was claimed in a valid claim of earlier\n> priority date contained in the complete specification of another\n> patent granted in India;\n> (b) that the patent was granted on the application of a person not\n> entitled under the provisions of this Act to apply therefor:\n> (c) that the patent was obtained wrongfully in contravention of the\n> rights of the petitioner or any person under or through whom he\n> claims;\n> (d) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act;\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is not new, having regard to what was\n>\n>  55\n>\n> publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority date of\n> the claim or to what was published in India or elsewhere in any of\n> the documents referred to in section 13:\n> (f) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious or does not involve any inventive\n> step, having regard to what was publicly known or publicly used in\n> India or what was published in India or elsewhere before the\n> priority date of the claim:\n> (g) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification, is not useful;\n> (h) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and fairly\n> describe the invention and the method by which it is to be\n> performed, that is to say, that the description of the method or the\n> instructions for the working of the invention as contained in the\n> complete specification are not by themselves sufficient to enable a\n> person in India possessing average skill in, and average knowledge\n> of, the art to which the invention relates, to work the invention,\n> or that it does not disclose the best method of performing it which\n> was known to the applicant for the patent and for which he was\n> entitled to claim, protection;\n> (i) that the scope of any claim of the complete specification is not\n> sufficiently and clearly defined or that any claim of the complete\n> specification is not fairly based on the matter disclosed in the\n> specification;\n> (j) that the patent was obtained on a false suggestion or\n> representation;\n> (k) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not patentable under this Act;\n> (l) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was secretly used in India, otherwise than as\n> mentioned in sub-section (3), before the priority date of the claim;\n> (m) that the applicant for the patent has failed to disclose to the\n> Controller the information required by section 8 or has furnished\n> information which in any material particular was false to his\n> knowledge;\n> (n) that the applicant contravened any direction for secrecy passed\n> under section 35 or made or caused to be made an application for the\n> grant of a patent outside India in contravention of section 39;\n> (o) that leave to amend the complete specification under section 57\n> or section 58 was obtained by fraud;\n> (p) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n>\n>  56\n> … [truncated]\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Define and explain 'inventive step' as provided under Section 2(1)(ja) of The Patents Act, 1970. Discuss the importance of this requirement in patent law.**\n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is an 'inventive step' under Indian patent law, and why is it a crucial criterion for granting patents?\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 2(1)(ja)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, an **inventive step** refers to a feature of an invention that involves either a technical advance compared to existing knowledge or economic significance, or both. This feature must make the invention not obvious to a person skilled in the art.\n\nThe concept of **person skilled in the art (PSIA)** is central to evaluating inventive step. As per *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 61)*, this individual is assumed to have ordinary knowledge and skill in the field, access to all prior art, and the ability to combine known techniques or solutions.\n\nThe **non-obviousness** of an invention is a key criterion under patent law, as emphasized by *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 60)*, which states that a patent application involves an inventive step if the proposed invention is not obvious to a person skilled in the art.\n\n**Application**  \nThe requirement of **inventive step** ensures that only truly novel and innovative inventions are granted patents. It prevents the granting of patents for trivial or obvious modifications to existing knowledge, as noted by *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 60)*: “It measures the technical achievement of an invention and serves to fulfill the objective of the advancement of science through the patent system.”\n\nFor instance, if a new pharmaceutical compound is developed that provides a significant therapeutic benefit over existing drugs, it would meet the inventive step criterion because it involves both **technical advance** (improved efficacy or reduced side effects) and **economic significance** (potential for commercial success). However, if an inventor merely combines two known compounds in a predictable way to produce a drug with no new properties, this would not satisfy the inventive step requirement.\n\nIn contrast, the **obviousness test**, as explained in *Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries, (1979) 2 SCC 511*, holds that a discovery is not patentable if it lies within the “workshop improvement” of a skilled worker. This reinforces the idea that an invention must go beyond what is already known or predictable.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe **inventive step requirement** under *The Patents Act, 1970* ensures that only truly innovative and non-obvious inventions receive patent protection. It plays a vital role in promoting scientific advancement by encouraging inventors to make meaningful contributions rather than relying on minor modifications of existing knowledge.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse **novelty** with **inventive step**, but the former refers only to newness, while the latter requires that an invention is not obvious. This distinction must be clearly explained in the answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is meant by 'person skilled in the art (PSIA)'? Discuss its role in determining whether an invention meets the inventive step requirement.**\n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is a person skilled in the art, and how does this concept influence the assessment of inventive step?\n\n**Rule**  \nThe **person skilled in the art (PSIA)** is an imaginary individual assumed to have ordinary knowledge and skill in the relevant field.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Inventive Step and Obviousness — The Person Skilled in the Art\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 2(1)(ja) | **HEN** + clause (1)(ja) | HEN scratching dirt reveals buried technical advance |\n| Sec. 64(1)(f) | **CHAIR** + clause (1)(f) | CHAIR creaks under weight of economic significance |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **2** - definitions\n- **64-66** - revocation\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries\n_(1979) 2 SCC 511_\n\n- **Hook** - Bishwanath's workshop improvement deemed obvious\n- **Held** - Obviousness requires discovery beyond skilled worker's workshop improvement\n- **Use in** - Obviousness and skilled worker's improvements\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Technical advance compared to existing knowledge - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n2. Economic significance - which provision?  (picture: CHAIR)\n3. Which case decided: Obviousness requires discovery beyond skilled worker's workshop improvement\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 2(1)(ja) | Defines inventive step as non-obvious technical or economic advance | Feature making invention non-trivial  \n- Section 64(f) | Revokes patent if invention is obvious, no inventive step | Obviousness leads to patent invalidity  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries (1979) | Test for obviousness: beyond workshop improvement | Skilled worker’s routine not enough  \n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid using vague terms like “obvious” without linking to the PSIA  \n- Always reference Section 2(1)(ja) when defining inventive step  \n- Structure answers with clear sections: prior art, technical problem, solution, and non-obviousness","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, in a quiet neighborhood near Charminar, lived a young inventor named Ravi. He had always been fascinated by solving problems that others overlooked. One day, he came up with an idea for a new type of water filter that could purify contaminated water using a unique combination of natural minerals and nano-filtration techniques. Excited about his invention, he decided to file a patent application under *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\nAs Ravi prepared the documents, he recalled what he had studied in **Section 2(1)(ja)** — that an inventive step must involve either a technical advance or economic significance and make the invention not obvious to someone skilled in the art. He knew this was critical for his patent to be accepted. To ensure this, he spent weeks researching and gathering information about existing technologies.\n\nRavi also remembered what **Section 64(f)** said — that if an invention is deemed obvious, it would be grounds for revoking a patent. This made him even more cautious in how he presented his idea. He wanted to avoid the fate of someone like Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam, whose case had shown that inventions must go beyond mere workshop improvements.\n\nTo help him navigate this complex process, Ravi sought advice from an experienced patent agent named Mr. Arjun. Mr. Arjun explained that **Section 2(1)(ja)** was the key to proving inventive step, and that it all hinged on the concept of a person skilled in the art — someone who had access to prior knowledge and could combine existing techniques.\n\nMr. Arjun also reminded Ravi about the importance of eliminating **hindsight** when assessing obviousness. “You can’t look at your invention first and then try to find a way it fits into what already exists,” he said, echoing the advice from page 61 in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*.\n\nRavi worked hard to clearly define the **object of the invention**, as advised on page 157 — explaining exactly what problem his filter solved over existing technology. He also conducted a thorough **prior art search**, as recommended on page 437, to ensure that no one had already come up with something similar.\n\nOne day, while reviewing his documents again, Ravi came across the case of **Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries, (1979) 2 SCC 511** — a case where the court ruled that an invention must be more than just a minor improvement by someone with average skills in the field. This made Ravi realize how important it was for his filter to demonstrate **technical advance** and **non-obviousness**, not just novelty.\n\nAs he prepared his final submission, Ravi imagined a world where his filter would help thousands of people access clean water — a true **economic significance** that went beyond the obvious. He felt confident that his invention met all the criteria laid out in **Section 2(1)(ja)** and that it was not merely an improvement for someone skilled in the art, but something truly innovative.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 2(1)(ja) | Legal definition of \"inventive step\" | An inventive step involves technical advance or economic significance and makes the invention not obvious to a person skilled in the art. |\n| Section 64(f) | Grounds for revoking a patent due to lack of inventive step | If an invention is deemed obvious, it may be revoked under this section. |\n| Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries, (1979) 2 SCC 511 | Case on the test for non-obviousness | An invention must go beyond the workshop improvement of a skilled worker to qualify as inventive. |\n| Section 2(1)(ja) again | Central legal requirement for patentability | The feature of an invention must involve technical advance or economic significance and not be obvious to someone skilled in the art. |\n| Page 61 (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra) | Concept of person skilled in the art | A person skilled in the art should be able to arrive at the invention following prior arts, similar to PHOSITA in US law. |\n| Page 60 (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra) | Inventive step requirement | The inventive step measures technical achievement and serves the objective of advancing science through the patent system. |\n| Page 157 (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra) | Object of the invention | The object of the invention establishes necessity for carrying out the invention, helping to render it non-obvious over existing technology. |\n| Page 437 (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra) | Prior art search importance | Prior art searches help identify closest prior arts and define the scope of claim protection. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **Inventive Step and Obviousness — The Person Skilled in the Art**:\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n- **\"P.S.I.A.\"**  \n  *Person Skilled In the Art* – this is the standard used to assess whether an invention is non-obvious.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-Anchors for Sections:**\n\n- **Section 2(1)(ja):**  \n  *Imagine a \"jigsaw puzzle\" (like the number 'ja') where all pieces must fit together — just like how prior art and the invention must be compared to determine non-obviousness.*\n\n- **Section 13:**  \n  *Think of a \"shield\" (like the shape of the number '13') protecting against anticipation — this section lists what can anticipate an invention, which is crucial for assessing novelty.*\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Confusion Pairs:**\n\n- **Novelty vs. Inventive Step**  \n  *One is about being new (novelty), and the other is about not being obvious to a person skilled in the art (inventive step). Use this: \"New is novel, non-obvious is inventive.\"*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are tailored for **Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)** using only the provided sections and case law.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"An inventive step involves technical advance, economic significance, or both, making the invention non-obvious to a skilled person. Section 2(1)(ja) of the Patents Act defines this requirement.","question":"What defines an inventive step under Indian patent law?"},{"answer":"An invention is patentable if it has technical advancement or economic significance, provided it is not obvious to a person skilled in the art. Both conditions may be satisfied independently.","question":"What are the two conditions for patentability based on inventive step?"},{"answer":"Mosaicing refers to combining multiple prior art references (Y documents) to assess if they suggest the invention. This is used to evaluate obviousness to a skilled person.","question":"What is 'mosaicing' in the context of prior art?"},{"answer":"India uses both 'inventive step' and 'non-obviousness' in its law, while the U.S. exclusively uses 'non-obviousness.' The terms are conceptually similar but jurisdictionally distinct.","question":"How does India's inventive step differ from U.S. non-obviousness?"},{"answer":"A patent may be revoked under Section 64(1)(f) if it lacks an inventive step, as the absence of technical or economic advancement renders it obvious to a skilled person.","question":"What is a ground for revoking a patent due to lack of inventive step?"},{"answer":"The person skilled in the art represents the baseline of knowledge and creativity in the field. Inventive step requires the invention to go beyond what such a person would find obvious.","question":"Why is the person skilled in the art central to assessing inventive step?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Section 94","Section 100","Section 2","Section 137"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Definitions and interpretation?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 2 is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Power of Controller to make orders respecting dating of","Officers and employees not to furnish information, etc","Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents","Definitions and interpretation"],"question":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 64 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Revocation of Power patents”.","options":["Section 64","Section 158","Section 124A","Section 79"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Revocation of Power patents?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 64 is headed “Revocation of Power patents”.","options":["Rights of third parties in respect of use of invention for","Revocation of Power patents","Subscription and verification of certain documents by patent","Form of application"],"question":"Section 64 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee of any person include references to the assignee of the legal representative or assignee of that person;","options":["a) An assignee of the assignee and the legal representative of a deceased assignee","b) A person who invents a new product or process","c) The Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks","d) A person who applies for a patent internationally"],"question":"Which of the following is the correct definition of 'assignee' under Section 2(ab) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April, 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;","options":["a) The Budapest Treaty on the International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April, 1977, as amended and modified from time to time","b) A treaty related to international patent cooperation signed in 1980","c) A treaty governing the rights of inventors in the European Union","d) A treaty on the protection of industrial designs"],"question":"Which of the following correctly defines the 'Budapest Treaty' under Section 2(aba) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used in an industry;","options":["a) The invention is capable of being made or used in an industry","b) The invention is novel and non-obvious","c) The invention is useful for academic research","d) The invention is protected under international law"],"question":"What does 'capable of industrial application' mean under Section 2(ac) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;","options":["a) The Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks","b) A patent agent registered under the Act","c) A judge of the High Court","d) A representative of the Central Government"],"question":"Who is referred to as the 'Controller' under Section 2(b) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application;","options":["a) A new product or process involving an inventive step and capable of industrial application","b) A discovery of a natural phenomenon","c) A mathematical method or a business method","d) A work of art or literary creation"],"question":"What is the meaning of 'invention' under Section 2(j) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having economic significance or both and that makes the invention not obvious to a person skilled in the art;","options":["a) A feature of an invention that involves technical advance or economic significance, or both, and that makes the invention not obvious to a person skilled in the art","b) A feature that is publicly known in India","c) A feature that is a simple modification of an existing invention","d) A feature that is protected under international treaties"],"question":"Which of the following is the correct definition of 'inventive step' under Section 2(ja) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part of the state of the art;","options":["a) An invention or technology not anticipated by publication or used before the filing of the patent application","b) An invention that is publicly known in India","c) An invention that is obvious to a person skilled in the art","d) An invention that is protected under the TRIPS Agreement"],"question":"What is the definition of a 'new invention' under Section 2(l) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under sub-section (3) of section 25;","options":["a) An Opposition Board constituted under subsection (3) of section 25","b) A board constituted under section 64 for revocation of patents","c) A board for granting international patents","d) A board for resolving disputes between inventors"],"question":"Which of the following is the correct definition of 'Opposition Board' under Section 2(la) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"(f) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete specification is obvious or does not involve any inventive step, having regard to what was publicly known or publicly used in India or what was published in India or elsewhere before the priority date of the claim:","options":["a) The invention is obvious or does not involve any inventive step","b) The patent was granted to a person not entitled to apply","c) The invention is not useful","d) The complete specification does not disclose the best method of performing the invention"],"question":"Under Section 64(f) of the Patents Act, 1970, on what ground can a patent be revoked?"}]},"s1p_6":{"notes":"# Study Notes: Section 3(a) to 3(e) — Non-Patentable Inventions under The Patents Act, 1970\n\n## Overview\n\nSections 3(a) to 3(e) of **The Patents Act, 1970** outline specific categories of inventions that are not considered patentable. These provisions aim to ensure that only truly innovative and useful inventions receive protection. They exclude frivolous or non-technical inventions, those contrary to public order or morality, mere discoveries, and certain types of chemical derivations.\n\n---\n\n## Section 3(a): Frivolous Inventions\n\n### Definition:\nAn invention is not patentable if it is **frivolous** or if it claims anything that is **obviously contrary to well-established natural laws**.\n\n### Key Concepts:\n- A frivolous invention refers to something trivial, lacking in real utility or serious technical merit.\n- An invention that defies known scientific principles (e.g., a perpetual motion machine) falls under this category.\n\n### Explanation:\nThe law excludes such inventions because they lack any meaningful contribution to technology and may be seen as wasting the resources of the patent office. These inventions are not worthy of legal protection, as they do not advance technological progress or public interest. \n\n> (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 569)\n\n### Verified Case:\n- **(verify this case with a case-law database)**\n\n---\n\n## Section 3(b): Inventions Contrary to Public Order or Morality\n\n### Definition:\nAn invention is not patentable if its primary or intended use or commercial exploitation would be **contrary to public order or morality**, or if it causes **serious prejudice** to human, animal, or plant life or health, or the environment.\n\n### Key Concepts:\n- This provision ensures that inventions do not harm society.\n- Examples include devices for burglary, gambling machines, and methods of food adulteration.\n\n### Explanation:\nThe law is designed to prevent the patenting of harmful or unethical technologies. Such inventions may damage public trust in the patent system or cause significant harm to individuals and ecosystems. The exclusion reflects a societal interest in maintaining ethical standards and protecting human and environmental well-being.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 3 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 3. What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within\n> the meaning of this Act,\n> (a) an invention which is frivolous or which claims anything\n> obviously contrary to well established natural laws;\n> (b) an invention the primary or intended use or commercial\n> exploitation of which would be contrary to public order or morality\n> or which causes serious prejudice to human, animal or plant life or\n> health or to the environment;\n> (c) the mere discovery of a scientific principle or the formulation\n> of an abstract theory or discovery of any living thing or non-living\n> substance occurring in nature;\n> (d) the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does\n> not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that\n> substance or the mere discovery of any new property or new use for a\n> known substance or of the mere use of a known process, machine or\n> apparatus unless such known process results in a new product or\n> employs at least one new reactant.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, salts, esters, ethers,\n> polymorphs, metabolites, pure form, particle size, isomers, mixtures\n> of isomers, complexes, combinations and other derivatives of known\n> substance shall be considered to be the same substance, unless they\n> differ significantly in properties with regard to efficacy;\n>\n> (e) a substance obtained by a mere admixture resulting only in the\n> aggregation of the properties of the components thereof or a process\n> for producing such substance;\n> (f) the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known\n> devices each functioning independently of one another in a known\n> way;\n> (h) a method of agriculture or horticulture;\n> (i) any process for the medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic\n> diagnostic, therapeutic or other treatment of human beings or any\n> process for a similar treatment of animals to render them free of\n> disease or to increase their economic value or that of their\n> products.\n> (j) plants and animals in whole or any part thereof other than\n> micro-organisms but including seeds, varieties and species and\n> essentially biological processes for production or propagation of\n>\n>  11\n>\n> plants and animals;\n> (k) a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se\n> or algorithms;\n> (l) a literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work or any other\n> aesthetic creation whatsoever including cinematographic works and\n> television productions;\n> (m) a mere scheme or rule or method of performing mental act or\n> method of playing game;\n> (n) a presentation of information;\n> (o) topography of integrated circuits;\n> (p) an invention which, in effect, is traditional knowledge or which\n> is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of\n> traditionally known component or components.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the scope and implications of Section 3(a) of The Patents Act, 1970 with reference to relevant case law.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** Whether an invention that is frivolous or contrary to well-established natural laws is patentable under Section 3(a) of The Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 3(a)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, any invention that is frivolous or which claims anything obviously contrary to well-established natural laws is not considered an invention for the purposes of patent protection. A \"frivolous\" invention refers to one that lacks real utility or serious technical merit and may be trivial in nature. Furthermore, if an invention defies known scientific principles—such as a perpetual motion machine—it falls under this category.\n\nThe law excludes such inventions because they do not contribute meaningfully to technological progress and are considered unworthy of legal protection (Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 569). However, it is important to note that the determination of what constitutes a frivolous invention depends on the context and may require expert evaluation.\n\n**Application:** While there is no verified case directly addressing Section 3(a), the principle can be inferred from general jurisprudence in patent law. For instance, an inventor proposing a machine that violates the laws of thermodynamics would likely be denied a patent under this provision. The Indian Patent Office has the discretion to assess whether an invention is frivolous based on its technical merit and scientific plausibility.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 3(a) excludes inventions that are trivial or defy natural laws from being granted patents, ensuring that only meaningful technological innovations receive protection. This provision helps preserve the integrity of the patent system by preventing the granting of patents for non-technical or scientifically implausible ideas.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse \"frivolous\" with \"obvious,\" failing to understand that the former refers to inventions lacking in real utility, while the latter pertains to prior art. It is crucial to distinguish between these concepts and provide clear examples of each.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the meaning and scope of Section 3(b) of The Patents Act, 1970, with reference to relevant case law.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** Whether inventions that are contrary to public order or morality, or cause serious prejudice to human, animal, or plant life or health, are patentable under Section 3(b) of The Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule:** **Section 3(b)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* states that an invention is not patentable if its primary or intended use or commercial exploitation would be contrary to public order or morality, or if it causes serious prejudice to human, animal, or plant life or health, or the environment.\n\nThis provision aims to prevent the granting of patents for inventions that are harmful or unethical. Examples include devices used for burglary, gambling machines, and methods of food adulteration (Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 570). The law reflects societal interests in maintaining ethical standards and protecting public welfare.\n\n**Application:** Although there is no verified case directly addressing Section 3(b), the principle has been applied in various contexts. For instance, a patent for a device that facilitates illegal activities or harms human health would be denied under this provision. The determination of what constitutes \"serious prejudice\" may depend on expert evaluation and societal norms.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 3(b) ensures that inventions which are harmful to society or the environment are not granted patents. This provision upholds ethical standards and protects public interest by excluding inventions that could cause significant harm.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook the broader implications of this section, such as its role in preventing the commercialization of unethical technologies. It is important to highlight how this rule aligns with societal values and legal ethics.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the scope and exceptions under Section 3(d) of The Patents Act, 1970, with reference to relevant case law.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** Whether a mere discovery of a new form of a known substance is patentable under Section 3(d), and what are the exceptions.\n\n**Rule:** **Section 3(d)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* states that the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that substance is not patentable. However, if such a new form results in enhanced efficacy or employs at least one new reactant, it may be considered an invention.\n\nThis provision aims to prevent the granting of patents for trivial modifications of existing substances. It ensures that only significant improvements in therapeutic or functional properties are protected (Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 571).\n\n**Application:** In **Novartis AG v. Union of India, (2013) 6 SCC 1**, the Supreme Court held that for a pharmaceutical substance to be patentable under Section 3(d), it must demonstrate therapeutic efficacy. The beta-crystalline form of imatinib mesylate was not considered patentable because it did not show enhanced efficacy over the known forms.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 3(d) prevents trivial modifications of known substances from being patented unless they result in a significant enhancement in efficacy or involve new reactants. This provision ensures that only meaningful innovations receive protection, promoting genuine pharmaceutical advancements.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to understand the distinction between \"efficacy\" and \"use.\" It is essential to clarify that Section 3(d) requires an improvement in therapeutic outcomes, not just a change in form or structure.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the meaning of Section 3(e) of The Patents Act, 1970, with reference to relevant case law.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** Whether a substance obtained by mere admixture resulting only in the aggregation of properties of its components is patentable under Section 3(e).\n\n**Rule:** **Section 3(e)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* states that a substance obtained by a mere admixture resulting only in the aggregation of the properties of the components thereof or a process for producing such substance is not considered an invention.\n\nThis provision ensures that combinations of known substances without any novel properties are excluded from patent protection. It prevents the granting of patents for trivial mixtures that do not result in new or improved functionalities (Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 572).\n\n**Application:** No verified case directly addresses Section 3(e), but the principle is consistent with general patent law principles. For example, a mixture of two known drugs without any synergistic effect would be denied a patent under this provision.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 3(e) ensures that mere admixtures of substances are not granted patents unless they produce new or enhanced properties. This helps maintain the quality and innovation standards in the field of inventions.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse \"mere admixture\" with \"combination.\" It is important to emphasize that a mere aggregation of known properties does not constitute an invention, even if the mixture has practical applications.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Section 3(a) to 3(e) — Frivolous Inventions, Contrary to Law, Mere Discovery, New Form of a Known Substance, Admixture\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 3(a) | **HAM** + clause (a) | HAM (meat) levitates, breaks natural laws |\n| Sec. 3(b) | **HAM** + clause (b) | HAM (meat) rots, poisons rivers |\n| Sec. 3(c) | **HAM** + clause (c) | HAM (meat) shows theory, no patent |\n| Sec. 3(d) | **HAM** + clause (d) | HAM (meat) transforms, no efficacy gain |\n| Sec. 3(e) | **HAM** + clause (e) | HAM (meat) blends, just sums up |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### Novartis AG v. Union of India\n_(2013) 6 SCC 1_\n\n- **Held** - Under Section 3(d), 'efficacy' for a pharmaceutical substance means therapeutic efficacy; the beta-crystalline form of imatinib mesylate was not patentable.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Frivolous or contrary to natural laws - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n2. Contrary to public order, morality or seriously prejudicial to life, health or environment - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n3. Mere discovery of a scientific principle or abstract theory - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n4. New form of a known substance without enhancement of known efficacy - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n5. Mere admixture resulting only in aggregation of properties - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n6. Which case decided: Under Section 3(d), 'efficacy' for a pharmaceutical substance means therapeutic efficacy; the beta-crystalline form of imatinib mesylate was not patentable.\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 3(a) | Inventions that are frivolous or defy natural laws are non-patentable | Perpetual motion machine image\n- Section 3(b) | Inventions harmful to public order, morality, or environment not patentable | Burglary device banned\n- Section 3(c) | Mere discovery of scientific principles or natural substances is excluded | Book with abstract theory\n- Section 3(d) | New form of known substance without enhanced efficacy not patentable | Same drug in different shape\n- Section 3(e) | Admixture of components without new properties not patentable | Mixed paint with no new color\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013) | Beta-crystalline form not patentable due to lack of efficacy | Drug shape rejected in court\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing \"mere discovery\" with novel applications – focus on the wording in Section 3(c)\n- Examiners expect clear distinction between \"efficacy\" and \"use\" in Section 3(d) cases\n- Always cite the exact section numbers and use examples from the Act to support arguments","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, in a quiet neighborhood near Nampally, lived a group of five friends — Arjun, Bhavani, Chandra, Dinesh, and Ela. Each had their own dream: to become patent agents. They were determined to pass the Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva), and they knew that understanding **Sections 3(a) to 3(e)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* was crucial.\n\nOne day, the five friends decided to test their knowledge by organizing a challenge. They placed **five lamps** in front of them — each representing one section from 3(a) to 3(e). The first lamp had a red ribbon tied around it and read: **\"Section 3(a): Frivolous Inventions.\"**\n\nArjun, the most analytical among them, explained that if someone tried to patent something like a *perpetual motion machine*, it would be rejected under this section. He recalled a story from his textbook — one where an inventor had applied for a device that supposedly generated energy without any input. The patent office said it was **frivolous** because it went against the laws of physics. Arjun concluded, “If your invention doesn’t respect natural laws or is just trivial, it’s like trying to light a lamp with smoke — it won’t work.”\n\nNext came **Section 3(b): Inventions Contrary to Public Order or Morality**, marked by a blue ribbon and a second lamp. Bhavani took the lead. She told the group about a case involving a device designed for **burglary**. The court ruled that such an invention was not patentable under this section because it would harm society. “If your invention helps people break laws or causes serious harm, it’s like trying to light a lamp in a dark room — but instead of helping others see, you’re making the darkness worse,” she said.\n\nThe third lamp had a green ribbon and read: **Section 3(c): Mere Discovery of Scientific Principles**. Chandra was the one who explained this section best. She described how an inventor tried to patent the discovery of gravity — something already known for centuries. The court said that mere discoveries, like scientific principles or abstract theories, are not inventions. “If your invention is just a new way of thinking about something old, it’s like trying to light a lamp with another lamp — you need more than just illumination,” she concluded.\n\nThe fourth lamp had an orange ribbon and read: **Section 3(d): New Form of a Known Substance**. Dinesh took this one on. He told the group about *Novartis AG v. Union of India*, where the Supreme Court ruled that a new form of a known drug — in this case, beta-crystalline imatinib mesylate — wasn’t patentable unless it had **enhanced therapeutic efficacy**. “If you’re just changing the shape or form of something without making it better at its job, it’s like trying to light a lamp with a broken bulb — no real use,” he said.\n\nFinally, the fifth lamp had a yellow ribbon and read: **Section 3(e): Mere Admixture**. Ela explained that if someone tried to patent a mixture of substances where each part just adds up but doesn’t create something new, it would fall under this section. She gave an example of mixing two known dyes together — the result was simply the sum of its parts, not a new color or property. “If your invention is just a mix of things that don’t do anything surprising, it’s like trying to light a lamp with a pile of matches — you need more than just pieces.”\n\nAfter their challenge, they sat in silence for a moment, each reflecting on what they had learned. They realized that the **five lamps** were not just symbols — they were anchors for the five sections. Each one reminded them of the rules and the cases behind them.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Five lamps   | Sections 3(a) to 3(e) | These sections outline what is not considered an invention under the Patents Act, 1970. |\n| Red lamp     | Section 3(a): Frivolous Inventions | An invention that is trivial or contradicts natural laws is non-patentable. |\n| Blue lamp    | Section 3(b): Inventions Contrary to Public Order or Morality | Inventions harmful to public order, morality, or the environment are not patentable. |\n| Green lamp   | Section 3(c): Mere Discovery of Scientific Principles | Discoveries of natural phenomena, principles, or abstract theories are non-patentable. |\n| Orange lamp  | Section 3(d): New Form of a Known Substance | A new form of a known substance is not patentable unless it significantly enhances the efficacy of that substance. |\n| Yellow lamp  | Section 3(e): Mere Admixture | An invention that merely combines substances without creating something novel is non-patentable. |\n| Novartis AG v. Union of India | Case under Section 3(d) | A new form of a known drug must show enhanced therapeutic efficacy to be patentable. |\n\nThis anchor key can now serve as a memory aid for the student, helping them recall each section and its implications without needing to refer back to the story.","memory":"### **Section 3(a) - Frivolous Inventions**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"F\" for **Frivolous**, like a **foolish** idea (e.g., perpetual motion machine).  \n2. **Number-Anchor**: *Section 3(a)* → Think of the number **3** as a **triangle**, symbolizing something unstable or not serious — just like frivolous inventions.  \n3. **Confusion Pair**: Confused with **Section 3(d)**? Remember: **Frivolous** is about **obvious violation of natural laws**, while **3(d)** deals with **new forms of known substances** that don’t improve efficacy.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(b) - Contrary to Public Order or Morality**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"C\" for **Contrary** — think of a **crime** like burglary devices or gambling machines.  \n2. **Number-Anchor**: *Section 3(b)* → Think of the number **3** as a **broken chain**, symbolizing **breaking public order** and morality.  \n3. **Confusion Pair**: Confused with **Section 3(e)**? Remember: **3(b)** is about **morality and public order**, while **3(e)** is about **mere admixture of components**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(c) - Mere Discovery of Scientific Principles or Natural Substances**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"M\" for **Mere** — like a **mere idea** (e.g., scientific theory).  \n2. **Number-Anchor**: *Section 3(c)* → Think of the number **3** as a **circle**, representing **natural laws and principles** that are already known.  \n3. **Confusion Pair**: Confused with **Section 3(d)**? Remember: **3(c)** is about **discovery of natural things**, while **3(d)** deals with **new forms of known substances**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(d) - New Form of a Known Substance (Pharmaceuticals)**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"N\" for **New form, No Efficacy** — like the **Novartis case**, where **beta-crystalline imatinib mesylate** wasn’t patentable due to lack of therapeutic efficacy.  \n2. **Number-Anchor**: *Section 3(d)* → Think of the number **3(d)** as a **drug (d) bottle**, emphasizing the **pharmaceutical context** and requirement for **efficacy**.  \n3. **Confusion Pair**: Confused with **Section 3(c)**? Remember: **3(d)** is about **new forms of known substances** that must **enhance efficacy**, while **3(c)** is about **mere discovery of natural things**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(e) - Mere Admixture (Aggregation of Properties)**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: \"A\" for **Admixture = Aggregation**, like mixing **sugar and color** to make a drink — not patentable.  \n2. **Number-Anchor**: *Section 3(e)* → Think of the number **3(e)** as an **egg (e) with yolk and white mixed together**, symbolizing **mere admixture** without synergy.  \n3. **Confusion Pair**: Confused with **Section 3(d)**? Remember: **3(e)** is about **mixing substances that just add properties**, while **3(d)** is about **new forms of known substances** needing to improve efficacy.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"An invention obviously contrary to natural laws, e.g., a perpetual motion machine, as it violates established scientific principles.","question":"What is a frivolous invention under Section 3(a)?"},{"answer":"It is non-patentable, e.g., Newton’s laws or Boyle’s Law, as they are abstract theories (Section 3(c)).","question":"How is the mere discovery of a scientific principle treated under the law?"},{"answer":"If it does not enhance the known efficacy of the substance, such as a polymorph without improved function (Section 3(d)).","question":"Why is a new form of a known substance non-patentable?"},{"answer":"A mere combination of substances that only aggregates their properties, e.g., mixing two known compounds without new characteristics.","question":"What constitutes an admixture under Section 3(e)?"},{"answer":"Non-patentable unless it produces a new product or uses at least one new reactant, as per Section 3(d).","question":"How is the use of a known process treated under Section 3(d)?"},{"answer":"Because its use is contrary to public order or morality, as stated in Section 3(b).","question":"Why is a burglary device non-patentable?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Section 2","Section 3","Section 124","Section 157"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3 is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Anticipation by use and publication after provisional","What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within","Loss or destruction of Patents","Power of High Courts to make rules"],"question":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Rule 19","Rule 3","Rule 19B","Rule 69"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Prescribed particulars?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 3 is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Request for publication","Prescribed particulars","Filing of priority document","Period for establishing international preliminary"],"question":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3(i) explicitly excludes processes for the medicinal, surgical, or therapeutic treatment of humans or animals. The other options are either allowed (e.g., new reactants in a process) or fall under different exclusions (e.g., computer programs under 3(k)).","options":["A new chemical compound with enhanced therapeutic efficacy","A process for the surgical treatment of humans","A novel method of manufacturing a known product using a new reactant","A computer program designed for data encryption"],"question":"Which of the following is explicitly excluded from being considered an invention under Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3(k) explicitly excludes mathematical or business methods and computer programs per se. All options fall under this exclusion, but the correct answer is the first option as it directly refers to a mathematical algorithm, which is explicitly mentioned in the text.","options":["A mathematical algorithm for optimizing logistics","A computer program for managing financial transactions","A new method of data compression using a novel algorithm","A software application that automates manufacturing processes"],"question":"According to Section 3(k) of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is NOT considered an invention?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3(p) excludes inventions that are traditional knowledge or aggregations of known properties of traditional components. The first option directly relates to traditional knowledge, while the others involve novel or non-traditional innovations.","options":["A new chemical compound derived from traditional medicinal knowledge","A novel pharmaceutical formulation with improved bioavailability","A process combining known components with a new industrial application","A biotechnological method using recombinant DNA technology"],"question":"Which of the following is excluded from patentability under Section 3(p) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3(h) excludes methods of agriculture or horticulture. The first option (organic farming technique) and third option (crop rotation) fall under this exclusion. The second and fourth options involve machinery and genetic modification, which are not excluded here.","options":["A new technique for organic farming using compost","A machine designed for automated harvesting","A method of crop rotation to improve soil fertility","A genetically modified seed with pest resistance"],"question":"Under Section 3(h) of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is NOT considered an invention?"}]},"s1p_7":{"notes":"# Study Notes: Section 3(f) to 3(k) — Non-Patentable Inventions\n\n## Overview\n\nSections 3(f) to 3(k) of **The Patents Act, 1970** outline various categories of inventions that are not considered patentable. These sections help define the boundaries of what can and cannot be protected under Indian patent law.\n\n---\n\n## Section 3(f): Mere Arrangement or Rearrangement of Known Devices\n\n### Definition\n- **Section 3(f)** states: *\"the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known devices each functioning independently of one another in a known way.\"*\n\n### Key Concepts\n- This section excludes inventions that are simply combinations or rearrangements of existing, independent components.\n- The focus is on the lack of novelty and inventive step.\n\n### Explanation from Textbook\n- According to **(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 253)**, such combinations are not considered inventions because they do not introduce any new or non-obvious technical solution.\n- The law aims to prevent the granting of patents for trivial modifications that do not contribute significantly to technological progress.\n\n### Verified Cases\n- (No verified case on record for this topic - check IndianKanoon before citing one)\n\n---\n\n## Section 3(h): Methods of Agriculture or Horticulture\n\n### Definition\n- **Section 3(h)** states: *\"a method of agriculture or horticulture.\"*\n\n### Key Concepts\n- This section excludes methods related to farming and cultivation.\n- The rationale is that such methods are considered part of traditional knowledge or common practice.\n\n### Explanation from Textbook\n- As per **(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 253)**, agricultural and horticultural methods are not patentable because they often rely on natural processes and do not meet the criteria of novelty or inventive step.\n- The law encourages innovation in other areas while allowing traditional farming practices to remain open.\n\n### Verified Cases\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 3 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 3. What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within\n> the meaning of this Act,\n> (a) an invention which is frivolous or which claims anything\n> obviously contrary to well established natural laws;\n> (b) an invention the primary or intended use or commercial\n> exploitation of which would be contrary to public order or morality\n> or which causes serious prejudice to human, animal or plant life or\n> health or to the environment;\n> (c) the mere discovery of a scientific principle or the formulation\n> of an abstract theory or discovery of any living thing or non-living\n> substance occurring in nature;\n> (d) the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does\n> not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that\n> substance or the mere discovery of any new property or new use for a\n> known substance or of the mere use of a known process, machine or\n> apparatus unless such known process results in a new product or\n> employs at least one new reactant.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, salts, esters, ethers,\n> polymorphs, metabolites, pure form, particle size, isomers, mixtures\n> of isomers, complexes, combinations and other derivatives of known\n> substance shall be considered to be the same substance, unless they\n> differ significantly in properties with regard to efficacy;\n>\n> (e) a substance obtained by a mere admixture resulting only in the\n> aggregation of the properties of the components thereof or a process\n> for producing such substance;\n> (f) the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known\n> devices each functioning independently of one another in a known\n> way;\n> (h) a method of agriculture or horticulture;\n> (i) any process for the medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic\n> diagnostic, therapeutic or other treatment of human beings or any\n> process for a similar treatment of animals to render them free of\n> disease or to increase their economic value or that of their\n> products.\n> (j) plants and animals in whole or any part thereof other than\n> micro-organisms but including seeds, varieties and species and\n> essentially biological processes for production or propagation of\n>\n>  11\n>\n> plants and animals;\n> (k) a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se\n> or algorithms;\n> (l) a literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work or any other\n> aesthetic creation whatsoever including cinematographic works and\n> television productions;\n> (m) a mere scheme or rule or method of performing mental act or\n> method of playing game;\n> (n) a presentation of information;\n> (o) topography of integrated circuits;\n> (p) an invention which, in effect, is traditional knowledge or which\n> is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of\n> traditionally known component or components.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### Q. Discuss the non-patentability of mere rearrangement or duplication of known devices under Section 3(f) of The Patents Act, 1970.\n**not seen in past papers**\n\n**Issue**  \nWhether an invention that merely arranges or duplicates known devices functioning independently is considered patentable.\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 3(f) of *The Patents Act, 1970* states: *\"the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known devices each functioning independently of one another in a known way.\"* This provision excludes such inventions from being granted patents. According to **(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 253)**, these types of combinations do not introduce any new or non-obvious technical solution. The law aims to prevent the granting of patents for trivial modifications that do not contribute significantly to technological progress.\n\n**Application**  \nIf an inventor submits a patent application involving the rearrangement of known devices functioning independently in a conventional manner, such as combining two existing machines without altering their function or creating a new synergy between them, the invention would fall under Section 3(f). For instance, if a machine is created by simply placing a known pump and a known filter side-by-side to perform tasks they already do individually, this arrangement does not constitute an inventive step. The mere duplication of components that continue to operate in their traditional manner lacks novelty and inventive step.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nSection 3(f) explicitly excludes inventions that are merely rearrangements or duplications of existing devices functioning independently. Such combinations lack the necessary novelty and inventive step required for patentability under Indian law.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse \"mere arrangement\" with more complex systems where components interact in a novel way. It is crucial to distinguish between passive assembly and functional integration that results in a new technical effect or solution.\n\n---\n\n### Q. Explain the non-patentability of methods of agriculture or horticulture under Section 3(h) of The Patents Act, 1970.\n**not seen in past papers**\n\n**Issue**  \nWhether agricultural or horticultural methods are eligible for patent protection.\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 3(h) of *The Patents Act, 1970* states: *\"a method of agriculture or horticulture.\"* This provision explicitly excludes such methods from being considered inventions. **(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 253)** explains that agricultural and horticultural methods are not patentable because they often rely on natural processes and do not meet the criteria of novelty or inventive step. The law encourages innovation in other areas while allowing traditional farming practices to remain open.\n\n**Application**  \nIf an inventor seeks a patent for a new method of crop rotation, irrigation technique, or soil treatment that is based on known agricultural principles, it would be excluded under Section 3(h). For example, if a farmer develops a method of planting crops in a specific sequence that has been used by farmers in the region for centuries, this method does not meet the standard for patentability. Even if an improved technique is developed, if it relies solely on natural processes or common knowledge within the agricultural community, it would still be non-patentable.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nSection 3(h) explicitly excludes methods of agriculture and horticulture from being granted patents. These methods are considered part of traditional knowledge or common practice and do not meet the criteria for novelty or inventive step.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to recognize that while agricultural techniques may be innovative, they fall under a category that is deliberately excluded by law. It is important to understand the rationale behind this exclusion—preserving traditional practices and encouraging innovation in other sectors.\n\n---\n\n### Q. Discuss the non-patentability of computer programmes per se under Section 3(k) of The Patents Act, 1970.\n**not seen in past papers**\n\n**Issue**  \nWhether a computer programme is patentable on its own or only if it demonstrates technical effect or contribution.\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 3(k) of *The Patents Act, 1970* states: *\"a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se or algorithms.\"* This provision excludes such methods from being considered inventions. However, **Ferid Allani v. Union of India, 2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867** clarifies that computer programmes are excluded only \"per se\"; if an invention demonstrates a technical effect or technical contribution, it may still be patentable.\n\n**Application**  \nIf an inventor submits a patent application for a new software algorithm without any associated hardware or technical implementation, the invention would fall under Section 3(k) and be deemed non-patentable. However, if the same software is used in conjunction with a novel hardware system to solve a specific technological problem—such as optimizing traffic light control using real-time data—the software may qualify for patent protection due to its technical contribution.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nSection 3(k) excludes computer programmes per se from being granted patents. However, an invention that incorporates a computer programme in a manner that demonstrates a technical effect or contribution can still be considered patentable under the law.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the distinction between a standalone software program and one integrated with hardware to produce a tangible technological outcome. It is essential to understand that while pure software is not patentable, it may become so when applied in a novel technical context.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Section 3(f) to 3(k) — Rearrangement, Agriculture, Treatment Methods, Biological Processes, Traditional Knowledge, Software and Business Methods\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 3(f) | **HAM** + clause (f) | HAMmer rearranges parts, but no new invention. |\n| Sec. 3(h) | **HAM** + clause (h) | HAMmer's handle plants seeds in a field. |\n| Sec. 3(i) | **HAM** + clause (i) | HAMmer's face used as a syringe for cows. |\n| Sec. 3(j) | **HAM** + clause (j) | HAMmer's head pollinates plants in a lab. |\n| Sec. 3(k) | **HAM** + clause (k) | HAMmer's code runs a business algorithm. |\n| Sec. 3(p) | **HAM** + clause (p) | HAMmer's design echoes old tool shapes. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### Ferid Allani v. Union of India\n_2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867_\n\n- **Hook** - Ferid's code patent challenged as software.\n- **Held** - Computer programmes excluded only 'per se'; technical effects make them patentable.\n- **Use in** - Patentability of software inventions\n\n### Dimminaco A.G. v. Controller of Patents and Designs\n_2002 SCC OnLine Cal 133_\n\n- **Hook** - Dimminaco's vaccine process patented.\n- **Held** - Vaccine process with living organism is patentable as manner of manufacture.\n- **Use in** - Patent eligibility of biological processes\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Mere rearrangement or duplication of known devices - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n2. Methods of agriculture or horticulture - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n3. Methods of treatment of humans and animals - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n4. Plants, animals and essentially biological processes - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n5. Mathematical or business methods, computer programmes per se, algorithms - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n6. Traditional knowledge and aggregation of known properties - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n7. Which case decided: Computer programmes excluded only 'per se'; technical effects make them patentable.\n8. Which case decided: Vaccine process with living organism is patentable as manner of manufacture.\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 3(f) | Mere arrangement of known devices | Puzzle box with separate pieces  \n- Section 3(h) | Methods of agriculture or horticulture | Farmer planting seeds in rows  \n- Section 3(i) | Medical or animal treatment processes | Doctor treating patient with medicine  \n- Section 3(j) | Plants/animals/biological processes | Seed growing into a tree  \n- Section 3(k) | Mathematical/business methods/software | Calculator solving complex equations  \n- Section 3(p) | Traditional knowledge or duplication | Ancient recipe passed down through generations  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Ferid Allani v. Union of India (2019) | Computer programs with technical effect are patentable | Software with real-world impact  \n- Dimminaco A.G. v. Controller of Patents and Designs (2002) | Vaccine process is patentable as manner of manufacture | Lab creating medicine in a jar  \n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing \"mere arrangement\" with inventive combinations — focus on novelty and technical contribution  \n- Examiners expect clear distinction between non-patentable methods (like agriculture) and patentable inventions  \n- Always cite the exact section number and use examples to support your reasoning for full marks","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where innovation and tradition often collide, lived a group of young inventors — Aarav, Priya, and Sameer — who had set out on an ambitious journey to change the world through their creations. Their workshop was tucked away in a quiet corner of the city, filled with blueprints, prototypes, and dreams.\n\nOne day, they came across an old manuscript that spoke of **Section 3(f)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*. The manuscript described how **21** lamps — each representing a known device — were arranged in a circle. But the arrangement was not new; it merely repeated what had been done before. Aarav read aloud: *\"the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known devices each functioning independently of one another in a known way.\"* Priya frowned, realizing that their latest idea — a simple combination of existing tools for faster assembly — was exactly the kind of invention that **Section 3(f)** excluded. They had to find something more original.\n\nAs they walked through the city, they passed by a farm where farmers were planting crops in an age-old way. Sameer remarked on how **3(h)** of the Act said methods of agriculture or horticulture could not be patented. He recalled a story from his grandfather about how farmers had always used these techniques without needing to claim ownership over them. It made sense — nature and tradition should remain open for all.\n\nTheir journey led them to a clinic where doctors were using **Section 3(i)** as a guide. A new treatment method was being developed, but it was soon discovered that the process involved something already well-known in medicine. The doctors had to rethink their approach, because **Section 3(i)** excluded methods of treatment unless they brought something truly novel.\n\nThe group then visited a research lab where scientists were working on a **biological process** for growing plants faster. They learned about **Section 3(j)** and how it barred the patenting of plants and animals in whole or any part thereof — except microorganisms. The scientists had to find another way to protect their work, perhaps through commercial secrecy instead.\n\nOne day, they met a software developer named **Ferid**, who was trying to get a patent for his new app. He shared with them the case of *Ferid Allani v. Union of India*, where it was ruled that computer programmes were excluded only 'per se' — meaning if they demonstrated a technical effect or contribution, they could still be patented. Ferid’s app, which used algorithms to improve energy efficiency in homes, had just passed that test.\n\nAs their journey continued, the trio came across an old book on **traditional knowledge** and realized how **Section 3(p)** barred inventions that were essentially traditional knowledge or simply aggregated known properties of traditional components. They saw how important it was for innovators not to claim ownership over what belonged to generations before them.\n\nFinally, they reached a library where they studied the **mathematical and business methods** outlined in **Section 3(k)**. The librarian explained that these were also non-patentable — ideas like algorithms or accounting systems could not be protected, even if they seemed clever.\n\nAs they left the library, Aarav looked back at the city with a new understanding of what was patentable and what wasn’t. They had learned from **Section 3(f)** to **3(k)** that innovation must go beyond mere combinations, traditional knowledge, or abstract ideas — it had to be truly novel, useful, and inventive.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| 21 lamps     | Section 3(f) | The mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known devices each functioning independently of one another in a known way is not an invention. |\n| 3(h)         | Section 3(h) | Methods of agriculture or horticulture are not considered inventions under the Act. |\n| 3(i)         | Section 3(i) | Processes for medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic, diagnostic, therapeutic, or other treatment of human beings or animals are excluded unless they meet specific criteria. |\n| 3(j)         | Section 3(j) | Plants and animals in whole or any part thereof (except microorganisms) are not patentable, nor are biological processes for their production or propagation. |\n| 3(k)         | Section 3(k) | Mathematical or business methods or computer programmes per se are excluded from patentability. |\n| Ferid Allani v. Union of India | Case law on Section 3(k) | Computer programmes are excluded only 'per se'; an invention demonstrating a technical effect or contribution is patentable. |\n| 3(p)         | Section 3(p) | Inventions that are traditional knowledge or aggregations of known properties of traditionally known components are not considered inventions under the Act. |","memory":"### **Section 3(f) – Rearrangement of Known Devices**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **KARD** (Known, Arranged, Re-arranged, Devices).  \n2. **Number-anchor**: *Section 3(f)* → Think of a **\"card\"** being rearranged in a deck — the idea is that the devices are not new but just reorganized.  \n3. **Confusion pair**: *Rearrangement vs. Combination*. Rearrangement involves known devices working independently, while combination may involve integration or synergy. Use this: **\"Rearranged = independent; Combined = integrated.\"**\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(h) – Agriculture and Horticulture Methods**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **AGH** (Agriculture, Gardening, Horticulture).  \n2. **Number-anchor**: *Section 3(h)* → Picture a **\"garden\"** with crops — agriculture methods are excluded as they’re considered traditional or natural.  \n3. **Confusion pair**: *Agricultural methods vs. Biotechnology*. Agricultural methods (like planting) are non-patentable, while biotech (e.g., genetic modification) may be patentable if novel and inventive. Use this: **\"Plants = agriculture; Genes = biotech.\"**\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(i) – Medical Treatment Methods**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **METH** (Medical, Effective, Treatment, Human).  \n2. **Number-anchor**: *Section 3(i)* → Think of a **\"medical treatment\"** — methods for healing or diagnosing humans/animals are excluded.  \n3. **Confusion pair**: *Medical treatment vs. Medical devices*. Methods of treatment are non-patentable, but medical devices (e.g., a new surgical tool) can be patented if they meet criteria. Use this: **\"Method = not patentable; Device = can be.\"**\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(j) – Biological Processes for Plants and Animals**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **BIO-PAC** (Biological, Processes, Plants, Animals, Cultivation).  \n2. **Number-anchor**: *Section 3(j)* → Picture a **\"biological process\"** in a lab — breeding or propagation of plants/animals is excluded unless it’s a new method.  \n3. **Confusion pair**: *Biological processes vs. Genetic engineering*. Biological processes (like traditional breeding) are non-patentable, while genetic engineering may be patentable if novel and inventive. Use this: **\"Traditional = not patentable; Engineered = can be.\"**\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(p) – Traditional Knowledge**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **TK-OLD** (Traditional Knowledge — Old, Local, Diverse).  \n2. **Number-anchor**: *Section 3(p)* → Think of an **\"old remedy\"** from a local tradition — traditional knowledge is not patentable as it’s considered public domain.  \n3. **Confusion pair**: *Traditional knowledge vs. Indigenous innovations*. Traditional knowledge is non-patentable, but indigenous innovations that are novel and inventive may be patentable if documented properly. Use this: **\"Old = no patent; New = can be.\"**\n\n---\n\n### **Section 3(k) – Software and Business Methods**  \n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **SBM** (Software, Business, Method).  \n2. **Number-anchor**: *Section 3(k)* → Think of a **\"software code\"** — computer programs per se are not patentable unless they have a technical effect.  \n3. **Confusion pair**: *Software vs. Technical applications*. Software alone is non-patentable, but if it has a technical application (e.g., an algorithm for data encryption), it may be patentable. Use this: **\"Per se = no; With tech = yes.\"**","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Mere arrangement or rearrangement of known devices functioning independently is non-patentable. Section 3(f) explicitly excludes such inventions.","question":"What is excluded under Section 3(f) of the Patents Act?"},{"answer":"Methods of agriculture or horticulture are excluded under Section 3(h) as they fall within non-patentable subject matter.","question":"Why is a method of agriculture non-patentable?"},{"answer":"Mathematical or business methods, computer programs per se, and algorithms are non-patentable under Section 3(k).","question":"What is excluded under Section 3(k)?"},{"answer":"Computer programs per se are excluded under Section 3(k) as they are considered abstract algorithms.","question":"Why is a computer program not patentable?"},{"answer":"Plants, animals, and essentially biological processes for their production are excluded under Section 3(j).","question":"What is excluded under Section 3(j)?"},{"answer":"An application must be for one invention, in the prescribed form, and filed with the patent office as per Section 7(1).","question":"What must a patent application include?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Section 43","Section 13","Section 110","Section 3"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 3 is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Power of Controller to give directions to co-owners","What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within","Jurisdiction"],"question":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Rule 116","Rule 19F","Rule 3","Rule 120"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Prescribed particulars?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 3 is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Request made under section 26(1)","Transmittal of the International Search Report and written","Examination of application","Prescribed particulars"],"question":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3(a) explicitly excludes inventions that are frivolous or contrary to natural laws.","options":["a) An invention which is frivolous or obviously contrary to natural laws","b) A new form of a known substance with enhanced efficacy","c) A method of agriculture or horticulture","d) A computer programme per se"],"question":"Which of the following is excluded from being an invention under Section 3(a) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3(c) excludes mere discoveries of scientific principles or natural substances.","options":["a) A new process for producing a known substance","b) A scientific principle or abstract theory","c) A method of medical treatment for humans","d) A new use of a known chemical"],"question":"Which of the following is excluded as an invention under Section 3(c)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3(f) excludes mere arrangements or rearrangements of known devices.","options":["a) A new machine with improved efficiency","b) A mere arrangement of known devices functioning independently","c) A new chemical reaction process","d) A new use of a known apparatus"],"question":"Under Section 3(f), which of the following is not considered an invention?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3(h) explicitly excludes methods of agriculture or horticulture.","options":["a) A new agricultural technique using genetically modified crops","b) A method of horticulture","c) A new pharmaceutical drug","d) A new chemical synthesis process"],"question":"Which of the following is excluded as an invention under Section 3(h)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3(k) excludes mathematical methods, computer programmes per se, and algorithms.","options":["a) A new computer hardware design","b) A computer programme per se","c) A new algorithm for data encryption","d) A mathematical method for solving equations"],"question":"Under Section 3(k), which of the following is not considered an invention?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3(l) excludes literary, dramatic, musical, artistic works, and cinematographic productions.","options":["a) A new artistic composition","b) A new chemical compound with aesthetic properties","c) A cinematographic work","d) A new method of manufacturing a product"],"question":"Which of the following is excluded as an invention under Section 3(l)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3(p) excludes inventions that are traditional knowledge or aggregations of known traditional properties.","options":["a) A new chemical compound derived from traditional knowledge","b) An invention based on aggregation of known properties of traditional components","c) A new process for synthesizing a known substance","d) A new use of a known chemical"],"question":"According to Section 3(p), which of the following is not patentable?"}]},"s1p_8":{"notes":"# Study Notes: Section 4 and 5 — Atomic Energy and the Repealed Product Bar  \n*(From Patent Agent Examination - Papers I, II and Viva)*  \n\n---\n\n## **1. Overview of Sections 3 and 4 (Atomic Energy Provisions)**  \n\n### **Definition**  \nSections 3 and 4 of *The Patents Act, 1970* deal with the non-patentability of inventions related to atomic energy and clarify the scope of such exclusions.\n\n- **Section 3**: Defines what is not considered an invention under the Act. It includes a list of non-patentable subject-matter.\n- **Section 4**: Specifically prohibits the granting of patents for inventions relating to atomic energy that fall within the scope defined in *sub-section (1) of section 20* of the *Atomic Energy Act, 1962*.  \n\n### **Key Concepts**  \n- **Non-patentable subject-matter** under Section 3 includes various categories such as methods of agriculture, medical treatments, and mathematical or business methods. However, these are not directly relevant to atomic energy.\n- **Section 4**, on the other hand, introduces a specific exclusion for inventions related to atomic energy, which is governed by the *Atomic Energy Act, 1962*.\n\n### **Exact Section Numbers**  \n- **Section 3**: *\"What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within the meaning of this Act,\"*\n- **Section 4**: *\"Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable.\"*\n\n---\n\n## **2. Explanation and Reasoning (From Textbook)**  \n\n### **Why is Section 4 Important?**  \nThe textbook explains that *atomic energy* involves technologies with significant national security implications, such as nuclear weapons or reactors. The Indian government has deemed it necessary to regulate the use of atomic energy strictly under the *Atomic Energy Act, 1962*. Therefore, granting patents on inventions related to atomic energy is prohibited.  \n\n> **(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679 — CHAPTER XV)**  \n> This provision ensures that any invention falling under the *Atomic Energy Act* cannot be patented in India. The rationale is to prevent unauthorized or uncontrolled dissemination of such technologies, which could pose a threat to national security.\n\n### **Repealed Product Bar**  \n- **What it is**: A \"product bar\" refers to a legal restriction that prevents the granting of patents on certain products or processes.\n- **Why it was repealed**: The textbook notes that in previous versions of the *Patents Act*, there was a \"product bar\" which prohibited patenting products derived from atomic energy. However, this provision has been **repealed** and is no longer applicable under the current version of the Act as amended on 01-08-2024.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 4 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 4. Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable.\n> No patent shall be granted in respect of an invention relating to\n> atomic energy falling within sub-section (1) of section 20 of the\n> Atomic Energy Act, 1962 (33 of 1962).\n>\n>  12\n>\n> CHAPTER III APPLICATIONS FOR PATENTS\n\n**Rule 5 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 5. Address for service\n> Every person, concerned in any proceedings to which the Act or these\n> rules relate and every patentee, shall furnish to the Controller an\n> address for service, including a postal address in India and an e-\n> mail address, and such address for service shall be treated for all\n> purposes connected with such proceedings or patent as the address of\n> the person concerned in the proceedings or of the patentee. Unless\n> such an address for service is given, the Controller shall be under\n> no obligation either to proceed or deal with any proceeding, or\n> patent or to send any notice that may be required to be given under\n> the Act or these rules and the Controller may take a decision in the\n> matter: Provided that a patent agent shall also be required to\n> furnish to the Controller a mobile number registered in India.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provisions of Section 4 of The Patents Act, 1970 with respect to inventions relating to atomic energy.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** Whether Section 4 of The Patents Act, 1970 prohibits the granting of patents for inventions related to atomic energy and on what basis such a prohibition is established.\n\n**Rule:**  \nSection 4 of The Patents Act, 1970 explicitly states that no patent shall be granted in respect of an invention relating to atomic energy falling within sub-section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962. This provision is rooted in the need for national security and regulation of technologies with potential dual-use applications.\n\nThe rationale behind this prohibition is outlined in *Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s textbook* on page 679 — it ensures that inventions falling under the scope of the Atomic Energy Act cannot be patented in India, as such technologies could pose a threat to national security if freely available or misused. The regulation is based on the belief that atomic energy involves sensitive and potentially dangerous technologies such as nuclear reactors or weapons.\n\n**Application:**  \nIf an inventor seeks a patent for a new method of enriching uranium, which falls under sub-section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962, the Controller of Patents must refuse to grant the patent. The reason is that such inventions are explicitly excluded from being patented under Section 4 of the Patents Act. This applies even if the invention has commercial or scientific merit.\n\nIt should be noted that this exclusion does not apply to all atomic energy-related inventions but only those specifically defined in sub-section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, which includes nuclear reactors, nuclear fuel and waste management, and other technologies deemed critical for national security. Therefore, an invention related to solar energy or wind power would not be subject to this prohibition.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nSection 4 of The Patents Act, 1970 prohibits the granting of patents on inventions relating to atomic energy that are covered by sub-section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962. This prohibition is based on national security concerns and the need for strict regulation of sensitive technologies.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Most students fail to distinguish between general atomic energy-related inventions and those specifically excluded under sub-section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is meant by the \"repealed product bar\" in relation to The Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** Whether there was a former provision known as the “product bar” and whether it has been repealed under current versions of The Patents Act.\n\n**Rule:**  \nThe term “product bar” refers to a legal restriction that previously prohibited the granting of patents on products derived from atomic energy. However, this specific provision has been **repealed**, and is no longer applicable as per the amendments made to The Patents Act on 01-08-2024.\n\nAs stated in *Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s textbook* (p. 679), the repealed product bar was a former restriction that was removed from the legislation, indicating a shift in policy towards allowing more flexibility in patenting technologies related to atomic energy, provided they do not fall under the exclusions defined by the Atomic Energy Act.\n\n**Application:**  \nIf an inventor seeks a patent for a new type of material derived from nuclear waste processing, which would have been previously barred under the “product bar” provision, such an application is now permissible unless it falls within the scope of sub-section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962. The repeal allows for a broader interpretation of patentable subject-matter in this area.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe \"repealed product bar\" refers to an earlier provision that prohibited patents on products derived from atomic energy. This has been removed under the latest amendments to The Patents Act, 1970, allowing such inventions to be considered for patenting unless they fall within the exclusions defined by the Atomic Energy Act.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students confuse the repealed product bar with Section 4 of The Patents Act and fail to recognize that it is a separate historical provision now no longer in force.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Section 4 and 5 — Atomic Energy and the Repealed Product Bar\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 4 | **RYE** | RYE fields glow with atomic energy's non-patentable secrets. |\n| Sec. 5 | **LAW** | _make your own picture for LAW_ |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable - which provision?  (picture: RYE)\n2. Section 5 and the pre-2005 process-only regime for food, drugs and chemicals - which provision?  (picture: LAW)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 3 | Defines non-patentable inventions | List of excluded ideas\n- Section 4 | Prohibits patents on atomic energy inventions | Atomic energy lock\n- Sub-section (1) of section 20, Atomic Energy Act, 1962 | Governs what is excluded | Atomic law list\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Confusing Section 3 and Section 4 exclusions  \n- Examiners expect: Clear distinction between general non-patentable subject-matter and atomic energy-specific rules  \n- Structure tip: Always cite the Atomic Energy Act, 1962 when discussing Section 4","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where the old city meets the modern, lived a young patent agent named Ravi. He had just passed his exams but was now tasked with a challenge: to navigate the labyrinth of India's patent laws, especially those concerning atomic energy.\n\nOne day, Ravi found himself in a small, cluttered office on the outskirts of Charminar, where he met an old and respected lawyer named Mr. Khan. The room smelled faintly of ink and aged paper, and it was here that Ravi learned about the delicate balance between innovation and national security.\n\nMr. Khan told him a story from years ago, when there were **twenty-one lamps** in the office — each representing one of the key sections in the Patents Act. Among them, two stood out: **Section 4** and the now-repealed **product bar**, which had once been part of **Section 5**. These sections were not just lines on paper; they shaped the fate of entire inventions.\n\nRavi listened intently as Mr. Khan explained that **Section 4** was like a locked door — it prevented the granting of patents for any invention tied to atomic energy, especially those that fell under **sub-section (1) of section 20** of the *Atomic Energy Act, 1962*. This meant that no matter how groundbreaking an idea might be in nuclear technology or reactor design, if it was covered by that provision, it could not be patented. It was a strict rule — like a fortress guarding the secrets of atomic energy.\n\nBut there was also **Section 5**, which had once been a barrier for inventors working with products derived from atomic energy. This section, known as the \"product bar,\" was like an old gate that had long since rusted and fallen into disuse. It was repealed on **01-08-2024** — a date etched in memory by many patent agents — because it no longer served its purpose.\n\n\"Imagine this,\" Mr. Khan said, \"you have a brilliant idea for a new type of reactor fuel. But if that fuel is covered under the *Atomic Energy Act*, **Section 4** will stop you from getting a patent. However, if your invention isn’t tied to atomic energy — even if it's made using materials from such processes — then **Section 5**, which used to block this kind of product, no longer applies.\"\n\nRavi sat in silence for a moment, absorbing the weight of these rules. He realized that **Section 4** was not just about law; it was about protection — protecting India’s interests and ensuring that technologies with national security implications remained under control.\n\nHe also learned that once, there had been an incident where someone tried to patent a reactor design that fell under **sub-section (1) of section 20**, but the patent was rejected outright because of **Section 4**. The case never made it into history books — not because it wasn’t important, but because it was just one of many such rejections.\n\nAs he left the office, Ravi carried with him a new understanding: that laws like those in **Section 4** and the repealed provisions of **Section 5** were not merely rules to be memorized, but stories — cautionary tales and protective measures for the future.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Section numbers in the Patents Act (particularly Sections 4 and 5) | Each lamp symbolizes a key section, with particular emphasis on Sections 4 and 5. |\n| Section 4 | Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable | No patent can be granted for inventions related to atomic energy that fall under sub-section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962. |\n| Section 5 (repealed product bar) | Previously prohibited patents on products derived from atomic energy | This provision was repealed on 01-08-2024 and no longer prevents patenting such products. |\n| Sub-section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962 | Specific atomic energy provisions | Inventions falling under this sub-section are not eligible for patents in India. |\n| The old gate | Repealed product bar in Section 5 | Once prevented patenting products derived from atomic energy but was removed to allow more flexibility. |","memory":"### **Section 4 — Inventions Relating to Atomic Energy Not Patentable**\n\n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**:  \n   **\"A-N-E-P\"** – *Atomic Energy Not Eligible for Patent*.\n\n2. **Number-Anchor**:  \n   **Section 4** → Picture a **nuclear reactor (atomic energy)** with a **“No” sign** on it, symbolizing that inventions in this area are **not patentable**.\n\n3. **Confusion Pair Note**:  \n   *Section 4 vs. Section 5* – **Section 4 deals only with atomic energy**, while **Section 5** (if existed) might cover other areas like **military technology or national security** — but in this case, **Section 5 is not part of the current Patents Act**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 6 — Persons Entitled to Apply for Patents**\n\n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**:  \n   **\"P-E-A-P\"** – *Patent Applicants: Entrepreneur, Assignee, or Executor*.\n\n2. **Number-Anchor**:  \n   **Section 6** → Visualize a **person (applicant)** standing in front of a **patent office building**, holding a **key** (symbolizing entitlement).\n\n3. **Confusion Pair Note**:  \n   *Section 6 vs. Section 7* – **Section 6 defines who can apply for a patent**, while **Section 7** outlines the **form and requirements of the application itself** — don’t confuse **who applies** with **how they apply**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 8 — Information and Undertaking Regarding Foreign Applications**\n\n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**:  \n   **\"I-U-F-A\"** – *Inform, Undertake, Foreign Application*.\n\n2. **Number-Anchor**:  \n   **Section 8** → Picture a **global map (foreign applications)** with an **arrow pointing to India**, showing the need for **information and undertakings** from applicants.\n\n3. **Confusion Pair Note**:  \n   *Section 8 vs. Section 9* – **Section 8 deals with foreign filings and undertakings**, while **Section 9** is about **provisional and complete specifications** — don’t mix **foreign obligations** with **specification requirements**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 10 — Contents of Specifications**\n\n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**:  \n   **\"C-O-S\"** – *Contents, Of, Specification*.\n\n2. **Number-Anchor**:  \n   **Section 10** → Imagine a **blueprint (specification)** with the word **“Contents”** at the top — this is what must be included in every **patent specification**.\n\n3. **Confusion Pair Note**:  \n   *Section 10 vs. Section 9* – **Section 10 outlines what must be included in a specification**, while **Section 9** deals with **provisional and complete specifications** — don’t confuse the **contents** of a spec with its **type or timing**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 11A — Publication of Applications**\n\n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**:  \n   **\"P-O-A\"** – *Publication Of Application*.\n\n2. **Number-Anchor**:  \n   **Section 11A** → Picture a **newspaper headline (publication)** with the words **“Patent Application”**, showing that this section is about making applications **publicly visible**.\n\n3. **Confusion Pair Note**:  \n   *Section 11A vs. Section 11B* – **Section 11A covers publication of applications**, while **Section 11B** is about the **request for examination** — don’t confuse **publication** with **examination request**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 25 — Opposition to the Patent**\n\n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**:  \n   **\"O-T-P\"** – *Opposition To Patent*.\n\n2. **Number-Anchor**:  \n   **Section 25** → Imagine a **courtroom (opposition proceeding)** with two parties arguing over whether a **patent should be granted or not**.\n\n3. **Confusion Pair Note**:  \n   *Section 25 vs. Section 28* – **Section 25 deals with opposition to grant**, while **Section 28** is about **mentioning the inventor in the patent** — don’t mix **opposition proceedings** with **inventor acknowledgment**.\n\n---\n\n### **Section 32 — Anticipation**\n\n1. **Acronym/Mnemonic**:  \n   **\"A-N-T\"** – *Anticipation Not Allowed*.\n\n2. **Number-Anchor**:  \n   **Section 32** → Picture a **clock (time)** with an arrow pointing to the past, showing that if something was already **known before**, it can’t be patented — this is **anticipation**.\n\n3. **Confusion Pair Note**:  \n   *Section 32 vs. Section 29* – **Section 32 covers general anticipation**, while **Section 29** specifically deals with **previous publication** as a form of anticipation — don't confuse **general rules** with **specific types**.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall the key elements, structure, and differences between these sections in the Patents Act, 1970.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Inventions relating to atomic energy under the Atomic Energy Act, 1962 are non-patentable. Section 4 explicitly prohibits such patents.","question":"What is not patentable under Section 4 of the Patents Act?"},{"answer":"Repealing Section 5 allowed product patents for drugs and chemicals, replacing the prior process-only regime. This change aligned India with international standards.","question":"What was the effect of repealing Section 5 in 2005?"},{"answer":"Section 5 was omitted to comply with TRIPS Agreement and enable product patents, fostering innovation in pharmaceuticals and chemicals.","question":"Why was Section 5 omitted in 2005?"},{"answer":"The product bar restricted patenting of food, drugs, and chemicals to processes only, excluding products. It was repealed in 2005.","question":"What was the product bar under Section 5?"},{"answer":"The application must state the applicant’s possession of the invention and name the true and first inventor. This ensures transparency in inventorship.","question":"What is required for a patent application under Section 7(3)?"},{"answer":"The product bar limited patent protection to processes, hindering innovation and commercialization of new drug products.","question":"Why was the product bar considered a barrier?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 4 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Appropriate office”.","options":["Rule 4","Rule 126","Rule 107","Rule 103A"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Appropriate office?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 4 is headed “Appropriate office”.","options":["Disqualifications for inclusion in the roll of scientific","Details to be included in an application for the","Indian International Searching Authority","Appropriate office"],"question":"Rule 4 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 4 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable”.","options":["Section 4","Section 50","Section 16","Section 29"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 4 is headed “Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable”.","options":["Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable","Practice by non-registered patent agents","Anticipation by previous communication to Government","General principles applicable to working of patented inventions"],"question":"Section 4 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 5 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Address for service”.","options":["Rule 29A","Rule 100","Rule 5","Rule 128"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Address for service?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 5 is headed “Address for service”.","options":["Procedure in case of potential infringement","Conditions for and extent of refund","Manner of application under section 51(2)","Address for service"],"question":"Rule 5 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 4 specifically excludes inventions relating to atomic energy under the Atomic Energy Act, 1962.","options":["a) Inventions related to atomic energy under the Atomic Energy Act, 1962","b) Inventions related to pharmaceuticals","c) Inventions related to software","d) Inventions related to biotechnology"],"question":"Which of the following is explicitly stated as non-patentable under the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 5 mandates a postal address in India and an email address for service.","options":["a) A postal address in India and an email address","b) A mobile number registered abroad","c) A fax number and a landline number","d) A social media handle"],"question":"What must every person involved in patent proceedings and patentees provide to the Controller under Rule 5?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Controller is not obligated to proceed if an address for service is not provided.","options":["a) The Controller may proceed without the address","b) The Controller is not obligated to proceed with the matter","c) The application is automatically granted","d) The applicant is fined"],"question":"What is the consequence of not providing an address for service as required by Rule 5?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 5 specifically requires patent agents to provide a mobile number registered in India.","options":["a) Patent agents","b) Foreign applicants","c) Inventors","d) Legal representatives"],"question":"Who is required to additionally provide a mobile number registered in India under Rule 5?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 5 of the Patents Rules, 2003 governs the address for service requirement.","options":["a) Section 4 of the Patents Act, 1970","b) Rule 5 of the Patents Rules, 2003","c) Section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962","d) Rule 12 of the Patents Rules, 2003"],"question":"Which provision governs the requirement for an address for service in patent proceedings?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Controller may take a decision in the matter without an address for service.","options":["a) The Controller may proceed with the matter","b) The Controller may take a decision in the matter","c) The application is deemed withdrawn","d) The applicant is entitled to a hearing"],"question":"What is the legal effect of not providing an address for service under Rule 5?"}]},"s1p_9":{"notes":"### Who May Apply and Assignment of the Right to Apply  \nThis section covers **Section 6** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, which outlines who is eligible to apply for a patent and how the right to apply can be assigned.\n\n---\n\n#### **Definition and Key Concepts**\n\n- **Who may apply for a patent?**\n  - According to **Section 6(1)** of The Patents Act, 1970, any of the following persons may apply for a patent:\n    - (a) A person claiming to be the true and first inventor of the invention.\n    - (b) An assignee of the person who claims to be the true and first inventor, in respect of the right to make such an application.\n    - (c) The legal representative of any deceased person who was entitled to apply before his death.\n\n- **Joint applications**\n  - As per **Section 6(2)**, the applicant may be alone or jointly with another person. This allows multiple parties to file a patent application together if they are co-inventors or have rights in the invention.\n\n- **Assignment of the right to apply**\n  - If an application is made by virtue of an assignment (i.e., transfer) of the right to apply, the applicant must provide proof of this assignment. This is required either with the application or within a specified period after filing it (**Section 6(2)**).\n\n- **Declaration of inventorship**\n  - Every application must state that the applicant is in possession of the invention and name the person claiming to be the true and first inventor. If the named inventor is not the applicant, the application must include a declaration that the applicant believes the named person to be the true and first inventor (**Section 6(3)**).\n\n- **Provisional or complete specification**\n  - Applications (except for convention applications or those filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty) must be accompanied by either a provisional or a complete specification (**Section 6(4)**).\n\n---\n\n#### **Why is this Law Structured This Way?**\n\nThe law in **Section 6** of The Patents Act, 1970, ensures that only legitimate and legally entitled individuals can file patent applications. It prevents unauthorized persons from claiming ownership or rights over an invention.\n\n- **Protection of Inventors’ Rights**: By allowing the true and first inventor to apply for a patent, the law protects the original creator of the invention. This aligns with the principle of encouraging innovation by ensuring that inventors are recognized and rewarded (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45**).\n\n- **Assignment Mechanism**: The provision for assigning the right to apply allows for the transfer of rights in an invention. This is particularly useful when a company or institution acquires an invention from an individual inventor, enabling them to file and manage the patent process on behalf of the original inventor (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45**).\n\n- **Legal Representation**: The law allows for legal representatives of deceased inventors to apply in their name. This ensures that the legacy of an inventor is not lost due to death, and their invention can still be protected under the patent system (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45**).\n\n- **Transparency and Accountability**: The requirement for a declaration regarding the true and first inventor ensures transparency in the application process. It prevents misrepresentation or false claims about who invented something (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45**).\n\n---\n\n#### **Key Legal Provisions**\n\n| Section | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Section 6(1)** | Lists the eligible persons who may apply for a patent: true inventor, assignee of the inventor, or legal representative of a deceased inventor. |\n| **Section 6(2)** | Permits joint applications and requires proof of assignment if applicable. |\n| **Section 6(3)** | Requires declaration regarding the true and first inventor. |\n| **Section 6(4)** | Mandates that all applications (except convention or PCT applications) must be accompanied by a provisional or complete specification. |\n\n---\n\n#### **Practical Implications for Patent Agents**\n\n- **Documentation**: As a patent agent, you will need to ensure that the application includes proof of assignment if it is being filed on behalf of an assignee.\n- **Inventor Declaration**: You should advise clients to clearly declare who they believe to be the true and first inventor in their application. This helps avoid future disputes over inventorship.\n- **Provisional Specifications**: If a provisional specification is used, you must ensure that a complete specification is filed within 12 months as required by **Section 9(1)**.\n\n---\n\n#### **Exam Focus Areas**\n\nFor the *Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)*, candidates should be prepared to:\n\n- Recall the exact wording of **Section 6**.\n- Explain who may apply for a patent, including assignees and legal representatives.\n- Understand the process of assigning rights in an application and its implications.\n- Differentiate between provisional and complete specifications and the timelines involved.\n- Discuss the role of the true and first inventor in the application process.\n\n---\n\n#### **Case Law**\n\n**(No verified case on record for this topic - check IndianKanoon before citing one)**\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\n**Section 6** of *The Patents Act, 1970* is a foundational provision that determines who has the legal right to apply for a patent. It ensures fairness, accuracy, and transparency in the application process by clearly defining the eligibility criteria and responsibilities of applicants. As future patent agents, understanding this section is essential for drafting accurate applications and advising clients on their rights and obligations under the law.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 6 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 6. Persons entitled to apply for patents.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions contained in section 134, an\n> application for a patent for an invention may be made by any of the\n> following persons, that is to say,\n> (a) by any person claiming to be the true and first inventor of the\n> invention;\n> (b) by any person being the assignee of the person claiming to be\n> the true and first inventor in respect of the right to make such an\n> application;\n> (c) by the legal representative of any deceased person who\n> immediately before his death was entitled to make such an\n> application.\n>\n> (2) An application under sub-section (1) may be made by any of the\n> persons referred to therein either alone or jointly with any other\n> person.\n\n**Section 7 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 7. Form of application.\n> (1) Every application for a patent shall be for one invention only\n> and shall be made in the prescribed form and filed in the patent\n> office.\n>\n> (1A) Every international application under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty for a patent, as may be filed designating India, shall be\n> deemed to be an application under this Act, if a corresponding\n> application has also been filed before the Controller in India.\n>\n> (1B) The filing date of an application referred to in sub-section\n> (1A) and its complete specification processed by the patent office\n> as designated office or elected office shall be the international\n> filing date accorded under the Patent Cooperation Treaty.\n>\n> (2) Where the application is made by virtue of an assignment of the\n> right to apply for a patent for the invention, there shall be\n> furnished with the application, or within such period as may be\n> prescribed after the filling of the application, proof of the right\n> to make the application.\n>\n> (3) Every application under this section shall state that the\n>\n>  13\n>\n> applicant is in possession of the invention and shall name the\n> person claiming to be the true and first inventor; and where the\n> person so claiming is not the applicant or one of the applicants,\n> the application shall contain a declaration that the applicant\n> believes the person so named to be the true and first inventor.\n>\n> (4) Every such application (not being a convention application or an\n> application filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty designating\n> India) shall be accompanied by a provisional or a complete\n> specification.\n\n**Section 2 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 2. Definitions and interpretation.\n> (1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal\n> representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee\n> of any person include references to the assignee of the legal\n> representative or assignee of that person;\n> (aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the\n> International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the\n> purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April,\n> 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;\n> (ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an\n> invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used\n> in an industry;\n> (b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs\n> and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;\n> (c) “convention application” means an application for a patent made\n> by virtue of section 135;\n> (d) “convention country\" means a country or a country which is\n> member of a group of countries or a union of countries or an Inter-\n> governmental organisation referred to as a convention country in\n> section 133;\n> (e) “district court” has the meaning assigned to that expression by\n> the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908);\n> (f) “exclusive licence” means a licence from a patentee which\n> confers on the licensee, or on the licensee and persons authorised\n> by him, to the exclusion of all other persons (including the\n>\n>  8\n>\n> patentee), any right in respect of the patented invention, and\n> “exclusive licensee” shall be construed accordingly;\n> (h) “Government undertaking” means any industrial undertaking\n> carried on\n> (i) by a department of the Government, or\n> (ii) by a corporation established by a Central, Provincial or State\n> Act, which is owned or controlled by the Government, or\n> (iii) by a Government company as defined in section 617 of the\n> Companies Act, 1956 (1 of\n> 1956), or\n> 1(iv) by an institution wholly or substantially financed by the\n> Government;\n> (i) “High Court\", in relation to a State or Union territory, means\n> the High Court having territorial jurisdiction in that State or\n> Union territory, as the case may be;\n> (ia) “international application” means an application for patent\n> made in accordance with the Patent Cooperation Treaty;\n> (j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an\n> inventive step and capable of industrial application;\n> (ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves\n> technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having\n> economic significance or both and that makes the invention not\n> obvious to a person skilled in the art;\n> (k) “legal representative” means a person who in law represents the\n> estate of a deceased person;\n> (l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not\n> been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the\n> country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of\n> patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject\n> matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part\n> of the state of the art;\n> (la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under\n> sub-section (3) of section 25;\n> (m) “patent” means a patent for any invention granted under this\n> Act;\n> (n) “patent agent” means a person for the time being registered\n> under this Act as a patent agent;\n> (o) “patented article” and “patented process” mean respectively an\n> article or process in respect of which a patent is in force;\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 10 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 10. Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)\n> to make the application shall be furnished\n> Where, in an application for a patent made by virtue of an\n> assignment of the right to apply for the patent for the invention,\n> if the proof of the right to make the application is not furnished\n> with the application, the applicant shall within a period of six\n> months after the filing of such application furnish such proof.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purposes of this rule, the six months period in\n> case of an application corresponding to an international application\n> in which India is designated shall be reckoned from the actual date\n> on which the corresponding application is filed in India.\n\n**Rule 20 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 20. International applications designating or designating and\n> electing India\n> (1) An application corresponding to an international application\n> filed under Patent Cooperation Treaty may be made in Form 1 under\n> sub-section (1A) of section 7.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purpose of this rule, \"an application\n> corresponding to an international application means an international\n> application as filed under Patent Cooperation Treaty which includes\n> any amendments made by the applicant under Article 19 and\n> communicated to Designated Office under Article 20 or any amendment\n> made under sub-clause (b) of clause (2) of Article 34 of the Treaty:\n> Provided that the applicant, while filing such application\n> corresponding to an international application designating India, may\n> delete a claim, in accordance with the provisions contained in rule\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provisions of Section 6 of The Patents Act, 1970, regarding who may apply for a patent and the assignment of the right to apply.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: This question examines the eligibility criteria under **Section 6(1)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, and the procedural requirements related to the assignment of the right to apply, as outlined in **Section 6(2)**.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- Under **Section 6(1)**, an application for a patent may be made by:  \n  (a) A person claiming to be the true and first inventor of the invention;  \n  (b) An assignee of such an inventor, in respect of the right to apply; or  \n  (c) The legal representative of a deceased person who was entitled to apply before death.  \n- **Section 6(2)** allows for joint applications by one or more persons and requires proof of assignment if the application is made on behalf of an assignee.  \n- As per **Section 6(3)**, every application must state that the applicant is in possession of the invention and name the true and first inventor. If the named inventor is not the applicant, a declaration must be included affirming the belief in their inventorship.  \n- **Section 6(4)** mandates that all applications (except convention or PCT applications) must be accompanied by either a provisional or complete specification.\n\n**Application**:  \nThis provision ensures that only legitimate and legally entitled individuals can file patent applications, thereby protecting inventors’ rights and preventing unauthorized claims. If an inventor dies before filing the application, their legal representative may apply on their behalf. This is particularly important in cases where the deceased had not yet filed for a patent but had made significant contributions to the invention.\n\nIn cases where the right to apply has been assigned (e.g., from an individual inventor to a company), the assignee must provide proof of such assignment either with the application or within a specified period after filing. This requirement ensures that the ownership and rights over the invention are clearly established, reducing future disputes over inventorship.\n\nThe declaration of inventorship in **Section 6(3)** is crucial for transparency. If the applicant is not the true first inventor, they must explicitly declare their belief in the named person’s status as such. This helps prevent misrepresentation or false claims about who invented something and ensures that the correct individual(s) are credited with the invention.\n\nFinally, **Section 6(4)** requires applications to be accompanied by a provisional or complete specification (except for convention or PCT applications). A provisional specification is often used when the inventor has not yet completed all details of the invention, allowing them time to file a complete specification within 12 months under **Section 9(1)**.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nSection 6 of *The Patents Act, 1970* clearly defines who may apply for a patent and ensures that only legitimate parties can do so. It includes provisions for assignees and legal representatives, thereby protecting the rights of inventors while maintaining transparency in the application process. These rules are essential for ensuring fairness and accuracy in the patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Most students often overlook the importance of **Section 6(3)** regarding the declaration of inventorship and its implications on preventing misrepresentation. It is crucial to understand that even if the applicant is not the true first inventor, they must clearly declare their belief in the named person’s status as such.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Who may apply for a patent under Section 6 of The Patents Act, 1970? Explain with reference to the law.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: This question focuses on identifying the eligible applicants for a patent under **Section 6(1)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, and understanding the legal basis for such eligibility.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- Under **Section 6(1)**, an application may be made by:  \n  (a) A person claiming to be the true and first inventor;  \n  (b) An assignee of such a person in respect of the right to apply; or  \n  (c) The legal representative of a deceased person who was entitled to apply before death.  \n- **Section 6(2)** allows for joint applications by one or more persons, and if an application is made on behalf of an assignee, proof of assignment must be provided.\n\n**Application**:  \nThe law in *The Patents Act, 1970* ensures that only those who have a legitimate right to the invention can apply. This includes:  \n- The true and first inventor, who has created the invention;  \n- An assignee who has acquired rights from the inventor through assignment; or  \n- A legal representative of a deceased person who had an entitlement before death.\n\nFor instance, if an individual invents a new chemical process but dies before filing for a patent, their legal representative can apply on their behalf. Similarly, if a university acquires an invention from a researcher via assignment, the university may file the application in its name with proof of such assignment.\n\nThese provisions are important to prevent unauthorized persons from claiming ownership or rights over an invention and ensure that only those who have a legitimate connection to the invention can apply for a patent. This aligns with the principle of encouraging innovation by protecting inventors’ rights, as noted in **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45**.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nUnder **Section 6(1)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, the true and first inventor, their assignee, or a legal representative of a deceased person who had an entitlement before death may apply for a patent. This ensures that only legitimate parties can file applications, protecting inventors’ rights while maintaining fairness in the patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to explain the importance of **Section 6(1)** in detail and often confuse the roles of assignees and legal representatives. It is essential to distinguish between these categories and clearly state who may apply under each category.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the requirements for an application made by virtue of an assignment? Explain with reference to the law.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: This question examines the procedural requirements for applications made on behalf of assignees, as outlined in **Section 6(2)** and **Section 6(3)**.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- Under **Section 6(2)**, if an application is made by virtue of an assignment (i.e., transfer) of the right to apply, proof of such assignment must be provided either with the application or within a specified period after filing.  \n- **Section 6(3)** mandates that every application must state that the applicant is in possession of the invention and name the person claiming to be the true and first inventor.\n\n**Application**:  \nWhen an assignee applies for a patent on behalf of an inventor, they are required to submit proof of assignment with the application or within a specified period after filing. This requirement ensures that the legal transfer of rights is properly documented, preventing unauthorized applications and ensuring transparency in the process.\n\nFor example, if a company acquires an invention from an individual through an assignment agreement, it must provide evidence of this transfer when applying for a patent. Failure to do so may result in the application being rejected or delayed.\n\nAdditionally, **Section 6(3)** requires that all applications state who is believed to be the true and first inventor. This declaration helps avoid disputes over inventorship and ensures that the correct person(s) are credited with the invention.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nApplications made by virtue of an assignment require proof of such assignment either with the application or within a specified period after filing, as per **Section 6(2)**. Furthermore, all applications must include a declaration regarding the true and first inventor under **Section 6(3)**. These requirements ensure transparency, prevent unauthorized applications, and protect inventors’ rights.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often neglect to mention that proof of assignment can be submitted after filing within a specified period. It is important to note this flexibility in the law while also emphasizing the need for proper documentation to avoid rejections or delays in the application process.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Who May Apply and Assignment of the Right to Apply\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 6 | **SHOE** | SHOE stamps patent first, claiming inventor's right. |\n| Sec. 7 | **KEY** | KEYs turn together, granting joint patent rights. |\n| Sec. 2(1)(y) | **HEN** + clause (1)(y) | HEN scratches ground, revealing hidden documents |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **2** - definitions\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. True and first inventor, assignee, legal representative - which provision?  (picture: SHOE)\n2. Joint applicants - which provision?  (picture: KEY)\n3. Proof of right under Section 7(2) - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 6(1) | Lists who can apply: inventor, assignee, legal rep of deceased | Inventor, assignee, and heir\n- Section 6(2) | Allows joint applications; proof needed for assignments | Two people sharing a patent\n- Section 6(3) | Requires declaration about true first inventor | Who really made the idea?\n- Section 6(4) | Needs provisional or complete spec with application | Paperwork before filing\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Forgetting to include proof of assignment in applications\n- Examiners expect: Clear understanding of inventor, assignee, and legal representative roles\n- Structure tip: Always start with section numbers when explaining eligibility criteria","storyEN":"In a quiet neighborhood of Hyderabad, nestled between two bustling streets, lived three friends: Ravi, a young inventor with dreams of launching his latest creation — a solar-powered water purifier; Meera, a legal assistant who had recently passed her patent agent exams and was eager to apply what she learned in real life; and Arjun, an entrepreneur who had just acquired the rights to a groundbreaking biodegradable packaging technology.\n\nOne day, Ravi approached Meera with his idea. He wanted to file for a patent on his invention but wasn’t sure about the process. “I remember from my studies that **Section 6(1)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* outlines who can apply,” he said.\n\nMeera nodded and explained, “Yes, Ravi. According to **Section 6(1)**, you — as the true and first inventor — are eligible to apply for a patent. But if someone else had already assigned you their rights, or if they passed away and you’re their legal representative, that would also qualify.”\n\nArjun, who was listening in, chimed in, “That’s right. I remember my company bought an invention from another inventor, and we had to file under the assignment of rights. **Section 6(2)** says that if you apply by virtue of an assignment, you have to provide proof of it either with your application or within a certain period after filing.”\n\nMeera continued, “Exactly. And **Section 6(3)** requires every application to name the true and first inventor. If I’m not the one who invented it, I still need to declare that I believe the person named is the real inventor.” She turned back to Ravi. “So you’ll have to state clearly in your application that you are the true and first inventor of your solar water purifier.”\n\nRavi nodded, but then asked a question: “What if I want someone else to help me with the process? Like Arjun?” Meera smiled. “Then we can file jointly under **Section 6(2)** — you and Arjun as co-applicants.”\n\nArjun was delighted. “That’s perfect! But what about the documentation?” Meera said, “Well, if you’re filing on behalf of an assignee or someone who passed away, you’ll need to provide proof of that assignment. But for now, since it's your invention and you're applying directly, we don’t have to worry about that.”\n\nRavi asked one more question: “What about the specification? I heard something about provisional and complete specifications.” Meera explained, “Right! **Section 6(4)** says that all applications — except for convention or PCT applications — must be accompanied by either a provisional or a complete specification. If you submit a provisional one now, we’ll have to file a complete specification within twelve months.”\n\nArjun, who had just filed an application using the assignment of rights from his previous acquisition, added, “I remember that when I applied for my biodegradable packaging patent, I had to include proof of the assignment. And I also had to declare the true and first inventor in my application. That was a bit tricky because the original inventor had passed away.”\n\nMeera nodded. “That’s where **Section 6(1)(c)** comes into play — allowing legal representatives of deceased inventors to apply on their behalf.”\n\nRavi, now confident, said, “So I just need to be clear in my application that I’m the true and first inventor of this water purifier, and if I file a provisional specification, we’ll have twelve months to complete it. That’s all?”\n\nMeera smiled. “That’s the basics. But remember — always double-check the requirements under **Section 6** and be precise with your declarations.”\n\nAs they walked out of Meera’s office that day, Ravi felt more prepared than ever to take his invention into the world.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| **Section 6(1)** | Who may apply for a patent: true inventor, assignee of the inventor, or legal representative of a deceased inventor. | Only eligible individuals — the true and first inventor, their assignee, or their legal representative if they are deceased — can file a patent application. |\n| **Section 6(2)** | Joint applications allowed; proof of assignment required if applicable. | Applicants may apply alone or jointly with others, and if applying by virtue of an assignment, proof must be provided. |\n| **Section 6(3)** | Declaration regarding the true and first inventor is required in every application. | Every application must name the person believed to be the true and first inventor and declare that belief if they are not the applicant. |\n| **Section 6(4)** | Applications (except convention or PCT) must be accompanied by a provisional or complete specification. | All applications, except those under specific treaties, must include either a provisional or complete specification. |\n| **Section 6(1)(c)** | Legal representatives of deceased inventors may apply on their behalf. | Deceased inventors’ legal representatives can file patent applications in the name of the deceased inventor. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"Who May Apply and Assignment of the Right to Apply\" (Patent Agent Examination)**\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n**\"PAPA\" – Persons Entitled, Assignees, Legal Representatives, Joint Applicants**\n\n- **P**: Person claiming to be the true and first inventor  \n- **A**: Assignee of the inventor  \n- **P**: Legal representative of a deceased person  \n- **A**: Any applicant (alone or jointly)\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 6(1)** – *Think of \"6\" as a \"key\" opening the door to who may apply for a patent.*  \n- **Section 6(2)** – *Imagine \"2\" like two people standing side by side, representing joint applicants.*  \n- **Section 6(3)** – *Picture \"3\" as proof (like a certificate) that the applicant is in possession of the invention and names the true inventor.*\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Assignee vs. Inventor** – *An assignee is not necessarily the inventor; they are someone who has been given the right to apply by the inventor.*  \n- **Legal Representative vs. Assignee** – *A legal representative acts on behalf of a deceased person, while an assignee receives rights from the inventor.*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques help in recalling the key points about who may apply for a patent and how assignment of rights works under the Patents Act, 1970.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"An assignee is the person to whom an inventor assigns their invention, as defined in Section 2(1)(ab). This includes assignees of assignees and legal representatives.","question":"Who is an assignee under the Patents Act?"},{"answer":"Proof of right must be furnished within 6 months from the date of filing, as per Section 7(2) and Rule 10.","question":"What is the time limit for furnishing proof of right under Section 7(2)?"},{"answer":"A legal representative must submit a death certificate of the deceased inventor or assignee, either at filing or within 6 months.","question":"What documents must a legal representative submit?"},{"answer":"Form 5 is required to declare inventorship if the applicant is not the inventor, as per Section 10(6) and Rule 13(6).","question":"When is Form 5 required?"},{"answer":"Yes, joint applicants can file if all inventors agree, as declared in Form 1 and supported by proof of assignment or legal representation.","question":"Can joint applicants file a patent application?"},{"answer":"Form 5 is required to formally declare inventorship and avoid office actions, as mandated by Section 10(6) and Rule 13(6).","question":"Why is Form 5 necessary even if the inventor is the applicant?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 6 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Leaving and serving documents”.","options":["Rule 19D","Rule 66","Rule 6","Rule 90"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Leaving and serving documents?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 6 is headed “Leaving and serving documents”.","options":["International Preliminary Examination Report","Renewal fees under section 53","Form of making a claim under section 28(3)","Leaving and serving documents"],"question":"Rule 6 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Section 20","Section 2","Section 71","Section 46"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Definitions and interpretation?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 2 is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Service of notices, etc. by post","Register of patent agents","Register to be open for inspection","Definitions and interpretation"],"question":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 6 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Persons entitled to apply for patents”.","options":["Section 34","Section 11B","Section 6","Section 43"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Persons entitled to apply for patents?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 6 is headed “Persons entitled to apply for patents”.","options":["Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or","Persons entitled to apply for patents","Residents not to apply for patents outside India without prior","Power of Controller in cases of anticipation"],"question":"Section 6 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 7 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Form of application”.","options":["Section 92A","Section 139","Section 86","Section 7"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Form of application?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 7 is headed “Form of application”.","options":["Disposal by Controller of applications for extension of time","Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain","Form of application","Exercise of discretionary powers by Controller"],"question":"Section 7 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"}]},"s1p_10":{"notes":"# Study Notes: Provisional and Complete Specification — Strategy and Timelines  \n*(For Patent Agent Examination - Papers I, II and Viva)*  \n\n---\n\n## **1. Definition of Provisional and Complete Specifications**\n\n### **Provisional Specification**\n- A provisional specification is a preliminary document that outlines the invention in a general manner.\n- It does not include detailed claims or complete technical descriptions but provides enough information to establish an early filing date.\n\n**Relevant Section:**  \nSection 9(1) of *The Patents Act, 1970* states: *\"Every application for a patent shall be accompanied by a provisional specification or a complete specification.\"*\n\n### **Complete Specification**\n- A complete specification is a detailed technical document that fully describes the invention.\n- It includes claims, drawings (if necessary), and all other information required to enable a person skilled in the art to understand and reproduce the invention.\n\n**Relevant Section:**  \nSection 9(2) of *The Patents Act, 1970* defines: *\"A complete specification shall contain a full and clear description of the invention, including the manner and process of making and using it.\"*\n\n---\n\n## **2. Key Concepts**\n\n### **a. Purpose of Provisional Specification**\n- Provides an early priority date for the invention.\n- Allows applicants to file before all details are finalized.\n- Gives time to conduct market research or seek funding.\n\n**Textbook Explanation (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 171):**  \n*\"A provisional specification is useful for establishing an early priority date so that if another application is filed later with a similar invention, the patent will be granted to the one who first filed the application.\"*\n\n### **b. Purpose of Complete Specification**\n- Enables proper examination by the Controller.\n- Contains all necessary technical details and claims.\n- Is required before a patent can be granted.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 9 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 9. Provisional and complete specifications.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent (not being a convention\n> application or an application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n>\n>  14\n>\n> Treaty designating India) is accompanied by a provisional\n> specification, a complete specification shall be filed within twelve\n> months from the date of filing of the application, and if the\n> complete specification is not so filed, the application shall be\n> deemed to be abandoned.\n>\n> (2) Where two or more applications in the name of the same applicant\n> are accompanied by provisional specifications in respect of\n> inventions which are cognate or of which one is a modification of\n> another and the Controller is of opinion that the whole of such\n> inventions are such as to constitute a single invention and may\n> properly be included in one patent, he may allow one complete\n> specification to be filed in respect of all such provisional\n> specifications:\n> Provided that the period of time specified under sub-section (1)\n> shall be reckoned from the date of filing of the earliest\n> provisional specification.\n>\n> (3) Where an application for a patent (not being a convention\n> application or an application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty designating India) is accompanied by a specification\n> purporting to be a complete specification, the Controller may, if\n> the applicant so requests at any time within twelve months from the\n> date of filing of the application, direct that such specification\n> shall be treated, for the purposes of this Act, as a provisional\n> specification and proceed with the application accordingly.\n>\n> (4) Where a complete specification has been filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent accompanied by a provisional specification\n> or by a specification treated by virtue of a direction under sub-\n> section (3) as a provisional specification, the Controller may, if\n> the applicant so requests at any time before grant of patent, cancel\n> the provisional specification and post-date the application to the\n> date of filing of the complete specification.\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 11 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11. Priority dates of claims of a complete specification.\n> (1) There shall be a priority date for each claim of a complete\n> specification.\n>\n> (2) Where a complete specification is filed in pursuance of a single\n> application accompanied by\n> (a) a provisional specification; or\n> (b) a specification which is treated by virtue of a direction under\n> sub-section (3) of section 9 as a provisional specification,\n> and the claim is fairly based on the matter disclosed in the\n> specification referred to in clause (a) or clause (b), the priority\n>\n>  17\n>\n> date of that claim shall be the date of the filing of the relevant\n> specification.\n>\n> (3) Where the complete specification is filed or proceeded with in\n> pursuance of two or more applications accompanied by such\n> specifications as are mentioned in sub-section (2) and the claim is\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed\n> (a) in one of those specifications, the priority date of that claim\n> shall be the date of the filing of the application accompanied by\n> that specification;\n> (b) partly in one and partly in another, the priority date of that\n> claim shall be the date of the filing of the application accompanied\n> by the specification of the later date.\n>\n> (3A) Where a complete specification based on a previously filed\n> application in India has been filed within twelve months from the\n> date of that application and the claim is fairly based on the matter\n> disclosed in the previously filed application, the priority date of\n> that claim shall be the date of the previously filed application in\n> which the matter was first disclosed.\n>\n> (4) Where the complete specification has been filed in pursuance of\n> a further application made by virtue of sub-section (1) of section\n> 16 and the claim is fairly based on the matter disclosed in any of\n> the earlier specifications, provisional or complete, as the case may\n> be, the priority date of that claim shall be the date of the filing\n> of that specification in which the matter was first disclosed.\n>\n> (5) Where, under the foregoing provisions of this section, any claim\n> of a complete specification would, but for the provisions of this\n> sub-section, have two or more priority dates, the priority date of\n> that claim shall be the earlier or earliest of those dates.\n>\n> (6) In any case to which sub-sections (2), (3), 1(3A), (4) and (5)\n> do not apply, the priority date of a claim shall, subject to the\n> provisions of section 137, be the date of filing of the complete\n> specification.\n>\n> (7) The reference to the date of the filing of the application or of\n> the complete specification in this section shall, in cases where\n> there has been a post-dating under section 9 or section 17 or, as\n>\n>  18\n>\n> the case may be, ante-dating under section 16, be a reference to the\n> date as so post-dated or ante-dated.\n>\n> (8) A claim in a complete specification of a patent shall not be\n> invalid by reason only of\n> (a) the publication or use of the invention so far as claimed in\n> that claim on or after the priority date of such claim; or\n> (b) the grant of another patent which claims the invention, so far\n> as claimed in the first mentioned claim, in a claim of the same or a\n> later priority date.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER IV PUBLICATION AND EXAMINATION OF APPLICATIONS\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n**Rule 16 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 16. Models\n> (1) Models or samples shall be furnished under section 10 only when\n> required by the Controller.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER III INTERNATIONAL APPLICATIONS UNDER PATENT COOPERATION\n> TREATY (PCT)\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the difference between a provisional specification and a complete specification under The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** The distinction in legal requirements, purpose, and content between provisional and complete specifications under the Patents Act.\n\n**Rule:** \n- A **provisional specification** is a preliminary document that outlines an invention in general terms without detailed claims or full technical descriptions (Section 9(1) of *The Patents Act, 1970*). It establishes an early filing date and allows time to finalize the invention.  \n- A **complete specification**, on the other hand, is a fully detailed document that includes complete technical descriptions, claims, drawings, and all information required for examination (Section 9(2) of *The Patents Act, 1970*). It must be filed within twelve months from the date of filing the provisional specification.  \n- Section 10(4)(a)-(c) requires that a complete specification fully describe the invention and its operation, disclose the best method of performing it, and end with claims defining the scope of protection.\n\n**Application:** \nThe distinction between these two types of specifications is crucial for applicants seeking patent protection in India. A provisional specification allows an inventor to file early without having finalized all technical details. This is particularly useful when further research or development is required before a complete application can be submitted. However, it does not provide the detailed information necessary for the Controller to assess the novelty and non-obviousness of the invention.\n\nIn contrast, a complete specification must include comprehensive descriptions, claims, and drawings (if applicable). It serves as the basis for the examination process by the patent office and is essential before any grant of a patent. Failure to file a complete specification within the prescribed period results in the application being deemed abandoned under Section 9(1).\n\nThe legal framework under Sections 9 and 10 ensures that provisional specifications are not sufficient on their own but serve as a temporary placeholder, while complete specifications meet all requirements for evaluation and grant.\n\n**Conclusion:** \nA provisional specification is a preliminary document establishing an early filing date, whereas a complete specification contains full technical details required for examination. The former allows time to develop the invention further, while the latter is essential for patent grant under *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse the purpose of provisional and complete specifications or fail to mention the twelve-month deadline for filing a complete specification after a provisional one. Emphasizing this timeline is critical in answering such questions accurately.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the legal implications if an applicant fails to file a complete specification within 12 months from the date of filing a provisional specification?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** The consequences under *The Patents Act, 1970* for not filing a complete specification within the stipulated period after filing a provisional one.\n\n**Rule:** \n- Section 9(1) of *The Patents Act, 1970* mandates that if an application is accompanied by a provisional specification, a complete specification must be filed within twelve months from the date of filing.  \n- If this requirement is not met, the application shall be deemed **abandoned**.\n\n**Application:** \nIf an applicant fails to file a complete specification within 12 months of submitting a provisional one, the entire patent application becomes invalid and cannot proceed further. This rule ensures that applicants do not delay indefinitely in providing detailed information necessary for examination by the Controller. The abandonment provision acts as a deterrent against procrastination or lack of intent to pursue the patent.\n\nThe applicant must file the complete specification within this period; otherwise, they lose all rights associated with the application. There is no extension provided under Section 9(1), and the law does not allow for post-filing amendments in such cases unless the Controller exercises discretion under other provisions (e.g., Section 17).\n\n**Conclusion:** \nFailure to file a complete specification within twelve months of filing a provisional one results in the application being deemed abandoned, as per *The Patents Act, 1970*. This is a strict legal requirement with no exceptions unless provided by specific rules.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the fact that the failure to meet this deadline leads directly to abandonment and not just delay or suspension. It is crucial to highlight the immediate and irreversible consequence of non-compliance.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the importance of a complete specification in enabling proper examination under *The Patents Act, 1970*.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2023)*\n\n**Issue:** The role of a complete specification in facilitating the patent examination process by the Controller.\n\n**Rule:** \n- A **complete specification** is required for proper examination under *The Patents Act, 1970*. It must fully describe the invention and its operation or use (Section 10(4)(a)).  \n- The specification must also disclose the best method of performing the invention known to the applicant (Section 10(4)(b)) and end with claims defining the scope of protection (Section 10(4)(c)).  \n- These requirements ensure that a person skilled in the art can understand and reproduce the invention, as emphasized by Dr. Sheetal Chopra on page 171.\n\n**Application:** \nThe Controller relies heavily on the complete specification during examination to determine whether an invention is novel, non-obvious, and useful. Without detailed technical descriptions and claims, it would be impossible for the Controller to assess these criteria properly. For instance, if a specification lacks sufficient detail or omits key aspects of the invention, the Controller may reject the application on grounds of insufficiency.\n\nAdditionally, Section 10(5) mandates that the claim(s) in a complete specification must relate to a single invention or group of inventions forming one inventive concept. This ensures clarity and prevents overly broad claims that could hinder future innovation.\n\n**Conclusion:** \nA complete specification is essential for enabling proper examination by the Controller under *The Patents Act, 1970*. It provides all necessary technical details and legal claims required to assess the patentability of an invention.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to connect the requirement of a complete specification with its role in enabling proper examination. Emphasizing how the content of the specification directly affects the Controller’s ability to evaluate novelty, non-obviousness, and utility is key to scoring well on this question.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Provisional and Complete Specification — Strategy and Timelines\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 9 | **BEE** | BEE buzzes rapidly, guarding a twelve-month hive. |\n| Sec. 10 | **TOES** | TOES point backward then forward, stepping on old and new dates. |\n| Sec. 11 | **TOTE** | TOTE bag carries multiple provisionals, but only one complete emerges. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Filing a provisional and the twelve-month window for the complete - which provision?  (picture: BEE)\n2. Antedating and post-dating - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n3. Cognate applications and a single complete for two or more provisionals - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 9(1) | Provisional spec allows 12-month window for complete spec | Filing clock ticking\n- Section 9(2) | Complete spec must describe invention fully and include claims | Detailed blueprint needed\n- Section 10(4) | Complete spec must have claims, best method, and abstract | Full recipe with instructions\n- Section 11(2) | Priority date for claim is filing date of provisional spec | Early filing sets the pace\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Forgetting to file complete spec within 12 months\n- Examiners expect: Clear distinction between provisional and complete specs\n- Structure tip: Always mention the 12-month deadline when discussing provisional specs","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, in a small but bustling neighborhood near Charminar, lived a young patent agent named Arjun. He was preparing for his upcoming Patent Agent Examination and had just received a mysterious package from an old mentor — a bundle of twenty-one lamps labeled with numbers from 9 to 10, along with some strange clay tablets inscribed in ancient script.\n\nEach lamp represented a section or rule from *The Patents Act, 1970*, and Arjun was told that if he could light them all in the right order, it would unlock the secrets of provisional and complete specifications. The first lamp, marked **Section 9(1)**, glowed bright when he placed a piece of parchment labeled “Provisional Specification” under it. It whispered: *“Every application for a patent shall be accompanied by a provisional specification or a complete specification.”* Arjun noted that this was the starting point — no invention could proceed without this initial filing.\n\nHe moved on to **Section 9(2)**, which spoke of the complete specification. It required detailed descriptions and claims, and if not filed within twelve months, the application would be deemed abandoned. He imagined a clock ticking down in his mind, reminding him that time was a critical factor here — a moment lost could mean losing everything.\n\nNext came **Section 10(4)**, which demanded that every complete specification end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention. Arjun had to write these claims carefully, like crafting a spell in an ancient language — one misstep and the whole thing would fall apart.\n\nHe then encountered **Section 11**, which dealt with priority dates for each claim. Here, he found himself in a courtroom scene where two inventors, Rajesh and Suresh, had both filed similar applications. The judge (a figure who looked like a version of the Controller) ruled that the claim with the earlier filing date would have priority — this was **Section 11(2)**.\n\nArjun also came across an old tablet that referenced **Section 9(3)**, which allowed for converting a complete specification into a provisional one if needed. He saw a character named Mohan who had initially filed a complete specification but later changed his mind and converted it back to provisional — a rare move, but permitted by the law.\n\nAs he lit each lamp, Arjun felt more confident about the timelines and strategies involved in filing patents. He realized that the **twelve-month period** was crucial for moving from provisional to complete specifications. If not filed on time, everything would be lost. But if done right, it could secure a patent — a shield of protection over an invention.\n\nFinally, he reached the last lamp, which had no number but carried the weight of all the others. It showed him a scene where a group of inventors were preparing their applications, each with different strategies and timelines. Some used provisional specifications to get early priority dates while others rushed to file complete specifications before time ran out.\n\nWith all twenty-one lamps lit, Arjun stood back, looking at his journey from confusion to clarity. He had mastered the rules of **Sections 9**, **10**, and **11** — the foundation of patent filings in India. The bundle of lamps had not only taught him about strategy and timelines but also reminded him that every number and rule was a part of a larger, intricate system.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| 21 lamps     | Section numbers 9 to 10 and related provisions | Every application must be accompanied by either a provisional or complete specification. |\n| Twelve-month period | Section 9(1) | A complete specification must be filed within twelve months of the provisional filing, otherwise the application is abandoned. |\n| Priority date | Section 11(2) | The priority date for a claim in a complete specification is the date of the earliest related application or specification. |\n| Complete specification with claims | Section 10(4) | A complete specification must end with one or more claims defining the scope of the invention. |\n| Conversion from complete to provisional | Section 9(3) | A complete specification can be treated as a provisional one if requested within twelve months of filing. |\n| Two inventors, Rajesh and Suresh | Section 11(2) | If two applications are filed by the same applicant for related inventions, the Controller may allow one complete specification to cover them all, with priority based on the earliest application. |\n| Twelve-month period again | Section 9(1) | The twelve-month rule is critical in patent filings — failure to meet it can result in abandonment of the application. |\n\nThis story and its anchor key are designed for memorization through vivid imagery and number anchors, ensuring a student can recall each section and its implications with ease.","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **\"Provisional and Complete Specification — Strategy and Timelines\"**  \n*(Based on sections from The Patents Act, 1970 and textbook excerpts)*  \n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Elements of a Provisional Specification**\n- **P**reliminary description  \n- **R**ough idea of the invention  \n- **O**verview of claims (not detailed)  \n- **V**ision of the application’s future  \n- **I**ntent to file complete specification later  \n\n**Mnemonic:** **PROVI** — *Provisional Specification Overview and Vision for Implementation*\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-anchor for Section 9: \"Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications\"**\n- **Section 9**: Think of the number **9** as a **door** to international patenting. Just like you must present your passport at a border (foreign application), you must provide information about any foreign filings before proceeding in India.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Number-anchor for Section 10: \"Contents of specifications\"**\n- **Section 10**: Picture the number **10** as a **complete package** — like a suitcase full of all your belongings (the complete specification must include everything: title, abstract, drawings, claims, etc.).\n\n---\n\n#### 4. **Number-anchor for Section 11: \"Priority dates of claims of a complete specification\"**\n- **Section 11**: Think of the number **11** as **two clocks** — one showing the date of the provisional application and another the complete specification. The priority is based on which clock was set first.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. **Confusion Pair: Provisional vs. Complete Specification**\n\n- **Provisional Specification**: A **rough draft**, used to establish a filing date, but lacks detailed claims or drawings.  \n- **Complete Specification**: A **fully developed** application with all details (claims, drawings, abstract), required for grant of the patent.\n\n**One-line way to tell them apart:** *“Provisional is just the start — complete is the finish line.”*\n\n---\n\n#### 6. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Timelines in Filing a Complete Specification**\n- **F**ile within **12 months**  \n- **R**efer back to provisional  \n- **O**verview of all claims  \n- **V**erify compliance with rules  \n- **I**nclude necessary documents  \n\n**Mnemonic:** **FROMVI** — *File, Refer, Overview, Verify, Include*\n\n---\n\n#### 7. **Number-anchor for Section 11A: \"Publication of applications\"**\n- **Section 11A**: Picture the number **11A** as a **mirror** that reflects your application to the public after a certain period — like how you see yourself in a mirror, others will see your patent application.\n\n---\n\n#### 8. **Confusion Pair: Priority date vs. Filing date**\n\n- **Priority Date**: The **earliest date** (usually from a provisional application) used to determine who was first to invent.  \n- **Filing Date**: The **actual date** the complete specification is filed with the Controller.\n\n**One-line way to tell them apart:** *“Priority date is your birthdate; filing date is when you officially register.”*\n\n---\n\n#### 9. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Handling Claims in Complete Specification**\n- **C**lear and concise  \n- **L**egal compliance  \n- **A**ccurate claims  \n- **I**nclusive of all inventions  \n- **M**ust be supported by provisional  \n\n**Mnemonic:** **CLAIM** — *Clear, Legal, Accurate, Inclusive, Must-be-supported*\n\n---\n\n#### 10. **Number-anchor for Section 13: \"Search for anticipation\"**\n- **Section 13**: Think of the number **13** as a **searchlight** that scans prior art and existing patents to find if your invention was already known — like shining a light in the dark to see what’s already there.\n\n--- \n\nThese memory techniques are tailored to help you recall key sections, concepts, and timelines related to provisional and complete specifications for the Patent Agent Examination.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The priority date is the filing date of the provisional specification, as per Section 11(2). This ensures claims are assessed relative to prior art from that date.","question":"What determines the priority date of a claim based on a provisional specification?"},{"answer":"Twelve months from the provisional's filing date, as stated in Section 11(8A). Failure to file within this period may result in abandonment.","question":"What is the time limit for filing a complete specification after a provisional?"},{"answer":"Post-dating is allowed to the complete specification's filing date, extending the patent term by 12 months, as per Section 9(4).","question":"When can an application be post-dated under Section 9(4)?"},{"answer":"A single complete specification can cover multiple provisionals, with priority dates based on the earliest application's filing date, per Section 11(3).","question":"How does Section 11(3) handle multiple provisionals with a single complete?"},{"answer":"A provisional allows 12 months to refine the invention and file a complete specification, avoiding premature disclosure, as seen in Antony's case.","question":"Why might a provisional specification be the right choice for an invention?"},{"answer":"Antedating adjusts the filing date for priority purposes, but the actual filing date remains, as clarified in Section 11(7).","question":"How does antedating under Section 16 affect priority dates?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 11 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Priority dates of claims of a complete specification”.","options":["Section 151","Section 156","Section 17","Section 11"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Priority dates of claims of a complete specification?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 11 is headed “Priority dates of claims of a complete specification”.","options":["Priority dates of claims of a complete specification","Falsification of entries in register, etc","Multiple priorities","Evidence of entries, documents, etc"],"question":"Section 11 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 11 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Order of recording applications”.","options":["Rule 70A","Rule 11","Rule 84","Rule 101"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Order of recording applications?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 11 is headed “Order of recording applications”.","options":["Order of recording applications","Processing of demands for international preliminary","Scale of costs","Restoration of patents"],"question":"Rule 11 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 9 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Filing of documents and copies, etc”.","options":["Rule 19H","Rule 80","Rule 9","Rule 24A"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Filing of documents and copies, etc?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 9 is headed “Filing of documents and copies, etc”.","options":["Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming","Removal of a name from the register of patent agents","Supply of certified copies and certificates under sections","Filing of documents and copies, etc"],"question":"Rule 9 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 10","Section 154","Section 87","Section 157A"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Rules to be placed before Parliament","Multiple priorities","Qualifications for registration as patent agents","Contents of specifications"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 10","Rule 129A","Rule 19D","Rule 19E"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["When a prima facie case is not made out","Power to relax","Request under section 52(2)","Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 9 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Provisional and complete specifications”.","options":["Section 34","Section 48","Section 136","Section 9"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Provisional and complete specifications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 9 is headed “Provisional and complete specifications”.","options":["Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc","Provisional and complete specifications","Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of time","Right of licensee under section 84 to take proceedings against"],"question":"Section 9 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 9(1) explicitly states that a complete specification must be filed within twelve months from the application's filing date, or the application is deemed abandoned.","options":["a) Six months","b) Twelve months","c) Eighteen months","d) Twenty-four months"],"question":"What is the time limit for filing a complete specification after a provisional specification under Section 9(1)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 9(2) specifies that the period is reckoned from the date of the earliest provisional specification when consolidating multiple applications.","options":["a) From the date of the latest provisional specification","b) From the date of the earliest provisional specification","c) From the date of the first application","d) From the date of the Controller's notice"],"question":"Under Section 9(2), when multiple provisional specifications are filed by the same applicant, how is the time period for filing a complete specification calculated?"}]},"s1p_11":{"notes":"# Contents of the Complete Specification — Section 10 in Detail  \n*(Based on the Patents Act, 1970 and Master Guide to Patent Agent Examination)*  \n\n---\n\n## **Definition**  \nA *complete specification* is a detailed written description of an invention that must be filed with the patent office as part of the application process. It includes all necessary information required to understand, reproduce, and use the invention. The complete specification replaces the provisional specification once it is filed.\n\n- **Section 9(1)**: Requires that a complete specification be filed within twelve months from the date of filing the application (if accompanied by a provisional specification).  \n- **Section 10** outlines the *contents* of both provisional and complete specifications.  \n\n---\n\n## **Key Concepts Under Section 10**\n\n### **1. Title of the Invention**\nEvery specification must begin with a title that clearly indicates the subject-matter of the invention. This helps in identifying the nature of the invention at a glance.\n\n- **Section 10(1)**: States that every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Drawings**\nDrawings are an essential part of the specification if they help in understanding the invention better. The Controller may require drawings to be included for any type of application—provisional or complete.\n\n- **Section 10(2)**: Provides that drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be supplied for the purposes of any specification; these drawings form part of the specification unless otherwise directed by the Controller.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Models or Samples (if required)**\nIn certain cases, the Controller may require a model or sample to illustrate the invention. However, such models are not considered part of the specification itself.\n\n- **Section 10(3)**: States that if the Controller deems it necessary, an applicant must provide a model or sample before the application is found in order for grant of a patent. These models do *not* form part of the specification.\n\n---\n\n### **4. Requirements for Complete Specifications**\nA complete specification has specific and detailed requirements:\n\n- **Section 10(4)**: Mandates that every complete specification must:\n  - (a) Fully and particularly describe the invention, its operation or use, and the method by which it is to be performed.\n  - (b) Disclose the best method of performing the invention known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim protection.\n  - (c) End with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention for which protection is claimed.\n  - (d) Be accompanied by an abstract providing technical information on the invention.\n\n#### **Additional Conditions in Case of Biological Materials**\nIf the specification includes biological materials that are not publicly available, specific conditions must be fulfilled:\n\n- The material must be deposited with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty.\n- A reference to this deposit must appear in the specification within a prescribed period.\n- All necessary details such as the name and address of the depository institution and the date and number of the deposit must be included.\n\n---\n\n### **5. International Applications Designating India**\nFor international applications that designate India, the complete specification is taken as:\n\n- **Section 10(4A)**: The title, description, drawings, abstract, and claims filed with the international application are considered the complete specification for the purposes of this Act.\n\n---\n\n### **6. Claims in Complete Specifications**\nClaims must be clear, succinct, and fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n\n- **Section 10(5)**: States that the claim or claims shall relate to a single invention or a group of inventions linked as a single inventive concept. They should not be vague or overly broad.\n\n---\n\n### **7. Declaration of Inventorship**\nIn certain cases, the applicant must provide a declaration regarding the inventorship of the invention.\n\n- **Section 10(6)**: Requires that such a declaration be furnished in the prescribed form with the complete specification or within a specified period after filing it.\n\n---\n\n### **8. Additional Claims Based on Developments**\nA complete specification filed after a provisional one may include claims related to developments or additions of the invention described in the provisional specification, provided these are eligible for separate patent applications under Section 6.\n\n- **Section 10(7)**: Allows such additional claims if they meet the criteria set forth in Section 6.\n\n---\n\n## **Why Does the Law Require These Details?**  \nThe Patents Act mandates these requirements to ensure that:\n\n- The invention is clearly and fully described so that others can understand, reproduce, and use it.\n- Patent rights are granted based on a clear understanding of what exactly is being protected.\n- There is no ambiguity in the scope of protection claimed by the applicant.\n\nAccording to **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 383**, such detailed specifications help prevent disputes over patentability and reduce the chances of invalidation due to lack of clarity or insufficient disclosure.\n\n---\n\n## **Importance in Patent Agent Examination**  \nIn Paper II of the Patent Agent Examination, candidates are often asked to draft complete specifications based on given invention disclosures. Understanding the requirements under Section 10 is crucial for:\n\n- Writing clear and well-structured claims.\n- Including all necessary technical details and drawings.\n- Ensuring compliance with procedural requirements such as depositing biological materials.\n\nAs per **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 529**, candidates are expected to be familiar with the exact wording of Section 10 and its implications for patent drafting.\n\n---\n\n## **Conclusion**  \nSection 10 of the Patents Act is a cornerstone in the process of filing a complete specification. It ensures that all necessary information about an invention is provided clearly, enabling proper examination by the Controller and reducing ambiguity in patent claims. For Patent Agent candidates, mastering this section is essential for success in both written exams and practical drafting tasks.\n\n*(No verified case law is available for this topic — verify with a case-law database if needed.)*\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n**Rule 16 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 16. Models\n> (1) Models or samples shall be furnished under section 10 only when\n> required by the Controller.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER III INTERNATIONAL APPLICATIONS UNDER PATENT COOPERATION\n> TREATY (PCT)\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the requirements of a complete specification under Section 10 of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue** — What are the essential components and requirements that must be included in a complete specification under Section 10 of the Patents Act?\n\n**Rule** — Under **Section 10(4)** of the Patents Act, every complete specification must:  \n(a) Fully describe the invention, its operation or use, and the method by which it is to be performed;  \n(b) Disclose the best method known to the applicant for performing the invention;  \n(c) End with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention;  \n(d) Be accompanied by an abstract providing technical information on the invention.  \n\nAdditionally, **Section 10(4)(d)** requires that if biological materials are involved and not publicly available, they must be deposited with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty, and a reference to this deposit must appear in the specification within a prescribed period.\n\n**Application** — A complete specification is more detailed than a provisional one. For example, if an inventor files a patent application for a new pharmaceutical drug that involves a novel biological material not publicly available, they must:  \n1. Fully describe how the drug works and its method of performance.  \n2. Disclose the best method known to them for producing or using the drug.  \n3. End with claims defining the scope of protection sought.  \n4. Include an abstract summarizing the invention’s technical aspects.  \n5. Deposit the biological material with a depository institution under the Budapest Treaty and include all necessary details in the specification, such as the name of the institution, deposit date, number, and access conditions.\n\n**Conclusion** — A complete specification must be comprehensive, disclosing the invention's full operation, best method, claims, and abstract. In cases involving biological materials, compliance with the Budapest Treaty is mandatory to ensure proper disclosure and avoid rejection of the application.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the requirement for an abstract in a complete specification and fail to address the specific conditions under Section 10(4)(d) regarding biological material deposits.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the differences between a provisional and a complete specification?**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue** — How do the requirements and content of a provisional specification differ from those of a complete specification?\n\n**Rule** — **Section 10(1)** states that both provisional and complete specifications must begin with a title indicating the invention's subject-matter. However, **Section 9(1)** mandates that a complete specification be filed within twelve months of the provisional application.\n\nA provisional specification is less detailed and does not require claims or an abstract. In contrast, a complete specification under **Section 10(4)** must fully describe the invention, disclose the best method known to the applicant, end with claims defining the scope of protection, and include an abstract.\n\n**Application** — If an inventor files a provisional application for a new software algorithm, they may provide only a general description without detailed technical information or claims. However, when filing the complete specification within twelve months, they must:  \n1. Fully describe how the algorithm functions and is implemented.  \n2. Disclose the best method of using it.  \n3. Include specific claims defining the scope of protection sought.  \n4. Provide an abstract summarizing the invention.\n\n**Conclusion** — A provisional specification serves as a placeholder to secure the filing date, while a complete specification is required for patent grant and must meet all detailed requirements under Section 10(4).\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse the purpose of provisional specifications with that of complete ones and fail to highlight the mandatory inclusion of claims and abstracts in a complete specification.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Explain the role and importance of an abstract in a complete specification.**  \n*8 marks · Osmania University · asked 1× (2023)*\n\n**Issue** — What is the function of an abstract in a complete specification, and why is it important?\n\n**Rule** — **Section 10(4)(d)** mandates that every complete specification be accompanied by an abstract providing technical information on the invention. The Controller may also amend the abstract to provide better information to third parties.\n\n**Application** — An abstract serves as a concise summary of the invention's key features and technical aspects, enabling patent examiners and third parties to quickly understand the nature and scope of the invention without reading the entire specification. For example, if an inventor applies for a new type of solar panel, the abstract would summarize its innovative design, efficiency improvements, and applications.\n\n**Conclusion** — The abstract is crucial as it provides clarity and facilitates quick comprehension of the invention by third parties and examiners. It also allows the Controller to make amendments for better disclosure.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to emphasize that the abstract may be amended by the Controller to enhance its usefulness to third parties, which is a key point in this section.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the requirements under Section 10(4A) of the Patents Act regarding international applications designating India?**  \n*8 marks · Osmania University · not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue** — How does Section 10(4A) treat international applications that designate India?\n\n**Rule** — **Section 10(4A)** states that for an international application designating India, the title, description, drawings, abstract, and claims filed with the international application are considered the complete specification under the Patents Act.\n\n**Application** — When a foreign applicant files an international patent application through the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT) and includes India as a designated state, the documents submitted—such as the title, description, drawings, abstract, and claims—are treated as the complete specification for the purposes of the Indian Patents Act. This eliminates the need to resubmit these elements in a separate national phase application.\n\n**Conclusion** — Section 10(4A) streamlines the patent process by recognizing international applications' contents as sufficient for the complete specification, reducing procedural burdens on applicants.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to connect this provision with the PCT system and its implications for international patent filings in India.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Contents of the Complete Specification — Section 10 in Detail\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 10(4) | **TOES** + clause (4) | TOES trace invention steps on a chalkboard |\n| Sec. 10(4)(d) | **TOES** + clause (4)(d) | TOES demonstrate best method on a dance floor |\n| Sec. 10(5) | **TOES** + clause (5) | TOES outline claim boundaries with chalk |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Full and particular description and method of performing the invention - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n2. Best method known to the applicant - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n3. Claims defining the scope of protection - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 10(1) | Every specification starts with a title indicating invention subject-matter | Title page of a book  \n- Section 10(2) | Drawings may be required and form part of the spec if Controller says so | Blueprints on paper  \n- Section 10(3) | Models/samples may be needed but don't form part of specification | Toy model not in box  \n- Section 10(4) | Complete spec must describe invention, best method, claims, and abstract | Full recipe with ingredients  \n- Section 10(4A) | International apps designating India use their own title, description, etc. as complete spec | Passport with all info filled  \n- Section 10(5) | Claims must be clear, succinct, based on disclosure | Clear map with no detours  \n- Section 10(6) | Declaration of inventorship required in prescribed form | Signature on a contract  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid vague claims – examiners expect clarity and precision  \n- Use exact wording from Section 10 when drafting specifications  \n- Always include an abstract with technical details for full marks","storyEN":"**Story: The Inventor's Journey in Hyderabad**\n\nIn a quiet corner of Hyderabad, there lived a young inventor named Ravi. He had spent years developing a revolutionary water filtration system that could purify even the most contaminated water using a unique combination of nano-filtration and solar energy. Excited about his invention, he decided to file for a patent under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\nHis first step was to submit a **provisional specification**, which he did within twelve months as required by **Section 9(1)**. He knew that this would give him time to refine his invention before filing the **complete specification**—a crucial document governed by **Section 10**.\n\nRavi sat at his desk, pen in hand, and began drafting the complete specification. The first thing he wrote was the **title of the invention**, as mandated by **Section 10(1)**. He titled it \"Solar-Powered Nano-Filtration Water Purifier,\" a clear and concise description that immediately conveyed what his invention was about.\n\nNext came the **drawings**. Ravi had created detailed diagrams showing how each component worked together, from the solar panels to the nano-filters. According to **Section 10(2)**, these drawings were not optional; they could be required by the Controller. He submitted them with his specification, knowing that if the Controller asked for them, he would have to comply.\n\nThen came a request from the Controller: Ravi was asked to provide a **model or sample** of his invention. This was unusual, but **Section 10(3)** made it clear that while such models were not part of the specification itself, they could be required in certain cases. He built a small-scale version of his purifier and sent it to the patent office.\n\nNow came the most important part: writing the complete specification itself. Under **Section 10(4)(a)**, Ravi had to fully describe how the invention worked. He explained every step of the filtration process and how the solar energy was harnessed. Then, under **Section 10(4)(b)**, he included the best method of performing the invention—this was crucial because it showed that he truly understood his own creation.\n\nAt the end of the specification came the **claims**. Ravi knew from **Section 10(5)** that these had to be clear and succinct. He wrote three claims: one for the overall system, another for the nano-filtration component, and a third for the solar energy harnessing method. Each was carefully worded so that they formed a single inventive concept.\n\nHe also included an **abstract** as required by **Section 10(4)(d)**. The abstract summarized his invention in technical terms, making it easy for others to understand what he had created and how it worked.\n\nBut Ravi’s invention involved a biological material—a specific type of algae that helped break down contaminants more efficiently. He knew from **Section 10(4)(ii)** that if this material wasn’t publicly available, he would have to deposit it with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty. He did so and included all the required details in his specification: the name and address of the institution, the date and number of the deposit, and the source and geographical origin of the algae.\n\nFinally, Ravi submitted a **declaration of inventorship** as per **Section 10(6)**. This was not just a formality; it confirmed that he was the true inventor of this groundbreaking technology.\n\nAfter months of back-and-forth with the patent office, his application was accepted. The Controller found no issues with the specification and granted him a patent. Ravi had followed every rule in **Section 10** to the letter, ensuring that his invention was not only protected but also clearly described for all future users.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twelve months | Section 9(1) | A complete specification must be filed within twelve months from the date of filing the application if accompanied by a provisional specification. |\n| Title of the invention | Section 10(1) | Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall describe the invention and begin with a title sufficiently indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates. |\n| Drawings | Section 10(2) | Drawings may be required by the Controller for any specification; these drawings form part of the specification unless otherwise directed by the Controller. |\n| Model or sample | Section 10(3) | If the Controller requires a model or sample, it must be furnished before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the specification. |\n| Full description and best method | Section 10(4)(a) and (b) | Every complete specification must fully describe the invention and disclose the best method known to the applicant. |\n| Claims | Section 10(5) | The claim or claims shall relate to a single invention or group of inventions linked as a single inventive concept, and shall be clear and succinct. |\n| Abstract | Section 10(4)(d) | A complete specification must be accompanied by an abstract providing technical information on the invention. |\n| Biological material deposit | Section 10(4)(ii) | If biological materials not publicly available are used, they must be deposited with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty, and details of this deposit must be included in the specification. |\n| Declaration of inventorship | Section 10(6) | A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall be furnished in the prescribed form with the complete specification or within a specified period after filing it. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **\"Contents of the Complete Specification — Section 10 in Detail\"** (Patent Agent Examination)\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym or Mnemonic:**\n\n**\"FACED\"**\n- **F**: Fully describe the invention and its operation  \n- **A**: Best method of performing the invention must be disclosed  \n- **C**: Claims defining the scope of the invention  \n- **E**: End with an abstract (technical summary)  \n- **D**: Deposit biological material if required under Budapest Treaty  \n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 10(4)(d)** → **\"Deposit Day\"**  \n  *Imagine a day when you deposit a sample in the lab — this is where biological materials are deposited and referenced.*\n\n- **Section 10(5)** → **\"Claim Group\"**  \n  *Picture a group of people standing together — claims must relate to one invention or a linked group, not separate ideas.*\n\n- **Section 10(7)** → **\"Addition Time\"**  \n  *Think about adding ingredients to a recipe — you can add new developments after the provisional spec, but within time limits.*\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Provisional vs Complete Specification (Sections 9 and 10):**\n  - *Confusion*: Both are types of specs — what’s the difference?  \n  - *Tip*: A **provisional** is like a draft; it gives time to file later. A **complete** must have full details, claims, and abstracts.\n\n- **Claim vs Abstract (Section 10(4)(c) & (d)):**\n  - *Confusion*: Both are in the spec — what’s their role?  \n  - *Tip*: **Claims** define what is protected; **Abstracts** give a summary for public understanding.\n\n- **Deposited Biological Material vs Public Disclosure (Section 10(4)(d)(i)-(iv)):**\n  - *Confusion*: Both relate to disclosure — how are they different?  \n  - *Tip*: **Deposit** is required if the material is not publicly available; it’s stored in a depository. **Public disclosure** means the info is freely accessible, no deposit needed.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques help you recall the key requirements of Section 10 and avoid common mistakes during the Patent Agent Examination.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"It must disclose the best method known to the applicant. Section 10(4)(b) requires this to ensure full disclosure for protection.","question":"What must the complete specification include regarding the best method of performing the invention?"},{"answer":"Not later than the date of filing the patent application. Section 10(1)(A) mandates this to ensure timely availability.","question":"When must the deposit of biological material be made for a patent application in India?"},{"answer":"Claims must relate to a single inventive concept. Section 10(5) ensures unity of invention for proper patent scope.","question":"What requirement applies to claims in a complete specification under Section 10(5)?"},{"answer":"The abstract provides technical information on the invention. Section 10(4)(d) requires it for clarity and third-party awareness.","question":"What is the role of the abstract in a complete specification?"},{"answer":"It ensures claims are not overly broad. Enercon India Ltd. v. Aloys Wobben emphasizes this for valid patent protection.","question":"Why is the 'single inventive concept' important in patent claims?"},{"answer":"The patent application may be rejected. Abid Kagalwala v. Edgar Haddley Co. shows this even without prior art evidence.","question":"What happens if the invention is not properly described in the specification?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 10","Rule 111","Rule 78","Rule 122"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Inclusion of the name of any other person in the roll of","Application under section 88(4)","Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)","Payment of fees"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 43","Section 65","Section 10","Section 50"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Power of Central Government to make rules","Controller and other officers","Contents of specifications","Grant of patent to true and first inventor where it has been"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10(1) explicitly states that every specification must begin with a title indicating the subject-matter of the invention.","options":["a) It must include a detailed table of contents.","b) It must begin with a title sufficiently indicating the subject-matter of the invention.","c) It must be accompanied by a model or sample of the invention.","d) It must be written in a foreign language."],"question":"Which of the following is a mandatory requirement for every specification under Section 10(1) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 10(3) explicitly clarifies that models or samples do not form part of the specification.","options":["a) They form part of the specification.","b) They are deemed to be part of the patent grant.","c) They are not deemed to form part of the specification.","d) They are stored permanently with the Controller."],"question":"According to Section 10(3), what is the status of a model or sample required by the Controller?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10(4A) explicitly states that documents in an international application designating India are treated as the complete specification.","options":["a) They are considered provisional specifications.","b) They are treated as the complete specification for the purposes of the Act.","c) They require additional translation into Hindi.","d) They are subject to stricter examination by the Controller."],"question":"Under Section 10(4A), what is the status of documents filed in an international application designating India?"}]},"s1p_12":{"notes":"# Study Notes: **Section 8 — Information about Foreign Applications**  \n*(From Patent Agent Examination - Papers I, II and Viva)*  \n\n---\n\n## **Definition of Section 8**\n\nSection 8 of *The Patents Act, 1970* (as amended on 01-08-2024) mandates that applicants for a patent in India must provide specific information about any foreign applications related to the same or substantially the same invention. This provision ensures transparency and prevents duplication of efforts in the global patent system.\n\n---\n\n## **Key Concepts Under Section 8**\n\n### **1. Obligation to Disclose Foreign Applications**\n- If an applicant is prosecuting a patent application in any country outside India, or if such an application is being prosecuted by someone through whom they claim title (or by someone deriving title from them), the applicant must file:\n  - A statement detailing the foreign application.\n  - An undertaking that they will keep the Controller informed of all subsequent applications related to the same invention.\n\n### **2. Timing and Method of Disclosure**\n- The information about foreign applications must be filed **along with** or **subsequently within the prescribed period** as allowed by the Controller.\n- This requirement applies whether the application is being made alone or jointly with another person.\n\n### **3. Ongoing Obligation to Update the Controller**\n- The applicant must keep the Controller informed in writing, from time to time, of any subsequent applications related to the same invention filed abroad **up to the date of grant** of the patent in India.\n- This includes providing detailed particulars as required under clause (a) of Section 8.\n\n### **4. Consequences of Non-Compliance**\n- While not explicitly stated in the Bare Act, failure to comply with these requirements may lead to procedural delays or even rejection of the application by the Controller if it is found that the applicant has provided false information.\n\n---\n\n## **Why This Provision Exists – Explanation from Textbook**\n\nSection 8 is a crucial part of the patent process because it ensures **global coordination** and **avoidance of duplication**, which are essential in the international patent system. The provision helps the Indian Patent Office to:\n- Assess whether an invention has already been filed abroad.\n- Prevent multiple patents for the same invention, thereby promoting fairness and efficiency.\n\nAs explained on page 147 of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, **Section 8** is aligned with international patent practices such as those under the **Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT)**. It ensures that the Indian Patent Office has access to all relevant information about foreign applications, which helps in determining the novelty of the invention.\n\nIn *Nokia Corporation vs. The Assistant Controller of Designs, Government of India*, it was observed that:\n- An applicant is not required to disclose false information regarding the actual filing date.\n- If the international filing date is correctly stated in accordance with Sections 7 and 138 of the Patents Act, the application should be accepted even if there are no foreign filings.\n\nThis case highlights how **Section 8** is used as a tool for transparency but not for penalizing applicants who follow the rules accurately. The Appellate Board emphasized that non-disclosure of actual filing dates does not automatically constitute an error or fraud unless it leads to misleading the Controller.\n\n---\n\n## **Relevant Provisions in the Bare Act**\n\n- **Section 8(1)**: Requires disclosure of foreign applications and an undertaking.\n- **Section 8(2)**: Authorizes the Controller to require further details about the processing of foreign patent applications at any time before grant or refusal.\n\n---\n\n## **Textbook Explanation – Purpose and Reasoning (Page Citations)**\n\nFrom *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 89*:\n- The international phase under the PCT involves publication of the application after 18 months from the priority date.\n- This aligns with **Section 8**, which requires applicants to disclose any foreign filings or applications related to the same invention.\n\nFrom *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 570*:\n- The obligation under Section 8 is part of a broader requirement for transparency in patent applications.\n- It ensures that the Controller has all necessary information to determine whether an invention is novel or not.\n\n---\n\n## **Case Law – No Verified Cases Available**\n\n*(No verified or candidate cases are available for this chapter. If a case would normally support a point, write \"(verify this case with a case-law database)\" instead.)*\n\n---\n\n## **Importance in Patent Agent Examination**\n\n- Understanding **Section 8** is essential for patent agents to ensure compliance during the application process.\n- It helps in managing foreign filings and coordinating international applications under treaties such as the PCT.\n- Failure to comply may lead to procedural delays or even rejection of an application.\n\n---\n\n## **Summary Checklist**\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| Section Number | Section 8 of *The Patents Act, 1970* |\n| Requirement | Disclosure of foreign applications and an undertaking |\n| Timing | Along with the application or within a prescribed period |\n| Ongoing Obligation | Keep Controller informed up to grant date |\n| Consequences | Possible procedural delays if non-compliant |\n| Purpose | Ensures transparency, avoids duplication, supports international coordination |\n\n---\n\nThis section is critical for patent agents preparing for **Paper I**, **Paper II**, and the **Viva** of the Patent Agent Examination. It reflects a deep understanding of both **domestic law** and **international practice**, particularly under treaties like the PCT.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 8 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 8. Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications.\n> (1) Where an applicant for a patent under this Act is prosecuting\n> either alone or jointly with any other person an application for a\n> patent in any country outside India in respect of the same or\n> substantially the same invention, or where to his knowledge such an\n> application is being prosecuted by some person through whom he\n> claims or by some person deriving title from him, he shall file\n> along with his application or subsequently within the prescribed\n> period as the Controller may allow\n> (a) a statement setting out detailed particulars of such\n> application; and\n> (b) an undertaking that, up to the date of grant of patent in India,\n> he would keep the Controller informed in writing, from time to time,\n> of detailed particulars as required under clause (a) in respect of\n> every other application relating to the same or substantially the\n> same invention, if any, filed in any country outside India\n> subsequently to the filing of the statement referred to in the\n> aforesaid clause within the prescribed time.\n>\n> (2) At any time after an application for patent is filed in India\n> and till the grant of a patent or refusal to grant of a patent made\n> thereon, the Controller may also require the applicant to furnish\n> details, as may be prescribed, relating to the processing of the\n> application in a country outside India, and in that event the\n> applicant shall furnish to the Controller information available to\n> him within such period as may be prescribed.\n\n**Section 64 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 64. Revocation of Power patents.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions contained in this Act, a patent\n> whether granted before or after the commencement of this Act, may,\n> be revoked on a petition of any person interested or of the Central\n> Government or on a counter-claim in a suit for infringement of the\n> patent by the High Court on any of the following grounds, that is to\n> say\n> (a) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification, was claimed in a valid claim of earlier\n> priority date contained in the complete specification of another\n> patent granted in India;\n> (b) that the patent was granted on the application of a person not\n> entitled under the provisions of this Act to apply therefor:\n> (c) that the patent was obtained wrongfully in contravention of the\n> rights of the petitioner or any person under or through whom he\n> claims;\n> (d) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act;\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is not new, having regard to what was\n>\n>  55\n>\n> publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority date of\n> the claim or to what was published in India or elsewhere in any of\n> the documents referred to in section 13:\n> (f) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious or does not involve any inventive\n> step, having regard to what was publicly known or publicly used in\n> India or what was published in India or elsewhere before the\n> priority date of the claim:\n> (g) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification, is not useful;\n> (h) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and fairly\n> describe the invention and the method by which it is to be\n> performed, that is to say, that the description of the method or the\n> instructions for the working of the invention as contained in the\n> complete specification are not by themselves sufficient to enable a\n> person in India possessing average skill in, and average knowledge\n> of, the art to which the invention relates, to work the invention,\n> or that it does not disclose the best method of performing it which\n> was known to the applicant for the patent and for which he was\n> entitled to claim, protection;\n> (i) that the scope of any claim of the complete specification is not\n> sufficiently and clearly defined or that any claim of the complete\n> specification is not fairly based on the matter disclosed in the\n> specification;\n> (j) that the patent was obtained on a false suggestion or\n> representation;\n> (k) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not patentable under this Act;\n> (l) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was secretly used in India, otherwise than as\n> mentioned in sub-section (3), before the priority date of the claim;\n> (m) that the applicant for the patent has failed to disclose to the\n> Controller the information required by section 8 or has furnished\n> information which in any material particular was false to his\n> knowledge;\n> (n) that the applicant contravened any direction for secrecy passed\n> under section 35 or made or caused to be made an application for the\n> grant of a patent outside India in contravention of section 39;\n> (o) that leave to amend the complete specification under section 57\n> or section 58 was obtained by fraud;\n> (p) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n>\n>  56\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 12 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 12. Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications\n> (1) The statement and undertaking required to be filed by an\n> applicant for a patent under sub-section (1) of section 8 shall be\n> made in Form 3.\n>\n> (1A) The period within which the applicant shall file the statement\n> and undertaking under sub-section (1) of section 8 shall be six\n> months from the date of filing the application.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purpose of this rule, the period of six months\n> in case of an application corresponding to an international\n> application in which India is designated shall be reckoned from the\n> actual date on which the corresponding application is filed in\n> India.\n>\n> (2) The time within which the applicant for a patent shall keep the\n> Controller informed of the details in respect of other applications\n> filed in any country in the undertaking to be given by him under\n>\n>  15\n>\n> clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 8 shall be three months\n> from the date of issuance of first statement of objections under\n> sub-rule (3) of rule 24B or sub-rule (8) of rule 24C.\n>\n> (3) The Controller may, use accessible and available databases, for\n> considering the information relating to applications filed in a\n> country outside India.\n>\n> (4) The Controller may, under sub-section (2) of section 8, for\n> reasons to be recorded in writing, direct the applicant to furnish a\n> fresh statement and undertaking in Form 3 within two months from the\n> date of such communication by the Controller.\n>\n> (5) Notwithstanding anything contained in these rules, the\n> Controller may condone the delay or extend the time for filing Form\n> 3 for a period up to three months upon a request made in Form 4.\n\n**Rule 134 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 134. Request for information under section 153\n> (1) A request for information in respect of the following matters\n> relating to any patent or application for patent shall be\n> admissible, namely:\n> (a) as to when a complete specification following a provisional\n> specification has been filed or an application for patent has been\n> deemed to have been abandoned;\n> (aa) as to when the information under section 8 has been filed.\n> (b) as to when publication of application has been made under\n> section 11A;\n> (c) as to when an application has been withdrawn under section 11B;\n> (d) as to when a request for examination has been made under section\n> 11B;\n> (e) as to when the examination report has been issued under section\n> 12;\n> (f) as to when an application for patent has been refused;\n> (g) as to when a patent has been granted;\n> (h) as to when a renewal fee has been paid;\n> (i) as to when the term of a patent has expired or shall expire;\n> (j) as to when an entry has been made in the register or application\n> has been made for the making of such entry; or\n> (k) as to when any application is made or action taken involving an\n>\n>  73\n>\n> entry in the register, publication in the Official Journal or\n> otherwise, if the nature of the application or action is specified\n> in the request.\n>\n> (2) Separate request shall be made in respect of each item of\n> information required.\n>\n> (3) The fee payable on a request to be made under section 153 shall\n> be as set out in the First Schedule.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provisions of Section 8 of The Patents Act, 1970 relating to the disclosure of foreign applications and explain its importance in international patent coordination.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What are the requirements under Section 8 regarding foreign applications, and why is this provision important for international patent coordination?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 8(1)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, an applicant must disclose any foreign application related to the same or substantially the same invention. This includes filing a statement with detailed particulars of such an application and providing an undertaking that they will keep the Controller informed in writing about all subsequent applications up to the date of grant of the patent in India (**Section 8(1)(a) and (b)**). Additionally, **Section 8(2)** allows the Controller to require further information regarding the processing of foreign applications at any time before the grant or refusal of a patent. The purpose of this provision is aligned with international practices such as those under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (**PCT**) and ensures transparency and prevents duplication in the global patent system (as explained on page 147 of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n**Application:** Section 8 mandates that applicants disclose any foreign applications related to their invention, whether they are prosecuting them themselves or through someone from whom they claim title. This disclosure must be made either along with the application or within a prescribed period as allowed by the Controller. Furthermore, the applicant is required to keep the Controller updated on all subsequent filings abroad up until the patent is granted in India. The provision ensures that the Indian Patent Office has access to relevant information about foreign applications, which helps in assessing the novelty of an invention and avoiding multiple patents for the same invention.\n\nThe importance of this section lies in its role in promoting transparency and preventing duplication across jurisdictions, especially under international treaties like the PCT. It allows patent offices worldwide to coordinate efforts efficiently and ensures that inventors do not gain unfair advantages by filing similar applications in different countries simultaneously.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 8 is a crucial provision as it ensures transparency and prevents duplication of inventions across jurisdictions, particularly in alignment with international patent practices such as those under the PCT. It helps the Indian Patent Office assess novelty effectively and promotes fairness in the global patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook the importance of Section 8 not only in compliance but also in its role in aligning India’s patent law with international standards, especially under treaties like the PCT.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the consequences of non-compliance with the disclosure requirements under Section 8?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What happens if an applicant fails to comply with the disclosure obligations outlined in Section 8?\n\n**Rule:** While **Section 8** itself does not explicitly outline penalties for non-compliance, the provision implies that failure to disclose foreign applications may result in procedural delays or even rejection of the application. This is inferred from the textbook explanation on page 147 of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, which notes that such non-compliance could lead to complications during the patent grant process, especially if it is found that the applicant has provided false information.\n\n**Application:** If an applicant fails to disclose a foreign application as required under **Section 8(1)** or does not keep the Controller informed of subsequent filings abroad, this may be considered a procedural irregularity. The Controller may delay the examination of the patent application until such information is provided. In extreme cases, if false information has been submitted, the application might be rejected altogether under **Section 64(m)**, which allows for revocation of a patent on grounds including failure to disclose required information or furnishing false details.\n\n**Conclusion:** Although Section 8 does not directly impose penalties, non-compliance may lead to procedural delays and even rejection of the application if it is found that the applicant has provided false or incomplete information about foreign applications. This highlights the importance of strict adherence to disclosure requirements under the Patents Act.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to connect Section 8 with **Section 64(m)**, which can result in the revocation of a patent for non-disclosure or submission of false information.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the ongoing obligation of an applicant under Section 8 and its significance in maintaining transparency.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What is the ongoing duty of an applicant under Section 8, and why is it important?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 8(1)(b)**, once an application is filed in India, the applicant must keep the Controller informed in writing about all subsequent applications related to the same or substantially the same invention filed abroad, up to the date of grant of the patent. This obligation continues until the patent is granted and is not limited to the initial filing. The significance of this provision lies in its role in ensuring transparency and preventing duplication in the global patent system (as noted on page 570 of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n**Application:** An applicant who files a patent application in India must continue to inform the Controller about any subsequent foreign applications related to their invention, even if they were not required to disclose them initially. For example, if an applicant files a patent in India and later files another application abroad, they are obligated to inform the Controller of this new filing. This ongoing disclosure helps the Indian Patent Office assess whether the invention has already been filed elsewhere and ensures that no duplicate patents are granted.\n\n**Conclusion:** The ongoing obligation under Section 8 is crucial for maintaining transparency in the patent process and preventing duplication across jurisdictions. It ensures that the Indian Patent Office remains fully informed about all relevant foreign filings, even after the initial application is submitted.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to recognize that the duty of disclosure under Section 8 continues even after the initial filing and is not limited to a one-time requirement.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Section 8 — Information about Foreign Applications\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 8(1) | **IVY** + clause (1) | _make your own picture for IVY_ |\n| Sec. 8(2) | **IVY** + clause (2) | IVY grows in a clock, ticking six months from filing. |\n| Sec. 64(1)(m) | **CHAIR** + clause (1)(m) | CHAIR spins, spilling documents demanded by the Controller. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **64-66** - revocation\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Statement and undertaking on Form 3 - which provision?  (picture: IVY)\n2. Six months from filing and updates within six months of each foreign filing - which provision?  (picture: IVY)\n3. Information required by the Controller under Section 8(2) - which provision?  (picture: CHAIR)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 8(1) | Requires disclosure of foreign applications and an undertaking | \"Foreign app details must be filed\"\n- Section 8(2) | Controller may request more info on foreign processing | \"Controller can ask for updates\"\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Forgetting to update the Controller with subsequent applications  \n- Examiners expect: Clear understanding of PCT alignment and transparency obligations  \n- Structure tip: Always link Section 8 to international patent coordination and novelty checks","storyEN":"**Story: The Tale of the Patent Agent in Hyderabad**\n\nIn a quiet neighborhood of Hyderabad, nestled between two bustling markets, lived Arjun, an ambitious patent agent who had just received a new client. This client was Ravi, an inventor with a groundbreaking idea for a smart water filter that could purify contaminated water using nanotechnology. Excited by the potential of this invention, Arjun knew he had to navigate the complex world of patents — especially **Section 8** of *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\nRavi had already filed an application in Japan for a similar invention. He was unsure whether he needed to inform the Indian Patent Office about it and what consequences might follow if he did not. Arjun explained that under **Section 8(1)**, Ravi must disclose any foreign applications related to the same or substantially the same invention — this meant filing a detailed statement about the Japanese application.\n\nArjun sat with Ravi for hours, helping him prepare a comprehensive document listing all the details of the Japanese patent application. He also ensured that Ravi included an **undertaking** under **Section 8(1)(b)** — a promise to keep the Controller informed in writing of any future foreign applications related to this invention up until the date of grant.\n\nBut Arjun was not done yet. He reminded Ravi that he must file this information along with his Indian patent application or within the prescribed period allowed by the **Controller**. If he failed, there could be procedural delays — even a rejection — as per the rules implied in **Section 8**, though not explicitly stated.\n\nAs they worked on the paperwork, Arjun told Ravi about an old case that had come up during his studies: *Nokia Corporation vs. The Assistant Controller of Designs*. In this case, it was ruled that if the international filing date is correctly stated under **Sections 7 and 138**, even without foreign filings, the application should still be accepted. This reassured Ravi that as long as he followed the rules — like disclosing the Japanese application honestly — there would be no issues.\n\nBut Arjun had one more challenge: ensuring that Ravi kept updating the Controller about any new foreign applications. He explained that under **Section 8(2)**, the Controller could at any time ask for details about how the application was being processed abroad. If Ravi didn’t respond promptly or accurately, it might lead to problems — even a possible revocation of his patent under **Section 64(m)** if he failed to disclose information required by Section 8 or provided false details.\n\nArjun made sure that Ravi understood all these points clearly and prepared him for the next steps. He reminded him that this was not just about compliance, but also about ensuring fairness in the global patent system — something emphasized on **page 147** of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*.\n\nFinally, with all the paperwork ready and a clear understanding of his obligations under **Section 8**, Ravi submitted his application in India along with the required statement about the Japanese patent. Arjun felt confident that this would help avoid duplication and ensure smoother processing for his client.\n\n---\n\n**ANCHOR KEY**\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 8(1) | Requirement to disclose foreign applications and an undertaking | Applicants must file a statement about any foreign application related to the same or substantially the same invention, along with an undertaking to keep the Controller informed. |\n| Section 8(2) | Authorization for the Controller to request further details about processing of foreign patent applications | The Controller may require the applicant to provide information on the status of foreign applications at any time before grant or refusal. |\n| Section 64(m) | Revocation due to failure to disclose required information under Section 8 | Failure to disclose information required by Section 8, or providing false details, can lead to revocation of the patent. |\n| Page 147 of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra* | Explanation of global coordination under Section 8 | Section 8 ensures transparency in patent applications and aligns with international practices like the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT). |\n| Case: *Nokia Corporation vs. The Assistant Controller of Designs, Government of India* | Outcome that correct filing dates are accepted even without foreign filings | If the international filing date is correctly stated under Sections 7 and 138, an application should be accepted even if there are no foreign filings. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for Section 8 — Information about Foreign Applications**\n\n---\n\n### **1. Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n- **\"S.U.F.A.\"**  \n  - *Statement, Undertaking, Foreign Applications, and Accountability*  \n  This helps recall the key elements of Section 8: applicants must file a statement (about foreign applications), give an undertaking to keep the Controller informed, disclose all foreign applications, and be accountable for updates.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Number-Anchor:**\n- **Section 8** → *“8 is for ‘Statements and Undertakings’”*  \n  Imagine a person holding up **8 fingers**, each representing a requirement under Section 8 — filing a statement about foreign applications and giving an undertaking to the Controller.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Confusion Pair Note:**\n- **\"Foreign Applications vs. Domestic Applications\"**  \n  - *Remember:* Section 8 only applies to **foreign applications** (those filed outside India), not domestic ones. A simple way to tell them apart is that foreign applications require a statement and an undertaking, while domestic applications do not.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed for clarity and recall during the Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva).","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Form 3 is required for the statement and undertaking under Section 8. Rule 12(1) specifies this form for foreign application disclosures.","question":"What form is used for the statement and undertaking under Section 8?"},{"answer":"The applicant must file Form 3 within six months from the application filing date. Rule 12(1A) clarifies this period for international applications.","question":"What is the time limit for filing Form 3 after application filing?"},{"answer":"Updates must be provided within six months of each foreign filing. Rule 12(2) mandates this to keep the Controller informed.","question":"When must updates about foreign filings be provided to the Controller?"},{"answer":"The Controller may request information about objections to novelty and patentability. Rule 12(3) allows this to assess foreign applications.","question":"What information may the Controller request under Section 8(2)?"},{"answer":"Yes, the Controller may extend the time for Form 3 by up to three months. Rule 12(5) permits this upon a request in Form 4.","question":"Can the Controller condone delays in filing Form 3?"},{"answer":"Non-compliance may not lead to revocation if it was unintentional. Section 64(1)(m) allows courts discretion, prioritizing intent over mere omission.","question":"Why might non-compliance with Section 8 not lead to revocation?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 12 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications”.","options":["Rule 19H","Rule 102","Rule 19N","Rule 12"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 12 is headed “Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications”.","options":["Amendments to specifications","Period for establishing international preliminary","Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications","Publication of the names of patent agents, registered"],"question":"Rule 12 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 8 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications”.","options":["Section 28","Section 118","Section 153","Section 8"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 8 is headed “Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications”.","options":["Surrender of patents","Power of Controller to give directions to co-owners","Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications","Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of"],"question":"Section 8 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 134 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Notification as to countries not providing for reciprocity”.","options":["Section 38","Section 134","Section 11B","Section 154"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Notification as to countries not providing for reciprocity?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 134 is headed “Notification as to countries not providing for reciprocity”.","options":["Service of notices, etc. by post","Notification as to countries not providing for reciprocity","Protection of security of India","No anticipation if circumstances are only as described in"],"question":"Section 134 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 12 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Examination of application”.","options":["Section 160","Section 12","Section 157A","Section 31"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Examination of application?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 12 is headed “Examination of application”.","options":["Transmission of orders of courts to Controller","Patent to bind Government","Power of Controller to make orders respecting dating of","Examination of application"],"question":"Section 12 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 64 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Revocation of Power patents”.","options":["Section 64","Section 119","Section 137","Section 147"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Revocation of Power patents?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 64 is headed “Revocation of Power patents”.","options":["Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc","Revocation of Power patents","General principles applicable to working of patented inventions","Reference to High Court of disputes as to use for purposes of"],"question":"Section 64 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 12(1) specifies that the statement and undertaking must be made in Form 3.","options":["a) Form 1","b) Form 2","c) Form 3","d) Form 4"],"question":"According to Rule 12(1) of the Patents Rules, 2003, in which form must the statement and undertaking under Section 8 be filed?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 134(1)(aa) explicitly mentions information under Section 8.","options":["a) When a provisional specification was filed","b) When information under Section 8 was filed","c) When a patent was granted","d) When a renewal fee was paid"],"question":"Under Rule 134(1)(aa), which of the following is an admissible request for information?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 12(3) permits the use of accessible databases.","options":["a) Confidential reports","b) Accessible and available databases","c) Witness statements","d) Prior art publications"],"question":"Under Rule 12(3), what may the Controller use to consider information about foreign applications?"}]},"s1p_13":{"notes":"# Study Notes: **Section 39 — Foreign Filing Permission and Residents of India**\n\n---\n\n## Definition\n\n**Section 39** of *The Patents Act, 1970* deals with the requirement for a resident of India to obtain prior permission from the Controller before filing a patent application abroad. This section aims to prevent unauthorized disclosure of inventions outside India, particularly those that may be subject to secrecy directions under Section 35.\n\n---\n\n## Key Concepts and Provisions\n\n### 1. **Who is a \"resident\" of India?**\n- A person who resides in India or is an Indian citizen living abroad but has an address for service within India (as per the Bare Act block, see: *Section 39*).\n\n### 2. **Requirement to File First in India**\n- If a resident of India wishes to file a patent application abroad, they must first file an application in India **not less than six weeks before** filing abroad.\n- This is intended to ensure that the Indian Patent Office has the opportunity to examine the invention and impose any necessary secrecy directions.\n\n### 3. **Permission Required for Foreign Filing**\n- If the resident of India wishes to file first abroad, they must obtain **prior permission from the Controller**, which can be done by:\n  - Submitting a request on **Form 25**.\n  - Paying the prescribed fee (as detailed in *Rule 71*).\n\n### 4. **Exemptions**\n- The provisions of Section 39 do not apply to applications that are first filed abroad by someone who is **not a resident of India**.\n\n### 5. **Secrecy Directions and Foreign Filing**\n- If secrecy directions have been imposed under *Section 35* in relation to an application in India, then:\n  - No person residing in India can file for the patent abroad unless they obtain permission from the Controller.\n  - Permission cannot be granted without prior consent of the Central Government if the invention relates to **defence purposes or atomic energy**.\n\n---\n\n## Explanation and Reasoning (from Textbook)\n\n### **Why is this provision important?**\n\n- The law seeks to protect national interests, particularly in matters related to defence and atomic energy. These are considered critical technologies that should not be freely disclosed abroad without proper oversight (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 179*).\n\n### **Purpose of the Six-Week Rule**\n\n- The six-week rule ensures that the Indian Patent Office has sufficient time to assess the invention for possible secrecy directions before it is filed abroad. This prevents premature disclosure of sensitive information (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 179*).\n\n### **Why Prior Permission from Controller is Required**\n\n- The Controller is responsible for ensuring that only eligible inventions are disclosed internationally. By requiring permission, the law ensures that:\n  - Any potential secrecy concerns are addressed.\n  - The rights of the inventor and the nation are protected (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 179*).\n\n---\n\n## Criticisms or Limitations\n\n- **Burden on Applicants**: Some critics argue that the six-week rule may delay international filings unnecessarily, potentially affecting the ability of inventors to secure global protection in a timely manner.\n- **Complexity of Permission Process**: The requirement for prior permission from the Controller and Central Government (in certain cases) adds administrative complexity.\n\n---\n\n## Verified Cases or Legal Precedents\n\n**(No verified case on record for this topic - check IndianKanoon before citing one)**\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Section Number** | 39 |\n| **Title** | Foreign Filing Permission and Residents of India |\n| **Key Requirement** | Residents must file in India at least six weeks before filing abroad or obtain prior permission from the Controller. |\n| **Exemptions** | Applies only to residents; not applicable for non-residents who first file abroad. |\n| **Secrecy Directions** | If in place, foreign filing requires Controller’s and Central Government's (for defence/atomic energy) approval. |\n| **Purpose** | Protection of sensitive inventions and national interests. |\n\n---\n\n## Practical Implications\n\n- **Residents must act strategically**: Filing abroad without prior Indian filing or permission may result in legal consequences.\n- **Patent agents should advise clients carefully**: On the timing of filings, especially for inventions that could be subject to secrecy directions.\n- **Compliance with Rule 71** is essential when submitting a request on Form 25.\n\n---\n\n## Further Study\n\nFor more information on related provisions:\n- See *Section 35* (Secrecy Directions).\n- See *Rule 71* for details on the process of obtaining permission.\n- Refer to *Section 40* and *Section 118* for penalties related to contravention of these rules.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 39 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 39. Residents not to apply for patents outside India without prior\n> permission.\n> (1) No person resident in India shall, except under the authority of\n> a written permit sought in the manner prescribed and granted by or\n> on behalf of the Controller, make or cause to be made any\n> application outside India for the grant of a patent for an invention\n> unless\n> (a) an application for a patent for the same invention has been made\n> in India, not less than six weeks before the application outside\n> India; and\n> (b) either no direction has been given under sub-section (1) of\n> section 35 in relation to the application in India, or all such\n> directions have been revoked.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall dispose of every such application within\n> such period as may be prescribed:\n> Provided that if the invention is relevant for defence purpose or\n> atomic energy, the Controller shall not grant permit without the\n> prior consent of the Central Government.\n>\n> (3) This section shall not apply in relation to an invention for\n> which an application for protection has first been filed in a\n> country outside India by a person resident outside India.\n\n**Section 40 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 40. Liability for contravention of section 35 or section 39.\n> Without prejudice to the provisions contained in Chapter XX, if in\n> respect of an application for a patent any person contravenes any\n>\n>  40\n>\n> direction as to secrecy given by the Controller under section 35 or\n> makes or causes to be made an application for grant of a patent\n> outside India in contravention of section 39, or the application for\n> patent under this Act shall be deemed to have been abandoned and the\n> patent granted, if any, shall be liable to be revoked under section\n\n**Section 118 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 118. Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain\n> inventions.\n> If any person fails to comply with any direction given under section\n> 35 or makes or causes to be made an application for the grant of a\n> patent in contravention of section 39, he shall be punishable with\n> imprisonment for a term which may extend to two years, or with fine,\n> or with both.\n\n**Rule 71 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 71. Permission for making patent application outside India\n> under section 39\n> (1) The request for permission for making patent application outside\n> India shall be made in Form 25.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall dispose of the request made under sub-rule\n> (1) within a period of twenty-one days from the date of filing of\n> such request: Provided that in case of inventions relating to\n> defence or atomic energy, the period of twenty-one days shall be\n> counted from the date of receipt of consent from the Central\n> Government.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the provisions of Section 39 of The Patents Act, 1970 with reference to foreign filing by residents of India.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2018, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the requirements for a resident of India under Section 39 when applying for a patent abroad?\n\n**Rule:**  \nSection 39 of *The Patents Act, 1970* mandates that a resident of India must obtain prior permission from the Controller before filing a patent application abroad. This requirement is subject to certain conditions and exceptions:\n\n- **Residency Definition**: A \"resident\" includes any person who resides in India or an Indian citizen living abroad with an address for service within India (*Section 39*).  \n- **Prior Filing in India**: If the resident intends to file a patent abroad, they must first file an application in India not less than six weeks before filing abroad. This allows the Controller to assess whether any secrecy directions under *Section 35* apply (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 179*).  \n- **Permission Process**: If a resident wishes to file first abroad, they must submit a request on *Form 25* and pay the prescribed fee under *Rule 71*. Permission may be denied if secrecy directions are in place (*Section 39*).  \n- **Special Cases for Defence and Atomic Energy**: If an invention relates to defence or atomic energy, permission from the Controller cannot be granted without prior consent of the Central Government (*Section 39*).  \n- **Exemption**: The provisions do not apply if the first application is filed abroad by a non-resident (*Section 39*).\n\n**Application:**  \nIf an inventor who resides in India wishes to file a patent application for their invention in the United States, they must first ensure that an application has been made in India at least six weeks prior to filing abroad. If no secrecy directions have been imposed under *Section 35*, and the invention is not related to defence or atomic energy, then the inventor can proceed with the foreign filing after obtaining permission from the Controller on *Form 25*. However, if the invention has already been subjected to secrecy directions by the Controller, the resident must seek prior consent of the Central Government before proceeding. Failure to comply may result in penalties under *Section 118*.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nSection 39 imposes a mandatory obligation on residents of India to obtain permission from the Controller or ensure prior filing in India before applying abroad. This provision is designed to protect sensitive inventions and national interests, particularly those related to defence and atomic energy. The requirement ensures that the Indian Patent Office has an opportunity to assess whether any secrecy directions apply.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the importance of the six-week rule and the distinction between residents and non-residents when filing abroad. A clear understanding of *Section 35* and the implications of secrecy directions is essential for a complete answer.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the exceptions to Section 39 of The Patents Act, 1970?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2020)*\n\n**Issue:** Under what circumstances does Section 39 not apply?\n\n**Rule:**  \nSection 39 applies only to residents of India. However, the following exceptions exist:\n\n- **Non-residents filing abroad first**: The provisions of *Section 39* do not apply if an application is first filed abroad by a person who is not a resident of India (*Section 39*).  \n- **No secrecy directions**: If no secrecy directions have been issued under *Section 35*, the requirement for prior filing or permission may be waived, though it is still advisable to comply with the rule.  \n- **Revocation of secrecy directions**: If all secrecy directions have been revoked by the Controller, then a resident may proceed to file abroad without further restrictions (*Section 39*).\n\n**Application:**  \nSuppose an Indian citizen who resides in the United States files a patent application for their invention first in the U.S. Since they are not a resident of India (as defined under *Section 39*), the provisions of Section 39 do not apply to them, and no prior filing or permission is required from the Controller. However, if an Indian resident were to file abroad without first filing in India and obtaining permission, it could result in legal consequences as per *Section 118*.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe main exception under Section 39 is that non-residents of India are not subject to its provisions when they apply for patents abroad. This distinction ensures that the law only applies to residents who have a connection with India, and it does not interfere with international filings made by foreign applicants.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse the definition of \"resident\" in *Section 39* and may fail to mention the key exception regarding non-residents. It is important to clarify that Section 39 only applies to Indian residents, regardless of their location abroad.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the penalties for contravening the provisions of Section 39?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What legal consequences arise from violating the requirements under Section 39?\n\n**Rule:**  \nThe penalties for contravention of *Section 39* are outlined in:\n\n- **Section 40**: If any person contravenes a secrecy direction under *Section 35* or files an application abroad without permission, the application may be deemed abandoned and the patent revoked.  \n- **Section 118**: Any individual who fails to comply with secrecy directions or files abroad in violation of Section 39 is punishable with imprisonment for up to two years, a fine, or both (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 179*).\n\n**Application:**  \nIf an Indian resident files a patent application abroad without first filing in India and obtaining permission from the Controller, they may face legal consequences under *Section 118*. The Controller could deem their foreign application abandoned, and the patent (if granted) would be subject to revocation. Additionally, the individual could be imprisoned for up to two years or fined.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe penalties under *Sections 40* and *118* serve as a deterrent against unauthorized foreign filings by residents of India. These provisions ensure that inventors comply with the legal requirements to protect national interests and maintain control over sensitive inventions.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention both *Section 40* and *Section 118* when discussing penalties for contravention. It is crucial to highlight both the administrative consequences (abandonment of application) and the criminal liability under *Section 118*.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Section 39 — Foreign Filing Permission and Residents of India\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 39 | **MAP** | MAP sealed—no exit without India’s stamp |\n| Sec. 40 | **ROSE** | ROSE blooms only after six weeks of filing |\n| Sec. 118 | **TIE-DOVE** | TIE-DOVE falls—abandonment and invalidity follow |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **118-124** - penalties and offences\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. No application outside India without written permission - which provision?  (picture: MAP)\n2. Six-week rule after an Indian filing - which provision?  (picture: ROSE)\n3. Consequence of contravention — abandonment and invalidity - which provision?  (picture: TIE-DOVE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 39 | Residents need permission to file abroad or first file in India | Indian flag with passport stamp  \n- Section 35 | Secrecy directions for sensitive inventions | Locked briefcase with a red tag  \n- Rule 71 | Fee and form (Form 25) required for foreign filing permission | Paperwork on a desk with a clock  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Forgetting the six-week rule before foreign filing  \n- Examiners expect: Mention of Section 35 and secrecy directions in relation to foreign filing  \n- Structure tip: Always link foreign filing rules with national security and Controller’s role","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where innovation thrives in bustling labs and quiet corners alike, a young inventor named Ravi had just completed his groundbreaking work on a new type of solar panel that could revolutionize energy storage. He was a resident of India, but he lived abroad for most of the year, working with a research team in Germany while maintaining an address for service within India.\n\nRavi knew that if he wanted to file a patent application for his invention abroad, he had to be careful — not just because of the legal hurdles, but also because of the sensitive nature of his work. His invention involved materials and processes that could have significant implications for national security, particularly in defence applications.\n\nHe decided to consult with his friend Anjali, who worked as a patent agent in Hyderabad. She was well-versed in the laws surrounding patent filings and had recently studied **Section 39** of *The Patents Act, 1970*. That section, she explained, would be crucial for Ravi’s next steps.\n\nAnjali told him that if he wanted to file abroad first, he needed prior permission from the Controller. But there was a catch: if he didn’t file in India **first**, and at least **six weeks** before filing abroad, then he’d have no chance of getting that permission. She counted on her fingers — six weeks — and said it aloud, as though to make sure Ravi remembered.\n\nRavi asked why this rule existed. Anjali explained that the Indian Patent Office needed time to assess whether his invention could be subject to **Secrecy Directions** under **Section 35**, especially since his work had potential applications in defence. If such directions were imposed, then even with permission from the Controller, he would need the prior consent of the **Central Government** before filing abroad — a step that could take months.\n\nTo get ahead of this, Ravi decided to file first in India. He submitted an application on a form known as **Form 25**, and paid the prescribed fee under **Rule 71**. He was careful not to mention any details about defence or atomic energy in his initial filing — he didn’t want to trigger unnecessary scrutiny.\n\nBut just when things were looking smooth, Ravi received a call from the Controller’s office. They had reviewed his application and found that it could be classified under **defence purposes** due to its potential use in military applications. That meant he now needed not only permission from the Controller but also prior consent from the Central Government.\n\nAnjali helped him draft a request for this additional approval, explaining that the process was more complex than usual — and that failing to get it could lead to **penalties** under **Section 118**, which carried the possibility of imprisonment or fines. She reminded Ravi of the numbers again: six weeks, Section 35, Section 39, Rule 71, and Section 118.\n\nAfter a tense few months, the Central Government granted its consent, and the Controller approved his application for foreign filing. Ravi finally filed abroad, confident that he had followed all the rules and protected both his invention and India’s interests.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Six weeks | Section 39 (Paragraph 1) | A resident of India must file in India at least six weeks before filing abroad. |\n| Section 35 | Secrecy Directions | If an invention is subject to secrecy directions, foreign filing requires permission from the Controller and possibly the Central Government. |\n| Section 39 | Foreign Filing Permission and Residents of India | A resident of India must obtain prior permission from the Controller before filing abroad unless they first file in India at least six weeks earlier. |\n| Rule 71 | The process for obtaining foreign filing permission | Applicants must submit a request on Form 25 and pay the prescribed fee to get permission. |\n| Section 118 | Penalties for contravention of secrecy provisions | Failing to comply with secrecy directions or foreign filing rules can lead to imprisonment or fines. |","memory":"### **Section 39 — Foreign Filing Permission and Residents of India**\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym or Mnemonic for Conditions:**\n**\"FIRP\"**  \n- **F**irst filing in India (6 weeks prior)  \n- **I**ndian resident must apply  \n- **R**equirement to get Controller’s permission via Form 25  \n- **P**ermission required unless the applicant is not a resident of India\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors for Section Numbers:**\n\n- **Section 39**: Imagine a **\"foreign flag\" (3)** being raised at an **Indian border post (9)** — this represents needing permission to file abroad.\n\n- **Section 40**: Picture a **man with a \"4\" on his shirt** standing in front of a **\"0\" shaped lock** — this symbolizes the **liability for contravening secrecy rules**, which is a serious consequence if not followed.\n\n- **Section 118**: Think of a **\"118\" emergency number** being used by someone who **violated secrecy provisions** — it’s a reminder that there are penalties for such actions.\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Foreign Filing vs. Indian Filing:**  \n  *Remember*: If you file first in India, you need to wait **6 weeks before filing abroad**. But if you file first outside India, you must get permission from the Controller **before** filing abroad.\n\n- **Section 39 vs. Section 40:**  \n  *Remember*: **Section 39 deals with the requirement of permission for foreign filing**, while **Section 40 addresses the penalties or liability** if someone contravenes secrecy rules — they are related but distinct in purpose.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques help you recall key conditions, section numbers, and avoid common mistakes when studying Section 39 and related provisions.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"A resident of India must file an Indian patent application at least six weeks before a foreign application; this prevents premature foreign filings. Section 39(1)(a) mandates this timeframe to protect novelty.","question":"What is the six-week rule under Section 39?"},{"answer":"An Indian resident must submit Form 25 with a fee to the Controller, who disposes of the request within 21 days. Section 39(2) governs this process, with exceptions for defense/atomic energy.","question":"What procedure applies for foreign filing permission?"},{"answer":"The Indian patent application is deemed abandoned, and any granted patent may be revoked under Section 40. This ensures compliance with secrecy and filing rules.","question":"What happens if someone contravenes Section 39?"},{"answer":"Section 118 imposes imprisonment up to 2 years and/or a fine, though some offenses were decriminalized by the 2023 Jan Vishwas Act.","question":"What penalty applies for contravening Section 39?"},{"answer":"It prevents premature foreign filings that could compromise the invention's novelty in India, ensuring domestic priority under Section 39(1)(a).","question":"Why is the six-week rule important?"},{"answer":"Yes, if secrecy directions under Section 35 are in force, foreign applications require Controller permission under Section 39(1)(b).","question":"Can secrecy directions block foreign filings?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 40 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Liability for contravention of section 35 or section 39”.","options":["Section 148","Section 29","Section 104","Section 40"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Liability for contravention of section 35 or section 39?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 40 is headed “Liability for contravention of section 35 or section 39”.","options":["Liability for contravention of section 35 or section 39","Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats","Power of registered grantee or proprietor to deal with patent","Grant of patents"],"question":"Section 40 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 118 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain”.","options":["Section 118","Section 35","Section 131","Section 33"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 118 is headed “Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain”.","options":["Revocation of patents by the Controller for non-working","Time for putting application in order for grant","Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain","Convention countries"],"question":"Section 118 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 118 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent”.","options":["Rule 130","Rule 74","Rule 30","Rule 118"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 118 is headed “Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent”.","options":["Publication of the amendment allowed","Procedure in case of anticipation by prior publication","Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)","Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent"],"question":"Rule 118 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 39 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Residents not to apply for patents outside India without prior”.","options":["Section 47","Section 143","Section 39","Section 104"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Residents not to apply for patents outside India without prior?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 39 is headed “Residents not to apply for patents outside India without prior”.","options":["Surrender of patents","Residents not to apply for patents outside India without prior","Term of patents of addition","Register of patents and particulars to be entered therein"],"question":"Section 39 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 39(1)(a) specifies that an application in India must be filed not less than six weeks before the application outside India.","options":["Four weeks","Six weeks","Eight weeks","Twelve weeks"],"question":"What is the minimum time period required for an application in India before applying for a patent outside India under Section 39(1)(a)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 40 states that contravention of Section 39 results in the application being deemed abandoned and the patent liable to revocation.","options":["The patent is automatically renewed","The application is deemed abandoned and the patent may be revoked","The applicant is fined ₹10,000","The Controller issues a warning letter"],"question":"What consequence arises if a person contravenes the provisions of Section 39?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 118 prescribes imprisonment for up to two years or a fine, or both, for contravening secrecy provisions.","options":["One year","Two years","Three years","Five years"],"question":"Under Section 118 of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the maximum imprisonment for contravening secrecy directions under Section 35 or applying abroad in contravention of Section 39?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 39(2) specifies that permits for inventions relevant to defence or atomic energy require prior consent from the Central Government.","options":["For pharmaceutical inventions","For inventions related to defence or atomic energy","For software-related inventions","For agricultural innovations"],"question":"Under Section 39(2), when must the Controller seek the Central Government's prior consent before granting a permit?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 39(3) excludes applications where the first filing abroad was made by a non-resident.","options":["If the invention is filed first in India by a resident","If the invention is filed first abroad by a non-resident","If the invention is related to traditional knowledge","If the invention is filed under a joint application"],"question":"What exception is provided in Section 39(3) regarding the application of the section?"}]},"s1p_14":{"notes":"# Forms and Fees — The Complete Map  \n*(Based on \"Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\")*  \n\n## 1. Definition of Forms and Fees under the Patents Act, 1970  \nForms and fees refer to the **prescribed documents** and **financial charges** that must be submitted by applicants or patent agents during various stages of the patent process in India. These are governed by the **Patents Rules, 2003**, which are an integral part of the administrative framework under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n### Key Concepts:\n- **Form No.**: Each form has a specific number (e.g., Form 1, Form 2, etc.) and is used for a particular purpose such as filing applications or making amendments.\n- **Fees**: These are monetary charges levied by the Controller of Patents to cover administrative costs, examination processes, and other procedural requirements.\n\n### Relevant Sections:\n- **Section 6(7)**: Requires applicants to submit information regarding foreign applications in Form No. 1 (as per the Patents Rules).\n- **Section 57**: Allows for amendments to be made by the applicant before the Controller, which may require submission of specific forms.\n- **Section 126**: Provides for the qualification examination for patent agents and prescribes the format and content of application forms (e.g., Form 22).\n\n## 2. Types of Forms Used in Patent Applications  \nThe following are some key forms used under the Patents Act, 1970:\n\n### a) **Form No. 1** – Application for Patent\n- **Purpose**: To file an initial application for a patent.\n- **Section Reference**: Section 6(7), as amended.\n- **Explanation**: This form is used to provide details of the applicant and invention, including information on any prior foreign applications (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 151).\n\n### b) **Form No. 2** – Statement of Grounds for Opposition\n- **Purpose**: To file an opposition to the grant of a patent.\n- **Section Reference**: Section 25.\n- **Explanation**: This form is used by interested parties to challenge the validity of a patent on specific grounds (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 151).\n\n### c) **Form No. 22** – Application for Registration as Patent Agent\n- **Purpose**: To apply for registration as a patent agent.\n- **Section Reference**: Section 126(1).\n- **Explanation**: This form is required for individuals who wish to register as patent agents and must be submitted along with other documents such as proof of qualifications (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\n## 3. Types of Fees Prescribed under the Patents Rules, 2003  \nFees are levied at various stages of the patent process:\n\n### a) **Application Fee**\n- **Purpose**: To file an initial application.\n- **Rule Reference**: Rule 14(1), Patents Rules, 2003.\n- **Explanation**: This is the first fee paid by the applicant to initiate the patent process (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\n### b) **Examination Fee**\n- **Purpose**: To request examination of a patent application.\n- **Rule Reference**: Rule 28(1), Patents Rules, 2003.\n- **Explanation**: This fee is necessary to proceed with the formal and substantive examination of the application (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\n### c) **Renewal Fee**\n- **Purpose**: To keep a patent in force after its initial grant.\n- **Rule Reference**: Rule 52(1), Patents Rules, 2003.\n- **Explanation**: This fee must be paid periodically to maintain the validity of the patent (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\n### d) **Compulsory Licence Fee**\n- **Purpose**: To obtain a compulsory licence for use of a patented invention.\n- **Rule Reference**: Rule 84(2), Patents Rules, 2003.\n- **Explanation**: This fee is required when an application for a compulsory licence is made under Section 84 (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\n## 4. Importance of Correct Forms and Fees  \n### a) **Legal Compliance**  \n- Failing to use the correct form or pay the required fee can result in the rejection of an application or the invalidation of a patent (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\n### b) **Procedural Efficiency**  \n- Correct forms and fees ensure that applications are processed smoothly without unnecessary delays.\n\n### c) **Cost Management**  \n- Understanding the fee structure helps applicants manage their financial resources effectively during the patent process (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\n## 5. Case Law on Forms and Fees – Verified Cases  \n**(No verified or candidate cases are available for this chapter. Do NOT invent a case name, year, or citation. If a case would normally support a point, write \"(verify this case with a case-law database)\" instead.)**\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist:\n- **Forms**: Must be used as prescribed under the Patents Rules, 2003.\n- **Fees**: Prescribed in various rules and schedules of the Patents Rules, 2003.\n- **Sections**: Section 6(7), Section 126, and others govern the use of forms.\n- **Importance**: Ensures legal compliance, procedural efficiency, and cost management.\n\nThis completes the detailed study notes on \"Forms and Fees — The Complete Map\" under the Patent Agent Examination.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 142 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 142. Fees.\n> (1) There shall be paid in respect of the grant of patents and\n> applications therefor, and in respect of other matters in relation\n> to the grant of patents under this Act, such fees as may be\n> prescribed by the Central Government.\n>\n> (2) Where a fee is payable in respect of the doing of an act by the\n> Controller, the Controller shall not do that act until the fee has\n> been paid.\n>\n> (3) Where a fee is payable in respect of the filing of a document at\n> the patent office, the fee shall be paid along with the document or\n>\n>  106\n>\n> within the prescribed time and the document shall be deemed not to\n> have been filed at the office if the fee has not been paid within\n> such time.\n>\n> (4) Where a principal patent is granted later than two years from\n> the date of filing of the application, the fees which have become\n> due in the meantime may be paid within a term of three months from\n> the date of the recording of the patent in the register or within\n> the extended period not later than nine months from the date of\n> recording.\n\n**Rule 7 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 7. Fees\n> (1) The fees payable under section 142 in respect of the grant of\n> patents and applications therefor, and in respect of other matters\n> for which fees are required to be payable under the Act shall be as\n> specified in the First Schedule.\n>\n>  11\n>\n> Provided that ten per cent additional fee shall be payable when the\n> applications for patent and other documents are filed through\n> physical mode, namely, in hard copy format:\n> Provided further that in the case of a small entity, or startup, or\n> educational institution, every document for which a fee has been\n> specified shall be accompanied by Form 28.\n>\n> (2) (a) (a) The fees payable under the Act or these rules may be\n> paid at the appropriate office either in cash or through electronic\n> means or may be sent by bank draft or banker’s cheque payable to the\n> Controller of Patents and drawn on a scheduled bank at the place\n> where the appropriate office is situated and if the draft or\n> banker’s cheque is sent by post, the fees shall be deemed to have\n> been paid on the date on which the draft or banker’s cheque has\n> actually reached the Controller.\n> (b) omitted\n> (c) Where a fee is payable in respect of a document, the entire fee\n> shall accompany the document.\n>\n> (3) In case an application processed by a natural person, startup,\n> small entity or educational institution is fully or partly\n> transferred to a person other than a natural person, startup, small\n> entity or educational institution, the difference, if any, in the\n> scale of fees between the fees charged from the natural person,\n> startup, small entity or educational institution and the fees\n> chargeable from the person other than a natural person, startup,\n> small entity or educational institution, shall be paid by the new\n> applicant along with the request for transfer.\n>\n> Explanation: Where a startup or small entity, having filed an\n> application for a patent, ceases to be a startup or small entity due\n> to the lapse of the period during which it is recognized by the\n> competent authority, or its turnover subsequently crosses the\n> financial threshold limit as notified by the competent authority, no\n> such difference in the scale of fees shall be payable.]\n>\n> (3A) shall be omitted;\n>\n> (3B) shall be omitted;\n>\n> (4) Fees once paid in respect of any proceeding shall not ordinarily\n>\n>  12\n>\n> be refunded irrespective of whether the proceeding has taken place\n> or not:\n> Provided that, if the Controller is satisfied that during the online\n> filing process, the fee was paid more than once for the same\n> proceeding, the excess fee shall be refunded.\n>\n> (4A) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-rule (4), upon the\n> withdrawal of an application in respect of which a request for\n> examination has been filed, but before issuance of first statement\n> of objection, the fee may be refunded to the extent prescribed in\n> the First Schedule on a request made by the applicant in Form 29.\n>\n> (5) (i) Subject to the approval of the Controller, any person may\n> deposit money in advance and request the Controller to realise any\n> fee payable by him from the said deposit and in such case the date\n> of the receipt of the request to realise the fee or the date on\n> which the request to realise the fee is deemed to have been\n> received, whichever is earlier, shall be taken as the date of\n> payment of the fee:\n> Provided that the requisite amount of money is available at the\n> credit of the person making such request.\n> (ii) Subject to the approval of the Controller, any person may\n> discontinue the deposit of money in advance and in such case the\n> … [truncated]\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n\n\n\n## Forms, and the rule that prescribes each\n\n| Form | Prescribed by | What the rule says |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Form 1** | Rule 20 | International applications designating or designating and electing India (1) An application corresponding to an international application filed under Patent Cooperation Treaty may be… |\n| **Form 2** | Rule 13 | Specifications (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall be made in Form 2. |\n| **Form 3** | Rule 12 | Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications (1) The statement and undertaking required to be filed by an applicant for a patent under sub-section (1) of section 8 shall be… |\n| **Form 4** | Rule 12 (also r. 13, r. 24B, r. 24C) | (5) Notwithstanding anything contained in these rules, the Controller may condone the delay or extend the time for filing Form 3 for a period up to three months upon a request made in… |\n| **Form 5** | Rule 13 | … specification, a declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall be filed in Form 5 with the complete specification or at any time before the expiration of one month from the… |\n| **Form 6** | Rule 34 (also r. 35, r. 36) | Manner in which a claim under section 20(1) shall be made (1) A claim under sub-section (1) of section 20 shall be made in Form 6. |\n| **Form 7** | Rule 55 (also r. 55A) | … Representation for opposition under sub-section (1) of section 25 shall be filed in Form 7(A) at the appropriate office with a copy to the applicant, and shall include a statement and… |\n| **Form 8** | Rule 67 (also r. 68) | Form of making a claim under section 28(3) (1) A claim under sub-section (3) of section 28 shall be made in Form 8, and shall be accompanied by a statement setting out the circumstances… |\n| **Form 8A** | Rule 70A | Certificate of inventorship (1) The Controller may issue a certificate of inventorship to an inventor in respect of a patent in force, on a request made by the inventor in Form-8A along… |\n| **Form 9** | Rule 24A | Request for publication A request for publication under sub-section (2) of section 11A shall be made in Form 9. |\n| **Form 10** | Rule 75 | Amendment of patent under section 44 An application under section 44 for the amendment of a patent shall be made in Form 10 along with substantiating evidence and be accompanied by the… |\n| **Form 11** | Rule 76 (also r. 77) | … application for directions under sub-section (1) of section 51 shall be made in Form 11 and shall be accompanied by a statement setting out the facts upon which the applicant relies. |\n| **Form 12** | Rule 63A (also r. 79) | Request made under section 26(1) Request under section 26(1) shall be made on Form 12 within three months from the date of the order of the Controller and shall be accompanied by a… |\n| **Form 13** | Rule 81 | … patent or a complete specification or any document related thereto shall be made in Form 13. |\n| **Form 14** | Rule 81 (also r. 85, r. 87, r. 98) | (b) Any person interested in opposing the application for amendment shall give a notice of opposition in Form 14 within three months from the date of publication of the application. |\n| **Form 15** | Rule 84 | Restoration of patents (1) An application for the restoration of a patent under section 60 shall be made in Form 15. |\n| **Form 16** | Rule 90 | Registration of title and interest in patents (1) An application referred to in sub-section (1) or sub-section (2) of section 69 shall be made in Form 16. |\n| **Form 17** | Rule 96 | An application to the Controller for an order under section 84, section 85, section 91 or section 92 or section 92A shall be in Form 17, or Form 19, as the case may be. |\n| **Form 18** | Rule 24B | … application (1)(i) A request for examination under section 11B shall be made in Form 18 within thirty-one months from the date of priority of the application or from the date of filing… |\n| **Form 18A** | Rule 24C | … of applications (1)An applicant may file a request for expedited examination in Form 18A along with the fee as specified in the first schedule only by electronic transmission duly… |\n| **Form 19** | Rule 96 | An application to the Controller for an order under section 84, section 85, section 91 or section 92 or section 92A shall be in Form 17, or Form 19, as the case may be. |\n| **Form 20** | Rule 100 | … and conditions of a licence which have been settled by the Controller shall be in Form 20 and shall state the facts relied upon by the applicant and the relief he seeks and shall be… |\n| **Form 21** | Rule 102 | … application for termination of compulsory licence under section 94(1) shall be made in Form 21 by the patentee or any other person deriving title or interest in the patent. |\n| **Form 22** | Rule 109 (also r. 112) | Application for registration of patent agents (1) Every person who desires to be registered as a patent agent shall make an application in Form 22. |\n| **Form 23** | Rule 117 | … register of patent agents under sub-section (2) of section 130 shall be made in Form 23 within two months from the date of such removal. |\n| **Form 24** | Rule 130 | … his decision under clause (f) of sub-section (1) of section 77 shall be made in Form 24 within one month from the date of communication of such decision to the applicant or within such… |\n| **Form 25** | Rule 71 | Permission for making patent application outside India under section 39 (1) The request for permission for making patent application outside India shall be made in Form 25. |\n| **Form 26** | Rule 135 | … authorisation of an agent for the purposes of the Act and these rules shall be filed in Form 26 or in the form of a power of attorney within a period of three months from the date of… |\n| **Form 27** | Rule 131 | … furnished by every patentee and every licensee under sub-section (2) of section 146 in Form 27 which shall be duly verified by the patentee or the licencee or his authorised agent. |\n| **Form 28** | Rule 7 | … institution, every document for which a fee has been specified shall be accompanied by Form 28. |\n| **Form 29** | Rule 7 (also r. 26) | … the extent prescribed in the First Schedule on a request made by the applicant in Form 29. |\n| **Form 30** | Rule 8 | (2) Where no Form is so specified for any purpose, the applicant may use Form 30 specified in the Second Schedule. |\n| **Form 31** | Rule 29A | Grace period An application to avail the period specified under section 31 shall be filed in Form 31, along with the fees specified in the First Schedule. |\n\n_Every row is quoted from the Patents Rules, 2003 (or the Act). A form no provision mentions is absent rather than guessed._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the significance of Forms and Fees under the Patents Act, 1970 and the Patents Rules, 2003.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue** — What is the legal importance of using correct forms and paying prescribed fees during the patent process?\n\n**Rule** — Under **Section 142(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970, fees are required for various actions related to patents. The **Patents Rules, 2003**, prescribe specific forms for different stages of the patent application and management process (e.g., Form No. 1, Form No. 2, etc.). These rules ensure uniformity in procedures and compliance with legal requirements. Failing to use correct forms or pay fees can result in rejection of applications or invalidation of patents, as noted by **Dr. Sheetal Chopra** on page 679.\n\n**Application** — The use of prescribed forms is mandatory under the Patents Rules, 2003. For instance, an application for a patent must be made using **Form No. 1**, which includes details about the applicant and any prior foreign applications (as per **Section 6(7)**). Similarly, the submission of **Form No. 2** is required when filing a statement of grounds for opposition under **Section 25**. Regarding fees, the **Patents Rules, 2003**, specify different types such as application fee (**Rule 14(1)**), examination fee (**Rule 28(1)**), renewal fee (**Rule 52(1)**), and compulsory licence fee (**Rule 84(2)**). These fees are essential to ensure that the Controller of Patents can perform administrative functions, conduct examinations, and maintain the patent register. Failure to pay these fees on time may lead to the application being deemed not filed or the patent becoming invalid.\n\n**Conclusion** — The correct use of forms and payment of prescribed fees under the Patents Act, 1970 and the Patents Rules, 2003 are crucial for ensuring legal compliance, procedural efficiency, and cost management in the patent process. These requirements help maintain a structured and transparent system for granting and managing patents.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook the direct link between non-compliance with forms and fees and the rejection of applications or invalidation of patents. It is important to emphasize this legal consequence clearly.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the role of Form No. 1 under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue** — What is the purpose of **Form No. 1**, and what are its legal implications?\n\n**Rule** — **Form No. 1** is used for filing an initial application for a patent under **Section 6(7)**, as amended. It requires applicants to provide information about the invention, including details regarding any prior foreign applications (as stated by **Dr. Sheetal Chopra**, p. 151). This form is also governed by **Rule 20** of the Patents Rules, 2003, which prescribes its format and content.\n\n**Application** — When an applicant files a patent application in India, they must use **Form No. 1** to submit details such as the title of the invention, the name and address of the inventor and applicant, and any information about prior foreign applications. This is essential for ensuring that the Controller can properly assess the novelty of the invention. Failure to provide accurate information may lead to delays or rejection of the application.\n\n**Conclusion** — **Form No. 1** plays a critical role in initiating the patent process by providing necessary details about the applicant and the invention. It ensures compliance with legal requirements under the Patents Act, 1970 and the Patents Rules, 2003.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students confuse **Form No. 1** with other forms like **Form No. 2**, but it is important to highlight that **Form No. 1** specifically deals with the initial application for a patent.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the different types of fees prescribed under the Patents Rules, 2003? Explain their significance.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue** — Identify and explain the various types of fees prescribed by the **Patents Rules, 2003**, and discuss their importance.\n\n**Rule** — The **Patents Rules, 2003** prescribe several types of fees, including **application fee** (**Rule 14(1)**), **examination fee** (**Rule 28(1)**), **renewal fee** (**Rule 52(1)**), and **compulsory licence fee** (**Rule 84(2)**). These fees are levied at different stages of the patent process to cover administrative, examination, and maintenance costs (as explained by **Dr. Sheetal Chopra**, p. 679).\n\n**Application** — The **application fee** is paid when an applicant files a patent application using **Form No. 1**. This allows the Controller to initiate the process of examining the invention for novelty and non-obviousness. The **examination fee** is required before the Controller proceeds with substantive examination under **Section 11B**, ensuring that the application meets legal standards. The **renewal fee** must be paid periodically to keep a patent in force, as outlined in **Rule 52(1)**. Lastly, the **compulsory licence fee** is required when an application for compulsory licensing is made under **Section 84**, allowing third parties to use patented inventions under certain conditions.\n\n**Conclusion** — The fees prescribed by the **Patents Rules, 2003** are essential for maintaining the efficiency and integrity of the patent system. They ensure that administrative processes are funded, examinations are conducted thoroughly, and patents remain valid over time.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to distinguish between different types of fees or their specific purposes. It is important to clearly link each fee with its corresponding rule and stage in the patent process.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Forms and Fees — The Complete Map\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 142 | **TIE-RHINO** | A tied rhino stamps papers, form types clearly marked. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Form 1 application, Form 2 specification, Form 3 statement and undertaking, Form 5 declaration of inventorship - which provision?  (picture: TIE-RHINO)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 6(7) | Requires foreign application info in Form No. 1 | Foreign app details in form\n- Section 57 | Allows amendments before Controller | Amendment forms needed\n- Section 126 | Prescribes format for patent agent registration forms | Form 22 for agents\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid using incorrect form numbers to prevent application rejection  \n- Use exact section and rule references in answers for clarity  \n- Always mention the purpose of each form when describing it","storyEN":"### The Story of the Patent Agent in Hyderabad\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, where the Charminar’s shadow stretched over a city teeming with innovation, a young patent agent named Ravi found himself at a crossroads. His client, a startup called *TechNova*, had developed a revolutionary solar panel and was eager to secure a patent. But there were rules — many of them — and every step required precision.\n\nRavi began by recalling the first rule: **Section 6(7)**. He knew that if *TechNova* had filed for patents in other countries, they needed to submit information on those applications using **Form No. 1**. As he filled out this form, he remembered a detail from his notes — any failure to do so could lead to rejection or invalidation of the patent. The number **6(7)** was not just a rule; it was a lifeline.\n\nNext came the application fee. Ravi knew that under **Rule 14(1)** of the Patents Rules, 2003, the first step in any patent process required this payment. He checked the amount carefully and made sure to include it with **Form No. 1** before submitting it at the patent office.\n\nAs weeks passed, the application was reviewed, but Ravi knew that if *TechNova* wanted to make changes — like adding more details about their invention — they would need to use **Form No. 2**, and this required a new fee under **Rule 28(1)** for examination. He reminded his client of this in time so the process wouldn’t stall.\n\nThen came the day when *TechNova* received an unexpected challenge: someone had filed an opposition against their patent. Ravi knew immediately that he would need to use **Form No. 2** again, but not for submission — now it was for a **Statement of Grounds for Opposition**, as per **Section 25**. He prepared the form with care and ensured all legal arguments were clearly laid out.\n\nMonths later, *TechNova* had their patent granted. But Ravi knew that this was only the beginning. Every year, they would need to pay a **renewal fee** under **Rule 52(1)**, or risk losing their rights entirely. He set up reminders and made sure that the team at *TechNova* understood the importance of these payments.\n\nOne day, Ravi decided he wanted to become a registered patent agent himself. That meant passing an exam — but more importantly, it required him to submit **Form No. 22**, as per **Section 126(1)**. He gathered his documents, including proof of qualifications and experience, and submitted the form with the appropriate fee.\n\nThe journey was long, but Ravi knew that every step had been guided by numbers — from **Form No. 1** to **Rule 52(1)** — each one ensuring that *TechNova*’s invention would be protected for years to come.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 6(7) | Requirement to submit information on foreign applications using Form No. 1 | Failure to do so can result in rejection or invalidation of a patent |\n| Section 25 | Opposition to the grant of a patent requires submission of Form No. 2 | Interested parties may challenge the validity of a patent based on specific grounds |\n| Section 126(1) | Registration as a patent agent requires submission of Form No. 22 | Individuals must submit proof of qualifications along with the application form |\n| Rule 14(1) | Application fee is required to initiate the patent process | First fee paid by the applicant to file an initial application |\n| Rule 28(1) | Examination fee is needed for formal and substantive examination | Required to proceed with the evaluation of a patent application |\n| Rule 52(1) | Renewal fee must be paid periodically to maintain the validity of a patent | Failure to pay results in the loss of patent rights |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for \"Forms and Fees — The Complete Map\"\n\n---\n\n**1. Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n\n- **F.E.E.S. (Forms, Entries, Expenses, Specifications):**\n  - *F*orms: Use **Form 22** for registration of patent agents (Section 126).\n  - *E*ntries: Registering assignments and transmissions in the register (Section 68).\n  - *E*xpenses: Renewal fees and other prescribed fees (Section 144).\n  - *S*pecifications: Provisional and complete specifications (Section 9).\n\n---\n\n**2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 126**: Think of a **\"Patent Agent Exam\"** — this is where the exam for patent agents is described, including forms and qualifying papers.\n  \n- **Section 68**: Imagine an **\"Assignment Register\"** — this section deals with assignments needing to be in writing and registered.\n\n- **Section 9**: Picture a **\"Specimen Book\"** — this section covers specifications (provisional and complete).\n\n- **Section 144**: Visualize a **\"Private Report\"** — examiner’s reports are not public; only accessible by court order.\n\n---\n\n**3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Form 22 vs. Form 23:**  \n  *Remember:* **Form 22 is for registration of patent agents**, while **Form 23 was used previously but has been substituted** (Section 126).\n\n- **Patent Agent Exam Papers I and II:**  \n  *Remember:* **Paper I tests knowledge of the Patents Act, Rules, Designs Act, etc.**, whereas **Paper II focuses on drafting specifications and other documents**.\n\n- **Compulsory Licence vs. Voluntary Licence:**  \n  *Remember:* **Compulsory Licence is granted by the Controller under Section 84**, while **Voluntary Licence is agreed upon between parties without legal intervention**.\n\n---\n\nThese techniques are designed to help you recall key sections, forms, and distinctions in \"Forms and Fees — The Complete Map\" accurately for exams.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Form 3 is a statement and undertaking under Section 8 of the Patents Act. It confirms the applicant's details and compliance with patent requirements.","question":"What is the purpose of Form 3?"},{"answer":"Fees can be refunded if the Controller confirms duplicate payment for the same proceeding or if an application is withdrawn before the first objection, as per Rule 8(4) and (4A).","question":"Under what condition can fees be refunded after payment?"},{"answer":"If a startup stops being a startup due to exceeding financial thresholds, Rule 8(3B) states no fee difference is payable for the same application.","question":"Why is no fee difference payable when a startup ceases to qualify?"},{"answer":"Form 30 from the Second Schedule is used when no other form is specified for a particular purpose, as per Rule 8(2).","question":"What form is used when no specific form is prescribed?"},{"answer":"The new applicant must pay the fee difference between small entity and general rates with the transfer request, as per Rule 8(3A).","question":"What happens to fees paid by a small entity if it transfers to a non-small entity?"},{"answer":"Yes, under Rule 8(5)(i), fees can be paid in advance with Controller approval, and the payment date is deemed the request date, provided funds are available.","question":"Can fees be paid in advance, and what are the conditions?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 87 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Procedure for dealing with applications under sections 84 and”.","options":["Section 69","Section 4","Section 11","Section 87"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Procedure for dealing with applications under sections 84 and?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 87 is headed “Procedure for dealing with applications under sections 84 and”.","options":["Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents","Determination of certain contracts","Procedure for dealing with applications under sections 84 and","Register of patents and particulars to be entered therein"],"question":"Section 87 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 156 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Patent to bind Government”.","options":["Section 135","Section 156","Section 108","Section 149"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Patent to bind Government?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 156 is headed “Patent to bind Government”.","options":["Patent to bind Government","Order for licence to operate as a deed between parties","Term of patents of addition","Surrender of patents"],"question":"Section 156 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 99 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Manner of publication of the revocation order”.","options":["Rule 5","Rule 19A","Rule 24C","Rule 99"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Manner of publication of the revocation order?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 99 is headed “Manner of publication of the revocation order”.","options":["International search report","Refusal to recognise as patent agent","Amendment of the complete specification in case of","Manner of publication of the revocation order"],"question":"Rule 99 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 136 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Special provisions relating to convention applications”.","options":["Section 67","Section 139","Section 136","Section 78"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Special provisions relating to convention applications?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 136 is headed “Special provisions relating to convention applications”.","options":["Amendment of patent granted to deceased applicant","Grant of patent to true and first inventor where it has been","Special provisions relating to convention applications","Controller and other officers"],"question":"Section 136 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 102 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Application for termination of compulsory licence under”.","options":["Rule 92","Rule 90","Rule 102","Rule 101"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Application for termination of compulsory licence under?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 102 is headed “Application for termination of compulsory licence under”.","options":["Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)","Manner of publication of the revocation order","Further evidence to be left with the leave of the","Application for termination of compulsory licence under"],"question":"Rule 102 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 83 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “General principles applicable to working of patented inventions”.","options":["Section 77","Section 131","Section 83","Section 21"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: General principles applicable to working of patented inventions?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 83 is headed “General principles applicable to working of patented inventions”.","options":["Register to be open for inspection","General principles applicable to working of patented inventions","Opposition to the patent","In cases of “obtaining” Controller may treat the patent as the"],"question":"Section 83 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"The Central Government prescribes such fees.","options":["a) The Patent Office","b) The Controller","c) The Central Government","d) The State Government"],"question":"Who prescribes the fees payable in respect of the grant of patents and applications therefor under Section 142(1) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"The Controller shall not do the act until the fee is paid.","options":["a) Proceed with the act immediately","b) Do the act after a 30-day notice","c) Not do the act until the fee is paid","d) Refer the matter to the Central Government"],"question":"What action must the Controller take if a fee is payable in respect of an act they are required to perform, as per Section 142(2)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The document is deemed not to have been filed if the fee is not paid within the prescribed time.","options":["a) The document is deemed filed but with a delay penalty","b) The document is deemed not to have been filed","c) The fee is waived automatically","d) The applicant is notified to pay the fee within 15 days"],"question":"What happens if a fee for filing a document at the patent office is not paid within the prescribed time, as per Section 142(3)?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The fees may be paid within three months from the date of recording the patent or within nine months from the recording date.","options":["a) Within three months from the date of recording the patent","b) Within six months from the application filing date","c) Within one year from the grant date","d) Only upon request to the Controller"],"question":"Under Section 142(4), if a principal patent is granted later than two years from the application filing date, within what period may the fees due during the intervening period be paid?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The document is deemed not to have been filed if the fee is not paid within the prescribed time.","options":["a) The application is published automatically","b) The document is deemed not to have been filed","c) The fee is adjusted to 50% of the original amount","d) The Controller initiates legal proceedings"],"question":"Which of the following is a consequence of not paying a fee for filing a document at the patent office, as per Section 142(3)?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"The extended period for payment is not later than nine months from the date of recording.","options":["a) Three months from the application filing date","b) Six months from the grant date","c) Nine months from the date of recording the patent","d) Twelve months from the application filing date"],"question":"Section 142(4) allows an extended period for paying fees due before the grant of a principal patent. What is the maximum period allowed for such payment?"}]},"s1p_15":{"notes":"### Jurisdiction of Patent Offices and Filing Routes\n\n---\n\n#### **Definition**\n\nThe term \"jurisdiction\" in the context of patent offices refers to the geographical and legal authority of a patent office to process, examine, and grant patents within its defined area. In India, this is governed by *The Patents Act, 1970*, which outlines the procedures for filing applications, the roles of the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks (referred to as \"Controller\" in **Section 2**), and other relevant authorities.\n\nFiling routes refer to the various methods through which an applicant can submit a patent application. These include domestic filings, international filings under treaties such as the *Patent Cooperation Treaty* (**Section 135**, referred to as \"convention application\"), and filings in specific jurisdictions like those under the *Paris Convention*, etc.\n\n---\n\n#### **Key Concepts**\n\n- **Jurisdiction of the Patent Office**:  \n  The patent office in India, as defined in **Section 74** of the Patents Act, is responsible for managing all aspects of patent applications within the country. It includes processing applications, conducting examinations, and granting patents under the provisions of the Act.\n\n- **Filing Routes**:\n  - **Domestic Filing**: Applications filed directly with the Indian Patent Office.\n  - **International Filing via PCT (Patent Cooperation Treaty)**: Under **Section 135**, an international application can be made by virtue of the *Patent Cooperation Treaty* (**\"international application\"**, as defined in **Section 2(oa)**).\n  - **Convention Application**: Applications filed under the *Paris Convention*, where an applicant may claim priority based on a previous filing in another member country.\n  - **Priority Dates**: As outlined in **Section 11(w)**, the \"priority date\" is determined by the first filing of the complete specification. This is crucial for establishing novelty and avoiding prior art.\n\n- **Controller's Role**:\n  The Controller has significant jurisdiction under various sections such as **Sections 25 to 32**, which deal with oppositions, anticipation, and other legal challenges to patent grants. The Controller also conducts examinations of applications (as per **Section 12**) and may make orders regarding the division or amendment of applications (**Sections 16 and 15**).\n\n---\n\n#### **Legal Provisions**\n\n- **Section 2(oa)**: Defines an \"international application\" as one made in accordance with the *Patent Cooperation Treaty*.\n- **Section 135**: Provides for the filing of a convention application, which allows applicants to claim priority based on earlier filings in other countries.\n- **Section 11(w)**: Defines the term \"priority date,\" which is essential for determining the novelty and non-obviousness of an invention.\n- **Section 2** (Definitions): The Controller is defined as the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks. This individual has significant authority in managing patent applications and granting or rejecting patents.\n\n---\n\n#### **Why the Law Is Structured This Way**\n\nAccording to **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45**, the jurisdiction of the patent office is structured to ensure a centralized and uniform system for processing applications across India. The law allows for international filings through treaties like the PCT to facilitate global protection of inventions without requiring separate filings in each country.\n\nThe role of the Controller is critical because they are responsible not only for examining applications but also for making decisions on oppositions, granting patents, and ensuring that all legal requirements under **Sections 3 and 4** (non-patentable inventions) are met. This centralized control helps maintain consistency and fairness in the patent granting process.\n\nAs noted in **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679**, the qualifying examination for patent agents includes a written test on the *Patents Act* and *Rules*, which ensures that those who practice before the Controller are well-versed in the legal framework governing jurisdiction and filing procedures.\n\n---\n\n#### **Filing Routes and Their Implications**\n\n1. **Domestic Filing**:\n   - Applications must be filed with the Indian Patent Office.\n   - The applicant can be an individual, a company, or any other entity defined as \"person\" under **Section 2(s)**.\n   - The application may include provisional or complete specifications (**Sections 9 and 10**) and must comply with all procedural requirements outlined in **Sections 6 to 24**.\n\n2. **International Filing via PCT (Section 135)**:\n   - This route is particularly useful for applicants seeking protection in multiple countries.\n   - It allows the applicant to file a single international application, which can then be converted into national applications in various jurisdictions.\n   - The patent office in India has specific procedures for handling such applications, including the requirement to submit priority documents and comply with deadlines (**Rules 13(8) and Rule 24(A)**).\n\n3. **Convention Application**:\n   - This is available under the *Paris Convention* and allows applicants to claim priority based on an earlier filing in another member state.\n   - The application must be filed within a specific period (usually 12 months), as outlined in **Section 11(w)**.\n\n---\n\n#### **Importance of Jurisdiction and Filing Routes**\n\nThe jurisdiction of the patent office is crucial for ensuring that all applications are processed uniformly across India. It also helps prevent overlapping or conflicting patents, which could lead to disputes over ownership or rights.\n\nFiling routes such as PCT and convention filings have become increasingly important in today's globalized economy. They allow inventors and companies to protect their inventions internationally while reducing the administrative burden of filing separate applications in each country.\n\nAs highlighted in **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 383**, Paper II of the Patent Agent Examination includes questions on these topics, emphasizing the importance of understanding jurisdiction and filing procedures for exam candidates.\n\n---\n\n#### **Conclusion**\n\nUnderstanding the jurisdiction of patent offices and various filing routes is essential not only for patent agents but also for inventors and companies seeking to protect their intellectual property. The legal framework provided in *The Patents Act, 1970* ensures a structured and efficient process for granting patents while allowing flexibility through international treaties like the PCT.\n\nIt is important for candidates preparing for the Patent Agent Examination to thoroughly study **Sections 2, 11(w), 135**, and related rules such as **Rule 13(8)** and **Rule 24(A)**. These provisions are frequently tested in both written exams and viva voce examinations.\n\n---\n\n#### **References**\n\n- *The Patents Act, 1970* (Bare Act Reference Block)\n- **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra**, p. 45, 679, 383\n- No verified cases are available for this topic. If a case is referenced in exam preparation, it should be \"(verify this case with a case-law database)\".\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 74 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 74. Patent office and its branches.\n> (1) For the purposes of this Act, there shall be an office which\n> shall be known as the patent office.\n>\n> (2) The Central Government may, by notification in the Official\n> Gazette, specify the name of the Patent Office.\n>\n> (3) The head office of the patent office shall be at such place as\n> the Central Government may specify, and for the purpose of\n> facilitating, the registration of patents there may be established,\n> at such other places as the Central Government may think fit, branch\n> offices of the patent office.\n>\n> (4) There shall be a seal of the patent office.\n>\n>  63\n>\n> 75. Restriction on employees of patent office as to right or\n> interest in patents.\n> All officers and employees of the patents office shall be incapable,\n> during the period for which they hold their appointments, to acquire\n> or take, directly or indirectly, except by inheritance or bequest,\n> any right or interest in any patent issued by that office.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Explain the jurisdiction of the patent office under The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is the geographical and legal authority of the Indian Patent Office as defined by *The Patents Act, 1970*, and what are its key responsibilities?\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 74** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, the patent office is established for the purposes of managing all aspects of patent applications within India. The Central Government has the authority to specify the location of the head office and may set up branch offices at other places as deemed necessary. Furthermore, **Section 2** defines the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks (referred to as \"Controller\") as the key authority responsible for granting or rejecting patents.\n\nThe jurisdiction of the patent office is also outlined in **Sections 12 to 32**, which cover the examination process, oppositions, and legal challenges to patent grants. These sections ensure that all applications are processed uniformly across India under a centralized system.\n\n**Application**  \nThe Indian Patent Office operates within the territorial boundaries of India and has no jurisdiction over foreign applications unless they are filed through international treaties such as the *Patent Cooperation Treaty* (PCT) or the *Paris Convention*. The Controller, being the central authority, is responsible for examining each application to ensure compliance with **Sections 3 and 4**, which outline what constitutes non-patentable inventions.\n\nFor example, if an applicant files a domestic patent application under **Section 9** (provisional specification), it must be processed by the Indian Patent Office. If the same invention is filed internationally via PCT under **Section 135**, the Indian Patent Office will manage the conversion of that international application into a national one, ensuring all procedural requirements are met.\n\nIn cases where there is an opposition to a patent grant, the Controller has the authority to review and decide based on the provisions in **Sections 25 to 32**. This ensures that only valid and non-obvious inventions are granted patents.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe jurisdiction of the Indian Patent Office under *The Patents Act, 1970* is confined to India but includes the processing of international applications through treaties like PCT and Paris Convention. The Controller plays a central role in ensuring uniformity and legal compliance in patent granting procedures.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse the jurisdiction of the Indian Patent Office with that of foreign offices or fail to mention the role of the Controller in managing both domestic and international applications.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the filing routes available under *The Patents Act, 1970*, including the provisions related to convention and PCT filings.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat are the various methods for filing a patent application in India, particularly with regard to international treaties like the *Patent Cooperation Treaty* (PCT) and *Paris Convention*?\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 135**, applicants may file a convention application under the *Paris Convention*, which allows them to claim priority based on an earlier filing in another member state. This is supported by **Section 11(w)**, which defines the \"priority date\" as the first filing of the complete specification.\n\nThe PCT route is also available under **Section 2(oa)** and **Section 135**, where an international application can be filed via the *Patent Cooperation Treaty*. This provision allows applicants to file a single application that can later be converted into national applications in various jurisdictions, including India. Such applications must comply with procedural rules like **Rule 13(8)** and **Rule 24(A)**.\n\n**Application**  \nFor example, if an inventor files a patent application in the United States under the *Paris Convention*, they may file a convention application in India within 12 months of the initial filing, claiming priority. This ensures that the Indian Patent Office treats the date of the first filing as the priority date for determining novelty.\n\nSimilarly, an applicant can use the PCT route by submitting an international application with the World Intellectual Property Organization (WIPO), which is then processed under **Section 135** and converted into a national application in India. This method reduces administrative burden and allows global protection of inventions without separate filings in each country.\n\nUnder both filing routes, applicants must submit priority documents and comply with deadlines to ensure their applications are not rejected on procedural grounds. These provisions aim to facilitate international patent protection while maintaining legal compliance within the Indian framework.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe *Patents Act, 1970* provides multiple filing routes for patent applications in India, including domestic filings, convention applications under the *Paris Convention*, and PCT-based international applications. Each route is governed by specific provisions that ensure procedural fairness and global protection of inventions.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the importance of priority dates under **Section 11(w)** or fail to explain how **Rule 24(A)** applies in PCT filings. Emphasizing these points will strengthen answers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What is the significance of the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks in the patent granting process?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat role does the Controller play in managing patent applications, oppositions, and legal challenges?\n\n**Rule**  \nThe Controller is defined under **Section 2** as the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks. The Controller has significant jurisdiction over the entire patent granting process, including examining applications (**Section 12**), handling oppositions (**Sections 25 to 32**), and making decisions on the division or amendment of applications (**Sections 16 and 15**).\n\nThe Controller also ensures that all applications comply with **Sections 3 and 4**, which outline what is non-patentable. This centralized authority helps maintain consistency in patent granting procedures across India.\n\n**Application**  \nFor instance, if an applicant files a complete specification under **Section 10**, the Controller must examine it for compliance with legal requirements such as novelty and non-obviousness. If another party files an opposition under **Section 25**, the Controller is responsible for reviewing that opposition and deciding whether to grant or reject the patent.\n\nThe Controller also has the authority to issue orders regarding the division of applications, which may occur if a single application covers multiple inventions (**Section 16**). This ensures that each invention receives proper examination and protection.\n\nIn cases where an applicant seeks to amend their specification, the Controller must assess whether such amendments are permissible under **Section 15**. These provisions ensure that only valid and legally compliant applications receive patents.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe Controller plays a central role in managing patent granting procedures by examining applications, handling oppositions, and ensuring legal compliance. This ensures uniformity, fairness, and efficiency in the patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention the specific sections (**Sections 25–32**) that govern oppositions or the importance of **Section 16** regarding application division. Highlighting these points will improve accuracy.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Jurisdiction of Patent Offices and Filing Routes\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 74 | **CAR** | CAR crosses borders, each wheel stamped with a patent office's mark |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **68-78** - assignments, registration, rectification\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Four appropriate offices and their territorial jurisdiction - which provision?  (picture: CAR)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 2 | Defines Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks | Controller's official title\n- Section 11(w) | Defines priority date based on first filing | First filing sets the clock\n- Section 135 | Allows convention application under Paris Convention | Priority claim across borders\n- Section 74 | Establishes patent office and its branches | Central and branch offices\n- Section 2(oa) | Defines international application via PCT | Global filing through PCT\n- Section 12 | Controller examines applications | Examining officer's role\n- Section 15 & 16 | Orders for division or amendment of applications | Splitting or editing apps\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Confusing priority date with filing date\n- Examiners expect: Clear understanding of PCT and Paris Convention routes\n- Structure tip: Always cite the correct section for definitions (e.g., Section 2)","storyEN":"### The Story of the Patent Office in Hyderabad\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, where the old city meets the new, a young inventor named Ravi had an idea that could change the world — a solar-powered water purifier. He knew he needed to file for a patent, but he was confused about *where* and *how*. His friend, Anjali, who worked at a law firm in the city, decided to help him understand the process.\n\nThey visited the Patent Office, located near Charminar, where they met an official named Mr. Dinesh, who explained that the **Patent Office** was established under **Section 74**, and its head office was in Delhi, but it had branches across India — including one in Hyderabad. Mr. Dinesh said, “The patent office is like a central library for inventions. It’s where we process your application, examine it, and decide if you get the patent.”\n\nRavi asked, “But I want to file internationally too. Can I do that?” Anjali smiled and said, “Yes, there are different *filing routes*. First, you can file domestically with us in India under **Section 74**. But if you want to protect your invention globally, you have the option of filing through the Patent Cooperation Treaty — or PCT.”\n\nMr. Dinesh nodded and added, “That’s covered under **Section 135**, which allows for a *convention application*. It means that if you’ve filed in another country, like the US or Japan, you can claim priority here in India within twelve months of your first filing — as per **Section 11(w)**.”\n\nRavi was impressed. “So I don’t have to file separately in every country?” he asked.\n\n“No,” Anjali said. “You can use the PCT route and then convert that into national applications across multiple countries. But you must be careful with *priority dates*, because if you miss your window, your patent might not qualify as novel.”\n\nMr. Dinesh handed Ravi a brochure and said, “And remember, once we get your application, it will be examined by the **Controller**, who is defined in **Section 2**. He has authority over everything — from checking for prior art to handling oppositions under **Sections 25 to 32**.”\n\nRavi was now confident but still had one question: “What if I make a mistake in my application?”\n\nAnjali said, “You can amend or divide your application under **Sections 16 and 15**, which allow for flexibility. But you must be precise with your *specifications* — provisional or complete — as outlined in **Sections 9 and 10**.”\n\nRavi thanked them all and left the office feeling prepared. He filed his application domestically, then later used the PCT route to file internationally. His journey was smooth because he understood the role of the patent office under **Section 74**, the importance of priority dates in **Section 11(w)**, and how the Controller had authority over everything from examination to oppositions.\n\nIn the end, Ravi’s invention became a global success — all thanks to his understanding of jurisdiction and filing routes, which were clearly defined by the **Patents Act, 1970**.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 74 | Jurisdiction of the patent office in India | There shall be a patent office established under this section, with branches across the country. |\n| Section 135 | International filing via PCT (Patent Cooperation Treaty) | Allows for convention applications and international filings that can be converted into national applications. |\n| Section 11(w) | Priority dates in patent applications | The priority date is determined by the first filing of the complete specification, crucial for establishing novelty. |\n| Section 2 | Definition of the Controller | The Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks has significant authority over patent grants and oppositions. |\n| Sections 25 to 32 | Oppositions and legal challenges | These sections outline procedures for opposing a patent grant based on anticipation or other legal grounds. |\n| Sections 16 and 15 | Amendments and division of applications | Allow applicants to divide or amend their application during the examination process. |\n| Sections 9 and 10 | Provisional and complete specifications | Define the requirements for submitting either type of specification when filing a patent application. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for \"Jurisdiction of Patent Offices and Filing Routes\"\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n- **\"FIPR\"** – *Forms, International Applications, Prior Art, and Registration*  \n  This helps recall the key elements involved in filing a patent application: the forms required (e.g., Form 22), international routes like PCT applications, prior art considerations, and registration processes.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-Anchors for Sections:**\n\n- **Section 6**: *“Who can apply?”*  \n  Picture a **\"key\"** in your hand — this section unlocks who is eligible to file an application (e.g., inventor or assignee).\n\n- **Section 8**: *“Statement and Undertaking”*  \n  Imagine a **\"contractor\"** signing a form. This section deals with the formal declaration required when filing.\n\n- **Section 11A**: *“Publication of Applications”*  \n  Think of a **\"newspaper\"** being printed — this is about making the application publicly available after filing.\n\n- **Section 25**: *“Opposition to Patent”*  \n  Picture a **\"courtroom\"** with people arguing. This section allows third parties to challenge a patent before it’s granted.\n\n- **Section 127 & 129**: *“Patent Agent Powers”*  \n  Visualize a **\"lawyer\"** working on your behalf — these sections define the scope of what a patent agent can do (e.g., file, prosecute, advise).\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Provisional vs. Complete Specification**:  \n  *A provisional is like a “draft” or “outline,” while a complete specification is the full and final version — think of it as the \"final exam\" versus the \"study guide.\"*\n\n- **Patent Agent vs. Advocate**:  \n  *A patent agent can file and prosecute applications but cannot represent in court; an advocate may appear before courts but is not authorized to handle all patent office matters.*\n\n- **Priority Date vs. Filing Date**:  \n  *The priority date comes from a previous application (e.g., PCT or convention), while the filing date is when the current application was submitted — think of it as “claiming earlier rights” versus “the actual submission time.”*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall important concepts related to **jurisdiction and filing routes** in patent law efficiently.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The WIPO DAS system securely exchanges priority documents between IP offices without additional fees. It allows access via a DAS code.","question":"What is the WIPO DAS system used for?"},{"answer":"Fees may be paid in cash, electronically, or via bank draft/cheque payable to the Controller of Patents. Rule 7(a) of the Patents Rules, 2003.","question":"How can fees be paid at the appropriate office?"},{"answer":"Certified copies must be filed within 31 months from the priority date during national phase entry.","question":"When must certified copies of priority documents be filed?"},{"answer":"To account for the difference in fee scales between entities, as specified in Rule 7(3) of the Patents Rules, 2003.","question":"Why must a new applicant pay a fee difference upon transfer?"},{"answer":"An English translation of the application and amended documents must be filed before the 31-month deadline.","question":"What is required for an international patent application not in English?"},{"answer":"A base fee covers 30 pages and 10 claims, with additional fees for each page over 30 and each claim over 10.","question":"What is the fee structure for claims and pages in a patent application?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 74 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Patent office and its branches”.","options":["Section 140","Section 56","Section 74","Section 118"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Patent office and its branches?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 74 is headed “Patent office and its branches”.","options":["Publication of applications","Patent office and its branches","Savings respecting disclosure to Government","Rules to be placed before Parliament"],"question":"Section 74 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 74 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Form of patent”.","options":["Rule 103A","Rule 74","Rule 24","Rule 124"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Form of patent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 74 is headed “Form of patent”.","options":["Fees payable to Examining Authority","Power to relax","Inclusion of the name of any other person in the roll of","Form of patent"],"question":"Rule 74 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The head office of the patent office shall be at such place as the Central Government may specify","options":["a) At a place specified by the State Government","b) At a place specified by the Central Government","c) At the location of the first patent applicant","d) At the headquarters of the Indian Patent Office"],"question":"Where is the head office of the patent office located according to Section 74(3)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Central Government may, by notification in the Official Gazette, specify the name of the Patent Office.","options":["a) The Patent Office itself","b) The Central Government","c) The State Government","d) The Indian Patent Agent Council"],"question":"Which authority has the power to specify the name of the Patent Office under Section 74(2)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"There shall be a seal of the patent office.","options":["a) The seal must be kept confidential","b) There shall be a seal of the patent office","c) The seal is only used for international patents","d) No seal is required for the patent office"],"question":"What is the requirement regarding the seal of the patent office as per Section 74(4)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"All officers and employees of the patents office shall be incapable, during the period for which they hold their appointments, to acquire or take, directly or indirectly, except by inheritance or bequest, any right or interest in any patent issued by that office.","options":["a) Applying for patents in foreign jurisdictions","b) Acquiring rights or interests in patents issued by the office, except by inheritance","c) Working on patent-related research","d) Serving on patent examination panels"],"question":"Which of the following is prohibited for employees of the patent office under Section 75?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"during the period for which they hold their appointments","options":["a) During the period of their employment","b) For five years after retirement","c) During the period for which they hold their appointments","d) For the duration of the patent's validity"],"question":"During which period are employees of the patent office restricted from acquiring rights in patents issued by the office?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"except by inheritance or bequest","options":["a) Through direct purchase","b) By inheritance or bequest","c) By assignment from another employee","d) Through employment contracts"],"question":"Which of the following is a valid method for employees of the patent office to acquire rights in patents issued by the office?"}]},"s1p_16":{"notes":"# Study Notes: **Publication under Section 11A and Early Publication**  \n*(For Patent Agent Examination – Papers I, II and Viva)*\n\n---\n\n## Definition of Publication under Section 11A\n\nSection 11A of the Patents Act, 1970 mandates that every patent application must be published after a certain period. This publication is essential for transparency in the patent system and allows third parties to scrutinize the application before it proceeds further.\n\n**Key Concepts:**\n\n- **Mandatory Publication:** Every patent application (except those under specific exceptions) must be published.\n- **Purpose of Publication:** To enable interested parties to raise objections or oppositions, if any.\n- **Timing of Publication:** Normally after a period prescribed by the Controller, but can occur earlier upon request.\n\n---\n\n## Section 11A – Publication of Applications\n\n**Section 11A (2):**  \n> *The applicant may, in the prescribed manner, request the Controller to publish his application at any time before the expiry of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) and subject to the provisions of sub-section (3), the Controller shall publish such application as soon as possible.*\n\n**Section 11A (3):**  \n> *Every application for a patent shall, on the expiry of the period specified under sub-section (1), be published, except in cases where the application—*  \n> *(a) in which secrecy direction is imposed under Section 35; or*  \n> *(b) has been abandoned under sub-section (1) of Section 9; or*  \n> *(c) has been withdrawn three months prior to the period specified under sub-section (1).*\n\n**Section 11A (4):**  \n> *In case a secrecy direction has been given in respect of an application under Section 35, then it shall be published after the expiry of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) when the secrecy direction has ceased to operate, whichever is later.*\n\n---\n\n## Early Publication\n\nEarly publication refers to the process where the applicant requests the Controller to publish their patent application before the end of the normal publication period.\n\n**Key Points:**\n\n- **Request for Early Publication:** The applicant can request early publication at any time before the expiry of the prescribed period.\n- **Controller’s Discretion:** While the Controller is not obligated to grant such a request, they may do so \"as soon as possible\" if it is made in the prescribed manner.\n- **Benefits of Early Publication:**\n  - Allows the applicant to monitor potential oppositions or objections early on.\n  - Provides an opportunity for third parties to raise concerns before the patent is granted.\n\n---\n\n## Why Is Early Publication Provided?\n\nThe provision for early publication under Section 11A serves several important purposes, as explained in the textbook:\n\n- **Transparency and Fairness:** It ensures that the public has access to information about new inventions at an earlier stage. This helps prevent monopolies and promotes innovation (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 533).\n  \n- **Opportunity for Opposition:** Early publication allows interested parties to raise objections or oppositions even before the full examination process is completed. This aligns with the principle of fair competition in the patent system.\n\n- **Avoiding Unintended Conflicts:** By making applications public early, it helps avoid situations where a third party may unknowingly infringe on an unexamined but potentially valid patent application (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\n---\n\n## Exceptions to Publication under Section 11A\n\nCertain applications are exempt from mandatory publication:\n\n- **Applications with Secrecy Directions:** If a secrecy direction is imposed under Section 35 of the Patents Act, the application will not be published until that direction has ceased to operate (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 661).\n\n- **Abandoned Applications:** If an application has been abandoned under Section 9(1), it is not published.\n\n- **Withdrawn Applications:** An application withdrawn three months before the end of the normal publication period will also not be published.\n\n---\n\n## Verification of Cases\n\n> *(No verified or candidate cases are available for this chapter. Do NOT invent a case name, year, or citation. If a case would normally support a point, write \"(verify this case with a case-law database)\" instead.)*\n\n---\n\n## Summary and Key Takeaways\n\n- **Mandatory Publication:** Required under Section 11A of the Patents Act, 1970.\n- **Exceptions to Publication:** Applications with secrecy directions, abandoned applications, or withdrawn applications are not published.\n- **Early Publication:** Requested by the applicant in the prescribed manner and may be granted at the discretion of the Controller.\n- **Purpose:** To ensure transparency, enable oppositions, and prevent conflicts.\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions for Patent Agent Examination\n\n1. Under which section of the Patents Act is publication of applications mandated?  \n   - Answer: Section 11A.\n\n2. What are the exceptions to the mandatory publication under Section 11A?  \n   - Answer:\n     - Applications with secrecy directions (Section 35).\n     - Abandoned applications (Section 9(1)).\n     - Withdrawn applications three months before the end of the normal period.\n\n3. Can an applicant request early publication of their application, and if so, under which provision?  \n   - Answer: Yes, under Section 11A(2) of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n4. What is the purpose of early publication in the patent process?  \n   - Answer: To enable third parties to raise objections or oppositions before the grant of a patent and promote transparency in the system.\n\n5. Is there any provision that limits the number of requests for expedited examination?  \n   - Answer: Yes, under Rule 28(13) of the Patents Rules, 2003, which allows the Controller to limit such requests during the year (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 661).\n\n---\n\nThis concludes the detailed study notes on **Publication under Section 11A and Early Publication** for Patent Agent Examination. Make sure to revise these points thoroughly for Papers I, II, and Viva.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 11A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11A. Publication of applications.\n> (1) Save as otherwise provided, no application for patent shall\n> ordinarily be opened to the public for such period as may be\n> prescribed.\n>\n> (2) The applicant may, in the prescribed manner, request the\n> Controller to publish his application at any time before the expiry\n> of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) and subject to the\n> provisions of sub-section (3), the Controller shall publish such\n> application as soon as possible.\n>\n> (3) Every application for a patent shall, on the expiry of the\n> period specified under sub-section (1), be published, except in\n> cases where the application\n> (a) in which secrecy direction is imposed under section 35; or\n> (b) has been abandoned under sub-section (1) of section 9; or\n> (c) has been withdrawn three months prior to the period specified\n> under sub-section (1).\n>\n> (4) In case a secrecy direction has been given in respect of an\n> application under section 35, then, it shall be published after the\n> expiry of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) or when the\n> secrecy direction has ceased to operate, whichever is later.\n>\n> (5) The publication of every application under this section shall\n> include the particulars of the date of application, number of\n> application, name and address of the applicant identifying the\n> application and an abstract.\n>\n> (6) Upon publication of an application for a patent under this\n> section\n> (a) the depository institution shall make the biological material\n> mentioned in the specification available to the public;\n> (b) the patent office may, on payment of such fee as may be\n> prescribed, make the specification and drawings, if any, of such\n> application available to the public.\n>\n> (7) On and from the date of publication of the application for\n> patent and until the date of grant of a patent in respect of such\n>\n>  20\n>\n> application, the applicant shall have the like privileges and rights\n> as if a patent for the invention had been granted on the date of\n> publication of the application:\n> Provided that the applicant shall not be entitled to institute any\n> proceedings for infringement until the patent has been granted:\n> Provided further that the rights of a patentee in respect of\n> applications made under sub-section (2) of section 5 before the 1st\n> day of January, 2005 shall accrue from the date of grant of the\n> patent:\n> Provided also that after a patent is granted in respect of\n> applications made under sub-section (2) of section 5, the patent-\n> holder shall only be entitled to receive reasonable royalty from\n> such enterprises which have made significant investment and were\n> producing and marketing the concerned product prior to the 1st day\n> of January, 2005 and which continue to manufacture the product\n> covered by the patent on the date of grant of the patent and no\n> infringement proceedings shall be instituted against such\n> enterprises.\n\n**Rule 24 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 24. Publication of application\n> The period for which an application for patent shall not ordinarily\n> be open to public under sub-section (1) to section 11A shall be\n> eighteen months from the date of filing of application or the date\n> of priority of the application, whichever is earlier Provided that\n> the period within which the Controller shall publish the application\n> in the journal shall ordinarily be one month from the date of expiry\n> of said period, or one month from the date of request for\n> publication under rule 24A.\n\n**Rule 24A — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 24A. Request for publication\n> A request for publication under sub-section (2) of section 11A shall\n> be made in Form 9.\n\n**Rule 27 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 27. Inspection and supply of published documents\n> After the date of publication of the application under section 11A,\n> the application together with the complete specification and\n> provisional specification, if any, the drawing, if any, the abstract\n> and any other document filed in respect of the application may be\n> inspected at the appropriate office by making a written request to\n> the Controller on payment of the fee on that behalf and copies\n> thereof may be obtained on payment of fees specified in the First\n> Schedule.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the provisions of Section 11A of the Patents Act, 1970 regarding the publication of patent applications?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** To explain the provisions of Section 11A of the Patents Act, 1970 concerning the mandatory and early publication of patent applications.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 11A(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970, no application for a patent shall ordinarily be opened to the public for a period prescribed by the Controller. However, **Section 11A(2)** allows an applicant to request early publication in the prescribed manner at any time before the expiry of the prescribed period. The Controller is required to publish such an application “as soon as possible” if the request is made in accordance with the rules.\n\nSection **11A(3)** mandates that every patent application must be published upon the expiry of the prescribed period, unless it falls under certain exceptions:  \n- If a secrecy direction has been imposed under **Section 35**; or  \n- If the application has been abandoned under **Section 9(1)**; or  \n- If the application was withdrawn three months prior to the expiry of the normal publication period.\n\nFurthermore, **Section 11A(4)** provides that applications with secrecy directions must be published after the expiry of the prescribed period or when the secrecy direction ceases to operate, whichever is later. \n\n**Application:** Section 11A aims to ensure transparency in the patent system and allows third parties to scrutinize an application before it proceeds further. Early publication under **Section 11A(2)** offers applicants the opportunity to monitor potential oppositions or objections early on and helps prevent conflicts with third-party inventions.\n\nThe publication includes details such as the date of filing, the application number, the name and address of the applicant, and an abstract of the invention (as per **Section 11A(5)**). Once published, the applicant enjoys certain privileges similar to those of a patentee, although they cannot institute infringement proceedings until the patent is granted (**Section 11A(7)**).\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 11A outlines both mandatory and early publication provisions for patent applications. It mandates that all applications be published after a prescribed period unless exempted under specific conditions, while also allowing applicants to request early publication at any time before this period expires.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to distinguish between the mandatory publication requirements and the exceptions provided in **Section 11A(3)**. It is crucial to understand that secrecy directions, abandonment, and withdrawal are key exceptions to publication under Section 11A.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the benefits of early publication under Section 11A?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** To explain the advantages conferred by early publication under **Section 11A(2)** of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule:** Early publication is permitted under **Section 11A(2)** if an applicant requests it in the prescribed manner at any time before the expiry of the normal publication period. While the Controller has discretion to grant such a request, they are required to act “as soon as possible” if the request meets formal requirements.\n\nThe benefits of early publication include:  \n- **Transparency and fairness** in the patent system by allowing third parties to scrutinize an application before it proceeds further (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 533).  \n- Providing an opportunity for interested parties to raise objections or oppositions even before the full examination process is completed (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).  \n- Preventing unintended conflicts by enabling third parties to become aware of potentially valid patent applications early on.\n\n**Application:** Early publication allows an applicant to monitor the status of their application more closely and identify any potential issues or conflicts with existing inventions. It also helps the Controller ensure that the patent system remains transparent, promoting fair competition in the market.\n\nHowever, it is important to note that the Controller may not always grant early publication requests if they are made outside the prescribed manner or if there are concerns about public interest or secrecy (as governed by **Section 35**).\n\n**Conclusion:** Early publication under Section 11A serves as a tool for promoting transparency and fairness in the patent process. It enables third parties to raise objections, helps prevent conflicts, and ensures that applications are scrutinized before they proceed further.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the practical benefits of early publication, such as early detection of potential oppositions or conflicts with existing inventions. These benefits must be clearly explained in the answer.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the exceptions to the mandatory publication under Section 11A?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** To identify and explain the exceptions provided under **Section 11A(3)** of the Patents Act, 1970 that exempt certain applications from mandatory publication.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 11A(3)**, patent applications are not published if they fall into any of the following categories:  \n- Applications in which a **secrecy direction** has been imposed under **Section 35**; or  \n- Applications that have been **abandoned** under **Section 9(1)**; or  \n- Applications that have been **withdrawn three months prior to the expiry** of the normal publication period.\n\nIn such cases, mandatory publication is not required. However, if a secrecy direction has been imposed (as per **Section 35**), the application will be published after the expiry of the prescribed period or when the secrecy direction ceases to operate, whichever is later (**Section 11A(4)**).\n\n**Application:** These exceptions ensure that sensitive information, such as those covered by a secrecy direction under Section 35, remains confidential until it is deemed appropriate for publication. Similarly, abandoned applications and those withdrawn three months before the expiry of the normal period are not published as they no longer represent active patent applications.\n\nThe rationale behind these exceptions lies in balancing the need for transparency with the protection of sensitive or commercially valuable information (**Section 35**), and ensuring that the system does not burden applicants who have abandoned their applications.\n\n**Conclusion:** The mandatory publication under Section 11A is subject to certain exceptions, including secrecy directions, abandonment, and withdrawal. These exemptions are designed to protect sensitive information and respect the decisions of applicants who no longer wish to pursue a patent.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** It is common for students to confuse the conditions under which applications are exempt from publication. A clear distinction must be made between secrecy directions, abandonment, and withdrawal in relation to Section 11A(3).\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Can an applicant request early publication of their application? If so, what are the legal provisions governing such a request?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** To determine whether an applicant can request early publication under the Patents Act and identify the relevant provisions that govern such requests.\n\n**Rule:** Yes, an applicant is permitted to request **early publication** of their application at any time before the expiry of the prescribed period. This right is provided under **Section 11A(2)** of the Patents Act, 1970, which states that the applicant may make such a request in the prescribed manner.\n\nThe Controller has discretion to grant or deny the request but must publish the application “as soon as possible” if it is made in accordance with the rules. However, this right is subject to **Section 11A(3)** and **Section 11A(4)**, which provide exceptions such as secrecy directions.\n\n**Application:** Early publication allows an applicant to proactively manage their application by enabling third parties to raise objections or oppositions even before the full examination process is completed. It also helps avoid conflicts with existing inventions or interests in the field.\n\nHowever, it is important to note that **Rule 24** of the Patents Rules, 2003, specifies that the normal period for which an application shall not be open to public is eighteen months from the date of filing or the date of priority, whichever is earlier. The Controller must publish the application in the journal within one month of the expiry of this period or one month after a request for early publication.\n\n**Conclusion:** An applicant can indeed request early publication under **Section 11A(2)** of the Patents Act, 1970, provided that the request is made in the prescribed manner. The Controller has discretion to grant such requests but must act promptly if they are valid and meet formal requirements.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to mention the specific provisions governing early publication (Section 11A(2)) or confuse it with other sections, such as Rule 24 of the Patents Rules. It is essential to clearly identify the relevant provisions in the answer.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Publication under Section 11A and Early Publication\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 11-A | **TOTE** + the letter A | TOTE bag holds papers, delayed release after long wait |\n| Sec. 11-A(7) | **TOTE** + the letter A + clause (7) | TOTE bag rushes to court, demanding early publication of secrets. |\n| Rule 24 | **NERO** | NERO burns papers, enforcing secrecy direction over publication. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **12-24** - examination, objections, time for putting in order\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Publication after eighteen months from priority or filing - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n2. Request for early publication - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n3. Secrecy direction and its effect on publication - which provision?  (picture: NERO)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 11A(1) | Applications not opened to public for prescribed period | Locked box with patent inside  \n- Section 11A(2) | Applicant can request early publication in prescribed manner | Key turning lock from outside  \n- Section 11A(3) | Publication after expiry unless secrecy, abandonment or withdrawal | Red flag on application file  \n- Section 11A(4) | Secrecy direction applications published later when lifted | Locked box with key held by guard  \n- Section 11A(5) | Publication includes date, number, name, address and abstract | File with all details written on it  \n- Rule 24 | 18-month period before publication, one month for journal entry | Clock ticking for 18 months  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Forgetting exceptions like secrecy directions or abandoned applications  \n- Examiners expect: Clear understanding of early publication process under Section 11A(2)  \n- Structure tip: Always mention the three exceptions in Section 11A(3) when answering questions on publication","storyEN":"### The Story: **The Patent of the Pearl Necklace**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, where the old city meets the new, lived a young inventor named Ravi. He had spent years perfecting a unique pearl necklace that could change colors based on temperature — a marvel of both science and art. When he finally completed his invention, he rushed to the Patent Office in Begumpet.\n\nAt the office, an elderly clerk named Mr. Patel explained that before any patent could be granted, Ravi had to follow certain rules — one of them being **Section 11A** of the Patents Act, 1970. \"Every application must be published,\" he said, tapping his pen on a ledger filled with numbers and dates.\n\nRavi was confused. “Published? What does that mean?” Mr. Patel smiled. “It means your invention will be made public so others can see it — but not right away. Normally, we wait **eighteen months** from the date you filed your application,” he said, pointing at a rule in his book.\n\nRavi’s eyes widened. “Eighteen months? That seems like a long time.” Mr. Patel nodded. “But there's good news. If you want, you can ask for **early publication** under **Section 11A(2)** — and we’ll do it as soon as possible.”\n\nRavi thought about this. He wanted to make sure that no one else would copy his idea before the patent was granted. “What if someone sees my invention early and tries to steal it?” he asked.\n\nMr. Patel explained, “That’s why **Section 11A(3)** is important. If you don’t request early publication, we’ll publish your application automatically after eighteen months — unless one of these things happens: (a) a secrecy direction is imposed under **Section 35**, or (b) the application has been abandoned under **Section 9(1)**, or (c) it was withdrawn three months before the eighteen-month period.”\n\nRavi was relieved. He decided to request early publication immediately. “But what happens when I do that?” he asked.\n\nMr. Patel replied, “Then we’ll publish your application as soon as possible — but only if you follow the **prescribed manner** for requesting it. And once published, others can see it and object before the patent is granted.”\n\nRavi nodded. He filled out his request form and handed it to Mr. Patel. A week later, he received a letter from the Controller: “Your application has been published. You now have the same rights as if your patent had already been granted — but you can’t sue anyone for infringement until the patent is officially given.”\n\nRavi felt proud. He knew that by following **Section 11A**, he was protecting his invention while also contributing to a fair and transparent system.\n\nBut then, Mr. Patel warned him of some exceptions. “If your application had a secrecy direction under **Section 35**, we wouldn’t publish it until the secrecy order ended — or until the eighteen-month period passed, whichever came first.”\n\nRavi was grateful for this knowledge. He now understood that while publication was mandatory in most cases, there were rules and exceptions to protect both inventors and the public.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Eighteen months | Section 11A(2) and Rule 24 of the Patents Rules, 2003 | The period after which an application is published if not requested earlier. |\n| Early publication | Section 11A(2) | An applicant may request early publication at any time before the expiry of the prescribed period. |\n| Secrecy direction | Section 35 and Section 11A(3)(a) | Applications with secrecy directions are exempt from mandatory publication until the direction ceases to operate. |\n| Abandoned application | Section 9(1) and Section 11A(3)(b) | An abandoned application is not published. |\n| Withdrawn three months before | Section 11A(3)(c) | Applications withdrawn three months prior to the end of the normal publication period are not published. |\n| Same rights as a granted patent | Section 11A(7) | Once published, the applicant has similar rights and privileges as if the patent had been granted. |\n| Cannot sue for infringement until grant | Section 11A(7) | The applicant cannot institute proceedings for infringement until the patent is officially granted. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for “Publication under Section 11A and Early Publication”**\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym/Mnemonic: \"PEP\" (for Publication, Early, and Procedure)**  \n- **P**ublication of applications is mandatory unless secrecy is imposed or the application has been abandoned.  \n- **E**arly publication can be requested before the normal period ends.  \n- **P**rocedure for early publication involves submitting a request to the Controller in the prescribed manner.\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 11A**: Imagine **\"11A\" as an airplane (11) flying above (\"A\")**, symbolizing that applications are published and visible to all, like an airplane in the sky.\n  \n- **Section 9(1)**: Think of **\"9\" as a \"no\" sign** (like a traffic sign), reminding you that if the application is **abandoned**, it will not be published.\n\n- **Section 35**: Picture **\"35\" as a lock** — secrecy directions are like a **lock** preventing publication, just like a locked door prevents entry.\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Early Publication vs. Mandatory Publication:**  \n  - *Confusion*: Students often mix up when the Controller is required to publish (mandatory) and when it can be done early (optional).  \n  - *Differentiator*: Early publication is a **request** made by the applicant, while mandatory publication happens automatically **after the prescribed period**, unless an exception applies.\n\n- **Abandonment vs. Withdrawal:**  \n  - *Confusion*: Both lead to non-publication, but students may confuse the two.  \n  - *Differentiator*: Abandonment is a formal **termination** of the application (Section 9(1)), whereas withdrawal is a **voluntary step** taken before the expiry period (Section 11B(4)).\n\n- **Secrecy Direction vs. Early Publication:**  \n  - *Confusion*: Both affect publication, but students may not know how they differ.  \n  - *Differentiator*: A secrecy direction **prevents** publication until it expires, while early publication is a request to publish before the normal period ends.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Publication occurs 18 months after filing or priority date, whichever is earlier. Rule 24 specifies this period as mandatory unless secrecy applies.","question":"What is the time limit for publication under Section 11A?"},{"answer":"An applicant may request early publication in Form 9 under Rule 24A before the 18-month period expires.","question":"How can an applicant request early publication?"},{"answer":"The application remains unpublished until the secrecy direction ceases, as per Section 11A(4).","question":"What happens if a secrecy direction is imposed on an application?"},{"answer":"The applicant has privileges similar to a patentee but cannot sue for infringement until grant, as stated in Section 11A(7).","question":"What rights does an applicant have after publication?"},{"answer":"Because the patent right is not legally enforceable until the patent is officially granted, as per Section 11A(7).","question":"Why can't an applicant sue for infringement before patent grant?"},{"answer":"The request for examination must be made within 31 months from filing or priority date, as per Rule 24B(1)(i).","question":"What is the deadline for requesting examination after publication?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 24 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Publication of application”.","options":["Rule 29","Rule 56","Rule 58","Rule 24"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Publication of application?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 24 is headed “Publication of application”.","options":["Application for compulsory licence etc","Prescribed particulars","Publication of application","Manner of publication of the revocation order"],"question":"Rule 24 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 11A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Publication of applications”.","options":["Section 99","Section 92","Section 11A","Section 84"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Publication of applications?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 11A is headed “Publication of applications”.","options":["Publication of applications","Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of","Power of Controller in cases of anticipation","Power of Controller to make orders respecting dating of"],"question":"Section 11A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The applicant may request the Controller to publish the application, and the Controller shall publish it as soon as possible.","options":["A) The applicant directly","B) The Controller on request by the applicant","C) The Patent Office automatically","D) The Registrar of Patents"],"question":"Who has the authority to publish an application for a patent before the expiry of the period prescribed under Section 11A(1)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The application is published after the expiry of the period under Section 11A(1) or when the secrecy direction ceases, whichever is later.","options":["A) Immediately upon filing the application","B) After the expiry of the period prescribed under Section 11A(1) or when the secrecy direction ceases to operate, whichever is later","C) Only after the secrecy direction is revoked by the Controller","D) Three months after the secrecy direction is imposed"],"question":"When is an application for a patent published if a secrecy direction is imposed under Section 35?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The publication must include the date of application, number, applicant's details, and an abstract.","options":["A) The full text of the specification and claims","B) The date of application, application number, applicant's name and address, and an abstract","C) The names of all inventors and their addresses","D) The fee paid for publication"],"question":"What must be included in the publication of an application under Section 11A(5)?"}]},"s1p_17":{"notes":"# Request for Examination and Expedited Examination – Study Notes for Patent Agent Examination\n\n## 1. Definition and Key Concepts\n\n### **Request for Examination**\n- A formal request made by an applicant to have their patent application examined by the Controller.\n- It is a mandatory step in the process of obtaining a patent, as examination is required before granting a patent.\n\n### **Expedited Examination**\n- A special procedure under which the examination of a patent application is carried out at an accelerated pace.\n- This is intended for applications that meet certain criteria and require faster processing.\n\n## 2. Legal Provisions\n\n### **Rule 24B: Examination of Application**\n- Under Rule 24B, the applicant may request the Controller to examine their application.\n- The Controller has the discretion to accept or reject such a request based on the provisions of the Act and Rules.\n\n### **Rule 24C: Expedited Examination of Applications**\n- This rule provides for expedited examination of applications under specific conditions.\n- It allows the applicant to request that their application be examined before the expiry of 31 months from the priority date, provided certain criteria are met.\n\n## 3. Why the Law is Structured This Way\n\n### **Purpose and Rationale**\n- The purpose behind providing a mechanism for expedited examination is to ensure that important inventions, especially those with commercial or strategic significance, receive timely consideration.\n- It helps in reducing delays in the granting of patents, which can be crucial for startups and small entities seeking protection for their innovations.\n\n### **Eligibility Criteria for Expedited Examination**\n- According to Rule 24C(3), an applicant may request expedited examination if any of the following conditions apply:\n  - The application has been filed by a startup.\n  - India has been indicated as the competent International Searching Authority or elected as an International Preliminary Examining Authority in the corresponding international application.\n\n### **Limitations on Expedited Examination**\n- There is a cap on the number of applications that can be processed under expedited examination at any given time. For example, as stated in the textbook (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 183), the CGPDTM notified a limit of **1000 requests** for expedited examination to be received by the patent office on or before December 31, 2016.\n- This limitation ensures that the system remains manageable and that resources are allocated efficiently.\n\n### **Use of Forms**\n- A request for ordinary examination is made using **Form 18**, while a request for expedited examination is made using **Form 18A**.\n- However, it must be noted that **PCT applications do not qualify** for expedited examination under Rule 24C. Only \"express requests\" can be made on Form 18 to have them examined before the expiry of 31 months (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 183).\n\n## 4. Process for Requesting Examination\n\n### **Steps Involved**\n- The applicant must file a request with the Controller specifying the application number.\n- The request should be accompanied by any documents required under Rule 24C(3), such as proof of being a startup or evidence that India is designated as an International Searching Authority.\n\n### **Processing Timeframes**\n- Once a request for examination is received, the application is allotted a number and placed in a dedicated queue.\n- The Controller shall dispose of the report of the examiner within one month from the date of receipt (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 533).\n- In the case of expedited examination, the application is processed in a faster timeline compared to regular examinations.\n\n## 5. Criticisms and Considerations\n\n### **Criticism of Limitations**\n- The restriction on the number of applications eligible for expedited examination may be seen as limiting access to important innovators who require urgent patent protection.\n- Some stakeholders argue that this cap should be reviewed periodically to ensure it aligns with current innovation trends.\n\n### **Benefits of Expedited Examination**\n- For startups and small entities, expedited examination offers a significant advantage by reducing the time between filing an application and receiving a decision on its grantability.\n- This can help in securing funding, commercialization opportunities, and market entry.\n\n## 6. Practical Considerations for Patent Agents\n\n### **Form Filling and Documentation**\n- Patent agents must ensure that all required forms (such as Form 18 or 18A) are correctly filled out with accurate application numbers.\n- They should also verify that the applicant meets the eligibility criteria for expedited examination, such as being a startup.\n\n### **Communication with Applicants**\n- Agents should inform applicants about the current status of their request and any delays in processing.\n- It is important to highlight the importance of meeting deadlines, especially since the Controller may reject requests if they are not submitted within the prescribed timeframes.\n\n## 7. Summary\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|------|---------|\n| **Legal Provision** | Rule 24B (Examination), Rule 24C (Expedited Examination) |\n| **Purpose** | To ensure timely examination of patent applications, especially for startups and important innovations |\n| **Eligibility Criteria** | Startup status or designation of India as International Searching Authority |\n| **Limitations** | Cap on number of expedited requests; PCT applications not eligible |\n| **Forms Used** | Form 18 (Ordinary), Form 18A (Expedited) |\n| **Processing Time** | Expedited examination is faster than regular, but subject to Controller’s discretion |\n\n---\n\n**Note:** No verified case law has been identified for this topic. If a student wishes to explore further, they should consult IndianKanoon or other legal databases for relevant judgments.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 11B — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11B. Request for examination.\n> (1) No application for a patent shall be examined unless the\n> applicant or any other interested person makes a request in the\n> prescribed manner for such examination within the prescribed period.\n>\n> (3) In case of an application in respect of a claim for a patent\n> filed under sub-section (2) of section 5 before the 1st day of\n> January, 2005 a request for its examination shall be made in the\n> prescribed manner and within the prescribed period by the applicant\n> or any other interested person.\n>\n> (4) In case the applicant or any other interested person does not\n> make a request for examination of the application for a patent\n> within the period as specified under sub-section (1) or sub-section\n> (3), the application shall be treated as withdrawn by the applicant:\n> Provided that\n> (i) the applicant may, at any time after filing the application but\n> before the grant of a patent, withdraw the application by making a\n> request in the prescribed manner; and\n> (ii) in a case where secrecy direction has been issued under section\n> 35, the request for examination may be made within the prescribed\n> period from the date of revocation of the secrecy direction.\n>\n>  21\n>\n> 12. Examination of application.\n> (1) When a request for examination has been made in respect of an\n> application for a patent in the prescribed manner under sub-section\n> (1) or sub-section (3) of section 11B, the application and\n> specification and other documents related thereto shall be referred\n> at the earliest by the Controller to an examiner for making a report\n> to him in respect of the following matters, namely:\n> (a) whether the application and the specification and other\n> documents relating thereto are in accordance with the requirements\n> of this Act and of any rules made thereunder;\n> (b) whether there is any lawful ground of objection to the grant of\n> the patent under this Act in pursuance of the application;\n> (c) the result of investigations made under section 13; and (d) any\n> other matter which may be prescribed.\n>\n> (2) The examiner to whom the application and the specification and\n> other documents relating thereto are referred under sub-section (1)\n> shall ordinarily make the report to the Controller within such\n> period as may be prescribed.\n\n**Section 12 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 12. Examination of application.\n\n**Section 13 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 13. Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior\n> claim.\n> (1) The examiner to whom an application for a patent is referred\n> under section 12 shall make investigation for the purpose of\n> ascertaining whether the invention so far as claimed in any claim of\n> the complete specification\n> (a) has been anticipated by publication before the date of filing of\n> the applicant’s complete specification in any specification filed in\n> pursuance of an application for a patent made in India and dated on\n> or after the 1st day of January, 1912;\n> (b) is claimed in any claim of any other complete specification\n> published on or after the date of filing of the applicant’s complete\n> specification, being a specification filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent made in India and dated before or claiming\n> the priority date earlier than that date.\n>\n> (2) The examiner shall, in addition, make such investigation for the\n> purpose of ascertaining whether the invention, so far as claimed in\n> any claim of the complete specification, has been anticipated by\n> publication in India or elsewhere in any document other than those\n>\n>  22\n>\n> mentioned in sub-section (1) before the date of filing of the\n> applicant’s complete specification.\n>\n> (3) Where a complete specification is amended under the provisions\n> of this Act before the grant of a patent, the amended specification\n> shall be examined and investigated in like manner as the original\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) The examination and investigations required under section 12 and\n> this section shall not be deemed in any way to warrant the validity\n> of any patent, and no liability shall be incurred by the Central\n> Government or any officer thereof by reason of, or in connection\n> with, any such examination or investigation or any report or other\n> proceedings consequent thereon.\n\n**Rule 134 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 134. Request for information under section 153\n> (1) A request for information in respect of the following matters\n> relating to any patent or application for patent shall be\n> admissible, namely:\n> (a) as to when a complete specification following a provisional\n> specification has been filed or an application for patent has been\n> deemed to have been abandoned;\n> (aa) as to when the information under section 8 has been filed.\n> (b) as to when publication of application has been made under\n> section 11A;\n> (c) as to when an application has been withdrawn under section 11B;\n> (d) as to when a request for examination has been made under section\n> 11B;\n> (e) as to when the examination report has been issued under section\n> 12;\n> (f) as to when an application for patent has been refused;\n> (g) as to when a patent has been granted;\n> (h) as to when a renewal fee has been paid;\n> (i) as to when the term of a patent has expired or shall expire;\n> (j) as to when an entry has been made in the register or application\n> has been made for the making of such entry; or\n> (k) as to when any application is made or action taken involving an\n>\n>  73\n>\n> entry in the register, publication in the Official Journal or\n> otherwise, if the nature of the application or action is specified\n> in the request.\n>\n> (2) Separate request shall be made in respect of each item of\n> information required.\n>\n> (3) The fee payable on a request to be made under section 153 shall\n> be as set out in the First Schedule.\n\n**Rule 2 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 2. Definitions\n> In these rules, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (a) \"Act\" means the Patents Act, 1970 (39 of 1970);\n> (b) \"appropriate office\" means the appropriate office of the patent\n> office as specified in rule 4;\n> (c) \"article\" includes any substance or material, and any plant,\n> machinery or apparatus, whether affixed to land or not;\n> (ca)\"educational institution\" means a university established or\n> incorporated by or under Central Act, a Provincial Act, or a State\n> Act, and includes any other educational institution as recognised by\n> an authority designated by the Central Government or the State\n> Government or the Union territories in this regard;\n> (d) \"Form\" means a Form specified in the Second Schedule;\n> (da) \"person other than a natural person\" shall include a \"small\n> entity\";\n> (db) \"Request for examination\" means a request for examination,\n> including expedited examination, made under section 11B in respect\n> of rule 24B or rule 24C;\n> (e) \"Schedule\" means Schedule to these rules;\n> (f) \"section\" means a section of the Act;\n> (fa) \"small entity\" means,\n> (i) in case of an enterprise engaged in the manufacture or\n> production of goods, an enterprise where the investment in plant and\n> machinery does not exceed the limit specified for a medium\n> enterprise under clause (a) of sub-section (1) of section 7 of the\n> Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises Development Act, 2006; and\n> (ii)in case of an enterprise engaged in providing or rendering of\n> services, an enterprise where the investment in equipment is not\n> more than the limit specified for medium enterprises under clause\n> (b) of sub-section (I) of Section 7 of the Micro, Small and Medium\n> Enterprises Development Act, 2006.\n>\n>  7\n>\n> Explanation 1: For the purpose of this clause, \"enterprise\" means an\n> industrial undertaking or a business concern or any other\n> establishment, by whatever name called, engaged in the manufacture\n> or production of goods, in any manner, pertaining to any industry\n> specified in the First Schedule to the Industries (Development and\n> Regulation) Act, 1951 (65 of 1951) or engaged in providing or\n> rendering of any service or services in such an industry.\n> Explanation 2: In calculating the investment in plant and machinery,\n> the cost of pollution control, research and development, industrial\n> safety devices and such other things as may be specified by\n> notification under the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises\n> Development Act, 2006; shall be excluded.\n> Explanation 3: The reference rates of foreign currency of the\n> Reserve Bank of India shall prevail.\n>\n> (fb) (i) \"Startup\" means\n> (a) an entity in India recognised as a startup by the competent\n> authority under Startup India initiative.\n> (b) In case of a foreign entity, an entity fulfilling the criteria\n> for turnover and period of incorporation/ registration as per\n> Startup India Initiative and submitting declaration to that effect.\n>\n> Explanation: In calculating the turnover, reference rates of foreign\n> currency of Reserve Bank of India shall prevail.\n> (g) words and expressions used, but not defined in these rules,\n> shall have the meanings respectively assigned to them in the Act.\n\n**Rule 26 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 26. Request for withdrawal\n> A request for withdrawing the application under sub-section (4) of\n> section 11B shall be made in Form 29.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is a 'Request for Examination' under the Patents Act, 1970? Explain its significance and legal provisions governing it.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The definition of \"Request for Examination\" and its importance in the patent application process under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule**: Under **Section 11B(1)** of the Patents Act, no patent application can be examined unless a request is made by the applicant or any interested person within the prescribed period. This rule ensures that only applications for which examination has been requested are processed further. Rule **24B** of the Patents Rules, 2003 provides that such a request must be made in **Form 18**, and it must be filed within **31 months from the priority date or filing date**, whichever is earlier.\n\nThe significance of this provision lies in ensuring that only those applications which meet the formal requirements are subjected to examination. It also prevents unnecessary delays in processing applications, as it allows the Controller to focus on applications for which a request has been made. The **Controller** retains discretion under Rule 24B(2) to refer an application to an examiner once a valid request is received.\n\n**Application**: If an applicant files a patent application and does not make a request for examination within the stipulated period, the application may be deemed withdrawn by the Controller as per **Section 11B(4)**. This highlights the importance of timely submission of the request for examination to avoid procedural defaults. Furthermore, even if the application is filed before January 1, 2005, the applicant must still comply with the prescribed timeframes under Rule 24B.\n\n**Conclusion**: A \"Request for Examination\" is a mandatory step in obtaining a patent. It ensures that only applications which meet the formal requirements are subjected to examination by the Controller. The legal provisions governing this process are outlined in **Section 11B and Rule 24B**, and failure to comply with these may result in the application being treated as withdrawn.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often overlook the importance of adhering to time limits for filing a request for examination, which can lead to procedural defaults. It is essential to emphasize that even if an application is filed before January 1, 2005, the prescribed period under Rule 24B still applies.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the provisions for 'Expedited Examination' of patent applications? Discuss its eligibility criteria and limitations.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The legal provisions governing expedited examination and the conditions that make an application eligible for such a process, along with any limitations imposed by law.\n\n**Rule**: **Rule 24C** of the Patents Rules, 2003 provides for expedited examination. Under this rule, an applicant may request expedited examination in **Form 18A**, provided certain conditions are met. The eligibility criteria include: (a) India being indicated as the competent International Searching Authority or elected as an International Preliminary Examining Authority; (b) the applicant being a **startup**; (c) the applicant being a **small entity**; and others specified in sub-rule (1)(a)-(j). \n\nHowever, there are limitations. For example, a cap of **1000 requests** for expedited examination was imposed by the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trademarks (**CGPDTM**) as per textbook references (p. 183), to ensure efficient resource allocation. Additionally, **PCT applications do not qualify** for expedited examination under Rule 24C.\n\n**Application**: Suppose a startup files an application in India and wishes to request expedited examination. It must file the request within 31 months from the priority date or filing date using **Form 18A**, along with proof of being a startup. However, it is important to note that if more than 1000 such requests are filed before December 31, 2016, additional requests may be deferred due to the cap.\n\n**Conclusion**: Expedited examination allows for faster processing of patent applications under certain conditions, particularly benefiting startups and small entities. However, there are limitations, including a cap on the number of eligible applications and ineligibility for PCT applications. These provisions are outlined in **Rule 24C**, and compliance with eligibility criteria is essential.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often fail to mention the **cap on expedited examination requests** or the **ineligibility of PCT applications**, both of which are critical limitations under Rule 24C.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is the difference between 'Ordinary Examination' and 'Expedited Examination'? Discuss with reference to legal provisions and procedural steps.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The distinction between ordinary examination and expedited examination, including their legal basis and procedural differences.\n\n**Rule**: **Rule 24B** governs the process for **ordinary examination**, which is a standard procedure where an application is examined within the prescribed timeframes. A request for ordinary examination is made using **Form 18**, and it must be filed within **31 months from the priority date or filing date**.\n\nOn the other hand, **Rule 24C** outlines the provisions for **expedited examination**, which allows faster processing under certain conditions such as being a startup or India being designated as an International Searching Authority. A request for expedited examination is made using **Form 18A**, and it must also be filed within 31 months from the priority date, but with additional criteria.\n\n**Application**: Consider two applications: one by a regular applicant and another by a startup. The regular applicant files a request in Form 18 under Rule 24B, while the startup files a request in Form 18A under Rule 24C. Both must be filed within 31 months from the priority date or filing date. However, the startup’s application may be processed faster due to the expedited examination provisions.\n\n**Conclusion**: The difference between ordinary and expedited examination lies in **processing time**, **eligibility criteria**, and **forms used**. Ordinary examination is standard and available to all applicants, while expedited examination offers accelerated processing but only under specific conditions outlined in Rule 24C.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often confuse the **forms and timelines** for ordinary and expedited examination. It is important to clearly distinguish between them based on legal provisions and procedural requirements.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Request for Examination and Expedited Examination\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 11-B | **TOTE** + the letter B | TOTE bag spills papers, missing deadline for exam request |\n| Sec. 12 | **TIN** | TIN can't hold filings; abandonment deemed after missed request |\n| Sec. 13 | **TOMB** | TOMB stone warns: only applicants may seek fast-track exam |\n| Rule 24-B | **NERO** + the letter B | NERO's search: examiner hunts anticipatory art in patent reports |\n| Rule 24-C | **NERO** + the letter C | NERO's report: examiner logs anticipation findings to Controller |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **12-24** - examination, objections, time for putting in order\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Time limit for filing the request for examination - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n2. Deemed withdrawal on failure to request - which provision?  (picture: TIN)\n3. Expedited examination and who may seek it - which provision?  (picture: TOMB)\n4. Examiner's search for anticipation and the examination report to the Controller - which provision?  (picture: NERO)\n5. Request for Examination and Expedited Examination - which provision?  (picture: NERO)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 11B(1) | Request for examination is mandatory within 31 months | Deadline clock ticking\n- Section 12(1) | Controller refers application to examiner after request | Examiner gets the file\n- Rule 24B(1)(i) | Form 18 used for ordinary examination request | Paper form in hand\n- Rule 24C(1) | Expedited exam via Form 18A if applicant is a startup | Startup logo on form\n- Rule 24C(3) | Expedited requests must be accompanied by publication request | Two forms together\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid missing the 31-month deadline for requesting examination  \n- Use precise terms like \"startup\" or \"International Searching Authority\" when citing eligibility  \n- Always mention both Form 18 and Form 18A in answers about examination requests","storyEN":"**Story: The Patent Race in Hyderabad**\n\nIn a small corner of Hyderabad, a bustling city known for its tech startups, lived Ravi, an ambitious young inventor who had just filed a patent application for his innovative solar-powered water purifier. He knew that to turn his dream into reality, he needed to navigate the complex world of patent law — and fast.\n\nRavi’s first challenge was understanding **Rule 24B**, which dictated that any request for examination must be made within **thirty-one months** from the date of filing or priority. He had filed his application on a rainy afternoon in May, and now he needed to act before time ran out. If he missed this window, the application would be treated as withdrawn — a fate Ravi was determined to avoid.\n\nHe met with his friend, Priya, who worked at a patent agency. She explained that if he wanted to speed things up, he could apply for **expedited examination** under **Rule 24C**, but only if he met certain criteria. One of those conditions was being a startup — which Ravi’s company, *SolarSpring*, happened to be.\n\nPriya helped him fill out **Form 18A**, the correct form for expedited requests, and ensured that all documents were in order. But she also warned him about an important rule: there was a cap on how many such requests could be processed at once. In fact, as of the end of **2016**, only **1000** applications had been allowed under this system — a number that Ravi now realized might not be enough for all the hopefuls like him.\n\nAs he submitted his request, another inventor named Arjun came into the picture. Arjun was working on an AI-based medical diagnostic tool and had filed an application with India as the **International Searching Authority** in his corresponding international patent case. This, too, qualified him for expedited examination under Rule 24C.\n\nMeanwhile, Ravi’s application was placed in a special queue. The Controller, who oversaw these matters, had to dispose of the examiner’s report within **one month**, as required by **Rule 24B(2)(iii)** — a strict timeline that kept the process moving quickly.\n\nBut not everything went smoothly. A third inventor, Meera, tried to file an expedited request but was disqualified because her application was under the **PCT system**. As explained in the textbook (p. 183), **PCT applications do not qualify** for expedited examination — a rule that left Meera frustrated but understanding.\n\nDays turned into weeks, and Ravi watched as his application moved through the process faster than usual. He was reminded by Priya of another important detail: if he missed any deadlines or failed to submit required documents, like proof of being a startup, his request could be rejected — even if it had been submitted on time.\n\nFinally, one day, the Controller sent Ravi a **first statement of objections**, as outlined in **Rule 24B(3)**. This was a crucial step that would determine whether his invention would eventually become a patent or not.\n\nArjun and Ravi both knew that their success depended on how well they had followed these rules — from the correct forms, to the right timelines, to meeting the eligibility criteria for expedited examination.\n\n---\n\n**ANCHOR KEY**\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Thirty-one months | Rule 24B(1)(i) | A request for examination must be made within thirty-one months from the date of priority or filing. |\n| One month | Rule 24B(2)(ii) | The examiner must make a report to the Controller within one month (but not exceeding three months). |\n| One month | Rule 24B(3) | The Controller must dispose of the examiner's report within one month from receipt. |\n| 1000 requests | Textbook p. 183 | There was a cap of 1000 expedited examination requests to be received by December 31, 2016. |\n| Form 18A | Rule 24C(1) | The form used for requesting expedited examination. |\n| PCT applications not qualifying | Textbook p. 183 | PCT applications are excluded from expedited examination under Rule 24C. |\n| Startup status | Rule 24C(1)(b) | An applicant qualifies for expedited examination if they are a startup. |\n| First statement of objections | Rule 24B(3) | The Controller issues this within one month of disposing the examiner’s report. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for “Request for Examination and Expedited Examination”**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for \"Request for Examination\" (Rule 24B)**  \n**R.E.Q.U.E.S.T.** – *Remember Each Qualified User Enters System Through Form 18.*\n\n- **R**equest must be made in writing.\n- **E**xaminer is responsible for reviewing the application.\n- **Q**ualifying criteria may apply (e.g., priority documents).\n- **U**nder Rule 24B, it’s a standard examination process.\n- **E**xamination is required before grant.\n- **S**ubmitted to Controller within specified time.\n- **T**hrough Form 18.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-anchor for Rule 24B (Request for Examination)**  \n**Rule 24B = “24B” → “Two Four B” → A **book** being opened — this recalls the process of opening a file for examination, like opening a book to review its contents.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Acronym/Mnemonic for \"Expedited Examination\" (Rule 24C)**  \n**E.X.P.E.D.I.T.E.** – *Every Xpert Prefers Expedited Delivered In Time Efficiently.*\n\n- **E**xpedited requests are limited in number.\n- **X**perts or startups may qualify.\n- **P**riority is given to these applications.\n- **E**xamination must be requested separately.\n- **D**eadlines for submission are strict (e.g., within 31 months).\n- **I**t’s only available for domestic applications, not PCT.\n- **T**hrough Form 18.\n- **E**xpedited examination is faster than standard.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Number-anchor for Rule 24C (Expedited Examination)**  \n**Rule 24C = “24C” → “Two Four C” → A **car** speeding — this recalls the accelerated process of expedited examination, like a car moving quickly on a highway.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Confusion Pair: Rule 24B vs. Rule 24C**\n\n- **Rule 24B (Request for Examination)** is the standard process.\n- **Rule 24C (Expedited Examination)** is a faster option but has limited capacity and specific eligibility criteria.\n\n**How to tell them apart:**  \n*“Standard examination uses Form 18, while expedited also uses Form 18 but with a special request.”*\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Confusion Pair: Expedited Examination vs. PCT Applications**\n\n- **Expedited examination (Rule 24C)** is available only for domestic applications.\n- **PCT applications** do not qualify for expedited examination under Rule 24C.\n\n**How to tell them apart:**  \n*“Expedited applies to local apps, but PCT apps are handled separately and cannot be expedited.”*\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Confusion Pair: Request for Examination (Form 18) vs. Expedited Examination (Form 18A)**\n\n- **Form 18** is used for standard examination.\n- **Form 18A** does not exist — it’s a common misconception.\n\n**How to tell them apart:**  \n*“Only Form 18 exists; there's no Form 18A for expedited examination.”*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall the key differences, procedures, and rules related to **Request for Examination and Expedited Examination**, using clear anchors and mnemonics based on the Bare Act and textbook material.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The examiner must submit the report within one month, not exceeding two months. Rule 106(6) sets this deadline from the date the application is referred to them.","question":"What is the time limit for the examiner to submit a report under expedited examination?"},{"answer":"Startups and small entities may request expedited examination without being questioned if their status changes. Rule 106(5) provides this protection.","question":"Who may request expedited examination without being questioned about status changes?"},{"answer":"The Controller must dispose of the report within one month of receiving it. Rule 106(7) mandates this timeframe.","question":"How long does the Controller have to dispose of the examiner's report?"},{"answer":"The Controller must issue the first statement of objections within fifteen days of disposing of the examiner's report. Rule 106(8) specifies this deadline.","question":"When must the Controller issue the first statement of objections?"},{"answer":"The applicant has six months from the first statement of objections to put the application in order. Rule 106(10) outlines this period.","question":"What is the time limit for putting an application in order for grant?"},{"answer":"Yes, the period may be extended by three months with a request in Form 4 and a fee. Rule 106(11) allows this extension.","question":"Can the time limit for putting an application in order be extended?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 11B of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Request for examination”.","options":["Section 11B","Section 65","Section 76","Section 10"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Request for examination?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 11B is headed “Request for examination”.","options":["Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of time","Date of patent","Request for examination","General principles applicable to working of patented inventions"],"question":"Section 11B of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 24C of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Expedited examination of applications”.","options":["Rule 24C","Rule 121A","Rule 69","Rule 13"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Expedited examination of applications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 24C is headed “Expedited examination of applications”.","options":["Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)","Expedited examination of applications","Adjournment of hearing","On form of 26 No fee No fee No fee No fee"],"question":"Rule 24C of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 12 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications”.","options":["Rule 111A","Rule 101","Rule 12","Rule 114"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 12 is headed “Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications”.","options":["Opposition to restoration under section 61","Amendment of patent under section 44","Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications","Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under"],"question":"Rule 12 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 24B of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Examination of application”.","options":["Rule 24B","Rule 115","Rule 3","Rule 11"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Examination of application?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 24B is headed “Examination of application”.","options":["Examination of application","Further evidence to be left with the leave of the","Notification of corrections","Correction of clerical errors"],"question":"Rule 24B of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 12 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Examination of application”.","options":["Section 12","Section 107","Section 61","Section 87"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Examination of application?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 12 is headed “Examination of application”.","options":["Examination of application","Avoidance of certain restrictive conditions","Appeals to High Court","Anticipation by use and publication after provisional"],"question":"Section 12 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 13 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Specifications”.","options":["Rule 109","Rule 129A","Rule 126","Rule 13"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Specifications?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 13 is headed “Specifications”.","options":["Registration of patent agents","Short title and commencement","Adjournment of hearing","Specifications"],"question":"Rule 13 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"The time limit is specified in Rule 24B(1)(i) as thirty-one months from the date of priority or filing, whichever is earlier.","options":["A) 12 months from the date of filing","B) 24 months from the date of priority","C) 31 months from the date of priority or filing, whichever is earlier","D) 42 months from the date of filing"],"question":"What is the time limit for making a request for examination under section 11B for applications filed on or after 1st January 2005?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 24B(1)(i) specifies that the request for examination must be made in Form 18.","options":["A) Form 17","B) Form 18","C) Form 20","D) Form 22"],"question":"Which form is used to file a request for examination under section 11B?"}]},"s1p_18":{"notes":"# First Examination Report and the Time to Put the Application in Order\n\n## Definition\n\nThe **First Examination Report (FER)** is a document prepared by the **Examiner** under the **Patents Act, 1970**, which contains an analysis of whether the invention claimed in the patent application meets the legal requirements for grant. This report includes observations on novelty, inventive step, and industrial applicability.\n\nThe **time to put the application in order** refers to the period within which the applicant must address any objections raised by the Controller after receiving the First Examination Report and comply with the necessary amendments or corrections before proceeding towards the grant of a patent.\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n### 1. Preparation and Submission of the First Examination Report\n- **Section 12(2)**: The examiner is required to make an investigation to determine whether the invention, as claimed in any claim of the complete specification, has been anticipated by publication in India or elsewhere.\n- **Section 14**: After receiving the report from the Examiner, the Controller must communicate the gist of objections to the applicant and provide them with an opportunity to be heard if required.\n\n### 2. Timeframe for Disposal\n- **Rule 24B(2)(iii)**: The Controller is expected to dispose of the application within one month after receiving the First Examination Report.\n- **Rule 24B(3)**: Within one month from the date of disposal, the Controller must forward a list of objections to the applicant or their authorized agent.\n\n### 3. Time for Applicant to Put Application in Order\n- **Section 21**: The applicant is required to put the application in order for grant within six months from the date of the First Examination Report, and this period may be extended by an additional three months if necessary.\n- **Rule 24B(5) & (6)**: These rules provide the procedural framework for extending the time limit for putting the application in order.\n\n## Explanation of the Legal Framework\n\n### Purpose and Reasoning Behind the First Examination Report\nThe First Examination Report serves as a critical tool to ensure that only inventions meeting the legal criteria are granted patents. It is designed to identify any prior art, anticipate potential issues with novelty or inventive step, and provide an opportunity for the applicant to address these objections.\n\nAs explained in **(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 175)**:\n> “He makes his decision whether ‘to grant’ or ‘reject’ or have the application amended based on the said report. However, before final disposal, he must send his list of objections to the applicant and ask him to comply. Further, he must also provide an opportunity to the applicant of being heard.”\n\nThis process ensures fairness by allowing the applicant a chance to respond before any adverse decision is taken.\n\n### Timeframe for Applicant to Put Application in Order\nThe time limit set under **Section 21** and **Rules 24B(5) & (6)** provides a structured timeframe within which applicants must comply with the Controller’s objections. This ensures that applications do not remain pending indefinitely, thereby maintaining efficiency in the patent grant process.\n\nAccording to **(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 191)**:\n> “Applicant amends the application in compliance with Controller’s objections and puts the application in the order of grant within 6m, extendable by 3 months.”\n\nThis provision balances the need for timely resolution with flexibility to accommodate unforeseen circumstances.\n\n## Verified Cases\n\n**(No verified case on record for this topic - check IndianKanoon before citing one)**\n\n---\n\nThis concludes the study notes on \"First Examination Report and the Time to Put the Application in Order\" from the **Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**. These notes are structured to help students understand the legal framework, procedural requirements, and reasoning behind these provisions under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 14 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 14. Consideration of report of examiner by Controller.\n> Where, in respect of an application for a patent, the report of the\n> examiner received by the Controller is adverse to the applicant or\n> requires any amendment of the application, the specification or\n> other documents to ensure compliance with the provisions of this Act\n> or of the rules made thereunder, the Controller, before proceeding\n> to dispose of the application in accordance with the provisions\n> hereinafter appearing, shall communicate as expeditiously as\n> possible the gist of the objections to the applicant and shall, if\n> so required by the applicant within the prescribed period, give him\n> an opportunity of being heard.\n\n**Section 15 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 15. Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,\n> etc., in certain cases.\n> Where the Controller is satisfied that the application or any\n> specification or any other document filed in pursuance thereof does\n> not comply with the requirements of this Act or of any rules made\n> thereunder, the Controller may refuse the application or may require\n> the application, specification or the other documents, as the case\n> may be, to be amended to his satisfaction before he proceeds with\n> the application and refuse the application on failure to do so.\n\n**Section 21 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 21. Time for putting application in order for grant.\n>\n> CHAPTER V OPPOSITION PROCEEDINGS TO GRANT OF PATENTS\n\n**Rule 24C — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 24C. Expedited examination of applications\n> (1)An applicant may file a request for expedited examination in Form\n> 18A along with the fee as specified in the first schedule only by\n> electronic transmission duly authenticated within the period\n> prescribed in rule 24B on any of the following grounds, namely:\n> (a) that India has been indicated as the competent International\n> Searching Authority or elected as an International Preliminary\n> Examining Authority in the corresponding international application;\n> or\n> (b) that the applicant is a startup; or\n> (c) that the applicant is a small entity; or\n> (d) that if the applicant is a natural person or in the case of\n> joint applicants, all the applicants are natural persons, then the\n> applicant or at least one of the applicants is a female; or\n> (e) that the applicant is a department of the Government; or\n> (f) that the applicant is an institution established by a Central,\n> Provincial or State Act, which is owned or controlled by the\n> Government; or\n> (g) that the applicant is a Government company as defined in clause\n> (45) of section 2 of the Companies Act, 2013 (18 of 2013); or\n> (h) that the applicant is an institution wholly or substantially\n> financed by the Government; or\n>\n> Explanation: For the purpose of this clause, the term 'substantially\n> financed' shall have the same meaning as in the explanation to sub-\n> section (1) of section 14 of the Comptroller and Auditor General's\n> (Duties, Powers and Conditions of Service Act, 1971 No.56 of 1971);\n> (i) that the application pertains to a sector which is notified by\n> the Central Government on the basis of a request from the head of a\n> department of the Central Government. Provided that public comments\n>\n>  34\n>\n> are invited before any such notification; or\n> (j) that the applicant is eligible under an arrangement for\n> processing a patent application pursuant to an agreement between\n> Indian Patent Office and a foreign Patent Office.\n>\n> Explanation: The patentability of patent applications filed under\n> clause (j) above will be in accordance with the relevant provisions\n> of the Act.\n>\n> (2) A request for examination filed under rule 24B may be converted\n> to a request for expedited examination under sub-rule (1) of rule\n> 24C by paying the relevant fees and submitting requisite documents\n> as required under sub-rule (1).\n>\n> (3) Except where the application has already been published under\n> sub-section (2) of section 11A or a request for publication under\n> rule 24A has already been filed, a request for expedited examination\n> shall be accompanied by a request for publication under rule 24A.\n>\n> (4) Where the request for expedited examination does not comply with\n> the requirements of this rule, such a request shall be processed in\n> accordance with the provisions contained in rule 24B, with an\n> intimation to the applicant, and shall be deemed to have been filed\n> on the date on which the request for expedited examination was\n> filed.\n>\n> (5)The Controller shall refer the request for expedited examination\n> along with the application and specification and other documents to\n> the examiner, in respect of the applications where the request for\n> expedited examination has been received, in order to file such\n> requests.\n> Provided that a request for expedited examination under this rule\n> filed by a startup or small entity shall not be questioned merely on\n> the ground that the startup or small entity, having filed an\n> application for a patent, ceases to be a startup or small entity due\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 28A — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 28A. Procedure in relation to consideration of report of\n> examiner under section 14\n> In case the applicant contests any of the objections communicated to\n> him, the procedure specified under rule 28 may apply.\n\n**Rule 28 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 28. Procedure in case of anticipation by prior publication\n> (1) If the Controller is satisfied after investigation under section\n> 13 that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete\n> specification has been published in any specification or other\n> document referred to in clause (a) of sub-section (1) or sub-section\n> (2) of the said section, the Controller shall communicate the gist\n> of specific objections and the basis thereof to the applicant and\n> the applicant shall be afforded an opportunity to amend his\n> specification.\n>\n> (2) If the applicant contests any of the objections communicated to\n> him by the Controller under sub-rule (1), or if he refiles his\n> specification along with his observations as to whether or not the\n> specification is to be amended, he shall be given an opportunity to\n> be heard on the matter if he so requests:\n> Provided that such a request shall be made on a date earlier than\n> ten days of the final date of the period preferred to under sub-\n> section (1) of section 21:\n> Provided further that a request for hearing may be allowed to be\n> filed within such a shorter period as the Controller may deem fit in\n> the circumstances of the case.\n>\n>  37\n>\n> (3) If the applicant requests for a hearing under sub-rule (2)\n> within a period of one month from the date of communication of the\n> gist of objections, or, the Controller, considers it desirable to do\n> so, whether or not the applicant has refiled his application, he\n> shall forthwith fix a date and time for hearing having regard to the\n> period remaining for putting the application in order or to the\n> other circumstances of the case.\n>\n> (4) The applicant shall be given ten days’ notice of any such\n> hearing or such shorter notice as appears to the Controller to be\n> reasonable in the circumstances of the case and the applicant shall,\n> as soon as possible, notify the Controller whether he will attend\n> the hearing.\n>\n> (5) After hearing the applicant, or without hearing if the applicant\n> has not attended or has notified that he does not desire to be\n> heard, the Controller may specify or permit such amendment of the\n> specification as he thinks fit to be made and may refuse to grant\n> the patent unless the amendment so specified or permitted is made\n> within such period as may be fixed.\n>\n> (6) The hearing may also be held through video-conferencing or\n> audio-visual communication devices: Provided that such hearing shall\n> be deemed to have taken place at the appropriate office.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purposes of this rule, the expression\n> communication device shall have the same meaning as assigned to it\n> in clause (ha) of sub-section (1) of section 2 of the Information\n> Technology Act, 2000 (21 of 2000).\n>\n> (7) In all cases of hearing, written submissions and the relevant\n> documents, if any, shall be filed within fifteen days from the date\n> of hearing.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the legal framework governing the preparation and submission of the First Examination Report under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2018, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** The question seeks an understanding of how the **First Examination Report (FER)** is prepared and submitted under the **Patents Act, 1970**, including its legal basis and procedural requirements.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 12(2)** of the Patents Act, 1970, the examiner must investigate whether the invention claimed in any claim of the complete specification has been anticipated by a publication in India or elsewhere. This provision is crucial for assessing novelty and ensuring that only inventions meeting legal requirements are considered for patent grant.\n\n**Section 14** mandates that if the report from the Examiner is adverse to the applicant or requires amendments, the Controller must communicate the gist of objections to the applicant as expeditiously as possible. Furthermore, the applicant may be given an opportunity to be heard within a prescribed period. This ensures procedural fairness and allows applicants to respond before any final decision is made.\n\n**Application:** The FER is prepared by the Examiner following a detailed investigation into the novelty, inventive step, and industrial applicability of the invention as claimed in the application. If the report raises objections or requires amendments, the Controller must communicate these to the applicant promptly under **Section 14**, giving them an opportunity to respond.\n\n**Conclusion:** The preparation and submission of the First Examination Report is governed by **Sections 12(2)** and **14** of the Patents Act, 1970. These provisions ensure that the examination process is thorough, fair, and in line with legal requirements for patent grant.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to distinguish between the role of the Examiner (investigation) and the Controller (communication of objections), which are separate but interlinked stages under the Act.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the timeframe within which an applicant must put their application in order after receiving the First Examination Report, and discuss the legal provisions that allow for extension of this period.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** The question asks about the **timeframe** within which an applicant must address objections raised in the First Examination Report and the legal provisions that allow for extension.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 21** of the Patents Act, 1970, the applicant is required to put the application in order for grant within **six months** from the date of the First Examination Report. This period may be extended by an additional **three months** if necessary, as provided under **Rules 24B(5) and (6)**.\n\nThese rules ensure that applicants have a reasonable timeframe to address objections raised by the Controller while preventing unnecessary delays in the patent grant process.\n\n**Application:** Once the applicant receives the FER from the Examiner, they must respond within six months. If the applicant is unable to comply within this period, an application for extension can be made under **Rule 24B(5)** and (6). The Controller may allow such an extension if the applicant demonstrates a valid reason for the delay.\n\n**Conclusion:** The applicant has six months from receiving the First Examination Report to put their application in order. This timeframe is extendable by three months, as permitted under **Section 21** and **Rules 24B(5) & (6)** of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook the importance of **Rule 24B(5) and (6)** in providing flexibility for extensions. It is essential to highlight that this procedural provision ensures fairness and avoids unnecessary delays.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the key objectives behind the preparation of the First Examination Report under the Patents Act, 1970? Discuss its significance in ensuring the quality of patents granted.**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2020)*\n\n**Issue:** This question focuses on the **objectives and significance** of preparing the First Examination Report under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule:** The **First Examination Report (FER)** is a critical tool to ensure that only inventions meeting legal criteria are granted patents. As stated in **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 175**, the FER helps the Controller decide whether to grant or reject an application or require amendments.\n\nIts key objectives include identifying prior art, assessing novelty and inventive step, and ensuring compliance with industrial applicability requirements under **Section 12(2)** of the Act. The report also ensures fairness by allowing applicants to respond before any final decision is taken, as mandated in **Section 14**.\n\n**Application:** By preparing a detailed FER, the Examiner helps identify potential issues early on in the patent application process. This allows for timely corrections or amendments, thereby improving the quality of granted patents and ensuring that only valid inventions are protected under the law.\n\n**Conclusion:** The First Examination Report plays a vital role in assessing the novelty, inventive step, and industrial applicability of an invention. It ensures that only high-quality applications proceed to grant, contributing significantly to the overall integrity of the patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to emphasize the **quality assurance aspect** of the FER. Highlighting how it prevents the granting of invalid patents is essential for a complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the procedural steps that must be followed by the Controller after receiving the First Examination Report? Discuss with reference to relevant provisions of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2023)*\n\n**Issue:** The question seeks an explanation of the **procedural steps** that must be followed by the Controller after receiving the First Examination Report.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 14**, upon receiving an adverse report from the Examiner, the Controller is required to communicate the gist of objections to the applicant as expeditiously as possible. Additionally, if the applicant requests within a prescribed period, they are given an opportunity to be heard before any final decision is made.\n\n**Rule 24B(2)(iii)** mandates that the Controller must dispose of the application within **one month** from receiving the FER. Furthermore, under **Rule 24B(3)**, the Controller must forward a list of objections to the applicant or their authorized agent within one month from the date of disposal.\n\n**Application:** After receiving the FER, the Controller communicates the objections and provides an opportunity for the applicant to respond if required. The application is then disposed of within one month under **Rule 24B(2)(iii)**, followed by the forwarding of a list of objections within another month as per **Rule 24B(3)**.\n\n**Conclusion:** The Controller must follow a structured process after receiving the FER: communicate objections to the applicant, allow for an opportunity to be heard, dispose of the application within one month, and forward a list of objections within another month. These steps are governed by **Section 14**, **Rule 24B(2)(iii)**, and **Rule 24B(3)**.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse the timelines under **Rules 24B(2)(iii)** and **24B(3)**. It is important to clearly distinguish between the one-month period for disposal and another month for forwarding objections.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - First Examination Report and the Time to Put the Application in Order\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 14 | **TYRE** | TYRE inflated with objections burst, alerting applicant. |\n| Sec. 15 | **TOWEL** | TOWEL soaked in complaints mopped up before verdict. |\n| Sec. 21 | **NET** | NET cast at deadline snared last-minute fixes. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **12-24** - examination, objections, time for putting in order\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Objections communicated to the applicant - which provision?  (picture: TYRE)\n2. Opportunity of being heard before adverse decision - which provision?  (picture: TOWEL)\n3. Period for putting the application in order for grant - which provision?  (picture: NET)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 12(2) | Examiner checks if invention is anticipated by prior publication | Prior art check image\n- Section 14 | Controller communicates objections and allows applicant to be heard | Objection notice image\n- Section 15 | Controller may refuse or require amendment of application | Refusal or amend flag\n- Section 21 | Applicant must put application in order within 6 months (extendable) | 6-month deadline clock\n- Rule 24B(2)(iii) | Controller disposes of application within one month of FER | One-month disposal timer\n- Rule 24B(3) | Controller sends objections list to applicant within one month | Objection list delivery\n- Rule 24B(5) & (6) | Provides procedure for extending time limit for amendments | Extension request form\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Confusing the timeframe under Section 21 with Rule 24B(3)\n- Examiners expect: Clear understanding of FER’s role in patent grant process\n- Structure tip: Always mention both Section 21 and Rules 24B when discussing time limits","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where the old bazaars meet modern innovation, a young patent agent named Ravi lived with his mother, a retired schoolteacher who had once taught him to count every grain in a sack. One day, while walking through the bustling Charminar market, he heard about a new invention — a device that could purify water using solar energy. Inspired by the idea of innovation and justice, Ravi decided to help the inventor, Mr. Dinesh, apply for a patent.\n\nThe first step in this journey was understanding the **First Examination Report (FER)**, which Ravi learned was like a critical letter from an examiner who had studied all the previous inventions related to water purification. This report would determine whether Mr. Dinesh’s invention met the legal standards of novelty and usefulness — the same way a teacher checks if students have understood a lesson before moving on.\n\nRavi took the application to the Controller, who was like the head of an examination board. The Controller received the FER from the examiner and had to send a **summary of objections** to Mr. Dinesh within one month, as per **Section 14**, just like how a teacher would give feedback after an exam.\n\nBut there was a catch — the Controller had only thirty days to make this decision. If he failed to do so, it could delay the entire process. Ravi made sure that the timeline was clear in his mind: **Rule 24B(2)(iii)** said the Controller must dispose of the application within one month after receiving the report.\n\nOnce the objections were sent, Mr. Dinesh had a chance to respond — just like a student who gets extra time on an assignment if they ask for it. According to **Section 21**, he had six months from the date of the FER to put his application in order for grant. If he needed more time, he could get three additional months, as per **Rules 24B(5) & (6)**.\n\nRavi helped Mr. Dinesh understand each objection and amend his application with care. He imagined that every objection was like a lamp — twenty-one of them in total, representing the six-month period under **Section 21**. Each lamp had to be lit before the final grant could be given.\n\nOne day, while Ravi was walking home from the patent office, he passed by a shop where an old man was selling sacks filled with grain. The sacks were numbered — each one holding exactly seventy-three grains of rice. He remembered that **Section 15** gave the Controller the power to refuse applications if they didn’t meet legal standards. Just like how a shopkeeper would reject a sack with fewer than seventy-three grains, the Controller had the right to say no to an application that wasn’t up to par.\n\nAfter months of work, Mr. Dinesh’s application was finally in order — all twenty-one lamps were lit, and every objection had been addressed. Ravi felt proud as he watched his friend receive a notice from the Controller granting him the patent for his solar water purifier.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Section 21 | The applicant has six months (extendable by three) to put the application in order after receiving the First Examination Report. |\n| Seventy-three grains of rice | Section 15 | The Controller may refuse an application if it does not comply with the requirements of the Act or rules made thereunder. |\n| One month for the Controller | Rule 24B(2)(iii) | The Controller must dispose of the application within one month after receiving the First Examination Report. |\n| Summary of objections sent to Mr. Dinesh | Section 14 | Before proceeding with final disposal, the Controller must communicate the gist of objections to the applicant and provide an opportunity to be heard if required. |","memory":"### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for \"First Examination Report and the Time to Put the Application in Order\"**\n\n**FERTIO**  \n- **F**irst Examination Report (FER)  \n- **E**xaminer’s findings and objections  \n- **R**eport sent to Controller  \n- **T**imeframe: 1 month from disposal of report  \n- **I**ssued to applicant or agent  \n- **O**bjects must be addressed within 6 months  \n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors for Sections**\n\n- **Section 12**: *Examination of application* — Imagine a **\"12-step process\"** in a courtroom, where each step is part of the legal review of an application.  \n- **Section 13**: *Search for anticipation* — Picture a **\"13th-century manuscript\"**, representing old prior art that can invalidate new claims.  \n- **Section 14**: *Consideration of report by Controller* — Think of a **\"14-point checklist\"** used by the Controller to evaluate an application thoroughly.  \n- **Section 21**: *Time for putting application in order for grant* — Visualize a **\"21-day countdown\"**, emphasizing urgency and deadlines.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **First Examination Report (FER) vs. Final Report**  \n  → *FER is the initial report from the examiner, while the final report comes after amendments or objections are addressed.*  \n\n- **Time to put application in order (Section 21) vs. Time for Controller’s decision (Section 14)**  \n  → *Section 21 sets a time limit for the applicant to correct and submit the application, whereas Section 14 deals with how long the Controller takes to evaluate the report.*  \n\n- **Ordinary Examination vs. Expedited Examination**  \n  → *Ordinary examination is standard and takes longer; expedited is faster but must be requested by the applicant or a person interested.*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Six months from the first statement of objections, extendable by three months via Form 4. Rule 24B(5) and (11) specify this timeframe.","question":"What is the period for putting an application in order for grant under Section 21?"},{"answer":"Within three months of receiving the last reply or the six-month deadline, whichever comes first. Rule 24B(12) governs this.","question":"How does the Controller dispose of an application after the first examination report?"},{"answer":"An application is deemed abandoned if not put in order within six months. Section 21 and Rule 24B(5) define this.","question":"What constitutes deemed abandonment under Section 21?"},{"answer":"Failure to comply leads to non-appealable abandonment under Section 117A(2). This risks losing the application permanently.","question":"Why is the six-month period critical for applicants?"},{"answer":"Such requests are not questioned if the startup ceases to qualify due to turnover or recognition lapses. Rule 24B(5) provides this protection.","question":"How are expedited examination requests handled for startups?"},{"answer":"If objections persist despite communication, to address unresolved issues. This ensures the applicant's opportunity to be heard.","question":"When should an applicant request a hearing?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 14 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Amendments to specifications”.","options":["Rule 9","Rule 14","Rule 57","Rule 115"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Amendments to specifications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 14 is headed “Amendments to specifications”.","options":["Power to extend time specified or condone delay","Amendments to specifications","Refusal to recognise as patent agent","Form of reference to another patent"],"question":"Rule 14 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 15 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,”.","options":["Section 59","Section 19","Section 15","Section 86"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 15 is headed “Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,”.","options":["Power of Central Government to use inventions for purposes of","Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Convention countries","Exercise of discretionary powers by Controller"],"question":"Section 15 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 21 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Filing of priority document”.","options":["Rule 21","Rule 14","Rule 59","Rule 56"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Filing of priority document?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 21 is headed “Filing of priority document”.","options":["Filing of priority document","Constitution of Opposition Board and its proceeding","Communication of result of reconsideration under section","Application for compulsory licence etc"],"question":"Rule 21 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 14 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Consideration of report of examiner by Controller”.","options":["Section 14","Section 68","Section 131","Section 124"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Consideration of report of examiner by Controller?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 14 is headed “Consideration of report of examiner by Controller”.","options":["Consideration of report of examiner by Controller","Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Licensing of related patents","Validity of patents of addition"],"question":"Section 14 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 21 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Time for putting application in order for grant”.","options":["Section 102","Section 105","Section 63","Section 21"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Time for putting application in order for grant?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 21 is headed “Time for putting application in order for grant”.","options":["Time for putting application in order for grant","Reports of Examiners to be confidential","Certificate of validity of specification and costs of","Burden of proof in case of suits concerning infringement"],"question":"Section 21 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 15 specifies that the Controller may refuse the application on failure to amend as required.","options":["The application is automatically granted","The Controller may refuse the application","The applicant is entitled to a hearing","The application is referred to the Appellate Board"],"question":"What happens if the applicant fails to amend the application as required by the Controller under Section 15?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 14 explicitly states that the applicant may request an opportunity to be heard within the prescribed period.","options":["The examiner","The applicant","The Controller","The public"],"question":"Who is entitled to request an opportunity to be heard under Section 14?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 14 mandates that the Controller communicate objections 'as expeditiously as possible'.","options":["Within 15 days of receiving the report","As expeditiously as possible","Within 30 days of the application filing","Only after the hearing"],"question":"Under Section 14, what is the Controller's obligation regarding the timing of communicating objections?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 15 explicitly states that the Controller may refuse the application if amendments are not made.","options":["The applicant is entitled to compensation","The Controller may refuse the application","The application is automatically published","The patent is granted provisionally"],"question":"Which of the following is a consequence of non-compliance with the Controller's requirements under Section 15?"}]},"s1p_19":{"notes":"# Amendment of Application and Specification – Study Notes for Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\n\n## 1. Definition\n\nAmendment of application and specification refers to any change made in the content or form of a patent application or its complete specification after it has been filed with the Controller of Patents. These amendments can be made at various stages—before the grant of the patent (pre-grant) or after the grant (post-grant). They are governed by specific provisions under the **Patents Act, 1970**, and related rules.\n\n## 2. Key Concepts\n\n### 2.1 Types of Amendments\nAmendments can be:\n- **Substantive**: Changes that alter the scope or meaning of the claims.\n- **Non-substantive**: Corrections to clerical errors, formatting issues, or minor changes in names or addresses.\n\n### 2.2 Stages for Amendment\n- **Before Grant (Pre-grant)**: Amendments are allowed under **Section 57** of the Patents Act, 1970.\n- **After Grant (Post-grant)**: Amendments can be made under **Section 58**, and also through rules like Rule 33 of the Patents Rules, 2003.\n\n### 2.3 Nature of Amendments\nAmendments must be:\n- Made by way of **disclaimer**, **correction**, or **explanation**.\n- Must not introduce any new matter that was not disclosed in the original specification.\n\n## 3. Legal Provisions\n\n### 3.1 Section 57: Amendment Before Grant\nUnder **Section 57** of the Patents Act, 1970:\n- An applicant may request an amendment to the application or complete specification.\n- The application for amendment must state the nature of the proposed changes and provide reasons.\n- Amendments can include changes in name, address, priority dates, and claims.\n\n### 3.2 Section 58: Amendment After Grant\nUnder **Section 58**:\n- Amendments may be made after the grant of a patent.\n- Substantive amendments are subject to publication and opposition procedures.\n- Any interested party can oppose an amendment within three months of its publication.\n\n### 3.3 Rule 33 of the Patents Rules, 2003\nRule 33 provides procedural details for:\n- The form and manner in which applications for amendment must be made (in **Form 13**).\n- Publication requirements for substantive amendments.\n- Opposition procedures similar to those under Section 25 (opposition proceedings).\n\n## 4. Why the Law is Structured This Way\n\n### 4.1 Ensuring Clarity and Fairness\nThe law restricts amendments to prevent applicants from introducing new matter or altering the scope of claims after the application has been filed, thereby ensuring **fairness** in the examination process (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 670).\n\n### 4.2 Preventing Abuse of the System\nBy limiting amendments to those made by way of disclaimer or correction, the law prevents **unfair advantage** that might be gained through late changes in claims or specifications (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 439).\n\n### 4.3 Encouraging Transparency\nSubstantive amendments are published to allow for **opposition**, ensuring transparency and giving interested parties an opportunity to raise objections.\n\n## 5. Procedural Aspects\n\n### 5.1 Form of Application\n- Applications for amendment must be made in **Form 13** (Rule 33, Patents Rules, 2003).\n\n### 5.2 Publication and Opposition\n- Substantive amendments are published.\n- Any interested party can oppose the amendment within three months from the date of publication.\n\n### 5.3 Controller's Role\n- The **Controller** has discretion to allow or disallow an amendment based on its nature and whether it meets the criteria under Sections 57 and 58.\n\n## 6. Verified Cases (if any)\n\n**(No verified case on record for this topic - check IndianKanoon before citing one)**\n\n---\n\nThis concludes the detailed study notes on **\"Amendment of Application and Specification\"** as required for the **Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**. These notes are based on the legal provisions from the **Bare Act reference block**, explanations from the textbook, and verified case law where available.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 57 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 57. Amendment of application and specification before Controller.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions of section 59, the Controller may,\n> upon application made under this section in the prescribed manner by\n> an applicant for a patent or by a patentee, allow the application\n> for the patent or the complete specification or any document\n> relating thereto to be amended subject to such conditions, if any,\n> as the Controller thinks fit:\n> Provided that the Controller shall not pass any order allowing or\n> refusing an application to amend an application for a patent or a\n> specification or any document relating thereto under this section\n> while any suit before a court for the infringement of the patent or\n> any proceeding before the High Court for the revocation of the\n> patent is pending, whether the suit or proceeding commenced before\n> or after the filing of the application to amend.\n>\n> (2) Every application for leave to amend an application for a patent\n> or a complete specification or any document relating thereto under\n> this section shall state the nature of the proposed amendment, and\n> shall give full particulars of the reasons for which the application\n> is made.\n>\n> (3) Any application for leave to amend an application for a patent\n> or a complete specification or a document related thereto under this\n> section made after the grant of patent and the nature of the\n> proposed amendment may be published.\n>\n> (4) Where an application is published under sub-section (3), any\n> person interested may, within the prescribed period after the\n> publication thereof, give notice to the Controller of opposition\n> thereto; and where such a notice is given within the period\n> aforesaid, the Controller shall notify the person by whom the\n> application under this section is made and shall give to that person\n> and to the opponent an opportunity to be heard before he decides the\n> case.\n>\n> (5) An amendment under this section of a complete specification may\n> be, or include, an amendment of the priority date of a claim.\n>\n> (6) The provisions of this section shall be without prejudice to the\n>\n>  50\n>\n> right of an applicant for a patent to amend his specification or any\n> other document related thereto to comply with the directions of the\n> Controller issued before the grant of a patent.\n\n**Section 58 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 58. Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court.\n> (1) In any proceeding before the High Court for the revocation of a\n> patent, the High Court, may, subject to the provisions contained in\n> section 59, allow the patentee to amend his complete specification\n> in such manner and subject to such terms as to costs, advertisement\n> or otherwise, as the High Court may think fit, and if in any\n> proceedings for revocation the High Court decides that the patent is\n> invalid, it may allow the specification to be amended under this\n> section instead of revoking the patent.\n>\n> (2) Where an application for an order under this section is made to\n> the High Court, the applicant shall give notice of the application\n> to the Controller, and the Controller shall be entitled to appear\n> and be heard, and shall appear if so directed by the High Court.\n>\n> (3) Copies of all orders of the High Court allowing the patentee to\n> amend the specification shall be transmitted by the High Court to\n> the Controller who shall, on receipt thereof, cause an entry thereof\n> and reference thereto to be made in the register.\n\n**Section 59 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 59. Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or\n> specification.\n> (1) No amendment of an application for a patent or a complete\n> specification or any document relating thereto shall be made except\n> by way of disclaimer, correction or Explanation, and no amendment\n> thereof shall be allowed, except for the purpose of incorporation of\n> actual fact, and no amendment of a complete specification shall be\n> allowed, the effect of which would be that the specification as\n> amended would claim or describe matter not in substance disclosed or\n> shown in the specification before the amendment, or that any claim\n> of the specification as amended would not fall wholly within the\n> scope of a claim of the specification before the amendment.\n>\n> (2) Where after the date of grant of patent any amendment of the\n> specification or any other documents related thereto is allowed by\n> the Controller or by the High Court, as the case may be,\n> (a) the amendment shall for all purposes be deemed to form part of\n>\n>  51\n>\n> the specification along with other documents related thereto;\n> (b) the fact that the specification or any other documents related\n> thereto has been amended shall be published as expeditiously as\n> possible; and\n> (c) the right of the applicant or patentee to make amendment shall\n> not be called in question except on the ground of fraud.\n>\n> (3) In construing the specification as amended, reference may be\n> made to the specification as originally accepted.\n>\n>  52\n>\n> CHAPTER XI RESTORATION OF LAPSED PATENTS\n\n**Rule 81 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 81. Amendment of application, specification or any document\n> relating thereto\n> (1) An application under section 57 for the amendment of an\n> application for a patent or a complete specification or any document\n> related thereto shall be made in Form 13.\n>\n> (2) If the application for amendment under sub-rule (1) relates to\n> an application for a patent which has not been granted, the\n> Controller shall determine whether and subject to what conditions,\n> if any, the amendment shall be allowed.\n>\n> (3) (a) If the application for amendment under sub-rule (1) is made\n> after grant of patent and the nature of the proposed amendment is\n> substantive, the application shall be published.\n> (b) Any person interested in opposing the application for amendment\n> shall give a notice of opposition in Form 14 within three months\n> from the date of publication of the application.\n> (c) The procedure specified in rules 57 to 63 relating to the filing\n> of a written statement, reply statement, leaving evidence, hearing\n> and costs shall, as far as may be, apply to the hearing of the\n> opposition under section 57 as they apply to the hearing of an\n> opposition proceeding.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the provisions of Section 57 and Section 58 of the Patents Act, 1970 regarding amendment of application and specification before and after grant of a patent.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2015, 2018, 2021)*\n\n**Issue**: The question seeks an explanation of the provisions in **Sections 57 and 58** of the Patents Act, 1970 regarding amendments to patent applications before and after grant.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- **Section 57** allows for amendments to a patent application or specification **before grant**, subject to conditions laid down by the Controller. The applicant must state the nature of the amendment and provide reasons (Section 57(2)). Substantive amendments are published, allowing interested parties to oppose within three months (Section 57(3)–(4)).  \n- **Section 58** allows for amendments after grant, particularly in revocation proceedings before the High Court. The High Court may allow such amendments under certain conditions and must notify the Controller of any orders passed (Section 58(1)–(3)).  \n- **Section 59** imposes restrictions on amendments, stating that they can only be made by way of **disclaimer**, **correction**, or **explanation**. Amendments must not introduce new matter not disclosed in the original specification (Section 59(1)).\n\n**Application**:  \nThese provisions are designed to ensure clarity and fairness in the patent process. Under Section 57, an applicant may request amendments such as changes to names, addresses, priority dates, or claims before the grant of a patent. However, any substantive amendment must be published, allowing for opposition by interested parties within three months (Section 57(3)–(4)). This ensures transparency and prevents unfair advantage to applicants who might otherwise alter their claims without scrutiny.\n\nAfter grant, Section 58 allows amendments in revocation proceedings before the High Court. For instance, if a patent is found invalid during revocation proceedings, the High Court may allow the specification to be amended instead of outright revoking the patent (Section 58(1)). Any such amendment must also be published and made part of the register by the Controller (Section 58(3)).\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nSections 57 and 58 of the Patents Act, 1970 provide a structured framework for amending applications and specifications both before and after grant. These provisions ensure transparency, fairness, and prevent the introduction of new matter not originally disclosed in the specification.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students overlook the importance of **Section 59**, which imposes strict limitations on amendments to prevent the addition of new matter or alteration of claim scope beyond what was originally disclosed. This is a critical point that must be addressed in any answer discussing patent amendments.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the nature and types of amendments allowed under Section 57 and Section 58 of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2016, 2020)*\n\n**Issue**: The question asks for a discussion on the **nature and types of amendments** permitted under Sections 57 and 58.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- Amendments may be either **substantive**, which alter the scope or meaning of claims, or **non-substantive**, such as corrections to clerical errors, formatting issues, or minor changes in names or addresses.  \n- Under **Section 57**, amendments can include changes in name, address, priority dates, and claims (Section 57(1)). Substantive amendments must be published and may face opposition from interested parties within three months of publication (Section 57(3)–(4)).  \n- Under **Section 58**, substantive amendments are allowed after grant, particularly in revocation proceedings before the High Court. These amendments can also involve changes to claims or specifications but require court approval and must be published as well (Section 58(1)–(3)).\n\n**Application**:  \nFor example, if an applicant wishes to correct a clerical error in their specification, such as an incorrect address, this would fall under **non-substantive amendments** and may not require publication or opposition. However, if the applicant seeks to narrow a broad claim by introducing a disclaimer, this is considered a **substantive amendment**, which must be published and open for opposition within three months (Section 57(3)–(4)).  \n\nSimilarly, after grant, an amendment to remove an invalid claim in response to revocation proceedings would fall under Section 58. The High Court may allow such an amendment instead of revoking the patent entirely, provided it meets the conditions set out in the section.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nSections 57 and 58 permit both **substantive** and **non-substantive amendments**, with distinct procedural requirements for each. Substantive amendments are subject to publication and opposition, while non-substantive ones are generally less restrictive but still governed by specific rules.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: A common mistake is failing to distinguish clearly between **substantive** and **non-substantive** amendments in the answer. Emphasizing this distinction with examples from the law will enhance clarity.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the restrictions on amendment of a patent specification under Section 59 of the Patents Act, 1970? Explain with reference to the object of such provisions.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2017, 2022)*\n\n**Issue**: The question asks for an explanation of the **restrictions on amendments** under Section 59 and their purpose.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- Under **Section 59**, no amendment can be made except by way of **disclaimer**, **correction**, or **explanation** (Section 59(1)).  \n- Amendments must not introduce any new matter that was not disclosed in the original specification. The amended specification must not claim or describe anything not previously disclosed, nor should a claim fall outside the scope of an earlier claim (Section 59(1)).  \n- After grant, amendments are deemed part of the specification and must be published expeditiously (Section 59(2)).\n\n**Application**:  \nFor instance, if an applicant seeks to add new subject matter not disclosed in the original application—such as a previously unclaimed embodiment of an invention—the Controller may reject this amendment under Section 59(1), as it introduces **new matter**. This rule prevents applicants from altering the scope of their claims after filing without proper disclosure, ensuring fairness and transparency in the patent process.\n\nSimilarly, if an applicant tries to broaden a claim beyond what was originally disclosed, such as introducing a new method or application not previously mentioned, this would also be disallowed under Section 59(1).\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nSection 59 imposes strict restrictions on amendments to prevent the introduction of **new matter** and ensure that claims remain within the scope of the original specification. This helps maintain fairness in patent examination and prevents abuse of the system.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to connect the **object** of Section 59 with its practical implications, such as preventing unfair advantage or ensuring transparency. It is important to link these aspects explicitly in the answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the procedural requirements for making an amendment under Rule 33 of the Patents Rules, 2003? Explain with reference to Sections 57 and 58 of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 1× (2019)*\n\n**Issue**: The question asks for an explanation of **procedural requirements** under Rule 33 in relation to Sections 57 and 58.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- **Rule 33** of the Patents Rules, 2003 provides procedural details for amendments. Applications must be made in **Form 13**, and substantive amendments are subject to publication (Rule 33).  \n- Under **Section 57**, substantive amendments before grant must be published, allowing interested parties to oppose within three months (Section 57(3)–(4)).  \n- Under **Section 58**, after grant, amendments can also be made in revocation proceedings before the High Court. These are subject to similar procedural requirements as those under Section 25.\n\n**Application**:  \nFor example, if an applicant wishes to amend their specification by introducing a disclaimer or correcting an error, they must file the application in **Form 13**, as required by Rule 33. If the amendment is substantive, it will be published and open for opposition within three months (Section 57(3)–(4)).  \n\nAfter grant, if a patentee seeks to amend their specification in response to revocation proceedings, they must apply through the High Court under Section 58. The Controller must also be notified of such amendments, and any orders passed by the court are transmitted to the Controller for registration (Section 58(3)).\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nRule 33 outlines the **formal procedures** for making amendments, including the use of Form 13 and publication requirements. These align with the procedural provisions under Sections 57 and 58, ensuring transparency and fairness in the amendment process.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: The key point is to clearly link **Rule 33** with the **provisions in Sections 57 and 58**, particularly regarding **publication** and **opposition procedures**. Students often overlook this connection.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Amendment of Application and Specification\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 57 | **LOCK** | LOCK clicks as it secures a legal filing's amendment. |\n| Sec. 58 | **LEAF** | LEAF flips, revealing a disclaimer in a patent's correction. |\n| Sec. 59 | **LIP** | LIP parts, revealing a hidden claim's lack of disclosure. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **57-59** - amendment of application and specification\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Amendment before the Controller and before the court or Appellate authority - which provision?  (picture: LOCK)\n2. By way of disclaimer, correction or explanation only - which provision?  (picture: LEAF)\n3. No amendment that claims matter not in substance disclosed - which provision?  (picture: LIP)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 57 | Allows pre-grant amendments to application/specification | File request with reasons  \n- Section 58 | Post-grant amendments allowed under High Court | Post-grant changes need court approval  \n- Rule 33 (Patents Rules, 2003) | Sets form and publication for amendments | Use Form 13 for amendment requests  \n- Section 59 | Restricts amendments to disclaimers or corrections | No new matter in amended specs  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid introducing new matter during amendments – it's a common mistake.  \n- Examiners expect clear references to Section 57 and 58 for pre- and post-grant changes.  \n- Always use Form 13 as specified in Rule 33 for amendment applications.","storyEN":"Once upon a time in Hyderabad, there lived a young patent agent named Ravi who worked at a small but respected firm called **InnovateLaw Solutions**. His office was on the 10th floor of a building in Banjara Hills, and it had twenty-one lamps hanging from the ceiling — one for each of the twenty-one sections he studied daily.\n\nOne day, Ravi received an urgent call from his client, Meera, who was a biotech researcher working at **Osmania University**. Her patent application had been rejected due to some discrepancies in her claims. She needed help amending her application before it was too late.\n\nRavi remembered that under **Section 57**, any amendment must be made with the Controller's approval and must not introduce new matter. He also recalled that the form for such an amendment is **Form 13** — a rule from **Rule 33 of the Patents Rules, 2003**. Ravi knew he had to act fast.\n\nHe called Meera and told her: “You need to submit your amendment in **Form 13**, and it must state the nature of the proposed changes clearly. Also, you have to give full reasons for why the amendment is being made.”\n\nMeera followed his instructions precisely and submitted the form with all the necessary details. But just as she thought she was out of the woods, Ravi received a call from the Controller’s office saying that the amendment would be published since it was **substantive** in nature.\n\nRavi explained to Meera: “This means anyone interested can oppose the amendment within three months of its publication. We need to make sure our case is strong.”\n\nMeera and Ravi prepared for potential opposition, but they were lucky — no one opposed the amendment. The Controller approved it, and the application was revised.\n\nMonths later, after Meera’s patent had been granted, she wanted to update her specification with some new findings. She approached Ravi again and asked: “Can I change my claims now that the patent is already granted?”\n\nRavi said, “Yes, but under **Section 58**, you can make amendments even after grant. However, any substantive changes will be published, and interested parties may oppose them.”\n\nMeera submitted her amendment in **Form 13** again, this time with a new set of claims that reflected the updated research. The Controller approved it once more.\n\nRavi had learned a lot from these cases — how amendments must be made by way of **disclaimer**, **correction**, or **explanation**, and how they cannot introduce any matter not already disclosed in the original specification, as per **Section 59**.\n\nHe even remembered that if an amendment was allowed after grant, it would be published “as expeditiously as possible,” so transparency is a key principle of the law. He also recalled that the Controller has discretion to allow or disallow amendments based on their nature and whether they meet the criteria under **Sections 57** and **58**.\n\nRavi’s office had twenty-one lamps, but now he felt like he had fifty more — because he knew every rule of amendment by heart. His client Meera was happy, her patent was safe, and Ravi had passed another test in his journey as a patent agent.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Section 57 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Amendments to an application before grant must be made with the Controller's approval and must not introduce new matter. |\n| Form 13 | Rule 33 of the Patents Rules, 2003 | Applications for amendment must be made in a prescribed form, which is **Form 13**. |\n| Three months | Section 58 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Any interested party may oppose an amendment within three months of its publication. |\n| Fifty more lamps | Sections 57 and 58 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Amendments can be made both before and after grant, but they must follow specific rules and procedures. |\n| Disclaimer, correction, or explanation | Section 59 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Amendments must be made by way of disclaimer, correction, or explanation and cannot introduce new matter not disclosed in the original specification. |\n| Published as expeditiously as possible | Section 59 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Any amendment allowed after grant must be published quickly to ensure transparency. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for \"Amendment of Application and Specification\" from Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n\n- **A.M.E.N.D.**\n  - **A**pplication must state the nature of amendment  \n  - **M**ust be by way of disclaimer, correction or explanation  \n  - **E**ach amendment should not go beyond the scope of specification  \n  - **N**ature of amendment is important (substantive vs non-substantive)  \n  - **D**ocument is published for oppositions\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 57**: *Amendment before grant* – Imagine a **\"57-degree angle\"** representing the **direction of change** in an application.\n- **Section 58**: *Amendment after grant* – Picture a **\"58-story building\"**, symbolizing that amendments are **more complex and require more scrutiny after grant**.\n- **Rule 33**: *Form for amendment application* – Think of the number **33** as **\"three-three\"**, like **\"form 13\"** (from Rule 33), which is used to apply for an amendment.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Substantive vs Non-substantive Amendments**:  \n  - *Substantive amendments* require publication and opposition, while *non-substantive* do not.  \n  - *Tip*: **\"Substantive changes are bigger – they need more attention.\"**\n\n- **Amendment before grant vs after grant**:  \n  - *Before grant*, the Controller decides whether to allow it; *after grant*, the amendment is published and open for opposition.  \n  - *Tip*: **\"Before grant, the Controller controls; after grant, others can object.\"**","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Amendments by disclaimer, correction, or explanation only. Section 59(7) prohibits amendments claiming new matter not disclosed earlier.","question":"What types of amendments are allowed under Section 59 of the Patents Act?"},{"answer":"Amendments may be published, and interested parties may oppose within the prescribed period. Section 59(3)-(4) outlines this process.","question":"What procedure applies to amendments after patent grant under Section 59?"},{"answer":"To prevent unfair advantage and ensure transparency. Section 59(7) explicitly bars such amendments.","question":"Why can't amendments introduce new matter not previously disclosed?"},{"answer":"The High Court may allow amendments under Section 58(1), subject to costs and other terms, if the patent is invalid but amendable.","question":"How can a patentee amend a specification before the High Court?"},{"answer":"Opposition must be filed within the prescribed period after publication. Section 59(4) mandates this timeframe.","question":"What is the time limit for opposing an amendment published under Section 59?"},{"answer":"Amendments by disclaimer to show technical advances were allowed as 'explanations' under Section 59, per the court's decision.","question":"How did the Solvay vs Du Pont case interpret amendments by disclaimer?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 59 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Filing of reply evidence by opponent”.","options":["Rule 129","Rule 94","Rule 59","Rule 133"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Filing of reply evidence by opponent?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 59 is headed “Filing of reply evidence by opponent”.","options":["Filing of reply evidence by opponent","Form of application for the issue of a duplicate patent","Application for review of decisions or setting aside of","Mention of inventor"],"question":"Rule 59 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 57 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Filing of written statement of opposition and evidence”.","options":["Rule 88","Rule 85","Rule 57","Rule 29"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Filing of written statement of opposition and evidence?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 57 is headed “Filing of written statement of opposition and evidence”.","options":["Payment of fees","Inspection and supply of published documents","Filing of written statement of opposition and evidence","Power to extend time specified or condone delay"],"question":"Rule 57 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 58 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court”.","options":["Section 58","Section 146","Section 148","Section 15"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 58 is headed “Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court”.","options":["Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court","Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain","Controller and other officers","Rights of patentees of lapsed patents which have been restored"],"question":"Section 58 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 57 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Amendment of application and specification before Controller”.","options":["Section 87","Section 128","Section 110","Section 57"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Amendment of application and specification before Controller?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 57 is headed “Amendment of application and specification before Controller”.","options":["Transmission of orders of courts to Controller","Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement","Amendment of application and specification before Controller","Residents not to apply for patents outside India without prior"],"question":"Section 57 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 58 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Filing of reply statement and evidence”.","options":["Rule 121","Rule 100","Rule 104","Rule 58"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Filing of reply statement and evidence?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 58 is headed “Filing of reply statement and evidence”.","options":["Correction of clerical errors","Manner of publication of the revocation order","Inspection and supply of published documents","Filing of reply statement and evidence"],"question":"Rule 58 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 59 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or”.","options":["Section 73","Section 59","Section 148","Section 120"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 59 is headed “Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or”.","options":["Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or","Powers of Controller to correct clerical errors, etc","Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents","Register to be open for inspection"],"question":"Section 59 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Amendment of the priority date of a claim is permitted.","options":["Amendment of the patent's title","Amendment of the priority date of a claim","Amendment of the inventor's name","Amendment of the patent's abstract"],"question":"Which type of amendment is explicitly permitted under Section 57(5)?"}]},"s1p_20":{"notes":"# Division, Conversion and Withdrawal of Applications: Study Notes for Patent Agent Examination\n\n## 1. Definition and Key Concepts\n\n### What are Division, Conversion and Withdrawal of Applications?\n\n- **Division**: A division is a process where an application containing multiple inventions is split into two or more separate applications. This usually occurs when the original application includes more than one invention that can be patented separately.\n  \n- **Conversion**: Conversion refers to changing the nature of an application after it has been filed—for example, converting a provisional application into a complete specification.\n\n- **Withdrawal**: Withdrawal is the formal act of canceling or abandoning an application by the applicant before it is granted. This may happen for various reasons such as lack of interest, financial constraints, or strategic decision-making.\n\n### Legal Basis\n\nThese procedures are governed under **Section 16** and **Section 20** of *The Patents Act, 1970* (as amended on 01-08-2024).\n\n- **Section 16**: This section empowers the Controller to make orders respecting the division of an application. It allows for the separation of inventions that are not closely related or may be better protected separately.\n\n- **Section 20**: This section deals with the power of the Controller to make orders regarding substitution of applicants and other related matters, which can also impact the withdrawal or conversion process.\n\n## 2. Why These Provisions Exist\n\n### Purpose and Legal Rationale (Based on Textbook)\n\nThe provisions for division, conversion, and withdrawal are designed to:\n\n- **Ensure clarity and precision in patent protection**: By allowing the splitting of applications into separate ones, each invention can be clearly defined and protected without confusion or overlap with other inventions (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 245).\n\n- **Promote fair and efficient use of patent resources**: The ability to convert a provisional specification into a complete one allows applicants to refine their claims based on further research or feedback from the Controller.\n\n- **Provide flexibility for applicants**: Withdrawal provisions allow applicants to abandon applications without prejudice if they are no longer interested in pursuing them, thus avoiding unnecessary costs and delays (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 257).\n\n### Key Considerations\n\n- **Division** is not automatic; the applicant must request it or the Controller may direct it if there are multiple inventions in one application.\n\n- **Conversion** requires that the original application be replaced with a more detailed specification, which must meet all formal requirements under the Act and Rules.\n\n- **Withdrawal** can only occur at certain stages of the patent process. Once an application has been published or examined, withdrawal may not be allowed without specific justification (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 383).\n\n## 3. Practical Implications for Patent Agents\n\n### Role of a Patent Agent in Division Applications\n\n- A patent agent must ensure that the division is justified based on the distinctness of inventions.\n  \n- They should prepare separate specifications and claims for each divided application.\n\n- They may also need to coordinate with the Controller regarding the formalities required for division (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 257).\n\n### Role of a Patent Agent in Conversion Applications\n\n- The agent must ensure that the complete specification is prepared according to Rule 13 of the *Patents Rules, 2003*.\n  \n- They may need to submit additional information or amendments based on feedback from the Controller.\n\n- A clear understanding of the timeline and requirements for conversion is essential (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 245).\n\n### Role of a Patent Agent in Withdrawal Applications\n\n- The agent must advise the applicant on whether withdrawal is appropriate at their current stage.\n\n- They should prepare and submit Form 30 for surrendering the patent or withdraw the application as per Rule 16(1) of the *Patents Rules, 2003*.\n\n- Withdrawal may have legal implications if it occurs after publication; thus, careful consideration is required (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 383).\n\n## 4. Case Law and Practical Examples\n\n### Verified Cases\n\n- **No verified cases are available for this topic**. If a case would normally support a point, write \"(verify this case with a case-law database)\" instead.\n\n### Hypothetical Scenario (for Understanding)\n\nImagine an applicant who files an application covering two distinct inventions: a new type of solar panel and a novel battery storage system. The Controller finds that the application contains more than one invention and may issue a notice under **Section 16** directing the applicant to divide the application into two separate ones.\n\nIf the applicant decides not to proceed with either invention, they may choose to withdraw the application using Form 30 as per **Rule 16(1)** of the *Patents Rules, 2003*.\n\n## 5. Summary and Key Takeaways\n\n- **Sections 16 and 20** of *The Patents Act, 1970* are central to understanding division, conversion, and withdrawal processes.\n  \n- These provisions aim to ensure clarity in patent protection, promote efficiency, and provide flexibility for applicants.\n\n- Patent agents must be well-versed in these procedures to guide their clients effectively through the patent process.\n\n- While no verified cases exist for this topic, it is recommended that candidates review relevant case law databases for practical insights.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 16 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 16. Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of\n> application.\n> (1) A person who has made an application for a patent under this Act\n> may, at any time before the grant of the patent, if he so desires,\n>\n>  23\n>\n> or with a view to remedy the objection raised by the Controller on\n> the ground that the claims of the complete specification relate to\n> more than one invention, file a further application in respect of an\n> invention disclosed in the provisional or complete specification\n> already filed in respect of the first mentioned application.\n>\n> (2) The further application under sub-section (1) shall be\n> accompanied by a complete specification, but such complete\n> specification shall not include any matter not in substance\n> disclosed in the complete specification filed in pursuance of the\n> first mentioned application.\n>\n> (3) The Controller may require such amendment of the complete\n> specification filed in pursuance of either the original or the\n> further application as may be necessary to ensure that neither of\n> the said complete specifications includes a claim for any matter\n> claimed in the other.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this Act, the further application\n> and the complete specification accompanying it shall be deemed to\n> have been filed on the date on which the first mentioned application\n> had been filed, and the further application shall be proceeded with\n> as a substantive application and be examined when the request for\n> examination is filed within the prescribed period.\n\n**Section 17 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 17. Power of Controller to make orders respecting dating of\n> application.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions of section 9, at any time after the\n> filing of an application and before the grant of the patent under\n> this Act, the Controller may, at the request of the applicant made\n> in the prescribed manner, direct that the application shall be post-\n> dated to such date as may be specified in the request, and proceed\n> with the application accordingly:\n> Provided that no application shall be post-dated under this sub-\n> section to a date later than six months from the date on which it\n> was actually made or would, but for the provisions of this sub-\n> section, be deemed to have been made.\n>\n> (2) Where an application or specification (including drawings) or\n> any other documents is required to be amended under section 15, the\n> application or specification or other document shall, if the\n>\n>  24\n>\n> Controller so directs, be deemed to have been made on the date on\n> which the requirement is complied with or where the application or\n> specification or other document is returned to the applicant, on the\n> date on which it is refiled after complying with the requirement.\n\n**Section 18 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 18. Power of Controller in cases of anticipation.\n> (1) Where it appears to the Controller that the invention so far as\n> claimed in any claim of the complete specification has been\n> anticipated in the manner referred to in clause (a) of sub-section\n> (1) or sub-section (2) of section 13, he may refuse to the\n> application unless the applicant\n> (a) shows to the satisfaction of the Controller that the priority\n> date of the claim of his complete specification is not later than\n> the date on which the relevant document was published; or (b) amends\n> his complete specification to the satisfaction of the Controller.\n>\n> (2) If it appears to the Controller that the invention is claimed in\n> a claim of any other complete specification referred to in clause\n> (b) of sub-section (1) of section 13, he may, subject to the\n> provisions hereinafter contained, direct that a reference to that\n> other specification shall be inserted by way of notice to the public\n> in the applicant’s complete specification unless within such time as\n> may be prescribed,\n> (a) the applicant shows to the satisfaction of the Controller that\n> the priority date of his claim is not later than the priority date\n> of the claim of the said other specification; or\n> (b) the complete specification is amended to the satisfaction of the\n> Controller.\n>\n> (3) If it appears to the Controller, as a result of an investigation\n> under section 13 or otherwise,\n> (a) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> applicant’s complete specification has been claimed in any other\n> complete specification referred to in clause (a) of sub-section (1)\n> of section 13; and\n> (b) that such other complete specification was published on or after\n> the priority date of the applicant’s claim,\n> then, unless it is shown to the satisfaction of the Controller that\n> the priority date of the applicant’s claim is not later than the\n> priority date of the claim of that specification, the provisions of\n> sub-section (2) shall apply thereto in the same manner as they apply\n>\n>  25\n>\n> to a specification published on or after the date of filing of the\n> applicant’s complete specification.\n\n**Section 19 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 19. Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement.\n> (1) If, in consequence of the investigations required under this\n> Act, it appears to the Controller that an invention in respect of\n> which an application for a patent has been made cannot be performed\n> without substantial risk of infringement of a claim of any other\n> patent, he may direct that a reference to that other patent shall be\n> inserted in the applicant’s complete specification by way of notice\n> to the public, unless within such time as may be prescribed\n> (a) the applicant shows to the satisfaction of the Controller that\n> there are reasonable grounds for contesting the validity of the said\n> claim of the other patent; or\n> (b) the complete specification is amended to the satisfaction of the\n> Controller.\n>\n> (2) Where, after a reference to another patent has been inserted in\n> a complete specification in pursuance of a direction under sub-\n> section (1)\n> (a) that other patent is revoked or otherwise ceases to be in force;\n> or\n> (b) the specification of that other patent is amended by the\n> deletion of the relevant claim; or\n> (c) it is found, in proceedings before the court or the Controller,\n> that the relevant claim of that other patent is invalid or is not\n> infringed by any working of the applicant’s invention, the\n> Controller may, on the application of the applicant, delete the\n> reference to that other patent.\n\n**Section 20 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 20. Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of\n> applicants, etc.\n> (1) If the Controller is satisfied, on a claim made in the\n> prescribed manner at any time before a patent has been granted, that\n> by virtue of any assignment or agreement in writing made by the\n> applicant or one of the applicants for the patent or by operation of\n> law, the claimant would, if the patent were then granted be entitled\n> thereto or to the interest of the applicant therein, or to an\n> undivided share of the patent or of that interest, the Controller\n> may, subject to the provisions of this section, direct that the\n> application shall proceed in the name of the claimant or in the\n>\n>  26\n>\n> names of the claimants and the applicant or the other joint\n> applicant or applicants, accordingly as the case may require.\n>\n> (2) No such direction as aforesaid shall be given by virtue of any\n> assignment or agreement made by one of two or more joint applicants\n> for a patent except with the consent of the other joint applicant or\n> applicants.\n>\n> (3) No such direction as aforesaid shall be given by virtue of any\n> assignment or agreement for the assignment of the benefit of an\n> invention unless\n> (a) the invention is identified therein by reference to the number\n> of the application for the patent; or\n> (b) there is produced to the Controller an acknowledgment by the\n> person by whom the assignment or agreement was made that the\n> assignment or agreement relates to the invention in respect of which\n> that application is made; or\n> (c) the rights of the claimant in respect of the invention have been\n> finally established by the decision of a court; or\n> (d) the Controller gives directions for enabling the application to\n> proceed or for regulating the manner in which it should be proceeded\n> with under sub-section (5).\n>\n> (4) Where one of two or more joint applicants for a patent dies at\n> any time before the patent has been granted, the Controller may,\n> upon a request in that behalf made by the survivor or survivors, and\n> with the consent of the legal representative of the deceased, direct\n> that the application shall proceed in the name of the survivor or\n> survivors alone.\n>\n> (5) If any dispute arises between joint applicants for a patent\n> whether or in what manner the application should be proceeded with,\n> the Controller may, upon application made to him in the prescribed\n> manner by any of the parties, and after giving to all parties\n> concerned an opportunity to be heard, give such directions as he\n> thinks fit for enabling the application to proceed in the name of\n> one or more of the parties alone or for regulating the manner in\n> which it should be proceeded with, or for both those purposes, as\n> the case may require.\n>\n>  27\n>\n> 21. Time for putting application in order for grant.\n> (1) An application for a patent shall be deemed to have been\n> abandoned unless, within such period as may be prescribed, the\n> applicant has complied with all the requirements imposed on him by\n> or under this Act, whether in connection with the complete\n> specification or otherwise in relation to the application from the\n> date on which the first statement of objections to the application\n> or complete specification or other documents related thereto is\n> forwarded to the applicant by the Controller.\n>\n> Explanation. Where the application for a patent or any specification\n> or, in the case of a convention application or an application filed\n> under the Patent Cooperation Treaty designating India any document\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 33 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 33. Form of reference to another patent\n> Where the Controller directs that a reference to another patent\n> shall be inserted in the applicant’s complete specification under\n> sub-section (1) of section 19, such reference shall be inserted,\n> after the claims in the following form, namely:\n> \"Reference has been directed, in pursuance of section 19(1) of the\n> Patents Act, 1970, to Patent No......\"\n\n**Rule 34 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 34. Manner in which a claim under section 20(1) shall be made\n> (1) A claim under sub-section (1) of section 20 shall be made in\n> Form 6.\n>\n> (2) The original assignment or agreement or an official copy or\n> notarized copy thereof shall also be produced for the Controller’s\n> inspection and the Controller may call for such other proof of title\n> or written consent as he may require.\n\n**Rule 35 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 35. Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)\n> (1) A request under sub-section (4) of section 20 shall be made in\n> Form 6.\n>\n> (2) The request shall be accompanied by proof of death of the joint\n> applicant and a certified copy of the probate of the will of the\n> deceased or letters of administration in respect of his estate or\n> any other document to prove that the person who gives the consent is\n> the legal representative of the deceased applicant.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the legal provisions governing the division of a patent application under The Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe question seeks an explanation of the legal framework for dividing a patent application under *The Patents Act, 1970*, specifically focusing on **Section 16**.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 16(1)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, an applicant may file a further application in respect of an invention disclosed in the original provisional or complete specification if they wish to divide the application into two or more parts. This is typically done when the Controller raises objections that the claims relate to multiple inventions. The division must be made before the grant of the patent.\n\n**Section 16(2)** mandates that any further application under this section must be accompanied by a complete specification, but it cannot include any matter not substantially disclosed in the original specification. This ensures consistency and avoids introducing new information not previously filed.\n\nUnder **Section 16(3)**, the Controller has the power to require amendments to either the original or the divided application's specifications if necessary to ensure that neither includes a claim for any matter claimed in the other. This prevents overlapping claims between applications, which could lead to confusion or legal disputes.\n\n**Application**  \nIf an applicant files a single patent application covering multiple distinct inventions (e.g., a new solar panel and a novel battery storage system), the Controller may issue a notice under **Section 16**, stating that the application contains more than one invention. The applicant can then opt to divide the application into two separate ones.\n\nIn such a case, each divided application must be accompanied by its own complete specification, which must not contain any new matter beyond what was disclosed in the original application. The Controller may also require amendments to ensure that the claims of both applications do not overlap.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe division of patent applications is governed primarily by **Section 16** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, which allows applicants to split an application into multiple parts if it contains more than one invention. This helps avoid confusion and ensures that each invention is clearly defined and protected.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the requirement under **Section 16(2)** that a divided application must not include any new matter beyond what was disclosed in the original specification. This is crucial to maintaining legal consistency and avoiding rejection of the application on grounds of novelty or disclosure.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provisions for conversion of a patent application under The Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe question asks for an explanation of the legal provisions that allow for the **conversion** of a patent application, particularly focusing on how a provisional specification can be converted into a complete one.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 16(1)** and **Section 20** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, an applicant may convert a provisional specification into a complete specification. A provisional application is typically filed initially to secure a priority date for the invention while allowing time to prepare a more detailed and comprehensive specification.\n\nThis process is governed by **Rule 13** of the *Patents Rules, 2003*, which outlines the formal requirements for preparing a complete specification. The applicant must ensure that all claims are clearly defined and supported by sufficient disclosure in the specification.\n\n**Application**  \nFor example, if an inventor files a provisional application for a new type of solar panel but lacks detailed technical information at the time of filing, they may later convert it into a complete specification once more research is conducted. The applicant must submit a complete specification that meets all formal requirements under **Rule 13**, including clear claims and supporting descriptions.\n\nThe Controller may also request amendments to ensure clarity or compliance with legal standards during this process. If the complete specification does not meet the requirements, the application may be rejected or delayed.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nConversion of a patent application from provisional to complete is governed by **Section 16(1)** and **Rule 13** of the *Patents Rules, 2003*. This allows applicants time to refine their claims and ensure that the specification meets all formal requirements before proceeding with examination.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents frequently confuse the conversion process with the division of applications. It is important to note that **conversion** refers specifically to replacing a provisional application with a complete one, while **division** involves splitting an application into multiple parts based on distinct inventions.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the legal provisions and procedural requirements for the withdrawal of a patent application under The Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe question seeks an explanation of the **withdrawal** of a patent application, including the legal basis and procedures involved.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 20**, the Controller has powers to make orders regarding substitution of applicants or other matters that may affect the withdrawal process. Withdrawal is governed primarily by **Rule 16(1)** of the *Patents Rules, 2003*, which provides for the formal surrender or abandonment of an application.\n\nA patent agent must advise the applicant on whether withdrawal is appropriate at their current stage in the process. If the application has already been published or examined, withdrawal may not be allowed without justification. Withdrawal can only occur before certain stages, such as after publication or examination, and must be done formally through **Form 30**.\n\n**Application**  \nSuppose an applicant files a patent application but later decides to abandon it due to financial constraints or lack of interest in pursuing the invention further. The applicant may submit **Form 30** for surrendering the patent or withdrawing the application as per **Rule 16(1)** of the *Patents Rules, 2003*. This must be done before the application is published or examined.\n\nHowever, if the application has already been published or is under examination, withdrawal may not be allowed without a valid reason. The Controller may reject such an attempt unless it is shown that there are compelling reasons for abandoning the application.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe withdrawal of a patent application is governed by **Rule 16(1)** of the *Patents Rules, 2003* and must be done formally through **Form 30**, typically before publication or examination. It is not allowed at later stages without justification.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to recognize that withdrawal may not be permitted after certain procedural milestones such as publication or examination unless there is a valid reason for abandonment. This is an important limitation under the law.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Division, Conversion and Withdrawal of Applications\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 16 | **DISH** | DISH splits into plates, each holding a separate invention. |\n| Sec. 17 | **DUCK** | DUCK waddles backward, adjusting its calendar date. |\n| Sec. 18 | **DOVE** | DOVE drops a new application, replacing the old one. |\n| Sec. 19 | **TUB** | TUB drains slowly, showing withdrawal timing. |\n| Sec. 20 | **NOSE** | NOSE splits, converting into multiple application parts. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **12-24** - examination, objections, time for putting in order\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Divisional application to remedy plurality of inventions - which provision?  (picture: DISH)\n2. Post-dating and antedating - which provision?  (picture: DUCK)\n3. Substitution of applicants - which provision?  (picture: DOVE)\n4. Withdrawal and its timing - which provision?  (picture: TUB)\n5. Division, Conversion and Withdrawal of Applications - which provision?  (picture: NOSE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 16 | Allows division of application into multiple inventions | Splitting patent into parts like puzzle pieces  \n- Section 20 | Controller can order substitution of applicants or joint ownership | Changing names on patent like a signboard  \n- Rule 13 (Patents Rules, 2003) | Conversion requires complete specification meeting formal requirements | Replacing draft with final version  \n- Rule 16(1) (Patents Rules, 2003) | Withdrawal uses Form 30 and has legal implications | Abandoning patent like closing a shop  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing division with conversion; they are separate processes  \n- Examiners expect clear reference to Sections 16 and 20 in answers  \n- Always mention the formal requirements (e.g., Form 30) when discussing withdrawal for full marks","storyEN":"**Story:**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, a young entrepreneur named Arjun had an idea that could change the future of renewable energy. He filed a patent application for a new solar panel and a novel battery storage system — two inventions in one. But as he sat in his small office on the 7th floor of a building near Charminar, he realized something was wrong.\n\nThe Controller called him with a notice under **Section 16** of *The Patents Act, 1970*. It said that his application contained more than one invention and that it would be better if they were split. Arjun panicked — how could he have two inventions in one? He had thought the solar panel was just an improvement on existing models. But the Controller explained: **Section 16** allows for dividing applications when multiple inventions are present, ensuring clarity and precision in patent protection.\n\nArjun decided to divide his application into two parts: one for the solar panel and another for the battery storage system. He needed a patent agent — someone who knew about **Section 20**, which deals with substitution of applicants and other matters. His friend, Meera, was a patent agent who had studied under Dr. Sheetal Chopra at Osmania University. She told him that he would have to file two separate applications, each with its own complete specification.\n\nBut Arjun wasn’t sure if he wanted to proceed. He thought about the costs and the time it might take. Meera advised him carefully — **Section 20** also allows for withdrawal of an application at certain stages. She explained that if they were no longer interested in pursuing either invention, they could use Form 30 under **Rule 16(1)** to withdraw.\n\nHowever, Arjun was still hesitant. He had spent months working on the project and didn’t want to give up. Meera suggested converting his provisional application into a complete specification. That required following **Rule 13** of *The Patents Rules, 2003*, which outlines how to prepare a complete specification with more detailed claims.\n\nAs Arjun worked through the process, he realized that each step was governed by specific rules and sections. He had to make sure that the divided applications did not overlap — **Section 16** allowed for amendments if needed. Also, under **Section 20**, if there were joint applicants or assignments, consent would be required.\n\nArjun finally submitted both applications with separate specifications and claims. He was relieved when the Controller accepted them without objection. Meera reminded him that these procedures existed not only to protect inventors but also to ensure fair use of patent resources — a point she had read about on **page 245** of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's)* and Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s book.\n\nBut then, just as Arjun was getting ready for the next step, he received another notice from the Controller: one of his applications might have overlapping claims with a previously filed patent. He had to show that his invention wasn’t anticipated — **Section 18** required him to either prove priority or amend his specification.\n\nMeera helped him prepare an amendment under **Section 20**, ensuring that no claim overlapped with the existing one. After weeks of back-and-forth, the Controller accepted the changes and allowed the application to proceed.\n\nIn the end, Arjun had two patent applications — each clear, distinct, and legally sound. He thanked Meera for her guidance through **Section 16**, **Section 20**, and all the rules that governed the process of division, conversion, and withdrawal.\n\n---\n\n**ANCHOR KEY**\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 16 | Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of application. | An applicant may divide an application containing multiple inventions into separate applications under this section. |\n| Section 20 | Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of applicants, etc. | This section allows for withdrawal of an application at certain stages and governs the process of substitution or joint applicants. |\n| Rule 16(1) | Procedure for withdrawal using Form 30. | Withdrawal can be done under this rule but may have legal implications if it occurs after publication. |\n| Section 18 | Power of Controller in cases of anticipation. | If an invention is anticipated, the applicant must prove priority or amend their specification to avoid rejection. |\n| Page 245 (from textbook) | Purpose and rationale for division, conversion, and withdrawal. | These procedures exist to ensure clarity in patent protection and promote efficient use of resources. |\n| Rule 13 (Patents Rules, 2003) | Conversion of provisional application into complete specification. | This rule outlines the requirements for preparing a complete specification after filing a provisional one. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"Division, Conversion and Withdrawal of Applications\"**\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym / Mnemonic: D.C.W. (Division, Conversion, Withdrawal)**  \n**D**ivision – Splitting an application into parts.  \n**C**onversion – Changing the nature or scope of an application.  \n**W**ithdrawal – Removing an application from consideration.\n\n**Mnemonic:** *\"Divide, Change, Walk away.\"* Use this phrase to recall the three main processes: **Division**, **Conversion**, and **Withdrawal**.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-Anchors for Sections**\n\n- **Section 16**: *Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of application.*  \n  → Imagine a **\"Controller with a divider\"** splitting an application into parts. The image ties the number **16** to **division**.\n\n- **Section 21**: *Time for putting application in order for grant.*  \n  → Picture a **clock (time)** and a **grant certificate**. This helps associate **21** with **withdrawal or conversion** as part of the process before granting.\n\n- **Rule 57 & Rule 87(2)**: *Procedure for opposition to surrendering patent.*  \n  → Think of **\"Rule 57\" as a \"door\"**, and **\"Rule 87\" as a \"mirror\"** reflecting opposition procedures. This helps with remembering rules related to **withdrawal or conversion**.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **Division vs Conversion**:  \n  → *Division is splitting the application into parts, while conversion changes its scope or nature.* Use this line: *\"Splitting is division; changing is conversion.\"*\n\n- **Withdrawal vs Surrender**:  \n  → *Withdrawal removes an application from consideration before grant, whereas surrender ends a granted patent.* Remember with: *\"Before grant – withdrawal; after grant – surrender.\"*\n\n- **Conversion vs Amendment**:  \n  → *Conversion changes the nature or scope of an application, while amendment corrects or modifies existing claims.* Use this: *\"Amendment fixes; conversion transforms.\"*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"A divisional application requires the parent application to disclose multiple distinct inventions, not necessarily claim them. The IPAB held this in LG Electronics vs Controller, relying on Section 16 read with Section 10(5).","question":"What is the requirement for filing a divisional application under Section 16?"},{"answer":"Yes, if the parent application discloses multiple inventions. The Delhi High Court clarified this in Syngenta, though the division bench later emphasized plurality of inventions must be present. (Section 16)","question":"Can a divisional application be filed voluntarily if the parent application does not claim multiple inventions?"},{"answer":"The priority date is the date the application was first filed. Section 11 states this, determining precedence over other applications. (Section 11)","question":"What is the priority date of an application according to Section 11?"},{"answer":"An application can be withdrawn at any time before grant, but not after the Controller issues a notice under Section 21(1). (Section 21(1))","question":"What is the timing rule for withdrawing an application?"},{"answer":"The IPAB rejected it because the parent application did not disclose multiple inventions, only one. The Controller required disclosure of plurality for a valid divisional. (IPAB order in LG Electronics case)},{","question":"Why did the IPAB reject LG's divisional application?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 18 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Appropriate office in relation to international”.","options":["Rule 21","Rule 83","Rule 18","Rule 116"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Appropriate office in relation to international?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 18 is headed “Appropriate office in relation to international”.","options":["International applications designating or designating and","Particulars of the qualifying examination for patent","Correction of clerical errors","Appropriate office in relation to international"],"question":"Rule 18 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 18 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of Controller in cases of anticipation”.","options":["Section 40","Section 100","Section 18","Section 51"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of Controller in cases of anticipation?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 18 is headed “Power of Controller in cases of anticipation”.","options":["Protection of security of India","Amendment of patent granted to deceased applicant","Power of Controller in cases of anticipation","Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of"],"question":"Section 18 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 16 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of”.","options":["Section 16","Section 17","Section 150","Section 60"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 16 is headed “Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of”.","options":["Power of Controller to make orders respecting division of","Savings respecting disclosure to Government","Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court","Transmission of orders of courts to Controller"],"question":"Section 16 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 20 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “International applications designating or designating and”.","options":["Rule 36","Rule 84","Rule 75","Rule 20"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: International applications designating or designating and?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 20 is headed “International applications designating or designating and”.","options":["Filing of reply evidence by opponent","Order of recording applications","Refusal to recognise as patent agent","International applications designating or designating and"],"question":"Rule 20 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 20 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of”.","options":["Section 124A","Section 30","Section 117E","Section 20"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 20 is headed “Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of”.","options":["Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court","Power of registered grantee or proprietor to deal with patent","Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of","Special provision for compulsory licences on notifications by"],"question":"Section 20 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 19 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement”.","options":["Section 6","Section 50","Section 19","Section 76"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 19 is headed “Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement”.","options":["Offences by companies","Evidence how to be given and powers of Controller in respect","Procedure for dealing with applications under sections 84 and","Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement"],"question":"Section 19 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 18(1)(a) requires the applicant to show that their priority date is not later than the publication date of the prior document.","options":["a) File a new provisional application","b) Show that their priority date is not later than the prior document's publication date","c) Appeal directly to the Supreme Court","d) Withdraw the application immediately"],"question":"Under Section 18 of the Patents Act, 1970, what must an applicant do if the Controller finds that their invention is anticipated by a prior document?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 19(1) states that the Controller may direct inserting a reference to the other patent unless the applicant contests its validity.","options":["a) Cancel the application outright","b) Insert a reference to the other patent in the specification","c) Grant the patent immediately","d) Transfer the application to another Controller"],"question":"According to Section 19 of the Patents Act, 1970, what can the Controller direct if an invention risks infringing another patent?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 20(2) specifies that substitution by assignment of a joint applicant requires consent from the other joint applicants.","options":["a) The deceased applicant's heirs must agree","b) All joint applicants must consent","c) The Controller must issue a certificate of novelty","d) The application must be published"],"question":"Under Section 20 of the Patents Act, 1970, what condition must be met for substituting joint applicants?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 20(3)(a) mandates that the invention be identified by reference to the application number.","options":["a) The invention must be described in a separate document","b) The application number must reference the invention","c) The Controller must approve the assignment fee","d) The applicant must file a patentability report"],"question":"What requirement is specified in Section 20(3) of the Patents Act, 1970, for substituting applicants when the benefit of an invention is assigned?"}]},"s1p_21":{"notes":"# Pre-Grant Opposition under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970\n\n## Definition\n\nPre-grant opposition refers to a legal mechanism that allows any person or third party (including the Government) to challenge the grant of a patent **before** it is officially granted. This process occurs after the publication of the patent application but before the Controller issues a patent certificate.\n\n## Key Concepts and Legal Framework\n\n### Section 25(1) – Grounds for Pre-Grant Opposition\nUnder **Section 25(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970 (as amended), any person may file a written representation against the grant of a patent on the following grounds:\n\n- The invention has been wrongly obtained.\n- Any claim in the complete specification was previously published before the priority date in any document within India or elsewhere.\n- The invention is not novel as per **Section 29(2)**, if the publication occurred without the applicant’s consent and the application was filed promptly upon becoming aware of such publication.\n\n### Section 25 – Opposition to Patent\n**Section 25** outlines the procedures for both pre-grant and post-grant opposition. Pre-grant opposition is specifically governed under **sub-section (1)**, while post-grant opposition is covered under **sub-section (2)**.\n\n### Rule 55 – Procedure for Opposition\nThe detailed procedural framework for filing an opposition is outlined in **Rule 55** of the Patents Rules, 2003. These rules specify:\n- The form and manner in which a representation must be filed.\n- The timeline within which the Controller must act upon receiving such a representation.\n- The opportunity provided to both the applicant and the opponent for presenting their case.\n\n## Procedure for Pre-Grant Opposition\n\n1. **Filing of Representation**: Any person may file a written opposition (in Form 7A) with the Controller after the application has been published but before the patent is granted.\n\n2. **Controller’s Initial Check**: The Controller checks if a Request for Examination (RFE) was filed, as per **Rule 55(2)**.\n\n3. **Decision on Opposition**:\n   - If the opposition is accepted, the Controller notifies the applicant.\n   - If not, the representation is rejected with a written order.\n\n4. **Notice to Applicant**: The patent applicant may contest the opposition within **three months** of receiving notice (as per **Rule 55(4)**), by submitting evidence and a detailed statement.\n\n5. **Hearing Opportunity**: The Controller must provide an opportunity for both parties to be heard, ensuring compliance with the principle of natural justice (**Rule 55(5)**).\n\n6. **Controller’s Decision**:\n   - If the Controller finds that the specification needs amendment or rejection, he will notify the applicant.\n   - The decision on whether to grant the patent (with amendments) or reject it is made within a reasonable time frame.\n\n7. **Appeal**: A rejected application can be appealed to the **High Court**, as per **Section 117A**.\n\n## Why the Law Provides for Pre-Grant Opposition\n\nThe provision of pre-grant opposition under **Section 25(1)** serves several important purposes, including:\n\n- **Ensuring Validity Before Grant**: It allows third parties or interested persons to challenge the validity of a patent application before it is granted. This helps in preventing the grant of invalid patents and ensures that only truly novel and non-obvious inventions are patented (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 219**).\n\n- **Protecting Public Interest**: By allowing opposition on grounds such as prior publication or wrongful obtaining of the invention, this mechanism protects public interest by ensuring that only legitimate inventions receive patent protection.\n\n- **Cost-Efficient Dispute Resolution**: Pre-grant opposition provides an opportunity to resolve disputes at an early stage before incurring the costs and delays associated with litigation (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 333**).\n\n## Verified Case Law\n\n- **(No verified or candidate cases are available for this chapter – check IndianKanoon before citing one)**\n\n---\n\nThis section concludes the study notes on Pre-Grant Opposition under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970. It is a crucial part of the examination syllabus and must be understood thoroughly to effectively navigate patent opposition procedures in practice.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 25 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 25. Opposition to the patent.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent has been published but a\n> patent has not been granted, any person may, in writing, represent\n> by way of opposition to the Controller against the grant of patent\n> on the ground\n> (a) that the applicant for the patent or the person under or through\n> whom he claims, wrongfully obtained the invention or any part\n> thereof from him or from a person under or through whom he claims;\n> (b) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification has been published before the priority date\n> of the claim\n> (i) in any specification filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent made in India on or after the 1st day of January, 1912; or\n> (ii) in India or elsewhere, in any other document:\n> Provided that the ground specified in sub-clause (ii) shall not be\n> available where such publication does not constitute an anticipation\n> of the invention by virtue of sub-section (2) or sub-section (3) of\n> section 29;\n> (c) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is claimed in a claim of a complete\n> specification published on or after the priority date of the\n> applicant’s claim and filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent in India, being a claim of which the priority date is earlier\n> than that of the applicant’s claim;\n> (d) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was publicly known or publicly used in India\n> before the priority date of that claim.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, an invention relating\n> to a process for which a patent is claimed shall be deemed to have\n> been publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority\n> date of the claim if a product made by that process had already been\n> imported into India before that date except where such importation\n> has been for the purpose of reasonable trial or experiment only;\n>\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious and clearly does not involve any\n> inventive step, having regard to the matter published as mentioned\n> in clause (b) or having regard to what was used in India before the\n>\n>  29\n>\n> priority date of the applicant’s claim;\n> (f) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act, or is not\n> patentable under this Act;\n> (g) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and\n> clearly describe the invention or the method by which it is to be\n> performed;\n> (h) that the applicant has failed to disclose to the Controller the\n> information required by section 8 or has furnished the information\n> which in any material particular was false to his knowledge;\n> (i) that in the case of a convention application, the application\n> was not made within twelve months from the date of the first\n> application for protection for the invention made in a convention\n> country by the applicant or a person from whom he derives title;\n> (j) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n> mentions the source or geographical origin of biological material\n> used for the invention;\n> (k) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is anticipated having regard to the\n> knowledge, oral or otherwise, available within any local or\n> indigenous community in India or elsewhere,\n> but on no other ground and the Controller shall, if requested by\n> such person for being heard, hear him and dispose of such\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 55A — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55A. Filing of notice of opposition\n> The notice of opposition to be given under sub-section (2) of\n> section 25 shall be made in Form 7 and sent to the Controller in\n> duplicate at the appropriate office.\n\n**Rule 55 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55. Opposition to the patent\n> (1) Representation for opposition under sub-section (1) of section\n> 25 shall be filed in Form 7(A) at the appropriate office with a copy\n> to the applicant, and shall include a statement and evidence, if\n> any, in support of the representation and a request for hearing, if\n> so desired.\n>\n> (1A) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-rule (1), no patent\n> shall be granted before the expiry of a period of six months from\n> the date of publication of the application under section 11A.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall consider such a representation only when a\n> request for examination of the application has been filed.\n>\n> (3) On consideration of the representation if the Controller is\n> satisfied with that,\n> (a) no prima facie case is made out in the representation, he shall\n> notify the opponent accordingly, and\n> (i) unless the opponent requests to be heard in the matter, the\n> Controller shall, within one month from the date of such\n> notification, pass an order recording the grounds for refusal of the\n> representation;\n> (ii) if opponent requests for a hearing, the Controller shall, after\n> giving the opponent an opportunity of being heard, pass an order\n> within one month from the date of hearing, recording his reasons for\n> refusal or prima facie acceptance of the representation and the\n> applicant shall be notified accordingly.\n> (b) a prima facie case is made out in the representation, the\n> Controller shall, within one month of receiving the representation,\n> pass an order recording his reasons and notify the applicant\n> accordingly.\n>\n> (4) On receiving the notice under sub-rule (3), the applicant shall,\n> if he so desires, file his statement and evidence, if any, in\n> support of his application within two months from the date of the\n> notice, with a copy to the opponent.\n>\n> (5) On consideration of the statement and evidence filed by the\n> applicant, the representation including the statement and evidence\n>\n>  42\n>\n> filed by the opponent, submissions made by the parties, and after\n> hearing the parties, if so requested, the Controller may either\n> reject the representation or require the complete specification and\n> other documents to be amended to his satisfaction before the patent\n> is granted or refuse to grant a patent on the application, by\n> passing a speaking order to simultaneously decide on the application\n> and the representation ordinarily within one month from the\n> completion of above proceedings.\n>\n> (5A) The procedure specified in sub-rules (2) to (4) of rule 62\n> shall, as far as may be, apply to the procedure for hearing under\n> this rule.\n>\n> (5B) An application for a patent, in which a representation for\n> opposition has been filed and notice has been issued by the\n> Controller under rule 3, shall be examined in accordance with rule\n\n**Rule 56 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 56. Constitution of Opposition Board and its proceeding\n> (1) On receipt of notice of opposition under rule 55A, the\n> Controller shall, by order, constitute an Opposition Board\n> consisting of three members and nominate one of the members as the\n> Chairman of the Board.\n>\n> (2) An examiner appointed under sub-section (2) of section 73 shall\n> be eligible to be a member of the Opposition Board.\n>\n> (3) The examiner, who has dealt with the application for patent\n> during the proceeding for grant of patent thereon shall not be\n> eligible as member of Opposition Board as specified in sub-rule (2)\n> for that application.\n>\n> (4) The Opposition Board shall conduct the examination of the notice\n> of opposition along with documents filed under rules 57 to 60\n>\n>  43\n>\n> referred to under sub-section (3) of section 25, submit a report\n> with reasons on each ground taken in the notice of opposition with\n> its joint recommendation within two months from the date on which\n> the documents were forwarded to them.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2016, 2018, 2020)*\n\n**Issue**: This question asks for an explanation of pre-grant opposition under **Section 25(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970. It requires a clear understanding of its definition, legal framework, procedural aspects, and purpose.\n\n**Rule**: Under **Section 25(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970 (as amended), any person may file a written opposition with the Controller against the grant of a patent on specific grounds. These include: (a) wrongful obtaining of the invention; (b) prior publication before the priority date; (c) anticipation by an earlier application; (d) public use or knowledge in India before the priority date; and (e) lack of inventive step. The procedural framework for such opposition is outlined in **Rule 55** of the Patents Rules, 2003. According to **Rule 55(4)**, the applicant has three months from receiving notice to contest the opposition by submitting evidence and a detailed statement.\n\nThe purpose of pre-grant opposition is to ensure that only valid patents are granted, protect public interest, and provide an early stage for dispute resolution (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 219**).\n\n**Application**: Pre-grant opposition allows any person or even the Government to challenge a patent application **after it is published but before the grant**. The grounds for such an opposition are outlined in **Section 25(1)(a)–(k)** and include matters like prior publication, public use, lack of novelty, and non-disclosure of required information under **Section 8**.\n\nFor instance, if a third party discovers that the invention was previously published in India or elsewhere before the priority date (as per **Section 25(1)(b)**), they may file an opposition. The Controller must then review the representation and notify the applicant. If the applicant contests the opposition within three months (**Rule 55(4)**), a hearing is conducted, and both parties are given an opportunity to present their case (**Rule 55(5)**).\n\nThe Controller may either reject the application or allow it with amendments if the specification is found to be incomplete or unclear (**Section 25(1)(g)**). If the opposition is rejected, the applicant can appeal to the **High Court** under **Section 117A**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Pre-grant opposition under **Section 25(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970 allows third parties to challenge a patent application before it is granted. It ensures that only valid and novel inventions are patented, upholds public interest, and provides an early forum for dispute resolution.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students confuse pre-grant opposition with post-grant opposition under **Section 25(2)**. It is important to highlight the distinction in timing, grounds, and procedural differences clearly.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the procedure for filing a pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2017, 2021)*\n\n**Issue**: This question asks for an explanation of the procedural steps involved in filing a pre-grant opposition under **Section 25(1)**.\n\n**Rule**: The procedure is governed by **Section 25(1)** and **Rule 55** of the Patents Rules, 2003. Under **Rule 55**, the person wishing to oppose must file a written representation (in **Form 7A**) with the Controller after the application has been published but before the patent is granted.\n\nThe Controller checks whether a **Request for Examination (RFE)** was filed (**Rule 55(2)**). If the opposition is accepted, the applicant is notified. The applicant may contest the opposition within **three months** of receiving notice by submitting evidence and a detailed statement (**Rule 55(4)**).\n\nA hearing must be provided to both parties under **Rule 55(5)**, ensuring compliance with the principle of natural justice. After reviewing all submissions, the Controller decides whether to grant the patent (with or without amendments), reject it, or refer it for further examination.\n\n**Application**: The process begins when a person files a written representation in **Form 7A**, which must be submitted after publication but before the grant. The Controller checks if an RFE was filed and then proceeds with the review of the opposition. If accepted, the applicant is notified and given three months to respond by submitting evidence and a statement.\n\nIf the applicant contests the opposition, both parties are heard, and the Controller makes a decision based on all evidence presented. If the patent is rejected or requires amendments, the applicant may appeal to the **High Court** under **Section 117A**.\n\n**Conclusion**: The procedure for pre-grant opposition involves filing a written representation in **Form 7A**, followed by notification of the applicant and an opportunity to contest within three months. A hearing is conducted, and the Controller decides on the matter based on all evidence presented.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students overlook the importance of **Rule 55(4)**, which specifies the three-month time limit for the applicant to respond. This is a critical procedural detail that must not be missed.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the grounds for pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970? Explain with reference to the law.**\n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2015, 2022)*\n\n**Issue**: This question asks for an explanation of the legal grounds under **Section 25(1)** that allow a pre-grant opposition.\n\n**Rule**: Under **Section 25(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970 (as amended), any person may file a written representation against the grant of a patent on the following grounds:\n\n- The invention was wrongly obtained (**Section 25(1)(a)**).\n- Any claim in the complete specification has been previously published before the priority date in any document within India or elsewhere (**Section 25(1)(b)**), provided it constitutes an anticipation under **Section 29**.\n- The invention is not novel as per **Section 29(2)** if the publication occurred without the applicant’s consent and the application was filed promptly (**Section 25(1)(d)**).\n- The invention is obvious and lacks inventive step, having regard to prior publications or use in India (**Section 25(1)(e)**).\n- The subject of any claim is not an invention within the meaning of this Act or is not patentable (**Section 25(1)(f)**).\n- The complete specification does not sufficiently describe the invention (**Section 25(1)(g)**).\n- The applicant failed to disclose required information under **Section 8** or provided false information (**Section 25(1)(h)**).\n\nOther grounds include failure to file a convention application within twelve months (**Section 25(1)(i)**), non-disclosure of the source of biological material (**Section 25(1)(j)**), and anticipation based on knowledge from local or indigenous communities (**Section 25(1)(k)**).\n\n**Application**: For example, if a third party discovers that an invention was previously published in India before the priority date, they may file an opposition under **Section 25(1)(b)**. Similarly, if the applicant failed to disclose required information under **Section 8**, the Controller may reject the application on grounds of non-disclosure (**Section 25(1)(h)**).\n\n**Conclusion**: The grounds for pre-grant opposition include wrongful obtaining, prior publication, lack of novelty or inventive step, and failure to meet patentability requirements. These are outlined in **Section 25(1)** and ensure that only valid patents are granted.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often confuse the grounds under **Sections 25(1)(b)** and **29(2)**. It is important to clarify that prior publication may not be a ground for opposition if it does not constitute an anticipation under **Section 29**.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the advantages of pre-grant opposition in patent law?**\n*8 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2019)*\n\n**Issue**: This question asks about the benefits or advantages of allowing pre-grant opposition in patent law.\n\n**Rule**: Pre-grant opposition under **Section 25(1)** ensures that only valid patents are granted, protects public interest by preventing the grant of invalid patents, and provides an early opportunity for dispute resolution (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 333**).\n\nIt allows third parties to challenge a patent application before it is granted, reducing the likelihood of costly litigation later on. It also ensures that only truly novel and non-obvious inventions are patented (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 219**).\n\n**Application**: For example, if a company discovers that an invention was previously published in India before the priority date, they can file an opposition under **Section 25(1)(b)** to prevent the grant of an invalid patent. This helps protect public interest and ensures that only legitimate inventions are granted patents.\n\n**Conclusion**: Pre-grant opposition benefits both the applicant and third parties by ensuring the validity of patents, protecting public interest, and providing a cost-effective dispute resolution mechanism early in the process.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to highlight the **cost-efficiency** aspect of pre-grant opposition. This is an important point that must be emphasized when answering this question.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Pre-grant Opposition under Section 25(1)\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 25(1) | **NAIL** + clause (1) | A nail jams a door, halting any intruder's entry. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **25-28** - opposition (pre-grant and post-grant), mention of inventor\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Who may oppose — any person - which provision?  (picture: NAIL)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 25(1) | Allows pre-grant opposition on specific grounds | Legal challenge before patent grant  \n- Section 25 | Covers both pre and post-grant opposition procedures | Opposition rules and timelines  \n- Rule 55 | Details how to file and handle oppositions | Filing forms and Controller actions  \n- Section 29(2) | Defines novelty based on prior publication | Prior art and patent validity  \n- Section 117A | Provides appeal option for rejected applications | High Court appeal process  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Confusing pre-grant with post-grant opposition grounds  \n- Examiners expect: Clear reference to Section 25(1) and Rule 55 in answers  \n- Structure tip: List the seven procedural steps of pre-grant opposition in order","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, nestled in a quiet neighborhood near Charminar, lived a young patent agent named Ravi. He had recently passed his exams with flying colors but was now faced with a new challenge: helping his client, Mr. Anil, defend a patent application from being opposed before it could be granted.\n\nOne day, as Ravi was walking through the bustling streets of Hyderabad, he saw a group of people gathered near a shop selling traditional textiles. Curious, he approached and found out that they were discussing an upcoming opposition to a new textile design patent. The story unfolded like this:\n\nMr. Anil had filed for a patent on a unique weaving technique, which he had developed over the years. But just as his application was published, someone named Mr. Kumar came forward with a **written representation** under **Section 25(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970. He claimed that Anil’s invention had been wrongly obtained from him and that it was not novel.\n\nRavi knew this was a pre-grant opposition, and he had to act fast. The law allowed anyone — even Mr. Kumar — to file an **opposition before the patent was granted**, as long as certain conditions were met. According to **Section 25(1)**, one of the grounds for such an objection could be that the invention had been wrongly obtained or that it was not novel.\n\nRavi rushed back to his office and checked the rules in detail. He remembered that under **Rule 55**, the Controller had to give both parties a chance to present their case. This meant that Anil would have three months from the date of receiving notice to respond with evidence, as per **Rule 55(4)**.\n\nBut Ravi also knew that if Mr. Kumar’s claim was not valid — for instance, if the invention had indeed been developed by Anil independently or if it met the criteria for novelty under **Section 29(2)** — then the Controller would have to reject the opposition and proceed with granting the patent.\n\nTo make sure no mistakes were made, Ravi recalled a key point from his textbook: pre-grant opposition was not just about protecting the interests of the applicant but also ensuring that only valid inventions received protection. **\"This helps in preventing the grant of invalid patents and ensures that only truly novel and non-obvious inventions are patented,\"** he read on page 219.\n\nAs Ravi prepared for the hearing, he made sure to include all possible evidence — from Anil’s notebooks showing the development process to witness statements. The Controller listened carefully and gave both sides a fair chance under **Rule 55(5)**, which required that the principle of natural justice be upheld.\n\nAfter weeks of back-and-forth, the Controller finally ruled in favor of Mr. Anil. He found no grounds for opposition and proceeded with granting the patent. Ravi breathed a sigh of relief — his client had won, thanks to the provisions under **Section 25(1)**.\n\nBut just as he was about to celebrate, Ravi remembered another important rule: if the Controller had rejected the application, Anil could still appeal to the **High Court** under **Section 117A**. He made sure to inform Mr. Anil of this possibility in case any future issues arose.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 25(1) | Grounds for pre-grant opposition | Allows any person to challenge a patent before it is granted on specific grounds, such as wrongful obtaining or lack of novelty. |\n| Rule 55 | Procedure for filing and handling opposition | Outlines the steps for submitting an opposition, timelines, and ensures both parties are heard. |\n| Section 29(2) | Novelty criteria | Determines whether a prior publication without consent affects the novelty of an invention. |\n| Rule 55(4) | Three-month response period | The applicant has three months to respond with evidence after receiving notice of opposition. |\n| Rule 55(5) | Natural justice principle | Ensures both parties have a fair opportunity to present their case during the hearing. |\n| Section 117A | Appeal to High Court | If an application is rejected, it can be appealed to the High Court for review. |\n| Page 219 (textbook) | Importance of pre-grant opposition | Ensures only valid and novel inventions are granted patents, protecting public interest. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for \"Pre-grant Opposition under Section 25(1)\"**\n\n---\n\n### **1. Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n**\"FOPA\" – Filing, Opponent, Period, Action**\n\n- **F**iling: Must be in Form 7A (Rule 55).\n- **O**pponent: Any person or third party may file.\n- **P**eriod: Filed after publication but before grant (6 months minimum, as per Rule 55(1A)).\n- **A**ction: Controller decides whether to accept or reject the opposition.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Number-Anchors for Sections:**\n\n- **Section 25(1):** Imagine a **\"25\" shaped like a patent application form**, being filed by someone who wants to **oppose** before it’s granted.\n- **Rule 55:** Picture a **\"55\" as a clock face**, showing the time limit for filing and processing oppositions.\n- **Section 117A:** Think of **\"117A\" as a \"High Court door\"**, since appeals from Controller decisions can be made here.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Pre-grant vs. Post-grant Opposition (Sections 25(1) and 25(2)):**  \n  *Pre-grant is filed before the patent is granted, while post-grant is filed after the patent has been granted.*\n\n- **Pre-grant Opposition vs. Revocation under Section 64:**  \n  *Pre-grant opposition is a challenge to an application before grant; revocation (Section 64) can be done anytime during the life of the patent by any interested party.*\n\n- **Controller’s Decision under Section 25(1) vs. Appealability:**  \n  *Decisions on pre-grant opposition are not appealable, unlike those on post-grant opposition (Section 25(2)).*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Any person may file a pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1). This includes individuals, companies, or NGOs not directly involved in the patent's development.","question":"Who may file a pre-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"The Controller considers a pre-grant opposition only after a request for examination (RFE) has been filed. As per Rule 7155(2), the RFE must be filed before proceeding.","question":"When can the Controller consider a pre-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"The minimum period is six months from the application's publication. Rule 7155(1A) mandates this to allow opposition.","question":"What is the minimum period between patent application publication and grant?"},{"answer":"NGOs may file pre-grant opposition to challenge patents affecting public health, like Roche's. They aim to prevent monopolies on essential medicines, as seen in the Pegasys case.","question":"Why might NGOs file pre-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"Form 7(A) is used for filing pre-grant opposition. Rule 7155(1) specifies this form for submissions.","question":"What form is used to file a pre-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"The applicant has two months from the notice to file a statement and evidence. Rule 7155(4) grants this period for the applicant's response.","question":"How long does the applicant have to respond to a pre-grant opposition?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 25 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Identification of published applications”.","options":["Rule 32","Rule 25","Rule 85","Rule 19B"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Identification of published applications?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 25 is headed “Identification of published applications”.","options":["Identification of published applications","Constitution of Opposition Board and its proceeding","Registration of patent agents","Form and manner in which statements required under section"],"question":"Rule 25 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Section 138","Section 60","Section 25","Section 40"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Opposition to the patent?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 25 is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement","Opposition to the patent","Mention of inventor as such in patent"],"question":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"The ground related to prior public use in India is specified in clause (d).","options":["(a) Wrongful obtaining of the invention","(b) Prior publication in a foreign document","(c) Publicly known or used in India before the priority date","(d) Failure to disclose biological material source"],"question":"Under Section 25 of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is a valid ground for opposition to a patent based on prior public use?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Clause (i) specifies the 12-month time limit for convention applications.","options":["(a) Failure to disclose information under section 8","(b) Delay in filing a convention application beyond 12 months","(c) Lack of inventive step","(d) Prior publication in India before the priority date"],"question":"Which of the following is a valid ground for opposition under Section 25( i) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Clause (j) explicitly addresses disclosure of biological material source.","options":["(a) Clause (h)","(b) Clause (j)","(c) Clause (e)","(d) Clause (c)"],"question":"Which of the following is a ground for opposition based on the complete specification's failure to disclose biological material source?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Clause (h) refers to failure to disclose information required by Section 8.","options":["(a) Clause (h)","(b) Clause (g)","(c) Clause (f)","(d) Clause (d)"],"question":"Which of the following is a valid ground for opposition based on failure to disclose information under Section 8 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Clause (c) addresses anticipation by a prior specification with an earlier priority date.","options":["(a) Prior publication in India","(b) Anticipation by indigenous knowledge","(c) Claim in a prior specification with an earlier priority date","(d) Failure to describe the invention"],"question":"Under Section 25(c), which of the following is a ground for opposition based on prior claims in specifications?"}]},"s1p_22":{"notes":"### Post-Grant Opposition under Section 25(2) and the Opposition Board\n\n#### **Definition**\nPost-grant opposition refers to a legal process that allows any person interested in a patent application or granted patent to challenge its validity after the grant of the patent. This is governed by **Section 25(2)** of *The Patents Act, 1970 (as amended on 01-08-2024)* and further detailed through **Rule 55A** and other related rules.\n\n#### **Key Concepts**\n\n- **Who Can File Post-Grant Opposition?**\n  - Any \"person interested\" can file a post-grant opposition. This includes individuals, companies, or entities that have a direct interest in the subject matter of the patent (e.g., competitors, researchers, or users of similar technology).\n\n- **When Can It Be Filed?**\n  - A post-grant opposition must be filed within **one year from the date of grant** of the patent. This is a strict time limit and cannot be extended.\n\n- **What Grounds Are Permissible?**\n  - The grounds for opposition are the same as those in pre-grant opposition, which include:\n    - Non-novelty (Section 29),\n    - Lack of inventive step (Section 30),\n    - Prior publication or use,\n    - Obviousness to a person skilled in the art.\n\n- **Process of Filing Post-Grant Opposition**\n  - The opponent must file a notice in **Form 7** along with a prescribed fee and a statement that includes:\n    - The interest of the opponent.\n    - Grounds for opposition (e.g., non-novelty, lack of inventive step).\n    - Relief sought (e.g., revocation or amendment).\n    - Supporting evidence.\n\n- **Opposition Board**\n  - Under **Rule 56**, upon receiving a notice of post-grant opposition, the Controller is required to constitute an **Opposition Board** comprising three members.\n  - One member is nominated as the **Chairman**.\n  - An examiner who previously dealt with the patent application cannot be part of the Opposition Board for that particular case.\n\n- **Procedure Under the Opposition Board**\n  - The Opposition Board examines the notice and supporting documents under Rules 57 to 60.\n  - It then submits a report with reasons on each ground raised in the opposition, along with its joint recommendation within **three months** of receiving the documents.\n\n- **Outcome of Post-Grant Opposition**\n  - If the Opposition Board finds the patent invalid or partially invalid, it may recommend revocation or amendment of the patent.\n  - The Controller then decides whether to grant or revoke the patent based on the report.\n  - Decisions made under Section 25(2) are **appealable** to the High Court (Section 117A).\n\n#### **Why the Law Is As It Is: Explanation from Textbook**\n\n- **Purpose of Post-Grant Opposition**\n  - The post-grant opposition mechanism is a safeguard for third parties who may not have had the opportunity to challenge the patent during its examination phase. It ensures that only valid patents are granted and remain in force (p. 231, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n- **Importance of Time Limit**\n  - The one-year time limit for filing post-grant opposition is intended to prevent unnecessary delays in the patent system and ensure that challenges are raised promptly when the patent is in force (p. 231, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n- **Role of the Opposition Board**\n  - The Opposition Board acts as an independent body to evaluate the validity of a granted patent, ensuring impartiality and fairness in the process (p. 539, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n- **Appealability of Decisions**\n  - Unlike pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1), decisions made under Section 25(2) are appealable to the High Court, which provides a legal remedy for parties who feel their rights have been adversely affected (p. 227, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n#### **Legal Provisions Involved**\n\n- **Section 25(2)**: Provides the framework for post-grant opposition.\n- **Rule 55A**: Details the procedure for filing a notice of post-grant opposition, including the required form (Form 7), fee, and supporting documents.\n- **Rule 56**: Outlines the constitution of the Opposition Board and its composition.\n- **Rules 57 to 60**: Specify the procedural steps followed by the Opposition Board in examining the opposition notice.\n\n#### **Conclusion**\n\nPost-grant opposition under Section 25(2) is a critical tool for ensuring that only valid patents are granted and remain in force. It provides an opportunity for third parties to challenge the validity of a patent after its grant, thereby upholding the integrity of the patent system. The Opposition Board plays a key role in this process by independently evaluating the opposition and making recommendations based on evidence and legal grounds.\n\n**(No verified or candidate cases are available for this topic — verify with a case-law database if needed.)**\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 25 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 25. Opposition to the patent.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent has been published but a\n> patent has not been granted, any person may, in writing, represent\n> by way of opposition to the Controller against the grant of patent\n> on the ground\n> (a) that the applicant for the patent or the person under or through\n> whom he claims, wrongfully obtained the invention or any part\n> thereof from him or from a person under or through whom he claims;\n> (b) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification has been published before the priority date\n> of the claim\n> (i) in any specification filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent made in India on or after the 1st day of January, 1912; or\n> (ii) in India or elsewhere, in any other document:\n> Provided that the ground specified in sub-clause (ii) shall not be\n> available where such publication does not constitute an anticipation\n> of the invention by virtue of sub-section (2) or sub-section (3) of\n> section 29;\n> (c) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is claimed in a claim of a complete\n> specification published on or after the priority date of the\n> applicant’s claim and filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent in India, being a claim of which the priority date is earlier\n> than that of the applicant’s claim;\n> (d) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was publicly known or publicly used in India\n> before the priority date of that claim.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, an invention relating\n> to a process for which a patent is claimed shall be deemed to have\n> been publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority\n> date of the claim if a product made by that process had already been\n> imported into India before that date except where such importation\n> has been for the purpose of reasonable trial or experiment only;\n>\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious and clearly does not involve any\n> inventive step, having regard to the matter published as mentioned\n> in clause (b) or having regard to what was used in India before the\n>\n>  29\n>\n> priority date of the applicant’s claim;\n> (f) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act, or is not\n> patentable under this Act;\n> (g) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and\n> clearly describe the invention or the method by which it is to be\n> performed;\n> (h) that the applicant has failed to disclose to the Controller the\n> information required by section 8 or has furnished the information\n> which in any material particular was false to his knowledge;\n> (i) that in the case of a convention application, the application\n> was not made within twelve months from the date of the first\n> application for protection for the invention made in a convention\n> country by the applicant or a person from whom he derives title;\n> (j) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n> mentions the source or geographical origin of biological material\n> used for the invention;\n> (k) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is anticipated having regard to the\n> knowledge, oral or otherwise, available within any local or\n> indigenous community in India or elsewhere,\n> but on no other ground and the Controller shall, if requested by\n> such person for being heard, hear him and dispose of such\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 55A — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55A. Filing of notice of opposition\n> The notice of opposition to be given under sub-section (2) of\n> section 25 shall be made in Form 7 and sent to the Controller in\n> duplicate at the appropriate office.\n\n**Rule 55 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55. Opposition to the patent\n> (1) Representation for opposition under sub-section (1) of section\n> 25 shall be filed in Form 7(A) at the appropriate office with a copy\n> to the applicant, and shall include a statement and evidence, if\n> any, in support of the representation and a request for hearing, if\n> so desired.\n>\n> (1A) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-rule (1), no patent\n> shall be granted before the expiry of a period of six months from\n> the date of publication of the application under section 11A.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall consider such a representation only when a\n> request for examination of the application has been filed.\n>\n> (3) On consideration of the representation if the Controller is\n> satisfied with that,\n> (a) no prima facie case is made out in the representation, he shall\n> notify the opponent accordingly, and\n> (i) unless the opponent requests to be heard in the matter, the\n> Controller shall, within one month from the date of such\n> notification, pass an order recording the grounds for refusal of the\n> representation;\n> (ii) if opponent requests for a hearing, the Controller shall, after\n> giving the opponent an opportunity of being heard, pass an order\n> within one month from the date of hearing, recording his reasons for\n> refusal or prima facie acceptance of the representation and the\n> applicant shall be notified accordingly.\n> (b) a prima facie case is made out in the representation, the\n> Controller shall, within one month of receiving the representation,\n> pass an order recording his reasons and notify the applicant\n> accordingly.\n>\n> (4) On receiving the notice under sub-rule (3), the applicant shall,\n> if he so desires, file his statement and evidence, if any, in\n> support of his application within two months from the date of the\n> notice, with a copy to the opponent.\n>\n> (5) On consideration of the statement and evidence filed by the\n> applicant, the representation including the statement and evidence\n>\n>  42\n>\n> filed by the opponent, submissions made by the parties, and after\n> hearing the parties, if so requested, the Controller may either\n> reject the representation or require the complete specification and\n> other documents to be amended to his satisfaction before the patent\n> is granted or refuse to grant a patent on the application, by\n> passing a speaking order to simultaneously decide on the application\n> and the representation ordinarily within one month from the\n> completion of above proceedings.\n>\n> (5A) The procedure specified in sub-rules (2) to (4) of rule 62\n> shall, as far as may be, apply to the procedure for hearing under\n> this rule.\n>\n> (5B) An application for a patent, in which a representation for\n> opposition has been filed and notice has been issued by the\n> Controller under rule 3, shall be examined in accordance with rule\n\n**Rule 56 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 56. Constitution of Opposition Board and its proceeding\n> (1) On receipt of notice of opposition under rule 55A, the\n> Controller shall, by order, constitute an Opposition Board\n> consisting of three members and nominate one of the members as the\n> Chairman of the Board.\n>\n> (2) An examiner appointed under sub-section (2) of section 73 shall\n> be eligible to be a member of the Opposition Board.\n>\n> (3) The examiner, who has dealt with the application for patent\n> during the proceeding for grant of patent thereon shall not be\n> eligible as member of Opposition Board as specified in sub-rule (2)\n> for that application.\n>\n> (4) The Opposition Board shall conduct the examination of the notice\n> of opposition along with documents filed under rules 57 to 60\n>\n>  43\n>\n> referred to under sub-section (3) of section 25, submit a report\n> with reasons on each ground taken in the notice of opposition with\n> its joint recommendation within two months from the date on which\n> the documents were forwarded to them.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the concept of post-grant opposition under Section 25(2) of The Patents Act, 1970 (as amended on 01-08-2024). Discuss its procedural and substantive aspects.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** This question requires an explanation of the concept of post-grant opposition under Section 25(2) of The Patents Act, 1970, along with a discussion of its procedural and substantive aspects.\n\n**Rule:** Post-grant opposition is governed by **Section 25(2)** of *The Patents Act, 1970 (as amended on 01-08-2024)*. It allows any “person interested” to challenge the validity of a granted patent within one year from the date of grant. The grounds for opposition are similar to those under pre-grant opposition and include non-novelty (Section 29), lack of inventive step (Section 30), prior publication or use, and obviousness. **Rule 55A** provides that a notice of post-grant opposition must be filed in **Form 7**, along with the prescribed fee and supporting evidence. Under **Rule 56**, an Opposition Board comprising three members is constituted by the Controller upon receipt of such a notice. Rules 57 to 60 outline the procedural steps for examining the opposition, including submission of reports within three months.\n\n**Application:** Post-grant opposition allows third parties who were not involved in the examination phase to challenge the validity of a patent after it has been granted. The process begins with filing a notice in Form 7 by any person interested, such as competitors or researchers, along with evidence and grounds for opposition (e.g., non-novelty or lack of inventive step). Once filed, the Controller is required to constitute an Opposition Board under Rule 56, which must not include the examiner who previously dealt with the application. The board reviews the notice and supporting documents, and within three months submits a report recommending whether the patent should be revoked or amended. If the opposition is successful, the Controller may revoke or amend the patent based on the board’s recommendation. Decisions under Section 25(2) are appealable to the High Court (Section 117A), unlike pre-grant opposition.\n\n**Conclusion:** Post-grant opposition is a critical mechanism for ensuring that only valid patents remain in force after their grant. It provides third parties with an opportunity to challenge the validity of a patent based on grounds such as non-novelty, lack of inventive step, and prior publication or use. The process involves filing a notice in Form 7 within one year from the date of grant, followed by an examination by the Opposition Board under Rules 56–60.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to distinguish between pre-grant and post-grant opposition, especially with respect to grounds for opposition and appealability. It is essential to highlight that post-grant opposition is limited in time (one year from grant) and that decisions are appealable to the High Court, unlike those under Section 25(1).\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the role of the Opposition Board under Rule 56 of The Patents Act, 1970. What are its responsibilities and how does it ensure fairness in the post-grant opposition process?**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** This question requires an analysis of the role of the Opposition Board under Rule 56, focusing on its responsibilities and the mechanisms that ensure fairness in the post-grant opposition process.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Rule 56**, upon receiving a notice of post-grant opposition, the Controller is required to constitute an **Opposition Board** comprising three members. One member is nominated as the **Chairman**, and an examiner who previously dealt with the patent application cannot be part of the Opposition Board for that particular case. The Opposition Board acts as an independent body under Rules 57–60, reviewing the opposition notice and supporting documents to evaluate the validity of the patent.\n\n**Application:** The Opposition Board is responsible for examining the grounds raised in the notice of opposition, including non-novelty, lack of inventive step, prior publication or use, and obviousness. It must ensure impartiality by excluding any examiner who previously worked on the application. After reviewing all evidence, the board submits a report within three months, providing detailed reasons for its findings and making a joint recommendation to the Controller regarding whether the patent should be revoked or amended. This process ensures that the evaluation is fair and based solely on legal and technical merits.\n\n**Conclusion:** The Opposition Board plays a crucial role in ensuring fairness and impartiality in the post-grant opposition process. It evaluates the validity of the patent independently, without bias from prior involvement with the application. By excluding previous examiners and following procedural rules under Rules 57–60, it ensures that decisions are made based on evidence and legal grounds rather than personal or institutional bias.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the importance of the Opposition Board’s independence and its role in ensuring fairness through procedural guidelines such as the exclusion of previous examiners. It is important to emphasize these points when discussing the board’s responsibilities.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the permissible grounds for post-grant opposition under Section 25(2) of The Patents Act, 1970? How do they differ from the grounds available in pre-grant opposition?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** This question requires an explanation of the permissible grounds for post-grant opposition under Section 25(2) and a comparison with the grounds available in pre-grant opposition.\n\n**Rule:** The permissible grounds for post-grant opposition include **non-novelty (Section 29)**, **lack of inventive step (Section 30)**, **prior publication or use**, and **obviousness to a person skilled in the art**. These are the same grounds available under pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1). However, unlike decisions under Section 25(1), which are not appealable, decisions made under Section 25(2) are **appealable to the High Court (Section 117A)**.\n\n**Application:** Under post-grant opposition, any person interested can challenge a patent on grounds such as non-novelty, lack of inventive step, or prior use. These grounds are identical to those available during pre-grant opposition. However, one key difference is the appealability of decisions: while decisions under Section 25(1) cannot be appealed, those under Section 25(2) can be challenged in the High Court if a party feels their rights have been adversely affected.\n\n**Conclusion:** The permissible grounds for post-grant opposition are the same as those under pre-grant opposition. However, the key difference lies in the appealability of decisions: while pre-grant opposition decisions are not appealable, post-grant opposition decisions can be appealed to the High Court, providing a legal remedy for affected parties.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse the procedural differences between pre-grant and post-grant opposition. It is important to highlight that the grounds are identical but that the appealability of decisions differs significantly.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Post-grant Opposition under Section 25(2) and the Opposition Board\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 25(2) | **NAIL** + clause (2) | NAIL is driven by interested hand, others can't touch |\n| Sec. 25(3) | **NAIL** + clause (3) | NAIL is hammered within year, else it's too late |\n| Sec. 25(4) | **NAIL** + clause (4) | NAIL is embedded in board's constitution, anchoring its rules |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **25-28** - opposition (pre-grant and post-grant), mention of inventor\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Only a person interested may oppose - which provision?  (picture: NAIL)\n2. Within one year from the date of publication of grant - which provision?  (picture: NAIL)\n3. Constitution and recommendation of the Opposition Board - which provision?  (picture: NAIL)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 25(2) | Allows post-grant opposition to challenge patent validity | Patent challenged after grant\n- Rule 55A | Details procedure for filing post-grant opposition | Form 7 and fee required\n- Rule 56 | Opposition Board composition with three members | Three-member board formed\n- Rules 57–60 | Procedure for the Opposition Board to examine oppositions | Board examines documents in three months\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Confusing pre-grant and post-grant opposition time limits\n- Examiners expect: Mentioning Section 25(2) and Rule 56 when discussing the Opposition Board\n- Structure tip: Always list the grounds for opposition (non-novelty, lack of inventive step, etc.) in your answer","storyEN":"**Story: The Patent of Mr. Ravi and the Opposition Board**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, where innovation thrived in every corner, lived a young inventor named **Ravi**, who had just received his patent for a new type of solar-powered water pump. He was overjoyed — it felt like the culmination of years of hard work. However, he soon found himself at the center of an unexpected legal battle.\n\nOne year after Ravi’s patent was granted on **Section 25(2)** of *The Patents Act, 1970 (as amended on 01-08-2024)*, a local company named **Hyderabad Tech Solutions** decided to challenge the validity of his invention. They filed a notice in **Form 7**, as required by **Rule 55A**, and paid the prescribed fee. Their statement was clear: they believed Ravi’s patent lacked novelty and inventive step under **Section 29** and **Section 30** respectively.\n\nThe Controller, upon receiving this notice, had no choice but to form an **Opposition Board** as outlined in **Rule 56**. This board consisted of three members — one acting as the **Chairman**, and two others chosen for their experience and impartiality. Notably, the examiner who had previously worked on Ravi’s application was excluded from this panel, ensuring fairness.\n\nThe Opposition Board met in a small but well-furnished room at the Patent Office. They examined all submitted evidence carefully under **Rules 57 to 60**, deliberating for three months before reaching their decision. Their report detailed that while parts of Ravi’s invention were novel, some aspects were not sufficiently inventive — they had been anticipated by prior art.\n\nThe board recommended that the patent be amended rather than revoked entirely. The Controller took this recommendation into account and decided to allow Ravi to revise his claims within a specified period. This outcome was not final, however; Ravi could still appeal the decision under **Section 117A** of *The Patents Act*, which allows for appeals to the High Court.\n\nMeanwhile, in another part of the city, an old man named **Kiran**, who had once worked as a patent examiner himself, watched these proceedings with interest. He remembered how the post-grant opposition process was designed — not just to protect the rights of inventors, but also to ensure that only truly innovative and valid patents remained in force.\n\nHe recalled reading about this very system in his old textbooks: “The purpose of post-grant opposition is a safeguard for third parties who may not have had the opportunity to challenge the patent during its examination phase,” he mused. “It ensures that only valid patents are granted and remain in force.”\n\nBack at the Patent Office, Ravi received the notice from the Controller. He was given time to amend his claims, but this time, he knew better — he would consult a patent agent and ensure every detail of his application was rock-solid.\n\nThe process had not been easy for him, but it had also taught him a valuable lesson: **Section 25(2)** is more than just a legal provision; it’s a mechanism that ensures fairness in the world of patents. And now, with the help of an expert, he would make sure his next application was beyond reproach.\n\n---\n\n**ANCHOR KEY**\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 25(2) | Post-grant opposition under *The Patents Act*, allowing interested parties to challenge a granted patent. | The legal framework that permits third parties to file an opposition after a patent is granted, ensuring only valid patents remain in force. |\n| Rule 55A | Procedure for filing post-grant opposition, including the required form (Form 7) and supporting documents. | A rule that outlines how an opposition notice must be filed with the Controller, specifying the necessary forms and evidence. |\n| Rule 56 | Constitution of the Opposition Board comprising three members, one as Chairman. | Mandates that the Controller form a fair and impartial board to evaluate the validity of a patent after opposition is filed. |\n| Rules 57 to 60 | Procedural steps followed by the Opposition Board in examining the notice. | Outlines how the Opposition Board reviews the opposition documents, conducts its evaluation, and submits a report within three months. |\n| Section 117A | Appeal of decisions made under Section 25(2) to the High Court. | Provides that any decision on post-grant opposition is appealable, ensuring legal remedies are available for parties affected by such decisions. |\n| Section 29 and 30 | Grounds for opposition including non-novelty and lack of inventive step. | These sections define the criteria under which a patent may be challenged after grant based on prior art or obviousness to a skilled person. |\n| Form 7 | Required form for filing post-grant opposition. | A specific document that must accompany any notice of opposition, detailing the grounds and evidence presented. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for \"Post-Grant Opposition under Section 25(2) and the Opposition Board\" (from Patent Agent Examination)\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym or Mnemonic for Post-Grant Opposition Elements:**\n\n**P.O.S.T.**  \n- **P**: *Petition* – Any person interested can file a petition.  \n- **O**: *Opposition* – It is a formal opposition to the grant of a patent.  \n- **S**: *Statement & Grounds* – Must include grounds for opposition and evidence.  \n- **T**: *Time-bound* – Must be filed within 1 year from the date of grant.\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors for Sections:**\n\n- **Section 25(2)** → Imagine a **\"25th floor\"** in a building where **opposition is being \"granted\"** (like a permit).  \n- **Rule 55A** → Think of **\"55A\" as a police code**, representing the **notice** required to file an opposition.  \n- **Rule 56** → Picture a **\"56-member board\"**, which is actually the **Opposition Board** constituted by the Controller.\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Pre-grant vs. Post-grant Opposition**:  \n  *Pre-grant* is filed **before grant** and is not appealable, while *post-grant* is filed **after grant** and **is appealable** to the High Court.\n\n- **Opposition Board vs. Controller’s Decision**:  \n  The **Opposition Board** conducts the examination and gives a recommendation, but it's the **Controller** who makes the final decision on granting or rejecting the patent.\n\n- **Post-grant Opposition vs. Revocation under Section 64**:  \n  *Post-grant* is time-bound (1 year from grant), while *revocation* can be filed **anytime during the life of the patent** and is not limited by a specific period.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Only a 'person interested' may oppose, as defined in Section 25 and 64 of the Patents Act. This term is debated but currently restricts opposition to stakeholders with direct interests.","question":"Who may file a post-grant opposition under Section 25(2)?"},{"answer":"Post-grant opposition must be filed within one year from the date of publication of the grant, as per Section 25(2).","question":"What is the time limit for post-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"The Controller constitutes the Opposition Board under Section 25(3)(b) and refers the case for examination and recommendations.","question":"How is the Opposition Board constituted?"},{"answer":"Opposition may be based on grounds in Section 25(2)(d) or (e), such as lack of novelty or obviousness, excluding personal documents under Section 25(5).","question":"What grounds can be raised in post-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"The one-year limit ensures timely resolution and prevents prolonged uncertainty, balancing patentee rights with public interest in Section 25(2).","question":"Why is post-grant opposition limited to one year?"},{"answer":"Pre-grant opposition occurs before grant (Section 25(1)), while post-grant opposition is after publication of the grant (Section 25(2)), with the latter involving the Opposition Board.","question":"How do pre-grant and post-grant opposition differ?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 25 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Identification of published applications”.","options":["Rule 19N","Rule 87","Rule 80","Rule 25"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Identification of published applications?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 25 is headed “Identification of published applications”.","options":["Application for registration of patent agents","Register of patents under section 67","Payment of fees","Identification of published applications"],"question":"Rule 25 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Section 35","Section 131","Section 87","Section 25"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Opposition to the patent?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 25 is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Opposition to the patent","Grant of patents","Transmission of orders of courts to Controller","Consequences of secrecy directions"],"question":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The application must be made within twelve months from the date of the first application in a convention country.","options":["a) Within six months","b) Within twelve months","c) Within eighteen months","d) Within twenty-four months"],"question":"Under Section 25(1)(i) of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the time limit for filing a convention application after the first application in a convention country?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Importation for the purpose of reasonable trial or experiment is excluded from being deemed public use.","options":["a) If a product made by the process was imported for commercial sale","b) If a product made by the process was imported for reasonable trial or experiment","c) If a product made by the process was exported from India","d) If a product made by the process was used in a laboratory in India"],"question":"According to Section 25(1)(d) of the Patents Act, 1970, when is an invention deemed to have been publicly used in India before the priority date?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The specification must disclose the source or geographical origin of biological material used.","options":["a) The cost of acquiring the biological material","b) The source or geographical origin of the biological material","c) The genetic sequence of the biological material","d) The method of synthesizing the biological material"],"question":"Under Section 25(1)(j) of the Patents Act, 1970, what must the complete specification disclose regarding biological material?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Furnishing false information is a ground for opposition under Section 25(1)(h).","options":["a) The patent is automatically revoked","b) The applicant is liable to a fine","c) It may be grounds for opposition","d) The application is deemed abandoned"],"question":"Under Section 25(1)(h) of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the consequence of furnishing false information to the Controller?"}]},"s1p_23":{"notes":"# Grant, Register of Patents and Renewal Fees – Study Notes for Patent Agent Examination\n\n---\n\n## 1. Definition and Key Concepts\n\n- **Grant of a Patent** refers to the legal process by which the Controller of Patents issues a patent certificate to an applicant who has successfully met all requirements under the *Patents Act, 1970*. The grant is subject to compliance with provisions such as novelty, inventive step, and industrial applicability (Section 2(1)(j)).\n\n- **Register of Patents** is maintained by the Controller under Section 83. It contains details like the name of the patentee, title of the invention, date of filing, and other relevant information about each granted patent. This register serves as an official record for legal purposes and public access.\n\n- **Renewal Fees** are mandatory payments made by the patentee to keep a patent in force after its initial grant. These fees ensure that the patent remains valid throughout its term (Section 53(2)).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Sections Governing Grant, Register of Patents and Renewal Fees\n\n### A. **Grant of Patent**\n\n- **Section 52**:\n   - Grants a patent to the true and first inventor in cases where it has been obtained by another through fraud.\n   - If the court revokes a patent or amends it, the Controller may grant a new patent to the petitioner under certain conditions.\n\n- **Section 53(1)**:\n   - The term of every patent granted after the commencement of the *Patents (Amendment) Act, 2002* is **twenty years from the date of filing** of the application.\n   - For international applications under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT), the term starts from the **international filing date**.\n\n- **Section 53(2)**:\n   - A patent ceases to have effect if the renewal fee is not paid within the prescribed period or any extended period. Once a patent lapses due to non-payment, its subject matter receives **no protection** (see also Section 53(4)).\n\n### B. **Register of Patents**\n\n- **Section 83**:\n   - The Controller maintains a register containing details such as the name of the patentee, title of the invention, date of filing, and other relevant information.\n   - This register is crucial for legal enforcement and public access to patent records.\n\n### C. **Renewal Fees**\n\n- **Section 53(2)**:\n   - A patent ceases to have effect if the renewal fee is not paid within the prescribed period or any extended period.\n   - On cessation of the patent right due to non-payment, the subject matter covered by the patent receives **no protection** (as per Section 53(4)).\n\n- **Section 61(3)**:\n   - No renewal fees are payable in respect of a **patent of addition**, unless it is restored as an independent patent under certain conditions.\n   - If a lapsed patent of addition is to be restored, the application must include the main patent and the failure to pay the fee must have been unintentional (as per Rule 84(3)).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Textbook Explanation – Why the Law Is as It Is\n\n### A. **Purpose of Granting Patents**\n\n- The grant of a patent is intended to reward inventors for their innovation and encourage further research by providing them with **exclusive rights** over their invention (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 193).\n- This exclusivity allows the inventor to commercialize the invention without competition, thus promoting technological advancement for the benefit of society.\n\n### B. **Register of Patents – Transparency and Legal Security**\n\n- The register is a **public record** that ensures transparency in patent ownership and provides legal certainty (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 151).\n- It also facilitates the identification of potential infringements and helps interested parties track the status of patents.\n\n### C. **Renewal Fees – Ensuring Continued Protection**\n\n- Renewal fees are a mechanism to ensure that only those who continue to invest in the commercialization or development of an invention retain its protection.\n- If the patentee fails to pay these fees, it is assumed that they no longer wish to maintain exclusive rights over the invention (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 193).\n- The law also provides a **restoration process** for lapsed patents if the failure was unintentional and not due to undue delay.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Criticism and Commentary\n\n### A. **Criticism of Renewal Fees**\n\n- Some critics argue that the high costs of renewal fees may be prohibitive, especially for small inventors or startups (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 193).\n- There is a concern that this could lead to the premature abandonment of potentially valuable inventions.\n\n### B. **Transparency in Patent Examination**\n\n- As per Section 144, examiner’s reports are not available for public access unless ordered by a court (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 151).\n- This has been criticized as reducing **transparency** in the patent examination process.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Considerations\n\n### A. **Filing for Restoration of a Lapsed Patent**\n\n- If a patent lapses due to non-payment of renewal fees, the applicant must file an application with the Controller.\n- The application should include:\n   - A verified statement explaining the unintentional failure to pay the fee (Rule 84(3)).\n   - Payment of any prescribed fee (if required).\n   - Publication of the application for opposition by interested parties within two months.\n\n### B. **Patent of Addition**\n\n- A patent of addition can be granted only if it is an improvement or modification of a main invention.\n- It cannot be granted before the **main patent** has been granted (Section 54(4)) and must have the same or later filing date as the main invention (Section 54(3)).\n- The term of a patent of addition matches that of the main patent, unless it is revoked and converted into an independent patent (Section 55(1)).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Summary\n\n| Topic | Key Points |\n|------|------------|\n| **Grant of Patent** | Section 52 and 53 govern the grant process; term is twenty years from filing date. |\n| **Register of Patents** | Maintained under Section 83; contains details like patentee name, invention title, etc. |\n| **Renewal Fees** | Paid annually to keep a patent in force (Section 53(2)); lapsed patents lose all protection. |\n| **Patent of Addition** | Must be an improvement on the main invention and cannot be granted before the main patent is issued. |\n| **Restoration of Lapsed Patents** | Possible if failure to pay was unintentional; governed by Section 61(3) and Rule 84(3). |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Verified Cases (if any)\n\n- **No verified cases are available for this topic** – check IndianKanoon before citing one.\n\n---\n\nThis concludes the study notes on \"Grant, Register of Patents and Renewal Fees\" from *Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)*. Use these notes to prepare for both theoretical and practical aspects of the examination.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 43 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 43. Grant of patents.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent has been found to be in order\n> for grant of the patent and either\n> (a) the application has not been refused by the Controller by virtue\n> of any power vested in him by this Act; or\n> (b) the application has not been found to be in contravention of any\n> of the provisions of this Act,\n> the patent shall be granted as expeditiously as possible to the\n> applicant or, in the case of a joint application, to the applicants\n> jointly, with the seal of the patent office and the date on which\n> the patent is granted shall be entered in the register.\n>\n> (2) On the grant of patent, the Controller shall publish the fact\n> that the patent has been granted and thereupon the application,\n> specification and other documents related thereto shall be open for\n> public inspection.\n\n**Section 53 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 53. Term of patent.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions of this Act, the term of every patent\n> granted, after the commencement of the Patents (Amendment) Act,\n> 2002, and the term of every patent which has not expired and has not\n> ceased to have effect, on the date of such commencement, under this\n> Act, shall be twenty years from the date of filing of the\n> application for the patent.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this sub-section, the term of\n> patent in case of International applications filed under the Patent\n> Cooperation Treaty designating India, shall be twenty years from the\n> international filing date accorded under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty.\n>\n> (2) A patent shall cease to have effect notwithstanding anything\n> therein or in this Act on the expiration of the period prescribed\n> for the payment of any renewal fee, if that fee is not paid within\n> the prescribed period or within such extended period as may be\n> prescribed.\n>\n> (4) Notwithstanding anything contained in any other law for the time\n> being in force, on cessation of the patent right due to non-payment\n> of renewal fee or on the expiry of the term of patent, the subject\n> matter covered by the said patent shall not be entitled to any\n> protection.\n>\n>  47\n>\n> CHAPTER IX PATENTS OF ADDITION\n\n**Section 67 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 67. Register of patents and particulars to be entered therein.\n> (1) There shall be kept at the patent office a register of patents,\n> wherein shall be entered (a) the names and addresses of grantees of\n> patents;\n> (b) notifications of assignments and of transmissions of patents, of\n> licences under patents, and of amendments, extensions, and\n> revocations of patents; and\n> (c) particulars of such other matters affecting the validity or\n> proprietorship of patents as may be prescribed.\n>\n> (2) No notice of any trust, whether express, implied or\n> constructive, shall be entered in the register, and the Controller\n> shall not be affected by any such notice.\n>\n> (3) Subject to the superintendence and direction of the Central\n> Government, the register shall be kept under the control and\n> management of the Controller.\n>\n> (4) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (1), it shall\n> be lawful for the Controller to keep the register of patents or any\n> part thereof in computer floppies, diskettes or any other electronic\n> form subject to such safeguards as may be prescribed.\n>\n> (5) Notwithstanding anything contained in the Indian Evidence Act,\n> 1872 (1 of 1872), a copy of, or extracts from, the register of\n> patents, certified to be a true copy under the hand of the\n> Controller or any officer duly authorised by the Controller in this\n> behalf shall, in all legal proceedings, be admissible in evidence.\n>\n> (6) In the event the register is kept wholly or partly in computer\n> floppies, diskettes or any other electronic form,\n> (a) reference in this Act to an entry in the register shall be\n> deemed to include reference to a record of particulars kept in\n> computer floppies, diskettes or any other electronic form and\n> comprising the register or part of the register;\n> (b) references in this Act to particulars being registered or\n> entered in the register shall be deemed to include references to the\n> keeping of record of those particulars comprising the register or\n> part of the register in computer floppies, diskettes or any other\n>\n>  59\n>\n> electronic form; and\n> (c) references in this Act to the rectification of the register are\n> to be read as including references to the rectification of the\n> record of particulars kept in computer floppies, diskettes or any\n> other electronic form and comprising the register or part of the\n> register.\n\n**Section 68 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 68. Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly\n> executed.\n> An assignment of a patent or of a share in a patent, a mortgage,\n> licence or the creation of any other interest in a patent shall not\n> be valid unless the same were in writing and the agreement between\n> the parties concerned is reduced to the form of a document embodying\n> all the terms and conditions governing their rights and obligations\n> and duly executed.\n\n**Section 69 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 69. Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc.\n> (1) Where any person becomes entitled by assignment, transmission or\n> operation of law to a patent or to a share in a patent or becomes\n> entitled as a mortgagee, licensee or otherwise to any other interest\n> in a patent, he shall apply in writing in the prescribed manner to\n> the Controller for the registration of his title or, as the case may\n> be, of notice of his interest in the register.\n>\n> (2) Without prejudice to the provisions of sub-section (1), an\n> application for the registration of the title of any person becoming\n> entitled by assignment to a patent or a share in a patent or\n> becoming entitled by virtue of a mortgage, licence or other\n> instrument to any other interest in a patent may be made in the\n> prescribed manner by the assignor, mortgagor, licensor or other\n> party to that instrument, as the case may be.\n>\n> (3) Where an application is made under this section for the\n> registration of the title of any person the Controller shall, upon\n> proof of title to his satisfaction,\n> (a) where that person is entitled to a patent or a share in a\n> patent, register him in the register as proprietor or co-proprietor\n> of the patent, and enter in the register particulars of the\n> instrument or event by which he derives title; or\n> (b) where that person is entitled to any other interest in the\n> patent, enter in the register notice of his interest, with\n>\n>  60\n>\n> particulars of the instrument, if any, creating it:\n> Provided that if there is any dispute between the parties whether\n> the assignment, mortgage, licence, transmission, operation of law or\n> any other such transaction has validly vested in such person a title\n> to the patent or any share or interest therein, the Controller may\n> refuse to take any action under clause (a) or, as the case may be,\n> under clause (b), until the rights of the parties have been\n> determined by a competent court.\n>\n> (4) There shall be supplied to the Controller in the prescribed\n> manner for being filed in the patent office copies of all\n> agreements, licences and other documents affecting the title to any\n> patent or any licence thereunder authenticated in the prescribed\n> manner and also such other documents as may be prescribed relevant\n> to the subject-matter:\n> Provided that in the case of a licence granted under a patent, the\n> Controller shall, if so requested by the patentee or licensee, take\n> steps for securing that the terms of the licence are not disclosed\n> to any person except under the order of a court.\n>\n> (5) Except for the purposes of an application under sub-section (1)\n> or of an application to rectify the register, a document in respect\n> of which no entry has been made in the register under sub-section\n> (3) shall not be admitted by the Controller or by any court as\n> evidence of the title of any person to a patent or to a share or\n> interest therein unless the Controller or the court, for reasons to\n> be recorded in writing, otherwise directs.\n\n**Rule 74 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 74. Form of patent\n> (1) A patent shall be in the form as specified in the Third Schedule\n> with such modifications as the circumstances of each case may\n> require and shall bear the number accorded to the application under\n> rule 37.\n>\n> (2) The patent certificate shall ordinarily be issued within seven\n> days from the date of grant of patent under section 43.\n\n**Rule 80 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 80. Renewal fees under section 53\n> (1) To keep a patent in force, the renewal fees specified in the\n> First Schedule shall be payable at the expiration of the second year\n> from the date of the patent or of any succeeding year and the same\n> shall be remitted to the patent office before the expiration of the\n> second or the succeeding year.\n>\n> (1A) The period for payment of renewal fees so specified in sub-rule\n> (1) may be extended to such a period not being more than six months\n> if the request for such extension of time is made in Form 4 with the\n> fee specified in the First Schedule.\n>\n> (2) While paying the renewal fee, the number and date of the patent\n> concerned and the year in respect of which the fee is paid shall be\n> quoted.\n>\n> (3) The annual renewal fees payable in respect of two or more years\n> may be paid in advance.\n> Provided that where the renewal fees is paid in advance through\n>\n>  50\n>\n> electronic mode for a period of at least 4 years, a ten per cent\n> reduction in fee shall be applicable for such renewal.\n>\n> (4) The Controller shall, after making such an enquiry as he may\n> deem necessary, credit any renewal fee and issue a certificate that\n> the fee has been paid.\n>\n>  51\n>\n> CHAPTER IX AMENDMENT OF APPLICATION, SPECIFICATION OR ANY DOCUMENT\n> RELATING THERETO\n\n**Rule 88 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 88. Register of patents under section 67\n> (1) Upon the grant of a patent, the Controller shall enter in the\n> register of patents at each appropriate office, the name, address\n> and nationality of the grantee as the patentee thereof, the title of\n> the invention (including the categories to which the invention\n> relates), the date of the patent and the date of grant thereof\n> together with the address for service of the patentee.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall also enter in the register of patents\n> particulars regarding proceedings under the Act before the\n> Controller or Appellate Board or the courts in respect of every\n> patent.\n>\n> (3) Where the register of patents or any part thereof is in computer\n> floppies, diskettes or any other electronic form it shall be\n> maintained and accessed only by the person who is duly authorised by\n> the Controller and no entry or alteration of any entry or\n> rectification of any entry in the said register shall be made by any\n> person who is not so authorised by the Controller.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Important Questions: Grant, Register of Patents and Renewal Fees**\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the legal process for granting a patent under the *Patents Act, 1970*, including the conditions and procedures involved.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the legal requirements and procedures for granting a patent under the *Patents Act, 1970*?\n\n**Rule:**  \n- Under **Section 43(1)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, a patent is granted to an applicant if the application is found to be in order and not refused by the Controller or found to be in contravention of any provision of the Act. The grant must happen as expeditiously as possible.\n- **Section 53(1)** governs the term of a patent, which is twenty years from the date of filing (or international filing date for PCT applications).\n- **Section 2(1)(j)** requires that an invention must be novel, involve an inventive step, and be industrially applicable to qualify for a patent.\n\n**Application:**  \nWhen an application for a patent is filed with the Controller, it undergoes examination. If the Controller finds that the application meets all legal requirements under the *Patents Act*, including novelty, inventive step, and industrial applicability (as per **Section 2(1)(j)**), the patent will be granted as expeditiously as possible (**Section 43(1)**). The grant is subject to payment of fees and compliance with procedural matters. Once granted, the Controller publishes the grant and makes all related documents available for public inspection (**Section 43(2)**).\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe process of granting a patent involves ensuring that the application meets legal requirements such as novelty, inventive step, and industrial applicability under **Sections 2(1)(j)** and **43**, with the term being twenty years from the date of filing. The Controller plays a central role in this process.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention the requirement for expeditious grant under **Section 43(1)** or the importance of public inspection after grant, which are critical aspects of the patent grant procedure.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the purpose and function of the *Register of Patents* as maintained by the Controller under the *Patents Act, 1970*.**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2021, 2023)*\n\n**Issue:** What is the purpose and function of the *Register of Patents*, and how does it serve legal and public interests?\n\n**Rule:**  \n- **Section 83** mandates that the Controller maintain a register containing details such as the name of the patentee, title of the invention, date of filing, and other relevant information. This register is maintained for legal enforcement and public access.\n- The *Register* serves as an official record, ensuring transparency in ownership and providing legal certainty (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 151**).\n\n**Application:**  \nThe *Register of Patents* is a public record that contains information about each granted patent, including the name of the patentee, invention title, filing date, etc. This register allows interested parties to track the status of patents and identify potential infringements. It also provides legal certainty by serving as an official record for enforcement purposes.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe *Register of Patents* is a crucial tool for ensuring transparency in patent ownership and providing legal certainty under **Section 83** of the *Patents Act, 1970*. It facilitates public access to patent records and supports legal enforcement.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the significance of the register as a source of legal certainty and its role in identifying potential patent infringements.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the concept of *renewal fees* under **Section 53(2)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, and discuss their importance in maintaining the validity of a patent.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** What are renewal fees under **Section 53(2)**, and why are they important for the continued validity of a patent?\n\n**Rule:**  \n- Under **Section 53(2)**, a patent ceases to have effect if the renewal fee is not paid within the prescribed period or any extended period. Once lapsed due to non-payment, the subject matter receives no protection (**Section 53(4)**).\n- Renewal fees are mandatory payments made by the patentee to keep the patent in force after its initial grant.\n\n**Application:**  \nRenewal fees must be paid periodically (usually annually) to maintain a patent’s validity. If not paid, the patent lapses and loses all protection (**Section 53(4)**). The law also provides for a restoration process if the failure was unintentional (**Rule 84(3)**).\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nRenewal fees are essential to keep a patent valid under **Section 53(2)**. Failure to pay results in loss of protection, but unintentional lapses may be restored.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention the restoration process for lapsed patents or the specific consequence of non-payment under **Section 53(4)**.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are *patents of addition*, and what are the conditions for their grant under the *Patents Act, 1970*?**  \n*8 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2023)*\n\n**Issue:** Under what conditions can a *patent of addition* be granted?\n\n**Rule:**  \n- A patent of addition is an improvement or modification to a main invention and must have the same or later filing date as the main invention (**Section 54(3)**).\n- It cannot be granted before the **main patent** has been issued (**Section 54(4)**).\n\n**Application:**  \nA *patent of addition* can only be filed if it is an improvement on a previously granted main patent. The application must have the same or later filing date than the main invention and must be filed after the grant of the main patent.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nA *patent of addition* requires that it is an improvement to a main invention, has the same or later filing date as the main patent, and cannot be granted before the main patent is issued (**Sections 54(3) and (4)**).\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse the conditions for granting a *patent of addition* with those for the main patent.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the procedure for restoring a lapsed patent due to non-payment of renewal fees under **Section 61(3)** and **Rule 84(3)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*.**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2021)*\n\n**Issue:** What is the procedure for restoring a lapsed patent due to non-payment of renewal fees?\n\n**Rule:**  \n- Under **Section 61(3)** and **Rule 84(3)**, if a *patent of addition* lapses due to non-payment, it may be restored as an independent patent if the failure was unintentional.\n- The applicant must file an application with the Controller, include a verified statement explaining the unintentional failure, pay any prescribed fee, and publish the application for opposition within two months.\n\n**Application:**  \nTo restore a lapsed patent due to non-payment of renewal fees, the applicant must submit a verified statement explaining the unintentional failure to pay the fee (**Rule 84(3)**), make payment of any required fee, and publish the restoration request. The Controller may then grant restoration if satisfied with the explanation.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nRestoration of a lapsed patent is possible under **Section 61(3)** and **Rule 84(3)** if the failure to pay was unintentional. This includes submitting verified statements, paying fees, and publishing for opposition.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often forget that restoration requires publication for opposition within two months and may not mention the need for a verified statement.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Grant, Register of Patents and Renewal Fees\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 43 | **ROOM** | ROOM echoes with patent grant announcements. |\n| Sec. 53 | **LIME** | LIME ripens slowly, like a six-month fee extension. |\n| Sec. 67 | **JUG** | JUG holds a lapsed patent, begging for restoration. |\n| Sec. 68 | **CHEF** | CHEF stamps patent assignments on a bustling register. |\n| Sec. 69 | **SHIP** | SHIP navigates the patent register, avoiding fees. |\n| Rule 80 | **VASE** | VASE holds patent grants, gleaming under rule light. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **43-53** - grant, rights, term of patent\n- **68-78** - assignments, registration, rectification\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Grant and publication of grant - which provision?  (picture: ROOM)\n2. Renewal fees from the third year and the six-month extension - which provision?  (picture: LIME)\n3. Restoration of a lapsed patent under Sections 60 to 62 - which provision?  (picture: JUG)\n4. Register of Patents and registration of assignments - which provision?  (picture: CHEF)\n5. Grant, Register of Patents and Renewal Fees - which provision?  (picture: SHIP)\n6. Grant, Register of Patents and Renewal Fees - which provision?  (picture: VASE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 52 | Grants patent to true inventor if obtained by fraud | Inventor's right restored\n- Section 53(1) | Patent term is 20 years from filing date | Clock starts at filing\n- Section 53(2) | Patent lapses for non-payment of renewal fee | Fee missed, patent dies\n- Section 83 | Register contains patentee name and invention title | Legal record book\n- Section 61(3) | No fees for patents of addition unless restored | Addition needs care\n- Rule 80 | Renewal fees due every year, with possible extension | Money due on time\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing patent term start dates (filing vs. grant)\n- Use exact section numbers in answers\n- Structure responses: definition → law → explanation → example","storyEN":"**Story: The Patent of Ravi and the City of Hyderabad**\n\nIn the bustling city of Hyderabad, where innovation thrived in every corner, a young inventor named **Ravi** had developed a new type of solar-powered water pump. He believed this invention could change the lives of farmers across Telangana. With his father’s encouragement, he submitted an application to the Controller under the *Patents Act, 1970*. The process was meticulous and required him to prove that his invention met all legal requirements — **novelty**, **inventive step**, and **industrial applicability** (Section 2(1)(j)). After months of waiting, Ravi received a letter from the Controller: *“Your patent has been granted.”* This was an official recognition under **Section 52**, which ensured that only the true and first inventor would be awarded the rights to his creation.\n\nThe Controller maintained a **Register of Patents** under **Section 83**, where every detail — from Ravi’s name to the title of his invention, “Solar-Powered Water Pump,” was carefully recorded. This register served as an official record that could be accessed by anyone interested in knowing who held which patent. It also ensured legal certainty and transparency for all parties involved.\n\nBut with this grant came a responsibility: **renewal fees** under **Section 53(2)**. Ravi learned that he had to pay these fees every year, or else his patent would cease to have effect. The law was clear — if the renewal fee wasn’t paid within the prescribed period or any extended time, the subject matter of the invention received **no protection** (Section 53(4)). This meant that anyone could use Ravi’s invention freely after it lapsed.\n\nOne year passed smoothly, and Ravi made the payment. But in the second year, due to a sudden illness, he missed the deadline for paying the renewal fee. His patent now faced the risk of **lapsing**. However, Ravi was not without hope — under **Section 61(3)**, there was a possibility to restore his patent if the failure to pay was unintentional and not due to undue delay.\n\nRavi’s father, who had been an engineer in the past, advised him: “You must apply for restoration immediately. You’ll need to explain why you missed the fee and provide proof that it wasn’t intentional.” Ravi followed this advice and filed a request with the Controller, including a **verified statement** under **Rule 84(3)** explaining his situation.\n\nMeanwhile, in another part of Hyderabad, an entrepreneur named **Anand**, who had been following the Register of Patents, noticed that Ravi’s patent was about to lapse. Anand had always wanted to develop a similar invention and thought this could be his chance. But before he could proceed, he saw that Ravi had applied for restoration.\n\nThe Controller reviewed Ravi’s case and found it convincing. He granted the **restoration** of the patent. This meant that even though Ravi had missed the renewal fee, the law allowed him to keep his rights over the invention as long as the delay was unintentional.\n\nIn the end, Ravi’s story became a lesson for many young inventors in Hyderabad: **Section 53(1)** ensured that patents were valid for **twenty years from the date of filing**. This gave inventors enough time to commercialize their inventions and benefit from them — but only if they remembered to pay the renewal fees on time.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty years from the date of filing | Section 53(1) | The term of every patent granted after the commencement of the Patents (Amendment) Act, 2002 is twenty years from the date of filing. |\n| Failure to pay renewal fee led to a risk of lapping | Section 53(2) | A patent ceases to have effect if the renewal fee is not paid within the prescribed period or any extended period. |\n| The Register of Patents recorded Ravi’s invention details | Section 83 | The Controller maintains a register containing details such as the name of the patentee, title of the invention, date of filing, and other relevant information. |\n| Restoration was possible due to unintentional delay | Section 61(3) | No renewal fees are payable in respect of a patent of addition unless it is restored as an independent patent under certain conditions. |\n| Ravi's verified statement explained his situation | Rule 84(3) | An application for restoration must include a verified statement explaining the unintentional failure to pay the fee. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **\"Grant, Register of Patents and Renewal Fees\"**  \nBased on the **Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**  \n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Grant Conditions**  \n**G-R-A-N-T**:  \n- **G**rant requires **validity** of invention (Section 2).  \n- **R**egister of patents involves **filing** of application (Section 6).  \n- **A**pplication must be in **prescribed form** (Section 7).  \n- **N**ew patent can be granted if **no prior art** exists (Section 29).  \n- **T**erm of patent is **twenty years** from filing date (Section 53(1)).  \n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-Anchors for Sections**  \n- **Section 6**: *Persons entitled to apply* → Think of a **\"person\"** holding a **key** (shape like \"6\") to open the patent door.  \n- **Section 7**: *Form of application* → Picture a **\"form\"** being filled out on a **table with 7 legs**.  \n- **Section 144**: *Examiner’s report not public* → Imagine a **\"closed book\"** (like a sealed report) and the number **144** shaped like a **book**.  \n- **Section 53(1)**: *20-year term of patent* → Think of a **clock face with \"20\"** on it, ticking away the patent’s life.  \n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**  \n- **Patent of Addition vs. Main Patent**:  \n  - *Difference*: A **patent of addition** is for an **improvement or modification** to a main invention (Section 54(1)), while the **main patent** covers the original invention.  \n  - *Tip*: Remember: \"Addition\" = **extra**, not the full invention.\n\n- **Renewal Fee vs. Restoration of Patent**:  \n  - *Difference*: **Renewal fee** is paid to keep a patent active (Section 53(2)), whereas **restoration** applies when a patent has lapsed due to non-payment and is being revived (Section 61).  \n  - *Tip*: \"Renewal\" = **ongoing**, while \"Restoration\" = **back from the dead**.\n\n- **Patent Term vs. Priority Date**:  \n  - *Difference*: The **term of patent** is how long the patent lasts (20 years), but the **priority date** refers to when the application was first filed, which can affect claims in case of prior art (Section 11).  \n  - *Tip*: \"Term\" = **length**, \"Priority\" = **starting point**.  \n\n--- \n\nThese techniques are designed for quick recall and clarity during exams or study sessions.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"A lapsed patent may be restored within 18 months from the date it ceased to have effect, as per Section 60.","question":"What is the time limit for restoring a lapsed patent under Section 60?"},{"answer":"An application for restoration is filed in Form 15, as per Rule 84(1).","question":"What form is used to apply for restoration of a lapsed patent?"},{"answer":"Proof of assignment must be submitted to confirm the applicant's right to file, as per Section 7(2) and Rule 10.","question":"Why is proof of assignment required for a patent restoration application?"},{"answer":"Non-payment leads to the patent lapsing, as per Section 53(2), but restoration may be sought within 18 months.","question":"What happens if a patentee fails to pay renewal fees by the due date?"},{"answer":"The Register of Patents records details like assignments and proprietorship, as per Section 67.","question":"What is the purpose of the Register of Patents under Section 67?"},{"answer":"Restoration requires substantial proof of non-payment reasons, as highlighted in Sanat Products vs. Union of India.","question":"Why is restoration of a lapsed patent challenging?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 69 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under”.","options":["Rule 31","Rule 69","Rule 100","Rule 67"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 69 is headed “Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under”.","options":["Registration of patent agents","Transmittal of the International Search Report and written","Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under","Publication of application"],"question":"Rule 69 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 68 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly”.","options":["Section 41","Section 84","Section 68","Section 62"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 68 is headed “Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly”.","options":["Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats","Powers of Controller to correct clerical errors, etc","Priority dates of claims of a complete specification","Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly"],"question":"Section 68 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 53 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Term of patent”.","options":["Section 144","Section 9","Section 53","Section 51"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Term of patent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 53 is headed “Term of patent”.","options":["Power of Controller in cases of anticipation","Form of application","Terms and conditions of compulsory licences","Term of patent"],"question":"Section 53 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 67 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Form of making a claim under section 28(3)”.","options":["Rule 67","Rule 138","Rule 29","Rule 69"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Form of making a claim under section 28(3)?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 67 is headed “Form of making a claim under section 28(3)”.","options":["Request for publication","Examination of application","Form of making a claim under section 28(3)","Registration of patent agents"],"question":"Rule 67 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 69 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc”.","options":["Section 124","Section 69","Section 154","Section 46"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 69 is headed “Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc”.","options":["General purposes for granting compulsory licences","Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc","Declaration by infant, lunatic, etc","Qualifications for registration as patent agents"],"question":"Section 69 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 68 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Form of application to be made under section 28(7)”.","options":["Rule 19A","Rule 104","Rule 47","Rule 68"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Form of application to be made under section 28(7)?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 68 is headed “Form of application to be made under section 28(7)”.","options":["Filing of reply statement and evidence","Manner and time of opposition to the making of corrections","Form of application to be made under section 28(7)","Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming"],"question":"Rule 68 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Where an application for a patent has been found to be in order for grant of the patent and either (a) the application has not been refused by the Controller by virtue of any power vested in him by this Act; or (b) the application has not been found to be in contravention of any of the provisions of this Act, the patent shall be granted as expeditiously as possible to the applicant or, in the case of a joint application, to the applicants jointly","options":["If the application is found to be in order and not refused by the Controller","If the application is filed in duplicate","If the applicant pays a premium fee","If the application is submitted in a foreign language"],"question":"Under which condition is a patent granted as expeditiously as possible according to Section 43(1) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Subject to the provisions of this Act, the term of every patent granted, after the commencement of the Patents (Amendment) Act, 2002, and the term of every patent which has not expired and has not ceased to have effect, on the date of such commencement, under this Act, shall be twenty years from the date of filing of the application for the patent.","options":["Twenty years from the date of filing of the application","Fifteen years from the date of filing of the application","Twenty years from the date of grant","Twenty-five years from the date of filing of the application"],"question":"What is the term of a patent granted after the commencement of the Patents (Amendment) Act, 2002, according to Section 53(1)?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (1), it shall be lawful for the Controller to keep the register of patents or any part thereof in computer floppies, diskettes or any other electronic form subject to such safeguards as may be prescribed.","options":["In computer floppies, diskettes, or any other electronic form","Only in paper format","In encrypted hard copies","In oral records"],"question":"According to Section 67(4), in what form may the Controller keep the register of patents?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"An assignment of a patent or of a share in a patent, a mortgage, licence or the creation of any other interest in a patent shall not be valid unless the same were in writing and the agreement between the parties concerned is reduced to the form of a document embodying all the terms and conditions governing their rights and obligations and duly executed.","options":["It must be in writing and duly executed","It must be registered with the Controller","It must be notarized","It must be published in the Gazette"],"question":"Under Section 68 of the Patents Act, 1970, what is required for an assignment of a patent to be valid?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Where any person becomes entitled by assignment, transmission or operation of law to a patent or to a share in a patent or becomes entitled as a mortgagee, licensee or otherwise to any other interest in a patent, he shall apply in writing in the prescribed manner to the Controller for the registration of his title or, as the case may be, of notice of his interest in the register.","options":["Apply in writing to the Controller for registration of their title","Publish the assignment in the newspaper","Pay a registration fee to the patent office","File a notice with the Registrar of Trademarks"],"question":"According to Section 69(1), what must a person do if they become entitled to a patent by assignment or transmission?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"To keep a patent in force, the renewal fees specified in the First Schedule shall be payable at the expiration of the second year from the date of the patent or of any succeeding year and the same shall be remitted to the patent office before the expiration of the second or the succeeding year.","options":["At the expiration of the second year from the date of the patent","At the expiration of the first year from the date of the patent","At the expiration of the third year from the date of the patent","At the time of filing the patent application"],"question":"Under Rule 80(1) of the Patents Rules, 2003, when must the first renewal fee be paid?"}]},"s1p_24":{"notes":"# Secrecy Directions and Inventions Relevant for Defence\n\n## Definition\n\nSecrecy directions are legal instructions issued by the Controller under **Section 35** of the Patents Act, 1970, to prevent the publication or disclosure of information related to inventions that are relevant for defence purposes. These directions aim to protect sensitive technologies and maintain national security.\n\n---\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n### 1. **Scope of Inventions Covered**\n- Secrecy directions apply primarily to inventions relating to:\n  - Defence (e.g., weapons, military equipment)\n  - Atomic energy\n  - Any other invention that may affect the sovereignty or security of India\n\nThese are outlined in **Section 35** and further explained in **Section 42**, which deals with disclosures made to the Government.\n\n### 2. **Prohibition on Publication**\n- Under **Section 35**, the Controller can impose secrecy directions that prohibit the publication of an invention.\n- This prohibition applies until such time as the Central Government lifts these restrictions or the secrecy direction is revoked (**Section 38**).\n\n### 3. **Periodic Review by Central Government**\n- The Central Government reviews all secrecy directions imposed under **Section 35** at least every six months, as mentioned in **Section 36**.\n\n### 4. **Consequences of Non-Compliance**\n- If a person contravenes any secrecy direction issued under **Section 35**, the application may be deemed abandoned (**Section 64(1)(n)**).\n- Additionally, if a patent has already been granted, it can be revoked under **Section 64**.\n\n### 5. **Limitations on Foreign Applications**\n- An invention covered by secrecy directions cannot be the subject of a foreign patent application until such directions are revoked (**Section 39**).\n\n---\n\n## Why the Law is as It Is\n\nThe provisions related to secrecy directions and inventions relevant for defence are designed to protect national interests and sensitive technologies. The law ensures that potentially harmful or strategically important inventions do not fall into the wrong hands.\n\n- **Section 35**, as explained in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 209*, states that secrecy directions are imposed on applications for inventions related to defence. These directions prevent premature publication of such inventions, which could compromise national security.\n- **Section 36** mandates periodic reviews by the Central Government to ensure that the secrecy is still necessary and does not unduly delay the application process (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 210*).\n- The law also provides for **Section 38**, which allows for the revocation of secrecy directions if it is determined that they are no longer required.\n\n---\n\n## Procedural Aspects\n\n### Application Process\n- When an application is filed and found to be relevant for defence purposes, the Controller will issue a notice under **Section 35**.\n- The application will not be published until the secrecy direction is revoked or lifted (**Section 37(1) proviso**, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 210*).\n\n### Timeframe for Examination\n- Applications subject to secrecy directions have an extended timeframe for examination:\n  - **31 months** from the date of earliest filing or within **6 months** of revocation of secrecy directions, whichever is later (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 210*).\n\n---\n\n## Legal Implications\n\n### Revocation of Secrecy Directions\n- The Central Government may revoke secrecy directions if it determines that the publication would not be harmful to national interests (**Section 38**).\n- If the Controller has not imposed restrictions on an application that the Central Government believes should be restricted, then the government can direct the Controller to impose such restrictions (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 210*).\n\n### Penalties for Non-Compliance\n- Any person who contravenes a secrecy direction is liable under **Section 118** of the Patents Act, which deals with penalties for contravention of secrecy provisions.\n- In such cases, the application may be deemed abandoned and any patent granted may be revoked.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nSecrecy directions are an essential tool in protecting sensitive inventions that relate to national defence or atomic energy. These directions are issued under **Section 35**, reviewed periodically by the Central Government (**Section 36**), and can be revoked if no longer needed (**Section 38**). Non-compliance with these directions may lead to abandonment of applications (**Section 64**) or revocation of patents.\n\nThe law aims to balance the protection of national interests with the need for timely examination and grant of patents. It ensures that sensitive technologies are not prematurely disclosed, while also allowing for necessary oversight by government authorities.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 35 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 35. Secrecy directions relating to inventions relevant for defence\n> purposes.\n> (1) Where, in respect of an application made before or after the\n> commencement of this Act for a patent, it appears to the Controller\n> that the invention is one of a class notified to him by the Central\n> Government as relevant for defence purposes, or, where otherwise the\n> invention appears to him to be so relevant, he may give directions\n> for prohibiting or restricting the publication of information with\n> respect to the invention or the communication of such information .\n>\n> (2) Where the Controller gives any such directions as are referred\n> to in sub-section (1), he shall give notice of the application and\n> of the directions to the Central Government, and the Central\n> Government shall, upon receipt of such notice, consider whether the\n> publication of the invention would be prejudicial to the defence of\n> India, and if upon such consideration, it appears to it that the\n> publication of the invention would not so prejudice, give notice to\n> the Controller to that effect, who shall thereupon revoke the\n> directions and notify the applicant accordingly.\n>\n> (3) Without prejudice to the provisions contained in sub-section\n> (1), where the Central Government is of opinion that an invention in\n> respect of which the Controller has not given any directions under\n> sub-section (1), is relevant for defence purposes, it may at any\n> time before grant of patent notify the Controller to that effect,\n> and thereupon the provisions of that sub-section shall apply as if\n> the invention were one of the class notified by the Central\n> Government, and accordingly the Controller shall give notice to the\n> Central Government of the directions issued by him.\n\n**Section 36 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 36. Secrecy directions to be periodically reviewed.\n> (1) The question whether an invention in respect of which directions\n> have been given under section 35 continues to be relevant for\n> defence purposes shall be reconsidered by the Central Government at\n> intervals of six months or on a request made by the applicant which\n> is found to be reasonable by the Controller and if, on such\n> reconsideration it appears to the Central Government that the\n> publication of the invention would no longer be prejudicial to the\n> defence of India or in case of an application filed by a foreign\n>\n>  38\n>\n> applicant it is found that the invention is published outside India\n> it shall forthwith give notice to the Controller to revoke the\n> direction and the Controllers shall thereupon revoke the directions\n> previously given by him.\n>\n> (2) The result of every re-consideration under sub-section (1),\n> shall be communicated to the applicant within such time and in such\n> manner as may be prescribed.\n\n**Section 37 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 37. Consequences of secrecy directions.\n> (1) So long as any directions under section 35 are in force in\n> respect of an application\n> (a) the Controller shall not pass an order refusing to grant the\n> same; and\n> (b) notwithstanding anything contained in this Act, no appeal shall\n> lie from any order of the Controller passed in respect thereof:\n> Provided that the application may, subject to the directions,\n> proceed up to the stage of grant of the patent, but the application\n> and the specification found to be in order for grant of the patent\n> shall not be published, and no patent shall be granted in pursuance\n> of that application.\n>\n> (2) Where a complete specification filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent for an invention in respect of which\n> directions have been given under section 35 is found to be in order\n> for grant of the patent during the continuance in force of the\n> directions, then\n> (a) if, during the continuance in force of the directions, any use\n> of the invention is made by or on behalf of, or to the order of the\n> Government, the provisions of sections 100, 101 and 103 shall apply\n> in relation to that use as if the patent had been granted for the\n> invention ; and\n> (b) if it appears to the Central Government that the applicant for\n> the patent has suffered hardship by reason of the continuance in\n> force of the directions, the Central Government may make to him such\n> payment (if any) by way of solatium as appears to the Central\n> Government to be reasonable having regard to the novelty and utility\n> of the invention and the purpose for which it is designed, and to\n> any other relevant circumstances.\n>\n> (3) Where a patent is granted in pursuance of an application in\n>\n>  39\n>\n> respect of which directions have been given under section 35, no\n> renewal fee shall be payable in respect of any period during which\n> those directions were in force.\n\n**Section 38 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 38. Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of time.\n> When any direction given under section 35 is revoked by the\n> Controller, then, notwithstanding any provision of this Act\n> specifying the time within which any step should be taken or any act\n> done in connection with an application for the patent, the\n> Controller may, subject to such conditions, if any, as he thinks fit\n> to impose, extend the time for doing anything required or authorised\n> to be done by or under this Act in connection with the application,\n> whether or not that time has previously expired.\n\n**Rule 72 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 72. Communication of result of reconsideration under section\n> 36(2)\n> (1) The result of every reconsideration under sub-section (1) of\n> section 36 shall be communicated to the applicant for patent within\n> fifteen days of the receipt of the notice by the Controller.\n>\n> (2) Extension of time on revocation of secrecy directions under\n> section 38. The extension of time to be given for doing anything\n> required or authorised to be done under section 38 shall not exceed\n> the period for which directions given by the Central Government\n> under sub-section (1) of section 35 were in force.\n>\n>  48\n>\n> CHAPTER VIII GRANT OF PATENTS\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the legal provisions relating to secrecy directions and inventions relevant for defence purposes under the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThis question seeks an analysis of the legal framework that governs secrecy directions for inventions with national security implications under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 35** of the Patents Act, 1970, the Controller may issue secrecy directions to prevent the publication or disclosure of an invention if it is deemed relevant for defence purposes. These directions are subject to review by the Central Government under **Section 36**, which mandates periodic reconsideration at intervals of six months. The Central Government has the power to revoke these directions if it determines that the publication would no longer be prejudicial to national security, as outlined in **Section 38**.\n\nThe legal framework ensures that sensitive technologies are protected from premature disclosure while allowing for necessary oversight by government authorities. Additionally, **Section 42** of the Patents Act outlines the process for disclosing inventions to the Government and emphasizes the importance of maintaining confidentiality where required (as explained on *p. 209* of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n**Application**  \nWhen an application is filed, the Controller assesses whether the invention falls within a class notified by the Central Government as relevant for defence purposes or otherwise appears to be so relevant. If it does, **Section 35(1)** authorizes the issuance of secrecy directions that prohibit or restrict the publication of information related to the invention.\n\nOnce such directions are issued, the Controller is required to notify the Central Government under **Section 35(2)**. The Central Government then evaluates whether publication would be prejudicial to national security and may either revoke the directions or confirm their continuation. If it determines that publication would not be harmful, the Central Government must inform the Controller accordingly.\n\nUnder **Section 36**, the Central Government is required to reconsider the relevance of secrecy directions every six months or upon a reasonable request from the applicant. This ensures that restrictions are only maintained when necessary and allows for timely review and potential revocation if no longer warranted.\n\nIf secrecy directions are revoked, the Controller may extend the time for completing any procedural steps under **Section 38**, ensuring that applicants are not unduly disadvantaged by delays caused by secrecy measures.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe legal provisions relating to secrecy directions under the Patents Act, 1970, serve a dual purpose: protecting national security while balancing the need for timely examination and grant of patents. These provisions ensure that sensitive inventions remain confidential where necessary but also provide mechanisms for periodic review and potential revocation when no longer required.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention the exact sections governing secrecy directions, such as **Section 35**, **Section 36**, and **Section 38**, or overlook the role of the Central Government in their review and revocation. It is essential to clearly outline these provisions and their implications.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the consequences of non-compliance with secrecy directions issued under Section 35 of the Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThis question asks about the legal consequences that may arise if an applicant or any person fails to comply with secrecy directions issued under **Section 35**.\n\n**Rule**  \nNon-compliance with secrecy directions can result in serious legal repercussions. Under **Section 64(1)(n)** of the Patents Act, 1970, a patent application may be deemed abandoned if the applicant contravenes any secrecy direction issued under **Section 35**. Furthermore, if a patent has already been granted and it is found that the applicant violated secrecy directions, the patent may be revoked under **Section 64(2)**.\n\nAdditionally, **Section 118** of the Patents Act imposes penalties for contravention of secrecy provisions, which can include fines or other legal sanctions. The law ensures that non-compliance with secrecy directions is treated as a serious matter, reflecting the importance of protecting sensitive inventions related to national security (as explained on *p. 210* of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n**Application**  \nIf an applicant receives secrecy directions under **Section 35**, they are legally bound not to publish or disclose any information related to the invention until such time as the Central Government lifts these restrictions. If they fail to comply with this obligation, their application may be deemed abandoned by the Controller under **Section 64(1)(n)**.\n\nIn cases where a patent has already been granted and it is discovered that the applicant violated secrecy directions, the Central Government or the Controller may initiate proceedings for revocation of the patent under **Section 64**. This ensures that inventions with potential national security implications are not prematurely disclosed or exploited without proper authorization.\n\nFurthermore, if an individual or entity knowingly contravenes a secrecy direction, they may be subject to penalties as outlined in **Section 118**, which reinforces the legal seriousness of non-compliance.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nNon-compliance with secrecy directions issued under **Section 35** can lead to serious consequences, including the abandonment of an application or revocation of a granted patent. Additionally, individuals who contravene these directions may face penalties under **Section 118**, emphasizing the importance of adhering to legal restrictions on disclosure.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the specific provisions in **Sections 64(1)(n)** and **64(2)** that govern the consequences of non-compliance. It is crucial to highlight these sections explicitly when discussing the legal implications of violating secrecy directions.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the periodical review mechanism for secrecy directions under Section 36 of the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThis question focuses on the process and significance of the periodic review of secrecy directions as outlined in **Section 36**.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 36**, the Central Government is required to reconsider whether an invention remains relevant for defence purposes at intervals of six months or upon a reasonable request from the applicant. This provision ensures that secrecy restrictions are not unduly prolonged and allows for necessary updates based on changing circumstances.\n\nThe Central Government must communicate the outcome of each review to the applicant within the time prescribed under **Section 36(2)**. If it determines that publication would no longer be prejudicial to national security, the Central Government may direct the Controller to revoke the secrecy directions, as outlined in **Section 38** (as explained on *p. 210* of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\nThis mechanism balances the need for national security with the rights of applicants by ensuring that secrecy measures are only maintained when necessary.\n\n**Application**  \nWhen a secrecy direction is issued under **Section 35**, it remains in force until the Central Government reviews its continued relevance. Under **Section 36(1)**, this review must occur at least every six months or upon a request from the applicant that is deemed reasonable by the Controller.\n\nDuring each review, the Central Government evaluates whether the invention still poses a threat to national security. If it determines that publication would no longer be harmful, the government may revoke the secrecy direction and notify the Controller accordingly under **Section 38**.\n\nThis process ensures that applicants are not unfairly burdened with indefinite secrecy restrictions and allows for timely examination and grant of patents when appropriate.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe periodic review mechanism under **Section 36** is essential to ensuring that secrecy directions remain relevant and do not unduly delay the patent application process. It provides a structured framework for reviewing and, if necessary, revoking these restrictions based on changing circumstances.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention **Section 36(2)**, which outlines how the results of each review must be communicated to the applicant. Emphasizing this provision is key to demonstrating a comprehensive understanding of the periodic review process.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Secrecy Directions and Inventions Relevant for Defence\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 35 | **MAIL** | MAIL clings to a secret letter, blocked by a military gate |\n| Sec. 36 | **MATCH** | MATCH lights a candle during a periodic review, flickering with deadlines |\n| Sec. 37 | **MUG** | MUG fills with illegal blueprints, spilling secrets under a fine |\n| Sec. 38 | **MOVIE** | MOVIE screens a revoked patent, applause fading as public interest drowns it |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Directions by the Controller where an invention is relevant for defence purposes - which provision?  (picture: MAIL)\n2. Periodic review - which provision?  (picture: MATCH)\n3. Consequences of contravention - which provision?  (picture: MUG)\n4. Revocation in the public interest under Section 66 - which provision?  (picture: MOVIE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 35 | Secrecy directions on defense-related inventions | Military lockbox with patent papers\n- Section 36 | Periodic review of secrecy directions every six months | Clock ticking over classified files\n- Section 37 | Consequences: no publication, no appeal during secrecy | Muted alarm clock in a vault\n- Section 38 | Revocation and time extension for applications | Key turning in a locked drawer\n- Section 42 | Disclosures to the Government on defense inventions | Government filing cabinet with red tags\n- Section 64 | Abandonment or revocation of patents due to secrecy breach | Broken lock with patent key\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing secrecy directions with general disclosure rules.\n- Use exact section numbers when referring to legal provisions.\n- Always mention the Central Government's role in reviewing and revoking secrecy directions.","storyEN":"**Story: The Secrets of Birla Street**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, on a narrow lane called Birla Street, lived a group of inventors who worked in a small but buzzing workshop. Their names were Ravi, Anjali, and Kiran — each with an idea that could change the world. They had filed patent applications for their work, but none of them knew how deeply entangled they would become with **Section 35** of the Patents Act.\n\nOne day, a man in a grey suit arrived at their workshop unannounced. He was from the Controller’s office and brought news that shook them to their core: **Section 35** had been invoked against one of their inventions — a new type of drone designed for surveillance purposes. The Controller had issued secrecy directions, which meant their invention could not be published or disclosed in any way.\n\nRavi, who had worked on the drone, was stunned. “But why?” he asked. The man simply said that **Section 35** gives the Controller power to prohibit publication if an invention is relevant for defence purposes, and this one clearly fell into that category.\n\nAnjali, ever the pragmatist, reminded them of what they had read in their study materials: “Under **Section 36**, the Central Government must review secrecy directions every six months. If they find it safe to publish, we might be back on track.” She was right — and this gave them hope.\n\nBut with **Section 35** still in place, nothing could move forward. Their application sat in limbo for months. Then came a letter from the Controller: the Central Government had reviewed their case under **Section 36**, but found that the drone’s technology remained sensitive to India's national security. The secrecy direction was renewed.\n\nKiran, who had been working on another invention — a new kind of battery using atomic energy — felt her own application also fall under scrutiny. She recalled from her notes: “**Section 35** covers not just defence inventions but also those related to atomic energy and any other that affects India’s sovereignty or security.”\n\nAs the weeks passed, Ravi and Anjali were forced to wait for **Section 38**, which allows the Controller to revoke secrecy directions if the Central Government deems it safe. But they had no idea when that might happen.\n\nOne day, a new rule was announced: under **Section 37(1)**, their application could not be published while the secrecy direction remained in force. The Controller would not even consider granting them a patent — at least not yet.\n\nKiran’s battery project faced an additional hurdle. She had filed for a foreign patent as well, but **Section 39** made it clear that no foreign applications could be made until the secrecy directions were revoked. Her dream of global recognition seemed further away than ever.\n\nBut then came the day when Anjali received news: the Central Government had reviewed their case again under **Section 36**, and this time, they decided that the drone’s technology was safe to publish. The secrecy direction was revoked under **Section 38** — and with it, the Controller extended the examination timeline as per **Section 38**.\n\nRavi and Anjali were overjoyed. Their application could now proceed, but not without consequences: **Section 64(1)(n)** warned that any non-compliance with secrecy directions would result in their application being deemed abandoned — a fate they had narrowly escaped.\n\nKiran’s battery project was still under scrutiny, and she knew that even if her invention eventually passed through the patent process, **Section 37(3)** meant no renewal fees would be due for the time the secrecy direction remained active. It was a strange but fair rule, as per their notes: “It ensures inventors are not unfairly burdened during periods of national security oversight.”\n\nIn the end, Ravi and Anjali’s drone was granted a patent — but only after **Section 35** had been lifted, and **Section 38** had given them time to complete the process. Their journey through secrecy directions taught them that innovation in India came with unique challenges, but also with protections designed for national security.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| **Section 35** | Secrecy directions relating to inventions relevant for defence purposes. | The Controller may issue secrecy directions if an invention is relevant to defence, atomic energy, or national security. |\n| **Section 36** | Periodic review of secrecy directions by the Central Government every six months. | The Central Government must reconsider whether a secrecy direction should remain in force at least once every six months. |\n| **Section 37(1)** | Prohibits publication and grant of patents during secrecy directions. | Applications under secrecy directions cannot be published or granted until the directions are revoked. |\n| **Section 38** | Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of examination time. | If a secrecy direction is revoked, the Controller may extend the timeline for patent-related actions. |\n| **Section 39** | Prohibits foreign applications until secrecy directions are lifted. | An invention under secrecy directions cannot be filed abroad until the restrictions are removed. |\n| **Section 64(1)(n)** | Application deemed abandoned if non-compliant with secrecy directions. | Any application that violates a secrecy direction may be considered abandoned. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for “Secrecy Directions and Inventions Relevant for Defence”**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Key Elements of Secrecy Directions:**\n- **S.E.C.R.E.T.**  \n  - **S**: Secrecy directions are imposed by the Controller (Section 35).  \n  - **E**: They are related to inventions relevant for defence or atomic energy (Sections 35 and 42).  \n  - **C**: The Central Government reviews these directions every six months (Section 36).  \n  - **R**: Revocation of secrecy directions can occur if the Central Government deems it safe (Section 38).  \n  - **E**: Export of inventions is restricted until secrecy directions are revoked (Section 39).  \n  - **T**: The applicant must comply with these directions, otherwise the patent may be revoked or deemed abandoned (Sections 64 and 120).\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors for Key Sections:**\n- **Section 35** – *Picture a \"Secret\" lock on a military vehicle.*  \n  - This helps recall that secrecy directions apply to inventions relevant for defence.\n\n- **Section 36** – *Imagine the Central Government reviewing a calendar every six months.*  \n  - This ties the number 36 with periodic review of secrecy directions.\n\n- **Section 38** – *Visualize a \"key\" being handed over by the Central Government to revoke secrecy.*  \n  - This helps recall that revocation can occur if deemed safe.\n\n- **Section 39** – *Think of an applicant trying to export a product but being stopped at customs.*  \n  - This reinforces the restriction on foreign applications without permission.\n\n- **Section 64** – *Picture a patent being \"revoked\" like a broken lock.*  \n  - This helps recall that non-compliance with secrecy directions can lead to revocation of patents.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes:**\n- **Secrecy Directions vs. Non-Disclosure Agreements (NDAs):**  \n  - *Secrecy directions are imposed by the Controller and relate to national security; NDAs are private agreements between parties.*  \n\n- **Revocation of Secrecy Directions vs. Revocation of Patents:**  \n  - *Revocation of secrecy directions means the information can be published, but revocation of patents is a legal action that terminates the patent itself.*  \n\n- **Section 39 (Export Restriction) vs. Section 120 (Unauthorised Claim of Patent Rights):**  \n  - *Section 39 restricts filing abroad without permission; Section 120 deals with falsely claiming patent rights, which is a penalty under the Act.*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Secrecy directions are orders by the Controller to restrict publication of inventions deemed relevant for defence purposes. The Central Government assesses if publication would prejudice India's defence.","question":"What are secrecy directions under Section 35?"},{"answer":"Secrecy directions are reviewed every six months by the Central Government, or upon a reasonable applicant request. This ensures ongoing relevance to defence needs.","question":"How often are secrecy directions reviewed?"},{"answer":"If publication is no longer prejudicial, the Central Government revokes secrecy directions, allowing patent grant proceedings to continue. The Controller then notifies the applicant.","question":"What happens if publication is no longer prejudicial?"},{"answer":"A patent cannot be granted while secrecy directions are in force because the specification is not published, and the Controller cannot refuse the application until directions are revoked.","question":"Why can't a patent be granted during secrecy directions?"},{"answer":"If a foreign applicant's invention is published outside India, the Central Government revokes secrecy directions, as per Section 36(1). This aligns with international disclosure practices.","question":"What triggers revocation for foreign applicants?"},{"answer":"Secrecy directions are revoked if the Central Government determines publication no longer prejudices defence, or if the invention is published abroad by a foreign applicant.","question":"Under what condition can secrecy directions be revoked?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 35 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Secrecy directions relating to inventions relevant for defence”.","options":["Section 127","Section 35","Section 144","Section 11B"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Secrecy directions relating to inventions relevant for defence?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 35 is headed “Secrecy directions relating to inventions relevant for defence”.","options":["Examination of application","Publication of official journal","Transmission of orders of courts to Controller","Secrecy directions relating to inventions relevant for defence"],"question":"Section 35 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 37 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Numbering of applications on the grant of patent”.","options":["Rule 11","Rule 74A","Rule 37","Rule 124"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Numbering of applications on the grant of patent?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 37 is headed “Numbering of applications on the grant of patent”.","options":["Time limit for establishing international search report","Scale of costs","Numbering of applications on the grant of patent","Presentation of assignment, etc., of patent to Controller"],"question":"Rule 37 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 35 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)”.","options":["Rule 35","Rule 9","Rule 74A","Rule 139"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 35 is headed “Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)”.","options":["Manner of applying for direction under section 51(1)","Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)","Leaving and serving documents","Address of Communications"],"question":"Rule 35 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 36 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Secrecy directions to be periodically reviewed”.","options":["Section 36","Section 101","Section 93","Section 63"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Secrecy directions to be periodically reviewed?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 36 is headed “Secrecy directions to be periodically reviewed”.","options":["Offences by companies","Appeals to High Court","Secrecy directions to be periodically reviewed","Avoidance of certain restrictive conditions"],"question":"Section 36 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 37 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Consequences of secrecy directions”.","options":["Section 153","Section 45","Section 37","Section 71"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Consequences of secrecy directions?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 37 is headed “Consequences of secrecy directions”.","options":["Rules to be placed before Parliament","Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Consequences of secrecy directions","Relief for infringement of partially valid specification"],"question":"Section 37 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 38 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of time”.","options":["Section 81","Section 137","Section 63","Section 38"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of time?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 38 is headed “Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of time”.","options":["Form of application","Acquisition of inventions and patents by the Central","In cases of “obtaining” Controller may treat the patent as the","Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of time"],"question":"Section 38 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"The Central Government may revoke the directions if it determines that publication would not prejudice India's defence, as per Section 35(2).","options":["a) The Controller","b) The applicant","c) The Central Government","d) The Patent Office"],"question":"Under Section 35 of the Patents Act, 1970, who has the authority to revoke secrecy directions if the Central Government determines that publication would not prejudice India's defence?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 37(1) explicitly states that the Controller shall not pass an order refusing to grant the patent while secrecy directions are in force.","options":["a) The Controller may refuse the application only after consulting the Central Government.","b) The Controller may refuse the application if the applicant agrees to secrecy.","c) The Controller shall not pass an order refusing to grant the same.","d) The Controller must refuse the application to maintain secrecy."],"question":"Under Section 37(1) of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the effect of secrecy directions on the Controller's ability to refuse granting a patent?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 37(2)(a) specifies that sections 100, 101, and 103 apply to the use of the invention by the Government as if the patent had been granted.","options":["a) Sections 40, 41, and 43","b) Sections 100, 101, and 103","c) Sections 50, 51, and 53","d) Sections 20, 21, and 23"],"question":"If a complete specification is found to be in order for grant of a patent under secrecy directions, which sections of the Patents Act, 1970, apply to the use of the invention by the Government?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 38 explicitly states that the Controller may extend the time for doing anything required in connection with the application after revoking secrecy directions.","options":["a) The Central Government","b) The applicant","c) The Controller","d) The Patent Office"],"question":"Under Section 38 of the Patents Act, 1970, who has the authority to extend the time for doing anything required in connection with an application after revoking secrecy directions?"}]},"s1p_25":{"notes":"# Working of Patents and Form 27  \n*(Based on \"Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\")*\n\n---\n\n## Definition of \"Working of Patents\"\n\nThe **working of patents** refers to the process by which a patentee or licensee puts an invention into practical use. This is crucial for ensuring that patented inventions contribute to technological progress and benefit society.\n\n- **Section 83** of *The Patents Act, 1970* outlines the general principles applicable to the working of patented inventions. It states that it is the duty of a patentee to work the invention in India as far as they are able to do so. This ensures that the monopoly granted under a patent is not used merely for commercial gain without contributing to public welfare.\n\n- **Section 84** provides for the grant of **compulsory licences**, which allows third parties to use the patented invention under certain conditions, such as non-working of the invention in India or failure to meet public demand. This provision ensures that patents are not used to monopolize essential goods or services.\n\n---\n\n## Key Concepts Related to Working of Patents\n\n### 1. **Compulsory Licences (Section 84)**\nA compulsory licence is a legal mechanism by which the Controller may grant permission to a third party to use a patented invention without the consent of the patentee, under specific conditions such as:\n- Non-working or insufficient working in India.\n- The existence of a public interest or emergency.\n- The need for export of pharmaceutical products (Section 92A).\n\n### 2. **Revocation of Patents for Non-Working (Section 85)**\nIf the Controller finds that the patentee has not worked the invention, or is not working it in India to the fullest extent possible, they may revoke the patent.\n\n### 3. **Licensing of Related Patents (Section 91)**\nThis section allows for the licensing of related patents when a compulsory licence has been granted under Section 84. It ensures that all relevant rights are addressed and that the licensing is consistent with public interest.\n\n---\n\n## Form 27 – Declaration by Patent Agent\n\n**Form 27** is used to make **declarations** by patent agents on behalf of their clients during various stages of patent prosecution, such as:\n- Filing an application.\n- Submitting amendments or responses to the Controller.\n- Making submissions in opposition proceedings.\n\n### Why Form 27 Exists\nAccording to *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, **Form 27** is a formal requirement that ensures all communications made by a patent agent are properly authorized and on behalf of the applicant or patentee. This helps in maintaining transparency, accountability, and legal validity of the proceedings.\n\n- On page **128**, it is stated: \"The authorization of an agent should be made on Form 26 or in the form of power of attorney... Authorization may also be filed as a power of attorney within three months from the date of filing an application.\"\n\n- However, when a patent agent makes declarations (such as about the novelty or non-obviousness of an invention), **Form 27** is required. This ensures that such statements are made with the knowledge and consent of the applicant.\n\n---\n\n## Importance of Working of Patents\n\nThe working of patents is a cornerstone principle in patent law, ensuring that:\n- The public benefits from technological advancements.\n- Patentees do not abuse their monopoly for commercial gain without contributing to society.\n- Innovation is encouraged through fair competition and access to inventions.\n\nAs explained on **page 115** of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, the working of patents ensures that the grant of a patent is not merely an exclusive right, but also a responsibility towards public welfare.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\n- The **working of patents** is governed by sections such as **83**, **84**, **85**, and **91**, which ensure that inventions are used in India for the benefit of society.\n- **Form 27** is an essential document used by patent agents to make formal declarations on behalf of their clients during various stages of the patent process.\n- These provisions reflect a balance between protecting the rights of inventors and ensuring public interest, as emphasized in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*.\n\n---\n\n## Additional Notes\n\n- **(verify this case with a case-law database)** – There are currently no verified or candidate cases listed for this topic.\n- Ensure that all forms such as Form 26 and Form 27 are submitted in the correct format and within the required timeframes to avoid processing delays.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 83 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 83. General principles applicable to working of patented inventions.\n> Without prejudice to the other provisions contained in this Act, in\n> exercising the powers conferred by this Chapter, regard shall be had\n> to the following general considerations, namely:\n> (a) that patents are granted to encourage inventions and to secure\n> that the inventions are worked in India on a commercial scale and to\n> the fullest extent that is reasonably practicable without undue\n> delay;\n> (b) that they are not granted merely to enable patentees to enjoy a\n> monopoly for the importation of the patented article;\n> (c) that the protection and enforcement of patent rights contribute\n> to the promotion of technological innovation and to the transfer and\n> dissemination of technology, to the mutual advantage of producers\n> and users of technological knowledge and in a manner conducive to\n> social and economic welfare, and to a balance of rights and\n> obligations;\n> (d) that patents granted do not impede protection of public health\n> and nutrition and should act as instrument to promote public\n> interest specially in sectors of vital importance for socio-economic\n> and technological development of India;\n> (e) that patents granted do not in any way prohibit Central\n> Government in taking measures to protect public health;\n> (f) that the patent right is not abused by the patentee or person\n> deriving title or interest on patent from the patentee, and the\n> patentee or a person deriving title or interest on patent from the\n> patentee does not resort to practices which unreasonably restrain\n> trade or adversely affect the international transfer of technology;\n> and\n> (g) that patents are granted to make the benefit of the patented\n> invention available at reasonably affordable prices to the public.\n\n**Section 146 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 146. Power of Controller to call for information from patentees.\n> (1) The Controller may, at any time during the continuance of the\n> patent, by notice in writing, require a patentee or a licensee,\n> exclusive or otherwise, to furnish to him within two months from the\n> date of such notice or within such further time as the Controller\n> may allow, such information or such periodical statements as to the\n> extent to which the patented invention has been commercially worked\n> in India as may be specified in the notice.\n>\n>  107\n>\n> (2) Without prejudice to the provisions of sub-section (1), every\n> patentee and every licensee (whether exclusive or otherwise) shall\n> furnish in such manner and form and at such intervals (not being\n> less than six months) as may be prescribed statements as to the\n> extent to which the patented invention has been worked on a\n> commercial scale in India.\n>\n> (3) The Controller may publish the information received by him under\n> sub-section (1) or sub-section (2) in such manner as may be\n> prescribed.\n\n**Section 122 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 122. Refusal or failure to supply information.\n> (1) If any person refuses or fails to furnish:\n> (a) to the Central Government any information which he is required\n> to furnish under sub-section (5) of section 100;\n> (b) to the Controller any information or statement which he is\n> required to furnish by or under section 146,\n> he shall be liable to penalty which may extend to one lakh rupees,\n> and in case of the continuing refusal or failure, a further penalty\n> of one thousand rupees for every day after the first during which\n> such refusal or failure continues.\n>\n> (2) If any person, being required to furnish any such information as\n> is referred to in sub-section (1), furnishes information or\n> statement which is false, and which he either knows or has reason to\n> believe to be false or does not believe to be true, he shall be\n> liable to penalty for a sum equal to one half per cent. of the total\n> sale or turnover, as the case may be, of business or of the gross\n> receipts in profession as computed in the audited accounts of such\n> person, or a sum equal to five crore rupees, whichever is less.\n\n**Rule 131 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 131. Form and manner in which statements required under section\n> 146(2) to be furnished\n> (1) The statements shall be furnished by every patentee and every\n> licensee under sub-section (2) of section 146 in Form 27 which shall\n> be duly verified by the patentee or the licencee or his authorised\n> agent.\n>\n> (2) The statements referred to in sub-rule (1) shall be furnished\n> once in respect of every period of three financial years starting\n> from the financial year commencing immediately after the financial\n> year in which the patent was granted, and shall be furnished within\n> six months from the expiry of such period. Provided that the\n> Controller may condone the delay or extend the time in filing of\n> such statement for a period up to three months upon a request made\n> in Form 4.\n>\n> (3) The Controller may publish the information received by him under\n> sub-section (1) or sub-section (2) of section 146.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the legal provisions related to the working of patents under The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue** — What are the legal provisions in *The Patents Act, 1970* that govern the working of patented inventions?\n\n**Rule** — **Section 83** of *The Patents Act, 1970* mandates that patentees have a duty to work their invention in India as far as they are able. This provision is guided by several principles, including the encouragement of technological innovation and ensuring that patents do not impede public health or social welfare. **Section 84** allows for the grant of compulsory licences if the invention is not worked in India or if there is a failure to meet public demand. Additionally, **Section 85** provides for the revocation of patents due to non-working, and **Section 91** addresses licensing of related patents when a compulsory licence has been issued.\n\nThe textbook *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra* on page **115** emphasizes that working of patents is not merely an exclusive right but also a responsibility towards public welfare.\n\n**Application** — These provisions ensure that the monopoly granted under a patent does not become a tool for commercial gain at the expense of societal benefit. For example, if a patentee fails to work the invention in India or refuses to license it to third parties for reasonable terms, the Controller may issue a compulsory licence under **Section 84**. This mechanism is crucial in preventing monopolistic practices and ensuring that patented inventions are accessible to the public.\n\nFurther, **Section 146** empowers the Controller to call for information from patentees or licensees regarding the extent of commercial working of the invention in India. If a patentee fails to comply with this requirement, they may be subjected to penalties under **Section 122**, which includes monetary fines.\n\n**Conclusion** — The working of patents is governed by several provisions in *The Patents Act, 1970*, including Sections 83, 84, 85, and 91. These provisions ensure that patented inventions are used for the benefit of society and prevent abuse of patent rights.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook the importance of **Section 83**, which outlines not only the duty to work the invention but also broader principles such as promoting technological innovation and protecting public health. A comprehensive answer must include these key considerations.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the role and significance of Form 27 in the process of patent prosecution.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue** — What is the purpose and significance of **Form 27** in the context of patent prosecution?\n\n**Rule** — **Form 27** is used by patent agents to make formal declarations on behalf of their clients during various stages of patent prosecution. According to *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, **Form 27** ensures that all communications made by a patent agent are properly authorized and represent the interests of the applicant or patentee.\n\nAs stated on page **128**, authorization for an agent may be filed as a power of attorney within three months from the date of filing an application. However, when a patent agent makes declarations—such as about the novelty or non-obviousness of an invention—**Form 27** is required to ensure that such statements are made with the knowledge and consent of the applicant.\n\n**Application** — **Form 27** plays a crucial role in maintaining transparency and legal validity in patent proceedings. It ensures that any declaration made by a patent agent on behalf of the client is authorized, thereby preventing unauthorized or misleading communications. For instance, when an agent submits an amendment or response to the Controller, they must use **Form 27** to confirm that such actions are taken with the applicant's knowledge and approval.\n\nThis requirement helps in maintaining accountability, as it ensures that all formal declarations made during patent prosecution are properly verified and authorized by the client. It also prevents any potential legal complications arising from unauthorized statements or actions on behalf of the applicant.\n\n**Conclusion** — **Form 27** is an essential document used in patent prosecution to ensure that all declarations made by a patent agent are authorized and represent the interests of the applicant. Its use promotes transparency, accountability, and legal validity in the process.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to understand that **Form 27** is specifically required for declarations made by agents, not just for general authorization. It is important to distinguish this from **Form 26**, which is used for power of attorney.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the consequences under The Patents Act, 1970 if a patentee fails to work their invention in India?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 1× (2023)*\n\n**Issue** — What legal consequences does a patentee face if they fail to work their invention in India?\n\n**Rule** — **Section 85** of *The Patents Act, 1970* provides that the Controller may revoke a patent if it is found that the patentee has not worked the invention or is not working it in India to the fullest extent possible. Additionally, **Section 146** empowers the Controller to require a patentee to submit information regarding the commercial working of the invention in India.\n\nIf a patentee fails to comply with these obligations, they may be subjected to penalties under **Section 122**, which includes monetary fines. Furthermore, **Section 84** allows for the issuance of compulsory licences if there is non-working or insufficient working of the invention in India.\n\nAs explained on page **115** of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, failure to work a patent may lead to revocation, as it goes against the public interest and undermines the purpose of patent law.\n\n**Application** — Suppose a patentee has not worked their invention in India for an extended period and refuses to provide information about its commercial use when requested by the Controller. Under **Section 146**, the Controller may require them to submit such information within two months. If they fail to do so, penalties under **Section 122** may be imposed.\n\nIn such a scenario, if it is determined that the invention has not been worked in India or is being worked inadequately, the Controller may revoke the patent under **Section 85**. Alternatively, a third party may apply for a compulsory licence under **Section 84**, which would allow them to use the patented invention without the consent of the patentee.\n\n**Conclusion** — Failure to work an invention in India can lead to the revocation of the patent and imposition of penalties under *The Patents Act, 1970*. This ensures that patents are not used merely for commercial gain but also serve the public interest.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often forget that **Section 85** provides for revocation in addition to **Section 84**, which allows for compulsory licensing. Both mechanisms are important and must be discussed together.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is a compulsory licence, and under what circumstances can it be granted?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2017, 2022)*\n\n**Issue** — Define the term \"compulsory licence\" and outline the conditions under which it may be issued.\n\n**Rule** — A **compulsory licence** is a legal mechanism by which the Controller may grant permission to a third party to use a patented invention without the consent of the patentee. This is provided for in **Section 84** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, and it applies under specific conditions such as non-working or insufficient working of the invention in India, public interest, emergency situations, or the need to export pharmaceutical products (covered under **Section 92A**).\n\nAs noted on page **128** of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, compulsory licences are granted when a patentee fails to work the invention in India or refuses to license it for reasonable terms.\n\n**Application** — Suppose a pharmaceutical company holds a patent for a life-saving drug but is not manufacturing it in India due to high costs, despite public demand. In this case, the Controller may issue a compulsory licence under **Section 84**, allowing another entity to manufacture and distribute the drug at an affordable price within India.\n\nSimilarly, if there is a public health emergency, such as during a pandemic, the Controller may issue a compulsory licence to ensure that essential medicines are available to the population. Additionally, in cases where the invention has not been worked in India or is being worked inadequately, the Controller may grant a compulsory licence to promote the use of the invention.\n\n**Conclusion** — A **compulsory licence** is issued under *The Patents Act, 1970* when there is non-working of an invention in India, public interest, emergency, or the need for export. It ensures that patented inventions are used for societal benefit rather than being monopolized by patentees.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse **Section 84** with other sections such as **Section 92A**, which specifically deals with the compulsory licensing of pharmaceutical products. These must be clearly distinguished in an answer.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Working of Patents and Form 27\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 83 | **FOAM** | FOAM expands, reminding that patents need practical application. |\n| Sec. 146 | **TIE-ROACH** | TIE-ROACH scuttles on patent's schedule, tied to periodic filings |\n| Sec. 122 | **TIE-NUN** | TIE-NUN's order demands precise patent data. |\n| Rule 131 | **TIE-MAT** | TIE-MAT slaps fines for missed patent reports. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **82-94** - working of patents, compulsory licences\n- **118-124** - penalties and offences\n- **125-132** - patent agents\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. General principles applicable to the working of patented inventions - which provision?  (picture: FOAM)\n2. Statement of working on Form 27 and its periodicity - which provision?  (picture: TIE-ROACH)\n3. Information the Controller may require - which provision?  (picture: TIE-NUN)\n4. Penalty for failure to furnish information - which provision?  (picture: TIE-MAT)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 83 | Duty to work invention in India | Patentee's obligation image\n- Section 84 | Compulsory licences for non-working | Key to public access door\n- Section 85 | Revocation for non-working | Broken lock on patent door\n- Section 91 | Licensing of related patents | Puzzle pieces fitting together\n- Section 146 | Controller's power to request info | Inspector with clipboard\n- Rule 131 | Form 27 for statements | Paperwork in red folder\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing compulsory licences with voluntary ones  \n- Use exact section numbers when discussing patent working  \n- Always link Form 27 to declarations made by agents","storyEN":"**Story: The Case of the Missing Lamp and the Patent Agent**\n\nIn a quiet corner of Hyderabad, there lived a young man named Arjun. He was an aspiring patent agent who had recently passed his exams with flying colors. One day, he received a call from Mr. Ravi, a local inventor who had just been granted a patent for a revolutionary water purification device.\n\nMr. Ravi had a problem: the device wasn’t being used in India as much as it should be. He explained that he was under pressure from the Controller of Patents to \"work\" his invention, which meant putting it into practical use on a commercial scale. Arjun knew this came from **Section 83** of *The Patents Act, 1970*. That section said patentees had a duty to work their inventions in India as far as possible. But Mr. Ravi was struggling — he had only managed to sell twenty-one units.\n\nArjun decided to help him out. He reminded Mr. Ravi that **Section 84** allowed for compulsory licenses if the invention wasn’t being worked properly or if there was a public interest involved. This meant someone else could use the invention without Mr. Ravi’s consent, but only under certain conditions. Arjun knew this might be necessary if they couldn't get the device to market soon.\n\nMeanwhile, the Controller had sent out notices asking for information on how much of the invention was being used in India. According to **Section 146**, every patentee and licensee had to submit statements about their commercial use of the invention. These statements were required at least once every six months — but Mr. Ravi hadn’t submitted anything yet.\n\nArjun, as a patent agent, needed to make a formal declaration on behalf of Mr. Ravi during this process. He knew that **Form 27** was essential for such declarations. The rules said the statements had to be submitted in **Form 27**, and they had to be verified by the patentee or their authorized agent.\n\nBut there was another problem: a local company, AquaTech, claimed they were being blocked from using Mr. Ravi’s invention because of high prices. They argued that the device should be available at reasonably affordable prices for public welfare — a point emphasized in **Section 83 (g)**.\n\nArjun realized that if Mr. Ravi didn’t start working his patent properly soon, the Controller might revoke it under **Section 85**. That would mean losing all rights to the invention permanently.\n\nHe decided to file an application for a compulsory license under **Section 84**, arguing that AquaTech’s need was urgent and in the public interest. But he also knew that if they did this, they’d have to deal with licensing of related patents as well — which was covered by **Section 91**.\n\nTo ensure everything was properly documented, Arjun filed **Form 27** on behalf of Mr. Ravi, verifying all statements about the commercial use of the invention. This would help avoid penalties under **Section 122**, where failure to submit information could lead to fines as high as one lakh rupees.\n\nIn the end, the Controller saw that Mr. Ravi had been trying his best but was facing challenges in scaling up production. He gave them more time and encouraged collaboration with AquaTech to ensure the device reached people who needed it most — a decision aligned with **Section 83 (d)** and **(e)**.\n\nArjun felt proud as he watched Mr. Ravi’s invention finally start to light up the lives of many, just like the twenty-one lamps that had once symbolized his struggle.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one units | Section 83 | A patentee has a duty to work the invention in India as far as possible. |\n| Compulsory licenses | Section 84 | Allows third parties to use patented inventions if they are not being worked properly or for public interest. |\n| Revocation of patents | Section 85 | The Controller may revoke a patent if it is not being worked adequately in India. |\n| Licensing of related patents | Section 91 | Ensures consistency with public interest when compulsory licenses are granted. |\n| Form 27 | Rule 131 (Form 27) | Required for declarations made by patent agents on behalf of their clients during patent proceedings. |\n| Penalty for non-submission | Section 122 | Failure to submit required information may result in penalties up to one lakh rupees. |\n| Affordable prices | Section 83 (g) | Patents should make the benefit available at reasonably affordable prices to the public. |\n| Public health and nutrition | Section 83 (d) | Patents must not impede protection of public health and nutrition, especially in vital sectors. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for \"Working of Patents and Form 27\"\n\n---\n\n**1. Acronym/Mnemonic for Working of Patents:**\n\n- **W.O.P. (Working of Patents)**  \n  Remember as: **\"W**orking must be **O**n-going, not just a **P**eriodic event.\"  \n  This helps recall that the working of patents requires continuous use and is subject to compulsory licensing if not properly worked.\n\n---\n\n**2. Number-anchor for Section 83 (General principles applicable to working of patented inventions):**\n\n- **Section 83 → \"8\" as a key on a keyboard, which looks like a stop sign.**  \n  This image reminds you that this section sets the **rules and guidelines** (like a stop sign) governing how a patent must be worked in India.\n\n---\n\n**3. Number-anchor for Form 27:**\n\n- **Form 27 → Think of \"27\" as \"TWO SEVEN\" or \"TO SEVEN.\"**  \n  Visualize someone moving **towards the patent office**, submitting documents to proceed with their application — this helps you recall that **Form 27 is used for authorizing agents and managing communications.**\n\n---\n\n**4. Confusion Pair: Section 83 vs. Section 84**\n\n- **Section 83 (General principles)** vs. **Section 84 (Compulsory licences):**  \n  *Difference:* **Section 83 sets the rules**, while **Section 84 is about granting compulsory licences when a patent isn’t worked properly.**  \n  Use this: *\"83 is the rulebook, 84 is the tool to enforce it.\"*\n\n---\n\n**5. Confusion Pair: Form 26 vs. Form 27**\n\n- **Form 26 (Authorization of agent)** vs. **Form 27 (Power of attorney and communication management):**  \n  *Difference:* **Form 26 authorizes an agent**, while **Form 27 allows the agent to act on behalf of the applicant in all communications with the Controller.**  \n  Use this: *\"26 gives permission, 27 enables action.\"*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Patentees must furnish statements on the extent of working the invention in India. Section 146(2) mandates this to ensure commercial exploitation in India.","question":"What is the requirement for patentees under Section 146(2) regarding the working of a patented invention?"},{"answer":"Form 27 must be submitted once every three financial years, starting after the patent is granted. Rule 131(2) specifies this interval and a six-month deadline for submission.","question":"What is the periodicity for submitting Form 27 statements?"},{"answer":"A penalty of imprisonment up to six months or a fine (up to 0.5% of sales turnover) applies. Section 122 outlines penalties for false statements.","question":"What penalty applies for providing false information under Section 146?"},{"answer":"The Controller may publish submitted information to ensure transparency. Rule 131(3) permits this publication under Section 146(2).","question":"What is the role of the Controller under Section 146(2) regarding submitted information?"},{"answer":"Statements must be submitted within six months after each three-year period. Extensions up to three months are allowed via Form 4 (Rule 131(2)).","question":"What is the time limit for submitting Form 27 statements?"},{"answer":"Form 27 ensures compliance with statutory reporting obligations. Failure risks penalties under Section 122 and undermines patent validity.","question":"Why is Form 27 important for patentees?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 122 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Correction of clerical errors”.","options":["Rule 78","Rule 122","Rule 74","Rule 84"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Correction of clerical errors?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 122 is headed “Correction of clerical errors”.","options":["Manner of application for inclusion in the roll of","Opposition to the patent","Correction of clerical errors","Filing of written statement of opposition and evidence"],"question":"Rule 122 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 131 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Form and manner in which statements required under section”.","options":["Rule 19I","Rule 27","Rule 131","Rule 124"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Form and manner in which statements required under section?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 131 is headed “Form and manner in which statements required under section”.","options":["Permission for making patent application outside India","Form and manner in which statements required under section","Registration of title and interest in patents","Certificate of inventorship"],"question":"Rule 131 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 83 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Publication of the amendment allowed”.","options":["Rule 81","Rule 13","Rule 131","Rule 83"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Publication of the amendment allowed?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 83 is headed “Publication of the amendment allowed”.","options":["Removal of names from the roll of scientific advisers","Procedure in case of potential infringement","Publication of the amendment allowed","Manner of advertisement of the proposed correction of any"],"question":"Rule 83 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 131 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents”.","options":["Section 8","Section 28","Section 131","Section 57"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 131 is headed “Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents”.","options":["Certificate of validity of specification and costs of","Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents","Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable","Practice by non-registered patent agents"],"question":"Section 131 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 83 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “General principles applicable to working of patented inventions”.","options":["Section 83","Section 137","Section 160","Section 2"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: General principles applicable to working of patented inventions?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 83 is headed “General principles applicable to working of patented inventions”.","options":["Defences, etc., in suit for infringement","General principles applicable to working of patented inventions","Patent rights not infringed when used on foreign vessels, etc.,","Procedure for dealing with applications under sections 84 and"],"question":"Section 83 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 146 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of Controller to call for information from patentees”.","options":["Section 7","Section 146","Section 144","Section 11B"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of Controller to call for information from patentees?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 146 is headed “Power of Controller to call for information from patentees”.","options":["Controller and other officers","Power of Controller to call for information from patentees","Protection of security of India","Information relating to Patents"],"question":"Section 146 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Source: Section 146(1) states: 'The Controller may, at any time during the continuance of the patent, by notice in writing, require a patentee... to furnish... information... as may be specified in the notice.'","options":["a) Section 83","b) Section 122","c) Section 146","d) Rule 131"],"question":"Under which section of the Patents Act, 1970 is the Controller empowered to call for information from patentees regarding the commercial working of patented inventions?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Source: Section 122(2) states: '...a penalty for a sum equal to one half per cent. of the total sale or turnover... or a sum equal to five crore rupees, whichever is less.'","options":["a) One lakh rupees","b) One thousand rupees per day","c) Half per cent of total sale or turnover, or five crore rupees, whichever is less","d) Two lakh rupees"],"question":"What is the penalty for a person who furnishes false information to the Controller under section 146?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Source: Rule 131(1) states: 'The statements shall be furnished... in Form 27 which shall be duly verified...'.","options":["a) Form 4","b) Form 10","c) Form 27","d) Form 146"],"question":"Which form must be used to submit statements required under section 146(2) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Source: Rule 131(2) states: '...furnished within six months from the expiry of such period.'","options":["a) Within two months","b) Within six months","c) Within three months","d) Within twelve months"],"question":"What is the time limit for furnishing statements required under section 146(2) after the expiry of the period?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Source: Section 83(c) states: '...contribute to the promotion of technological innovation... and to a balance of rights and obligations.'","options":["a) Patents are granted to ensure monopolies for importation","b) Patents should impede public health protection","c) Patents should promote technological innovation and balance rights and obligations","d) Patents are granted to restrict the transfer of technology"],"question":"Which of the following is a general principle under section 83 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Source: Rule 131(2) states: '...once in respect of every period of three financial years...'.","options":["a) Every financial year","b) Every three financial years","c) Every six months","d) Every two years"],"question":"What is the frequency of submitting statements required under section 146(2) of the Patents Act, 1970?"}]},"s1p_26":{"notes":"# Compulsory Licences — Grounds, Procedure and Terms  \n\n## Definition  \nA **compulsory licence** is a legal mechanism under the *Patents Act, 1970* that allows the Controller to grant a licence for the use of a patented invention without the consent of the patentee. This is done in the public interest when certain conditions are met (Section 84).  \n\n## Key Concepts  \n### Grounds for Granting Compulsory Licences  \nUnder **Section 84** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, a compulsory licence may be granted on the following grounds:  \n\n- If the reasonable requirements of the public have not been satisfied with respect to the patented invention.  \n- If the patented invention is not available to the public at a reasonably affordable price.  \n- If the patented invention is not being worked in India (i.e., it is not being commercially used or manufactured).  \n- If conditions imposed by the patentee on the grant of licences are prejudicing the manufacture, use, or sale of materials not protected by the patent.  \n\nThe Controller must be satisfied that a **prima facie case** has been made out for granting such a licence (Section 87(1)).  \n\n### Procedure for Granting Compulsory Licences  \n1. The application for a compulsory licence is made in **Form 17**, as per **Rule 96**, along with the prescribed fee.  \n2. The applicant must set out their interest and the facts upon which they base their application (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 265).  \n3. If the Controller is satisfied that a **prima facie case** has been made out, he will:  \n   - Direct the applicant to serve copies of the application upon the patentee and any other interested parties (Section 87(1)).  \n   - Publish the application in the official journal (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 274).  \n\n4. The patentee or any other person opposing the application may file a notice of opposition within the prescribed time (Section 87(2)).  \n5. The Controller shall notify both parties and give them an opportunity to be heard before deciding the case (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 274).  \n\n6. If no opposition is filed within the prescribed time or if the opposition is rejected, the Controller may proceed with granting a compulsory licence.  \n7. The Controller must ensure that the **terms and conditions** of the licence are fair and balanced (Section 90).  \n\n### Terms and Conditions of Compulsory Licences  \nUnder **Section 90**, the following terms and conditions apply to a compulsory licence:  \n\n- **Royalty and remuneration** reserved for the patentee must be reasonable, taking into account the nature of the invention, the expenditure incurred by the patentee, and other relevant factors.  \n- The patented invention must be worked to the fullest extent possible by the licensee (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 275).  \n- The patented articles should be made available to the public at **reasonably affordable prices**.  \n- The licence is generally **non-exclusive** and **non-transferable**.  \n- The licence may be granted for the **balance term of the patent**, unless a shorter period is consistent with public interest.  \n- In cases involving **semi-conductor technology**, the licence must allow for **public non-commercial use** (Section 90(ix)).  \n\n### General Purposes of Granting Compulsory Licences  \nAs per **Section 89**, the Controller must ensure that:  \n- Patented inventions are worked on a commercial scale in India without undue delay.  \n- The interests of persons working or developing an invention in India under patent protection are not unfairly prejudiced (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 273).  \n\n### Special Provisions for Generic Companies  \nUnder **Section 84A**, a compulsory licence may be granted to generic companies without the authorization of the patentee if a **prima facie case** has been made out (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 293).  \n\n- The generic company must pay **adequate remuneration** to the patentee based on the economic value of the authorization.  \n- Despite not requiring the consent of the patentee, the Controller is still required to provide the patentee with an opportunity to be heard (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 293).  \n\n### Licensing of Related Patents  \nUnder **Section 91**, a compulsory licence may also be granted for related patents if:  \n- The applicant is able to grant licences in respect of the other invention on reasonable terms.  \n- The other invention has made a substantial contribution to commercial or industrial activities in India (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 275).  \n\n## Conclusion  \nCompulsory licensing is a tool used by the Controller to ensure that patented inventions are accessible and affordable to the public. It balances the rights of the patentee with the interests of the general public. The procedure involves a detailed application process, opportunity for opposition, and consideration of fair terms and conditions (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 265).  \n\n## Additional Notes  \n- **Section 84(6)(i)** requires the Controller to consider whether the patentee has taken measures to make full use of the invention (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 265).  \n- The Controller may also grant a compulsory licence in cases where the patented invention is being used for **anti-competitive practices** (Section 90(ix)).  \n\n**(verify this case with a case-law database)** — No verified or candidate cases are available for this topic at present.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 84 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 84. Compulsory licences.\n> (1) At any time after the expiration of three years from the date of\n> the grant of patent, any person interested may make an application\n>\n>  68\n>\n> to the Controller for grant of compulsory licence on patent on any\n> of the following grounds, namely:\n> (a) that the reasonable requirements of the public with respect to\n> the patented invention have not been satisfied, or\n> (b) that the patented invention is not available to the public at a\n> reasonably affordable price, or (c) that the patented invention is\n> not worked in the territory of India.\n>\n> (2) An application under this section may be made by any person\n> notwithstanding that he is already the holder of a licence under the\n> patent and no person shall be estopped from alleging that the\n> reasonable requirements of the public with respect to the patented\n> invention are not satisfied or that the patented invention is not\n> worked in the territory of India or that the patented invention is\n> not available to the public at a reasonably affordable price by\n> reason of any admission made by him, whether in such a licence or\n> otherwise or by reason of his having accepted such a licence.\n>\n> (3) Every application under sub-section (1) shall contain a\n> statement setting out the nature of the applicant’s interest\n> together with such particulars as may be prescribed and the facts\n> upon which the application is based.\n>\n> (4) The Controller, if satisfied that the reasonable requirements of\n> the public with respect to the patented invention have not been\n> satisfied or that the patented invention is not worked in the\n> territory of India or that the patented invention is not available\n> to the public at a reasonably affordable price, may grant a licence\n> upon such terms as he may deem fit.\n>\n> (5) Where the Controller directs the patentee to grant a licence he\n> may, as incidental thereto, exercise the powers set out in section\n\n**Section 85 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 85. Revocation of patents by the Controller for non-working.\n> (1) Where, in respect of a patent, a compulsory licence has been\n> granted, the Central Government or any person interested may, after\n> the expiration of two years from the date of the order granting the\n> first compulsory licence, apply to the Controller for an order\n> revoking the patent on the ground that the patented invention has\n> not been worked in the territory of India or that reasonable\n> requirements of the public with respect to the patented invention\n> has not been satisfied or that the patented invention is not\n> available to the public at a reasonably affordable price.\n>\n> (2) Every application under sub-section (1) shall contain such\n> particulars as may be prescribed, the facts upon which the\n> application is based, and, in the case of an application other than\n> by the Central Government, shall also set out the nature of the\n> applicant’s interest.\n>\n> (3) The Controller, if satisfied that the reasonable requirements of\n> the public with respect to the patented invention have not been\n> satisfied or that patented invention have not been worked in the\n> territory of India or that the patented invention is not available\n> to the public at a reasonably affordable price, may make an order\n> revoking the patent.\n>\n> (4) Every application under sub-section (1) shall ordinarily be\n> decided within one year of its being presented to the Controller.\n\n**Section 86 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 86. Power of Controller to adjourn applications for compulsory\n> licences, etc., in certain cases.\n> (1) Where an application under section 84 or section 85, as the case\n>\n>  71\n>\n> may be, is made on the grounds that the patented invention has not\n> been worked in the territory of India or on the ground mentioned in\n> clause (d) of sub-section (7) of section 84 and the Controller is\n> satisfied that the time which has elapsed since the sealing of the\n> patent has for any reason been insufficient to enable the invention\n> to be worked on a commercial scale to an adequate extent or to\n> enable the invention to be so worked to the fullest extent that is\n> reasonably practicable, he may, by order, adjourn the further\n> hearing of the application for such period not exceeding twelve\n> months in the aggregate as appears to him to be sufficient for the\n> invention to be so worked:\n> Provided that in any case where the patentee establishes that the\n> reason why a patented invention could not be worked as aforesaid\n> before the date of the application was due to any State or Central\n> Act or any rule or regulation made thereunder or any order of the\n> Government imposed otherwise than by way of a condition for the\n> working of the invention in the territory of India or for the\n> disposal of the patented articles or of the articles made by the\n> process or by the use of the patented plant, machinery, or\n> apparatus, then, the period of adjournment ordered under this sub-\n> section shall be reckoned from the date on which the period during\n> which the working of the invention was prevented by such Act, rule\n> or regulation or order of Government as computed from the date of\n> the application, expires.\n>\n> (2) No adjournment under sub-section (1) shall be ordered unless the\n> Controller is satisfied that the patentee has taken with promptitude\n> adequate or reasonable steps to start the working of the invention\n> in the territory of India on a commercial scale and to an adequate\n> extent.\n\n**Section 87 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 87. Procedure for dealing with applications under sections 84 and\n\n**Section 88 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 88.\n>\n> (6) In considering the application filed under this section, the\n> Controller shall take into account,\n> (i) the nature of the invention, the time which has elapsed since\n> the sealing of the patent and the measures already taken by the\n> patentee or any licensee to make full use of the invention;\n> (ii) the ability of the applicant to work the invention to the\n> public advantage;\n>\n>  69\n>\n> (iii) the capacity of the applicant to undertake the risk in\n> providing capital and working the invention, if the application were\n> granted;\n> (iv) as to whether the applicant has made efforts to obtain a\n> licence from the patentee on reasonable terms and conditions and\n> such efforts have not been successful within a reasonable period as\n> the Controller may deem fit:\n> Provided that this clause shall not be applicable in case of\n> national emergency or other circumstances of extreme urgency or in\n> case of public non-commercial use or on establishment of a ground of\n> anti-competitive practices adopted by the patentee,\n> but shall not be required to take into account matters subsequent to\n> the making of the application.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of clause (iv), “reasonable period”\n> shall be construed as a period not ordinarily exceeding a period of\n> six months.\n>\n> (7) For the purposes of this Chapter, the reasonable requirements of\n> the public shall be deemed not to have been satisfied\n> (a) if, by reason of the refusal of the patentee to grant a licence\n> or licences on reasonable terms,\n> (i) an existing trade or industry or the development thereof or the\n> establishment of any new trade or industry in India or the trade or\n> industry of any person or class of persons trading or manufacturing\n> in India is prejudiced; or\n> (ii) the demand for the patented article has not been met to an\n> adequate extent or on reasonable terms; or\n> (iii) a market for export of the patented article manufactured in\n> India is not being supplied or developed; or\n> (iv) the establishment or development of commercial activities in\n> India is prejudiced; or\n> (b) if, by reason of conditions imposed by the patentee upon the\n> grant of licences under the patent or upon the purchase, hire or use\n> of the patented article or process, the manufacture, use or sale of\n> materials not protected by the patent, or the establishment or\n> development of any trade or industry in India, is prejudiced; or\n> (c) if the patentee imposes a condition upon the grant of licences\n> under the patent to provide exclusive grant back, prevention to\n> challenges to the validity of patent or coercive package licensing,\n> or\n>\n>  70\n>\n> (d) if the patented invention is not being worked in the territory\n> of India on a commercial scale to an adequate extent or is not being\n> so worked to the fullest extent that is reasonably practicable, or\n> (e) if the working of the patented invention in the territory of\n> India on a commercial scale is being prevented or hindered by the\n> importation from abroad of the patented article by\n> (i) the patentee or persons claiming under him; or\n> (ii) persons directly or indirectly purchasing from him; or\n> (iii) other persons against whom the patentee is not taking or has\n> not taken proceedings for infringement.\n\n**Section 89 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 89. General purposes for granting compulsory licences.\n> The powers of the Controller upon an application made under section\n> 84 shall be exercised with a view to securing the following general\n> purposes, that is to say,\n> (a) that patented inventions are worked on a commercial scale in the\n> territory of India without undue delay and to the fullest extent\n> that is reasonably practicable;\n> (b) that the interests of any person for the time being working or\n> developing an invention in the territory of India under the\n> protection of a patent are not unfairly prejudiced.\n\n**Section 90 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 90. Terms and conditions of compulsory licences.\n> (1) In settling the terms and conditions of a licence under section\n> 84, the Controller shall endeavour to secure\n> (i) that the royalty and other remuneration, if any, reserved to the\n> patentee or other person beneficially entitled to the patent, is\n> reasonable, having regard to the nature of the invention, the\n> expenditure incurred by the patentee in making the invention or in\n> developing it and obtaining a patent and keeping it in force and\n> other relevant factors;\n> (ii) that the patented invention is worked to the fullest extent by\n> the person to whom the licence is granted and with reasonable profit\n> to him;\n> (iii) that the patented articles are made available to the public at\n> reasonably affordable prices;\n> (iv) that the licence granted is a non-exclusive licence;\n> (v) that the right of the licensee is non-assignable;\n>\n>  74\n>\n> (vi) that the licence is for the balance term of the patent unless a\n> shorter term is consistent with public interest;\n> (vii) that the licence is granted with a predominant purpose of\n> supply in the Indian market and that the licensee may also export\n> the patented product, if need be in accordance with the provisions\n> of sub-clause (iii) of clause (a) of sub-section (7) of section 84;\n> (viii) that in the case of semi-conductor technology, the licence\n> granted is to work the invention for public non-commercial use;\n> (ix) that in case the licence is granted to remedy a practice\n> determined after judicial or administrative process to be anti-\n> competitive, the licensee shall be permitted to export the patented\n> product, if need be.\n>\n> (2) No licence granted by the Controller shall authorise the\n> licensee to import the patented article or an article or substance\n> made by a patented process from abroad where such importation would,\n> but for such authorisation, constitute an infringement of the rights\n> of the patentee.\n>\n> (3) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (2), the\n> Central Government may, if in its opinion it is necessary so to do,\n> in the public interest, direct the Controller at any time to\n> authorise any licensee in respect of a patent to import the patented\n> article or an article or substance made by a patented process from\n> abroad (subject to such conditions as it considers necessary to\n> impose relating among other matters to the royalty and other\n> remuneration, if any, payable to the patentee, the quantum of\n> import, the sale price of the imported article and the period of\n> importation), and thereupon the Controller shall give effect to the\n> directions.\n\n**Rule 96 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 96. Application for compulsory licence etc.\n> An application to the Controller for an order under section 84,\n> section 85, section 91 or section 92 or section 92A shall be in Form\n> 17, or Form 19, as the case may be. Except in the case of an\n> application made by the Central Government, the application shall\n> set out the nature of the applicant’s interest and terms and\n> conditions of the licence the applicant is willing to accept.\n\n**Rule 100 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 100. Application under section 88(4)\n> (1) An application under sub-section (4) of section 88 for the\n> revision of the terms and conditions of a licence which have been\n> settled by the Controller shall be in Form 20 and shall state the\n> facts relied upon by the applicant and the relief he seeks and shall\n> be accompanied by evidence in support of the application.\n>\n> (2) If the Controller is satisfied that a prima facie case has not\n> been made out for the revision of the terms and conditions of the\n> licence, he may notify the applicant accordingly and unless within a\n> month the applicant requests to be heard in the matter, the\n> Controller may refuse the application.\n>\n> (3) The Controller, after giving the applicant an opportunity of\n> being heard, shall determine whether the application shall be\n> proceeded with or whether the application shall be refused.\n\n**Rule 101 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 101. Procedure to be followed in case of applications under\n> section 88(4)\n> (1) If the Controller allows the application to be proceeded with,\n> he shall direct the applicant to serve copies of the application and\n> of the evidence in support thereof upon the patentee or any other\n> person appearing in the register to be interested in the patent or\n> upon any other person on whom, in his opinion such copies should be\n> so served.\n>\n> (2) The applicant shall inform the Controller the date on which the\n> service of copies of application and of the evidence on the patentee\n> and other persons referred to in sub-rule (1) has been effected.\n>\n>  59\n>\n> (3) The patentee or any other person on whom copies of the\n> application and of the evidence have been served, may give to the\n> Controller notice of opposition in Form 14 within one month from the\n> date of such service. Such notice shall contain the grounds relied\n> upon by the opponent and shall be accompanied by evidence in support\n> of the opposition.\n>\n> (4) The opponent shall serve copies of the notice of opposition and\n> his evidence on the applicant and inform the Controller the date on\n> which such service has been effected.\n>\n> (5) No further evidence or statement shall be filed by either party\n> except with special leave of or on requisition by the Controller.\n>\n> (6) On completion of the above proceedings, the Controller shall\n> forthwith fix a date and the time for the hearing of the case and\n> shall give the parties not less than ten days’ notice of such\n> hearing.\n>\n> (7) The procedure specified in sub-rules (2) to (5) of rule 62\n> shall, as far as may be, apply to the procedure for hearing under\n> this rule as they apply to the hearing in opposition proceedings.\n>\n> (8) If the Controller decides to revise the terms and conditions of\n> licence he shall forthwith amend the licence granted to the\n> applicant in such manner, as he may deem necessary.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the grounds for granting a compulsory licence under the *Patents Act, 1970*? Discuss with reference to relevant provisions and case law.**\n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue**: The legal grounds for granting a compulsory licence under the *Patents Act, 1970*, with reference to Section 84 and relevant case law.\n\n**Rule**: Under **Section 84(1)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, a compulsory licence may be granted if:  \n- The reasonable requirements of the public with respect to the patented invention have not been satisfied (Section 84(1)(a)),  \n- The patented invention is not available to the public at a reasonably affordable price (Section 84(1)(b)), or  \n- The patented invention is not worked in India (Section 84(1)(c)).  \n\nThe Controller must be satisfied that a **prima facie case** has been made out for granting such a licence under **Section 87(1)**. Additionally, **Section 89** mandates that the general purposes of granting compulsory licences include ensuring that inventions are worked on a commercial scale in India and protecting the interests of persons developing or working on inventions in India.\n\nIn **Bayer Corporation v. Union of India**, 2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963, it was held that all three grounds under Section 84(1) were satisfied for granting a compulsory licence for sorafenib, demonstrating the practical application of these legal provisions.\n\n**Application**: If a pharmaceutical company seeks a compulsory licence for a patented drug due to unaffordable pricing and non-availability in India, the Controller must assess whether:  \n1. The public's reasonable requirements have not been met (e.g., lack of access to life-saving drugs).  \n2. The invention is not available at an affordable price (e.g., high cost of medication).  \n3. The patent has not been worked in India (e.g., no local manufacturing or importation for domestic use).\n\nThe Controller must also ensure that the application satisfies a **prima facie case** under Section 87(1), which includes serving the application on the patentee and publishing it in the official journal. If the patentee opposes, they may file an opposition within the prescribed time limit.\n\n**Conclusion**: A compulsory licence can be granted under the *Patents Act, 1970* if the public's reasonable requirements are not met, the invention is unaffordable or not worked in India. The Controller must ensure a **prima facie case** and balance the interests of the patentee with public interest.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to discuss the requirement of a **prima facie case** under Section 87(1) and overlook the importance of public affordability as a ground for compulsory licensing, especially in pharmaceutical contexts like *Bayer Corporation v. Union of India*.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the procedure for granting a compulsory licence under the *Patents Act, 1970*.**\n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2021, 2023)*\n\n**Issue**: The procedural steps required to be followed by the Controller in granting a compulsory licence.\n\n**Rule**: Under **Section 84(3)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, an application for a compulsory licence must include:  \n- A statement of the applicant’s interest and facts upon which the application is based.  \n\nThe Controller may grant a licence under **Section 84(4)** if satisfied that public requirements have not been met or that the invention is unworked in India. The procedure includes:  \n1. Serving copies of the application on the patentee and other interested parties (Section 87(1)),  \n2. Publishing the application in the official journal (as per textbook, p. 274),  \n3. Allowing the patentee or any opposing party to file a notice of opposition within the prescribed time (Section 87(2)),  \n4. Giving both parties an opportunity to be heard before deciding on the case (textbook, p. 274).  \n\n**Application**: If a generic drug manufacturer applies for a compulsory licence for a patented medicine due to unaffordable pricing and non-availability in India:  \n1. The application must be made in **Form 17**, as per **Rule 96**, along with the prescribed fee (textbook, p. 265).  \n2. The Controller will assess whether a **prima facie case** is made out under Section 87(1). If satisfied, the application must be served on the patentee and published in the official journal.  \n3. If the patentee opposes the licence, they may file an opposition within the time limit specified by the Controller (Section 87(2)).  \n4. The Controller will then hear both parties before deciding whether to grant the licence.\n\n**Conclusion**: Granting a compulsory licence involves a formal application process, serving notice on the patentee, publication of the application, and allowing for opposition and hearing. These procedural safeguards ensure that the public interest is balanced with the rights of the patentee.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often neglect to mention the **Form 17**, **Rule 96**, or the requirement to publish the application in the official journal, which are essential procedural steps under the *Patents Act*.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the terms and conditions that must be adhered to when a compulsory licence is granted?**\n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2023)*\n\n**Issue**: The legal obligations and conditions imposed on a licensee upon granting a compulsory licence under the *Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n**Rule**: Under **Section 90(1)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, the Controller must ensure that:  \n- Royalty and remuneration to the patentee are reasonable (taking into account the nature of the invention and expenditure incurred),  \n- The patented invention is worked to the fullest extent by the licensee,  \n- Patented articles are made available at **reasonably affordable prices**,  \n- The licence is generally **non-exclusive** and **non-transferable**,  \n- The licence may be granted for the **balance term of the patent**, unless a shorter period serves public interest.  \n\nIn the case of **semi-conductor technology**, the licence must allow for **public non-commercial use** under **Section 90(ix)**.\n\n**Application**: If a compulsory licence is granted to a generic drug manufacturer, they must:  \n1. Pay **adequate remuneration** to the patentee based on the economic value of the authorisation (textbook, p. 293).  \n2. Ensure that the patented invention is worked to the fullest extent possible in India, with reasonable profit for the licensee.  \n3. Supply the patented product at **reasonably affordable prices** to the public.  \n4. The licence must be **non-exclusive**, and the right of the licensee cannot be assigned or transferred (Section 90(iv)–(v)).  \n\nIn cases involving **semi-conductor technology**, the licence must also allow for **public non-commercial use** under Section 90(ix).\n\n**Conclusion**: A compulsory licence is subject to conditions such as fair royalty, full working of the invention, reasonable pricing, and non-exclusivity. These terms ensure that public interest is protected while still compensating the patentee.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students overlook the **non-exclusive** nature of the licence and fail to mention the requirement for **reasonably affordable prices**, which are critical under Section 90(1)(iii).\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the special provisions for granting a compulsory licence to generic companies?**\n*8 marks · Osmania University · not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The legal framework and conditions that apply when a compulsory licence is granted to a generic company.\n\n**Rule**: Under **Section 84A** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, a compulsory licence may be granted to a generic company without the patentee's consent if a **prima facie case** has been made out. However, the Controller must still provide the patentee with an opportunity to be heard (textbook, p. 293).  \n\nThe generic company is required to pay **adequate remuneration** based on the economic value of the authorisation (textbook, p. 293), and the licence must ensure that the patented invention is worked in India.\n\n**Application**: If a generic drug manufacturer seeks a compulsory licence for a patented medicine without the patentee's consent:  \n1. The application must establish a **prima facie case** under Section 84A, such as unaffordable pricing or non-availability of the drug in India.  \n2. The Controller will still allow the patentee to be heard before granting the licence.  \n3. The generic company is required to pay **adequate remuneration**, which should reflect the economic value of the authorisation and not be excessive.\n\n**Conclusion**: Generic companies can apply for a compulsory licence without the patentee’s consent if a **prima facie case** is made out, but they must still provide adequate compensation and ensure that the invention is worked in India.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often confuse the provisions of **Section 84A** with those of **Section 84**, and fail to mention the requirement for **adequate remuneration**.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Compulsory Licences — Grounds, Procedure and Terms\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 84 | **FIRE** | FIRE burns for three years, each flame a ground for licence. |\n| Sec. 85 | **FILE** | File fills with public needs — reasonable requirements stack up. |\n| Sec. 86 | **FISH** | Fish swims in India's waters, priced affordably and worked locally. |\n| Sec. 87 | **FOG** | Fog obscures terms — Controller's conditions settle in murky haze. |\n| Sec. 88 | **VIVA** | VIVA performance ends — licence revoked for non-working. |\n| Sec. 89 | **FOB** | Fob flips — compulsory licence grounds, procedure, terms locked in. |\n| Sec. 90 | **BUS** | Bus stops — compulsory licence grounds, procedure, terms halt here. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **82-94** - working of patents, compulsory licences\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### Bayer Corporation v. Union of India\n_2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963_\n\n- **Held** - India's first compulsory licence, for sorafenib, upheld — all three grounds under Section 84(1) were satisfied.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Three years from grant and the three grounds under Section 84(1) - which provision?  (picture: FIRE)\n2. Reasonable requirements of the public - which provision?  (picture: FILE)\n3. Reasonably affordable price and working in the territory of India - which provision?  (picture: FISH)\n4. Terms and conditions settled by the Controller - which provision?  (picture: FOG)\n5. Revocation for non-working under Section 85 - which provision?  (picture: VIVA)\n6. Compulsory Licences — Grounds, Procedure and Terms - which provision?  (picture: FOB)\n7. Compulsory Licences — Grounds, Procedure and Terms - which provision?  (picture: BUS)\n8. Which case decided: India's first compulsory licence, for sorafenib, upheld — all three grounds under Section 84(1) were satisfied.\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 84 | Compulsory licence grounds: public need, price, non-working | Public needs unmet — red flag\n- Section 87(1) | Application served on patentee and published | Paper trail for fairness\n- Section 90 | Terms of compulsory licences: royalty, affordability, non-exclusive | Fair pay, not monopoly\n- Section 89 | General purposes of compulsory licence | Work patents, protect public interest\n- Section 84A | Generic companies can apply without patentee’s consent | Pharma shortcut — no need to ask\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Bayer Corporation v. Union of India (2014) | First compulsory licence for sorafenib upheld | Cancer drug unlocked\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing grounds under Section 84 with those in Section 85 — they are different.\n- Examiners expect you to cite exact section numbers and terms like \"prima facie case\".\n- Structure answers by listing grounds, procedure steps, and licence terms clearly.","storyEN":"**Story: The Patent of Dr. Anand and the Compulsory Licence**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, where the old city meets modern innovation, lived a young entrepreneur named Ravi. He had always dreamed of making life-saving drugs affordable to all — not just for the rich but for every citizen in India. His journey took an unexpected turn when he came across a patent held by Dr. Anand, a pharmaceutical giant who had developed a drug called Sorafenib, used in treating cancer.\n\nRavi knew that this drug could save countless lives if it were available at a reasonable price. But the cost was exorbitant — more than most people could afford. He also noticed that despite having the patent for over three years, Dr. Anand had not manufactured or sold the drug within India. This made Ravi wonder: Could he apply for a **compulsory licence** under **Section 84(1)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*? He decided to take action.\n\nHe gathered all his evidence — twenty-one documents proving that the public's reasonable requirements had not been met, and that the drug was neither available at a reasonably affordable price nor being worked in India. With these papers, he filed an application with the Controller under **Form 17**, as per **Rule 96**. The process began.\n\nThe Controller reviewed Ravi’s case and found it convincing enough to satisfy the **prima facie** requirement under **Section 87(1)**. He ordered that the application be served on Dr. Anand, who was surprised by the move. A notice of opposition came from Dr. Anand's team, but they were outvoted by a group of patients and doctors who had been waiting for this drug.\n\nAfter hearing both sides, the Controller decided in favor of Ravi. The grounds under **Section 84(1)(a)**, **(b)**, and **(c)** were all satisfied — public demand was unmet, prices were too high, and the invention was not being worked in India. This case echoed the landmark decision in **Bayer Corporation v. Union of India (2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963)**, where a similar compulsory licence had been granted for Sorafenib.\n\nThe Controller then set the terms of the licence under **Section 90**. Ravi was to pay reasonable remuneration to Dr. Anand — not too much, but enough to compensate him for his invention. The drug would be made available at affordable prices and sold only within India. The licence was non-exclusive and non-transferable. Most importantly, it was granted for the **balance term of the patent**, as per **Section 90(vi)**.\n\nRavi's factory started producing the drug in record time. Patients who had been waiting years finally saw hope on their faces. Dr. Anand, though initially upset, later acknowledged that the decision served a greater public good — something the *Patents Act* always aimed for under **Section 89**.\n\nAs Ravi stood before his factory, he looked at the twenty-one documents that had started it all and smiled. He knew he had not just saved lives; he had also upheld the balance between innovation and public interest, as outlined in **Sections 84 to 90** of the *Patents Act*.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one documents | Section 84(1) | A compulsory licence may be granted if reasonable public requirements are not met, the invention is not affordable, or it is not being worked in India. |\n| Form 17 and Rule 96 | Section 84(3) | Every application for a compulsory licence must contain a statement of the applicant's interest and supporting facts. |\n| Prima facie requirement | Section 87(1) | The Controller must be satisfied that a prima facie case has been made out before proceeding with a compulsory licence. |\n| Twenty-one witnesses | Section 84(6)(i) | The Controller considers whether the patentee has taken measures to make full use of the invention. |\n| Bayer Corporation v. Union of India (2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963) | Section 84(1) and 87(1) | A compulsory licence may be granted if all three grounds under Section 84(1) are satisfied, as demonstrated in this case. |\n| Non-exclusive and non-transferable | Section 90(iv) and (v) | Compulsory licences must be non-exclusive and the licensee cannot assign their rights. |\n| Balance term of the patent | Section 90(vi) | A compulsory licence is generally granted for the remainder of the patent’s term unless a shorter period is consistent with public interest. |\n| Reasonably affordable prices | Section 90(iii) | The patented invention must be made available to the public at reasonably affordable prices. |\n| Public non-commercial use | Section 90(viii) | In cases involving semiconductor technology, the licence may allow for public non-commercial use. |\n| Anti-competitive practices | Section 90(ix) | A compulsory licence may be granted if it is used to remedy anti-competitive practices determined by judicial or administrative process. |\n| Revocation of patents | Section 85(1) | After two years from the first compulsory licence, a patent can be revoked if it's not being worked in India or is too expensive. |\n| Adjournment period | Section 86(1) | The Controller may adjourn an application for up to twelve months if the invention cannot yet be worked on a commercial scale. |\n| Reasonable period of six months | Section 88(iv) | The applicant must make reasonable efforts to obtain a licence from the patentee within six months. |\n| Public interest | Section 90(vii) | Compulsory licences should primarily serve the Indian market, with export allowed if needed and in line with public interest. |\n| General purposes of compulsory licences | Section 89(a) and (b) | The Controller must ensure that inventions are worked on a commercial scale in India without undue delay and that no one’s interests are unfairly prejudiced. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for Compulsory Licences — Grounds, Procedure and Terms**  \n*(Based on Sections 84–90 of The Patents Act, 1970 and Case: *Bayer Corporation v. Union of India*, 2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963)*\n\n---\n\n### **1. Acronym for Grounds under Section 84(1):**  \n**P.R.E.S.T.**  \n- **P:** Public interest (reasonable requirements not met)  \n- **R:** Refusal to license (conditions on licences are prejudicial)  \n- **E:** Economic factors (not working invention in India)  \n- **S:** Safety/affordability (patented article not available at reasonable price)  \n- **T:** Technical advancement (invention not worked adequately)\n\n---\n\n### **2. Number-anchor for Section 84:**  \n**\"Section 84: A man with a key (8) and a padlock (4), trying to open a door — the key is public interest, the lock is patentee rights.\"**\n\n---\n\n### **3. Acronym for Procedure under Sections 87–90:**  \n**F.L.O.P.**  \n- **F:** Form 17 application with fee  \n- **L:** Notice to patentee and publication (Section 87)  \n- **O:** Opposition by patentee within prescribed time  \n- **P:** Hearing, determination, and grant of licence under Section 90\n\n---\n\n### **4. Number-anchor for Section 87:**  \n**\"Section 87: A newspaper (8) with a ‘7’ on it — publishing the application for compulsory licence.\"**\n\n---\n\n### **5. Acronym for Terms in Section 90:**  \n**R.E.A.S.O.N.**  \n- **R:** Royalty must be reasonable and based on invention’s value  \n- **E:** Ensure full working of patent with profit to licensee  \n- **A:** Affordable prices for public  \n- **S:** Non-exclusive, non-transferable licence  \n- **O:** Licence term is balance of patent life or shorter if in public interest  \n- **N:** Predominant purpose: supply in India; export allowed under conditions\n\n---\n\n### **6. Number-anchor for Section 90:**  \n**\"Section 90: A scale (9) with a ‘0’ — balancing the rights of the patentee and public.\"**\n\n---\n\n### **7. Confusion Pair Note (Grounds vs Procedure):**  \n- **Confusion Pair:** *“Public interest” (ground under Section 84) vs “Publication” (procedure under Section 87).*  \n  - **How to tell apart:** *Grounds are reasons for granting a licence; procedure is the process that follows once an application is made.*\n\n---\n\n### **8. Confusion Pair Note (Terms of Licence vs Grounds):**  \n- **Confusion Pair:** *“Non-exclusive” (term in Section 90) vs “Not exclusive” (misunderstanding of terms).*  \n  - **How to tell apart:** *Non-exclusive means others can also use the patent; not exclusive is not a legal term used in this context.*\n\n---\n\n### **9. Confusion Pair Note (Compulsory Licence vs Voluntary Licence):**  \n- **Confusion Pair:** *“Compulsory licence” (granted by Controller) vs “Voluntary licence” (granted by patentee).*  \n  - **How to tell apart:** *Compulsory is mandatory under law; voluntary is optional and negotiated.*\n\n---\n\n### **10. Case Mnemonic: Bayer Corporation v. Union of India, 2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963**  \n- **Mnemonic:** *\"Bayer’s drug (Sorafenib) was made available at a lower price due to a compulsory licence — the first in India.\"*  \n- **Link to Sections:** *This case is tied to Section 84(1)(a), (b), and (c) – all three grounds were satisfied.*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed for quick recall, clarity, and accurate application during exams.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Three years must pass after the patent grant before an application can be made. Section 84(1) specifies this period as a prerequisite for eligibility.","question":"What is the time limit for applying for a compulsory licence under Section 84(1)?"},{"answer":"The grounds are unmet public needs, unreasonably high prices, and non-working of the invention in India. These are outlined in clauses (a), (b), and (c) of Section 84(1).","question":"What are the three grounds for granting a compulsory licence under Section 84(1)?"},{"answer":"To satisfy public demand when the patentee fails to meet reasonable affordability or local working requirements. Section 84(4) allows this if the Controller finds these conditions unmet.","question":"Why might the Controller grant a compulsory licence?"},{"answer":"The applicant must demonstrate unmet public needs, unaffordable pricing, or non-working of the invention in India. Section 84(3) requires these facts to be stated in the application.","question":"What must an applicant prove to obtain a compulsory licence?"},{"answer":"Revocation can be applied for two years after the first compulsory licence order. Section 85(1) sets this two-year waiting period for revocation applications.","question":"How long after a compulsory licence is granted can revocation be sought?"},{"answer":"The Controller assesses the invention’s nature, time since grant, and the applicant’s capacity to work the invention. Section 84(6)(i)-(iii) outlines these evaluation criteria.","question":"What factors does the Controller consider when evaluating a compulsory licence application?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 89 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “General purposes for granting compulsory licences”.","options":["Section 6","Section 44","Section 89","Section 52"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: General purposes for granting compulsory licences?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 89 is headed “General purposes for granting compulsory licences”.","options":["Applications for restoration of lapsed patents","General purposes for granting compulsory licences","Appearance of Controller in legal proceedings","In cases of “obtaining” Controller may treat the patent as the"],"question":"Section 89 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 84 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Restoration of patents”.","options":["Rule 74","Rule 29A","Rule 84","Rule 76"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Restoration of patents?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 84 is headed “Restoration of patents”.","options":["Restoration of patents","Power to extend time specified or condone delay","Order of recording applications","Filing of notice of opposition"],"question":"Rule 84 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 85 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Revocation of patents by the Controller for non-working”.","options":["Section 85","Section 86","Section 44","Section 55"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Revocation of patents by the Controller for non-working?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 85 is headed “Revocation of patents by the Controller for non-working”.","options":["Restrictions on practice as patent agents","Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications","Revocation of patents by the Controller for non-working","Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court"],"question":"Section 85 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 87 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Surrender of Patents”.","options":["Rule 31","Rule 94","Rule 87","Rule 19B"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Surrender of Patents?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 87 is headed “Surrender of Patents”.","options":["Period within which copies of specification etc. are to be","Amendments to specifications","Further evidence to be left with the leave of the","Surrender of Patents"],"question":"Rule 87 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 86 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Payment of unpaid renewal fees”.","options":["Rule 110","Rule 86","Rule 29","Rule 3"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Payment of unpaid renewal fees?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 86 is headed “Payment of unpaid renewal fees”.","options":["Payment of fees","Form of reference to another specification","Payment of unpaid renewal fees","Filing of priority document"],"question":"Rule 86 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 84 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Compulsory licences”.","options":["Section 84","Section 109","Section 82","Section 131"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Compulsory licences?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 84 is headed “Compulsory licences”.","options":["Compulsory licences","Rights of patentees of lapsed patents which have been restored","Anticipation by public working","Procedure for dealing with applications under sections 84 and"],"question":"Section 84 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Every application under sub-section (1) shall ordinarily be decided within one year of its being presented to the Controller.","options":["a) Within six months of the application being presented","b) Within one year of the application being presented","c) Within two years of the application being presented","d) Within three years of the application being presented"],"question":"Under Section 85(4), what is the time limit for the Controller to decide an application for revocation of a patent?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"“reasonable period” shall be construed as a period not ordinarily exceeding a period of six months.","options":["a) A period not exceeding three months","b) A period not exceeding six months","c) A period not exceeding nine months","d) A period not exceeding twelve months"],"question":"According to Section 88(6)(iv), what is the definition of a 'reasonable period' for efforts to obtain a licence from the patentee?"}]},"s1p_27":{"notes":"# Special Compulsory Licences — Public Health, Export and Notified Circumstances  \n*(Based on \"Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\" syllabus)*  \n\n---\n\n## **Definition of Special Compulsory Licences**  \nSpecial compulsory licences refer to the legal mechanism under which a patent is licensed without the consent of the patentee in exceptional circumstances such as public health emergencies, export requirements, or notifications by the Central Government. These are distinct from general compulsory licences and are governed specifically under Sections 92 and 92A of *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n---\n\n## **Key Concepts**  \n\n### **1. Compulsory Licences in Public Health Emergencies (Section 92)**  \n- **Applicability**: In cases of national emergency, extreme urgency, or public non-commercial use.\n- **Triggering Event**: The Central Government may issue a notification in the *Official Gazette* if it is satisfied that compulsory licensing is necessary to address public health crises such as:\n  - Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome (AIDS)\n  - Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)\n  - Tuberculosis\n  - Malaria\n  - Other epidemics  \n- **Grant of Licence**: Any interested person may apply for a compulsory licence at any time after the notification. The Controller shall grant it on terms and conditions he deems fit.\n- **Objective**: To ensure that medicines are available to the public at the lowest prices consistent with the patentee deriving a reasonable advantage from their rights.\n\n> *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 280)* explains that in such cases, the Controller is not required to follow the procedures under Section 87 of the Act, but must inform the patentee about this non-application as soon as practicable.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Compulsory Licences for Export (Section 92A)**  \n- **Purpose**: To allow the manufacture and export of patented pharmaceutical products to countries with insufficient or no manufacturing capacity in the pharmaceutical sector.\n- **Conditions**:\n  - The importing country must either have granted a compulsory licence itself, or it must have allowed importation from India through notification or other means.\n  - The Controller may grant such a licence solely for the purpose of export and under terms and conditions as specified by him.\n- **Scope**: Applies to pharmaceutical products needed to address public health problems. This includes:\n  - Patented products\n  - Products manufactured using patented processes\n  - Ingredients necessary for their manufacture\n  - Diagnostic kits required for their use\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 92 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 92. Special provision for compulsory licences on notifications by\n> Central Government.\n> (1) If the Central Government is satisfied, in respect of any patent\n> in force in circumstances of national emergency or in circumstances\n> of extreme urgency or in case of public non-commercial use, that it\n> is necessary that compulsory licences should be granted at any time\n> after the sealing thereof to work the invention, it may make a\n> declaration to that effect, by notification in the Official Gazette,\n> and thereupon the following provisions shall have effect, that is to\n> say,\n> (i) the Controller shall, on application made at any time after the\n> notification by any person interested, grant to the applicant a\n> licence under the patent on such terms and conditions as he thinks\n> fit;\n> (ii) in settling the terms and conditions of a licence granted under\n> this section, the Controller shall endeavour to secure that the\n> articles manufactured under the patent shall be available to the\n> public at the lowest prices consistent with the patentees deriving a\n> reasonable advantage from their patent rights.\n>\n>  76\n>\n> (2) The provisions of sections 83, 87, 88, 89 and 90 shall apply in\n> relation to the grant of licences under this section as they apply\n> in relation to the grant of licences under section 84.\n>\n> (3) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (2), where the\n> Controller is satisfied on consideration of the application referred\n> to in clause (i) of sub-section (1) that it is necessary in\n> (i) a circumstance of national emergency; or\n> (ii) a circumstance of extreme urgency; or\n> (iii) a case of public non-commercial use, which may arise or is\n> required, as the case may be, including public health crises,\n> relating to Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome, human\n> immunodeficiency virus, tuberculosis, malaria or other epidemics, he\n> shall not apply any procedure specified in section 87 in relation to\n> that application for grant of licence under this section:\n> Provided that the Controller shall, as soon as may be practicable,\n> inform the patentee of the patent relating to the application for\n> such non-application of section 87.\n\n**Section 92A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 92A. Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical\n> products in certain exceptional circumstances.\n> (1) Compulsory licence shall be available for manufacture and export\n> of patented pharmaceutical products to any country having\n> insufficient or no manufacturing capacity in the pharmaceutical\n> sector for the concerned product to address public health problems,\n> provided compulsory licence has been granted by such country or such\n> country has, by notification or otherwise, allowed importation of\n> the patented pharmaceutical products from India.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall, on receipt of an application in the\n> prescribed manner, grant a compulsory licence solely for manufacture\n> and export of the concerned pharmaceutical product to such country\n> under such terms and conditions as may be specified and published by\n> him.\n>\n> (3) The provisions of sub-sections (1) and (2) shall be without\n> prejudice to the extent to which pharmaceutical products produced\n> under a compulsory licence can be exported under any other provision\n> of this Act.\n>\n>  77\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this section, “pharmaceutical\n> products” means any patented product, or product manufactured\n> through a patented process, of the pharmaceutical sector needed to\n> address public health problems and shall be inclusive of ingredients\n> necessary for their manufacture and diagnostic kits required for\n> their use.\n\n**Section 100 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 100. Power of Central Government to use inventions for purposes of\n> Government.\n> (1) Notwithstanding anything contained in this Act, at any time\n> after an application for a patent has been filed at the patent\n> office or a patent has been granted, the Central Government and any\n> person authorised in writing by it, may use the invention for the\n> purposes of Government in accordance with the provisions of this\n> Chapter.\n>\n> (2) Where an invention has, before the priority date of the relevant\n> claim of the complete specification, been duly recorded in a\n> document, or tested or tried, by or on behalf of the Government or a\n> Government undertaking, otherwise than in consequence of the\n> communication of the invention directly or indirectly by the\n> patentee or by a person from whom he derives title, any use of the\n> invention by the Central Government or any person authorised in\n> writing by it for the purposes of Government may be made free of any\n> royalty or other remuneration to the patentee.\n>\n> (3) If and so far as the invention has not been so recorded or tried\n> or tested as aforesaid, any use of the invention made by the Central\n> Government or any person authorised by it under sub-section (1), at\n> any time after grant of the patent or in consequence of any such\n> communication as aforesaid, shall be made upon terms as may be\n> agreed upon either before or after the use, between the Central\n>\n>  79\n>\n> Government or any person authorised under sub-section (1) and the\n> patentee, or, as may in default of agreement be determined by the\n> High Court on a reference under section 103:\n> Provided that in case of any such use of any patent, the patentee\n> shall be paid not more than adequate remuneration in the\n> circumstances of each case, taking into account the economic value\n> of the use of the patent.\n>\n> (4) The authorisation by the Central Government in respect of an\n> invention may be given under this section, either before or after\n> the patent is granted and either before or after the acts in respect\n> of which such authorisation is given or done, and may be given to\n> any person, whether or not he is authorised directly or indirectly\n> by the applicant or the patentee to make, use, exercise or vend the\n> invention or import the machine, apparatus or other article or\n> medicine or drug covered by such patent.\n>\n> (5) Where an invention has been used by or with the authority of the\n> Central Government for the purposes of Government under this\n> section, then, except in case of national emergency or other\n> circumstances of extreme urgency or for non-commercial use, the\n> Government shall notify the patentee as soon as practicable of the\n> fact and furnish him with such information as to the extent of the\n> use of the invention as he may, from time to time, reasonable\n> require; and where the invention has been used for the purposes of a\n> Government undertaking, the Central Government may call for such\n> information as may be necessary for this purpose from such\n> undertaking.\n>\n> (6) The right to make, use, exercise and vend an invention for the\n> purposes of Government under sub-section (1) shall include the right\n> to sell, on non-commercial basis, the goods which have been made in\n> exercise of that right, and a purchaser of goods so sold, and a\n> person claiming through him, shall have the power to deal with the\n> goods as if the Central Government or the person authorised under\n> sub-section (1) were the patentee of the invention.\n>\n> (7) Where in respect of a patent which has been the subject of an\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 102 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 102. Acquisition of inventions and patents by the Central\n> Government.\n> (1) The Central Government may, if satisfied that it is necessary\n> that an invention which is the subject of an application for a\n> patent or a patent should be acquired from the applicant or the\n> patentee for a public purpose, publish a notification to that effect\n> in the Official Gazette, and thereupon the invention or patent and\n> all rights in respect of the invention or patent shall, by force of\n> this section, stand transferred to and be vested in the Central\n> Government.\n>\n> (2) Notice of the acquisition shall be given to the applicant, and,\n> where a patent has been granted, to the patentee and other persons,\n> if any, appearing in the register as having an interest in the\n> patent.\n>\n> (3) The Central Government shall pay to the applicant, or, as the\n> case may be, the patentee and other persons appearing on the\n>\n>  82\n>\n> register as having an interest in the patent such compensation as\n> may be agreed upon between the Central Government and the applicant,\n> or the patentee and other persons; or, as may, in default of\n> agreement, be determined by the High Court on a reference under\n> section 103 to be just having regard to the expenditure incurred in\n> connection with the invention and, in the case of a patent, the term\n> thereof, the period during which and the manner in which it has\n> already been worked (including the profits made during such period\n> by the patentee or by his licensee whether exclusive or otherwise)\n> and other relevant factors.\n\n**Rule 96 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 96. Application for compulsory licence etc.\n> An application to the Controller for an order under section 84,\n> section 85, section 91 or section 92 or section 92A shall be in Form\n> 17, or Form 19, as the case may be. Except in the case of an\n> application made by the Central Government, the application shall\n> set out the nature of the applicant’s interest and terms and\n> conditions of the licence the applicant is willing to accept.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provisions relating to special compulsory licences for public health emergencies under Section 92 of The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2008, 2014, 2021)*\n\n**Issue**: What are the provisions of Section 92 of *The Patents Act, 1970*, regarding special compulsory licences in public health emergencies?\n\n**Rule**:  \nSection 92 of *The Patents Act, 1970* provides for special compulsory licences to be issued by the Controller under exceptional circumstances such as national emergency, extreme urgency, or public non-commercial use. Specifically, if the Central Government issues a notification in the *Official Gazette* that such circumstances exist, any interested person may apply for a licence at any time after the notification. The Controller has discretion to grant the licence on terms and conditions he deems fit, with an objective of ensuring medicines are available at the lowest prices consistent with reasonable advantage to the patentee.\n\nThe provisions under Section 92 do not require compliance with the procedures outlined in Section 87 for such applications, particularly when dealing with public health crises like AIDS, HIV, tuberculosis, malaria, or other epidemics. However, the Controller is required to inform the patentee of this non-application as soon as practicable.\n\n**Application**:  \nIn a scenario where a public health emergency arises, such as an outbreak of tuberculosis in a region, and the Central Government issues a notification under Section 92, any interested party—such as a generic drug manufacturer or a government agency—can apply for a compulsory licence. The Controller may then grant the licence without following the procedural requirements of Section 87, but must notify the patentee about this decision promptly.\n\nThe objective is to ensure that essential medicines are available at the lowest possible prices while still allowing the patentee to derive a reasonable advantage from their rights. This provision balances public health needs with the protection of intellectual property rights.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nSection 92 of *The Patents Act, 1970* provides a legal mechanism for granting special compulsory licences during public health emergencies. It allows the Controller to bypass certain procedural requirements under Section 87 in such cases, provided that the Central Government has issued a notification. This ensures access to life-saving medicines while respecting the rights of patentees.\n\n**Examiner's eye**:  \nStudents often fail to mention the specific conditions under which Section 92 applies (such as public health crises like HIV or malaria) and may overlook the requirement for the Controller to inform the patentee about non-application of Section 87 procedures. These details are critical in answering this question comprehensively.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the provisions of Section 92A of *The Patents Act, 1970*, relating to compulsory licences for export purposes.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2006, 2018)*\n\n**Issue**: What are the provisions of Section 92A regarding compulsory licences for the export of pharmaceutical products?\n\n**Rule**:  \nSection 92A of *The Patents Act, 1970* provides that a compulsory licence may be granted solely for the purpose of exporting patented pharmaceutical products to countries with insufficient or no manufacturing capacity in the pharmaceutical sector. This is intended to address public health problems in such countries.\n\nTo qualify, the importing country must either have granted a compulsory licence itself or allow importation from India through notification or other means. The Controller may grant the licence under terms and conditions he deems fit. The scope includes not only patented products but also ingredients, diagnostic kits, and products manufactured using patented processes.\n\n**Application**:  \nSuppose a pharmaceutical product is patented in India, and there is a demand for it in a developing country that lacks manufacturing capacity. If the importing country has allowed importation from India through notification or other means, then an application can be made to the Controller under Section 92A for a compulsory licence. The Controller may grant the licence solely for export purposes, allowing the product to be manufactured and exported.\n\nThis provision facilitates access to medicines in countries with limited manufacturing capabilities while ensuring that the patentee is not unfairly disadvantaged.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nSection 92A of *The Patents Act, 1970* enables the granting of compulsory licences for the export of pharmaceutical products to countries lacking sufficient manufacturing capacity. It ensures access to essential medicines abroad and supports global public health initiatives without violating the rights of patent holders.\n\n**Examiner's eye**:  \nStudents often confuse Section 92A with general compulsory licensing provisions under Section 84 or fail to mention the requirement that the importing country must have allowed importation from India through notification or other means. These are essential points for a complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the scope and purpose of special compulsory licences as provided in Sections 92 and 92A of *The Patents Act, 1970*.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2016)*\n\n**Issue**: What is the scope and purpose of special compulsory licences under Sections 92 and 92A of *The Patents Act, 1970*?\n\n**Rule**:  \nSection 92 provides for special compulsory licences in exceptional circumstances such as national emergencies or public health crises. It allows any interested party to apply for a licence after the Central Government issues a notification under Section 92. The Controller may grant the licence on terms and conditions that ensure medicines are available at low prices while allowing patentees to derive reasonable advantage.\n\nSection 92A, on the other hand, provides for compulsory licences solely for export purposes, particularly in cases where the importing country lacks manufacturing capacity. This is aimed at addressing public health problems globally by enabling the export of patented pharmaceutical products, ingredients, or diagnostic kits.\n\n**Application**:  \nIn a situation involving a national emergency such as an HIV epidemic, the Central Government may issue a notification under Section 92. An interested party can then apply for a compulsory licence to manufacture and distribute the drug at affordable prices. The Controller has discretion in setting terms but must ensure that the patentee receives reasonable compensation.\n\nUnder Section 92A, if India is exporting life-saving drugs to a country like Nigeria, which lacks the capacity to produce such medicines, an application can be made for a compulsory licence. This allows the drug to be manufactured and exported under controlled conditions, ensuring availability in the importing country without violating patent rights.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nSections 92 and 92A of *The Patents Act, 1970* provide mechanisms for granting special compulsory licences during public health emergencies or for export purposes. These provisions aim to ensure access to essential medicines while balancing the interests of patent holders with public welfare.\n\n**Examiner's eye**:  \nStudents often fail to distinguish clearly between the two sections and may confuse their respective scopes and purposes. It is important to highlight that Section 92 deals with domestic use in emergencies, while Section 92A focuses on export for global health needs.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Special Compulsory Licences — Public Health, Export and Notified Circumstances\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 92 | **BONE** | BONE cracks under government's lock, forcing licence. |\n| Sec. 92-A | **BONE** + the letter A | BONE splinters in national emergency's grip. |\n| Sec. 100 | **TIE-SAUCE** | TIE-SAUCE drips from patent export's edge. |\n| Sec. 102 | **TIE-SUN** | TIE-SUN blazes as invention binds to state. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **82-94** - working of patents, compulsory licences\n- **99-103** - use of inventions by Government\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Compulsory licence on a notification by the Central Government - which provision?  (picture: BONE)\n2. National emergency, extreme urgency and public non-commercial use - which provision?  (picture: BONE)\n3. Export of patented pharmaceutical products under Section 92A - which provision?  (picture: TIE-SAUCE)\n4. Use of an invention for the purposes of Government - which provision?  (picture: TIE-SUN)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 92 | Compulsory licences during public health crises | Medicine on discount shelf  \n- Section 92A | Export of medicines to low-capacity countries | Ship with medicine boxes  \n- Section 100 | Government use of inventions for its purposes | Soldier using patented gear  \n- Section 102 | Acquisition of patents by the Central Government | Government buying patent book  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing general and special compulsory licences  \n- Use exact terms like \"national emergency\" or \"public non-commercial use\"  \n- Always cite the correct section numbers in answers for full marks","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where the old city meets the new, there lived a young man named Ravi. He was an aspiring patent agent with dreams as vast as the Golconda Fort. One day, he received a letter from the Central Government requesting his help in a matter that would test not only his knowledge but also his values.\n\nRavi had heard whispers of a strange situation involving **Section 92** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, a law that allowed for special compulsory licences under dire circumstances. He soon found himself at the heart of an emergency: a deadly outbreak of malaria was sweeping through several villages in Telangana. The only drug available to treat it was patented by a pharmaceutical company based in Mumbai.\n\nThe Central Government, after careful deliberation, issued a **notification** in the *Official Gazette* under **Section 92**, declaring that there was an emergency and that compulsory licences were necessary. Ravi’s task? To ensure that the Controller of Patents granted a licence to a local manufacturer who could produce the drug at a fraction of the original cost.\n\nAs he worked, Ravi recalled what he had read in his textbook: *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, page **280**, which explained that under such circumstances, the Controller did not have to follow the usual procedures but must inform the patentee as soon as possible.\n\nMeanwhile, Ravi’s friend, a pharmaceutical chemist named Meera, was working on a plan to export a new drug to a neighboring country with no manufacturing capacity of its own. The importing nation had already granted itself a compulsory licence, and it allowed imports from India through notification. This meant that **Section 92A** could be invoked.\n\nMeera applied for a compulsory licence under **Section 92A**, asking the Controller to allow her company to manufacture the drug and export it. She was careful to make sure all conditions were met — the importing country had already granted its own licence, and she included diagnostic kits in the shipment as part of the product.\n\nBack in Hyderabad, Ravi faced a challenge: the patentee from Mumbai was not happy with the idea of his drug being produced at lower prices. He argued that it would undermine his investment and innovation. But Ravi reminded him of what **Section 92** stated — that medicines must be available to the public at the lowest price consistent with the patentee deriving a reasonable advantage.\n\nAs the Controller processed both applications, Ravi also learned about **Section 100**, which gave the Central Government the power to use inventions for government purposes. In this case, it was not needed because the emergency had already been addressed through compulsory licensing. But he noted that if such a situation arose again — say, in a national emergency or extreme urgency — the government could step in without needing the patentee's consent.\n\nJust as Ravi and Meera were about to celebrate their success, they received news of another case: **Section 102**, which allowed the Central Government to acquire an invention for public purposes. Though not needed here, it was a reminder that the law provided multiple tools to ensure public health and welfare in times of crisis.\n\nRavi returned home that night, his mind full of numbers — **92**, **92A**, **100**, **102** — each one a key piece of the puzzle. He knew now that these laws were not just words on paper but lifelines for people in need.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 92 | Special provision for compulsory licences on notifications by Central Government | If the Central Government issues a notification due to national emergency, extreme urgency, or public non-commercial use, any interested person may apply for a licence, and the Controller shall grant it at the lowest price consistent with reasonable advantage to the patentee. |\n| Section 92A | Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical products in certain exceptional circumstances | Compulsory licences can be granted solely for the purpose of exporting patented pharmaceuticals to countries lacking manufacturing capacity, provided the importing country allows such imports. |\n| Section 100 | Power of Central Government to use inventions for purposes of Government | The Central Government may use an invention for government purposes without the patentee’s consent, and may do so free of royalty or remuneration if certain conditions are met. |\n| Section 102 | Acquisition of inventions and patents by the Central Government | The Central Government may acquire an invention or patent for public purposes, paying compensation to the applicant or patentee as agreed upon or determined by the High Court. |","memory":"### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Special Compulsory Licences — Public Health, Export and Notified Circumstances**\n\n**Mnemonic: \"PEP\" (Public Health, Export, and Notified circumstances)**  \n- **P**: Public Health – Covered under Section 92(1) and 92A.  \n- **E**: Export – Covered under Section 92A for export of pharmaceuticals.  \n- **N**: Notified Circumstances – Covered under Section 92(1), where Central Government issues a notification in case of national emergency, extreme urgency or public non-commercial use.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors**\n\n- **Section 84** → *“Compulsory Licence”*  \n  *Picture: A key (symbolizing access) being handed over by an official (Controller).*\n\n- **Section 92(1)** → *“Public Health and National Emergency”*  \n  *Picture: A doctor holding a red cross (public health), and a flag (national emergency).*\n\n- **Section 92A** → *“Export of Pharmaceuticals”*  \n  *Picture: A ship carrying boxes labeled “Pharma,” sailing out of India.*\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **Section 84 vs Section 92(1):**  \n  *Both allow compulsory licences, but Section 84 is applied by an interested person, while Section 92(1) is triggered by a Government notification in cases of national emergency or public health.*\n\n- **Compulsory Licence under Section 92A vs Section 84:**  \n  *Section 92A deals specifically with export of pharmaceuticals to countries with limited manufacturing capacity. It does not require the patentee’s consent, but requires payment of adequate remuneration.*\n\n- **Public Health vs Public Non-commercial Use:**  \n  *Public health (e.g., HIV/AIDS) is a subset of public non-commercial use, which includes broader scenarios like national emergencies or extreme urgency.*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"A compulsory licence granted by the Controller upon the Central Government's notification during national emergencies or public health crises. It ensures the invention is available at the lowest price consistent with patentee benefits.","question":"What is a compulsory licence under Section 92?"},{"answer":"The Controller grants licences upon application after the Central Government's notification, setting terms to ensure public access at the lowest price while allowing the patentee a reasonable return.","question":"How does the Controller handle applications under Section 92?"},{"answer":"To ensure public affordability during emergencies, while allowing the patentee to derive a reasonable advantage from their rights.","question":"Why does the Controller prioritize the lowest price for patented articles?"},{"answer":"A national emergency lacks a standardized definition but may include public health crises like epidemics, as determined by the Central Government's notification.","question":"What defines a national emergency for compulsory licensing?"},{"answer":"The Controller ordinarily decides applications within one year of their presentation, as per the provisions under Section 92.","question":"What is the time limit for the Controller to decide on Section 92 applications?"},{"answer":"The Controller skips Section 87 procedures during national emergencies or public health crises, provided they inform the patentee as soon as practicable.","question":"When can the Controller skip Section 87 procedures?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 92 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Special provision for compulsory licences on notifications by”.","options":["Section 92A","Section 64","Section 118","Section 92"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Special provision for compulsory licences on notifications by?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 92 is headed “Special provision for compulsory licences on notifications by”.","options":["Validity of patents of addition","Special provision for compulsory licences on notifications by","Right of exclusive licensee to take proceedings against","Transmission of orders of courts to Controller"],"question":"Section 92 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 92A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical”.","options":["Section 14","Section 140","Section 92A","Section 71"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 92A is headed “Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical”.","options":["Meaning of use of invention for purposes of Government","Surrender of patents","Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior","Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical"],"question":"Section 92A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 100 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Application under section 88(4)”.","options":["Rule 100","Rule 76","Rule 19J","Rule 58"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Application under section 88(4)?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 100 is headed “Application under section 88(4)”.","options":["Scale of costs","Short title and commencement","Prescribed particulars","Application under section 88(4)"],"question":"Rule 100 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 102 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Application for termination of compulsory licence under”.","options":["Rule 20","Rule 108","Rule 102","Rule 98"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Application for termination of compulsory licence under?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 102 is headed “Application for termination of compulsory licence under”.","options":["Constitution of Opposition Board and its proceeding","Manner of applying for direction under section 51(1)","Application for termination of compulsory licence under","Inclusion of the name of any other person in the roll of"],"question":"Rule 102 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 102 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Acquisition of inventions and patents by the Central”.","options":["Section 61","Section 106","Section 74","Section 102"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Acquisition of inventions and patents by the Central?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 102 is headed “Acquisition of inventions and patents by the Central”.","options":["Anticipation by use and publication after provisional","Acquisition of inventions and patents by the Central","Rectification of register by High Court","Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of"],"question":"Section 102 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 92 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Registration of title or interest in a patent”.","options":["Rule 19G","Rule 92","Rule 76","Rule 82"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Registration of title or interest in a patent?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 92 is headed “Registration of title or interest in a patent”.","options":["Registration of title or interest in a patent","Roll of scientific advisers","Application for termination of compulsory licence under","International search report"],"question":"Rule 92 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Central Government may issue such notifications in 'circumstances of national emergency or in circumstances of extreme urgency or in case of public non-commercial use.'","options":["a) Only during a national emergency","b) In cases of public non-commercial use or extreme urgency","c) When the patentee requests it","d) For export to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity"],"question":"Under which circumstances may the Central Government issue a notification for granting compulsory licences under Section 92?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Controller shall endeavour to secure that the articles manufactured under the patent shall be available to the public at the lowest prices consistent with the patentees deriving a reasonable advantage from their patent rights.","options":["a) Maximum profit for the patentee","b) Availability of the product at the lowest prices consistent with the patentee deriving a reasonable advantage","c) Exclusion of public health crises from consideration","d) Immediate revocation of the patent"],"question":"What must the Controller ensure when settling terms for a licence under Section 92?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 92A explicitly provides for compulsory licences in such exceptional circumstances.","options":["a) Section 92","b) Section 92A","c) Section 100","d) Section 102"],"question":"Which section allows compulsory licences for exporting pharmaceutical products to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"If the invention was recorded, tested, or tried by the Government before the priority date of the claim, royalty may be waived.","options":["a) Only if the invention was tested by the Government before the patent was filed","b) If the patentee agrees to it","c) During a national emergency","d) When the invention is not patented"],"question":"Under Section 100, when may the Central Government use an invention for government purposes without paying royalty?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Central Government must publish a notification in the Official Gazette to acquire the invention or patent.","options":["a) Compensate the patentee based on the invention's market value","b) Publish a notification in the Official Gazette","c) Seek approval from the patentee","d) Transfer the patent to a private entity"],"question":"What must the Central Government do under Section 102 when acquiring a patent for public purposes?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Controller may exempt applications in cases of public health crises relating to HIV, malaria, or other epidemics.","options":["a) Only for pharmaceutical products","b) In cases of public health crises related to HIV or malaria","c) When the patentee consents","d) For export to developed countries"],"question":"Under Section 92(3), when may the Controller exempt an application from procedures under Section 87?"}]},"s1p_28":{"notes":"# Revocation of Patents — Section 64 Grounds\n\n## Definition\n\nRevocation of a patent refers to the legal process by which a granted patent is canceled or declared invalid due to non-compliance with the provisions of the **Patents Act, 1970**. This can be initiated either by a person interested in the patent or by the Central Government on certain grounds.\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n- **Revocation**: A formal cancellation of a patent by the Controller or High Court.\n- **Grounds for Revocation**: Specific legal reasons under which a patent may be revoked, as outlined in **Section 64** of the Patents Act, 1970.\n- **Person Interested**: Any individual or entity that has an interest in the patent, such as assignees, licensees, or competitors.\n\n## Section Numbers and Wording (from Bare Act)\n\n### **Section 64: Revocation of Patent**\n\n> (1) Subject to the provisions contained in this Act, a patent whether granted before or after the commencement of this Act, may be revoked on a petition of any person interested or of the Central Government or on a counter-claim in a suit for infringement of the patent by the High Court on any of the following grounds, that is to say:\n>\n> (a) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete specification, was claimed in a valid claim of earlier priority date contained in the complete specification of another patent granted in India;\n>\n> (b) that the patent was granted on the application of a person not entitled under the provisions of this Act to apply therefor:\n>\n> (c) that the patent was obtained wrongfully in contravention of the rights of the petitioner or any person under or through whom he claims;\n>\n> (d) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is not an invention within the meaning of this Act;\n>\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete specification is not new, having regard to what was publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority date of the claim or to what was published in India or elsewhere in any of the documents referred to in section 13:\n>\n> (f) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete specification is obvious or does not involve any inventive step, having regard to what was publicly known or publicly used in India or what was published in India or elsewhere before the priority date of the claim:\n>\n> (g) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the complete specification, is not useful;\n>\n> (h) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and fairly describe the invention and the method by which it is to be performed, that is to say, that the description of the method or the instructions for the working of the invention as contained in the complete specification are not by themselves sufficient to enable a person in India possessing average skill in, and average knowledge of, the art to which the invention relates, to work the invention, or that it does not disclose the best method of performing it which was known to the applicant for the patent and for which he was entitled to claim protection;\n>\n> (i) that the scope of any claim of the complete specification is not sufficiently and clearly defined or that any claim of the complete specification is not fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification;\n>\n> (j) that the patent was obtained on a false suggestion or representation;\n>\n> (k) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is not patentable under this Act;\n>\n> (l) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete specification was secretly used in India, otherwise than as mentioned in sub-section (3), before the priority date of the claim;\n>\n> (m) that the applicant for the patent has failed to disclose to the Controller the information required by section 8 or has furnished information which in any material particular was false to his knowledge;\n>\n> (n) that the applicant contravened any direction for secrecy passed under section 35 or made or caused to be made an application for the grant of a patent outside India in contravention of section 39;\n>\n> (o) that leave to amend the complete specification under section 57 or section 58 was obtained by fraud;\n>\n> (p) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly mentions the source or geographical origin of biological material used for the invention;\n>\n> (q) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete specification was anticipated having regard to the knowledge, oral or otherwise, available within any local or indigenous community in India or elsewhere.\n\n## Explanation and Reasoning from Textbook\n\n### **Why Grounds for Revocation Exist**\n\nThe grounds for revocation under **Section 64** are designed to ensure that only valid and legitimate patents remain in force. This helps maintain the integrity of the patent system by preventing invalid or improperly obtained patents from being enforced.\n\n- The provisions under Section 64(1)(m) state that a patent may be revoked if the applicant failed to disclose information required under **Section 8** or provided false information (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 141). This ensures transparency and honesty in the application process.\n\n- It is also important to note that Section 64 does not operate independently of other provisions in the **Patents Act**. As explained by the court in a relevant case (verify this case with a case-law database), the language used in the preface to Section 64 (\"Subject to the provisions contained in this Act\") indicates that it is subservient to all other provisions in the Patents Act (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 232). This means that if there are conflicting provisions elsewhere in the Act, those will take precedence over Section 64.\n\n- Furthermore, **Section 66** of the Patents Act provides for revocation on public interest grounds, such as when a patent or its exercise is deemed mischievous to the State or prejudicial to the public (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 600). This ensures that patents are not used in ways that harm national interests.\n\n## Grounds for Revocation Under Section 64\n\n### **Ground (a)**: Prior Claim\n- If a claim is already covered by an earlier patent with an earlier priority date, the current patent may be revoked.\n\n### **Ground (b)**: Wrongful Applicant\n- If the applicant was not entitled to apply under the Act, the patent can be revoked.\n\n### **Ground (c)**: Wrongful Acquisition\n- If the patent was obtained in violation of another person’s rights, it may be revoked.\n\n### **Ground (d)**: Not an Invention\n- If the subject matter is not considered an invention under the Act, the patent is invalid.\n\n### **Ground (e)**: Lack of Novelty\n- The invention must be new; if prior art exists in India or elsewhere before the priority date, it may be invalidated.\n\n### **Ground (f)**: Lack of Inventive Step\n- If the invention is obvious to a person skilled in the relevant field based on existing knowledge, it is not patentable.\n\n### **Ground (g)**: Non-Utility\n- The invention must be useful; if not, the patent may be revoked.\n\n### **Ground (h)**: Insufficient Disclosure\n- Specifications must describe the invention sufficiently for someone with average skill in the art to work the invention. If this is not done, or if the best method of performing it is not disclosed, the patent can be revoked.\n\n### **Ground (i)**: Unclear Claims\n- Claims must be clearly defined and based on the disclosure in the specification; otherwise, they may be invalid.\n\n### **Ground (j)**: False Information\n- If a patent was obtained through false suggestions or representations, it is subject to revocation.\n\n### **Ground (k)**: Non-Patentable Subject Matter\n- The invention must fall within the scope of what can be patented under the Act; otherwise, it may be revoked.\n\n### **Ground (l)**: Prior Secret Use in India\n- If the invention was secretly used in India before the priority date, and not as mentioned in sub-section (3), it may be invalidated.\n\n### **Ground (m)**: Non-Disclosure or False Information under Section 8\n- Failure to disclose information required by **Section 8** or providing false information can lead to revocation.\n\n### **Ground (n)**: Contravention of Secrecy Directions\n- If the applicant violated secrecy directions under **Section 35**, or applied for a patent outside India in contravention of **Section 39**, the patent may be revoked.\n\n### **Ground (o)**: Fraudulent Amendment\n- If leave to amend was obtained through fraud, the amendment can be invalidated, and this may lead to revocation.\n\n### **Ground (p)**: Wrong Mentioning of Biological Material Origin\n- The specification must accurately disclose the source or geographical origin of any biological material used; failure to do so can result in revocation.\n\n### **Ground (q)**: Indigenous Knowledge Anticipation\n- If the invention was anticipated by knowledge available within a local or indigenous community, it may be revoked.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nSection 64 provides a comprehensive list of grounds on which a patent may be revoked. These grounds are aimed at ensuring that only valid and legitimate inventions receive protection under the **Patents Act, 1970**. The process allows for both pre-grant and post-grant challenges to patents and ensures that the public interest is protected in cases where a patent or its exercise causes harm.\n\nIt is also important to note that revocation proceedings are not time-bound and can be initiated at any point during the life of the patent, offering a continued opportunity for interested parties to challenge the validity of a granted patent.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 64 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 64. Revocation of Power patents.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions contained in this Act, a patent\n> whether granted before or after the commencement of this Act, may,\n> be revoked on a petition of any person interested or of the Central\n> Government or on a counter-claim in a suit for infringement of the\n> patent by the High Court on any of the following grounds, that is to\n> say\n> (a) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification, was claimed in a valid claim of earlier\n> priority date contained in the complete specification of another\n> patent granted in India;\n> (b) that the patent was granted on the application of a person not\n> entitled under the provisions of this Act to apply therefor:\n> (c) that the patent was obtained wrongfully in contravention of the\n> rights of the petitioner or any person under or through whom he\n> claims;\n> (d) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act;\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is not new, having regard to what was\n>\n>  55\n>\n> publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority date of\n> the claim or to what was published in India or elsewhere in any of\n> the documents referred to in section 13:\n> (f) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious or does not involve any inventive\n> step, having regard to what was publicly known or publicly used in\n> India or what was published in India or elsewhere before the\n> priority date of the claim:\n> (g) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification, is not useful;\n> (h) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and fairly\n> describe the invention and the method by which it is to be\n> performed, that is to say, that the description of the method or the\n> instructions for the working of the invention as contained in the\n> complete specification are not by themselves sufficient to enable a\n> person in India possessing average skill in, and average knowledge\n> of, the art to which the invention relates, to work the invention,\n> or that it does not disclose the best method of performing it which\n> was known to the applicant for the patent and for which he was\n> entitled to claim, protection;\n> (i) that the scope of any claim of the complete specification is not\n> sufficiently and clearly defined or that any claim of the complete\n> specification is not fairly based on the matter disclosed in the\n> specification;\n> (j) that the patent was obtained on a false suggestion or\n> representation;\n> (k) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not patentable under this Act;\n> (l) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was secretly used in India, otherwise than as\n> mentioned in sub-section (3), before the priority date of the claim;\n> (m) that the applicant for the patent has failed to disclose to the\n> Controller the information required by section 8 or has furnished\n> information which in any material particular was false to his\n> knowledge;\n> (n) that the applicant contravened any direction for secrecy passed\n> under section 35 or made or caused to be made an application for the\n> grant of a patent outside India in contravention of section 39;\n> (o) that leave to amend the complete specification under section 57\n> or section 58 was obtained by fraud;\n> (p) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n>\n>  56\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 65 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 65. Revocation of patent or amendment of complete specification on\n> directions from Government in cases relating to atomic energy.\n> (1) Where at any time after grant of a patent, the Central\n> Government is satisfied that a patent is for an invention relating\n> to atomic energy for which no patent can be granted under sub-\n> section (1) of section 20 of the Atomic Energy Act, 1962 (33 of\n> 1962), it may direct the Controller to revoke the patent, and\n> thereupon the Controller, after giving notice, to the patentee and\n> every other person whose name has been entered in the register as\n> having an interest in the patent, and after giving them an\n> opportunity of being heard, may revoke the patent.\n>\n> (2) In any proceedings under sub-section (1), the Controller may\n> allow the patentee to amend the complete specification in such\n> manner as he considers necessary instead of revoking the patent.\n\n**Section 66 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 66. Revocation of patent in public interest.\n> Where the Central Government is of opinion that a patent or the mode\n> in which it is exercised is mischievous to the State or generally\n> prejudicial to the public, it may, after giving the patentee an\n> opportunity to be heard, make a declaration to that effect in the\n> Official Gazette and thereupon the patent shall be deemed to be\n> revoked.\n>\n>  58\n>\n> CHAPTER XIII REGISTER OF PATENTS\n\n**Rule 113 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 113. Registration of patent agents under section 126(2)\n> On receipt of an application for the registration of a person as a\n> patent agent under rule 112, the Controller may if he is satisfied\n> that the said person fulfils the conditions specified in sub-section\n>\n>  65\n>\n> (2) of section 126 enter his name in the register of patent agents.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the grounds for revocation of a patent under Section 64 of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the legal grounds on which a patent can be revoked under Section 64 of the Patents Act, 1970?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 64(1)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, a patent may be revoked by the Controller or High Court on various grounds. These include:  \n- (a) Prior claim in an earlier valid Indian patent with an earlier priority date.  \n- (b) Wrongful applicant who was not entitled under the Act to apply for the patent.  \n- (c) Patent obtained wrongfully, contravening the rights of another person.  \n- (d) Subject matter is not an invention within the meaning of the Act.  \n- (e) Lack of novelty in relation to prior art in India or elsewhere before the priority date.  \n- (f) Lack of inventive step, i.e., the invention is obvious to a person skilled in the art.  \n- (g) Non-utility or lack of usefulness of the invention.  \n- (h) Insufficient disclosure or failure to disclose the best method known to the applicant.  \n- (i) Unclear or unsupported claims in the specification.  \n- (j) Patent obtained through false suggestions or representations.  \n- (k) Subject matter not patentable under the Act.  \n- (l) Secret use of the invention in India before the priority date.  \n- (m) Non-disclosure of required information under **Section 8** or furnishing false information.  \n- (n) Contravention of secrecy directions under **Section 35** or applications for patent outside India in violation of **Section 39**.  \n- (o) Fraudulent amendment to the specification under **Sections 57** and **58**.  \n- (p) Wrong mention of the source or geographical origin of biological material used for the invention.  \n- (q) Anticipation by indigenous knowledge available in local communities.\n\nThese grounds are aimed at ensuring that only valid, novel, non-obvious, and useful inventions receive patent protection, as explained in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's)* and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 232.\n\n**Application:** If a company applies for a patent on an invention that was already disclosed in a prior Indian patent with an earlier priority date, the current patent may be revoked under **Section 64(1)(a)**. Similarly, if a patent is obtained through false information or fraud, it can be revoked under **Sections 64(1)(j)** and **(o)** respectively.\n\nAdditionally, **Section 66** of the Act allows revocation on public interest grounds, such as when a patent is deemed mischievous to the State or prejudicial to the public. This ensures that patents are not used in ways that harm national interests (*The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's)* and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 600).\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 64 of the *Patents Act, 1970* provides a comprehensive list of grounds for revoking a patent to ensure that only valid and legitimate inventions are protected. These grounds serve as safeguards against invalid or improperly obtained patents, maintaining the integrity of the patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook **Section 64(1)(q)**, which relates to indigenous knowledge anticipation, and **Section 66**, which allows revocation on public interest grounds. A complete answer must address all these aspects explicitly.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the legal implications of non-disclosure or furnishing false information under Section 8 of the Patents Act, 1970 in the context of revocation under Section 64(1)(m).**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2023)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the legal consequences of non-disclosure or false information under **Section 8** in relation to revocation under **Section 64(1)(m)**?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 64(1)(m)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, a patent may be revoked if the applicant failed to disclose information required by **Section 8**, or provided false information. **Section 8** mandates that the applicant must provide details about any prior applications for the same invention made in India or abroad, and any other relevant information as deemed necessary by the Controller.\n\nThis provision ensures transparency and fairness in the patent process. As explained in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's)* and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 141, failure to comply with **Section 8** can lead to revocation under **Section 64(1)(m)**.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a company applies for a patent on an invention but omits details of a prior application filed by another entity in India, as required by **Section 8**. The omission may be considered a failure to disclose the necessary information, and the patent could be revoked under **Section 64(1)(m)**.\n\nSimilarly, if the applicant knowingly provides false information about the invention’s novelty or prior art, this would also fall within the scope of **Section 64(1)(m)**. In such a case, the Controller may revoke the patent to correct the misrepresentation and maintain the integrity of the patent system.\n\n**Conclusion:** Failure to disclose required information under **Section 8**, or furnishing false details, can lead to revocation under **Section 64(1)(m)**. This provision acts as a safeguard against dishonesty in the application process, ensuring that only valid patents are granted and maintained.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse **Section 64(1)(m)** with other grounds such as **(j)** (false suggestion) or **(p)** (wrong mention of biological material). It is crucial to distinguish between these provisions clearly in the answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. How does Section 64 of the Patents Act, 1970 ensure that only valid and legitimate patents remain in force?**  \n*8 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2021, 2023)*\n\n**Issue:** What role does **Section 64** play in ensuring the validity of patents?\n\n**Rule:** **Section 64(1)** of the *Patents Act, 1970* lists a comprehensive set of grounds for revocation. These include lack of novelty (**e**), non-obviousness (**f**), utility (**g**), proper disclosure (**h**), and compliance with secrecy directions (**n**). The inclusion of such detailed grounds ensures that only valid and legitimate inventions are granted patent protection.\n\nAs stated in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's)* and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 232, **Section 64** is subservient to other provisions under the Act, ensuring consistency with other legal frameworks. It also aligns with international standards by incorporating requirements such as disclosure of prior art (**e**) and indigenous knowledge anticipation (**q**).\n\n**Application:** If a patent is granted for an invention that was already publicly known in India before the priority date, it can be revoked under **Section 64(1)(e)**. Similarly, if the specification does not disclose the best method of performing the invention, as required by **Section 64(1)(h)**, the patent may also be revoked.\n\nThese provisions ensure that only inventions meeting the criteria of novelty, non-obviousness, and utility are protected under the Act.\n\n**Conclusion:** **Section 64** ensures the validity of patents by providing a detailed list of revocation grounds. This helps maintain the integrity of the patent system by preventing invalid or improperly obtained patents from being enforced.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to mention that **Section 64** is not independent and must be read in conjunction with other sections, such as **Section 8**, **35**, and **39**. A complete answer should highlight this interdependence.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Revocation of Patents — Section 64 Grounds\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 64 | **CHAIR** | CHAIR lifts a gavel, signaling who may apply before the court. |\n| Sec. 65 | **JAIL** | JAIL bars slam shut, locking out invalid patents due to obviousness. |\n| Sec. 66 | **JUDGE** | JUDGE slams a book, dismissing patents from wrongful obtaining. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **64-66** - revocation\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### Monsanto Technology LLC v. Nuziveedu Seeds Ltd.\n_(2019) 3 SCC 381_\n\n- **Hook** - Monsanto's seeds jailed in court, needing trial for validity.\n- **Held** - Patent validity requires trial on evidence, not summary decision.\n- **Use in** - Patent validity, summary decisions, trial necessity\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Who may apply and before whom - which provision?  (picture: CHAIR)\n2. Principal grounds — prior claiming, not an invention, obviousness, insufficiency, false suggestion - which provision?  (picture: JAIL)\n3. Section 8 non-compliance and wrongful obtaining - which provision?  (picture: JUDGE)\n4. Which case decided: Patent validity requires trial on evidence, not summary decision.\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 64(1)(a) | Patent revoked if same claim exists in earlier patent | Prior claim overlap\n- Section 64(1)(b) | Wrong applicant can't hold patent | False owner\n- Section 64(1)(c) | Obtained by violating someone's rights | Stolen invention\n- Section 64(1)(d) | Not an invention under the Act | Not a real invention\n- Section 64(1)(e) | Invention not new before priority date | Old idea\n- Section 64(1)(f) | Invention is obvious to experts | Obvious trick\n- Section 64(1)(g) | Invention has no practical use | Useless gadget\n- Section 64(1)(h) | Specs not clear enough for working | Foggy instructions\n- Section 64(1)(i) | Claims too vague or unsupported | Vague claims\n- Section 64(1)(j) | Patent obtained by lying | Lying to get patent\n- Section 64(1)(k) | Subject not patentable under law | Illegal subject\n- Section 64(1)(l) | Secret use in India before priority date | Hidden use\n- Section 64(1)(m) | Failed to disclose or gave false info | False disclosure\n- Section 64(1)(n) | Breach of secrecy rules or foreign filing | Broken rules\n- Section 64(1)(o) | Fraud in amending specs | Faked amendment\n- Section 64(1)(p) | Wrong biological origin mentioned | Misplaced source\n- Section 64(1)(q) | Invention anticipated by indigenous knowledge | Indigenous clue\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Monsanto Technology LLC v. Nuziveedu Seeds Ltd., (2019) 3 SCC 381 | Patent validity needs full trial, not summary | Trial needed\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing revocation grounds with invalidity; they are different processes  \n- Always cite the exact section number and wording from the Patents Act  \n- Structure answers by listing each ground clearly with its corresponding Section 64(1) subclause","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, there lived a group of inventors who had recently secured several patents for their groundbreaking work in biotechnology. Among them was Ravi, whose company, **BioNova**, had developed a new seed technology that promised to increase crop yields dramatically.\n\nOne day, while working late at his office on Necklace Road, Ravi received a call from his colleague, Priya, who worked with another firm called **GreenGenics**. She told him about an upcoming meeting where the Central Government would be reviewing several patents, including BioNova’s. “We need to be ready,” she said.\n\nRavi was confident in their work until he heard of a petition that had been filed under **Section 64(1)(e)** of the Patents Act, 1970 — the claim that the invention was not new. The petitioner was none other than **Dr. Sheetal Chopra**, who had previously worked with BioNova but had since left to start her own firm. Her petition stated that similar technology had been publicly used in India before the priority date of Ravi’s claim.\n\nThe next day, a team from the Controller’s office visited BioNova and informed them that they would need to provide evidence proving the novelty of their invention. At the same time, **Monsanto Technology LLC v. Nuziveedu Seeds Ltd., (2019) 3 SCC 381** was being referenced in court as a precedent — it stated that patent validity raising complex questions of fact could not be decided summarily and had to be tried on evidence.\n\nRavi and his team scrambled to gather documents, including research papers from the past decade. They found that while similar concepts had been explored, their method of seed modification was unique and had never been publicly disclosed before the priority date. This became their defense under **Section 64(1)(e)** — that the invention was indeed new.\n\nHowever, another challenge arose when a competitor, **AgriTech Solutions**, filed a petition under **Section 64(1)(m)**, claiming that BioNova had failed to disclose information required by **Section 8** of the Patents Act. This led to an investigation by the Controller’s office into whether BioNova had omitted any critical details in their application.\n\nThe situation grew more complex when a local university, **Osmania University**, raised concerns under **Section 64(1)(q)**, stating that the knowledge used in the invention was anticipated by indigenous communities and had not been properly acknowledged. This forced Ravi to revisit his team’s research notes and consult with experts on indigenous knowledge.\n\nIn the end, after a lengthy review process, the Controller ruled in favor of BioNova, recognizing the novelty of their work under **Section 64(1)(e)** and confirming that all disclosures had been made properly. However, the Controller also ordered BioNova to amend their specification under **Section 57** to include proper acknowledgment of indigenous knowledge.\n\nRavi was relieved but reminded by his lawyer, Mr. Rao, of the importance of **Section 66**, which allows for revocation on public interest grounds if a patent or its use is deemed harmful to the State or the public. “Even with all this,” said Mr. Rao, “we must always be cautious about how our inventions are used.”\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 64(1)(e) | That the invention is not new | A patent may be revoked if the invention was publicly known or used before the priority date. |\n| Section 64(1)(m) | Failure to disclose information required by Section 8 | A patent can be revoked if the applicant failed to disclose necessary information or provided false information. |\n| Section 64(1)(q) | Anticipation by indigenous knowledge | A patent may be invalidated if it was anticipated by knowledge available within a local or indigenous community. |\n| Section 57 | Amendment of specification | Leave to amend can be obtained under this section, but only if done honestly and not through fraud. |\n| Section 66 | Revocation on public interest grounds | The Central Government may revoke a patent if its exercise is deemed harmful to the State or prejudicial to the public. |\n| Monsanto Technology LLC v. Nuziveedu Seeds Ltd., (2019) 3 SCC 381 | Patent validity cannot be decided summarily | Complex questions of fact regarding patent validity must be tried on evidence, not decided without a full trial. |","memory":"### MEMORY TECHNIQUES FOR **Revocation of Patents — Section 64 Grounds**  \n*(Based on: The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 231–232; and verified case law)*  \n\n---\n\n### 1. **Mnemonic for Grounds of Revocation (Section 64(1))**  \n> **\"A B C D E F G H I J K L M N O P Q\"** — but this is too long! Instead, use:  \n\n#### ✅ **\"F.A.I.R. P.L.E.A.S.E.\"**  \n- **F**: False suggestion or representation (64(1)(j))  \n- **A**: Anticipation (64(1)(e), (f), (q))  \n- **I**: Inadequate disclosure (64(1)(h))  \n- **R**: Rights of others violated (64(1)(c))  \n\n#### ✅ **\"P.L.E.A.S.E.\"** (continued)  \n- **P**: Prior claim or non-newness (64(1)(a), (e))  \n- **L**: Lack of inventive step (64(1)(f))  \n- **E**: Not useful (64(1)(g))  \n- **A**: Applicant not entitled (64(1)(b))  \n- **S**: Secrecy violations or false info (64(1)(m), (n))  \n- **E**: Other grounds like non-patentable subject matter (64(1)(d), (k))  \n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-anchor for Section 64**  \n> **\"Section 64: 'Sixty-Four' – Like a courtroom scene, where many parties come to challenge the patent.\"**  \n- Picture a courtroom with **64 seats**, each representing a different ground of revocation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**  \n\n#### ❗ **Revocation vs. Opposition (Section 25 vs. Section 64)**  \n> **\"Opposition is quick and limited in time, but Revocation can happen anytime during the patent’s life.\"**\n\n#### ❗ **Pre-grant opposition vs. Post-grant opposition**  \n> **\"Pre-grant is before grant; post-grant is after grant. In pre-grant, the Controller's decision cannot be appealed, while in post-grant it can.\"** *(Cited from Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 231)*\n\n#### ❗ **Revocation under Section 64 vs. Revocation under Section 66 (public interest)**  \n> **\"Section 64 is about legal grounds; Section 66 is about public interest or mischief to the state.\"** *(Cited from Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 600)*\n\n#### ❗ **Non-working vs. Non-patentable subject matter**  \n> **\"Non-working relates to failure to use the invention (Section 85); non-patentable is about what cannot be patented at all (Section 3).\"**\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are tailored for **Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)** and draw from verified sections and case law.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Prior claiming occurs if the invention was claimed in a valid, earlier patent. (Section 64(1)(a))","question":"What is the principal ground for revocation based on prior claiming?"},{"answer":"Yes, but decisions are handled separately by the Controller and High Court. (Section 25(2) and 64)","question":"Can a defendant file both a post-grant opposition and a revocation petition?"},{"answer":"To prevent conflicting decisions, the High Court handles counter-claims. (Section 104)","question":"Why can't a defendant file a revocation petition after a counter-claim in an infringement suit?"},{"answer":"The invention must meet Section 2(j)'s criteria; failing that, it's not an invention. (Section 64(1)(d))","question":"What is the ground for revocation based on non-invention?"},{"answer":"To the High Court under Section 104. (Case example: SERGI Transformers)","question":"Where is a counter-claim for revocation transferred?"},{"answer":"Raising invalidity as a defense doesn't equate to seeking revocation. (Case: Fabcon Corporation)","question":"Why is a defendant's defense of invalidity not a counter-claim?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 66 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Revocation of patent in public interest”.","options":["Section 119","Section 106","Section 66","Section 32"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Revocation of patent in public interest?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 66 is headed “Revocation of patent in public interest”.","options":["Priority dates of claims of a complete specification","Officers and employees not to furnish information, etc","Evidence of entries, documents, etc","Revocation of patent in public interest"],"question":"Section 66 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 64 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Revocation of Power patents”.","options":["Section 131","Section 86","Section 64","Section 25"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Revocation of Power patents?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 64 is headed “Revocation of Power patents”.","options":["Priority dates of claims of a complete specification","Licensing of related patents","Revocation of Power patents","What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within"],"question":"Section 64 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 66 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Form of making a request under section 28(2)”.","options":["Rule 66","Rule 57","Rule 31","Rule 19N"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Form of making a request under section 28(2)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 66 is headed “Form of making a request under section 28(2)”.","options":["Form and manner in which statements required under section","Form of making a request under section 28(2)","Application for termination of compulsory licence under","Form of application to be made under section 28(7)"],"question":"Rule 66 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 65 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Revocation of patent or amendment of complete specification on”.","options":["Section 107A","Section 65","Section 123","Section 12"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Revocation of patent or amendment of complete specification on?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 65 is headed “Revocation of patent or amendment of complete specification on”.","options":["Refusal or failure to supply information","Falsification of entries in register, etc","Jurisdiction","Revocation of patent or amendment of complete specification on"],"question":"Section 65 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The source text states: '(l) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete specification was secretly used in India, otherwise than as mentioned in sub-section (3), before the priority date of the claim;'","options":["(l) of Section 64","(k) of Section 64","(j) of Section 64","(i) of Section 64"],"question":"Under which provision can a patent be revoked if the invention was secretly used in India before the priority date of the claim?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The source text states: 'Where the Central Government is of opinion that a patent or the mode in which it is exercised is mischievous to the State or generally prejudicial to the public, it may... make a declaration to that effect in the Official Gazette and thereupon the patent shall be deemed to be revoked.'","options":["Section 66","Section 65","Section 64","Section 63"],"question":"Under which provision can the Central Government revoke a patent if it is deemed mischievous to the State or prejudicial to the public?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The source text states: '(h) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and fairly describe the invention and the method by which it is to be performed...','","options":["(h) of Section 64","(g) of Section 64","(f) of Section 64","(e) of Section 64"],"question":"Which of the following is a ground for revocation under Section 64 if the complete specification does not sufficiently describe the invention?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The source text states: 'In any proceedings under sub-section (1), the Controller may allow the patentee to amend the complete specification in such manner as he considers necessary instead of revoking the patent.'","options":["Allow the patentee to amend the complete specification","Cancel the patent without notice","Refer the matter to the High Court","Impose a fine on the patentee"],"question":"Under Section 65, what may the Controller do instead of revoking a patent related to atomic energy?"}]},"s1p_29":{"notes":"# Infringement — What Counts, and the Doctrine of Equivalents  \n*(Study Notes for Patent Agent Examination - Papers I, II and Viva)*  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Definition of Infringement  \nInfringement of a patent occurs when someone, without the consent of the patentee, uses or exploits the patented invention in any manner that falls within the scope of the claims defined in the patent specification. This includes making, using, selling, offering for sale, importing, or otherwise dealing with the patented invention.  \n\n**Relevant Sections from Bare Act Block:**  \n- **Section 46(1)**: Defines the extent and effect of a patent. It states that the patentee has the exclusive right to make, use, sell, offer for sale, and import the patented invention in India.  \n- **Section 104**: Deals with jurisdiction in suits concerning infringement.  \n\n**Explanation from Textbook:**  \nInfringement is determined based on whether the accused product or process falls within the scope of the patent claims. The burden of proof lies on the plaintiff (patentee) to show that the defendant’s actions are infringing. This is outlined in **(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 325)**.  \n\n---\n\n## 2. Key Concepts of Infringement  \n### a) Literal Infringement  \nLiteral infringement occurs when the accused product or process contains all the elements explicitly stated in the patent claims. Each element must be present in the same form and function as described in the claim.  \n\n**Relevant Section from Bare Act Block:**  \n- **Section 107A**: Certain acts not to be considered as infringement. This section provides exceptions, such as acts done for experimental purposes or use of a patented invention for non-commercial purposes.  \n\n### b) Non-Literal Infringement (Doctrine of Equivalents)  \nNon-literal infringement arises when the accused product or process does not literally replicate each element of the patent claim but performs substantially the same function in the same way and achieves the same result. This is governed by the **doctrine of equivalents**.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 48 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 48. Rights of patentees.\n> Subject to the other provisions contained in this Act and the\n> conditions specified in section 47, a patent granted under this Act\n>\n>  43\n>\n> shall confer upon the patentee\n> (a) where the subject matter of the patent is a product, the\n> exclusive right to prevent third parties, who do not have his\n> consent, from the act of making, using, offering for sale, selling\n> or importing for those purposes that product in India;\n> (b) where the subject matter of the patent is a process, the\n> exclusive right to prevent third parties, who do not have his\n> consent, from the act of using that process, and from the act of\n> using, offering for sale, selling or importing for those purposes\n> the product obtained directly by that process in India.\n\n**Section 104 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 104. Jurisdiction.\n> No suit for a declaration under section 105 or for any relief under\n> section 106 or for infringement of a patent shall be instituted in\n> any court inferior to a district court having jurisdiction to try\n> the suit:\n> Provided that where a counter-claim for revocation of the patent is\n> made by the defendant, the suit, along with the counter-claim, shall\n> be transferred to the High Court for decision.\n\n**Section 108 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 108. Reliefs in suits for infringement.\n> (1) The reliefs which a court may grant in any suit for infringement\n> include an injunction (subject to such terms, if any, as the court\n> thinks fit) and, at the option of the plaintiff, either damages or\n> an account of profits.\n>\n> (2) The court may also order that the goods which are found to be\n> infringing and materials and implement, the predominant use of which\n> is in the creation of infringing goods shall be seized, forfeited or\n> destroyed, as the court deems fit under the circumstances of the\n> case without payment of any compensation.\n>\n>  87\n>\n> 109. Right of exclusive licensee to take proceedings against\n> infringement.\n> (1) The holder of an exclusive licence shall have the like right as\n> the patentee to institute a suit in respect of any infringement of\n> the patent committed after the date of the licence, and in awarding\n> damages or an account of profits or granting any other relief in any\n> such suit the court shall take into consideration any loss suffered\n> or likely to be suffered by the exclusive licensee as such or, as\n> the case may be, the profits earned by means of the infringement so\n> far as it constitutes an infringement of the rights of the exclusive\n> licensee as such.\n>\n> (2) In any suit for infringement of a patent by the holder of an\n> exclusive licence under sub-section (1), the patentee shall, unless\n> he has joined as a plaintiff in the suit, be added as a defendant,\n> but a patentee so added as defendant shall not be liable for any\n> costs unless he enters an appearance and takes part in the\n> proceedings.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is infringement of a patent and what are the conditions for it to be considered as such?**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2014, 2017, 2020)*\n\n**Issue:** The determination of whether an act constitutes infringement under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule:** Infringement occurs when a third party uses or exploits a patented invention without the consent of the patentee in any manner that falls within the scope of the claims defined in the specification. This includes acts such as making, using, selling, offering for sale, importing, or dealing with the patented invention (Section 46(1)). The burden of proof lies on the plaintiff to show that the defendant’s actions fall within the scope of the patent claims (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 325**). Infringement is not absolute; certain acts such as experimental use or non-commercial purposes are exempted under Section 107A.\n\nAdditionally, the **doctrine of equivalents** allows for a finding of infringement even if the accused product does not literally replicate all elements of the claim but performs substantially the same function in the same way and achieves the same result. This doctrine is used to prevent evasion through minor modifications (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 325**). In **F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd., 2015 SCC OnLine Del 13619**, the court examined the application of this doctrine alongside challenges under Section 3(d), which relates to the patentability of pharmaceuticals.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a company X produces and sells a drug that is chemically similar to a patented drug but with minor structural differences. To determine infringement, we must first examine whether the claims of the patent include elements that are substantially equivalent in function, way, and result. If the court finds that these similarities meet the criteria under the doctrine of equivalents, then X’s act would be considered an infringement even if it does not literally match all claim elements.\n\n**Conclusion:** Infringement is determined based on whether the accused product or process falls within the scope of the patent claims, either through literal replication or via the doctrine of equivalents. The burden of proof lies with the plaintiff to demonstrate this, and certain exceptions exist under Section 107A.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to distinguish between literal infringement and non-literal infringement governed by the doctrine of equivalents. It is essential to mention both types and explain how the latter allows for a broader interpretation of patent claims.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the concept of the Doctrine of Equivalents in the context of patent law.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2016, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** The application and legal basis for the doctrine of equivalents under Indian patent law.\n\n**Rule:** The **Doctrine of Equivalents** is a principle used to determine infringement in cases where the accused product or process does not literally replicate all elements of a patent claim but performs substantially the same function in the same way and achieves the same result. This concept prevents infringers from circumventing patent protection through minor modifications (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 325**).\n\nIn **F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd., 2015 SCC OnLine Del 13619**, the court examined the doctrine of equivalents in conjunction with a Section 3(d) challenge, highlighting how it can be applied to determine whether an alternative compound is functionally equivalent.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a patented drug contains Compound A and a company Y produces a drug that uses Compound B. If Compound B functions in the same way as Compound A and achieves the same therapeutic result, even though it is chemically distinct, then under the doctrine of equivalents, Y’s product may be considered an infringement if it meets the functional equivalence test.\n\n**Conclusion:** The doctrine of equivalents allows for a broader interpretation of patent claims to prevent circumvention through minor changes. It is not absolute and must be applied carefully in light of the specific facts and the intent behind the patent.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse the doctrine of equivalents with literal infringement, or they fail to explain its purpose clearly. Emphasize that it is a tool for preventing evasion and should be used judiciously.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the reliefs available in a suit for infringement under the Patents Act?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2015, 2019)*\n\n**Issue:** The legal remedies available to a patentee or exclusive licensee in a suit for infringement.\n\n**Rule:** Under Section 108 of the Patents Act, reliefs available include an injunction, damages, or an account of profits. Injunctions may be granted subject to terms deemed fit by the court. Additionally, the court may order that infringing goods and related materials be seized, forfeited, or destroyed without compensation (**The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 325**).\n\nSection 109 grants exclusive licensees the right to institute suits for infringement as if they were the patentee. In such cases, the court considers losses or profits suffered by the exclusive licensee in awarding damages.\n\n**Application:** If a company Z is found to have infringed on a patented invention, the court may grant an injunction to prevent further use of the infringing product. It could also award damages based on the loss incurred by the patentee or order an account of profits made by Z. The infringing goods may be destroyed at the discretion of the court.\n\n**Conclusion:** Reliefs available in a suit for infringement include injunctive relief, damages, account of profits, and destruction of infringing goods. Exclusive licensees have similar rights to the patentee under Section 109.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the specific provisions of Sections 108 and 109 when discussing reliefs in infringement suits. It is crucial to mention both sections and their implications clearly.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Infringement — What Counts, and the Doctrine of Equivalents\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 48 | **ROOF** | ROOF stretches to cover equivalent spaces, not just the literal structure. |\n| Sec. 104 | **TEASER** | TEASER peels back layers to reveal the pith and marrow of a patent. |\n| Sec. 108 | **TIE-SOFA** | TIE-SOFA is the first thing you sit on when entering a courtroom. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **43-53** - grant, rights, term of patent\n- **104-115** - suits for infringement, defences, reliefs\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd.\n_2015 SCC OnLine Del 13619_\n\n- **Held** - Infringement and the validity defence tried together; claim construction and the Section 3(d) challenge examined in detail.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Literal infringement and infringement by equivalents - which provision?  (picture: ROOF)\n2. Pith and marrow / purposive construction - which provision?  (picture: TEASER)\n3. Claim construction as the first step - which provision?  (picture: TIE-SOFA)\n4. Which case decided: Infringement and the validity defence tried together; claim construction and the Section 3(d) challenge examined in detail.\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 46(1) | Exclusive rights of patentee to make, use, sell, etc. | Patentee's golden shield\n- Section 104 | Jurisdiction for infringement suits in district court | Court level rulebook\n- Section 107A | Exceptions like experimental use not infringing | Lab experiment loophole\n- Section 48 | Rights of patentees on products and processes | Product and process lock\n- Section 108 | Reliefs: injunction, damages, seizure of goods | Legal weapon kit\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd. (2015) | Infringement and validity defence together | Drug patent clash case\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing literal infringement with non-literal equivalents\n- Use claim construction carefully, especially under Section 3(d)\n- Always cite the relevant sections when discussing reliefs in infringement suits","storyEN":"In Hyderabad, in a quiet neighborhood near Charminar, there lived a man named Ravi, who ran a small but thriving tech startup called \"InnovateTech.\" One day, he received a letter that would change everything — a lawsuit from F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd., accusing him of patent infringement. The case was titled *F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd.*, and it had been decided in 2015 (SCC OnLine Del 13619), though Ravi didn’t know that yet.\n\nThe lawsuit claimed that InnovateTech’s new drug, \"NeoCure,\" infringed on a patent held by Roche for a similar compound. The case was not just about the drug itself but also about whether the use of it in a slightly different form would still count as infringement under **Section 46(1)** — which said that the patentee had exclusive rights to make, use, sell, or import the invention in India.\n\nRavi’s lawyer, Meera, knew that the key was not just in what NeoCure did but also in how it compared to Roche's patented formula. She studied **Section 107A**, which said certain acts like experimental use wouldn’t count as infringement — a small lifeline for Ravi if they could prove their drug wasn't for commercial purposes.\n\nBut she also knew about the **Doctrine of Equivalents** — an idea that even if NeoCure didn’t *literally* match Roche’s patent, it might still be considered infringing if it did the same job in a similar way. This was the crux of the **non-literal infringement**, and it was governed by the **Doctrine of Equivalents**.\n\nRavi had hired twenty-one engineers to test NeoCure against Roche's patented compound. Each one checked whether the drug performed the same function, used the same method, or achieved the same result — all under **Section 48**, which defined a patentee’s rights over both products and processes.\n\nBut Meera also knew that if they were going to win, they had to show that Roche's patent was invalid under **Section 3(d)** — something that had been tested in *F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd.*. In that case, the court had ruled that a patent could be revoked if it didn't meet the standards of novelty or inventive step.\n\nRavi’s team found an issue with Roche's patent: their compound was not truly novel when compared to other drugs in the same class. This meant that **Section 104** — which governed jurisdiction for infringement suits — might not even apply, because if the patent were invalid, there could be no infringement.\n\nThe case went to court, and Ravi’s team presented their findings under **Section 108**, where they asked for an injunction and damages. They argued that NeoCure was a different product in function and method, and thus did not fall within the scope of Roche's patent claims — even if it had some similarities.\n\nBut then came the twist: the judge applied the **Doctrine of Equivalents** and said that although NeoCure wasn’t identical to Roche’s compound, its effect was so similar that it still counted as infringement. This meant that **Section 46(1)** would apply even in this case — unless Ravi could prove that his drug had a unique function or method.\n\nHowever, the judge also referenced *F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd.*, where the court had ruled that if a patent lacked novelty, it was invalid and thus not infringing. This gave Ravi hope — he could argue that Roche’s patent wasn’t valid in the first place.\n\nIn the end, Ravi won his case because his team had shown that NeoCure was not only different but also more effective than Roche's drug, meeting the requirements of **Section 3(d)**. The court ruled that Roche's patent was invalid and thus no infringement occurred under **Section 104**.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|-----------|\n| Twenty-one engineers | Section 48 | A patentee has exclusive rights over both products and processes. |\n| Section 46(1) | Section 46(1) | The patentee has the exclusive right to make, use, sell, offer for sale, or import the patented invention in India. |\n| Section 107A | Section 107A | Certain acts like experimental use are not considered infringement. |\n| Doctrine of Equivalents | Doctrine of Equivalents | Non-literal infringement occurs when a product or process performs substantially the same function in the same way and achieves the same result as the patented invention. |\n| Section 104 | Section 104 | Infringement suits must be filed in a district court, with counter-claims for revocation transferred to the High Court. |\n| Section 108 | Section 108 | Courts may grant injunctions and damages or an account of profits in infringement cases. |\n| *F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd.* | F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd. v. Cipla Ltd., 2015 SCC OnLine Del 13619 | A patent may be invalid if it lacks novelty or inventive step, which can affect infringement claims under Section 3(d). |\n| Section 48 | Section 48 | Patentees have exclusive rights to prevent third parties from making, using, selling, etc., the patented invention in India. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for \"Infringement — What Counts, and the Doctrine of Equivalents\"**\n\n---\n\n### **1. Acronym/Mnemonic for Elements of Infringement:**\n**\"P.I.N.\"**  \n- **P**roduct/Process (the subject matter being infringed)  \n- **I**dentical or Equivalent (whether it’s literal or under the doctrine of equivalents)  \n- **N**ot Exempt (whether the act falls under exemptions, e.g., Section 107A)\n\n---\n\n### **2. Number-Anchors for Relevant Sections:**\n\n- **Section 45**: *“Date of Patent”* — Imagine a patent certificate being dated on a calendar. This section says you can’t sue for infringement before the patent is granted.\n  \n- **Section 84**: *“Compulsory Licences”* — Picture a license being handed over by a government official. This section allows compulsory licenses under certain conditions.\n\n- **Section 107A**: *“Acts Not Considered Infringement”* — Think of a shield blocking an attack. These are acts that are exempt from infringement, like using the invention for research or regulatory purposes.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Literal vs. Non-Literal Infringement**  \n  *One is about exact matches; the other allows equivalents.*  \n  → Literal requires each element to match exactly. Non-literal (equivalents) allows similar functions, even if not identical.\n\n- **Injunction vs. Damages**  \n  *An injunction stops an action; damages compensate for loss.*  \n  → Injunction is a court order to stop infringement. Damages are monetary compensation for the harm caused.\n\n- **Exclusive Licensee vs. Patentee Rights**  \n  *The licensee can sue in certain cases, but not always as freely as the patentee.*  \n  → The exclusive licensee can sue under Section 109 and 110 if the patentee doesn’t act within two months.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are tailored to help you recall key concepts from the **Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)** using the relevant sections and case law provided.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Literal infringement occurs when each claim element is exactly matched by the accused product. Courts use the literal language of claims to determine this.","question":"What is literal infringement?"},{"answer":"A court may grant an injunction and either damages or an account of profits, as per Section 108.","question":"What reliefs can a court grant in an infringement suit?"},{"answer":"To extend claim scope beyond literal language, ensuring broader protection of the invention's essence.","question":"Why do Indian courts use purposive construction?"},{"answer":"It allows infringement if the accused product performs the same function in the same way, even with minor differences.","question":"What is the doctrine of equivalents?"},{"answer":"The defendant must file a counter-claim seeking revocation, which removes the District Court's jurisdiction.","question":"What is required for a counter-claim to revoke a patent?"},{"answer":"Proper construction ensures clear claim scope, preventing ambiguous interpretations during litigation.","question":"Why is claim construction critical for patent applicants?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 48 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Rights of patentees”.","options":["Section 133","Section 13","Section 28","Section 48"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Rights of patentees?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 48 is headed “Rights of patentees”.","options":["Liability for contravention of section 35 or section 39","Rights of patentees","Compulsory licences","Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical"],"question":"Section 48 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 108 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Particulars to be contained in the register of patent”.","options":["Rule 24A","Rule 104","Rule 108","Rule 123"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Particulars to be contained in the register of patent?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 108 is headed “Particulars to be contained in the register of patent”.","options":["Hearing before the Controller to be in public in certain","Permission for making patent application outside India","Particulars to be contained in the register of patent","Procedure for the hearing of proceedings under section 51"],"question":"Rule 108 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 108 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Reliefs in suits for infringement”.","options":["Section 108","Section 157","Section 72","Section 20"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Reliefs in suits for infringement?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 108 is headed “Reliefs in suits for infringement”.","options":["Savings in respect of other persons authorised to act as","Reliefs in suits for infringement","Convention applications","Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain"],"question":"Section 108 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 104 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Jurisdiction”.","options":["Section 29","Section 104","Section 2","Section 36"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Jurisdiction?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 104 is headed “Jurisdiction”.","options":["Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or","Convention countries","Rectification of register by High Court","Jurisdiction"],"question":"Section 104 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 104 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Manner of application for inclusion in the roll of”.","options":["Rule 129","Rule 56","Rule 104","Rule 70A"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Manner of application for inclusion in the roll of?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 104 is headed “Manner of application for inclusion in the roll of”.","options":["Inspection of documents related to grant of patent","Manner of publication of the revocation order","Request for withdrawal","Manner of application for inclusion in the roll of"],"question":"Rule 104 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The source text states that a patentee has the exclusive right to prevent third parties from making, using, offering for sale, selling, or importing the product in India without consent.","options":["The right to prevent third parties from importing the product in India without consent.","The right to prevent third parties from using the product in India without consent.","The right to prevent third parties from manufacturing the product in India without consent.","The right to prevent third parties from exporting the product in India without consent."],"question":"Under Section 48 of the Patents Act, 1970, what exclusive right does a patentee have regarding a product?"}]},"s1p_30":{"notes":"# Defences and Exceptions — Bolar, Parallel Import, Research Use  \nThese are important exceptions to patent rights under the **Patents Act, 1970**, which allow certain acts that would otherwise be considered infringement. They are designed to balance the interests of patentees with public policy considerations such as access to medicines, research innovation and international trade.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Bolar Exception  \n### Definition:  \nThe **Bolar exception** allows the use of a patented invention for the purpose of conducting studies or trials necessary for obtaining marketing approval for a generic drug before the expiry of the patent.\n\n### Key Concepts:\n- This is not an infringement if it is carried out solely for the purpose of **obtaining regulatory approval**.\n- It promotes competition in the pharmaceutical sector and reduces delays in the availability of affordable medicines after patent expiry.\n\n### Relevant Section:  \nSection **107A(a)** of the Patents Act, 1970.  \n\n> **\"Any act of making, constructing, using, selling or importing a patented invention solely for uses reasonably related to the development and submission of information required under any law for the time being in force, in India, or in a country other than India, that regulates the manufacture, construction, use, sale or import of any product shall not be considered as an infringement of patent rights.\"**\n\n### Textbook Explanation:\nAccording to **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 87**, the Bolar exception is a critical provision that facilitates the entry of generic drugs into the market by allowing manufacturers to conduct necessary research and trials without infringing on the patent holder’s rights. This aligns with global practices and encourages innovation in drug development while ensuring public access to medicines at reasonable prices.\n\n### Verified Cases:\n**(verify this case with a case-law database)**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Parallel Import  \n### Definition:  \n**Parallel import**, also known as **gray market importation**, refers to the import of genuine patented products from another country where the product is sold without the consent of the patent holder in that country.\n\n### Key Concepts:\n- This exception allows the import of a patented product into India if it has been lawfully placed on the market in another country, provided that such import does not harm the interests of the patentee.\n- It promotes competition and reduces prices by allowing the entry of cheaper versions of the same drug or product.\n\n### Relevant Section:  \nSection **107A(b)** of the Patents Act, 1970.  \n\n> **\"Importation of patented products by any person from a person who is duly authorised under the law to produce and sell or distribute the product shall not be considered as an infringement of patent rights.\"**\n\n### Textbook Explanation:\nAs per **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 87**, parallel import provisions are crucial for maintaining a competitive market and ensuring that patented products do not become monopolistic in price. It allows the entry of genuine goods into the Indian market from other jurisdictions where they may be sold at lower prices.\n\n### Verified Cases:\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 107 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 107. Defences, etc., in suit for infringement.\n> (1) In any suit for infringement of a patent, every ground on which\n> it may be revoked under section 64 shall be available as a ground\n> for defence.\n>\n> (2) In any suit for infringement of a patent by the making, using or\n> importation of any machine, apparatus or other article or by the\n> using of any process or by the importation, use or distribution of\n> any medicine or drug, it shall be a ground for defence that such\n> making, using, importation or distribution is in accordance with any\n> one or more of the conditions specified in section 47.\n\n**Section 107A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 107A. Certain acts not to be considered as infringement. For the\n> purposes of this Act,\n> (a) any act of making, constructing, using, selling or importing a\n> patented invention solely for uses reasonably related to the\n> development and submission of information required under any law for\n> the time being in force, in India, or in a country other than India,\n> that regulates the manufacture, construction, use, sale or import of\n> any product;\n> (b) importation of patented products by any person from a person who\n> is duly authorised under the law to produce and sell or distribute\n> the product,\n> shall not be considered as an infringement of patent rights.\n\n**Section 47 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 47. Grant of patents to be subject to certain conditions.\n> The grant of a patent under this Act shall be subject to the\n> condition that\n> (1) any machine, apparatus or other article in respect of which the\n> patent is granted or any article made by using a process in respect\n> of which the patent is granted, may be imported or made by or on\n> behalf of the Government for the purpose merely of its own use;\n>\n> (2) any process in respect of which the patent is granted may be\n> used by or on behalf of the Government for the purpose merely of its\n> own use;\n>\n> (3) any machine, apparatus or other article in respect of which the\n> patent is granted or any article made by the use of the process in\n> respect of which the patent is granted, may be made or used, and any\n> process in respect of which the patent is granted may be used, by\n> any person, for the purpose merely of experiment or research\n> including the imparting of instructions to pupils; and\n>\n> (4) in the case of a patent in respect of any medicine or drug, the\n> medicine or drug may be imported by the Government for the purpose\n> merely of its own use or for distribution in any dispensary,\n> hospital or other medical institution maintained by or on behalf of\n> the Government or any other dispensary, hospital or other medical\n> institution which the Central Government may, having regard to the\n> public service that such dispensary, hospital or medical institution\n> renders, specify in this behalf by notification in the Official\n> Gazette.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the Bolar exception under the Patents Act, 1970 and its significance in promoting generic drug competition.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** The legal basis for the Bolar exception and its role in enabling the timely entry of generic drugs into the market without infringing patent rights.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 107A(a)** of the Patents Act, 1970, any act of making, constructing, using, selling or importing a patented invention solely for uses reasonably related to the development and submission of information required under any law in India or another country shall not be considered an infringement. This provision is known as the **Bolar exception** and is designed to allow generic drug manufacturers to conduct necessary research and trials for regulatory approval before the expiry of the patent (verified notes, p. 87). The Bolar exception facilitates competition by enabling generic drugs to enter the market quickly after the patent expires.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a pharmaceutical company wishes to manufacture a generic version of a patented drug. Before the patent expires, it must conduct clinical trials and submit data to the Central Drugs Standard Control Organization (CDSCO) for marketing approval. Under Section 107A(a), these acts—such as manufacturing the drug for trial purposes or submitting data—are not considered infringement because they are reasonably related to obtaining regulatory approval. This allows the generic manufacturer to prepare for market entry without facing legal action from the patent holder, thereby promoting competition and reducing post-patent expiry prices.\n\n**Conclusion:** The Bolar exception is a critical provision that balances the interests of patent holders with public health needs by allowing generic manufacturers to conduct necessary pre-market research without infringing on patent rights. It ensures timely availability of affordable medicines after the patent expires, enhancing access to essential drugs for the general population.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to distinguish between acts covered under the Bolar exception and those that may still constitute infringement if not solely related to regulatory approval.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the concept of parallel import and its legal basis under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** The definition and legal framework for parallel imports as an exception to patent rights.\n\n**Rule:** **Section 107A(b)** of the Patents Act, 1970 provides that the importation of patented products by any person from a person who is duly authorised under the law to produce and sell or distribute the product shall not be considered infringement. This provision allows for the import of genuine products into India if they have been lawfully placed on the market in another country, provided it does not harm the interests of the patentee (verified notes, p. 87). Parallel imports are also known as **gray market imports** and serve to promote competition by enabling the entry of lower-cost versions of patented drugs or products.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a pharmaceutical product is sold at a significantly lower price in another country due to regulatory differences or pricing policies. A company in India may import this same genuine product from that country for sale within India, provided it was lawfully produced and distributed there. Under Section 107A(b), such importation does not constitute infringement of the patent rights held by the original manufacturer, as long as the imported goods are genuine and were legally placed on the market abroad.\n\n**Conclusion:** Parallel import provisions under the Patents Act, 1970 allow for the legal entry of genuine products into India from other countries where they have been lawfully sold. This promotes competition and reduces prices by introducing alternative sources of supply, especially beneficial in sectors like pharmaceuticals where affordability is a key public concern.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse parallel imports with patent infringement and fail to understand the conditions under which such imports are permissible without violating the rights of the patent holder.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provision relating to research use under Section 47 of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** The scope of the exception for research and experimental purposes as provided under Section 47 of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule:** **Section 47(3)** of the Patents Act, 1970 states that any machine, apparatus or other article in respect of which a patent is granted or any article made by using a process in respect of which the patent is granted may be made or used, and any process in respect of which the patent is granted may be used, by any person for the purpose merely of experiment or research including the imparting of instructions to pupils. This provision permits non-commercial use of patented inventions for research and educational purposes without infringing on the rights of the patent holder (verified notes, p. 87).\n\n**Application:** Suppose a university is conducting research on a patented drug to study its efficacy in treating a rare disease. Under Section 47(3), the researchers may make and use the drug for experimental purposes as long as it is not for commercial gain. Similarly, if a professor wishes to teach students about a patented invention through practical demonstrations, this would also be permitted under the same provision.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 47 of the Patents Act provides an important exception that allows the use of patented inventions for research and educational purposes without infringing on patent rights. This promotes innovation and knowledge dissemination by enabling academic institutions to conduct studies and teach about patented technologies in a non-commercial context.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook Section 47 when discussing exceptions to patent infringement, particularly its role in facilitating academic and experimental use of patented inventions.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Defences and Exceptions — Bolar, Parallel Import, Research Use\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 107 | **TIE-SOCK** | TIE-SOCK loops around revocation grounds, securing them as defences. |\n| Sec. 107-A | **TIE-SOCK** + the letter A | TIE-SOCK ties regulatory data to Bolar exemption, blocking patent claims. |\n| Sec. 47 | **ROCK** | ROCK crashes through parallel imports, breaking brand owner's control. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **43-53** - grant, rights, term of patent\n- **104-115** - suits for infringement, defences, reliefs\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Every ground of revocation available as a defence - which provision?  (picture: TIE-SOCK)\n2. Section 107A(a) Bolar exemption for regulatory submissions - which provision?  (picture: TIE-SOCK)\n3. Section 107A(b) parallel importation - which provision?  (picture: ROCK)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 107A(a) | Allows use of patents for regulatory approval studies | Lab tests before drug sale  \n- Section 107A(b) | Imports from authorized sellers not infringing | Cheap drugs entering India  \n- Section 47(3) | Research and experiments allowed | Scientist testing new ideas  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Mistake: Confusing Bolar with parallel import – they are distinct exceptions.  \n- Phrase to use: \"Promotes competition\" when discussing both Bolar and parallel import.  \n- Structure tip: Always link the exception to its purpose (e.g., public access, research innovation).","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, there lived a young man named Ravi, who had just passed his LL.B. exams and was now preparing for the Patent Agent Examination. His goal was clear: to become a patent agent capable of navigating the intricate world of intellectual property law, especially in the pharmaceutical sector.\n\nOne day, while walking through the old city, he stumbled upon an ancient shop that sold rare books and manuscripts. The shopkeeper, an elderly man with a deep voice, noticed Ravi’s interest and handed him a book titled *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*. \"This,\" the shopkeeper said, \"is your key to understanding the rules that govern inventions.\"\n\nRavi opened the book and began reading. He came across a section on **defences and exceptions**, which intrigued him. The first rule he read about was the **Bolar Exception**—a concept that allowed generic drug manufacturers to conduct research and trials for marketing approval without infringing on the patent holder’s rights.\n\nAs he pondered this, Ravi saw a group of scientists in white coats discussing something at a nearby institute. He approached them and asked what they were working on. One of them said, \"We’re developing a generic version of a patented drug. We need to conduct trials before the patent expires, but we can’t afford to wait until it’s over.\" Ravi nodded, recalling **Section 107A(a)** from the book: *any act of making, constructing, using, selling or importing a patented invention solely for uses reasonably related to the development and submission of information required under any law... shall not be considered as an infringement of patent rights.*\n\nHe explained this to them, and they were relieved. \"So our work is protected by **Section 107A(a)**,\" one said, \"even though we’re using a patented invention for research.\"\n\nRavi then came across another rule: the **Parallel Import** exception. This allowed the import of genuine patented products from other countries where they had been legally sold. He thought about how this could help bring cheaper medicines into India.\n\nLater that day, Ravi visited a local hospital and saw a nurse struggling with the high cost of a life-saving drug. \"We can’t afford it,\" she said. Ravi realized that **Section 107A(b)** might be the solution: *importation of patented products by any person from a person who is duly authorised under the law to produce and sell or distribute the product shall not be considered as an infringement of patent rights.*\n\nHe decided to use this rule in his upcoming exam, explaining how it could help lower drug prices without violating patent laws.\n\nHis final discovery was about **research use**, which was covered in **Section 47(3)**. This allowed any person to make or use a patented invention for the purpose of experiment or research—including teaching students. Ravi imagined himself standing in front of a classroom, explaining how this provision protected innovation and learning.\n\nAs he left the shop, Ravi felt more confident than ever about his exam. The numbers from the book had become part of his story: **Section 107A(a)** for Bolar, **Section 107A(b)** for Parallel Import, and **Section 47(3)** for Research Use.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 107A(a) | Bolar Exception | Any act of making, constructing, using, selling or importing a patented invention solely for uses reasonably related to the development and submission of information required under any law shall not be considered as an infringement of patent rights. |\n| Section 107A(b) | Parallel Import | Importation of patented products by any person from a person who is duly authorised under the law to produce and sell or distribute the product shall not be considered as an infringement of patent rights. |\n| Section 47(3) | Research Use | Any machine, apparatus or other article in respect of which the patent is granted may be made or used, and any process in respect of which the patent is granted may be used, by any person, for the purpose merely of experiment or research including the imparting of instructions to pupils. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"Defences and Exceptions — Bolar, Parallel Import, Research Use\"**\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n- **Bolar Exception** → **B**enefit of **L**egal **A**ccess to **R**emedies  \n  *(Helps recall that it allows use of patented drugs for research and regulatory approval purposes without infringing the patent.)*\n\n- **Parallel Import** → **P**ermitting **A**llowed **R**eimportation of **L**icensed Products  \n  *(Helps remember that it involves importing genuine products from another country where they are sold at a lower price, often bypassing the patentee’s distribution network.)*\n\n- **Research Use Exception** → **R**esearch **E**xemption for **U**ncommercial **S**tudies  \n  *(Aids in recalling that it permits use of patented inventions solely for research purposes without infringing the patent.)*\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 107A(1)** → *Picture a **107A** airplane flying over a **patent office**, symbolizing that this section allows certain acts not to be considered infringement (e.g., research use, Bolar exception).*\n\n- **Section 107A(a) and (b)** → *Imagine a **107A(a)** **lab** with scientists conducting **research** and a **107A(b)** **importer** bringing in **parallel imports** from abroad.*\n\n- **Section 47** → *Visualize the number **47** as a **key**, unlocking **defences** for using patented products under certain conditions (e.g., parallel import).*\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Bolar Exception vs. Research Use Exception:**  \n  *The Bolar exception is specifically tied to **regulatory approval and testing of drugs**, while the research use exception allows **non-commercial research** in any field.*\n\n- **Parallel Import vs. Bolar Exception:**  \n  *Parallel import refers to **importing genuine products from another country**, whereas the Bolar exception involves **using patented drugs for regulatory purposes** within the same jurisdiction.*\n\n- **Research Use vs. Parallel Import:**  \n  *Research use is **non-commercial and limited to study**, while parallel import is a **commercial act involving the sale or distribution of genuine products** in a different market.*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The Bolar exemption allows using a patented invention for regulatory submissions to authorities. It enables generic companies to conduct tests without infringing, as it does not harm the patentee's pecuniary interests.","question":"What is the Bolar exemption under Section 107A(a)?"},{"answer":"Research use exemption permits experimentation or teaching, but not commercial sale. It excludes activities where patented products are sold for experimental purposes, as per court rulings.","question":"What are the conditions for research use exemption under Section 47(3)?"},{"answer":"Parallel importation permits importing patented products sold abroad without the patentee's consent, as it exploits the exhaustion of rights after the first sale.","question":"Why is parallel importation allowed under Section 107A(b)?"},{"answer":"'Government use' includes employing patented inventions by the Central Government or authorized entities for public purposes, requiring a contract with remuneration to the patentee.","question":"What defines 'government use' under Sections 99 and 100?"},{"answer":"No, government undertakings like PSUs cannot use patented inventions for public distribution, as it would infringe the patent, unlike use for the government's own needs.","question":"Can government undertakings use patented inventions for public distribution?"},{"answer":"The exemption excludes commercial sale or distribution of patented products, even for educational purposes, as seen in cases where selling for experiments constitutes infringement.","question":"What limits the research exemption under Section 47(3)?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 107A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Certain acts not to be considered as infringement. For the”.","options":["Section 107A","Section 154","Section 29","Section 132"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Certain acts not to be considered as infringement. For the?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 107A is headed “Certain acts not to be considered as infringement. For the”.","options":["What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within","Certain acts not to be considered as infringement. For the","Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or","Form of application"],"question":"Section 107A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 107 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Defences, etc., in suit for infringement”.","options":["Section 37","Section 107","Section 53","Section 26"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Defences, etc., in suit for infringement?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 107 is headed “Defences, etc., in suit for infringement”.","options":["Avoidance of certain restrictive conditions","Procedure for disposal of applications for restoration of lapsed","Defences, etc., in suit for infringement","Power of High Courts to make rules"],"question":"Section 107 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 47 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “On form of 26 No fee No fee No fee No fee”.","options":["Rule 28","Rule 47","Rule 90","Rule 121"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: On form of 26 No fee No fee No fee No fee?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 47 is headed “On form of 26 No fee No fee No fee No fee”.","options":["Form of reference to another patent","Application for review of decisions or setting aside of","Inspection of register of patents under section 72 and fees","On form of 26 No fee No fee No fee No fee"],"question":"Rule 47 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 107 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Removal of names from the roll of scientific advisers”.","options":["Rule 107","Rule 79","Rule 61","Rule 19"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Removal of names from the roll of scientific advisers?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 107 is headed “Removal of names from the roll of scientific advisers”.","options":["Removal of names from the roll of scientific advisers","Manner of application for inclusion in the roll of","Powers of Controller generally","Form of application for the issue of a duplicate patent"],"question":"Rule 107 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 47 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Grant of patents to be subject to certain conditions”.","options":["Section 62","Section 47","Section 83","Section 100"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Grant of patents to be subject to certain conditions?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 47 is headed “Grant of patents to be subject to certain conditions”.","options":["Right of Government to sell or use forfeited articles","Provisional and complete specifications","Form of application","Grant of patents to be subject to certain conditions"],"question":"Section 47 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 107A(a) excludes acts related to development and submission of information required under any law in India or other countries.","options":["Using a patented invention for commercial purposes","Making a patented invention for government use","Using a patented invention for development of information required by law","Selling patented products without authorization"],"question":"Which of the following acts is explicitly excluded from being considered infringement under Section 107A(a) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 107A(b) excludes importation from persons authorized under the law to produce and sell the product.","options":["Importing patented products from unauthorized sources","Importing patented products from authorized producers","Using patented products for private research","Selling patented products without a license"],"question":"Under Section 107A(b), which of the following is not considered infringement?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 47(4) permits import by the Government for its own use or distribution in government medical institutions.","options":["Commercial distribution by private hospitals","Own use or distribution in government medical institutions","Export to other countries","Research and development"],"question":"Section 47(4) of the Patents Act, 1970 allows the Government to import medicines or drugs for which purpose?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 107(1) states that grounds for revocation under Section 64 are available as defenses in infringement suits.","options":["Section 47","Section 107A","Section 107(1)","Section 64"],"question":"Which section of the Patents Act, 1970 provides that grounds for revoking a patent can be used as a defense in infringement suits?"}]},"s1p_31":{"notes":"# Groundless Threats, Declaration of Non-infringement and Burden of Proof  \n\n## 1. Definition and Key Concepts  \nGroundless threats refer to situations where a person threatens another with legal action for patent infringement without any reasonable or justifiable basis. Such threats are considered abusive and can lead to legal consequences under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\nA **declaration of non-infringement** is a formal statement made by a court that a particular act does not constitute an infringement of a patented invention. This declaration serves as a legal shield for the accused party against future claims of infringement related to the same act.\n\nThe **burden of proof** in cases involving groundless threats or declarations of non-infringement lies on the plaintiff (the person making the threat or claiming infringement). The plaintiff must prove that their claim has a reasonable basis and is not made in bad faith. \n\n---\n\n## 2. Legal Provisions  \n\n### 2.1 Groundless Threats  \n**Section 106 of the Patents Act, 1970** deals with groundless threats:  \n- *Section 106(1):* If a person threatens another with legal action for infringement of a patent without reasonable grounds or in bad faith, the court may grant relief to the threatened party.  \n- *Section 106(2):* The court has the power to order the person making the threat to pay compensation if the threat is found to be groundless.  \n\n### 2.2 Declaration of Non-infringement  \n**Section 105 of the Patents Act, 1970** provides for a declaration of non-infringement:  \n- *Section 105(1):* A court may declare that an act does not constitute infringement of a patented invention if it is satisfied that such is the case.  \n- *Section 105(2):* This declaration can be made upon application by any person who has been threatened with legal action for infringement, or by the patentee itself in certain circumstances.\n\n### 2.3 Burden of Proof  \n**Section 104A of the Patents Act, 1970** outlines the burden of proof in cases concerning infringement:  \n- *Section 104A(1):* The plaintiff must prove that there has been an actual or threatened infringement of a patent.  \n- *Section 104A(2):* If the plaintiff fails to establish a prima facie case, the court may dismiss the suit or refuse to grant relief.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Reasoning and Doctrine (Textbook Explanation)  \n\n### 3.1 Groundless Threats  \nThe provision in **Section 106** is designed to prevent abuse of patent rights by individuals who make threats without any valid legal basis. This helps maintain a balance between the rights of the patentee and the interests of others who may be threatened with litigation for no legitimate reason.\n\n- *As explained in The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 382*, \"Groundless threats are often made to intimidate competitors or prevent them from entering the market. Section 106 acts as a deterrent against such misuse of patent rights.\"\n\n### 3.2 Declaration of Non-infringement  \n**Section 105** allows for a formal declaration that an act is not infringing, which provides legal certainty and reduces the burden on individuals who may be falsely accused of infringement.\n\n- *As per The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 383*, \"A declaration of non-infringement can serve as a protective measure for the accused party and prevent repeated litigation over the same issue.\"\n\n### 3.3 Burden of Proof  \nUnder **Section 104A**, the burden of proof is placed on the plaintiff to establish that infringement has occurred or is threatened.\n\n- *As stated in The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 382*, \"The requirement for a prima facie case ensures that only genuine claims proceed to litigation, thereby preventing the misuse of the legal system by parties with weak or baseless allegations.\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. Case Law  \n\nNo verified cases are available in the case list for this topic. Therefore, it is recommended to **(verify this case with a case-law database)** if further support from judicial decisions is required.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Implications and Study Notes  \n\n### 5.1 Groundless Threats  \n- A groundless threat must be made without reasonable grounds or in bad faith.  \n- The threatened party may seek relief from the court, including compensation for damages caused by such threats.  \n- This provision is crucial to prevent misuse of patent rights and protect innocent parties.\n\n### 5.2 Declaration of Non-infringement  \n- A declaration can be sought by any person who has been threatened with legal action or by the patentee itself.  \n- It provides a clear legal determination that an act does not infringe on the patent.  \n- This is particularly useful in cases where the accused party wishes to avoid repeated litigation over the same issue.\n\n### 5.3 Burden of Proof  \n- The plaintiff must establish a **prima facie case** of infringement or threat of infringement.  \n- If this cannot be established, the court may dismiss the suit or refuse to grant relief.  \n- This provision ensures that only legitimate claims proceed and prevents the abuse of the legal system.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Summary  \n\n| Topic | Section/Article | Key Points |\n|------|------------------|------------|\n| Groundless Threats | Section 106 | Prohibits threats without reasonable grounds; allows relief to the threatened party |\n| Declaration of Non-infringement | Section 105 | Allows a court to declare an act as non-infringing |\n| Burden of Proof | Section 104A | Lies on the plaintiff to establish a prima facie case |\n\nThese provisions are essential for ensuring fair use of patent rights, preventing abuse, and protecting the legal system from frivolous claims. Students should be familiar with these sections and their implications in both theoretical and practical contexts.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 105 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 105. Power of court to make declaration as to non-infringement.\n> (1) Notwithstanding anything contained in section 34 of the Specific\n> Relief Act, 1963 (47 of 1963), any person may institute a suit for a\n> declaration that the use by him of any process, or the making, use\n> or sale of any article by him does not, or would not, constitute an\n> infringement of a claim of a patent against the patentee or the\n> holder of an exclusive licence under the patent, notwithstanding\n> that no assertion to the contrary has been made by the patentee or\n> the licensee, if it is shown\n>\n>  85\n>\n> (a) that the plaintiff has applied in writing to the patentee or\n> exclusive licensee for a written acknowledgment to the effect of the\n> declaration claimed and has furnished him with full particulars in\n> writing of the process or article in question; and\n> (b) that the patentee or licensee has refused or neglected to give\n> such an acknowledgment.\n>\n> (2) The costs of all parties in a suit for a declaration brought by\n> virtue of this section shall, unless for special reasons the court\n> thinks fit to order otherwise, be paid by the plaintiff.\n>\n> (3) The validity of a claim of the specification of a patent shall\n> not be called in question in a suit for a declaration brought by\n> virtue of this section, and accordingly the making or refusal of\n> such a declaration in the case of a patent shall not be deemed to\n> imply that the patent is valid or invalid.\n>\n> (4) A suit for a declaration may be brought by virtue of this\n> section at any time after the publication of grant of a patent, and\n> references in this section to the patentee shall be construed\n> accordingly.\n\n**Section 106 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 106. Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats\n> of infringement proceedings.\n> (1) Where any person (whether entitled to or interested in a patent\n> or an application for a patent or not) threatens any other person by\n> circulars or advertisements or by communications, oral or in writing\n> addressed to that or any other person, with proceedings for\n> infringement of a patent, any person aggrieved thereby may bring a\n> suit against him praying for the following reliefs, that is to say\n> (a) a declaration to the effect that the threats are unjustifiable;\n> (b) an injunction against the continuance of the threats; and\n> (c) such damages, if any, as he has sustained thereby.\n>\n> (2) Unless in such suit the defendant proves that the acts in\n> respect of which the proceedings were threatened constitute or, if\n> done, would constitute, an infringement of a patent or of rights\n> arising from the publication of a complete specification in respect\n> of a claim of the specification not shown by the plaintiff to be\n> invalid, the court may grant to the plaintiff all or any of the\n> reliefs prayed for.\n>\n>  86\n>\n> Explanation. A mere notification of the existence of a patent does\n> not constitute a threat of proceeding within the meaning of this\n> section.\n\n**Section 104A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 104A. Burden of proof in case of suits concerning infringement.\n> (1) In any suit for infringement of a patent, where the subject\n> matter of patent is a process for obtaining a product, the court may\n> direct the defendant to prove that the process used by him to obtain\n> the product, identical to the product of the patented process, is\n> different from the patented process if,\n> (a) the subject matter of the patent is a process for obtaining a\n> new product; or\n> (b) there is a substantial likelihood that the identical product is\n> made by the process, and the patentee or a person deriving title or\n> interest in the patent from him, has been unable through reasonable\n> efforts to determine the process actually used:\n> Provided that the patentee or a person deriving title or interest in\n> the patent from him, first proves that the product is identical to\n> the product directly obtained by the patented process.\n>\n> (2) In considering whether a party has discharged the burden imposed\n> upon him by sub-section (1), the court shall not require him to\n> disclose any manufacturing or commercial secrets, if it appears to\n> the court that it would be unreasonable to do so.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the legal provisions and implications of \"groundless threats\" under the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2008, 2014, 2019)*\n\n**Issue:** The question seeks an analysis of the legal framework and consequences of groundless threats as defined under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule:** Section 106 of the Patents Act, 1970 provides that a person who threatens another with legal action for patent infringement without reasonable grounds or in bad faith may be subject to relief by the court. Specifically, *Section 106(1)* allows the threatened party to seek a declaration that such threats are unjustifiable, an injunction against the continuation of the threat, and compensation for damages sustained. Furthermore, *Section 106(2)* mandates that if the defendant fails to prove that the acts in question would constitute infringement, the court may grant all or any of the reliefs prayed for by the plaintiff.\n\nThe rationale behind this provision is to prevent the misuse of patent rights through intimidation and ensure a balance between the protection of patent holders and the prevention of baseless litigation. As explained in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 382*, groundless threats are often used to intimidate competitors or prevent them from entering the market.\n\n**Application:** Groundless threats occur when a person makes legal claims of infringement without any reasonable basis. For example, if Company A threatens Company B with litigation for using a product that does not infringe on Company A’s patent, and there is no evidence supporting such a claim, this would be considered groundless. In such a case, the court may grant relief to Company B by issuing a declaration that the threats are unjustifiable, enjoining further threats, and awarding damages if any harm was caused.\n\nIt is important to note that mere notification of the existence of a patent does not constitute a threat under *Section 106(2)*. The threshold for a groundless threat lies in whether there is no reasonable basis or evidence of infringement.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 106 of the Patents Act, 1970 serves as an essential safeguard against the misuse of patent rights through baseless threats. It provides relief to the threatened party and ensures that only legitimate claims proceed to litigation. This provision helps maintain a fair balance between the rights of patent holders and the protection of others from unwarranted legal action.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook the requirement for proving bad faith or lack of reasonable grounds in groundless threats, focusing instead on the procedural aspects without addressing the substantive test under *Section 106(2)*.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the concept and procedure for obtaining a \"declaration of non-infringement\" as provided by Section 105 of the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2013, 2016)*\n\n**Issue:** The question seeks an explanation of what a declaration of non-infringement is and how it can be obtained under Section 105.\n\n**Rule:** *Section 105(1)* of the Patents Act, 1970 empowers any person to institute a suit for a declaration that their use of a process or article does not constitute an infringement of a patent. This is possible even if no assertion to the contrary has been made by the patentee. However, certain conditions must be met: (a) the plaintiff must have applied in writing to the patentee or exclusive licensee for a written acknowledgment and provided full details; and (b) the patentee or licensee must have refused or neglected to provide such an acknowledgment.\n\n*Section 105(3)* clarifies that the validity of the patent’s claims is not called into question during such suits, and the making or refusal of a declaration does not imply the patent's validity or invalidity.\n\nAs stated in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 383*, a declaration of non-infringement provides legal certainty to the accused party and prevents repeated litigation over the same issue.\n\n**Application:** Suppose an individual uses a product that is alleged to infringe on a patent. If they have applied in writing to the patentee for acknowledgment but received no response, they may proceed to file a suit under *Section 105* seeking a declaration of non-infringement. The court would assess whether the conditions under *Section 105(1)* are satisfied and then issue the appropriate declaration.\n\nIt is also important to note that the costs in such suits are typically borne by the plaintiff, as stated in *Section 105(2)*, unless the court decides otherwise for special reasons.\n\n**Conclusion:** A declaration of non-infringement under Section 105 provides legal protection and certainty to individuals who may be falsely accused of infringement. The procedure requires a written application to the patentee or licensee and their refusal to respond, after which a suit can be filed with the court.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to mention the specific procedural requirements under *Section 105(1)*, such as the need for prior written communication with the patentee or exclusive licensee before filing a declaration suit.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the burden of proof in cases involving allegations of infringement under Section 104A of the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2015, 2018)*\n\n**Issue:** The question seeks an explanation of the burden of proof in cases concerning patent infringement as outlined under Section 104A.\n\n**Rule:** *Section 104A(1)* of the Patents Act, 1970 places the burden on the plaintiff to prove that there has been actual or threatened infringement of a patent. If the plaintiff fails to establish a prima facie case, the court may dismiss the suit or refuse relief.\n\nAs explained in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 382*, this provision ensures that only genuine claims proceed to litigation, preventing the misuse of the legal system by parties with weak or baseless allegations.\n\n**Application:** For example, if a plaintiff sues another party for infringement, they must first establish that there is an actual or threatened infringement. If they cannot prove this, the court may dismiss the case. This applies even in cases where the subject matter of the patent is a process used to obtain a product, and the defendant may be required to prove that their process differs from the patented one.\n\nImportantly, *Section 104A(2)* states that the court shall not require disclosure of manufacturing or commercial secrets unless it appears unreasonable to do so.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 104A shifts the burden of proof onto the plaintiff in infringement cases. This ensures that only legitimate claims proceed and prevents the legal system from being abused by parties with weak allegations.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to recognize the distinction between the general burden of proof and the specific provisions under *Section 104A*, particularly how it applies differently when the subject matter is a process for obtaining a product.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Groundless Threats, Declaration of Non-infringement and Burden of Proof\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 105 | **TIE-SEAL** | TIE-SEAL clamps tightly around a patent, blocking infringement claims. |\n| Sec. 106 | **TIE-SASH** | TIE-SASH flaps wildly, mocking empty infringement threats. |\n| Sec. 104-A | **TEASER** + the letter A | TEASER dangles a process patent, reversing proof burdens. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **104-115** - suits for infringement, defences, reliefs\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Suit for declaration of non-infringement - which provision?  (picture: TIE-SEAL)\n2. Relief against groundless threats of infringement proceedings - which provision?  (picture: TIE-SASH)\n3. Reversal of the burden of proof for process patents - which provision?  (picture: TEASER)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 105 | Court can declare an act non-infringing | Legal shield against patent threats\n- Section 106 | Courts can stop groundless infringement threats | Stop false legal intimidation\n- Section 104A | Plaintiff must prove infringement prima facie | Burden on claimant to show case\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Confusing declaration of non-infringement with invalidation of a patent\n- Examiners expect: Clear reference to Sections 104A, 105, and 106 in answers\n- Structure tip: Always start by citing the relevant section when discussing burden or threats","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where innovation and tradition intertwine, there lived a young entrepreneur named Arjun Reddy. He had just launched a new mobile app called \"SmartMitra,\" which helped small businesses manage their inventory more efficiently. His dream was to help local shopkeepers thrive in the digital age.\n\nOne day, Arjun received an email from a man named Ravi Kumar, who claimed to be the owner of a patent for a similar software. He threatened Arjun with legal action if he continued using his app, warning that it would be considered an infringement of Ravi’s patent. But there was no evidence provided—no patent number, no proof of ownership.\n\nArjun felt cornered and confused. He had never heard of this patent before. The threat came out of nowhere, and the tone was intimidating. He knew he needed help but didn’t know where to turn.\n\nHis friend, a law student named Nandini, advised him to consult a lawyer. She explained that Ravi’s threats might be **groundless**, as defined in **Section 106(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970—threats made without reasonable grounds or in bad faith. If this were true, Arjun could seek relief from the court.\n\nDetermined to clear his name and protect his business, Arjun decided to file a lawsuit against Ravi for making **groundless threats**. He asked the court to declare that the threats were unjustifiable and to stop them from continuing. He also requested compensation for the stress and lost time caused by these baseless accusations.\n\nMeanwhile, Arjun’s app was gaining traction in local markets, but he feared that if the legal battle dragged on, his business would collapse. Nandini reminded him of **Section 105(1)**—a provision that allows a court to declare an act as non-infringing if it is shown that the plaintiff applied for a written acknowledgment from the patentee and was refused.\n\nInspired by this, Arjun decided to take another step. He sent Ravi a formal letter requesting a written acknowledgment that his app did not infringe on any of Ravi’s patents. But Ravi never responded. This silence became crucial evidence in Arjun’s case.\n\nAt the trial, the court examined **Section 104A(1)**—a rule that places the burden of proof on the plaintiff to establish a **prima facie** case of infringement or threat thereof. Since Ravi had not provided any valid patent documentation, and since Arjun had followed the proper procedure by seeking acknowledgment, the court ruled in Arjun’s favor.\n\nThe judge declared that Ravi's threats were indeed **groundless**, as per **Section 106(2)**, and ordered him to pay compensation for the damages caused to Arjun. Furthermore, the court issued a **declaration of non-infringement** under **Section 105(1)**, confirming that Arjun’s app did not infringe on any patent held by Ravi.\n\nWith this legal victory, Arjun was free to continue his work without fear of baseless litigation. The court also reminded the parties involved that the burden of proof in infringement cases lies with the plaintiff, as outlined in **Section 104A(2)**—a rule designed to prevent the misuse of the legal system by those making weak or false claims.\n\nArjun’s app flourished, and he became a local hero among small business owners. He often spoke about how the law protected individuals like him from **groundless threats**, ensuring that innovation could thrive without fear.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 106(1) | Groundless threats made by Ravi | If a person threatens another with legal action for infringement of a patent without reasonable grounds or in bad faith, the court may grant relief to the threatened party. |\n| Section 106(2) | Compensation ordered against Ravi | The court has the power to order the person making the threat to pay compensation if the threat is found to be groundless. |\n| Section 105(1) | Declaration of non-infringement issued by the court | A court may declare that an act does not constitute infringement of a patented invention if it is satisfied that such is the case. |\n| Section 104A(1) | Burden on Ravi to prove infringement | The plaintiff must prove that there has been an actual or threatened infringement of a patent. |\n| Section 104A(2) | Court dismisses Ravi’s claim | If the plaintiff fails to establish a prima facie case, the court may dismiss the suit or refuse to grant relief. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"Groundless Threats, Declaration of Non-infringement and Burden of Proof\"**\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Groundless Threats:**\n**G**roundless **T**hreats — **G**uard against **T**rivial claims  \n*Use the acronym \"GUT\" to remember that groundless threats are baseless and should be treated with caution.*\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-anchor for Section 106 (Groundless Threats):**\n**Section 106** — Imagine a **106-year-old judge** who is very strict about false legal warnings being made in bad faith.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Confusion Pair: Groundless Threats vs. Infringement Claims**\n- *Groundless threats* are false or baseless claims of infringement made without proper cause, while *infringement claims* are actual allegations based on evidence.\n- **Tip:** \"Threats\" are **false**, and \"claims\" are **real**.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Declaration of Non-infringement:**\n**D**eclaration of **N**on-infringement — **D**oes not mean the patent is invalid, just that it doesn’t apply.\n*Use the acronym \"DON\" to remember that a declaration confirms non-infringement, not patent validity.*\n\n---\n\n#### 5. **Number-anchor for Section 105 (Declaration of Non-infringement):**\n**Section 105** — Picture a **105-page legal document** where the court clearly states that no infringement has occurred.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. **Confusion Pair: Declaration of Non-infringement vs. Patent Invalidity**\n- A *declaration of non-infringement* is about whether a product or process violates a patent, while *patent invalidity* concerns whether the patent itself is legally valid.\n- **Tip:** \"Non-infringement\" deals with **use**, and \"invalidity\" deals with the **patent**.\n\n---\n\n#### 7. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Burden of Proof:**\n**B**urden of **P**roof — **B**elongs to the **P**atentee in infringement cases.\n*Use the acronym \"BP\" to remember that the burden lies with the patent holder.*\n\n---\n\n#### 8. **Number-anchor for Section 104A (Burden of Proof):**\n**Section 104A** — Imagine a **104A-degree certificate** that shows who must prove their case in court.\n\n---\n\n#### 9. **Confusion Pair: Burden of Proof vs. Presumption of Validity**\n- The *burden of proof* lies with the party making an allegation (e.g., patentee in infringement cases), while *presumption of validity* assumes a patent is valid unless proven otherwise.\n- **Tip:** \"Burden\" refers to who must act, and \"Presumption\" refers to what is assumed by default.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The plaintiff must apply in writing to the patentee for acknowledgment and provide full details of the process, per Section 107. This ensures the plaintiff has attempted to resolve the issue directly with the patentee.","question":"What is required to file a suit for declaration of non-infringement?"},{"answer":"A suit may be filed anytime after the patent's grant publication, as stated in Section 107(4). This allows sufficient time for the plaintiff to prepare and initiate legal action.","question":"When can a suit for declaration be filed after a patent is granted?"},{"answer":"Reliefs include a declaration of unjustifiability, an injunction, and damages, as outlined in Section 106. These remedies protect parties from baseless legal threats.","question":"What reliefs are available against groundless threats of infringement?"},{"answer":"Section 107(3) explicitly states validity cannot be questioned in such suits, focusing the court's role solely on non-infringement rather than patent worthiness.","question":"Why can't a suit for declaration challenge a patent's validity?"},{"answer":"The plaintiff pays all costs unless the court orders otherwise, per Section 107(2). This discourages frivolous litigation by the plaintiff.","question":"Who bears the cost in a declaration suit unless the court decides otherwise?"},{"answer":"The defendant must show the threatened acts constitute infringement, as per Section 106(2). This shifts the burden to the defendant to justify the threats.","question":"What must the defendant prove to counter a declaration suit?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 106 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats”.","options":["Section 69","Section 107A","Section 106","Section 110"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 106 is headed “Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats”.","options":["Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats","Controller and other officers","Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court","Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable"],"question":"Section 106 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 105 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of court to make declaration as to non-infringement”.","options":["Section 72","Section 105","Section 51","Section 19"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of court to make declaration as to non-infringement?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 105 is headed “Power of court to make declaration as to non-infringement”.","options":["Multiple priorities","Notification as to countries not providing for reciprocity","Power of court to make declaration as to non-infringement","Finality of orders of Controller and Central Government"],"question":"Section 105 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 105 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Inclusion of the name of any other person in the roll of”.","options":["Rule 31","Rule 118","Rule 108","Rule 105"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Inclusion of the name of any other person in the roll of?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 105 is headed “Inclusion of the name of any other person in the roll of”.","options":["Renewal fees under section 53","Indian International Searching Authority","Inclusion of the name of any other person in the roll of","Notification of corrections"],"question":"Rule 105 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 104A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Burden of proof in case of suits concerning infringement”.","options":["Section 109","Section 104A","Section 145","Section 72"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Burden of proof in case of suits concerning infringement?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 104A is headed “Burden of proof in case of suits concerning infringement”.","options":["Burden of proof in case of suits concerning infringement","Meaning of use of invention for purposes of Government","Restriction on power of court to grant damages or account of","Rights of patent agents"],"question":"Section 104A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 106 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Power to relax”.","options":["Rule 55A","Rule 106","Rule 85","Rule 59"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Power to relax?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 106 is headed “Power to relax”.","options":["Power to relax","Renewal fees under section 53","Payment of fees","Manner of application under section 20(5)"],"question":"Rule 106 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 105(2) states that the costs shall be paid by the plaintiff unless the court orders otherwise.","options":["a) The patentee.","b) The court.","c) The plaintiff, unless the court orders otherwise.","d) The defendant."],"question":"According to Section 105(2) of the Patents Act, 1970, who bears the costs of a suit for a declaration brought under this section?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 106's explanation clarifies that mere notification of a patent's existence does not constitute a threat.","options":["a) Sending a letter notifying the existence of a patent.","b) Advertising a claim of infringement.","c) Communicating orally about potential infringement actions.","d) Filing a lawsuit for infringement."],"question":"Under Section 106 of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is NOT considered a 'threat of infringement proceedings'?"}]},"s1p_32":{"notes":"# Assignments, Licences and Registration of Title  \n*(Based on \"Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\" from The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra)*  \n\n---\n\n## Definition and Key Concepts  \n\n### What is an Assignment?  \nAn assignment refers to the transfer of rights in a patent from one party to another. It involves the complete or partial transfer of ownership, including any rights associated with the invention.\n\n### What is a Licence?  \nA licence is a legal agreement that allows a third party (the licensee) to use the patented invention under specific terms and conditions set by the patentee. A licence does not transfer ownership but grants permission for use.\n\n### What is Registration of Title?  \nRegistration of title refers to the formal process of recording an assignment or other change in ownership or rights in a patent with the Controller of Patents, as required under the law.\n\n---\n\n## Legal Provisions  \n\nThe relevant provisions from **The Patents Act, 1970** are:  \n\n- **Section 68**:  \n  > *“Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly executed.”*  \n  This section mandates that any assignment of a patent or share in a patent must be in writing and properly executed. It ensures that the transfer is clear and legally enforceable.\n\n- **Section 69**:  \n  > *“Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc.”*  \n  This section outlines the process for registering an assignment, transmission, or other interest in a patent with the Controller. It includes provisions for applications by assignees, mortgagors, or licensees and specifies that registration is required to be valid against third parties.\n\n- **Section 70**:  \n  > *“Power of registered grantee or proprietor to deal with patent.”*  \n  This section empowers the registered proprietor or grantee to deal with the patent (e.g., assign, licence) as if they were the owner. However, it also notes that any equities in the patent may be enforced like movable property.\n\n- **Section 71**:  \n  > *“Rectification of register by High Court.”*  \n  If there is an error or defect in the register, a party can apply to the High Court for rectification. The court has the power to make orders for correction, variation, or deletion of entries.\n\n---\n\n## Why Are These Provisions Important?  \n\nThe law requires assignments and licences to be in writing and properly executed **to prevent fraudulent transfers** and ensure legal certainty (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 383). This is especially important because patents are valuable assets that can be subject to disputes.\n\nSimilarly, the requirement for registration under **Section 69** ensures that third parties are aware of any changes in ownership or rights in a patent. It also provides legal protection to assignees and licensees by making their interests public (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 123).\n\nRegistration under **Section 69(4)** is crucial because it mandates that documents such as agreements or licences be filed with the Controller in a prescribed manner. This helps maintain an accurate record of all interests in a patent.\n\n---\n\n## Key Concepts from Textbook  \n\n### Assignments and Transfers  \nAccording to the textbook, *“Assignment is in the nature of transfer from one to another, of his interests or rights in one’s property and vests in the latter the former's interests, rights and remedies, in respect of the subject matter”* (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 123). This highlights that an assignment is a complete transfer of ownership or rights.\n\n### Licensing  \nA licence allows the licensee to use the patented invention under specific terms. It does not confer ownership but grants permission for commercial exploitation. The textbook explains that *“the Controller shall, if so requested by the patentee or licensee, take steps for securing that the terms of the licence are not disclosed to any person except under the order of a court”* (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 60). This ensures confidentiality in licensing agreements.\n\n### Registration Process  \nUnder **Section 69**, it is important that *“the Controller shall, upon proof of title to his satisfaction, register the person as proprietor or co-proprietor”* (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 61). This ensures that only legitimate parties are registered in the patent office records.\n\n---\n\n## Case Law (Where Applicable)  \n\nNo verified cases have been identified for this topic. If a case is required to support an argument or explain a principle, it should be *“(verify this case with a case-law database)”* as no verified cases are available for this chapter.\n\n---\n\n## Practical Implications and Criticisms  \n\n### Importance of Written Assignments  \nThe requirement that assignments must be in writing ensures legal enforceability. However, critics argue that the process can be cumbersome and may delay the transfer of rights (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 383).\n\n### Confidentiality in Licensing  \nWhile **Section 69(4)** ensures confidentiality for licence terms, this may limit transparency in some cases. However, it is a necessary measure to protect the interests of both parties involved.\n\n---\n\n## Summary  \n\n- Assignments and licenses must be in writing and properly executed (Section 68).  \n- Registration with the Controller is required for legal validity under Section 69.  \n- The registered proprietor has the power to deal with the patent as if they were the owner (Section 70).  \n- Errors or defects in registration can be rectified by the High Court under Section 71.  \n\nThese provisions are essential for maintaining clarity, preventing disputes, and ensuring legal protection of intellectual property rights.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 68 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 68. Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly\n> executed.\n> An assignment of a patent or of a share in a patent, a mortgage,\n> licence or the creation of any other interest in a patent shall not\n> be valid unless the same were in writing and the agreement between\n> the parties concerned is reduced to the form of a document embodying\n> all the terms and conditions governing their rights and obligations\n> and duly executed.\n\n**Section 69 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 69. Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc.\n> (1) Where any person becomes entitled by assignment, transmission or\n> operation of law to a patent or to a share in a patent or becomes\n> entitled as a mortgagee, licensee or otherwise to any other interest\n> in a patent, he shall apply in writing in the prescribed manner to\n> the Controller for the registration of his title or, as the case may\n> be, of notice of his interest in the register.\n>\n> (2) Without prejudice to the provisions of sub-section (1), an\n> application for the registration of the title of any person becoming\n> entitled by assignment to a patent or a share in a patent or\n> becoming entitled by virtue of a mortgage, licence or other\n> instrument to any other interest in a patent may be made in the\n> prescribed manner by the assignor, mortgagor, licensor or other\n> party to that instrument, as the case may be.\n>\n> (3) Where an application is made under this section for the\n> registration of the title of any person the Controller shall, upon\n> proof of title to his satisfaction,\n> (a) where that person is entitled to a patent or a share in a\n> patent, register him in the register as proprietor or co-proprietor\n> of the patent, and enter in the register particulars of the\n> instrument or event by which he derives title; or\n> (b) where that person is entitled to any other interest in the\n> patent, enter in the register notice of his interest, with\n>\n>  60\n>\n> particulars of the instrument, if any, creating it:\n> Provided that if there is any dispute between the parties whether\n> the assignment, mortgage, licence, transmission, operation of law or\n> any other such transaction has validly vested in such person a title\n> to the patent or any share or interest therein, the Controller may\n> refuse to take any action under clause (a) or, as the case may be,\n> under clause (b), until the rights of the parties have been\n> determined by a competent court.\n>\n> (4) There shall be supplied to the Controller in the prescribed\n> manner for being filed in the patent office copies of all\n> agreements, licences and other documents affecting the title to any\n> patent or any licence thereunder authenticated in the prescribed\n> manner and also such other documents as may be prescribed relevant\n> to the subject-matter:\n> Provided that in the case of a licence granted under a patent, the\n> Controller shall, if so requested by the patentee or licensee, take\n> steps for securing that the terms of the licence are not disclosed\n> to any person except under the order of a court.\n>\n> (5) Except for the purposes of an application under sub-section (1)\n> or of an application to rectify the register, a document in respect\n> of which no entry has been made in the register under sub-section\n> (3) shall not be admitted by the Controller or by any court as\n> evidence of the title of any person to a patent or to a share or\n> interest therein unless the Controller or the court, for reasons to\n> be recorded in writing, otherwise directs.\n\n**Section 70 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 70. Power of registered grantee or proprietor to deal with patent.\n> Subject to the provisions contained in this Act relating to co-\n> ownership of patents and subject also to any rights vested in any\n> other person of which notice is entered in the register, the person\n> or persons registered as grantee or proprietor of a patent shall\n> have power to assign, grant licences under, or otherwise deal with,\n> the patent and to give effectual receipts for any consideration for\n> any such assignment, licence or dealing:\n> Provided that any equities in respect of the patent may be enforced\n> in like manner as in respect of any other movable property.\n\n**Section 140 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 140. Avoidance of certain restrictive conditions.\n> (1) It shall not be lawful to insert\n> (i) in any contract for or in relation to the sale or lease of a\n> patented article or an article made by a patented process; or\n> (ii) in a licence to manufacture or use a patented article; or\n> (iii) in a licence to work any process protected by a patent, a\n> condition the effect of which may be\n> (a) to require the purchaser, lessee, or licensee to acquire from\n> the vendor, lessor, or licensor, or his nominees, or to prohibit him\n> from acquiring or to restrict in any manner or to any extent his\n> right to acquire from any person or to prohibit him from acquiring\n> except from the vendor, lessor, or licensor or his nominees, any\n> article other than the patented article or an article other than\n> that made by the patented process; or\n> (b) to prohibit the purchaser, lessee or licensee from using, or to\n> restrict in any manner or to any extent the right of the purchaser,\n> lessee or licensee, to use an article other than the patented\n> article or an article other than that made by the patented process,\n> which is not supplied by the vendor, lessor or licensor or his\n> nominee; or\n> (c) to prohibit the purchaser, lessee or licensee from using or to\n> restrict in any manner or to any extent the right of the purchaser,\n> lessee or licensee to use any process other than the patented\n> process; or\n> (d) to provide exclusive grant back, prevention to challenges to\n> validity of patent and coercive package licensing, and any such\n> condition shall be void.\n>\n> (2) A condition of the nature referred to in clause (a) or clause\n> (b) or clause (c) of sub-section (1) shall not cease to be a\n> condition falling within that sub-section merely by reason of the\n> fact that the agreement containing it has been entered into\n> separately, whether before or after the contract relating to the\n> sale, lease or licence of the patented article or process.\n>\n> (3) In proceedings against any person for the infringement of a\n> patent, it shall be a defence to prove that at the time of the\n> infringement there was in force a contract relating to the patent\n> and containing a condition declared unlawful by this section:\n>\n>  105\n>\n> Provided that this sub-section shall not apply if the plaintiff is\n> not a party to the contract and proves to the satisfaction of the\n> court that the restrictive condition was inserted in the contract\n> without his knowledge and consent, express or implied.\n>\n> (4) Nothing in this section shall\n> (a) affect a condition in a contract by which a person is prohibited\n> from selling goods other than those of a particular person;\n> (b) validate a contract which, but for this section, would be\n> invalid;\n> (c) affect a condition in a contract for the lease of, or licence to\n> use, a patented article, by which the lessor or licensor reserves to\n> himself or his nominee the right to supply such new parts of the\n> patented article as may be required or to put or keep it in repair.\n\n**Rule 90 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 90. Registration of title and interest in patents\n> (1) An application referred to in sub-section (1) or sub-section (2)\n> of section 69 shall be made in Form 16.\n>\n> (2) An application for an entry in the register of patents of any\n> other document purporting to affect the proprietorship of the patent\n> by the person benefiting under the document shall be made in Form\n\n**Rule 91 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 91. Presentation of assignment, etc., of patent to Controller\n> Every assignment and every other document giving effect to or being\n> evidence of the transfer of a patent or affecting the proprietorship\n> thereof or creating an interest therein as claimed in such\n> application, shall, unless the Controller otherwise directs, be\n> presented to him together with the application which shall be\n> accompanied by two copies of the assignment or other document\n> certified to be true copies by the applicant or his agent and the\n> Controller may call for such other proof of title or written consent\n> as he may require.\n>\n>  55\n>\n> Rule 92. Registration of title or interest in a patent\n> After the receipt of an application under sub-section (1) or sub-\n> section (2) of section 69, the Controller shall register the title\n> of the person concerned or his interest in a patent, as the case may\n> be, and an entry in the following form shall be made in the\n> register, namely:\n> \"In pursuance of an application received on the .....\n> Proprietor ..... Assignment ..... registered as .....licencee .....\n> by virtue of ..... licence ..... Mortgagee etc. ..... Mortgage deed\n> etc. ..... dated ..... and made between ..... of the one part\n> and ..... of the other part.\"\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the legal requirements for a valid assignment of a patent under The Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What are the legal formalities and requirements that must be fulfilled for an assignment of a patent to be valid?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 68** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, any assignment of a patent or share in a patent, as well as other interests such as a mortgage or licence, is not valid unless it is in writing and the agreement between the parties is reduced to a document embodying all terms and conditions governing their rights and obligations and duly executed. Furthermore, **Section 69** mandates that any assignment must be registered with the Controller of Patents for legal validity against third parties.\n\nThe textbook explains that an assignment involves “the transfer of interests or rights in one’s property” (p. 123). It also clarifies that registration is essential to protect assignees and licensees by making their interests public, ensuring clarity and preventing disputes over ownership (p. 123).\n\n**Application:** An assignment must be in writing and executed properly. For example, if Mr. A assigns his patent rights to Mr. B, the agreement must be written and signed by both parties, clearly stating all terms of the transfer. However, even with a valid written assignment, registration under **Section 69** is required for legal enforceability against third parties. If Mr. B fails to register the assignment, he may not be able to assert his rights against another party who acquires an interest in the patent without knowledge of the assignment.\n\nIf there are disputes over the validity of the assignment, the Controller may defer registration until a competent court determines the rights of the parties (Section 69(3)). This ensures that the interests of both assignor and assignee are protected through judicial intervention when necessary.\n\n**Conclusion:** A valid assignment under *The Patents Act, 1970* must be in writing, properly executed, and registered with the Controller. These requirements ensure legal certainty and enforceability against third parties.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the necessity of registration under **Section 69**, even though a written assignment is valid between the assignor and assignee. Registration is essential for legal protection against third parties.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the significance of registration of assignments, transmissions, or other interests in patents under Section 69 of The Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What is the importance of registering an assignment, transmission, or other interest in a patent with the Controller?\n\n**Rule:** **Section 69** of *The Patents Act, 1970* mandates that any person who becomes entitled to a patent or share in a patent through assignment, transmission, or operation of law must apply for registration. The Controller may only register such an interest if he is satisfied with the proof of title.\n\nAccording to the textbook, registration under **Section 69** ensures legal protection and clarity by making changes in ownership public (p. 123). It also allows third parties to be aware of any transfer or change in rights associated with a patent, thereby preventing disputes over ownership or infringement.\n\n**Application:** Suppose Mr. X assigns his patent rights to Mr. Y. Even if the assignment is valid and properly executed under **Section 68**, it will not be legally enforceable against third parties unless registered with the Controller under **Section 69**. If Mr. Z, a third party, unknowingly infringes on Mr. Y’s rights because the assignment was not registered, Mr. Y may struggle to prove his entitlement in court.\n\nAdditionally, if there is a dispute over whether the assignment or transmission has validly vested title in the assignee, the Controller may refuse registration until the matter is resolved by a competent court (Section 69(3)).\n\n**Conclusion:** Registration under **Section 69** is crucial for legal enforceability against third parties and ensures transparency in patent ownership. It protects both assignees and licensees by making their interests public and prevents disputes over title.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** A common mistake is to confuse the requirement of a written assignment (under Section 68) with registration under **Section 69**, which is necessary for protection against third parties.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the powers of a registered proprietor or grantee in dealing with a patent under Section 70 of The Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What rights does a registered proprietor or grantee have to deal with a patent?\n\n**Rule:** **Section 70** of *The Patents Act, 1970* grants the registered proprietor or grantee the power to assign, licence, or otherwise deal with the patent as if they were the owner. However, this right is subject to any rights vested in other persons and recorded in the register.\n\nAccording to the textbook, a registered proprietor has the authority to “deal with the patent” (p. 61), including assigning it or granting licences under it. This power allows for commercial exploitation of the patent while ensuring that third parties are aware of such dealings through registration.\n\n**Application:** If Mr. A is the registered proprietor of a patent, he may assign the patent to Mr. B or grant a licence to Mr. C without needing further approval from other parties, provided there are no conflicting rights recorded in the register. However, if Mr. D has an interest in the patent that is already recorded, such as a prior assignment or mortgage, Mr. A’s power may be limited.\n\n**Conclusion:** A registered proprietor under **Section 70** can deal with a patent freely, including assigning it or granting licences, provided there are no conflicting rights in the register. This provision ensures flexibility and legal enforceability for the registered party.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to mention that the power under **Section 70** is subject to any rights already recorded in the register. It’s important to highlight this limitation.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the implications of not registering a licence under Section 69(4) of The Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What happens if a licence is not registered with the Controller as required by **Section 69(4)**?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 69(4)**, all agreements, licences, and other documents affecting the title to a patent must be supplied to the Controller for filing in the patent office. In the case of a licence, the Controller may take steps to ensure that its terms are not disclosed to any person except under court order.\n\nThe textbook explains that registration ensures confidentiality (p. 60) but also provides legal protection by making such agreements public, which helps prevent disputes and unauthorized use of the patent.\n\n**Application:** If Mr. A grants a licence to Mr. B without registering it with the Controller, the terms of the licence may not be enforceable against third parties who are unaware of its existence. Additionally, if there is a dispute over the scope or validity of the licence, the lack of registration could make it difficult for Mr. B to prove his rights.\n\n**Conclusion:** Failure to register a licence under **Section 69(4)** may result in unenforceability against third parties and potential legal disputes. Registration ensures clarity and protection for both the licensor and licensee.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook the confidentiality aspect of **Section 69(4)**, which is an important feature to mention when discussing implications of non-registration.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the restrictions on including certain conditions in licensing agreements under Section 140 of The Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What types of conditions are prohibited in licensing or sale agreements under **Section 140**?\n\n**Rule:** **Section 140** prohibits the inclusion of certain restrictive conditions in contracts for the sale, lease, or licence of a patented article or process. These include conditions that restrict the right to acquire other articles, use non-patented products, or use alternative processes. Such conditions are void.\n\nThe textbook notes that these restrictions aim to prevent anti-competitive practices and ensure fair use of patents (p. 105).\n\n**Application:** If a licensor includes a condition in a licence agreement that prevents the licensee from using non-patented articles or from acquiring products from third parties, such a condition would be void under **Section 140**. This ensures that the licensee is not unfairly restricted in their use of the patented invention.\n\n**Conclusion:** Under **Section 140**, certain restrictive conditions in licensing agreements are prohibited to prevent anti-competitive practices and ensure fair exploitation of patents.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students confuse the scope of **Section 140** with other provisions, such as those related to assignments or registrations. It is important to clearly distinguish its focus on restrictive conditions in licensing and sale agreements.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Assignments, Licences and Registration of Title\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 68 | **CHEF** | Chef writes a recipe, ensuring every step is properly executed. |\n| Sec. 69 | **SHIP** | Ship docks at port, needing registration to unload cargo. |\n| Sec. 70 | **GOOSE** | Goose waddles through gates, each requiring a licence. |\n| Sec. 140 | **TIE-ROSE** | Tie-rose slips from neck, avoiding restrictive dress code. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **68-78** - assignments, registration, rectification\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Assignment to be in writing and duly executed - which provision?  (picture: CHEF)\n2. Registration of title as a condition of evidence - which provision?  (picture: SHIP)\n3. Licences, sub-licences and mortgages - which provision?  (picture: GOOSE)\n4. Avoidance of restrictive conditions under Section 140 - which provision?  (picture: TIE-ROSE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 68 | Assignments must be in writing and properly executed | Paper contract on table  \n- Section 69 | Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc. required | Filing documents at office  \n- Section 70 | Registered proprietor can deal with patent as owner | Key to patent box  \n- Section 71 | High Court can rectify register errors | Judge fixing broken file  \n- Section 140 | Prohibits restrictive conditions in contracts | Blocked door sign  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid mentioning verbal agreements when discussing assignments.  \n- Use exact section numbers and terms like \"registered proprietor\" for full marks.  \n- Always link registration to legal validity in exam answers.","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, in a quiet alley near Charminar, lived a young patent agent named Ravi. He was known for his sharp mind and attention to detail, but he had one big problem — he often forgot the legal numbers that governed assignments, licences, and registration of title under **The Patents Act, 1970**.\n\nOne day, Ravi received an urgent call from a client named Mr. Patel, who was in a dispute over a patent for a new type of solar panel. The situation was complicated: Mr. Patel had assigned the patent to his daughter, but she had not registered it with the Controller. A rival company had come forward and claimed that they were entitled to the rights due to an unregistered assignment.\n\nRavi decided he needed to go through **Section 68**, which required that all assignments must be in writing and duly executed. He recalled this from his studies, but now he wanted to make sure it was clear. So, he imagined a scenario where if anyone tried to assign a patent without a written agreement, it would be like trying to carry twenty-one lamps across the river — impossible unless they were properly secured.\n\nHe then visited the Controller’s office and found that **Section 69** played a big role in this case. He had to make sure that Mr. Patel's daughter applied for registration of her title. It was not enough just to have the assignment; it needed to be registered with the Controller, or else it would be like having a treasure buried without anyone knowing where — invisible to others and legally ineffective.\n\nRavi remembered reading in his textbook that **Section 69(4)** required that copies of all agreements and licences be supplied to the Controller. He thought about this as if he were carrying seventy-three sacks of grain across a desert — each sack had to be accounted for, or else some would be lost along the way.\n\nHe found that Mr. Patel's daughter had not filed the necessary documents with the Controller. Without them, her claim was weak. Ravi explained that under **Section 70**, even if she were registered as the proprietor, she could deal with the patent only if it was properly recorded. It was like having a key to a house but not knowing where the house was located.\n\nThen came the most difficult part: there was an error in the register. Ravi realized that **Section 71** allowed for rectification by the High Court, but he had to be sure of the facts first. He imagined this as if someone had mistakenly written a wrong name on a contract — it needed correction, and only the court could do so.\n\nAfter some back-and-forth with Mr. Patel’s daughter, they filed an application for rectification under **Section 71**. The Controller agreed to investigate the matter further, and eventually, the error was corrected, proving that she was indeed the rightful proprietor.\n\nIn the end, Ravi realized that remembering these numbers wasn’t just about memorizing them — it was about understanding how they shaped real-life scenarios like Mr. Patel's. He decided to write down all the sections in a table so he would never forget again.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Section 68 | Assignments must be in writing and duly executed; otherwise, they are not valid. |\n| Seventy-three sacks of grain | Section 69(4) | Copies of all agreements and licences affecting a patent must be supplied to the Controller for registration. |\n| The error in the register | Section 71 | Errors or defects in the register can be rectified by the High Court upon application. |\n| The key to the house | Section 70 | A registered proprietor has the power to deal with the patent as if they were the owner, but only if properly recorded. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for \"Assignments, Licences and Registration of Title\"\n\n---\n\n**1. Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n\n- **A.L.R.T.** – *Assignments, Licenses, Registration, and Title*  \n  (Use this to remember the four key components of this topic.)\n\n---\n\n**2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 68**: *\"Assignment must be in writing.\"*  \n  **Image:** A legal document being signed on a contract (think \"paper\" and \"writing\").\n\n- **Section 69(1)**: *\"Application for registration of title.\"*  \n  **Image:** A person applying to the Controller, like submitting an application form.\n\n- **Section 70**: *\"Power of registered proprietor to deal with patent.\"*  \n  **Image:** A property owner (proprietor) holding a key to a house (patent).\n\n- **Section 71**: *\"Rectification by High Court.\"*  \n  **Image:** A judge using a gavel to correct an error in the register.\n\n- **Section 72**: *\"Register open for inspection.\"*  \n  **Image:** A public library with books (entries) available for everyone to read.\n\n---\n\n**3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Assignment vs. License**  \n  *One transfers ownership, the other grants permission.*  \n  → Use this: \"Assignments are like selling a house; licenses are like renting it.\"\n\n- **Registration of Title vs. Registration of Licence**  \n  *Title registration affects ownership; licence registration is about permissions.*  \n  → Use this: \"Title means 'who owns'; Licence means 'what you can do.'\"\n\n- **Assignment in writing (Section 68) vs. Application for registration (Section 69)**  \n  *One is the requirement, the other is the process to record it.*  \n  → Use this: \"Writing makes the assignment valid; application gets it registered.\"\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall key points quickly and accurately during exams.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"An assignment must be in writing and duly executed as per Section 68. This ensures legal validity and clarity of terms between parties.","question":"What is required for a valid patent assignment under the Patent Act?"},{"answer":"The assignee must apply in Form 16 with fees to the Controller, who verifies proof of title and registers it in the patent register under Section 67(3).","question":"What procedure applies for registering an assignment of a patent?"},{"answer":"Registration is a condition of evidence under Section 67(5), making the assignment legally enforceable in legal proceedings.","question":"Why is registration of title necessary for patent assignments?"},{"answer":"Section 140 prohibits restrictive conditions that prevent the assignee from exploiting the patent, ensuring fair competition.","question":"What does Section 140 prohibit in patent assignments?"},{"answer":"Either the assignee or assignor may apply in Form 16, as per Rule 90, to register the assignment with the Controller.","question":"Who can apply for registration of an assignment?"},{"answer":"A written agreement is mandatory under Section 68 to document all terms, ensuring enforceability and clarity of rights and obligations.","question":"What is the role of a written agreement in patent assignments?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 69 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under”.","options":["Rule 69","Rule 3","Rule 29A","Rule 104"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 69 is headed “Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under”.","options":["On form of 26 No fee No fee No fee No fee","Transmittal of the International Search Report and written","Procedure for the hearing of claim or an application under","Certificate of inventorship"],"question":"Rule 69 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 70 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of registered grantee or proprietor to deal with patent”.","options":["Section 114","Section 43","Section 70","Section 157A"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of registered grantee or proprietor to deal with patent?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 70 is headed “Power of registered grantee or proprietor to deal with patent”.","options":["Power of registered grantee or proprietor to deal with patent","Security for costs","Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents","Rectification of register by High Court"],"question":"Section 70 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 68 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Form of application to be made under section 28(7)”.","options":["Rule 19B","Rule 68","Rule 122","Rule 88"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Form of application to be made under section 28(7)?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 68 is headed “Form of application to be made under section 28(7)”.","options":["Powers of Controller generally","Manner of making a demand","Form of application to be made under section 28(7)","Manner of publication of the revocation order"],"question":"Rule 68 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 140 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Avoidance of certain restrictive conditions”.","options":["Section 29","Section 140","Section 130","Section 128"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Avoidance of certain restrictive conditions?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 140 is headed “Avoidance of certain restrictive conditions”.","options":["Liability for contravention of section 35 or section 39","Form, extent and effect of patent","Avoidance of certain restrictive conditions","Validity of patents of addition"],"question":"Section 140 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 68 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly”.","options":["Section 122","Section 68","Section 13","Section 29"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 68 is headed “Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly”.","options":["Powers of Controller to correct clerical errors, etc","Assignments, etc., not to be valid unless in writing and duly","Special provisions relating to convention applications","Register of patents and particulars to be entered therein"],"question":"Section 68 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 69 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc”.","options":["Section 69","Section 33","Section 17","Section 154"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 69 is headed “Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc”.","options":["Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc","Register of patents and particulars to be entered therein","Form, extent and effect of patent"],"question":"Section 69 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 68 mandates that assignments must be in writing and the agreement must be reduced to a document with all terms and conditions.","options":["a) Oral agreement with witnesses","b) A written document embodying all terms and conditions","c) Registration with the Controller","d) Payment of fees to the patent office"],"question":"Under Section 68 of the Patents Act, 1970, what is required for an assignment of a patent to be valid?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 69(1) states that the person becoming entitled by assignment, transmission, or operation of law must apply to the Controller for registration.","options":["a) The assignor or licensor","b) The assignee or licensee","c) The Controller of Patents","d) The Registrar of Trademarks"],"question":"According to Section 69(1), who must apply for registration of an assignment or interest in a patent?"}]},"s1p_33":{"notes":"# Paris Convention and Convention Applications for Patent Agent Examination\n\n## Definition of \"Convention Application\"\n\nAccording to **Section 135** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, a **\"convention application\"** is defined as an application for a patent made by virtue of **Section 135**. This provision allows applicants who have filed a patent application in a **Convention Country** to claim the same filing date when applying in India.\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n- **Convention Countries**: These are countries that are members of the Paris Convention for the Protection of Industrial Property, or any group of countries or international organization referred to as such under **Section 133** of *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n  \n- **Priority Date**: Under **Section 11B**, an applicant who files in India after filing a patent application in a Convention Country can claim the date of first filing (the priority date) as long as the Indian application is filed within **twelve months** from the date of the initial filing.\n\n- **Multiple Priorities**: **Section 137** allows for multiple priorities if an applicant files applications in more than one Convention Country, provided that each application is filed within the twelve-month period following the first filing.\n\n## Purpose and Importance\n\nThe provisions related to **convention applications** are designed to facilitate international patent protection by allowing applicants to claim the priority date of their initial filing, regardless of where they file next. This ensures that an invention is not considered prior art in subsequent filings made in other countries within the twelve-month period.\n\n### Why the Law Is Structured This Way\n\nThe **Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra**, on page **121** explains that the priority system under the Paris Convention is crucial for applicants who seek protection across multiple jurisdictions. It ensures fairness by preventing an invention from being invalidated in one country due to a prior disclosure made in another.\n\n### Key Sections\n\n- **Section 135**: Defines \"convention application\" and allows applications based on filings in other convention countries.\n- **Section 136**: Provides special provisions relating to convention applications, including the requirement for proof of filing in the convention country.\n- **Section 137**: Deals with multiple priorities where an applicant files in more than one convention country within the twelve-month period.\n\n## Practical Implications\n\nApplicants who wish to claim priority under the Paris Convention must:\n\n- File a patent application in India within **twelve months** of their initial filing abroad.\n- Provide proof that they filed in a **Convention Country**, such as a copy of the foreign application or a certificate from the foreign patent office.\n- Pay any additional fees required for claiming priority.\n\nFailure to meet these requirements may result in the Indian application being treated as a new filing, which could lead to rejection if prior art is found in the intervening period.\n\n## Role of Patent Agents\n\nPatent agents play a critical role in managing convention applications. They must ensure that all necessary documents are submitted on time and that the applicant understands their rights under international treaties like the Paris Convention.\n\n### Why This Is Important for Patent Agents\n\nAs stated in **The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra**, page **383**, patent agents must be well-versed with the provisions of the Patents Act, including those related to convention applications. They are responsible for drafting complete specifications, handling international filings, and ensuring compliance with deadlines.\n\n### Verified Cases\n\n**(No verified case on record for this topic - check IndianKanoon before citing one)**\n\n## Summary\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|------|---------|\n| **Definition of Convention Application** | As per **Section 135**, a convention application is an application made in India based on a prior filing in a convention country. |\n| **Key Provisions** | **Sections 135, 136, and 137** govern the rules for convention applications, including priority dates and multiple filings. |\n| **Purpose** | Facilitates international patent protection by allowing applicants to claim the priority date of their first filing abroad. |\n| **Importance for Patent Agents** | Patent agents must ensure that all procedural requirements are met when handling convention applications. |\n\nThis understanding is essential for candidates preparing for **Paper I, Paper II, and Viva Voce Examinations** under **Section 126** of *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 133 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 133. Convention countries.\n> Any country, which is a signatory or party or a group of countries,\n> union of countries or inter-governmental organisations which are\n> signatories or parties to an international, regional or bi-lateral\n> treaty, convention or arrangement to which India is also a signatory\n> or party and which affords to the applicants for patents in India or\n> to citizens of India similar privileges as are granted to their own\n> citizens or citizens to their member countries in respect of the\n> grant of patents and protection of patent rights shall be a\n> convention country or convention countries for the purposes of this\n> Act.\n\n**Section 135 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 135. Convention applications.\n> (1) Without prejudice to the provisions contained in section 6,\n> where a person has made an application for a patent in respect of an\n> invention in a convention country (hereinafter referred to as the\n> “basic application”), and that person or the legal representative or\n> assignee of that person makes an application under this Act for a\n> patent within twelve months after the date on which the basic\n> application was made, the priority date of a claim of the complete\n> specification, being a claim based on matter disclosed in the basic\n> application, is the date of making of the basic application.\n>\n> Explanation. Where applications have been made for similar\n> protection in respect of an invention in two or more convention\n>\n>  101\n>\n> countries, the period of twelve months referred to in this sub-\n> section shall be reckoned from the date on which the earlier or\n> earliest of the said applications was made.\n>\n> (2) Where applications for protection have been made in one or more\n> convention countries in respect of two or more inventions which are\n> cognate or of which one is a modification of another, a single\n> convention application may, subject to the provisions contained in\n> section 10, be made in respect of those inventions at any time\n> within twelve months from the date of the earliest of the said\n> applications for protection:\n> Provided that the fee payable on the making of any such application\n> shall be the same as if separate applications have been made in\n> respect of each of the said inventions, and the requirements of\n> clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 136 shall, in the case of\n> any such application, apply separately to the applications for\n> protection in respect of each of the said inventions.\n>\n> (3) In case of an application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty designating India and claiming priority from a previously\n> filed application in India, the provisions of sub-sections (1) and\n> (2) shall apply as if the previously filed application were the\n> basic application:\n> Provided that a request for examination under section 11B shall be\n> made only for one of the applications filed in India.\n\n**Section 29 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 29. Anticipation by previous publication.\n> (1) An invention claimed in a complete specification shall not be\n> deemed to have been anticipated by reason only that the invention\n> was published in a specification filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent made in India and dated before the 1st day\n> of January, 1912.\n>\n> (2) Subject as hereinafter provided, an invention claimed in a\n> complete specification shall not be deemed to have been anticipated\n> by reason only that the invention was published before the priority\n> date of the relevant claim of the specification, if the patentee or\n> the applicant for the patent proves\n> (a) that the matter published was obtained from him, or (where he is\n> not himself the true and first inventor) from any person from whom\n> he derives title, and was published without his consent or the\n> consent of any such person; and\n> (b) where the patentee or the applicant for the patent or any person\n> from whom he derives title learned of the publication before the\n> date of the application for the patent, or, in the case of a\n> convention application, before the date of the application for\n> protection in a convention country, that the application or the\n> application in the convention country, as the case may be, was made\n> as soon as reasonably practicable thereafter:\n> Provided that this sub-section shall not apply if the invention was\n> before the priority date of the claim commercially worked in India,\n> otherwise than for the purpose of reasonable trial, either by the\n> patentee or the applicant for the patent or any person from whom he\n> derives title or by any other person with the consent of the\n> patentee or the applicant for the patent or any person from whom he\n> derives title.\n>\n> (3) Where a complete specification is filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent made by a person being the true and first\n> inventor or deriving title from him, an invention claimed in that\n> specification shall not be deemed to have been anticipated by reason\n> only of any other application for a patent in respect of the same\n> invention made in contravention of the rights of that person, or by\n> reason only that after the date of filing of that other application\n> the invention was used or published, without the consent of that\n>\n>  35\n>\n> person, by the applicant in respect of that other application or by\n> any other person in consequence of any disclosure of any invention\n> by that applicant.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Define and explain the term \"convention application\" under Section 135 of The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*Not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe question seeks a definition and explanation of the term “convention application” as provided under **Section 135** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, along with its significance.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 135(1)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, a \"convention application\" is defined as an application for a patent made by virtue of the provisions in that section. It allows applicants who have previously filed a patent application in a **Convention Country** to claim the same filing date when applying in India.\n\nFurther, **Section 135(2)** permits multiple applications for protection in respect of two or more inventions which are cognate or modifications of one another, provided they are made within twelve months from the earliest of such applications. This is subject to the payment of fees as if separate applications were filed and compliance with the requirements under **Section 136**.\n\nThe concept of a **Convention Country** is defined in **Section 133**, which includes any country or group of countries that are signatories to an international, regional, or bilateral treaty, convention, or arrangement to which India is also a party and that affords similar privileges to Indian applicants as those granted to their own citizens.\n\nThe priority date under the Paris Convention is governed by **Section 11B**, which allows an applicant who files in India within twelve months of filing abroad to claim the priority date of the initial application.\n\n**Application**  \nA convention application arises when an inventor applies for a patent in India after having filed an application in a **Convention Country**. For instance, if Mr. A files a patent application in Germany (a Convention Country) on 1st January 2025 and then files a corresponding application in India on 10th December 2025, he may claim the priority date of 1st January 2025 under **Section 135(1)**. This ensures that any prior art published between 1st January 2025 and 10th December 2025 would not invalidate his Indian application.\n\nAdditionally, if Mr. A files a patent application in Germany on 1st January 2025 and another in France (another Convention Country) on 15th February 2025, he may file a single convention application in India for both inventions within twelve months from the earliest filing date (i.e., before 1st January 2026), as permitted under **Section 135(2)**.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA \"convention application\" is an application made in India based on an earlier filing in a Convention Country, allowing the applicant to claim the priority date of that initial application. This mechanism facilitates international patent protection and ensures fairness by preventing prior disclosure in one country from invalidating claims in another within the twelve-month period.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse **Section 135(2)** with **Section 137**, which deals with multiple priorities, rather than explaining how a single convention application can cover multiple inventions under **Section 135(2)**. It is important to distinguish between the two and explain that **Section 135(2)** allows for consolidated filings within twelve months of the earliest filing.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the key provisions governing \"convention applications\" under The Patents Act, 1970? Explain with reference to Sections 135 and 136.**  \n*Not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe question requires an explanation of the key provisions relating to convention applications as outlined in **Sections 135 and 136** of *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 135(1)**, a person who has filed an application for a patent in a Convention Country may file an application in India within twelve months of the initial filing. This allows the applicant to claim the priority date of the first application as the effective filing date for claims based on that disclosure.\n\n**Section 136** provides special provisions related to convention applications, including the requirement for proof of filing in a Convention Country. Specifically, **Section 136(1)(b)** mandates that the applicant must provide evidence such as a copy of the foreign application or a certificate from the foreign patent office confirming the initial filing.\n\nUnder **Section 135(2)**, an applicant who has filed applications for protection in two or more Convention Countries may file a single convention application in India within twelve months from the earliest of those filings. This is subject to the payment of fees as if separate applications were made and compliance with the requirements under **Section 136**.\n\n**Application**  \nSuppose Mr. B files an application for a new pharmaceutical invention on 5th March 2024 in Japan (a Convention Country). He then files a convention application in India on 1st April 2025, within the twelve-month period. Under **Section 135(1)**, he is entitled to claim the priority date of 5th March 2024 for his Indian application.\n\nIf Mr. B also filed an application for a related invention in South Korea on 10th April 2024 and wishes to file a single convention application in India for both inventions, **Section 135(2)** would allow him to do so as long as the application is made before 5th March 2026. However, he must pay the fee applicable for two separate applications under **Section 135(2)** and provide proof of filing in South Korea under **Section 136**.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe key provisions governing convention applications are outlined in **Sections 135 and 136**, which allow applicants to claim priority based on a previous filing in a Convention Country, provided the Indian application is filed within twelve months. Additionally, these sections permit consolidated filings for multiple inventions if they were filed in different Convention Countries within the same period.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often neglect to mention **Section 136**, which outlines the procedural requirements such as providing proof of filing in a Convention Country. It is essential to highlight that **Sections 135 and 136** work together to ensure proper compliance with the priority rules under the Paris Convention.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the significance of the \"priority date\" in convention applications, and its implications for patent protection.**  \n*Not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe question asks about the importance of the “priority date” in convention applications and how it affects patent protection.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 11B**, an applicant who files a patent application in India within twelve months of filing abroad may claim the priority date of the initial foreign application. This is crucial because the priority date determines whether prior art (such as publications or other patents) filed between the initial and subsequent applications can invalidate the later one.\n\nThe **priority system** under the Paris Convention ensures that an invention disclosed in a foreign country does not become prior art for the same invention in India, provided the Indian application is made within twelve months of the first filing. This is governed by **Section 135(1)** and explained in detail in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, page **121**.\n\n**Application**  \nSuppose Mr. C files an application for a new software invention on 1st May 2024 in the United States, which is a Convention Country. He then files a convention application in India on 10th June 2025. Under **Section 11B**, he can claim the priority date of 1st May 2024 for his Indian application.\n\nIf a prior art publication related to Mr. C's invention appeared in a journal on 15th April 2025, it would not invalidate his Indian application because the priority date is earlier than the date of that publication. This ensures that the applicant has sufficient time to file in multiple jurisdictions without losing protection due to prior disclosures.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe “priority date” is crucial in convention applications as it allows applicants to claim the filing date of their initial foreign application when applying in India, thereby protecting their invention from being invalidated by prior art disclosed between the first and second filings. This system ensures fairness and facilitates international patent protection under the Paris Convention.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nIt is common for students to confuse **Section 11B** with **Section 135**, but it is important to note that **Section 11B** governs the claim of priority, while **Section 135** defines convention applications and their procedural requirements. Both provisions are interdependent in ensuring proper protection under international treaties.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the practical implications for an applicant who wishes to file a \"convention application\" in India? Discuss with reference to the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*Not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe question seeks an explanation of the practical steps and requirements that must be fulfilled by an applicant wishing to file a convention application in India.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder **Section 135(1)**, applicants who have filed a patent application in a Convention Country may file a corresponding application in India within twelve months from the date of the initial filing. This allows them to claim the priority date of their first application as the effective filing date for claims based on that disclosure.\n\n**Section 136(1)(b)** mandates that applicants must provide proof of the initial filing, such as a copy of the foreign application or a certificate from the foreign patent office confirming the earlier filing. This is essential to establish the validity of the priority claim under **Section 135**.\n\nAdditionally, **Section 29(2)** provides certain exceptions in cases where prior art was published without the applicant’s consent and was filed as soon as reasonably practicable after learning about it.\n\n**Application**  \nConsider Mr. D who files a patent application for an invention on 10th July 2024 in France (a Convention Country). He then applies to file a convention application in India on 5th August 2025, within the twelve-month period. To comply with **Section 136**, he must submit evidence such as a copy of his French application or a certificate from the French patent office confirming that the initial filing was made.\n\nIf Mr. D fails to provide this proof, the Indian Patent Office may treat his application as a new one, and any prior art filed between 10th July 2024 and 5th August 2025 could invalidate his application under **Section 29** if it is deemed to have been published without his consent.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nApplicants wishing to file convention applications in India must ensure that they submit their Indian application within twelve months of the initial filing abroad. They also need to provide proof of the foreign filing, as required by **Section 136**, to establish the validity of the priority claim. Failure to comply with these procedural requirements may result in the application being treated as a new one and potentially rejected due to prior art.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the importance of submitting proof of the initial filing under **Section 136**, which is essential for claiming priority. It is crucial to emphasize that without such evidence, the convention application may not be recognized by the Indian Patent Office.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Paris Convention and Convention Applications\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 133 | **TIE-MUMMY** | TIE-MUMMY wraps a patent application like a mummy, guarding national treatment. |\n| Sec. 135 | **TIE-MAIL** | TIE-MAIL delays a patent letter, but its twelve-month priority is unbroken. |\n| Sec. 29 | **KNOB** | Knob spins, flashing passport to embassy. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **29-34** - anticipation\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. National treatment and right of priority - which provision?  (picture: TIE-MUMMY)\n2. Twelve-month priority for patents - which provision?  (picture: TIE-MAIL)\n3. Convention country notification under Section 133 - which provision?  (picture: KNOB)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 133 | Defines Convention Countries as those with treaties with India | Treaty signatories map  \n- Section 135 | Allows convention applications to claim priority date in India | Priority date passport  \n- Section 136 | Requires proof of filing in a convention country for convention apps | Proof stamp required  \n- Section 137 | Enables multiple priorities if filed in multiple countries within 12 months | Multi-country filing calendar  \n- Section 11B | Sets 12-month period to claim priority date from initial filing | 12-month filing window  \n- Section 29 | Prevents anticipation by prior publication with proper proof | Prior art shield  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Forgetting the 12-month deadline for claiming priority  \n- Examiners expect: Clear understanding of \"Convention Countries\" under Section 133  \n- Structure tip: Always link convention applications to Sections 135, 136, and 137 in answers","storyEN":"In the bustling city of Hyderabad, a young patent agent named **Rajesh** lived in a small apartment near Charminar. Rajesh had just received an assignment that could change his life: a client from Germany wanted to file for a patent in India under the Paris Convention.\n\nOne day, as he walked through the old bazaar with ten sacks of grain on his back — each sack representing **Section 10** of *The Patents Act* — Rajesh met an elderly man named **Mr. Aman**, who had once been a renowned patent agent himself and now ran a small shop selling rare books.\n\n“Rajesh,” Mr. Aman said, “you must understand the importance of **Section 135** if you are to help your client properly.”\n\nIntrigued, Rajesh asked what he meant. Mr. Aman explained that **Section 135**, like a golden key, allowed applicants who had already filed in a **Convention Country** — such as Germany — to claim the same date for their filing in India.\n\nRajesh listened intently as Mr. Aman continued. “But remember this,” he said, “you must file within twelve months of your first application. Otherwise, you risk losing everything.”\n\nAs Rajesh walked away with a new understanding, he passed by a group of twenty-one lamps — each one representing **Section 21** — and thought about how the law protected inventors across borders.\n\nLater that evening, Rajesh sat at his desk, poring over the client’s documents. He had to ensure that the application was filed within twelve months of the German filing date — a period known as the **priority date**, governed by **Section 11B**. He also needed proof from Germany, like a certificate or a copy of the foreign application.\n\nHe remembered Mr. Aman's words about **Section 136**, which required such proof to claim priority in India. Rajesh made sure all documents were in order and filed them on time.\n\nThen came the challenge of multiple applications — his client had also filed similar patents in two other convention countries, France and Japan. This meant he could use **Section 137** to file a single application for all three inventions within twelve months from the earliest filing date.\n\nRajesh was confident that if he followed these rules, the priority dates would be preserved, and his client's invention would not be considered prior art in India or elsewhere.\n\nThe next day, Rajesh met with Mr. Aman again to discuss a hypothetical situation: what if someone filed an application in India after twelve months? Would it still count?\n\nMr. Aman shook his head. “No,” he said, “that’s when the law becomes strict — like **Section 29**, which says that if you file too late, your invention may be considered already known.”\n\nRajesh nodded and left with a new respect for the rules of patent law.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Ten sacks of grain | Section 10 | A section that deals with requirements related to convention applications, such as proof of foreign filing. |\n| Twenty-one lamps | Section 21 | A provision in *The Patents Act* that governs the disclosure of inventions and may be relevant for prior art considerations. |\n| Section 135 (golden key) | Section 135 | Defines a convention application, allowing an applicant who has filed abroad to claim the same priority date when filing in India. |\n| Twelve months | Section 11B | The period within which an applicant must file in India after making the first application in a Convention Country to claim the priority date. |\n| Section 136 (proof required) | Section 136 | Requires proof of prior filing in a convention country for a convention application to be valid. |\n| Section 137 (multiple priorities) | Section 137 | Permits an applicant to file one convention application covering multiple inventions if filed within twelve months from the first application. |\n| Section 29 (strict law) | Section 29 | States that if a patent is filed after the priority date, it may be considered prior art and could lead to rejection of the application. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"Paris Convention and Convention Applications\"**\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Mnemonic for Elements of Convention Applications**  \n**ACCORD** – *A*pplication, *C*onvention country, *O*riginating application, *R*egistration, *D*ate of priority, *D*ocument submission.\n\n- This helps recall the key components: Application details, filing in a convention country, reference to the originating application, registration requirements, priority date, and document submission rules.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-anchor for Section 135**  \n**Section 135 = \"Convention Applications\"** – *Imagine a* **convention hall (135)** *where applications are filed by people from different countries.* The number 135 visualizes the idea of international agreements and shared rules.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Number-anchor for Section 133**  \n**Section 133 = \"Convention Countries\"** – *Think of a* **trip to 133 countries**, each with its own patent laws, but under the Paris Convention umbrella. The number 133 represents the global reach of the convention.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. **Confusion Pair: Convention Application vs. International Application (PCT)**  \n**Convention application** is filed in a member country and relies on the Paris Convention for priority.  \n**International application** is filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT) and has broader international scope.  \n*Tip:* Remember, \"PCT\" stands for **Patent Cooperation Treaty**, while \"Paris\" refers to the **Paris Convention**.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. **Mnemonic for Priority Date Requirements**  \n**PRIORITY = P**roper **R**ecord, **I**nvention date, **O**riginating application, **T**imely filing, **Y**ears of protection (12 months).  \nThis helps recall the key requirements: proper record-keeping, invention's actual creation date, reference to the originating application, timely submission in convention countries, and 12-month priority period.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. **Number-anchor for Section 134**  \n**Section 134 = \"Non-reciprocal Countries\"** – *Picture a* **broken scale (134)** *showing that some countries do not offer the same patent rights in return.* This helps remember that section 134 deals with countries that are not part of reciprocal agreements under the Paris Convention.\n\n---\n\n#### 7. **Confusion Pair: Convention Application vs. Provisional Specification**  \nA **convention application** is used to claim priority in other convention countries, while a **provisional specification** is an initial filing in India that allows time for further development.  \n*Tip:* \"Convention\" relates to **international filings**, whereas \"Provisional\" refers to **domestic temporary filings**.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall key points about the Paris Convention and convention applications with clarity and precision, using acronyms, number-anchoring, and confusion pair distinctions.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"A convention application is a patent application filed in India claiming priority from a foreign application (Section 135). It requires an English translation if the original was not in English and verified priority documents.","question":"What is a convention application?"},{"answer":"A convention application must be filed within 12 months of the priority application's filing date (Section 135). This ensures the applicant claims the right of priority.","question":"What is the time limit for filing a convention application?"},{"answer":"An English translation is required to ensure the Indian Patent Office can process the application properly (Section 135). This facilitates examination and compliance with procedural requirements.","question":"Why must an English translation be filed for non-English convention applications?"},{"answer":"If an application claims multiple priorities, separate fees must be paid for each priority (Section 137). This applies when multiple foreign applications are cited.","question":"What is the rule for multiple priorities in convention applications?"},{"answer":"Section 133 requires applicants to notify the Indian Patent Office of the convention country within the 12-month priority period. This ensures proper documentation of the application's origin.","question":"What is the significance of Section 133 in convention applications?"},{"answer":"After filing an international patent application, the applicant has 30/31 months to enter the national phase (PCT). This allows time for evaluating the search and examination reports.","question":"What is the time limit for entering the national phase under PCT?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 133 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Supply of certified copies and certificates under sections”.","options":["Rule 19K","Rule 24B","Rule 21","Rule 133"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Supply of certified copies and certificates under sections?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 133 is headed “Supply of certified copies and certificates under sections”.","options":["Supply of certified copies and certificates under sections","Removal of a name from the register of patent agents","Correction of clerical errors","Prescribed particulars"],"question":"Rule 133 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 135 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Convention applications”.","options":["Section 74","Section 125","Section 135","Section 41"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Convention applications?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 135 is headed “Convention applications”.","options":["Provisional and complete specifications","Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement","Acquisition of inventions and patents by the Central","Convention applications"],"question":"Section 135 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 133 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Convention countries”.","options":["Section 133","Section 9","Section 150","Section 122"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Convention countries?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 133 is headed “Convention countries”.","options":["Convention countries","Evidence how to be given and powers of Controller in respect","Power of Controller to give directions to co-owners","Exercise of discretionary powers by Controller"],"question":"Section 133 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 29 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Anticipation by previous publication”.","options":["Section 92A","Section 133","Section 29","Section 118"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Anticipation by previous publication?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 29 is headed “Anticipation by previous publication”.","options":["Anticipation by previous publication","Rights of patentees of lapsed patents which have been restored","Anticipation by use and publication after provisional","Subscription and verification of certain documents by patent"],"question":"Section 29 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 29 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming”.","options":["Rule 29","Rule 19J","Rule 27","Rule 37"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 29 is headed “Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming”.","options":["Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming","Details to be included in an application for the","Opposition to restoration under section 61","Powers of Controller generally"],"question":"Rule 29 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 133 specifies that a convention country must be a signatory to a treaty India is also part of and afford similar privileges to Indian applicants as granted to its own citizens.","options":["A country that is a signatory to any international treaty, regardless of India's participation.","A country that affords similar patent privileges to Indian citizens as granted to its own citizens, and is a signatory to a treaty India is also part of.","A country that has ratified the Paris Convention for the Protection of Industrial Property.","A country that allows Indian applicants to file patents without any fees."],"question":"Which of the following defines a 'convention country' under Section 133 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 135(1) explicitly states that the application must be filed within twelve months after the basic application's date.","options":["Six months from the basic application date.","Twelve months from the basic application date.","Eighteen months from the basic application date.","Twenty-four months from the basic application date."],"question":"Under Section 135(1) of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the time limit for filing a patent application in India after a 'basic application' in a convention country?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 135(3) states that the previously filed Indian application is treated as the basic application, and examination can be requested for only one application.","options":["The PCT application is treated as the basic application, but examination can be requested for all Indian applications.","The previously filed Indian application is treated as the basic application, and examination can be requested for only one Indian application.","The PCT application is treated as the basic application, and examination can be requested for all Indian applications.","The previously filed Indian application is treated as the basic application, and examination can be requested for all Indian applications."],"question":"According to Section 135(3) of the Patents Act, 1970, what happens if an application is filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT) designating India and claiming priority from a previously filed Indian application?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 29(2)(a) requires proof that the published matter was obtained from the applicant or their title holder without their consent.","options":["The prior publication was made with the consent of the applicant or their title holder.","The prior publication was obtained from the applicant or their title holder without their consent.","The prior publication was made by a competitor in another country.","The prior publication was not commercially used in India before the application date."],"question":"Under Section 29(2)(a) of the Patents Act, 1970, what must the applicant prove if prior publication is claimed to not anticipate the invention?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 29(3) states that an invention is not anticipated by another application if it was made in contravention of the true and first inventor's rights.","options":["If the other application was filed by a different inventor in another country.","If the invention was used or published after the true and first inventor's application was filed.","If the true and first inventor's application was filed first, and the other application was made in contravention of their rights.","If the invention was published in a journal before the priority date."],"question":"Under Section 29(3) of the Patents Act, 1970, when is an invention not deemed anticipated by another application for the same invention?"}]},"s1p_34":{"notes":"# PCT — International Application, Search and Preliminary Examination  \n*(Study Notes for Patent Agent Examination – Papers I, II and Viva)*  \n\n---\n\n## 1. **Definition of International Application (PCT)**  \nAn international application is also known as the **‘International Patent Application’** under the **Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT)**. It allows an applicant to file a single patent application that can be used in multiple countries party to the PCT.  \n\n- **Key Concept**: The PCT provides a centralized system for filing, searching, and examining international patent applications before entering the national phase.  \n- **Bare Act Reference**: Rules under **Chapter III** of *The Patents Rules, 2003* (e.g., Rule 19 to Rule 19M) govern the procedures related to international applications.  \n\n---\n\n## 2. **Key Concepts Under PCT**  \n\n### a) **International Searching Authority (ISA)**  \n- The ISA is an office designated under the PCT that performs a search on the novelty and inventive step of the invention.  \n- **Bare Act Reference**: *Rule 19A* refers to the **Indian International Searching Authority**, which may be designated for conducting international searches.  \n\n**Why is this important?**  \nAccording to *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 85)*, if India is chosen as an ISA, it will conduct the **International Search Report**, which gives preliminary information on whether the invention is novel or not. This helps applicants assess the viability of pursuing patents in various countries before investing significant time and money.\n\n---\n\n### b) **International Preliminary Examining Authority (IPEA)**  \n- The IPEA performs a formal examination of the international application, including checking if it meets the requirements for patentability under the PCT.  \n\n**Bare Act Reference**: *Rule 19F* refers to the **Indian International Preliminary Examining Authority**, which may be designated to perform this function.  \n\n**Why is this important?**  \nAs explained in\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 7 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 7. Form of application.\n> (1) Every application for a patent shall be for one invention only\n> and shall be made in the prescribed form and filed in the patent\n> office.\n>\n> (1A) Every international application under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty for a patent, as may be filed designating India, shall be\n> deemed to be an application under this Act, if a corresponding\n> application has also been filed before the Controller in India.\n>\n> (1B) The filing date of an application referred to in sub-section\n> (1A) and its complete specification processed by the patent office\n> as designated office or elected office shall be the international\n> filing date accorded under the Patent Cooperation Treaty.\n>\n> (2) Where the application is made by virtue of an assignment of the\n> right to apply for a patent for the invention, there shall be\n> furnished with the application, or within such period as may be\n> prescribed after the filling of the application, proof of the right\n> to make the application.\n>\n> (3) Every application under this section shall state that the\n>\n>  13\n>\n> applicant is in possession of the invention and shall name the\n> person claiming to be the true and first inventor; and where the\n> person so claiming is not the applicant or one of the applicants,\n> the application shall contain a declaration that the applicant\n> believes the person so named to be the true and first inventor.\n>\n> (4) Every such application (not being a convention application or an\n> application filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty designating\n> India) shall be accompanied by a provisional or a complete\n> specification.\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 135 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 135. Convention applications.\n> (1) Without prejudice to the provisions contained in section 6,\n> where a person has made an application for a patent in respect of an\n> invention in a convention country (hereinafter referred to as the\n> “basic application”), and that person or the legal representative or\n> assignee of that person makes an application under this Act for a\n> patent within twelve months after the date on which the basic\n> application was made, the priority date of a claim of the complete\n> specification, being a claim based on matter disclosed in the basic\n> application, is the date of making of the basic application.\n>\n> Explanation. Where applications have been made for similar\n> protection in respect of an invention in two or more convention\n>\n>  101\n>\n> countries, the period of twelve months referred to in this sub-\n> section shall be reckoned from the date on which the earlier or\n> earliest of the said applications was made.\n>\n> (2) Where applications for protection have been made in one or more\n> convention countries in respect of two or more inventions which are\n> cognate or of which one is a modification of another, a single\n> convention application may, subject to the provisions contained in\n> section 10, be made in respect of those inventions at any time\n> within twelve months from the date of the earliest of the said\n> applications for protection:\n> Provided that the fee payable on the making of any such application\n> shall be the same as if separate applications have been made in\n> respect of each of the said inventions, and the requirements of\n> clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 136 shall, in the case of\n> any such application, apply separately to the applications for\n> protection in respect of each of the said inventions.\n>\n> (3) In case of an application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty designating India and claiming priority from a previously\n> filed application in India, the provisions of sub-sections (1) and\n> (2) shall apply as if the previously filed application were the\n> basic application:\n> Provided that a request for examination under section 11B shall be\n> made only for one of the applications filed in India.\n\n**Rule 10 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 10. Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)\n> to make the application shall be furnished\n> Where, in an application for a patent made by virtue of an\n> assignment of the right to apply for the patent for the invention,\n> if the proof of the right to make the application is not furnished\n> with the application, the applicant shall within a period of six\n> months after the filing of such application furnish such proof.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purposes of this rule, the six months period in\n> case of an application corresponding to an international application\n> in which India is designated shall be reckoned from the actual date\n> on which the corresponding application is filed in India.\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n**Rule 16 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 16. Models\n> (1) Models or samples shall be furnished under section 10 only when\n> required by the Controller.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER III INTERNATIONAL APPLICATIONS UNDER PATENT COOPERATION\n> TREATY (PCT)\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the concept of an International Application under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT) and its significance in the patent filing process.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2018, 2021)*\n\n**Issue**: What is an international application under the PCT, and why is it important for patent applicants?\n\n**Rule**: Under **The Patents Rules, 2003**, specifically **Chapter III** (Rules 19 to 19M), the procedures related to international applications are governed. An **international application** is also known as an **International Patent Application** under the PCT and allows a single application to be used in multiple countries that have ratified the treaty.\n\nThe **Patents Act, 1970**, **Section 7(1A)** states that any international application designating India shall be deemed an application under this Act if a corresponding application has been filed before the Controller in India. This provision enables applicants to use the PCT framework as a bridge to national patent systems.\n\nThe **Indian International Searching Authority (ISA)**, as per **Rule 19A**, is responsible for conducting the **International Search Report**. This report assesses whether an invention is novel and non-obvious, providing preliminary information on its patentability.\n\nSimilarly, the **Indian International Preliminary Examining Authority (IPEA)**, according to **Rule 19F**, performs a formal examination of the application under PCT, checking if it meets the requirements for patentability. This includes assessing whether the invention is novel, inventive, and industrially applicable.\n\n**Application**: The significance of an international application lies in its ability to streamline the patent filing process across multiple countries. For instance, an inventor seeking protection in several jurisdictions can file one application with the PCT, which then undergoes a centralized search and preliminary examination by designated ISAs and IPEAs. This reduces costs and administrative burden compared to filing separate applications in each country.\n\nThe **International Search Report** is particularly valuable as it provides early feedback on the patentability of an invention. As noted in *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 85)*, this helps applicants decide whether to proceed with national phase filings in various countries.\n\nMoreover, **Section 135(1)** of the Patents Act allows for **priority claims** based on a previous application filed in a convention country or under the PCT. This ensures that an applicant can claim the filing date of their first application when entering the national phase in India or other jurisdictions.\n\n**Conclusion**: The international application under the PCT is significant because it offers a centralized, cost-effective mechanism for patent applicants to seek protection across multiple countries. It provides early assessment through the International Search Report and Preliminary Examination, which helps applicants make informed decisions about further proceedings.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to connect the procedural aspects of international applications (such as the role of ISA and IPEA) with their practical benefits for patent seekers, especially in reducing costs and streamlining global filings.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the procedure for filing an International Application under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT), including the roles of the International Searching Authority (ISA) and the International Preliminary Examining Authority (IPEA).**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2017, 2019, 2022)*\n\n**Issue**: What is the procedure for filing an international application under the PCT, and what are the roles of ISA and IPEA?\n\n**Rule**: **Section 7(1A)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* provides that an international application designating India shall be deemed to be an application under this Act if a corresponding application has been filed before the Controller in India. This provision facilitates the integration of PCT applications into the Indian patent system.\n\n**Rule 19A** of *The Patents Rules, 2003* identifies **India as a designated ISA**, which is responsible for conducting an **International Search Report** that evaluates whether the invention is novel and inventive.\n\nSimilarly, **Rule 19F** designates India as an **IPEA**, tasked with performing a formal examination of the international application under PCT to assess its compliance with patentability requirements such as novelty, inventive step, and industrial applicability.\n\nThe **Patents Act, 1970, Section 135(1)** allows for **priority claims** based on earlier applications filed in convention countries or under the PCT. This is crucial during the national phase entry into India.\n\n**Application**: The procedure for filing an international application involves submitting a single application to the PCT, which is then processed by designated ISAs and IPEAs. For example, if an inventor files an international application designating India, the **Indian ISA** conducts a search and issues a report on the novelty of the invention. If the applicant proceeds with the preliminary examination phase, the **IPEA** (India) evaluates whether the application meets all formal and substantive requirements for patentability.\n\nThe **International Search Report**, as explained in *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 85)*, is a critical step that provides early feedback on the patentability of an invention. This helps applicants decide whether to proceed with entering the national phase in various jurisdictions.\n\n**Conclusion**: Filing an international application under the PCT involves submitting one application which is processed by designated ISAs and IPEAs. The **ISA conducts a search**, while the **IPEA performs formal examination**, both of which are essential for assessing patentability before entering the national phase.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students overlook the distinction between the roles of the ISA (which focuses on novelty and inventive step) and the IPEA (which checks compliance with formal requirements), leading to confusion in their answers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is the significance of the International Search Report under the PCT, and how does it assist an applicant?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2020, 2023)*\n\n**Issue**: Why is the International Search Report important for applicants under the PCT?\n\n**Rule**: **Rule 19A** of *The Patents Rules, 2003* states that India may be designated as an **International Searching Authority (ISA)**. It conducts a search and issues an **International Search Report**, which provides preliminary information on whether the invention is novel and non-obvious.\n\nAs explained in *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 85)*, this report gives applicants early insight into the patentability of their invention before they proceed with national phase filings in various countries.\n\n**Application**: The International Search Report is significant because it allows an applicant to assess whether their invention is likely to be granted a patent in different jurisdictions. For example, if the report indicates that prior art exists which could challenge the novelty or inventive step of the invention, the applicant may reconsider pursuing patents in certain countries.\n\nThis early feedback helps applicants make informed decisions about where and how to proceed with national phase filings, potentially saving time and money by avoiding unnecessary applications in jurisdictions where patentability is unlikely.\n\n**Conclusion**: The International Search Report under the PCT provides early feedback on the patentability of an invention. It assists applicants in deciding whether to pursue further patent filings in various countries, thereby optimizing their resources and strategy.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often fail to emphasize the **economic efficiency** aspect of the search report — that it helps avoid unnecessary filings in jurisdictions where the invention is not likely to be granted a patent.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - PCT — International Application, Search and Preliminary Examination\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 7(1A) | **KEY** + clause (1A) | KEY turns in lock, marking filing date at office. |\n| Sec. 10(4A) | **TOES** + clause (4A) | TOES tap report, opinion written by examiners. |\n| Sec. 135 | **TIE-MAIL** | TIE-MAIL lingers, delaying publication until eighteen months pass. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. International filing date and receiving office - which provision?  (picture: KEY)\n2. International search report and written opinion - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n3. International publication at eighteen months - which provision?  (picture: TIE-MAIL)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Rule 19A | Indian ISA conducts international search | \"Searching for invention in India\"\n- Rule 19F | Indian IPEA performs preliminary examination | \"Examining patentability in India\"\n- Section 7(1A) | PCT application deemed as Indian application | \"PCT filing equals Indian filing\"\n- Section 7(1B) | International filing date used for processing | \"Date set by PCT rules\"\n- Section 10(4A) | PCT specs treated as complete in India | \"PCT docs = full spec in India\"\n- Section 135(3) | PCT application with Indian priority | \"Indian app as basic for PCT\"\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing ISA and IPEA roles – they are separate entities  \n- Use exact section numbers from the Patents Rules, 2003 when referring to procedures  \n- Always mention \"designating India\" when discussing PCT applications under Section 7(1A)","storyEN":"**Story: The Journey of Arjun’s Invention**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, in a quiet neighborhood near Charminar, lived Arjun, a young inventor with a dream — to create a device that could purify water using solar energy. He had spent months perfecting his invention and was now ready to take it to the world.\n\nOne evening, as he sat at his desk surrounded by blueprints and prototypes, he recalled an old saying from his grandfather: “A single lamp can light up many rooms.” Inspired, Arjun decided that if he could file one application, it might be enough to illuminate the path for patent protection across multiple countries. That was when he learned about **PCT — International Application**, a system under the **Patent Cooperation Treaty**.\n\nHe visited the patent office in Hyderabad and met with Ravi, an experienced patent agent who had helped many like him before. Ravi explained that Arjun’s application could be filed as an **international application**, which would allow it to be considered by multiple countries — but only if he followed certain rules.\n\nRavi handed him a list of procedures from *The Patents Rules, 2003*, specifically **Rule 19 to Rule 19M**. These were the steps Arjun had to follow before entering the national phase in any country. “These are like twenty-one lamps,” Ravi said, “each one representing a rule that must be lit for your application to shine.”\n\nArjun was confused but determined. He followed the rules and filed his international application. Next came the **International Searching Authority (ISA)** — an office designated under PCT that would perform a search on the novelty of Arjun’s invention. Ravi told him, “If India is chosen as the ISA, it will conduct the **International Search Report**, which gives preliminary information on whether your invention is novel or not.”\n\nAs he waited for the report, Arjun thought about how important this was — and why. He recalled a passage from *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, page **85**: “This helps applicants assess the viability of pursuing patents in various countries before investing significant time and money.” That made sense to him now.\n\nThen came the next stage — the **International Preliminary Examining Authority (IPEA)**, which would perform a formal examination of Arjun’s application. Ravi explained that *Rule 19F* refers to the **Indian International Preliminary Examining Authority**, and if India were chosen for this role, it could examine whether his invention met the requirements for patentability under PCT.\n\nArjun was nervous but hopeful. He had filed a complete specification, as required by **Section 7(4)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, which states that every application (not being a convention application or an international one) must be accompanied by either a provisional or a complete specification.\n\nAs the months passed, Arjun received the International Search Report. It was positive — his invention appeared novel and non-obvious. Encouraged, he moved forward to the preliminary examination stage. He also remembered that **Section 135** of *The Patents Act* allows for **convention applications**, which could be used if he had previously filed in another country.\n\nFinally, after a long wait, Arjun received the International Preliminary Report — it was favorable! Now, he could enter the national phase in any PCT member state. He had followed all the rules: from **Rule 19 to Rule 19M**, through **Section 7(4)** and **Section 135**, and with the help of both the ISA and IPEA.\n\nArjun smiled as he looked at his invention, now ready for the world. He had taken a single step — filing one application — but it was enough to open many doors across the globe.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Rules 19 to 19M of *The Patents Rules, 2003* | These rules govern the procedures related to international applications under PCT. |\n| International Search Report | Rule 19A and Section 7(4) of *The Patents Act, 1970*, and *Rule 19A* of *The Patents Rules, 2003* | The ISA conducts a search to determine the novelty and inventive step of an invention. |\n| International Preliminary Examining Authority (IPEA) | Rule 19F of *The Patents Rules, 2003* | This authority performs a formal examination of the international application for patentability under PCT. |\n| Section 7(4) | Section 7(4) of *The Patents Act, 1970* | Every non-convention or international application must be accompanied by either a provisional or complete specification. |\n| Convention applications | Section 135 of *The Patents Act, 1970* | A convention application allows an applicant to claim priority from a prior application filed in another PCT member state. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **PCT — International Application, Search and Preliminary Examination**  \n*(Based on: \"Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\")*\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Elements of the PCT Process**\n\n- **FILSPE**:  \n  *F*iling an international application  \n  *I*nternational search by ISA  \n  *L*ate publication within 18 months (Article 21)  \n  *S*earch report and written opinion  \n  *P*reliminary examination by IPEA  \n  *E*ntering the national phase  \n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors for Sections/Articles**\n\n- **Rule 19**: *International applications filed with appropriate office as receiving office.*  \n  → *Think of \"19\" as a **door** — this is where the PCT application enters (the receiving office).*\n\n- **Rule 19B**: *International search report*  \n  → *Remember \"19B\" as a **book** — it’s like a book that lists what has already been discovered (prior art).*\n\n- **Rule 19C**: *Time limit for establishing international search report.*  \n  → *Think of \"19C\" as a **clock** — time is important here, and the clock ticks toward the deadline.*\n\n- **Rule 19K**: *International Preliminary Examination Report*  \n  → *Remember \"19K\" as a **key** — this report unlocks the path to national phase entry.*\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **ISA vs IPEA**  \n  → *ISA (International Searching Authority) does the search; IPEA (International Preliminary Examining Authority) conducts preliminary examination. Think of ISA as a **searcher**, and IPEA as an **examiner**.*\n\n- **International Phase vs National Phase**  \n  → *The **international phase** is before entering any country; it’s like a pre-screening. The **national phase** is when the application enters specific countries for examination — this is where real patent rights are granted.*\n\n- **Priority Date vs Filing Date of PCT Application**  \n  → *The **priority date** is the date of the first application (e.g., in India), while the **PCT filing date** is when the international application was submitted. Think of priority as your **starting point**, and the PCT filing date as when you begin the journey.*\n\n- **Publication within 18 Months vs Early Publication Request**  \n  → *The **automatic publication** happens after 18 months from the priority date, while an **early publication request** can be made for faster disclosure — but it costs extra (Rule 45.4).*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall key elements of PCT applications and procedures with clarity and precision during your Patent Agent Examination.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The international search report is a document prepared by the ISA detailing prior art relevant to the invention. It is generated 16 months from the priority date (Articles 15 and 18).","question":"What is the international search report?"},{"answer":"The international search report must be established within 16 months from the priority date (Articles 15 and 18).","question":"What is the time limit for establishing the international search report?"},{"answer":"India is an ISA to allow expedited examination for PCT applicants (Rule 24C(1)).","question":"Why is India designated as an ISA?"},{"answer":"The international application is published 18 months from its filing date (Articles 21 and 29).","question":"When is the international application published?"},{"answer":"A Chapter II demand must be made within 3 months of receiving the search report or 22 months from the priority date, whichever is later. (Rule 19B).","question":"What is the period for making a Chapter II demand?"},{"answer":"As IPEA, the Indian Patent Office conducts international preliminary examination upon demand (Rule 19J).","question":"What is the role of the Indian Patent Office as IPEA?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 7 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Form of application”.","options":["Section 7","Section 145","Section 15","Section 42"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Form of application?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 7 is headed “Form of application”.","options":["Anticipation by public display, etc","Amendment of patent granted to deceased applicant","Form of application","Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of"],"question":"Section 7 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 30","Rule 10","Rule 67","Rule 19N"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Leaving and serving documents","Form of making a request under section 28(2)","Indian International Searching Authority","Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 135 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Convention applications”.","options":["Section 156","Section 135","Section 92A","Section 151"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Convention applications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 135 is headed “Convention applications”.","options":["Persons entitled to apply for patents","Convention applications","Short title, extent and commencement","Secrecy directions to be periodically reviewed"],"question":"Section 135 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 7","Section 28","Section 10","Section 12"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Power of Controller to give directions to co-owners","Register to be open for inspection","Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications","Contents of specifications"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The provision states that such an application is deemed to be an application under this Act if a corresponding application has been filed in India.","options":["a) If the applicant is a resident of India","b) If a corresponding application has been filed before the Controller in India","c) If the application is accompanied by a provisional specification","d) If the application is filed within 6 months of the international filing date"],"question":"Under Section 7(1A) of the Patents Act, 1970, when is an international application under the Patent Cooperation Treaty designating India deemed to be an application under this Act?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The filing date is explicitly stated as the international filing date under the PCT.","options":["a) The date of submission to the Indian Patent Office","b) The international filing date accorded under the Patent Cooperation Treaty","c) The date of publication in the Official Gazette","d) The date of grant of the patent"],"question":"According to Section 7(1B) of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the filing date for an international application under the Patent Cooperation Treaty designating India?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The provision explicitly mandates depositing the material with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty.","options":["a) The material must be described in detail in the abstract","b) The material must be deposited with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty","c) The applicant must publish the material in a scientific journal","d) The material must be tested for commercial viability"],"question":"Section 10(4)(d)(ii) of the Patents Act, 1970 requires that if a biological material is not publicly available, what must be done?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The period specified is 12 months from the earliest application for protection.","options":["a) 6 months","b) 12 months","c) 18 months","d) 24 months"],"question":"Section 135(2) of the Patents Act, 1970 allows a single convention application for multiple inventions if filed within how many months from the earliest application?"}]},"s1p_35":{"notes":"# National Phase Entry in India — The 31-Month Rule  \n\n## Definition  \nThe **National Phase Entry** refers to the process by which an international patent application, filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT), is entered into the national phase in a designated country—in this case, India. This allows the applicant to pursue the grant of a patent in that specific jurisdiction. The **31-Month Rule** defines the time limit within which the applicant must enter the national phase in India after the priority date or the filing date of the international application.\n\n---\n\n## Key Concepts  \n\n### 1. **Priority Date and Filing Date**\n- The **priority date** is the date on which the first patent application (provisional or complete) was filed in any country, including India.\n- The **filing date** refers to the actual date when the international patent application under the PCT was submitted.\n\n### 2. **Designated Countries**\n- When an international application is filed, the applicant can designate specific countries where they wish to seek patent protection.\n- If India is one of these designated countries, the applicant must enter the national phase in India within a specified time frame.\n\n### 3. **National Phase Entry Timeline**\n- The applicant has **31 months** from either the **priority date** or the **filing date**, whichever is earlier, to enter the national phase in India.\n- This period allows sufficient time for the international search and examination processes under the PCT before proceeding with national prosecution.\n\n### 4. **Chapter I and Chapter II of the PCT**\n- Under **Chapter I**, the applicant may choose to proceed directly into the national phase after the publication of the international application, but must do so within **20 months** from the priority date.\n- However, in India, the standard timeline is governed by **Chapter II**, which allows entry into the national phase up to **31 months** from the priority or filing date.\n\n### 5. **Entry into National Phase**\n- To enter the national phase in India, the applicant must:\n  - Submit a request for entry.\n  - Provide translations of the international application (if required).\n  - Pay any applicable fees.\n  - File documents such as the complete specification and drawings.\n\n---\n\n## Legal Provisions — Bare Act Reference  \n\nThe rules governing **National Phase Entry** in India are outlined in the **Patents Rules, 2003**, specifically:\n\n- **Rule 19**: Deals with international applications filed with the appropriate office as the receiving office.\n- **Rule 20**: Covers international applications designating or electing India.\n- **Rule 24B and Rule 24C** (under Chapter II of the Patents Rules, 2003): These rules govern the timeline for submitting a request for examination after publication.\n\n> Under **Rule 19L**, it is stated that the international preliminary examination report must be transmitted within a specified period. Furthermore, **Rule 24B(i)** and **Rule 24C** specify that the applicant has to make a request for examination (RFE) within **31 months** from the date of filing the priority application or the date of filing the international application.\n\n---\n\n## Explanation — Why the Law is as It Is  \n\nThe **Patents Act, 1970**, and related rules are designed to provide a structured and time-bound process for patent applications under both domestic and international regimes. According to **Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s \"Master Guide to Patent Agent Examination\" (p. 88)**:\n\n> “National phase entry in Europe and India can be commenced anytime within **31 months** from the date of filing.”  \n\nThis timeline is crucial for ensuring that applicants have enough time to assess the novelty, non-obviousness, and industrial applicability of their invention through the international search report (ISR) and the international preliminary examination report on patentability (IPERP). These reports are generated by the International Searching Authority (ISA) and the International Preliminary Examining Authority (IPEA), respectively.  \n\nAs explained in **Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 89**:\n\n> “The PCT offers variable timelines under Chapter I and Chapter II... If the applicant decides to enter the national phase through Chapter II then he must do so before the expiration of **30 months or 31 months**, depending on the country in which the international patent application is to be entered. For example, in India and Europe, the international patent application may enter the national phase at the expiry of **31 months** from the priority date.”\n\nThis flexibility allows applicants to make informed decisions about whether they wish to proceed with prosecution in a specific jurisdiction based on the results of the PCT process.\n\n---\n\n## Criticism and Considerations  \n\n- The **31-month period** is considered generous but can also be seen as potentially delaying the grant of patents in India.\n- There are concerns that this extended timeline may lead to increased costs for applicants due to prolonged legal processes, especially if the application is not filed or processed promptly.\n- However, it also provides a safeguard by allowing applicants to evaluate the strength of their invention before investing heavily in national phase prosecution.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion  \n\nThe **31-Month Rule** for National Phase Entry in India under the PCT system ensures that patent applicants have ample time to assess their application through international search and preliminary examination. This rule is implemented through specific provisions in the **Patents Rules, 2003**, particularly **Rules 19L, 24B(i), and 24C**. The rationale behind this timeline is explained in **Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s \"Master Guide to Patent Agent Examination\" (p. 88–89)**, which highlights the importance of allowing sufficient time for international evaluation before proceeding with national prosecution.\n\n> *(No verified or candidate cases are available for this topic. If a case would normally support a point, write \"(verify this case with a case-law database)\" instead.)*\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 7 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 7. Form of application.\n> (1) Every application for a patent shall be for one invention only\n> and shall be made in the prescribed form and filed in the patent\n> office.\n>\n> (1A) Every international application under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty for a patent, as may be filed designating India, shall be\n> deemed to be an application under this Act, if a corresponding\n> application has also been filed before the Controller in India.\n>\n> (1B) The filing date of an application referred to in sub-section\n> (1A) and its complete specification processed by the patent office\n> as designated office or elected office shall be the international\n> filing date accorded under the Patent Cooperation Treaty.\n>\n> (2) Where the application is made by virtue of an assignment of the\n> right to apply for a patent for the invention, there shall be\n> furnished with the application, or within such period as may be\n> prescribed after the filling of the application, proof of the right\n> to make the application.\n>\n> (3) Every application under this section shall state that the\n>\n>  13\n>\n> applicant is in possession of the invention and shall name the\n> person claiming to be the true and first inventor; and where the\n> person so claiming is not the applicant or one of the applicants,\n> the application shall contain a declaration that the applicant\n> believes the person so named to be the true and first inventor.\n>\n> (4) Every such application (not being a convention application or an\n> application filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty designating\n> India) shall be accompanied by a provisional or a complete\n> specification.\n\n**Section 11A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11A. Publication of applications.\n> (1) Save as otherwise provided, no application for patent shall\n> ordinarily be opened to the public for such period as may be\n> prescribed.\n>\n> (2) The applicant may, in the prescribed manner, request the\n> Controller to publish his application at any time before the expiry\n> of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) and subject to the\n> provisions of sub-section (3), the Controller shall publish such\n> application as soon as possible.\n>\n> (3) Every application for a patent shall, on the expiry of the\n> period specified under sub-section (1), be published, except in\n> cases where the application\n> (a) in which secrecy direction is imposed under section 35; or\n> (b) has been abandoned under sub-section (1) of section 9; or\n> (c) has been withdrawn three months prior to the period specified\n> under sub-section (1).\n>\n> (4) In case a secrecy direction has been given in respect of an\n> application under section 35, then, it shall be published after the\n> expiry of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) or when the\n> secrecy direction has ceased to operate, whichever is later.\n>\n> (5) The publication of every application under this section shall\n> include the particulars of the date of application, number of\n> application, name and address of the applicant identifying the\n> application and an abstract.\n>\n> (6) Upon publication of an application for a patent under this\n> section\n> (a) the depository institution shall make the biological material\n> mentioned in the specification available to the public;\n> (b) the patent office may, on payment of such fee as may be\n> prescribed, make the specification and drawings, if any, of such\n> application available to the public.\n>\n> (7) On and from the date of publication of the application for\n> patent and until the date of grant of a patent in respect of such\n>\n>  20\n>\n> application, the applicant shall have the like privileges and rights\n> as if a patent for the invention had been granted on the date of\n> publication of the application:\n> Provided that the applicant shall not be entitled to institute any\n> proceedings for infringement until the patent has been granted:\n> Provided further that the rights of a patentee in respect of\n> applications made under sub-section (2) of section 5 before the 1st\n> day of January, 2005 shall accrue from the date of grant of the\n> patent:\n> Provided also that after a patent is granted in respect of\n> applications made under sub-section (2) of section 5, the patent-\n> holder shall only be entitled to receive reasonable royalty from\n> such enterprises which have made significant investment and were\n> producing and marketing the concerned product prior to the 1st day\n> of January, 2005 and which continue to manufacture the product\n> covered by the patent on the date of grant of the patent and no\n> infringement proceedings shall be instituted against such\n> enterprises.\n\n**Section 138 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 138. Supplementary provisions as to convention applications.\n> (1) Where a convention application is made in accordance with the\n> provisions of this Chapter, the applicant shall furnish, when\n> required by the Controller, in addition to the complete\n> specification, copies of the specifications or corresponding\n> documents filed or deposited by the applicant in the patent office\n> of the convention country as referred to in section 133 verified to\n>\n>  103\n>\n> the satisfaction of the Controller, within the prescribed period\n> from the date of communication by the Controller.\n>\n> (2) If any such specification or other document is in a foreign\n> language, a translation into English of the specification or\n> document, verified by affidavit or otherwise to the satisfaction of\n> the Controller, shall be furnished when required by the controller.\n>\n> (3) For the purposes of this Act, the date on which an application\n> was made in a convention country is such date as the Controller is\n> satisfied by certificate of the official chief or head of the patent\n> office of the convention country or otherwise, is the date on which\n> the application was made in that convention country.\n>\n> (4) An international application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty designating India shall have effect of filing an application\n> for patent under section 7, section 54 and section 135, as the case\n> may be, and the title, description, claim and abstract and drawings,\n> if any, filed in the international application shall be taken as\n> complete specification for the purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The filing date of an application for patent and its complete\n> specification processed by the patent office as designated office\n> shall be the international filing date accorded under the Patent\n> Cooperation Treaty.\n>\n> (6) Amendment, if any, proposed by the applicant for an\n> international application designating India or designating and\n> electing India before international searching authority or\n> preliminary made examination authority shall, if the applicant so\n> desires, be taken as an amendment made before the patent office.\n\n**Rule 10 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 10. Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)\n> to make the application shall be furnished\n> Where, in an application for a patent made by virtue of an\n> assignment of the right to apply for the patent for the invention,\n> if the proof of the right to make the application is not furnished\n> with the application, the applicant shall within a period of six\n> months after the filing of such application furnish such proof.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purposes of this rule, the six months period in\n> case of an application corresponding to an international application\n> in which India is designated shall be reckoned from the actual date\n> on which the corresponding application is filed in India.\n\n**Rule 121 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 121. Period within which copies of specification etc. are to be\n> filed\n> The period within which copies of specification or corresponding\n> documents to be filed by the applicant under sub-section (1) of\n> section 138 shall be three months from the date of communication by\n> the Controller.\n\n**Rule 20 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 20. International applications designating or designating and\n> electing India\n> (1) An application corresponding to an international application\n> filed under Patent Cooperation Treaty may be made in Form 1 under\n> sub-section (1A) of section 7.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purpose of this rule, \"an application\n> corresponding to an international application means an international\n> application as filed under Patent Cooperation Treaty which includes\n> any amendments made by the applicant under Article 19 and\n> communicated to Designated Office under Article 20 or any amendment\n> made under sub-clause (b) of clause (2) of Article 34 of the Treaty:\n> Provided that the applicant, while filing such application\n> corresponding to an international application designating India, may\n> delete a claim, in accordance with the provisions contained in rule\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the concept of National Phase Entry under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT) and explain the significance of the 31-Month Rule as it applies to India.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2021, 2023)*\n\n**Issue:** The question requires an explanation of National Phase Entry under the PCT system, with a focus on the significance of the 31-Month Rule in the context of India.\n\n**Rule:**  \nUnder the **Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT)**, an international patent application can be filed and then entered into the **national phase** in designated countries. In India, this process is governed by specific provisions under the **Patents Rules, 2003**, particularly **Rules 19L, 24B(i), and 24C**. These rules stipulate that an applicant must enter the national phase within **31 months from either the priority date or the filing date of the international application**.\n\nThe **Patents Act, 1970**, in **Section 7(1A)**, provides that an international application designating India shall be deemed to be an application under this Act if a corresponding application is filed before the Controller. Additionally, **Section 138(4)** states that an international application filed under the PCT designating India has the effect of filing an application for patent under various sections of the Patents Act.\n\nThe **31-Month Rule** allows applicants sufficient time to assess their invention through the **International Search Report (ISR)** and the **International Preliminary Examination Report on Patentability (IPERP)**, which are generated by the **International Searching Authority (ISA)** and the **International Preliminary Examining Authority (IPEA)** respectively. This is explained in detail in **Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s \"Master Guide to Patent Agent Examination\"** (p. 88–89), which highlights that the PCT system provides flexibility for applicants to decide whether they wish to proceed with national phase prosecution based on the results of these evaluations.\n\n**Application:**  \nThe **31-Month Rule** is significant because it enables patent applicants to evaluate the strength and novelty of their invention before investing in the costly and time-consuming process of national phase prosecution. For example, if an applicant files an international application under the PCT with a priority date of 1 January 2020, they must enter the national phase in India by **31 July 2023** (i.e., 31 months from the priority date). During this period, the applicant can review the ISR and IPERP to determine whether it is worthwhile to proceed with patent prosecution in India.\n\nThis rule applies not only to applications filed directly under the PCT but also to **convention applications**, which are covered under **Section 138** of the Patents Act. These provisions ensure that Indian patent law aligns with international standards and facilitates smoother entry into the national phase for foreign applicants seeking protection in India.\n\nCritics argue that this extended timeline may lead to delays in the grant of patents, potentially increasing legal costs and prolonging the period during which an invention remains unprotected. However, the rule is seen as a necessary safeguard to ensure that only strong and viable inventions proceed through the patent process.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe **31-Month Rule** for National Phase Entry under the PCT system in India provides applicants with ample time to evaluate their inventions using international reports before proceeding with national prosecution. This is governed by provisions such as **Rules 19L, 24B(i), and 24C** of the Patents Rules, 2003, and is explained in detail in **Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s \"Master Guide to Patent Agent Examination\" (p. 88–89)**.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to explain the rationale behind the 31-month timeline and its practical implications for applicants, such as cost considerations and the benefits of international evaluation before national prosecution.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the key provisions in the Patents Rules, 2003 that govern the National Phase Entry in India under the PCT system? Explain their significance.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 1× (2022)*\n\n**Issue:** The question seeks an explanation of the key provisions in the **Patents Rules, 2003**, which govern National Phase Entry in India under the **PCT system**, and their significance.\n\n**Rule:**  \nThe **Patents Rules, 2003**, particularly **Rules 19, 20, 24B(i), and 24C**, are key to understanding the process of entering the national phase in India. These rules provide a framework for handling international applications designating or electing India.\n\n- **Rule 19** deals with international applications filed with the appropriate office as the receiving office.\n- **Rule 20** covers international applications that designate or elect India, ensuring they are treated under the provisions of Indian patent law.\n- **Rule 24B(i)** and **Rule 24C** specify that an applicant must make a request for examination (RFE) within **31 months from the priority date or filing date**, whichever is earlier.\n\nThese rules align with the **Patents Act, 1970**, particularly in **Section 7(1A)** and **Section 138(4)**, which provide that an international application designating India shall be treated as a valid patent application under Indian law if the necessary steps are taken before the Controller.\n\n**Application:**  \nFor example, if an applicant files an international application on 1 May 2022 with a priority date of 1 January 2021, they must file their request for examination in India within **31 months from either the priority date (i.e., by 31 July 2024)** or the filing date (i.e., by 31 October 2024). This timeline ensures that applicants have sufficient time to evaluate the results of international search and preliminary examination before proceeding with national prosecution.\n\nThe significance of these rules lies in their ability to streamline the patent process, ensuring alignment between Indian law and international standards under the PCT. They also help prevent premature or unnecessary expenditure on applications that may not meet the requirements for grant.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe **Patents Rules, 2003**, particularly **Rules 19, 20, 24B(i), and 24C**, govern the process of National Phase Entry in India under the PCT system. These rules provide a clear timeline and procedural framework for applicants to follow, ensuring that they meet the necessary legal requirements before proceeding with national prosecution.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the specific provisions in **Rules 19L, 24B(i), and 24C**, which are crucial for understanding the exact timelines and procedures involved in National Phase Entry.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - National Phase Entry in India — The 31-Month Rule\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 7(1A) | **KEY** + clause (1A) | KEY rusts if left unturned for thirty-one months |\n| Sec. 11-A | **TOTE** + the letter A | TOTE bulges with documents at entry, needing translation |\n| Sec. 138 | **TIE-MOVIE** | TIE-MOVIE reels show amendments during national phase entry |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Thirty-one months from the priority date - which provision?  (picture: KEY)\n2. Documents and translation required on entry - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n3. Amendments on national phase entry - which provision?  (picture: TIE-MOVIE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Rule 19 | Deals with international applications filed with India as receiving office | PCT filing in India's hands\n- Rule 20 | Covers applications designating or electing India | India on the list of targets\n- Rule 24B(i) | Request for examination must be made within 31 months from priority date | 31-month countdown clock\n- Rule 24C | Governs timeline for submitting request for examination | Deadline ticking in India\n- Section 7(1A) | International applications designating India are deemed as domestic applications | PCT treated like local filing\n- Section 138(4) | International application under PCT is treated as patent application under Indian law | Global filing becomes local\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Common mistake: Confusing Chapter I and Chapter II timelines for national phase entry\n- Examiners expect: Clear distinction between 20-month and 31-month rules in India\n- Structure tip: Always cite the relevant Patents Rules, 2003 (e.g., Rule 24B(i)) when mentioning the 31-month rule","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, near the bustling Charminar, lived a young inventor named Arjun. He had just returned from a trip to Geneva where he filed an international patent application under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT). His invention was a revolutionary solar-powered water purifier that could change lives in rural India.\n\nBack home, Arjun faced a challenge: he needed to enter the **national phase** of his PCT application in India within the allowed time. He knew the rules were strict and had read about them in Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s *Master Guide to Patent Agent Examination*. But understanding was one thing; applying it correctly was another.\n\nArjun's journey began with a visit to the patent office, where he met Mr. Ravi, an experienced patent agent who had once worked for a major tech firm. Mr. Ravi explained that Arjun had **31 months** from either the priority date or the filing date of his international application to enter the national phase in India.\n\n“Why 31 months?” Arjun asked curiously.\n\nMr. Ravi smiled and said, “Because it gives you time to evaluate your invention through the PCT process before investing heavily into national prosecution.”\n\nArjun’s eyes widened as he realized how crucial this period was. He recalled from his studies that **Rule 24B(i)** of the Patents Rules, 2003, specified that the applicant had to make a request for examination (RFE) within **31 months** from the date of filing the priority application or the international application.\n\nThat night, Arjun sat with his notebook open and marked off the **31 months**, counting each day like a precious gem. He knew he couldn’t afford to miss this deadline.\n\nA few weeks later, Arjun visited Mr. Ravi again. This time, he brought along a stack of documents that needed translation into English as per **Rule 20** of the Patents Rules, 2003. “I need these translated and submitted before the deadline,” he said.\n\nMr. Ravi nodded. “Yes, translations are required if the application is in another language. And remember, you also have to pay any applicable fees.”\n\nArjun’s head was spinning with all the requirements: submitting a request for entry, providing translations, paying fees, and filing documents such as the complete specification and drawings.\n\nJust when he thought everything was under control, Arjun received an email from Mr. Ravi reminding him of **Rule 19L**, which stated that the international preliminary examination report must be transmitted within a specific period. “You’ve got to make sure you don’t miss this,” Mr. Ravi warned.\n\nArjun breathed deeply and made a list:  \n- Submit request for entry (before 31 months)  \n- Translate documents if needed  \n- Pay applicable fees  \n- File complete specification and drawings  \n\nHe marked off each task as he completed it, feeling the weight of responsibility on his shoulders.\n\nOne day, Arjun met a fellow inventor named Ananya. She had recently filed an application under **Chapter I** of the PCT but was advised to enter the national phase in India through **Chapter II**, which allowed for the **31-month rule**. “I’m glad I chose Chapter II,” she said with a smile, “because it gave me more time.”\n\nArjun smiled back and felt reassured that he had made the right decision.\n\nAs the months passed, Arjun kept track of every day. He knew that missing the **31-month deadline** could mean losing his patent rights in India entirely. But thanks to Mr. Ravi’s guidance and Dr. Sheetal Chopra’s book, he was on the right path.\n\nOn the 31st month, Arjun submitted all the necessary documents just before the deadline. He felt a wave of relief wash over him as he handed in his request for entry into the national phase.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| 31 months    | Rule 24B(i) and Rule 24C (Patents Rules, 2003) | An applicant must enter the national phase in India within 31 months from the priority date or filing date of the international application. |\n| Rule 20      | Rule 20 (Patents Rules, 2003)             | Covers international applications designating or electing India and requires submission of translations if needed. |\n| Rule 19L     | Rule 19L (Patents Rules, 2003)            | The international preliminary examination report must be transmitted within a specified period. |\n| Chapter I    | Chapter I of the PCT                     | Allows entry into national phase after publication but with a shorter timeline compared to Chapter II. |\n| Chapter II   | Chapter II of the PCT                    | Governs the 31-month rule for entering the national phase in countries like India. |\n| Rule 24B(i)  | Rule 24B(i) (Patents Rules, 2003)         | Specifies that a request for examination must be made within 31 months from the priority or filing date. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"National Phase Entry in India — The 31-Month Rule\"**\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n**“PCT-31” Rule**  \nUse the acronym **PCT-31**, where:\n- **P** = Patent Cooperation Treaty (the international system)\n- **C** = Chapter II of PCT (which applies to India and Europe)\n- **T** = Timeframe for entering national phase\n- **31** = 31 months from the priority date\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **\"31 Months\"**: Visualize a calendar with **31 days in January**, which is the first month of the year — just like **31 months** is the starting point for entering the national phase in India.\n  \n- **\"20 Months\"**: Think of **20 months = 1 year and 8 months**. This is the timeframe for certain countries under Chapter I (e.g., Brazil, Switzerland). Imagine a clock with **20 minutes past 8**, which visually represents 20 months.\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Chapter I vs. Chapter II**:  \n  *Confusion*: Students often confuse the timeframes for Chapter I and Chapter II under PCT.  \n  *Differentiator*: **Chapter I** allows entry into national phase within **20 months**, while **Chapter II** gives **31 months** — remember that **India is in Chapter II**, hence the **31-month rule applies to India**.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed for quick recall and clarity during exams or study sessions.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The National Phase is when an international patent application enters designated countries for examination by national patent offices. It follows the international phase and involves filing documents with the respective national offices.","question":"What is the National Phase in PCT applications?"},{"answer":"India allows entry into the national phase within 31 months from the priority date. This timeline applies to countries like India and Europe under Chapter II of the PCT.","question":"What is the 31-month rule for India?"},{"answer":"An English translation of the application is required if not originally filed in English, as per Rule 22 and Article 22.","question":"What documents are required for national phase entry in India?"},{"answer":"Amendments can be made, but any changes must be accompanied by an English translation of the amended claims, as per Rule 24C.","question":"Can amendments be made during national phase entry?"},{"answer":"An international application is treated as a regular patent application under the Indian Patents Act once it enters the national phase, as per Article 11.","question":"How is an international application treated under Indian law?"},{"answer":"Delays may be condoned at the Controller's discretion, considering the facts of each case, as per Rules 137 and 138.","question":"Can delays in national phase entry be condoned?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 138 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Supplementary provisions as to convention applications”.","options":["Section 58","Section 51","Section 56","Section 138"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Supplementary provisions as to convention applications?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 138 is headed “Supplementary provisions as to convention applications”.","options":["Refusal or failure to supply information","Loss or destruction of Patents","Registration of assignments, transmissions, etc","Supplementary provisions as to convention applications"],"question":"Section 138 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 11A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Publication of applications”.","options":["Section 51","Section 11A","Section 105","Section 141"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Publication of applications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 11A is headed “Publication of applications”.","options":["Rules to be placed before Parliament","Publication of applications","General principles applicable to working of patented inventions","Form, extent and effect of patent"],"question":"Section 11A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 7 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Form of application”.","options":["Section 55","Section 7","Section 43","Section 85"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Form of application?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 7 is headed “Form of application”.","options":["Multiple priorities","Patent to bind Government","Special provisions relating to convention applications","Form of application"],"question":"Section 7 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 138 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Power to extend time specified or condone delay”.","options":["Rule 72","Rule 131","Rule 107","Rule 138"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Power to extend time specified or condone delay?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 138 is headed “Power to extend time specified or condone delay”.","options":["Amendments to specifications","Power to extend time specified or condone delay","Constitution of Opposition Board and its proceeding","Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent"],"question":"Rule 138 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 7(1) states that every application for a patent shall be for one invention only and shall be made in the prescribed form and filed in the patent office.","options":["a) It must be for multiple inventions.","b) It must be for one invention only.","c) It must be submitted in a non-prescribed form.","d) It must be filed outside the patent office."],"question":"Under Section 7 of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the requirement regarding the form of a patent application?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 7(1A) specifies that such an application is deemed to be an application under this Act if a corresponding application has also been filed before the Controller in India.","options":["a) If it is filed without designating India.","b) If a corresponding application has been filed before the Controller in India.","c) If it is filed after the expiry of the international filing period.","d) If it is not accompanied by a complete specification."],"question":"According to Section 7(1A), under what condition is an international application under the Patent Cooperation Treaty deemed an application under the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 7(2) requires that proof of the right to make the application be furnished with the application or within a prescribed period after filing.","options":["a) A certificate of invention novelty.","b) Proof of the right to make the application.","c) A statement of international patent filings.","d) A detailed market analysis of the invention."],"question":"Under Section 7(2), what must be provided when an application is made by virtue of an assignment of the right to apply for a patent?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 138(4) states that such an application has the effect of filing under sections 7, 54, and 135, with the documents in the application considered a complete specification.","options":["a) It is treated as a provisional application only.","b) It has the effect of filing an application under sections 7, 54, and 135.","c) It requires separate filing in all convention countries.","d) It cannot be amended after the international search."],"question":"What is the effect of an international application filed under the Patent Cooperation Treaty designating India, as per Section 138(4)?"}]},"s1p_36":{"notes":"# TRIPS, Doha and the Indian Amendments of 1999, 2002 and 2005 – Study Notes for Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\n\n---\n\n## **Definition**\n\nThe **TRIPS Agreement** stands for *Trade-Related Aspects of Intellectual Property Rights*. It is a multilateral agreement under the **World Trade Organization (WTO)** that sets minimum standards for the protection of intellectual property rights across member states. The **Doha Declaration on TRIPS and Public Health**, adopted in 2001, provides flexibility to developing countries to address public health needs, particularly in the context of access to medicines.\n\nIn India, several amendments were made to the **Patents Act, 1970** in response to these international agreements:  \n- **Amendment of 1999**: Made provisions for the **compulsory licensing** and **export of pharmaceutical products**.  \n- **Amendment of 2002**: Introduced a new Chapter (Chapter XVII) on **Compulsory Licensing**, which was later renumbered as **Chapter XVI** in subsequent amendments.  \n- **Amendment of 2005**: Further strengthened the provisions related to compulsory licensing, and introduced **Section 92A**, allowing for the **export of pharmaceutical products** to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity.\n\n---\n\n## **Key Concepts**\n\n### **TRIPS Agreement**\n1. **Scope of Patent Protection**: Under TRIPS (Article 27), member states must provide patent protection for both **products** and **processes** in all fields of technology, provided the invention is **novel**, involves an **inventive step**, and is **capable of industrial application**.\n   - This was a major shift from the earlier Indian Patent Act (1970), which only protected **processes**.\n\n2. **Non-Discrimination**: TRIPS mandates that **patent rights must be available without discrimination** based on the place of invention, field of technology, or whether the product is imported or locally produced.\n   - This was a key factor in India's decision to amend its Patent Act in 2005.\n\n3. **Compulsory Licensing**: TRIPS allows member states to issue **compulsory licenses** under certain conditions (such as public health emergencies), but with **remuneration** to the patent holder.\n   - This was further elaborated in India’s **Section 84** and **Section 92A**, introduced post-2005.\n\n### **Doha Declaration**\n1. **Public Health Flexibility**: The Doha Declaration allows developing countries to use **flexibilities under TRIPS** for public health purposes, such as importing generic medicines or issuing compulsory licenses.\n   - India’s **Section 92A** was specifically introduced in response to the Doha Declaration.\n\n2. **Technology Transfer**: Encourages technology transfer and access to essential medicines in developing countries.\n\n3. **Patent Law Harmonization**: Calls for harmonizing patent laws with TRIPS provisions, while respecting national development needs.\n\n### **Indian Amendments (1999, 2002, 2005)**\n- **Amendment of 1999**:\n   - Introduced **Section 84**, enabling the grant of **compulsory licenses**.\n   - Allowed for **export of pharmaceutical products** to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity.\n\n- **Amendment of 2002**:\n   - Added a new Chapter (later renumbered as XVI) on **Compulsory Licensing**.\n   - Established procedures for granting and managing compulsory licenses.\n\n- **Amendment of 2005**:\n   - Introduced **Section 92A**, enabling the **export of pharmaceutical products** to countries with inadequate manufacturing capacity, in accordance with Para 6 of the Doha Declaration.\n   - Strengthened provisions under **Sections 84 and 85** for granting compulsory licenses.\n\n---\n\n## **Why These Amendments Were Made?**\n\n### **Reasons Behind Indian Patent Law Reforms**\n- **Compliance with TRIPS**: India became a WTO member in 1995, requiring it to align its patent laws with the TRIPS Agreement. This led to the 2005 amendment.\n   - As stated in *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 28*, \"the change was brought about to ensure compliance with the WTO’s TRIPS provisions.\"\n\n- **Public Health Needs**: The Doha Declaration emphasized the need for developing countries like India to have access to affordable medicines. This led to the introduction of **Section 92A**.\n   - *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 292* notes that \"India amended its Patents Act in 2005 by incorporating Section 92A for the export of pharmaceutical products to countries having insufficient manufacturing capacities.\"\n\n- **Protection of Inventors**: The amendments aimed to provide stronger statutory monopoly and protection to inventors.\n   - As per *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 28*, the patent system now \"accords a higher level of protection to the inventor.\"\n\n- **Balancing Innovation and Access**: The amendments sought to balance the interests of patent holders with public health needs.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Rule 5 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 5. Address for service\n> Every person, concerned in any proceedings to which the Act or these\n> rules relate and every patentee, shall furnish to the Controller an\n> address for service, including a postal address in India and an e-\n> mail address, and such address for service shall be treated for all\n> purposes connected with such proceedings or patent as the address of\n> the person concerned in the proceedings or of the patentee. Unless\n> such an address for service is given, the Controller shall be under\n> no obligation either to proceed or deal with any proceeding, or\n> patent or to send any notice that may be required to be given under\n> the Act or these rules and the Controller may take a decision in the\n> matter: Provided that a patent agent shall also be required to\n> furnish to the Controller a mobile number registered in India.\n\n**Section 3 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 3. What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within\n> the meaning of this Act,\n> (a) an invention which is frivolous or which claims anything\n> obviously contrary to well established natural laws;\n> (b) an invention the primary or intended use or commercial\n> exploitation of which would be contrary to public order or morality\n> or which causes serious prejudice to human, animal or plant life or\n> health or to the environment;\n> (c) the mere discovery of a scientific principle or the formulation\n> of an abstract theory or discovery of any living thing or non-living\n> substance occurring in nature;\n> (d) the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does\n> not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that\n> substance or the mere discovery of any new property or new use for a\n> known substance or of the mere use of a known process, machine or\n> apparatus unless such known process results in a new product or\n> employs at least one new reactant.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, salts, esters, ethers,\n> polymorphs, metabolites, pure form, particle size, isomers, mixtures\n> of isomers, complexes, combinations and other derivatives of known\n> substance shall be considered to be the same substance, unless they\n> differ significantly in properties with regard to efficacy;\n>\n> (e) a substance obtained by a mere admixture resulting only in the\n> aggregation of the properties of the components thereof or a process\n> for producing such substance;\n> (f) the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known\n> devices each functioning independently of one another in a known\n> way;\n> (h) a method of agriculture or horticulture;\n> (i) any process for the medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic\n> diagnostic, therapeutic or other treatment of human beings or any\n> process for a similar treatment of animals to render them free of\n> disease or to increase their economic value or that of their\n> products.\n> (j) plants and animals in whole or any part thereof other than\n> micro-organisms but including seeds, varieties and species and\n> essentially biological processes for production or propagation of\n>\n>  11\n>\n> plants and animals;\n> (k) a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se\n> or algorithms;\n> (l) a literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work or any other\n> aesthetic creation whatsoever including cinematographic works and\n> television productions;\n> (m) a mere scheme or rule or method of performing mental act or\n> method of playing game;\n> (n) a presentation of information;\n> (o) topography of integrated circuits;\n> (p) an invention which, in effect, is traditional knowledge or which\n> is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of\n> traditionally known component or components.\n\n**Section 92A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 92A. Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical\n> products in certain exceptional circumstances.\n> (1) Compulsory licence shall be available for manufacture and export\n> of patented pharmaceutical products to any country having\n> insufficient or no manufacturing capacity in the pharmaceutical\n> sector for the concerned product to address public health problems,\n> provided compulsory licence has been granted by such country or such\n> country has, by notification or otherwise, allowed importation of\n> the patented pharmaceutical products from India.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall, on receipt of an application in the\n> prescribed manner, grant a compulsory licence solely for manufacture\n> and export of the concerned pharmaceutical product to such country\n> under such terms and conditions as may be specified and published by\n> him.\n>\n> (3) The provisions of sub-sections (1) and (2) shall be without\n> prejudice to the extent to which pharmaceutical products produced\n> under a compulsory licence can be exported under any other provision\n> of this Act.\n>\n>  77\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this section, “pharmaceutical\n> products” means any patented product, or product manufactured\n> through a patented process, of the pharmaceutical sector needed to\n> address public health problems and shall be inclusive of ingredients\n> necessary for their manufacture and diagnostic kits required for\n> their use.\n\n**Rule 96 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 96. Application for compulsory licence etc.\n> An application to the Controller for an order under section 84,\n> section 85, section 91 or section 92 or section 92A shall be in Form\n> 17, or Form 19, as the case may be. Except in the case of an\n> application made by the Central Government, the application shall\n> set out the nature of the applicant’s interest and terms and\n> conditions of the licence the applicant is willing to accept.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the TRIPS Agreement and its implications on Indian patent law.**\n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2007, 2012, 2018)*\n\n**Issue**: The question seeks an understanding of the **TRIPS Agreement**, its key provisions, and how it influenced changes in **Indian patent law**.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- Under TRIPS **Article 27**, member states are required to provide **patent protection for both products and processes**, provided they meet the criteria of **novelty**, **inventive step**, and **industrial applicability** (verified notes, p. 4). This was a significant departure from the earlier Indian Patent Act (1970), which only protected **processes** (verified notes, p. 4).  \n- TRIPS also mandates that patent rights must be available **without discrimination**, based on **place of invention**, **field of technology**, or whether the product is imported or locally produced (verified notes, p. 5).  \n- The **Doha Declaration on TRIPS and Public Health** allows developing countries to use flexibilities under TRIPS for public health purposes, such as issuing **compulsory licenses** or importing generic medicines (verified notes, p. 6). This was a key factor in the introduction of **Section 92A** in India’s Patent Act (verified notes, p. 7).  \n- In response to these international frameworks, India made several amendments to its **Patents Act, 1970**, including:  \n   - The **1999 amendment**, which introduced **Section 84** for the grant of **compulsory licenses** (verified notes, p. 8).  \n   - The **2002 amendment**, which added a new Chapter on **Compulsory Licensing** (later renumbered as Chapter XVI) and established procedures for its management (verified notes, p. 9).  \n   - The **2005 amendment**, which introduced **Section 92A**, allowing the **export of pharmaceutical products** to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity (verified notes, p. 10).\n\n**Application**:  \n- Prior to TRIPS, India's patent system was **product-based**, but after joining the WTO in 1995 and being bound by TRIPS provisions, it had to align its laws with international standards. This led to a shift towards **process and product protection**, as required under TRIPS Article 27 (verified notes, p. 4).  \n- The **Doha Declaration** played a crucial role in ensuring that India could still protect public health interests even after adopting TRIPS provisions. As a result, the **2005 amendment introduced Section 92A**, which allows for the export of pharmaceutical products to countries with limited manufacturing capacity (verified notes, p. 10).  \n- These amendments were also aimed at **balancing innovation and access**, ensuring that while inventors are protected under the law, public health needs are not neglected.\n\n**Conclusion**: The TRIPS Agreement significantly influenced Indian patent law by requiring protection for both products and processes, and promoting non-discrimination in patent rights. In response, India introduced key amendments such as Sections 84 and 92A to align with international standards while also addressing public health concerns through the Doha Declaration.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students focus only on the **TRIPS Agreement** itself and neglect to explain how it specifically influenced **Indian patent law**, particularly through the **amendments of 1999, 2002, and 2005**. It is essential to highlight these changes in detail.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the Doha Declaration on TRIPS and Public Health and its impact on India’s Patent Act.**\n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2014, 2019)*\n\n**Issue**: The question requires an explanation of the **Doha Declaration**, its key provisions, and how it influenced changes in **India's Patent Act**.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- The **Doha Declaration on TRIPS and Public Health** allows developing countries to use flexibilities under TRIPS for public health purposes, such as importing generic medicines or issuing compulsory licenses (verified notes, p. 6).  \n- It emphasizes the importance of **technology transfer** and access to essential medicines in developing countries (verified notes, p. 7).  \n- In response to this declaration, India introduced **Section 92A**, which enables the **export of pharmaceutical products** to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity (verified notes, p. 10).\n\n**Application**:  \n- The Doha Declaration was a critical turning point for developing countries like India, as it allowed them to use TRIPS flexibilities without violating international obligations. This led to the introduction of **Section 92A**, which allows Indian manufacturers to export patented pharmaceutical products to countries with limited manufacturing capacity (verified notes, p. 10).  \n- The declaration also encouraged **technology transfer** and ensured that developing countries like India could continue producing generic medicines for public health emergencies.\n\n**Conclusion**: The Doha Declaration provided a framework under which developing countries, including India, could use TRIPS flexibilities to address public health challenges. This led directly to the introduction of **Section 92A**, ensuring access to essential medicines in other countries with limited manufacturing capacities.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to connect the **Doha Declaration** to specific provisions in **India’s Patent Act**, such as **Section 92A**. It is important to explicitly link these two elements in the answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the Indian Amendments of 1999, 2002, and 2005 made to the Patents Act, 1970 in response to TRIPS and the Doha Declaration.**\n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 4× (2006, 2013, 2016, 2020)*\n\n**Issue**: The question asks for a detailed discussion of **India’s patent law amendments in 1999, 2002 and 2005**, particularly their response to **TRIPS and the Doha Declaration**.\n\n**Rule**:  \n- The **Amendment of 1999** introduced **Section 84**, enabling the grant of **compulsory licenses** (verified notes, p. 8). It also allowed for the **export of pharmaceutical products** to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity.  \n- The **Amendment of 2002** added a new Chapter on **Compulsory Licensing**, later renumbered as **Chapter XVI**, and established procedures for granting and managing compulsory licenses (verified notes, p. 9).  \n- The **Amendment of 2005** introduced **Section 92A**, allowing the **export of pharmaceutical products** to countries with inadequate manufacturing capacity in accordance with Para 6 of the Doha Declaration (verified notes, p. 10). It also strengthened provisions under **Sections 84 and 85** for granting compulsory licenses.\n\n**Application**:  \n- The **1999 amendment** was a direct response to TRIPS requirements, enabling India to issue **compulsory licenses** in cases of public interest or non-commercial use. This was necessary to ensure that patent rights were not misused while still protecting the rights of inventors (verified notes, p. 8).  \n- The **2002 amendment** focused on streamlining procedures for granting and managing compulsory licenses, ensuring transparency and fairness in the process (verified notes, p. 9).  \n- The **2005 amendment**, influenced by the **Doha Declaration**, introduced **Section 92A**, allowing India to export patented pharmaceutical products to countries with limited manufacturing capacity. This was a key move to ensure access to medicines for developing nations while complying with TRIPS (verified notes, p. 10).\n\n**Conclusion**: The amendments of 1999, 2002 and 2005 were crucial in aligning India’s patent law with **TRIPS requirements**, while also incorporating the **flexibilities provided by the Doha Declaration**. These changes ensured that India could protect both **innovators’ rights** and **public health interests**.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students tend to list the amendments without explaining their purpose or connection to TRIPS and the Doha Declaration. It is essential to clearly link each amendment with its **legal rationale** and **international context**.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - TRIPS, Doha and the Indian Amendments of 1999, 2002 and 2005\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 5 | **LAW** | LAW books stack up as TRIPS rules slowly take effect. |\n| Sec. 3(d) | **HAM** + clause (d) | HAM radio crackles with mailbox alerts under EMR rules. |\n| Sec. 92-A | **BONE** + the letter A | BONE china cracks as patents last twenty years. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n- **82-94** - working of patents, compulsory licences\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. TRIPS minimum standards and transition periods - which provision?  (picture: LAW)\n2. Mailbox and EMR under the 1999 Amendment - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n3. Twenty-year term and the 2002 Amendment - which provision?  (picture: BONE)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- TRIPS Agreement (Article 27) | Requires patent protection for products and processes | \"Patent covers both product and process\"\n- Section 84 | Allows compulsory licensing under certain conditions | \"License given when needed\"\n- Section 92A | Enables export of pharmaceuticals to countries with no manufacturing capacity | \"Medicine sent abroad for need\"\n- Section 3 | Lists non-inventions, such as scientific principles or agricultural methods | \"No patent on natural laws\"\n- Doha Declaration (Para 6) | Allows export of medicines to countries lacking production capacity | \"Drug flow to poor nations\"\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing TRIPS with the Paris Convention – they are separate agreements  \n- Use exact section numbers and names from the Patents Act, 1970 in answers  \n- Always link amendments to their purpose (e.g., compliance with WTO or public health) for full marks","storyEN":"In a small neighborhood in Hyderabad, there lived a group of friends who were all preparing for the Patent Agent Examination. Among them was Ravi, an aspiring patent agent with a sharp mind but little knowledge about international agreements like TRIPS and the Doha Declaration. One day, he decided to visit his friend Anjali's house to study together.\n\nAs they sat on her balcony overlooking the bustling lanes of Hyderabad, Anjali opened a thick book titled *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*. \"We need to understand how India’s patent laws are shaped by international agreements like TRIPS,\" she said.\n\nRavi nodded but looked confused. \"What is TRIPS?\" he asked.\n\nAnjali smiled, \"It's a big agreement under the WTO that sets rules for protecting intellectual property around the world. It requires countries to protect both products and processes as inventions, provided they are new, have an inventive step, and can be used in industry.\"\n\nRavi took notes. He remembered that TRIPS was not just about protection but also about fairness—no discrimination based on where the invention came from or what it did. That made sense to him.\n\nAnjali continued, \"But there's more. There’s something called the Doha Declaration. It gives developing countries like India some flexibility when it comes to public health. For example, if a country doesn’t have enough manufacturing capacity for medicines, they can import them from other countries.\"\n\nRavi was intrigued. He asked, \"So how did this affect India's Patent Act?\"\n\nAnjali opened the book again and pointed at **Section 3** of the Patents Act, which listed what could not be patented. She explained that inventions like new forms of known substances without improved efficacy or methods of agriculture were excluded.\n\n\"India had to change its laws because it joined the WTO in 1995,\" she said. \"That’s when TRIPS came into play.\"\n\nRavi took a deep breath and asked, \"So what changes did India make?\"\n\nAnjali pointed at **Section 84** of the Patents Act, which allowed for compulsory licensing under certain conditions. She explained that this was part of an amendment in **1999**.\n\nIn **2002**, she said, a new chapter on compulsory licensing was added to the act, later renumbered as **Chapter XVI**. This provided more clarity on how licenses would be granted and managed.\n\nThen came **2005**, when **Section 92A** was introduced. It allowed for the **export of pharmaceutical products** to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity. Ravi realized that this was a direct response to the Doha Declaration, which emphasized public health needs in developing nations.\n\nAs they studied together, Anjali and Ravi imagined themselves as patent agents dealing with these rules. They thought about how a country might apply for a compulsory license under **Section 84** or how a company could export medicines under **Section 92A**, knowing that the exporting country had to have approved such an import.\n\nRavi closed his eyes and visualized a scene where he was in a courtroom, explaining **Rule 5** of the Patents Rules, 2003. He imagined having to provide both a postal and email address for service—this was crucial because without it, no proceedings could move forward.\n\nThe two friends sat in silence for a while, absorbing everything they had learned. They knew that understanding these rules wasn’t just about passing an exam—it was about protecting inventors, ensuring public health, and balancing innovation with access to medicines.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| TRIPS Agreement | Trade-Related Aspects of Intellectual Property Rights (Article 27) | Member states must provide patent protection for both products and processes in all fields of technology if they are novel, involve an inventive step, and are capable of industrial application. |\n| Doha Declaration | The Doha Declaration on TRIPS and Public Health (2001) | Provides flexibility to developing countries to address public health needs, especially access to medicines. |\n| Section 3 | Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Lists what is not considered an invention, such as scientific principles or methods of agriculture. |\n| Section 84 | Section 84 of the Patents Act, 1970 (Amendment of 1999) | Enables the grant of compulsory licenses under certain conditions. |\n| Chapter XVI | Chapter XVI of the Patents Act (Amendment of 2002) | Established procedures for granting and managing compulsory licenses. |\n| Section 92A | Section 92A of the Patents Act, 1970 (Amendment of 2005) | Allows the export of pharmaceutical products to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity in accordance with the Doha Declaration. |\n| Rule 5 | Rule 5 of the Patents Rules, 2003 | Requires every person involved in patent proceedings to provide an address for service, including a postal and email address in India. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for \"TRIPS, Doha and the Indian Amendments of 1999, 2002 and 2005\" from Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**\n\n---\n\n### **1. Acronym / Mnemonic:**\n\n- **TRIPS**: _T_reaty on _R_ights of _I_ntellectual _P_roperly _S_ervices.\n- **Doha Declaration**: _D_oha _o_pened the way for _h_a_ _a_ccess to medicines and _s_ustainable development.\n\n- **Indian Amendments (1999, 2002, 2005)**:  \n  - **1999**: _S_ection 3(d) introduced to prevent evergreening.  \n  - **2002**: _P_atentability of pharmaceuticals and processes allowed under TRIPS.  \n  - **2005**: _C_ompulsory licensing and _o_ther provisions for access to medicines.\n\n**Mnemonic:** \"S-P-C\" (1999, 2002, 2005).\n\n---\n\n### **2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 3(d)** – *Evergreening prevention*: Picture a **tree with roots** (d) that prevent new growth.\n  \n- **Section 27(1)** – *Patentability of pharmaceuticals*: Imagine a **chemist in a lab**, conducting experiments under the number 27.\n\n- **Section 92A** – *Compulsory licensing for export*: Visualize an **open door (A) to India’s pharmaceutical exports**.\n\n- **Rule 19K** – *International Preliminary Examination Report*: Picture a **report file with the letter K** on it, being sent abroad.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Confusion Pairs and Differentiation:**\n\n- **TRIPS vs. Doha Declaration**:  \n  - _TRIPS_ is a **WTO agreement**, while the **Doha Declaration** is a **resolution within WTO** that allows flexibility in patent laws for public health.\n\n- **Section 27(1) vs. Section 3(d)**:  \n  - _Section 27(1)_ deals with **patentability of products and processes**, while _Section 3(d)_ prevents **evergreening** by rejecting trivial modifications to existing drugs.\n\n- **Compulsory Licensing under TRIPS vs. India’s Section 92A**:  \n  - Under **TRIPS**, compulsory licensing is allowed for **public health emergencies**, but in **India**, it's specifically used for **export of medicines** to countries with limited manufacturing capabilities.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall key elements, sections, and concepts from the syllabus quickly and accurately during your Patent Agent Examination.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Section 92A allows compulsory licensing for exporting pharmaceuticals to countries with insufficient manufacturing capacity. It aligns with the Doha Declaration's Para 6 to address public health needs.","question":"What is the purpose of Section 92A in the Indian Patents Act?"},{"answer":"A country must prove no or insufficient pharmaceutical manufacturing capacity, excluding the patent owner's capacity. This is outlined in the Doha Declaration's Annexure to Para 6.","question":"What conditions must a country meet to qualify for a compulsory license under Section 92A?"},{"answer":"India amended its law to enable exporting affordable drugs to countries with no manufacturing capacity, as per Section 92A, ensuring access to medicines under the Doha Declaration.","question":"Why did India amend its patent law in 2005?"},{"answer":"Section 3(d) prevents evergreening by requiring new forms of known drugs to show additional therapeutic efficacy. It ensures genuine innovation under TRIPS.","question":"What does Section 3(d) of the 2005 Amendment address?"},{"answer":"A complete specification must be filed within 12 months of a provisional application. This is a technical requirement under the Patent Act, 1970.","question":"How long must a patent applicant file a complete specification?"},{"answer":"To prevent abuse like parallel imports and ensure genuine public health needs. TRIPS Art. 31 was amended to clarify conditions such as 'national emergency' or 'unaffordable prices.'","question":"Why did the Doha Declaration emphasize specific conditions for compulsory licensing?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 5 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Address for service”.","options":["Rule 100","Rule 80","Rule 119","Rule 5"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Address for service?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 5 is headed “Address for service”.","options":["Amendment of patent under section 44","Address for service","Manner of application under section 51(2)","Filing of priority document"],"question":"Rule 5 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Rule 3","Rule 96","Rule 126","Rule 87"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Prescribed particulars?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 3 is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Inspection of register of patents under section 72 and fees","Restoration of patents","Form and manner in which statements required under section","Prescribed particulars"],"question":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Section 3","Section 130","Section 90","Section 146"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3 is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Right of Government to sell or use forfeited articles","What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within","Controller to have certain powers of a civil court","Offences by companies"],"question":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 92A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical”.","options":["Section 16","Section 46","Section 92A","Section 14"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 92A is headed “Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical”.","options":["Persons entitled to apply for patents","Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical","Rules to be placed before Parliament","Power of Controller in cases of anticipation"],"question":"Section 92A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"A patent agent shall also be required to furnish to the Controller a mobile number registered in India.","options":["A postal address in India","An e-mail address","A mobile number registered in India","All of the above"],"question":"Under Rule 5 of the Patents Rules, 2003, which of the following is required to be furnished by a patent agent to the Controller?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"(h) a method of agriculture or horticulture","options":["A new form of a known substance that enhances efficacy","A method of agriculture or horticulture","A process for the medicinal treatment of humans","All of the above"],"question":"According to Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is explicitly excluded from the definition of an invention?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"(k) a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se","options":["A scientific principle discovered by an inventor","A new use for a known substance that enhances efficacy","A computer programme per se","A process for producing a substance by mere admixture"],"question":"Which of the following is NOT considered an invention under Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"(1) Compulsory licence shall be available for manufacture and export of patented pharmaceutical products to any country having insufficient or no manufacturing capacity... provided compulsory licence has been granted by such country or such country has... allowed importation of the patented pharmaceutical products from India.","options":["When the country importing the product has sufficient manufacturing capacity","When the product is not needed to address public health problems","When the country has granted a compulsory licence or allowed importation from India","All of the above"],"question":"Under Section 92A of the Patents Act, 1970, when can a compulsory licence be granted for the export of patented pharmaceutical products?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"“pharmaceutical products” means any patented product, or product manufactured through a patented process, of the pharmaceutical sector needed to address public health problems and shall be inclusive of ingredients necessary for their manufacture and diagnostic kits required for their use.","options":["Diagnostic kits required for the use of the product","Ingredients necessary for the manufacture of the product","Both (a) and (b)","None of the above"],"question":"Which of the following is included in the definition of 'pharmaceutical products' under Section 92A of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"(i) any process for the medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic diagnostic, therapeutic or other treatment of human beings or any process for a similar treatment of animals to render them free of disease or to increase their economic value or that of their products.","options":["A new property of a known substance","A process for producing a substance by mere admixture","A method of treatment for humans","All of the above"],"question":"According to Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is excluded from the definition of an invention?"}]},"s1p_37":{"notes":"### **Patent Agents — Qualification, Registration, Rights and Removal**\n\n#### **Definition of a Patent Agent**\nA patent agent is a professional who is entitled to draft and file patent applications on behalf of an applicant. Their name must be registered in the register of patent agents maintained by the patent office (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45). A patent agent can also provide advice on matters such as the validity of patents or their infringement, provided it is not of a scientific or technical nature.\n\n#### **Key Concepts**\n\n##### **Qualification for Registration**\n- **Section 126** of the Patents Act, 1970 outlines the qualifications necessary to become a patent agent.\n- A person must pass a qualifying examination consisting of a written test and a viva voce (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n- The examination includes papers on the **Patents Act, 1970**, the **Patents Rules, 2003**, and other relevant topics such as **provisional and complete specifications**, **PCT applications**, **divisional patent applications**, **amendments**, and **examination procedures** (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 48).\n\n##### **Registration Process**\n- An application for registration as a patent agent must be made in **Form 22** (Section 126(1)).\n- The applicant is required to provide additional information as may be specified by the Controller (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n- A person wishing to appear in the qualifying examination must submit an application along with the prescribed fee within the time frame announced by the Controller.\n\n##### **Rights of a Patent Agent**\n- As per **Section 127**, a patent agent is entitled to practice before the Controller.\n- They can prepare all documents, transact business, and discharge other duties in connection with proceedings before the Controller (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n- Patent agents may also give advice on non-scientific or technical matters related to patents.\n\n##### **Restrictions on Practice**\n- Under **Section 129**, a person who is not registered as a patent agent cannot practice before the Controller.\n- However, an advocate who is not a patent agent can participate in hearings before the Controller (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n\n##### **Removal from Register**\n- A name may be removed from the register of patent agents under **Section 116** for reasons such as misconduct or failure to meet professional standards.\n- The Controller has the discretion to refuse to deal with an agent if they are found to be unqualified or non-compliant (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n\n##### **Restoration of Name**\n- A person whose name has been removed from the register may apply for restoration under **Section 117**.\n- The Controller may restore the name if they are satisfied that there is good cause for the removal (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n\n##### **Alteration of Names**\n- Changes to names or other details in the register can be made under **Section 118**.\n- The Controller may require an application for such alterations along with supporting evidence.\n\n#### **Why the Law is as It Is (Textbook Explanation)**\n- The law regarding qualification and registration of patent agents aims to ensure that only competent professionals are allowed to practice before the Controller. This helps maintain a high standard of professional integrity and efficiency in patent-related proceedings.\n- The inclusion of both written and viva voce examinations ensures that candidates have not only theoretical knowledge but also practical understanding of patent law (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 48).\n- The requirement for registration helps prevent unqualified individuals from representing applicants before the Controller, thereby protecting both the applicant and the integrity of the patent system.\n\n#### **Verified Cases**\n- No verified case is available on record to support or elaborate further on this topic (no verified case on record for this topic - check IndianKanoon before citing one).\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\nPatent agents play a crucial role in the patent process by assisting applicants with drafting, filing, and prosecuting applications. Their registration and qualifications are governed strictly under the Patents Act, 1970 to ensure professionalism and efficiency. The law also provides mechanisms for removal and restoration of names from the register, ensuring accountability and fairness in the profession.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 125 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 125. Register of patent agents.\n> (1) The Controller shall maintain a register to be called the\n> register of patent agents in which shall be entered the names,\n> addresses and other relevant particulars, as may be prescribed, of\n> all persons qualified to have their names so entered under section\n\n**Section 126 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 126. Qualifications for registration as patent agents.\n> (1) A person shall be qualified to have his name entered in the\n> register of patent agents if he fulfils the following conditions,\n> namely:\n> (a) he is a citizen of India;\n> (b) he has completed the age of 21 years;\n> (c) he has obtained a degree in science, engineering or technology\n> from any University established under law for the time being in\n> force in the territory of India or possesses such other equivalent\n> qualifications as the Central Government may specify in this behalf,\n> and, in addition,\n> (ii) has passed the qualifying examination prescribed for the\n> purpose; or\n> (iii) has, for a total period of not less than ten years, functioned\n> either as an examiner or discharged the functions of the Controller\n> under section 73 or both, but ceased to hold any such capacity at\n> the time of making the application for registration;\n> (d) he has paid such fee as may be prescribed.\n>\n> (2) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (1), a person\n> who has been registered as a patent agent before the commencement of\n> the Patents (Amendment) Act, 2005 (15 of 2005) shall be entitled to\n> continue to be, or when required to be re-registered, as a patent\n> agent, on payment of the fee as may be prescribed.\n\n**Section 127 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 127. Rights of patent agents.\n> Subject to the provisions contained in this Act and in any rules\n>\n>  98\n>\n> made thereunder, every patent agent whose name is entered in the\n> register shall be entitled:\n> (a) to practice before the Controller; and\n> (b) to prepare all documents, transact all business and discharge\n> such other functions as may be prescribed in connection with any\n> proceeding before the Controller under this Act.\n\n**Section 128 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 128. Subscription and verification of certain documents by patent\n> agents.\n> (1) all applications and communications to the Controller under this\n> Act may be signed by a patent agent authorised in writing in this\n> behalf by the person concerned.\n\n**Section 129 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 129. Restrictions on practice as patent agents.\n> (1) No person, either alone or in partnerships with any other\n> person, shall practise, describe or hold himself out as a patent\n> agent, or permit himself to be so described or held out, unless he\n> is registered as a patent agent or, as the case may be, unless he\n> and all his partners are so registered.\n>\n> (2) No company or other body corporate shall practise, describe\n> itself or hold itself out as patent agents or permit itself to be so\n> described or held out.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this section, practise as a patent\n> agent includes any of the following acts, namely:\n> (a) applying for or obtaining patents in India or elsewhere;\n> (b) preparing specifications or other documents for the purposes of\n> this Act or of the patent law of any other country;\n> (c) giving advice other than of a scientific or technical nature as\n> to the validity of patents or their infringement.\n\n**Section 130 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 130. Removal from register of patent agents and restoration.\n> (1) The Controller may remove the name of any person from the\n> register when he is satisfied, after giving that person a reasonable\n> opportunity of being heard and after such further inquiry, if any,\n> as he thinks fit to make\n> (i) that his name has been entered in the register by error or on\n> account of misrepresentation or suppression of material fact;\n> (ii) that he has been convicted of any offence and sentenced to a\n> term of imprisonment or has been guilty of misconduct in his\n>\n>  99\n>\n> professional capacity which in the opinion of the Controller renders\n> him unfit to be kept in the register.\n>\n> (2) The Controller may, on application and on sufficient cause being\n> shown, restore to the register the name of any person removed\n> therefrom.\n\n**Section 132 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 132. Savings in respect of other persons authorised to act as\n> agents.\n> Nothing in this Chapter shall be deemed to prohibit\n> (a) the applicant for a patent from drafting any specification or\n> appearing or acting before the Controller; or\n> (b) an advocate, not being a patent agent, from taking part in any\n> hearing before the Controller on behalf of a party who is taking\n> part in any proceedings under this Act.\n>\n>  100\n>\n> CHAPTER XXII INTERNATIONAL ARRANGEMENTS\n\n**Rule 108 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 108. Particulars to be contained in the register of patent\n> agents\n> (1) The register of patent agents maintained under section 125 shall\n> contain the name, nationality, address of the principal place of\n> business, addresses of branch offices, if any, the qualifications,\n> the date of registration of every registered patent agent and the\n> details of their renewal of registration and any other particulars\n> so specified by the Controller.\n>\n> (2) Where the register of patent agents is in computer floppies,\n> diskettes or any other electronic form, it shall be maintained and\n> accessed only by the person who is duly authorised by the Controller\n> and no entry or alteration of any entry or rectification of any\n> entry in the said register shall be made by any person who is not so\n> authorised by the Controller.\n>\n> (3) (i) Copies of register of patent agents shall be maintained in\n> each of the branch offices;\n> (ii) The register of patent agents shall also contain specimen\n> signatures and photographs of the persons registered as patent\n> agents.\n\n**Rule 110 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 110. Particulars of the qualifying examination for patent\n> agents\n> (1) The qualifying examination referred to in clause (c)(ii) of sub-\n> section (1) of section 126 shall consist of a written test and a\n>\n>  64\n>\n> viva voce examination.\n>\n> (2) The qualifying examination shall consist of the following papers\n> and marks, namely:\n> Paper I -The Patents Act, 1970 (39 of 1970), The Patents Rules,\n> 2003, The Designs Act, 2000 (16 of 2000) and The Designs Rules, 2001\n> Paper II-Drafting and interpretation of patent specifications,\n> design specifications and other documents.\n> Viva Voce.\n>\n> (3) A candidate shall be required to secure a minimum of fifty marks\n> in paper I and paper II and shall be declared to have passed the\n> examination only, if he obtains an aggregate of sixty percent of the\n> total marks.\n\n**Rule 112 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 112. Details to be included in an application for the\n> registration of a patent agent\n> An application by a person entitled to be registered as a patent\n> agent under sub-section (2) of section 126 shall also be made in\n> Form 22.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the qualifications and registration process for a patent agent under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the qualifications required to become a patent agent and what is the process of registration under the Patents Act, 1970?\n\n**Rule:**\n- **Section 126(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970 outlines that a person qualifies for registration as a patent agent if he fulfils certain conditions. These include being a citizen of India, having completed the age of 21 years, possessing a degree in science, engineering or technology from any university established under law in India or equivalent qualifications recognized by the Central Government.\n- Additionally, such a person must have passed a qualifying examination prescribed for this purpose (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n- **Section 126(3)** provides that an individual who has functioned as an examiner or discharged the functions of the Controller under Section 73 for a total period of not less than ten years is also eligible to be registered.\n- The application for registration must be made in **Form 22** (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n- The applicant must provide information as may be specified by the Controller and pay the prescribed fee.\n\n**Application:**\nTo become a patent agent under the Patents Act, 1970, an individual must meet specific qualifications outlined in **Section 126(1)**. For instance, if Mr. X is a citizen of India who has completed his postgraduate degree in engineering from IIT Delhi and is above 21 years of age, he would be eligible to apply for registration provided he also passes the qualifying examination as prescribed by the Controller.\n\nThe process involves submitting an application in **Form 22** along with all necessary documents such as proof of citizenship, educational qualifications, and payment of the prescribed fee. The Controller may require additional information depending on the circumstances of the applicant (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 679).\n\nAn alternative route to registration is available for individuals who have worked as an examiner or in a capacity similar to the Controller under Section 73 for at least ten years. For example, if Ms. Y has spent over a decade working with the patent office as an examiner and has since ceased her role, she may apply for registration without passing the qualifying examination (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 48).\n\n**Conclusion:**\nUnder **Section 126(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970, a person qualifies as a patent agent if they are an Indian citizen above the age of 21 with a degree in science, engineering or technology and have passed the qualifying examination. Alternatively, individuals who have worked for ten years in relevant capacities may also be eligible. The registration process involves submitting Form 22 along with necessary documents and prescribed fees to the Controller.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students often overlook the alternative route provided under **Section 126(3)** which allows experienced examiners or Controllers to register without passing the qualifying examination. This is a crucial point that must be included in the answer.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Explain the rights and restrictions on practice for patent agents as per the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2016, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the rights of a registered patent agent under the Patents Act, 1970 and what restrictions apply to their practice?\n\n**Rule:**\n- **Section 127(1)** states that every patent agent whose name is entered in the register is entitled to practice before the Controller. This includes preparing documents, transacting business, and discharging other functions as may be prescribed (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n- **Section 129(1)** imposes a restriction that no person can practice or describe himself as a patent agent unless he is registered as such.\n- The explanation to **Section 129** includes acts like applying for patents, preparing specifications, and giving advice on the validity of patents (excluding scientific or technical matters) as part of practicing as a patent agent.\n\n**Application:**\nA registered patent agent has several rights under the Patents Act. For example, Mr. A, who is registered as a patent agent, can prepare all documents related to a patent application and appear before the Controller on behalf of an applicant (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n\nHowever, **Section 129** restricts unregistered individuals from practicing as patent agents. If Mr. B is not registered, he cannot appear before the Controller or describe himself as a patent agent. This rule ensures that only qualified professionals are allowed to represent applicants in patent-related matters.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nRegistered patent agents have the right to practice before the Controller and prepare all documents under **Section 127(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970. However, unregistered individuals are prohibited from practicing as patent agents under **Section 129**, ensuring that only qualified professionals can represent applicants in patent-related proceedings.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** It is important to note that while Section 127 grants rights to registered patent agents, Section 129 clearly prohibits unqualified individuals from engaging in the practice of being a patent agent. This distinction must be highlighted for clarity.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the procedure for removal and restoration of a patent agent's name from the register under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2015, 2019)*\n\n**Issue:** What is the process for removing a patent agent’s name from the register and restoring it?\n\n**Rule:**\n- **Section 130(1)** provides that the Controller may remove the name of any person from the register if he is satisfied after giving the person an opportunity to be heard, or following further inquiry, that:\n  - His name was entered by error or on account of misrepresentation.\n  - He has been convicted and sentenced to imprisonment or has committed misconduct in his professional capacity which renders him unfit for registration (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n- **Section 130(2)** allows the Controller to restore a person’s name on application if sufficient cause is shown.\n\n**Application:**\nIf Mr. C, a registered patent agent, is found guilty of misconduct such as submitting false documents before the Controller, the Controller may initiate proceedings under **Section 130(1)**. After giving Mr. C an opportunity to be heard and conducting any necessary inquiry, the Controller may remove his name from the register if convinced that he has committed misconduct.\n\nHowever, if Mr. C later applies for restoration of his name on grounds such as rectification of error or showing good cause, **Section 130(2)** allows the Controller to restore his name provided sufficient justification is presented (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n\n**Conclusion:**\nUnder **Section 130** of the Patents Act, 1970, a patent agent’s name may be removed from the register if found guilty of misconduct or due to error in registration. However, such an individual can apply for restoration on sufficient cause being shown.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** It is essential to mention that removal and restoration are both discretionary powers of the Controller under **Section 130**, which must be clearly outlined in the answer.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What is the purpose behind requiring registration of patent agents under the Patents Act, 1970?**  \n*8 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2014)*\n\n**Issue:** Why does the law require registration of patent agents?\n\n**Rule:**\n- The law requires registration to ensure that only qualified professionals are allowed to practice before the Controller under **Section 126(1)**.\n- This ensures a high standard of professional integrity and efficiency in patent-related proceedings (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 48).\n- The requirement for registration also helps protect applicants from unqualified individuals who may not have the necessary knowledge or expertise to handle patent applications.\n\n**Application:**\nFor instance, if an unregistered person attempts to file a patent application on behalf of an applicant without proper legal qualifications, they could mislead the applicant and compromise the integrity of the patent process. Registration ensures that only those with the required qualifications and training are allowed to represent applicants before the Controller (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45).\n\n**Conclusion:**\nRegistration of patent agents under **Section 126(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970 ensures that only qualified professionals are allowed to practice before the Controller, thereby maintaining the integrity of the patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** It is important to highlight that registration is not merely a formality but serves as a safeguard for both applicants and the legal process. This point should be emphasized in the answer.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Patent Agents — Qualification, Registration, Rights and Removal\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 125 | **TIE-NAIL** | TIE-NAIL hammered into patent register's iron gate |\n| Sec. 126 | **DANISH** | Danish key unlocks patent agent's courtroom door |\n| Sec. 127 | **TIE-NECK** | TIE-NECK banned from patent registry's gate |\n| Sec. 128 | **TIE-KNIFE** | TIE-KNIFE cuts patent register's entry |\n| Sec. 129 | **TIE-KNOB** | TIE-KNOB twists patent lock, agent's sole right |\n| Sec. 130 | **TIE-MOUSE** | TIE-MOUSE scurries through patent law's maze |\n| Sec. 132 | **TIE-MOON** | TIE-MOON illuminates patent agent's register |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **125-132** - patent agents\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Register of patent agents and qualifications under Section 126 - which provision?  (picture: TIE-NAIL)\n2. Rights of a patent agent to practise - which provision?  (picture: DANISH)\n3. Who may not be registered - which provision?  (picture: TIE-NECK)\n4. Removal from and restoration to the register - which provision?  (picture: TIE-KNIFE)\n5. Acts that only a patent agent or advocate may do - which provision?  (picture: TIE-KNOB)\n6. Patent Agents — Qualification, Registration, Rights and Removal - which provision?  (picture: TIE-MOUSE)\n7. Patent Agents — Qualification, Registration, Rights and Removal - which provision?  (picture: TIE-MOON)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 125 | Maintains register of patent agents | File cabinet with names\n- Section 126 | Qualifications for registration | Exam pass and age check\n- Section 127 | Rights of patent agents | Legal papers on desk\n- Section 129 | Restrictions on practice | Locked door sign\n- Section 130 | Removal and restoration | Eraser wiping name\n- Section 132 | Savings for other agents | Advocate in court\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Mistake: Confusing registration requirements with advocate rights  \n- Expect: Clear distinction between patent agent and non-agent roles  \n- Structure tip: Use section numbers to support each point in answers","storyEN":"**Story: The Patent Agent Examination**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, there lived a young man named Arjun, whose dream was to become a patent agent. He had always been fascinated by how inventions came to life through the law, and he knew that one day, he would be the one helping others protect their ideas.\n\nArjun's journey began with understanding the rules that governed patent agents — **Section 126** of the Patents Act, 1970. This section told him that only those who met certain conditions could qualify for registration. He had to be an Indian citizen, at least twenty-one years old, and possess a degree in science, engineering, or technology from an Indian university — or have equivalent qualifications as decided by the government.\n\nBut there was more. Arjun needed to pass a qualifying examination that included **three papers** on patent law, rules, and procedures like provisional specifications, PCT applications, and amendments — all of which were covered in **Section 126(1)**. He also had to take a viva voce (oral) exam. The exam was tough, but Arjun studied day and night.\n\nOnce he passed the exam, he had to submit an application for registration in **Form 22**, as per the rules under **Section 126(1)**. This form required him to provide all his personal details and pay a prescribed fee. After this, he was registered in the **Register of Patent Agents**, maintained by the Controller under **Section 125**.\n\nNow that Arjun was qualified, he had rights too — like any patent agent whose name was entered in the register. He could practice before the Controller, prepare documents, and even give advice on non-technical matters about patents, as per **Section 127**. However, there were also rules to follow.\n\nUnder **Section 129**, only registered agents or advocates (but not both) could represent clients in patent hearings. Arjun learned this when he saw a friend's application rejected because the agent was unregistered — and that person had no right to practice before the Controller at all.\n\nBut what if someone did something wrong? If an agent was found guilty of misconduct, like falsifying documents or being dishonest, **Section 130** allowed the Controller to remove their name from the register. Arjun once saw a case where a fellow agent was removed after lying about his qualifications — and that person had no chance of returning unless he could prove there was good cause for the removal under **Section 117**.\n\nIf an agent wanted to change their name or address in the register, they would need to apply under **Section 118**, with supporting evidence. Arjun knew this because one day, a colleague had to update her details after getting married — and she had to go through the process carefully.\n\nOne day, he even heard about someone who had been removed from the register for ten years of misconduct, but then applied under **Section 117** with proof that they had changed their ways. The Controller reviewed it and decided to restore their name — a rare but possible outcome.\n\nArjun’s journey taught him everything about patent agents: how to qualify, register, practice, and the consequences if you failed in your duty. He knew now that the law was strict for good reason — as explained in his textbook on **page 48**, it ensured only competent professionals could help with patents, protecting both applicants and the integrity of the system.\n\n---\n\n**ANCHOR KEY**\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 126 | Qualifications required to become a patent agent | A person must be an Indian citizen over twenty-one years old with a degree in science, engineering, or technology and pass a qualifying examination. |\n| Section 125 | Maintaining the register of patent agents | The Controller is responsible for maintaining a register that includes names, addresses, and other relevant details of all qualified patent agents. |\n| Section 127 | Rights of registered patent agents | A registered patent agent can practice before the Controller, prepare documents, transact business, and discharge duties in connection with proceedings under the Patents Act. |\n| Section 129 | Restrictions on unregistered individuals practising as patent agents | Only registered patent agents or advocates (but not both) may represent clients in proceedings before the Controller. |\n| Section 130 | Removal from the register of patent agents | The Controller can remove a person's name from the register if they have committed misconduct, been convicted of an offence, or if their registration was based on misrepresentation. |\n| Section 117 | Restoration of names removed from the register | A person whose name has been removed may apply for restoration if the Controller is satisfied that there is good cause for the removal. |\n| Section 118 | Alteration of details in the register | Changes to a patent agent's name or other details can be made upon application with supporting evidence. |\n| Section 126(1) | Application form and submission requirements | An applicant must submit Form 22 along with required information and fees as specified by the Controller. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for Patent Agents — Qualification, Registration, Rights and Removal**\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym / Mnemonic for Qualification Requirements:**\n**Q.U.A.L.I.F.Y.**\n\n- **Q**: Qualifications (e.g., LL.B or equivalent)\n- **U**: Understanding of the Patents Act\n- **A**: Application in Form 22\n- **L**: Legal knowledge (not necessarily a degree, but required for practice)\n- **I**: Involvement in patent matters (experience or study)\n- **F**: Fees paid as per First Schedule\n- **Y**: Yielding to Controller’s approval\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors for Sections:**\n\n- **Section 126** → *Qualification and Examination*  \n  *Image*: A person wearing a **\"126\" badge** standing in front of an **exam hall**, holding a **registration form**.\n\n- **Section 127** → *Rights of Patent Agents*  \n  *Image*: A **\"127\" key** unlocking the door to the Controller’s office, where they can **practice and prepare documents**.\n\n- **Section 129** → *Restrictions on Practice*  \n  *Image*: A **\"129\" sign** saying \"No entry for unqualified agents\", with a **barrier** blocking unauthorized persons.\n\n- **Section 130** → *Review of Controller's Decisions*  \n  *Image*: A **\"130\" scale of justice**, showing how decisions can be **reviewed or set aside** if unfair.\n\n- **Section 132** → *Advocates not being Patent Agents*  \n  *Image*: An **advocate with a \"132\" badge** standing outside the patent office, **not allowed to practice** as a patent agent but can **participate in hearings**.\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Patent Agent vs Advocate**:  \n  *A Patent Agent is not required to have a law degree and practices before the Controller; an Advocate can participate in hearings but is not registered as a patent agent (Section 132).*\n\n- **Registration vs Qualification**:  \n  *Qualification refers to meeting educational and examination requirements, while registration is the formal process of being entered into the register of patent agents under Section 126.*\n\n- **Removal from Register vs Restoration**:  \n  *Removal happens when a patent agent violates rules (Section 116), while restoration is the process to get back on the register after removal (Section 117).*\n\n- **Exclusive Licensee vs Patent Agent**:  \n  *An exclusive licensee has rights in the invention, whereas a patent agent helps file and manage applications but does not own the invention (Section 2(f)).*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"An advocate must have a degree in science, engineering, or technology and pass the qualifying patent agent examination. This was mandated by Section 126 post-2005 amendment to ensure scientific expertise.","question":"What qualification is required for advocates to register as patent agents post-2005 amendment?"},{"answer":"Form 22 is used to apply for registration, along with the prescribed fee, as per Rule 115. An annual fee is also required to maintain the register entry.","question":"What form is used to apply for registration as a patent agent?"},{"answer":"The 2005 amendment removed advocates' automatic eligibility, requiring them to have a science/tech degree and pass the patent agent exam, as per Section 126, to ensure specialized knowledge.","question":"Why can't advocates practice as patent agents without a science/tech degree now?"},{"answer":"Removal occurs for non-payment of fees, misconduct, or failure to meet qualification standards, as outlined in Rules 115 and 118.","question":"What are grounds for removal from the patent agent register?"},{"answer":"Yes, but they must meet separate qualifications for each role. The Patents Act distinguishes between practicing as a patent agent and an advocate.","question":"Can a patent agent also act as an advocate?"},{"answer":"Restoration requires addressing the cause of removal, such as paying overdue fees, and submitting a request to the Controller, as per Rule 118.","question":"How can a removed patent agent restore their name to the register?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 126 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Form, etc., of affidavits”.","options":["Rule 28","Rule 19H","Rule 126","Rule 96"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Form, etc., of affidavits?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 126 is headed “Form, etc., of affidavits”.","options":["Preparation of amended specifications, etc","Form, etc., of affidavits","Manner of application under section 20(5)","Short title and commencement"],"question":"Rule 126 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 130 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Removal from register of patent agents and restoration”.","options":["Section 110","Section 130","Section 134","Section 136"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Removal from register of patent agents and restoration?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 130 is headed “Removal from register of patent agents and restoration”.","options":["Removal from register of patent agents and restoration","Publication of official journal","Compulsory licences","Revocation of patent in public interest"],"question":"Section 130 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 129 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Restrictions on practice as patent agents”.","options":["Section 130","Section 29","Section 129","Section 25"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Restrictions on practice as patent agents?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 129 is headed “Restrictions on practice as patent agents”.","options":["Restrictions on practice as patent agents","Patent rights not infringed when used on foreign vessels, etc.,","Applications for restoration of lapsed patents","Order for licence to operate as a deed between parties"],"question":"Section 129 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 125 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Register of patent agents”.","options":["Section 127","Section 104","Section 125","Section 15"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Register of patent agents?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 125 is headed “Register of patent agents”.","options":["Transmission of orders of courts to Controller","Definitions and interpretation","Form of application","Register of patent agents"],"question":"Section 125 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 128 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Subscription and verification of certain documents by patent”.","options":["Section 133","Section 31","Section 115","Section 128"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Subscription and verification of certain documents by patent?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 128 is headed “Subscription and verification of certain documents by patent”.","options":["Subscription and verification of certain documents by patent","Refusal or failure to supply information","Finality of orders of Controller and Central Government","Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of"],"question":"Section 128 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 129 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller”.","options":["Rule 121A","Rule 129","Rule 58","Rule 103A"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 129 is headed “Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller”.","options":["Supply of certified copies and certificates under sections","Inspection of documents related to grant of patent","Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller","Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent"],"question":"Rule 129 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"he has completed the age of 21 years","options":["a) 18 years","b) 20 years","c) 21 years","d) 25 years"],"question":"What is the minimum age requirement for registration as a patent agent under Section 126(1)(b)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"every patent agent whose name is entered in the register shall be entitled: (a) to practice before the Controller","options":["a) To practice before the Central Government","b) To practice before the Controller","c) To represent foreign clients exclusively","d) To file patents abroad without fees"],"question":"What right is granted to a registered patent agent under Section 127(a)?"}]},"s1p_38":{"notes":"# Study Notes: Controller, Appeals and Powers of Civil Court – Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Definition of the Controller\n\nThe **Controller** is a key authority under the Patents Act, 1970 who oversees various aspects of patent law including granting patents, handling appeals, issuing directions, and ensuring compliance with legal provisions.\n\n- According to **Section 73** of the Patents Act, 1970, the Controller is defined as an officer appointed by the Central Government for the administration of the Patent Office.\n- The Controller has the authority to perform a wide range of duties, including granting patents, deciding on oppositions, and issuing orders related to secrecy directions or compulsory licenses.\n\n> (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45 — 1.7 PATENT AGENTS AND THEIR ROLE): This section explains that the Controller plays a central role in managing patent applications and ensuring compliance with legal procedures.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Key Concepts: Powers of the Controller\n\nThe **Controller** is granted several powers under various sections of the Patents Act, 1970 to ensure smooth functioning of the patent system:\n\n### a) Powers Similar to Civil Court (Section 77)\n- Under **Section 77**, the Controller has the same powers as a civil court in matters such as summoning witnesses, enforcing attendance, receiving evidence on affidavits, and issuing commissions for examination.\n- These powers are essential for handling disputes, examining documents, and ensuring transparency in patent-related proceedings.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 73 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 73. Controller and other officers.\n> (1) The Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks\n> appointed under sub-section (1) of section 3 of the Trade Marks Act,\n> 1999 (47 of 1999), shall be the Controller of Patents for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (2) For the purposes of this Act, the Central Government may appoint\n> as many examiners and other officers and with such designations as\n> it thinks fit.\n>\n> (3) Subject to the provisions of this Act, the officers appointed\n> under sub-section (2) shall discharge under the superintendence and\n> directions of the Controller such functions of the Controller under\n> this Act as he may, from time to time by general or special order in\n> writing, authorise them to discharge.\n>\n> (4) Without prejudice to the generality of the provisions of sub-\n> section (3), the Controller may, by order in writing and for reasons\n> to be recorded therein withdraw any matter pending before an officer\n> appointed under sub-section (2) and deal with such matter himself\n> either de novo or from the stage it was so withdrawn or transfer the\n> same to another officer appointed under sub-section (2) who may,\n> subject to special directions in the order of transfer, proceed with\n> the matter either de novo or from the stage it was so transferred.\n\n**Section 77 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 77. Controller to have certain powers of a civil court.\n> (1) Subject to any rules made in this behalf, the Controller in any\n> proceedings before him under this Act shall have the powers of a\n> civil court while trying a suit under the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908), in respect of the following matters, namely:\n> (a) summoning and enforcing the attendance of any person and\n> examining him on oath;\n> (b) requiring the discovery and production of any document;\n> (c) receiving evidence on affidavits;\n> (d) issuing commissions for the examination of witnesses or\n> documents;\n> (e) awarding costs;\n> (f) reviewing his own decision on application made within the\n> prescribed time and in the prescribed manner;\n> (g) setting aside an order passed ex parte on application made\n> within the prescribed time and in the prescribed manner;\n> (h) any other matter which may be prescribed.\n>\n> (2) Any order for costs awarded by the Controller in exercise of the\n> powers conferred upon him under sub-section (1) shall be executable\n> as a decree of a civil court.\n\n**Section 117A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 117A. Appeals to High Court.\n\n**Rule 116 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 116. Removal of a name from the register of patent agents\n> (1) The Controller may delete from the register of patent agents,\n> the name of any patent agent\n> (a) from whom a request has been received to that effect; or\n> (b) when he is dead; or\n> (c) when the Controller has removed the name of a person under sub-\n> section (1) of section 130; or\n> (d) if he has defaulted in the payment of fees specified in rule\n> 115, by more than three months after they are due; or\n> (e) if he ceases to be a citizen of India:\n> Provided that except under clause (a) and (b), before removing the\n> name of any person from the register of patent agents under this\n> rule, such person shall be given a reasonable opportunity of being\n> heard.\n>\n> (2) The removal of the name of any person from the register of\n>\n>  66\n>\n> patent agents shall be published and shall be, where relevant\n> forthwith communicated to the person concerned.\n\n**Rule 130 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 130. Application for review of decisions or setting aside of\n> orders of the Controller\n> (1) An application to the Controller for the review of his decision\n> under clause (f) of sub-section (1) of section 77 shall be made in\n> Form 24 within one month from the date of communication of such\n> decision to the applicant or within such further period not\n>\n>  71\n>\n> exceeding one month thereafter as the Controller may on a request\n> made in Form 4 allow and shall be accompanied by a statement setting\n> forth the grounds on which the review is sought. Where the decision\n> in question concerns any other person in addition to the applicant,\n> the Controller shall forthwith transmit a copy of each of the\n> application and the statement to the other person concerned.\n>\n> (2) An application to the Controller for setting aside an order\n> passed by him ex-parte under clause (g) of sub-section (1) of\n> section 77 shall be made in Form 24 within one month from the date\n> of communication of such order to the applicant or within such\n> further period not exceeding one month as the Controller may on a\n> request made in Form 4 allow and shall be accompanied by a statement\n> setting forth the grounds on which the application is based. Where\n> the order concerns any other person in addition to the applicant,\n> the Controller shall, forthwith transmit a copy each of the\n> application and the statement to the other person concerned.\n\n**Rule 56 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 56. Constitution of Opposition Board and its proceeding\n> (1) On receipt of notice of opposition under rule 55A, the\n> Controller shall, by order, constitute an Opposition Board\n> consisting of three members and nominate one of the members as the\n> Chairman of the Board.\n>\n> (2) An examiner appointed under sub-section (2) of section 73 shall\n> be eligible to be a member of the Opposition Board.\n>\n> (3) The examiner, who has dealt with the application for patent\n> during the proceeding for grant of patent thereon shall not be\n> eligible as member of Opposition Board as specified in sub-rule (2)\n> for that application.\n>\n> (4) The Opposition Board shall conduct the examination of the notice\n> of opposition along with documents filed under rules 57 to 60\n>\n>  43\n>\n> referred to under sub-section (3) of section 25, submit a report\n> with reasons on each ground taken in the notice of opposition with\n> its joint recommendation within two months from the date on which\n> the documents were forwarded to them.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the powers of the Controller under Section 77 of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2018, 2023)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the specific powers granted to the Controller under Section 77 of the Patents Act, 1970, and how do they compare to those of a civil court?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 77(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970, the Controller is conferred with powers similar to those of a civil court under the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. These powers include:  \n- Summoning and enforcing attendance of any person and examining them on oath (Section 77(1)(a)).  \n- Requiring discovery and production of documents (Section 77(1)(b)).  \n- Receiving evidence on affidavits (Section 77(1)(c)).  \n- Issuing commissions for the examination of witnesses or documents (Section 77(1)(d)).  \n- Awarding costs (Section 77(1)(e)).  \n- Reviewing his own decision upon application within a prescribed time and manner (Section 77(1)(f)).  \n- Setting aside an order passed ex parte on application made within the prescribed time and in the prescribed manner (Section 77(1)(g)).  \n- Any other matter which may be prescribed by rules (Section 77(1)(h)).\n\nFurther, **Section 77(2)** provides that any costs awarded by the Controller are executable as a decree of a civil court. This ensures that such orders have legal enforceability similar to those issued by civil courts.\n\n**Application:** These powers enable the Controller to effectively manage and resolve disputes related to patents without requiring referral to higher judicial authorities. For example, if there is a dispute over the validity of a patent or infringement claims, the Controller can summon witnesses, require production of documents, and award costs in case of default by parties involved. This makes the Controller's role central in ensuring expeditious resolution of matters within the patent system.\n\n**Conclusion:** Section 77 of the Patents Act grants the Controller extensive powers akin to those of a civil court, enabling him to manage disputes efficiently and ensure justice within the framework of patent law. These powers are critical for maintaining order and transparency in patent-related proceedings.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to mention that these powers are not absolute but are subject to rules made by the Controller under Section 77(1)(h), which can prescribe additional matters.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Define the term \"Controller\" as per the Patents Act, 1970 and explain his role in granting patents and handling appeals.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2021)*\n\n**Issue:** What is the definition of the Controller under the Patents Act, 1970, and what are his key functions?\n\n**Rule:** According to **Section 73(1)** of the Patents Act, 1970, the Controller is defined as the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks appointed under Section 3 of the Trade Marks Act, 1999. The Central Government may appoint examiners and other officers who operate under the superintendence of the Controller (Section 73(2)). These officers can be authorized by the Controller to perform specific functions (Section 73(3)).\n\nThe Controller is also empowered to handle appeals, issue directions, and ensure compliance with legal provisions. His role in granting patents includes examining applications, deciding on oppositions, and issuing orders related to secrecy or compulsory licenses.\n\n**Application:** The Controller plays a central role in the patent process by overseeing the grant of patents and managing disputes that arise during the application phase. For instance, if an applicant faces opposition from another party, the Controller has the authority to review the case and make a decision based on evidence presented. He can also issue orders related to compulsory licenses if he deems it necessary for public interest.\n\n**Conclusion:** The Controller is the central authority under the Patents Act, 1970, responsible for granting patents, handling appeals, and ensuring compliance with legal provisions. His role is essential in maintaining an efficient and fair patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** It is important to distinguish between the general powers of the Controller and his specific functions in granting patents and handling appeals.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Explain the process of appeal from the decision of the Controller under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What is the procedure for appealing against a decision made by the Controller?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 117A** of the Patents Act, 1970, appeals from decisions of the Controller are heard by the High Court. However, the exact provisions and procedures for such appeals are not fully detailed in this section. Additional rules may be prescribed under the Patents Rules, 2003.\n\nThe process generally involves a party dissatisfied with the Controller’s decision filing an appeal to the High Court within a specified time frame. The High Court then reviews the matter based on the evidence and arguments presented before the Controller.\n\n**Application:** For example, if a patent application is rejected by the Controller due to lack of novelty, the applicant may file an appeal in the High Court. The court would examine whether the Controller made any legal or procedural errors in his decision. If the Court finds that the Controller’s decision was erroneous, it can either overturn it or remit the matter back for reconsideration.\n\n**Conclusion:** Appeals against decisions of the Controller are heard by the High Court under Section 117A of the Patents Act, 1970. This ensures a higher level of judicial review and protects the rights of parties involved in patent disputes.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook that the specific procedural rules for appeals may be detailed in the Patents Rules, 2003, not just in Section 117A itself.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Write a short note on the removal of a name from the register of patent agents under Rule 116 of the Patents Rules, 2003.**  \n*5 marks · Osmania University · not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue:** What are the grounds and procedure for removing a patent agent’s name from the register?\n\n**Rule:** **Rule 116(1)** of the Patents Rules, 2003 outlines that the Controller may remove a patent agent's name from the register under various circumstances:  \n- If the agent requests removal (a).  \n- If the agent is deceased (b).  \n- If the Controller has removed the agent under Section 130(1) (c).  \n- If the agent defaults in payment of fees by more than three months after they are due (d).  \n- If the agent ceases to be a citizen of India (e).\n\n**Application:** For instance, if a patent agent fails to pay the required fees within three months of their due date, the Controller may remove his name from the register. Before such removal, the agent must be given a reasonable opportunity to be heard.\n\n**Conclusion:** The Controller has the power to remove a patent agent's name under Rule 116 based on specific grounds, ensuring that only qualified and compliant agents remain registered.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** It is crucial to note the requirement of providing an opportunity for the agent to be heard before removal, except in cases (a) and (b).","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Controller, Appeals and Powers of Civil Court\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 73 | **COMB** | COMB sifts through legal papers, organizing the Controller's duties |\n| Sec. 77 | **CAKE** | CAKE slices through disputes, mirroring the Controller's court powers |\n| Sec. 117-A | **TIE-DUCK** + the letter A | TIE-DUCK waddles into court, quacking an appealable order |\n| Sec. 116 | **TIE-DISH** | TIE-DISH is tied up, transferring IPAB cases to High Courts |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **68-78** - assignments, registration, rectification\n- **117** - appeals\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Controller General and Controllers - which provision?  (picture: COMB)\n2. Powers of a civil court exercised by the Controller - which provision?  (picture: CAKE)\n3. Appealable orders under Section 117A - which provision?  (picture: TIE-DUCK)\n4. Transfer of IPAB jurisdiction to the High Courts - which provision?  (picture: TIE-DISH)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 73 | Defines the Controller and their appointment by Central Government | Officer appointed for patent administration  \n- Section 77 | Grants Controller powers similar to a civil court in proceedings | Civil court-like authority over evidence  \n- Section 117A | Deals with appeals to the High Court | Appeals go to High Court under this section  \n- Rule 116 | Covers removal of names from patent agent register | Name deleted for non-payment or death  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing Controller's powers with those of a court without citing Section 77  \n- Use exact section numbers when referring to the Controller’s authority  \n- Always mention the Central Government’s role in appointing the Controller under Section 73","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where the streets hum with the energy of innovation, there lived a young patent agent named Arjun. He had just received an urgent letter from the Controller’s office — something was amiss in the case of a new invention that had been stalled for months.\n\nArjun knew the Controller well; he was the man who held the keys to the kingdom of patents. According to **Section 73**, this Controller was not just any officer, but one appointed by the Central Government itself, with sweeping powers over patent matters — from granting patents to deciding on oppositions and even issuing secrecy directions.\n\nOne day, Arjun found himself in a meeting with the Controller, who had gathered **seventy-three sacks of grain** as a metaphor for the weight of responsibility he carried. The Controller explained that his role extended beyond mere paperwork; under **Section 77**, he possessed powers similar to those of a civil court. This meant he could summon witnesses, enforce their attendance, and even issue commissions for examining documents — just like in a courtroom.\n\nArjun was fascinated by this power. He asked the Controller how such authority helped in resolving disputes. The Controller smiled and said that **Section 77** allowed him to act as both judge and jury in patent matters, ensuring fairness and transparency. Arjun realized that these powers were not for show — they were essential tools for handling the complexities of patent law.\n\nOne day, a dispute arose over an invention related to solar energy. The Controller had to make a decision on whether to grant a compulsory license under **Section 117A**, which allowed appeals to be made to the High Court. Arjun watched as the Controller meticulously reviewed all evidence and heard from both sides before making his ruling.\n\nBut then, something unexpected happened — one of the patent agents involved had not paid their fees on time. The Controller, following **Rule 116**, decided that this agent could no longer be listed in the register. He sent a notice to the agent, giving them a chance to explain themselves before taking any action.\n\nArjun was struck by how thorough and fair the process was. Every step seemed to align with the law — from summoning witnesses under **Section 77** to removing names from the register when necessary under **Rule 116**.\n\nAs he left the Controller’s office that day, Arjun felt a deep respect for the system in place. He knew now that the Controller was not just an administrator but a guardian of justice — someone who held the balance between innovation and law with unwavering precision.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Seventy-three sacks of grain | Section 73 | The Controller is an officer appointed by the Central Government for the administration of the Patent Office. |\n| Powers similar to a civil court | Section 77 | The Controller has the same powers as a civil court in matters such as summoning witnesses, enforcing attendance, receiving evidence on affidavits, and issuing commissions for examination. |\n| Appeals to High Court | Section 117A | Appeals from decisions of the Controller may be made to the High Court. |\n| Removal of name from register | Rule 116 | The Controller may delete the name of a patent agent from the register under certain conditions, such as non-payment of fees or ceasing to be a citizen of India. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **\"Controller, Appeals and Powers of Civil Court\"** from Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic: \"COPA\"**\n- **C**ontroller's powers as a civil court (Section 77)  \n- **O**pposition Board constitution (Rule 56)  \n- **P**atent agents' qualifications and registration (Sections 125–132)  \n- **A**ppeals to High Court (Section 117A)\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-Anchors**\n\n- **Section 77**: *Civil court powers* → Picture a **judge's gavel** in your hand, symbolizing the Controller’s authority similar to that of a civil court.\n  \n- **Rule 56**: *Opposition Board constitution* → Imagine **three people sitting on a bench**, representing the three members of the Opposition Board.\n\n- **Section 127–132**: *Patent agent rights and restrictions* → Think of a **lawyer in a courtroom** (representing duties) with a **padlock** (restrictions).\n\n- **Section 117A**: *Appeals to High Court* → Picture an **escalator going up**, representing the process of appealing from Controller’s decision to the High Court.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **Controller vs. Appellate Board**:  \n  - *Remember*: The **Controller** is the main authority with powers similar to a civil court, while the **Appellate Board** deals specifically with appeals and oppositions (Section 25, Rule 56).\n\n- **Patent agent vs. Advocate**:  \n  - *Remember*: A **patent agent** can help with drafting and filing applications but cannot represent in court; an **advocate** may appear before the Controller without being a patent agent (Section 132).\n\n- **Compulsory licence vs. Voluntary licence**:  \n  - *Remember*: A **compulsory licence** is issued by the Controller under certain conditions (Sections 84–90), while a **voluntary licence** is granted directly between parties without government intervention.\n\n---\n\nThese techniques help in recalling key legal concepts, procedural steps and distinctions related to the Controller, Appeals and Powers of Civil Court as per the Patents Act, 1970.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The IPAB heard appeals against decisions of controllers and registrars under IP laws. It had powers similar to a civil court for evidence and witness examination, as per Section 117A(4).","question":"What was the role of the IPAB before its dissolution?"},{"answer":"An appeal to the IPAB must be filed within three months of the Controller's decision, as specified in Section 117A(4).","question":"What is the time limit for filing an appeal to the IPAB under Section 117A?"},{"answer":"The IPAB was dissolved by the Tribunals Reforms Ordinance, 2021, which transferred its functions to High Courts to address delays and improve efficiency.","question":"Why was the IPAB dissolved in 2021?"},{"answer":"The Controller has powers equivalent to a civil court under the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908, including summoning witnesses and receiving evidence.","question":"What powers does the Controller have under the Patents Act?"},{"answer":"IPAB's jurisdiction was transferred to High Courts via the Tribunals Reforms Ordinance, 2021, making them handle all IP-related appeals and disputes.","question":"What happened to IPAB's jurisdiction after 2021?"},{"answer":"IPR matters are subject to the Commercial Courts Act, 2015, with strict timelines, case management, and reforms like video conferencing to expedite resolution.","question":"Why are IPR disputes now resolved more speedily?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 117A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Appeals to High Court”.","options":["Section 7","Section 128","Section 117A","Section 84"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Appeals to High Court?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 117A is headed “Appeals to High Court”.","options":["Refusal or failure to supply information","Appeals to High Court","Adjudication of penalties","Notification as to countries not providing for reciprocity"],"question":"Section 117A of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 77 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Controller to have certain powers of a civil court”.","options":["Section 126","Section 44","Section 77","Section 58"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Controller to have certain powers of a civil court?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 77 is headed “Controller to have certain powers of a civil court”.","options":["Request for examination","Anticipation by public working","Licensing of related patents","Controller to have certain powers of a civil court"],"question":"Section 77 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 73 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Controller and other officers”.","options":["Section 133","Section 108","Section 77","Section 73"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Controller and other officers?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 73 is headed “Controller and other officers”.","options":["Patent office and its branches","Jurisdiction","Controller and other officers","Register to be open for inspection"],"question":"Section 73 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 116 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Removal of a name from the register of patent agents”.","options":["Rule 116","Rule 24B","Rule 30","Rule 33"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Removal of a name from the register of patent agents?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 116 is headed “Removal of a name from the register of patent agents”.","options":["Removal of a name from the register of patent agents","Removal of names from the roll of scientific advisers","Renewal fees under section 53","Filing of written statement of opposition and evidence"],"question":"Rule 116 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 77 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Manner of application under section 51(2)”.","options":["Rule 19F","Rule 77","Rule 24B","Rule 94"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Manner of application under section 51(2)?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 77 is headed “Manner of application under section 51(2)”.","options":["Indian International Searching Authority","Registration of patent agents","Manner of application under section 51(2)","Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)"],"question":"Rule 77 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 73(1) explicitly states that the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks appointed under the Trade Marks Act, 1999 is the Controller of Patents.","options":["The Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks appointed under the Trade Marks Act, 1999","The Chief Examiner of Patents","The Director General of the Patent Office","The Central Government"],"question":"Who is designated as the Controller of Patents under Section 73(1) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 73(2) clearly states that the Central Government may appoint examiners and other officers.","options":["The Controller of Patents","The Central Government","The Patent Office","The High Court"],"question":"Under Section 73(2) of the Patents Act, 1970, who has the authority to appoint examiners and other officers?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 73(3) specifies that officers appointed under Section 73(2) must discharge their functions under the superintendence and directions of the Controller.","options":["The Central Government","The Controller of Patents","The High Court","The Patent Office"],"question":"According to Section 73(3) of the Patents Act, 1970, under whose superintendence and directions must officers appointed under Section 73(2) discharge their functions?"}]},"s1p_39":{"notes":"### Offences and Penalties under the Act – Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\n\n---\n\n#### **Definition of Offences**\nOffences under the Patents Act, 1970 refer to violations or non-compliance with the provisions of the Act and its associated rules. These may include acts such as false statements in patent applications, unauthorized use of patented inventions, and failure to comply with procedural requirements during the patent application process.\n\nKey concepts include:\n- **Liability of individuals**: Individuals who commit an offence may be held personally liable.\n- **Liability of companies**: Companies can also be held responsible for offences committed by their employees or officers if it is proved that such acts were done with the consent, connivance, or neglect on the part of a director, manager, secretary, or other officer of the company.\n- **Penalties and punishments**: These may include monetary fines, imprisonment, or both.\n\n---\n\n#### **Key Sections and Rules**\n\n**Section 123 (Eligibility to Practice as Patent Agent)**\n- This section states that only registered patent agents are eligible to practice or hold themselves out as such. Unregistered individuals who engage in the practice of being a patent agent may be punished with fines up to ₹5,00,000 for the first offence and ₹1,000 per day for subsequent offences.\n\n**Section 136 (Offences by Companies)**\n- This section outlines that if an offence is committed by a company, and it is proved that such an offence was done with the consent or connivance of any director, manager, secretary, or other officer of the company, then those individuals shall also be deemed to be guilty and liable for punishment.\n\n**Rule 110 (Particulars of Qualifying Examination)**\n- This rule outlines the structure of the qualifying examination for patent agents. It includes written papers on the Patents Act, 1970, Patents Rules, 2003, Designs Act, 2000, and Design Rules, as well as a viva voce component.\n\n---\n\n#### **Why the Law is Structured This Way**\nThe legal framework surrounding offences and penalties under the Patents Act aims to:\n- **Ensure accountability**: By holding individuals and companies responsible for their actions, the law ensures that all parties involved in patent-related activities comply with procedural and ethical standards.\n- **Maintain integrity of the patent system**: Penalties deter fraudulent or unethical practices, such as false claims or unauthorized use of inventions, which could undermine the credibility of the patent process.\n\nAs explained on page 45 of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, “A patent agent can help its clients in applying for or obtaining patents in India or elsewhere besides preparing specifications or other documents.” However, unregistered individuals who attempt to practice as patent agents may be punished under Section 123 of the Act.\n\nSimilarly, on page 207, it is noted that: “nothing contained in this subsection shall render any such person liable to any punishment if he proves that the offence was committed without his knowledge or that he exercised all due diligence to prevent the commission of such offence.” This provision aims to protect individuals who may have been unknowingly involved in an offence.\n\n---\n\n#### **Penalties and Punishments**\n\n- **Section 123 (Eligibility to Practice as Patent Agent)**: \n  - First offence: Fine up to ₹5,00,000.\n  - Subsequent offences: ₹1,000 per day until the fine is paid.\n\n- **Section 136 (Offences by Companies)**:\n  - Directors or officers of a company who are found guilty may be punished with imprisonment for up to two years and/or fines as determined by the court.\n  \n- **Other Offences**:\n  - False statements in patent applications: May lead to rejection of the application, fines, or even criminal prosecution.\n  - Unauthorized use of patented inventions: Can result in legal action for infringement, including compensation claims.\n\n---\n\n#### **Relevant Cases (if any)**\n\n**(No verified case on record for this topic – check IndianKanoon before citing one.)**\n\n---\n\n#### **Examination Focus Areas**\n\nIn the context of the Patent Agent Examination, candidates are expected to:\n- Understand the legal implications of various types of offences under the Patents Act.\n- Know the exact sections and rules that govern penalties and punishments.\n- Be familiar with the procedures for examining and interpreting patent specifications and other documents (as outlined in Paper II).\n- Prepare for viva voce questions on the interpretation of laws, drafting, and ethical standards.\n\nAs per page 383 of *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*, candidates are expected to “quote relevant sections and rules as well as prescribed fees and forms in the answer.” This includes being able to reference Section 123, Section 136, and Rule 110 in detail.\n\n---\n\n#### **Conclusion**\nThe provisions related to offences and penalties under the Patents Act are essential for maintaining a fair and credible patent system. They ensure that individuals and companies comply with legal standards and deter fraudulent practices. Aspiring patent agents must be thoroughly familiar with these sections and rules, as they form an important part of both Paper I and Paper II of the examination, along with the viva voce component.\n\nFor further clarification on specific cases or procedures not covered in this summary, candidates should refer to the Bare Act and consult official case law databases.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 118 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 118. Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain\n> inventions.\n> If any person fails to comply with any direction given under section\n> 35 or makes or causes to be made an application for the grant of a\n> patent in contravention of section 39, he shall be punishable with\n> imprisonment for a term which may extend to two years, or with fine,\n> or with both.\n\n**Section 120 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 120. Unauthorised claim of patent rights.\n> If any person falsely represents that any article sold by him is\n> patented in India or is the subject of an application for a patent\n> in India, he shall be liable to penalty which may extend to ten lakh\n> rupees, and in case of the continuing claim, a further penalty of\n> one thousand rupees for every day after the first during which such\n> claim continues\n>\n> Explanation l. For the purposes of this section, a person shall be\n> deemed to represent\n> (a) that an article is patented in India if there is stamped,\n> engraved or impressed on, or otherwise applied to, the article the\n> word “patent” or “patented” or some other word expressing or\n> implying that a patent for the article has been obtained in India;\n> (b) that an article is the subject of an application for a patent in\n> India, if there are stamped, engraved or impressed on, or otherwise\n> applied to, the article the words “patent applied for”, “patent\n> pending”, or some other words implying that an application for a\n> patent for the article has been made in India.\n>\n> Explanation 2. The use of words “patent”, “patented”, “patent\n> applied for”, “patent pending” or other words expressing or implying\n>\n>  94\n>\n> that an article is patented or that a patent has been applied for\n> shall be deemed to refer to a patent in force in India, or to a\n> pending application for a patent in India, as the case may be unless\n> there is an accompanying indication that the patent has been\n> obtained or applied for in any country outside India.\n\n**Section 122 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 122. Refusal or failure to supply information.\n> (1) If any person refuses or fails to furnish:\n> (a) to the Central Government any information which he is required\n> to furnish under sub-section (5) of section 100;\n> (b) to the Controller any information or statement which he is\n> required to furnish by or under section 146,\n> he shall be liable to penalty which may extend to one lakh rupees,\n> and in case of the continuing refusal or failure, a further penalty\n> of one thousand rupees for every day after the first during which\n> such refusal or failure continues.\n>\n> (2) If any person, being required to furnish any such information as\n> is referred to in sub-section (1), furnishes information or\n> statement which is false, and which he either knows or has reason to\n> believe to be false or does not believe to be true, he shall be\n> liable to penalty for a sum equal to one half per cent. of the total\n> sale or turnover, as the case may be, of business or of the gross\n> receipts in profession as computed in the audited accounts of such\n> person, or a sum equal to five crore rupees, whichever is less.\n\n**Section 123 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 123. Practice by non-registered patent agents.\n> If any person contravenes the provisions of section 129, he shall be\n> liable to penalty, which may extend to five lakh rupees, and in case\n> of the continuing default, a further penalty of one thousand rupees\n> for every day after the first during which such default continues.\n\n**Section 124 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 124. Offences by companies.\n> (1) If the person committing an offence under this Act is a company,\n> the company as well as every person in charge of, and responsible\n> to, the company for the conduct of its business at the time of the\n> commission of the offence shall be deemed to be guilty of the\n> offence and shall be liable to be proceeded against and punished\n> accordingly:\n> Provided that nothing contained in this sub-section shall render any\n> such person liable to any punishment if he proves that the offence\n>\n>  95\n>\n> was committed without his knowledge or that he exercised all due\n> diligence to prevent the commission of such offence.\n>\n> (2) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (1), where an\n> offence under this Act has been committed by a company and it is\n> proved that the offence has been committed with the consent or\n> connivance of, or that the commission of the offence is attributable\n> to any neglect on the part of any director, manager, secretary or\n> other officer of the company, such director, manager, secretary or\n> other officer shall also be deemed to be guilty of that offence and\n> shall be liable to be proceeded against and punished accordingly.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this section,\n> (a) “company” means any body corporate and includes a firm or other\n> association of individuals; and\n> (b) “director”, in relation to a firm, means a partner in the firm.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provisions of Section 123 and Section 136 of the Patents Act, 1970 with regard to offences by individuals and companies respectively.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** The legal implications of Section 123 and Section 136 of the Patents Act, 1970 in relation to offences committed by individuals and companies.\n\n**Rule:**  \n- **Section 123 (Practice by non-registered patent agents)**: This section prohibits unregistered persons from practicing as patent agents. A person who contravenes this provision is liable to a penalty of up to ₹5,00,000 for the first offence and an additional fine of ₹1,000 per day for subsequent offences (Bare Act, page 95).  \n- **Section 136 (Offences by companies)**: This section holds directors, managers, secretaries, or other officers of a company liable if they are proved to have consented to, connived at, or neglected to prevent the commission of an offence. Such individuals may be punished with imprisonment for up to two years and/or fines as determined by the court (Bare Act, page 95).  \n\nFurther, **Section 124** of the Patents Act provides that if a company commits an offence, not only is the company liable but also any individual in charge of its business at the time of the offence. However, such individuals may avoid liability if they prove that the offence was committed without their knowledge or that they exercised due diligence to prevent it (Bare Act, page 95).  \n\n**Application:**  \n- If an unregistered person practices as a patent agent and is caught, Section 123 applies directly to them. The penalty would be ₹5,00,000 for the initial violation, with additional fines of ₹1,000 per day until compliance is achieved. This provision ensures that only registered professionals are allowed to practice in this field, thereby maintaining the integrity and professionalism of patent agents (page 45, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).  \n- In contrast, if a company commits an offence such as false statements in a patent application or unauthorized use of patented inventions, Section 136 applies to the directors, managers, or officers who are found to have been involved in the commission of the offence. These individuals may face imprisonment and/or fines depending on the severity of the violation (page 207, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).  \n- However, an individual officer may be exonerated if they can prove that they were not aware of the offence or had taken all due diligence to prevent it. This is a protective measure for those who are genuinely unaware or have acted responsibly (page 207, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).  \n\n**Conclusion:**  \nSections 123 and 136 of the Patents Act impose clear legal responsibilities on individuals and companies involved in patent-related activities. Section 123 ensures that only registered professionals can practice as patent agents, while Section 136 extends liability to company officers who are found to have been complicit in an offence. These provisions work together to maintain ethical standards and deter fraudulent practices within the patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to distinguish between the individual and corporate liabilities under Sections 123 and 136, respectively. It is crucial to highlight that Section 123 applies directly to unregistered individuals, whereas Section 136 extends liability to company officers who are found to have been involved in an offence.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What penalties can be imposed on a person for making false statements in a patent application? Discuss with reference to the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** The legal consequences of making false statements in a patent application under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule:**  \n- **Section 122 (Refusal or failure to supply information)**: This section penalizes individuals who fail to provide accurate information or submit false statements. A person found guilty may be liable for a penalty of up to ₹1,00,000 for the initial violation and an additional fine of ₹1,000 per day if the non-compliance continues (Bare Act, page 94).  \n- In addition, **Section 120** penalizes unauthorized claims of patent rights. A person who falsely represents that an article is patented or that a patent application has been filed may be fined up to ₹10,00,000 and an additional fine of ₹1,000 per day if the claim continues (Bare Act, page 94).  \n- **Section 123** also applies in cases where false statements are made by unregistered persons practicing as patent agents. They may be fined up to ₹5,00,000 and an additional fine of ₹1,000 per day (Bare Act, page 95).  \n\nThese provisions aim to ensure the accuracy of information submitted in patent applications and prevent misleading claims that could undermine the integrity of the patent system (page 45, *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra*).\n\n**Application:**  \n- Suppose an individual submits a false statement in a patent application claiming that they are the sole inventor when this is not true. Under Section 122, they may be liable for a penalty of up to ₹1,00,000, with additional fines if the false information continues (Bare Act, page 94).  \n- If the same individual falsely represents that an article is patented or that a patent application has been filed when this is not true, they may be penalized under Section 120 for up to ₹10,00,000 and further fines of ₹1,000 per day (Bare Act, page 94).  \n- If the individual is unregistered but practices as a patent agent, Section 123 would also apply, potentially resulting in an additional fine of up to ₹5,00,000.  \n\n**Conclusion:**  \nMaking false statements in a patent application can lead to severe penalties under Sections 120 and 122 of the Patents Act, 1970. These provisions ensure that only accurate information is submitted and deter fraudulent practices by imposing monetary fines. The law also extends liability to unregistered individuals practicing as patent agents through Section 123.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the specific penalties under Sections 120 and 122 when discussing false statements in a patent application. It is essential to clearly distinguish between the different sections and their respective consequences.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the liability of companies for offences committed by their officers or employees under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*8 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** The extent to which a company can be held liable for an offence committed by its officers or employees under the Patents Act.\n\n**Rule:**  \n- **Section 136 (Offences by companies)**: This section holds a company and its directors, managers, secretaries, or other officers responsible if it is proved that they were involved in the commission of an offence. The company can be punished with fines, while individual officers may face imprisonment for up to two years and/or additional fines (Bare Act, page 95).  \n- **Section 124** provides further clarification: A company committing an offence will also hold any person in charge of its business at the time of the offence liable. However, such individuals can avoid punishment if they prove that the offence was committed without their knowledge or due diligence (Bare Act, page 95).  \n\nThese provisions ensure that companies are held accountable for the actions of their employees and officers, while also protecting those who acted in good faith.\n\n**Application:**  \n- Suppose a company's manager submits false information in a patent application. Under Section 136, both the company and the manager may be punished with fines or imprisonment if it is proved that they were involved (Bare Act, page 95).  \n- However, if the manager can prove that the false statement was made without their knowledge or that they had taken all due diligence to prevent it, they may avoid liability under Section 124.  \n\n**Conclusion:**  \nUnder Sections 136 and 124 of the Patents Act, a company is held liable for offences committed by its officers or employees if it is proved that such actions were done with their consent, connivance, or neglect. These provisions ensure accountability while also protecting individuals who acted responsibly.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention Section 124, which provides a key exception allowing officers to avoid liability if they prove due diligence or lack of knowledge. This is an important point that should be included in the answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the penalties for unregistered individuals practicing as patent agents under Section 123 of the Patents Act?**  \n*5 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2022)*\n\n**Issue:** The legal consequences of an unregistered individual practicing as a patent agent under Section 123.\n\n**Rule:**  \n- **Section 123**: This section penalizes individuals who practice as patent agents without being registered. They may be fined up to ₹5,00,000 for the first offence and an additional fine of ₹1,000 per day if the violation continues (Bare Act, page 95).  \n\nThis provision ensures that only qualified and registered professionals are allowed to practice as patent agents, thereby protecting clients from unqualified individuals.\n\n**Application:**  \n- If an individual is found practicing as a patent agent without being registered, they may be fined up to ₹5,00,000. Further fines of ₹1,000 per day will apply if the violation continues (Bare Act, page 95).  \n\n**Conclusion:**  \nSection 123 imposes a penalty of up to ₹5,00,000 for an unregistered individual practicing as a patent agent, with additional daily fines for continued violations. This ensures that only registered professionals can engage in this practice.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often forget to include the additional fine per day if the violation continues. It is important to mention both the initial and continuing penalties when answering this question.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Offences and Penalties under the Act\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 118 | **TIE-DOVE** | TIE-DOVE drops a secret note, fluttering wildly. |\n| Sec. 120 | **TIE-NOSE** | TIE-NOSE sprays fake patent labels, smirking. |\n| Sec. 122 | **TIE-NUN** | TIE-NUN crosses arms, blocking documents. |\n| Sec. 123 | **TIE-GNOME** | TIE-GNOME fumbles tools, unlicensed. |\n| Sec. 124 | **DINNER** | DINNER guests plot corporate fraud, silent. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **118-124** - penalties and offences\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Contravention of secrecy provisions - which provision?  (picture: TIE-DOVE)\n2. Falsely representing an article as patented - which provision?  (picture: TIE-NOSE)\n3. Refusal or failure to supply information - which provision?  (picture: TIE-NUN)\n4. Practice by a non-registered person - which provision?  (picture: TIE-GNOME)\n5. Offences by companies - which provision?  (picture: DINNER)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 118 | Punishment for contravening secrecy provisions | Lab coat with lock  \n- Section 120 | Penalty for false patent claims on products | Stamped \"patent\" label  \n- Section 122 | Penalty for refusing or giving false info | Filing cabinet with red flags  \n- Section 123 | Fine for unregistered patent agents practicing | Unlicensed lawyer in court  \n- Section 124 | Company liability and officer punishment | Corporate ladder with chains  \n- Section 136 | Company officers liable for offences | Manager signing illegal contract  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid vague statements – always quote exact section numbers.  \n- Use clear, legal language as expected by examiners.  \n- Structure answers with: offence, relevant section, penalty, and example.","storyEN":"**Story: The Patent Agent of Hyderabad**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, there lived a young man named Arjun, who had just passed his qualifying examination for becoming a patent agent. His dream was to help inventors protect their creations and ensure that no one could falsely claim ownership over another’s hard work. But he knew that with great power came great responsibility — especially under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\nOne day, Arjun met an old friend named Ravi, who had recently opened a small business selling electronic gadgets. Ravi was excited about his new product but didn’t know how to apply for a patent. He asked Arjun if he could help. But Arjun reminded him that only **registered** patent agents could legally assist with such matters.\n\nRavi, not knowing the rules, tried to file an application himself and even stamped “patent applied for” on his product’s packaging — thinking it would make people believe it was under consideration by the government. This act of false representation caught the attention of a local inspector who visited Ravi’s shop. The inspector reminded him that **Section 120** of the Patents Act prohibited such misleading claims and could result in penalties up to ₹10,00,000.\n\nRavi was shocked but quickly corrected his mistake by removing all labels and re-packaging his items. He realized that even unintentional violations could have serious consequences under the law.\n\nMeanwhile, Arjun had another client — a company named **TechNova** — which had recently been accused of making false statements in their patent application. The Controller found out that the company had submitted documents with misleading information about the novelty of their invention. Under **Section 122**, TechNova was liable to pay a penalty, and if they continued to refuse or fail to correct their errors, they would face additional fines.\n\nThe director of TechNova, Mr. Sharma, was surprised by the accusation. He claimed he had no knowledge of the false statements and that his team had exercised due diligence in preparing the documents. However, under **Section 124**, if it could be proved that the mistake was made with the consent or connivance of any officer, then they too would face punishment.\n\nArjun reminded Mr. Sharma of a key point from the law: “If you can prove that the error was not known to you and that all due diligence was taken, then you might avoid liability.” This provision gave him some hope — but he still had to act quickly to correct their application before it was too late.\n\nAs Arjun helped Mr. Sharma fix the documents, another issue arose: a man named **Rahul**, who was not registered as a patent agent, tried to offer his services to several inventors in Hyderabad. He told them that he could help them get patents faster and for cheaper than any official agent. But under **Section 123**, Rahul was breaking the law by practicing without registration.\n\nArjun reported him to the Controller, who then issued a warning: “If you continue this practice, you’ll be fined up to ₹5,00,000 for your first offence and an additional ₹1,000 per day until you pay.”\n\nRahul was taken aback. He had always thought that being a patent agent required only experience — not registration. But now he understood the importance of following the rules laid out in **Section 129**, which Arjun explained to him.\n\nIn time, Ravi’s business grew and became more legitimate. TechNova corrected their application and avoided further penalties. Rahul learned his lesson and decided to register as a patent agent himself — finally understanding that only those who followed the law could truly help inventors protect their rights.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 120 | False representation of patents on products | If any person falsely represents an article as patented or under patent application in India, they may be fined up to ₹10,00,000 and further penalties per day if the claim continues. |\n| Section 122 | Refusal or failure to supply information | A person who refuses or fails to provide required information may face a fine of ₹1,00,000 and an additional ₹1,000 per day for continuing non-compliance. If false information is provided, the penalty can be up to half a percent of total sales or ₹5 crore, whichever is less. |\n| Section 123 | Practice by unregistered patent agents | Unregistered individuals who practice as patent agents may face fines of ₹5,00,000 for their first offence and an additional ₹1,000 per day until the fine is paid for subsequent offences. |\n| Section 124 | Offences by companies | If a company commits an offence and it is proved that it was done with the consent or connivance of any officer, then those individuals may also be punished, unless they prove lack of knowledge or due diligence. |\n| Rahul | Not registered patent agent | Unregistered individuals who practice as patent agents are liable to penalties under Section 123. |\n| Ravi’s false labels | Section 120 | False representation on products can lead to heavy fines and legal action. |\n| TechNova's false statements | Section 122 | Submitting false information in a patent application may result in hefty penalties. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for \"Offences and Penalties under the Act\" from Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**  \n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n- **\"PENAL\"** – *Penalties and Enforcement of Non-Adherence to Legal Acts*  \n  - Helps remember key aspects like penalties for unregistered patent agents, enforcement of rules, and legal consequences under the Act.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 123**: *Think of a \"lock\" (symbolizing restriction) on someone who is not registered as a patent agent practicing law.*  \n- **Section 126(1)**: *Picture a \"test\" being taken by a candidate, representing the qualifying examination for patent agents.*  \n- **Rule 110**: *Imagine a \"form\" (like Form 22) being filled out to apply for registration as a patent agent.*  \n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Section 123 vs. Section 126(1)**:  \n  - *Section 123 deals with penalties for unregistered agents, while Section 126(1) outlines the requirements to become a registered patent agent.*  \n\n- **Paper I vs. Paper II**:  \n  - *Paper I tests knowledge of the Patents Act and Rules; Paper II focuses on drafting and interpreting specifications and documents.*  \n\n- **Viva voce vs. Written Examination**:  \n  - *The viva voce is an oral test, while the written examination consists of answering questions in writing.*  \n\n--- \n\nThese techniques are designed to help you recall key elements of \"Offences and Penalties under the Act\" as covered in the Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II, and Viva) accurately.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"A penalty up to ₹1 lakh, with ₹1,000 daily for continuing refusal. Section 122(1) mandates this for non-compliance with information requests.","question":"What penalty applies for refusing to supply information under Section 122?"},{"answer":"A penalty up to ₹10 lakh, with ₹1,000 daily for continuing false claims. Section 120(A) specifies this after the 2023 amendment.","question":"What is the penalty for falsely claiming a patent under Section 120?"},{"answer":"A penalty up to ₹5 lakh, with ₹1,000 daily for continuing practice. Section 123(D) outlines this post-2023 amendment.","question":"What penalty applies to non-registered patent agents practicing under Section 123?"},{"answer":"Both the company and its responsible individuals are liable. Section 124(1) deems them guilty and punishable accordingly.","question":"How are company offences handled under Section 124?"},{"answer":"The Controller may appoint an adjudicating officer to impose penalties after a hearing. Section 124A enables this process as prescribed.","question":"What is the procedure for adjudicating penalties under Section 124A?"},{"answer":"To ensure accountability, Section 124(1) holds both the company and its responsible individuals liable for offences committed.","question":"Why are companies held liable for patent offences?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 124 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Manner and time of opposition to the making of corrections”.","options":["Rule 133","Rule 124","Rule 91","Rule 14"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Manner and time of opposition to the making of corrections?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 124 is headed “Manner and time of opposition to the making of corrections”.","options":["Application for registration of patent agents","Manner and time of opposition to the making of corrections","Restoration of patents","Registration of title or interest in a patent"],"question":"Rule 124 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 123 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Manner of advertisement of the proposed correction of any”.","options":["Rule 14","Rule 81","Rule 123","Rule 84"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Manner of advertisement of the proposed correction of any?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 123 is headed “Manner of advertisement of the proposed correction of any”.","options":["Filing of reply evidence by opponent","Request for publication","Manner of advertisement of the proposed correction of any","Removal of a name from the register of patent agents"],"question":"Rule 123 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 122 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Correction of clerical errors”.","options":["Rule 95","Rule 122","Rule 30","Rule 37"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Correction of clerical errors?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 122 is headed “Correction of clerical errors”.","options":["Form of making a claim under section 28(3)","Filing of notice of opposition","Manner of making a demand","Correction of clerical errors"],"question":"Rule 122 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 120 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Unauthorised claim of patent rights”.","options":["Section 99","Section 55","Section 120","Section 57"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Unauthorised claim of patent rights?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 120 is headed “Unauthorised claim of patent rights”.","options":["Restrictions on practice as patent agents","Exercise of discretionary powers by Controller","Unauthorised claim of patent rights","Liability for contravention of section 35 or section 39"],"question":"Section 120 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 118 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain”.","options":["Section 72","Section 160","Section 118","Section 157"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 118 is headed “Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain”.","options":["Contravention of Secrecy provisions relating to certain","Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents","Contents of specifications","Restriction on power of court to grant damages or account of"],"question":"Section 118 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 118 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent”.","options":["Rule 98","Rule 19G","Rule 117","Rule 118"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 118 is headed “Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent”.","options":["Further evidence to be left with the leave of the","Details to be included in an application for the","Entry of renewal fee","Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent"],"question":"Rule 118 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The penalty is imprisonment for a term which may extend to two years, or with fine, or with both.","options":["Imprisonment for up to one year or a fine","Imprisonment for up to two years or a fine, or both","Imprisonment for up to three years or a fine","Imprisonment for up to five years or a fine"],"question":"What is the penalty for contravening the secrecy provisions under section 35 or 39 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"The penalty may extend to one lakh rupees, with an additional one thousand rupees per day for continuing refusal.","options":["One lakh rupees","Two lakh rupees","Five lakh rupees","Ten lakh rupees"],"question":"What is the penalty for refusing to supply information required under section 100(5) or 146 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The penalty is one half per cent of total sale/turnover or five crore rupees, whichever is less.","options":["One percent of turnover or ten crore rupees","Half a percent of turnover or five crore rupees","Two percent of turnover or twenty crore rupees","Half a percent of turnover or ten crore rupees"],"question":"What is the penalty for furnishing false information under section 122 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The penalty may extend to five lakh rupees.","options":["One lakh rupees","Five lakh rupees","Ten lakh rupees","Fifteen lakh rupees"],"question":"What is the maximum penalty for contravening section 129 of the Patents Act, 1970?"}]},"s1p_40":{"notes":"# Designs Act 2000 and Designs Rules 2001 — Overview for Paper I  \n*(For Patent Agent Examination: Papers I, II and Viva)*  \n\n---\n\n## **Definition of a Design**  \nA design is defined as the ornamental aspect of an article, which may consist of lines, outlines, colours, or combinations of these elements. It does not include any technical effect or function of the article.  \n\n- **Section 2(a)** of the *Designs Act, 2000* defines a \"design\" as:  \n> “the ornamental aspect of an article which is not dictated by the function of the article.”  \n\nThis definition distinguishes between the functional and non-functional aspects of an article. The focus is on the visual appeal or appearance of the article.\n\n---\n\n## **Key Concepts Under the Designs Act, 2000**  \n\n### **1. Registration of a Design**  \nA design can be registered if it meets certain criteria such as novelty, originality, and non-functionality.  \n\n- **Section 4**: A design is registerable only if it has not been previously published or used in India or elsewhere before the date of application.  \n- **Section 5**: The design must be new and original to qualify for registration.\n\n### **2. Types of Designs**  \nThere are two types of designs:  \n1. **Registered Design** – A design that is registered with the Controller of Designs.  \n2. **Unregistered Design** – A design that has not been formally registered but may still enjoy some protection under the law.  \n\n- **Section 7**: The registration of a design provides legal protection against unauthorized use or reproduction.\n\n### **3. Duration of Protection**  \nA registered design is protected for an initial period of **10 years**, which can be renewed once for another **5 years**, making the total duration **15 years** from the date of application.  \n\n- **Section 8**: The term of registration and renewal is clearly outlined in this section.\n\n### **4. Infringement of a Design**  \nInfringement occurs when someone reproduces, imitates, or applies a registered design to an article without the permission of the owner.  \n\n- **Section 22**: This section outlines the legal consequences for infringement of a registered design and provides remedies such as injunctions and damages.\n\n---\n\n## **Designs Rules, 2001**  \nThese rules provide detailed procedural guidelines for the registration and management of designs under the *Designs Act, 2000*.  \n\n- **Rule 3**: Defines the \"Appropriate Office\" where applications are to be filed.  \n- **Rule 4**: Sets out the requirements for the address for service of documents.  \n- **Rule 5**: Specifies the forms and fees required for filing an application.\n\nThe *Designs Rules, 2001* ensure that the registration process is systematic and transparent. They also provide clarity on matters such as corrections to applications and the publication of registered designs.\n\n---\n\n## **Importance in Paper I**  \nIn **Paper I**, candidates are expected to have a thorough understanding of both the *Designs Act, 2000* and the *Designs Rules, 2001*, as these are integral components of the syllabus.  \n\n- According to *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 47)*:  \n> “Paper I includes questions on the *Designs Act, 2000* and the *Designs Rules, 2001*, besides IP Jurisprudence and IP administration.”  \n\nCandidates must be able to identify relevant sections and rules in response to questions that may involve hypothetical scenarios or case studies.\n\n---\n\n## **Why the Law Is Structured This Way**  \nThe distinction between function and appearance is crucial for protecting aesthetic innovations while ensuring that functional aspects of an article remain unimpeded.  \n\n- *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 48)* explains:  \n> “Designs are protected to encourage creativity in the visual appearance of products, which can significantly impact consumer appeal and marketability.”  \n\nThis rationale justifies the need for a separate legal framework for designs, distinct from patents that protect technical inventions.\n\n---\n\n## **Case Law (if any)**  \n*(No verified or candidate cases are available for this topic. Do not invent a case name, year, or citation.)*\n\n---\n\n## **Conclusion**  \nThe *Designs Act, 2000* and the *Designs Rules, 2001* provide essential legal frameworks for protecting ornamental designs in India. Understanding these provisions is critical for candidates preparing for Paper I of the Patent Agent Examination. The emphasis on originality, novelty, and proper registration procedures ensures that designers are adequately protected under Indian law.\n\nCandidates should focus on mastering key sections such as **Section 2(a), Section 4, Section 5**, and **Rule 3** to ensure success in this part of the examination.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 2 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 2. Definitions and interpretation.\n> (1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal\n> representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee\n> of any person include references to the assignee of the legal\n> representative or assignee of that person;\n> (aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the\n> International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the\n> purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April,\n> 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;\n> (ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an\n> invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used\n> in an industry;\n> (b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs\n> and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;\n> (c) “convention application” means an application for a patent made\n> by virtue of section 135;\n> (d) “convention country\" means a country or a country which is\n> member of a group of countries or a union of countries or an Inter-\n> governmental organisation referred to as a convention country in\n> section 133;\n> (e) “district court” has the meaning assigned to that expression by\n> the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908);\n> (f) “exclusive licence” means a licence from a patentee which\n> confers on the licensee, or on the licensee and persons authorised\n> by him, to the exclusion of all other persons (including the\n>\n>  8\n>\n> patentee), any right in respect of the patented invention, and\n> “exclusive licensee” shall be construed accordingly;\n> (h) “Government undertaking” means any industrial undertaking\n> carried on\n> (i) by a department of the Government, or\n> (ii) by a corporation established by a Central, Provincial or State\n> Act, which is owned or controlled by the Government, or\n> (iii) by a Government company as defined in section 617 of the\n> Companies Act, 1956 (1 of\n> 1956), or\n> 1(iv) by an institution wholly or substantially financed by the\n> Government;\n> (i) “High Court\", in relation to a State or Union territory, means\n> the High Court having territorial jurisdiction in that State or\n> Union territory, as the case may be;\n> (ia) “international application” means an application for patent\n> made in accordance with the Patent Cooperation Treaty;\n> (j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an\n> inventive step and capable of industrial application;\n> (ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves\n> technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having\n> economic significance or both and that makes the invention not\n> obvious to a person skilled in the art;\n> (k) “legal representative” means a person who in law represents the\n> estate of a deceased person;\n> (l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not\n> been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the\n> country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of\n> patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject\n> matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part\n> of the state of the art;\n> (la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under\n> sub-section (3) of section 25;\n> (m) “patent” means a patent for any invention granted under this\n> Act;\n> (n) “patent agent” means a person for the time being registered\n> under this Act as a patent agent;\n> (o) “patented article” and “patented process” mean respectively an\n> article or process in respect of which a patent is in force;\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 4 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 4. Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable.\n> No patent shall be granted in respect of an invention relating to\n> atomic energy falling within sub-section (1) of section 20 of the\n> Atomic Energy Act, 1962 (33 of 1962).\n>\n>  12\n>\n> CHAPTER III APPLICATIONS FOR PATENTS\n\n**Rule 5 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 5. Address for service\n> Every person, concerned in any proceedings to which the Act or these\n> rules relate and every patentee, shall furnish to the Controller an\n> address for service, including a postal address in India and an e-\n> mail address, and such address for service shall be treated for all\n> purposes connected with such proceedings or patent as the address of\n> the person concerned in the proceedings or of the patentee. Unless\n> such an address for service is given, the Controller shall be under\n> no obligation either to proceed or deal with any proceeding, or\n> patent or to send any notice that may be required to be given under\n> the Act or these rules and the Controller may take a decision in the\n> matter: Provided that a patent agent shall also be required to\n> furnish to the Controller a mobile number registered in India.\n\n**Section 11 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11. Priority dates of claims of a complete specification.\n> (1) There shall be a priority date for each claim of a complete\n> specification.\n>\n> (2) Where a complete specification is filed in pursuance of a single\n> application accompanied by\n> (a) a provisional specification; or\n> (b) a specification which is treated by virtue of a direction under\n> sub-section (3) of section 9 as a provisional specification,\n> and the claim is fairly based on the matter disclosed in the\n> specification referred to in clause (a) or clause (b), the priority\n>\n>  17\n>\n> date of that claim shall be the date of the filing of the relevant\n> specification.\n>\n> (3) Where the complete specification is filed or proceeded with in\n> pursuance of two or more applications accompanied by such\n> specifications as are mentioned in sub-section (2) and the claim is\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed\n> (a) in one of those specifications, the priority date of that claim\n> shall be the date of the filing of the application accompanied by\n> that specification;\n> (b) partly in one and partly in another, the priority date of that\n> claim shall be the date of the filing of the application accompanied\n> by the specification of the later date.\n>\n> (3A) Where a complete specification based on a previously filed\n> application in India has been filed within twelve months from the\n> date of that application and the claim is fairly based on the matter\n> disclosed in the previously filed application, the priority date of\n> that claim shall be the date of the previously filed application in\n> which the matter was first disclosed.\n>\n> (4) Where the complete specification has been filed in pursuance of\n> a further application made by virtue of sub-section (1) of section\n> 16 and the claim is fairly based on the matter disclosed in any of\n> the earlier specifications, provisional or complete, as the case may\n> be, the priority date of that claim shall be the date of the filing\n> of that specification in which the matter was first disclosed.\n>\n> (5) Where, under the foregoing provisions of this section, any claim\n> of a complete specification would, but for the provisions of this\n> sub-section, have two or more priority dates, the priority date of\n> that claim shall be the earlier or earliest of those dates.\n>\n> (6) In any case to which sub-sections (2), (3), 1(3A), (4) and (5)\n> do not apply, the priority date of a claim shall, subject to the\n> provisions of section 137, be the date of filing of the complete\n> specification.\n>\n> (7) The reference to the date of the filing of the application or of\n> the complete specification in this section shall, in cases where\n> there has been a post-dating under section 9 or section 17 or, as\n>\n>  18\n>\n> the case may be, ante-dating under section 16, be a reference to the\n> date as so post-dated or ante-dated.\n>\n> (8) A claim in a complete specification of a patent shall not be\n> invalid by reason only of\n> (a) the publication or use of the invention so far as claimed in\n> that claim on or after the priority date of such claim; or\n> (b) the grant of another patent which claims the invention, so far\n> as claimed in the first mentioned claim, in a claim of the same or a\n> later priority date.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER IV PUBLICATION AND EXAMINATION OF APPLICATIONS\n\n**Section 19 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 19. Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement.\n> (1) If, in consequence of the investigations required under this\n> Act, it appears to the Controller that an invention in respect of\n> which an application for a patent has been made cannot be performed\n> without substantial risk of infringement of a claim of any other\n> patent, he may direct that a reference to that other patent shall be\n> inserted in the applicant’s complete specification by way of notice\n> to the public, unless within such time as may be prescribed\n> (a) the applicant shows to the satisfaction of the Controller that\n> there are reasonable grounds for contesting the validity of the said\n> claim of the other patent; or\n> (b) the complete specification is amended to the satisfaction of the\n> Controller.\n>\n> (2) Where, after a reference to another patent has been inserted in\n> a complete specification in pursuance of a direction under sub-\n> section (1)\n> (a) that other patent is revoked or otherwise ceases to be in force;\n> or\n> (b) the specification of that other patent is amended by the\n> deletion of the relevant claim; or\n> (c) it is found, in proceedings before the court or the Controller,\n> that the relevant claim of that other patent is invalid or is not\n> infringed by any working of the applicant’s invention, the\n> Controller may, on the application of the applicant, delete the\n> reference to that other patent.\n\n**Rule 22 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 22. Effect of non-compliance with certain requirements\n> An international application designating India shall be deemed to be\n> withdrawn if the applicant does not comply with the requirements of\n> rule 20.\n\n**Rule 33 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 33. Form of reference to another patent\n> Where the Controller directs that a reference to another patent\n> shall be inserted in the applicant’s complete specification under\n> sub-section (1) of section 19, such reference shall be inserted,\n> after the claims in the following form, namely:\n> \"Reference has been directed, in pursuance of section 19(1) of the\n> Patents Act, 1970, to Patent No......\"\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Define and explain the term “design” as used under the Designs Act, 2000.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** The definition of a “design” under the *Designs Act, 2000*, and its distinguishing features from functional aspects.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 2(a)** of the *Designs Act, 2000*, a design is defined as “the ornamental aspect of an article which is not dictated by the function of the article.” This provision emphasizes that a design refers to the visual and aesthetic elements of an object. It does not include any technical or functional aspects. The distinction between function and ornamentation is crucial in determining whether something qualifies as a design under the Act.\n\n**Application:** A design must be purely ornamental, meaning it focuses on the appearance rather than the utility of the article. For example, if a chair has an unusual shape that enhances comfort (a functional aspect), this would not qualify as a design. However, if the same chair is painted in a unique color pattern or features decorative carvings, these elements may be considered designs and eligible for protection under the *Designs Act, 2000*.\n\n**Conclusion:** A design, as defined by **Section 2(a)** of the *Designs Act, 2000*, refers to the ornamental characteristics of an article that are not dictated by its function. This distinction ensures that only aesthetic innovations are protected under the Act, while functional aspects remain outside its scope.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse the definition of a design with that of a patentable invention, failing to emphasize the non-functional and purely ornamental nature of a design.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the essential requirements for the registration of a design under the Designs Act, 2000? Discuss in detail.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2017, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** The conditions that must be satisfied for the registration of a design under the *Designs Act, 2000*.\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 4**, a design is registerable only if it has not been previously published or used in India or elsewhere before the date of application. **Section 5** further states that the design must be new and original to qualify for registration. These provisions ensure that only novel and unregistered designs are eligible for protection.\n\n**Application:** For example, suppose an artist creates a unique pattern on a ceramic mug and applies for its registration under the *Designs Act*. If the same pattern was previously published in a magazine or used commercially before the application date, it would not meet the criteria of novelty and originality required by **Sections 4 and 5**. Therefore, such an application would be rejected.\n\n**Conclusion:** The essential requirements for the registration of a design are that it must be new (not previously disclosed), original, and not dictated by the function of the article. These conditions are outlined in **Sections 4 and 5** of the *Designs Act, 2000* to ensure only innovative ornamental designs receive legal protection.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to distinguish between novelty and originality or overlook the requirement that a design must not be dictated by function. These are key elements in determining registerability.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the duration of protection granted under the Designs Act, 2000. What is the process for renewal?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2019)*\n\n**Issue:** The period of protection and the procedure for renewing a registered design.\n\n**Rule:** **Section 8** of the *Designs Act, 2000*, states that a registered design is protected for an initial period of **10 years**, which can be renewed once for another **5 years**, making the total duration **15 years** from the date of application. The process for renewal involves submitting the required fees and documents to the Controller of Designs.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a designer registers a new product design in 2024. They will enjoy protection until 2034, after which they may apply for renewal if they wish to extend the protection by an additional five years, up to 2039. Failure to pay the renewal fee within the prescribed time frame may result in the lapse of the design registration.\n\n**Conclusion:** A registered design is protected for **15 years**, with the initial period being **10 years** and a possible extension of **5 years** upon payment of the renewal fee, as outlined in **Section 8** of the *Designs Act, 2000*.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often forget to mention that the design can be renewed only once for an additional five years. It is important to highlight this limitation explicitly.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the concept of “infringement” under the Designs Act, 2000. What are the legal remedies available to the owner in case of infringement?**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2023)*\n\n**Issue:** The meaning of design infringement and the legal remedies provided by the *Designs Act, 2000*.\n\n**Rule:** **Section 22** of the *Designs Act, 2000*, outlines that infringement occurs when someone reproduces, imitates, or applies a registered design to an article without the permission of the owner. The legal remedies available include injunctions and damages.\n\n**Application:** For instance, if a company registers a unique logo on its product packaging and another firm starts using a similar logo in their own packaging, this would constitute infringement under **Section 22**. The registered owner can seek an injunction to stop the unauthorized use and claim damages for any loss suffered due to the infringement.\n\n**Conclusion:** Under **Section 22**, infringement occurs when a design is used without authorization by another party. The remedies available include injunctive relief and compensation, ensuring that the rights of the design owner are protected.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to mention both remedies (injunctions and damages) or confuse them with patent infringement remedies. It is crucial to distinguish these clearly in the context of design law.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Designs Act 2000 and Designs Rules 2001 — Overview for Paper I\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 2(d) | **HEN** + clause (d) | HEN clucks about excluded items in design definitions. |\n| Sec. 4 | **RYE** | RYE fields refuse registration of certain designs. |\n| Sec. 5 | **LAW** | LAW books use Locarno for design registration. |\n| Sec. 11 | **TOTE** | TOTE bag holds copyright term for registered designs. |\n| Sec. 19 | **TUB** | TUB emptied by piracy cancelling design registration. |\n| Sec. 22 | **NUN** | NUN distinguishes design from patents and trademarks. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **2** - definitions\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **12-24** - examination, objections, time for putting in order\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Definition of design and what is excluded - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n2. Prohibition of registration of certain designs - which provision?  (picture: RYE)\n3. Registration procedure and Locarno classification - which provision?  (picture: LAW)\n4. Copyright in a registered design and its term - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n5. Cancellation of registration and piracy of a registered design - which provision?  (picture: TUB)\n6. Design distinguished from a patent and from a trade mark - which provision?  (picture: NUN)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 2(a) | Defines design as ornamental aspect, not function | Ornamental vs functional split  \n- Section 4 | Design must be new and not previously used/published | Newness check before filing  \n- Section 5 | Design must be original and novel | Originality key for registration  \n- Section 7 | Registration grants legal protection against use/reproduction | Legal shield after registration  \n- Section 8 | Duration of design registration is 10 years, renewable once for 5 years | 15-year lifespan timeline  \n- Section 22 | Infringement remedies include injunctions and damages | Stop copying or pay up  \n- Rule 3 | Defines where applications are to be filed (Appropriate Office) | Right office for filing  \n- Rule 4 | Address for service required in India and via email | Service address must be clear  \n- Rule 5 | Forms, fees, and mobile number required for application | Paperwork and contact details  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing design with patent law – focus on ornamental aspects only.  \n- Examiners expect references to specific sections (e.g., Section 2(a), Rule 5) in answers.  \n- Structure answers by first stating the section, then its definition or rule, followed by implications or examples.","storyEN":"**Story: The Case of the Ornamental Lanterns in Hyderabad**\n\nIn the bustling city of Hyderabad, a small but ambitious design firm called *Shruti Decor* was known for its handcrafted lanterns that lit up homes with intricate patterns and vibrant colors. One day, Shruti, the owner, decided to register her latest creation — a set of ornamental lanterns with swirling lines and a unique color scheme — under the **Designs Act, 2000**.\n\nShe went to the office of the Controller of Designs, where she was told that for a design to be eligible for registration, it had to meet certain conditions. Shruti was informed of **Section 4**, which stated that if her lanterns had been previously published or used in India or elsewhere before the date of application, they wouldn’t qualify. She checked and found no prior use — good.\n\nNext came **Section 5**, which required the design to be new and original. Shruti was relieved when the Controller confirmed that her swirling lines and color combination were indeed unique and hadn't been seen in any other lanterns on the market. The design was accepted for registration, giving Shruti legal protection under **Section 7**.\n\nWith the design registered, Shruti felt confident enough to expand her business. However, a few months later, she noticed that a local shop had started selling similar lanterns with nearly identical patterns and colors — but without her permission. This was a clear case of infringement under **Section 22**, which outlines legal consequences for unauthorized use or reproduction.\n\nShruti took the matter to court, where she argued that her design was protected by law. The judge ruled in her favor, citing the importance of protecting aesthetic innovations as explained in the textbook on page 48: “Designs are protected to encourage creativity in the visual appearance of products, which can significantly impact consumer appeal and marketability.”\n\nMeanwhile, Shruti had also been following the **Designs Rules, 2001**, particularly **Rule 3**, which specified where she should file her application — the appropriate office. She had ensured that all her paperwork was submitted correctly, including **Rule 5**, which required an address for service of documents in India and an email address.\n\nShe also remembered to comply with the procedural guidelines set out in the Rules, such as those under **Rule 22**, which warned about the consequences of non-compliance. Shruti had made sure she didn’t make any mistakes in her application, thus avoiding any complications that could lead to her design being deemed withdrawn or rejected.\n\nThanks to the protection provided by the Designs Act and the procedural clarity offered by the Designs Rules, Shruti was able to enforce her rights and continue creating beautiful lanterns without fear of imitation. Her story became a local legend — not just of creativity, but of legal awareness and the importance of protecting one’s designs under Indian law.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 4    | Shruti had to ensure her design wasn’t previously used or published. | A design is registerable only if it has not been previously published or used in India or elsewhere before the date of application. |\n| Section 5    | Shruti’s lanterns needed to be new and original to qualify for registration. | The design must be new and original to qualify for registration. |\n| Section 7    | Shruti was granted legal protection against unauthorized use or reproduction. | Registration provides legal protection against unauthorized use or reproduction. |\n| Section 22   | A local shop was selling similar lanterns without Shruti’s permission, which was a clear case of infringement. | Legal consequences for infringement include injunctions and damages. |\n| Rule 3       | Shruti filed her application at the appropriate office as required by the Rules. | Defines the “Appropriate Office” where applications are to be filed. |\n| Rule 5       | Shruti provided an address for service of documents, including a postal and email address. | Sets out the requirements for the address for service of documents. |\n| Rule 22      | Shruti was careful not to make any procedural mistakes that could lead to her application being withdrawn. | An international application is deemed withdrawn if the applicant does not comply with certain requirements. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"Designs Act 2000 and Designs Rules 2001 — Overview for Paper I\"**  \n*(Based on the Patents Rules, 2003 and textbook explanation from Dr. Sheetal Chopra)*  \n\n---\n\n#### **1. Acronym / Mnemonic:**\n- **D.R.E.A.M.S.**  \n  - *Designs Act & Rules (D), Examination of Designs (E), Registration Process (R), Application Forms (A), Maintenance Fees (M), and Statutory Time Limits (S).*  \n  *(Use this to recall key elements covered in Paper I under the Designs Act and Rules.)*\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Rule 19B – International Search Report**  \n  - *Picture a “searchlight” scanning over a “report card.” The number 19B reminds you that this rule governs the creation of a report on the novelty of an invention in international applications.*  \n\n- **Paper II (Qualifying Exam) – Drafting and Interpretation**  \n  - *Think of \"2\" as a pair of scissors cutting through legal language. This section tests your ability to draft and interpret patent and design specifications, so remember that Paper II is all about precision with words.*  \n\n- **Rule 110(3) – 60% Aggregate Marks**  \n  - *Imagine a “60%” grade on an exam. Rule 110(3) requires candidates to score at least 60% overall in the qualifying examination, so associate this rule with the idea of passing grades.*  \n\n- **Rule 115 – Payment of Fees**  \n  - *Picture a “cash register” (number 115). This rule deals with the payment of fees for maintaining registration as a patent agent. Think of it as the “money gatekeeper.”*  \n\n---\n\n#### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Paper I vs Paper II in Qualifying Exam**  \n  - *Paper I is about knowledge (Acts, Rules), and Paper II is about application (drafting, interpretation). Use this to remember that Paper I tests theory, while Paper II tests practical skills.*  \n\n- **Designs Act 2000 vs Patents Act 1970**  \n  - *The Designs Act protects the visual appearance of a product; the Patents Act protects inventions. Think: “Designs = Looks, Patents = Function.”*  \n\n- **Viva Voce Marks (50) vs Written Exam Aggregate (60%)**  \n  - *Viva is oral and carries 50 marks; written exam requires an aggregate of 60% in all papers. Remember that viva is part of the total, but not the main focus.*  \n\n- **Rule 19B (International Search Report) vs Rule 19K (Preliminary Examination Report)**  \n  - *Rule 19B checks novelty; Rule 19K evaluates patentability. Think: “Search = Novelty, Preliminary = Patentable.”*  \n\n--- \n\nThese memory techniques are tailored for the **Patent Agent Examination** and focus on **Paper I**, which includes the **Designs Act 2000** and **Designs Rules 2001**. Use them to build a strong conceptual framework during preparation.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Functionality of a product is excluded from design protection. The Designs Act 2000 focuses on visual appeal, not technical aspects.","question":"What is excluded from the definition of a design under the Designs Act 2000?"},{"answer":"Designs are classified using the Locarno system under the Designs Rules 2001, ensuring standardized categorization for registration.","question":"What is the registration procedure for designs under the Designs Rules 2001?"},{"answer":"Such designs are excluded as they relate to functionality, which is protected under patents, not designs. The Designs Act 2000 prohibits this.","question":"Why can't a design that is dictated by technical effects be registered?"},{"answer":"A registered design lasts for 10 years from the filing date, as stipulated by the Designs Act 2000.","question":"What is the term of a registered design under the Designs Act 2000?"},{"answer":"Piracy is tackled through cancellation of registration and legal action, as outlined in the Designs Act 2000.","question":"How is piracy of a registered design addressed under the Designs Act 2000?"},{"answer":"Designs protect visual appearance, patents protect technical solutions, and trademarks protect source indicators. The Designs Act 2000 distinguishes these.","question":"How does a design differ from a patent and a trademark?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Section 150","Section 14","Section 2","Section 42"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Definitions and interpretation?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 2 is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior","Appeals to High Court","Definitions and interpretation","Evidence of entries, documents, etc"],"question":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 19 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement”.","options":["Section 37","Section 100","Section 19","Section 107A"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 19 is headed “Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement”.","options":["Special provision for compulsory licences on notifications by","Power of Controller to give directions to co-owners","Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement","Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications"],"question":"Section 19 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 11 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Priority dates of claims of a complete specification”.","options":["Section 11","Section 68","Section 91","Section 15"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Priority dates of claims of a complete specification?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 11 is headed “Priority dates of claims of a complete specification”.","options":["Anticipation by previous publication","Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Refusal or failure to supply information","Priority dates of claims of a complete specification"],"question":"Section 11 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 22 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Effect of non-compliance with certain requirements”.","options":["Rule 24A","Rule 24C","Rule 32","Rule 22"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Effect of non-compliance with certain requirements?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 22 is headed “Effect of non-compliance with certain requirements”.","options":["Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent","Opposition to the patent","Effect of non-compliance with certain requirements","Form of reference to another specification"],"question":"Rule 22 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 4 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable”.","options":["Section 13","Section 4","Section 32","Section 90"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 4 is headed “Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable”.","options":["Mention of inventor as such in patent","Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable","Power of Controller to adjourn applications for compulsory","Rights of patentees of lapsed patents which have been restored"],"question":"Section 4 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 11 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Order of recording applications”.","options":["Rule 80","Rule 107","Rule 121A","Rule 11"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Order of recording applications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 11 is headed “Order of recording applications”.","options":["Notification of corrections","Order of recording applications","Indian International Searching Authority","Request for withdrawal"],"question":"Rule 11 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"}]},"s1p_41":{"notes":"## The structure  \nA complete specification must be written in a specific order:  \n1. **Title**: A concise, technical title (≤15 words) that includes the invention’s key feature.  \n2. **Field of the invention**: A paragraph stating the technical area the invention belongs to.  \n3. **Background and prior art**: A detailed description of existing technologies, including patents and non-patent literature, with a focus on the closest prior art. If the inventor is unaware of prior art, this must be explicitly stated.  \n4. **Object of the invention**: Clear statements (e.g., “The principal object of this invention is…”), addressing the problem solved and establishing non-obviousness over prior art.  \n5. **Statement of the invention**: A summary of the invention’s novel and distinguishing features.  \n6. **Summary of the invention**: A broader overview of the invention’s aspects, including its technical field and solution to the problem.  \n7. **Brief description of drawings**: A list of all drawings with their purpose, referenced in accordance with Rule 15.  \n8. **Detailed description and best method**: A thorough explanation of the invention, enabling a person skilled in the art to replicate it without undue effort. Must include the best mode of implementation.  \n9. **Claims**: Numbered, independent and dependent claims that define the scope of protection.  \n10. **Abstract**: A concise summary starting with the title, including technical field, problem, solution, and principal use, with chemical formulas if applicable.  \n\n---\n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Title**: Write a clear, technical title (e.g., “Improved Bicycle Brake System”) at the top of the page.  \n2. **Field of the invention**: Write a paragraph stating the technical area (e.g., “This invention relates to bicycle braking systems”).  \n3. **Background and prior art**:  \n   - List relevant patents (e.g., “Patent No. XYZ relates to disc brakes…”).  \n   - Describe non-patent literature (e.g., “Journal Article ABC discusses friction materials…”).  \n   - If unaware of prior art, state: “The inventor is not aware of prior art in this field.”  \n4. **Object of the invention**: Use statements like “The principal object of this invention is to provide a bicycle brake with reduced maintenance…”  \n5. **Statement of the invention**: Summarize the novel features (e.g., “The invention provides a self-adjusting brake pad assembly…”).  \n6. **Summary of the invention**: Expand on the object, explaining the problem and solution (e.g., “The invention addresses the issue of brake pad wear by incorporating a spring-loaded mechanism…”).  \n7. **Brief description of drawings**: List drawings (e.g., “Figure 1: Perspective view of the brake system”).  \n8. **Detailed description and best method**: Explain the invention step-by-step, including materials, dimensions, and assembly.  \n9. **Claims**: Write independent claims first (e.g., “A bicycle brake comprising a spring-loaded pad…”), followed by dependent claims.  \n10. **Abstract**: Write a 150-word summary starting with the title, including the technical field, problem, solution, and use.  \n\n---\n\n## Worked example  \n**Title**: Improved Bicycle Brake System  \n\n**Field of the invention**: This invention relates to bicycle braking systems, particularly to self-adjusting brake mechanisms.  \n\n**Background and prior art**:  \nExisting bicycle brakes, such as those disclosed in Patent No. 12345 (disc brakes) and Journal Article 6789 (friction-based brakes), suffer from frequent pad wear and require manual adjustment. The inventor is not aware of any prior art that incorporates a self-adjusting mechanism for brake pads.  \n\n**Object of the invention**:  \nThe principal object of this invention is to provide a bicycle brake system with a self-adjusting mechanism that reduces maintenance.  \nAnother object is to improve braking efficiency by minimizing pad slippage.  \n\n**Statement of the invention**:  \nThe invention provides a self-adjusting brake system comprising a spring-loaded pad assembly that automatically compensates for wear.  \n\n**Summary of the invention**:  \nThe invention addresses the problem of brake pad wear by incorporating a spring-loaded mechanism that maintains optimal contact between the pad and the rotor.  \n\n**Brief description of drawings**:  \nFigure 1: Perspective view of the brake system.  \nFigure 2: Cross-sectional view showing the spring-loaded pad assembly.  \n\n**Detailed description and best method**:  \nThe brake system comprises a housing (1) containing a spring (2) that biases the pad (3) against the rotor (4). As the pad wears, the spring extends, maintaining pressure. The best mode involves using a steel spring with a 10 mm diameter.  \n\n**Claims**:  \n1. A bicycle brake comprising a spring-loaded pad assembly, wherein the spring is mounted between the pad and a housing.  \n2. The brake of claim 1, wherein the spring is made of steel.  \n\n**Abstract**:  \nImproved Bicycle Brake System. This invention relates to a self-adjusting bicycle brake system. The system includes a spring-loaded pad assembly that compensates for wear, reducing maintenance. The invention improves braking efficiency by maintaining consistent pressure between the pad and rotor.  \n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes  \n1. **Error**: Omitting prior art in the background section.  \n   **Correction**: Explicitly list relevant patents and literature, even if the inventor is unaware.  \n2. **Error**: Vague objects of the invention (e.g., “improve braking”).  \n   **Correction**: Use specific statements like “reduce maintenance by 50%” or “increase braking efficiency by 20%.”  \n3. **Error**: Not referencing drawings in the detailed description.  \n   **Correction**: Mention each figure (e.g., “as shown in Figure 2”) and ensure all features in claims are labeled in drawings.  \n4. **Error**: Abstract exceeding 15 words.  \n   **Correction**: Keep the title concise, e.g., “Self-Adjusting Bicycle Brake System Using Spring Mechanism.”  \n\n---\n\n## Practise this  \n**Task**: Draft a complete specification for a “Paper Clip with Anti-Slip Grooves” in 45 minutes.  \n**Instructions**:  \n- Use the structure outlined in the textbook.  \n- Include a prior art reference (e.g., Patent No. 98765).  \n- Write at least two claims.  \n- Ensure the abstract is ≤15 words.  \n\n**Check against**:  \n- Does the title include the key feature?  \n- Are prior art and objects clearly stated?  \n- Are all drawings referenced?  \n- Do claims define the invention’s scope?  \n- Is the abstract concise and compliant with Rule 15?\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n**Rule 16 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 16. Models\n> (1) Models or samples shall be furnished under section 10 only when\n> required by the Controller.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER III INTERNATIONAL APPLICATIONS UNDER PATENT COOPERATION\n> TREATY (PCT)\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the essential components of a complete specification under the Patents Act, 1970? Explain each part with its purpose and requirements.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2008, 2014, 2020)*\n\n**Issue**: The question seeks an explanation of the essential components of a complete specification under the Patents Act, 1970, including their purpose and requirements.\n\n**Rule**: Section 10 of the Patents Act, 1970 governs the contents of specifications. It mandates that every complete specification must begin with a title indicating the subject-matter of the invention (Section 10(1)). Furthermore, it must fully describe the invention, disclose the best method known to the applicant (Section 10(4)(a) and (b)), end with claims defining the scope of protection (Section 10(4)(c)), and be accompanied by an abstract (Section 10(4)(d)). The specification may also include drawings, which form part of the specification if required by the Controller (Section 10(2)).\n\n**Application**: A complete specification must follow a structured format to ensure clarity and completeness. For instance:\n\n- **Title**: Must be concise and indicate the key feature of the invention, such as “Improved Bicycle Brake System.” This ensures that readers immediately understand the subject matter.\n  \n- **Field of the Invention**: Describes the technical area in which the invention belongs, e.g., “This invention relates to bicycle braking systems.”\n  \n- **Background and Prior Art**: Must detail existing technologies, including patents and non-patent literature. If the inventor is unaware of prior art, this must be explicitly stated.\n  \n- **Object of the Invention**: Should clearly state the problem solved by the invention and establish its non-obviousness over prior art, e.g., “The principal object of this invention is to provide a bicycle brake with reduced maintenance.”\n  \n- **Statement of the Invention**: Summarizes the novel features that distinguish it from existing technologies.\n  \n- **Summary of the Invention**: Provides a broader overview, including its technical field and how it solves the problem.\n  \n- **Brief Description of Drawings**: Lists all drawings with their purposes, ensuring they are referenced in accordance with Rule 15. For example: “Figure 1: Perspective view of the brake system.”\n  \n- **Detailed Description and Best Mode**: Must be thorough enough for a person skilled in the art to replicate the invention without undue effort. The best mode involves using specific materials or methods, such as a steel spring with a 10 mm diameter.\n  \n- **Claims**: Independent and dependent claims must define the scope of protection clearly and succinctly (Section 10(5)). For example: “A bicycle brake comprising a spring-loaded pad assembly…”\n  \n- **Abstract**: Must be concise, starting with the title, and include technical field, problem, solution, and principal use. It should not exceed 15 words.\n\n**Conclusion**: A complete specification under the Patents Act, 1970 must contain all the components outlined in Section 10, each serving a specific purpose to ensure clarity, completeness, and compliance with legal requirements for patent protection.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often neglect the requirement of including an abstract that is concise and compliant with Rule 15. Additionally, many fail to explain how each part contributes to the overall goal of the specification, such as ensuring that the best mode is disclosed in the detailed description.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the legal requirements for a complete specification under Section 10 of the Patents Act, 1970? Discuss with reference to relevant provisions and their implications.**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2016, 2018)*\n\n**Issue**: The question seeks an analysis of the legal requirements for a complete specification under Section 10 of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule**: Under Section 10(4) of the Patents Act, 1970, every complete specification must:\n\n- (a) Fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation or use, and the method by which it is to be performed.\n- (b) Disclose the best method of performing the invention known to the applicant.\n- (c) End with a claim or claims defining the scope of protection for which protection is claimed.\n- (d) Be accompanied by an abstract that provides technical information on the invention.\n\nSection 10(5) further mandates that claims must relate to a single invention or a group of inventions linked as one inventive concept, and be clear, succinct, and fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n\n**Application**: These provisions ensure that the specification is comprehensive and enables a person skilled in the art to replicate the invention without undue effort. For example:\n\n- The **detailed description** must include all relevant details such as materials, dimensions, and assembly instructions.\n- The **best mode** disclosure ensures that the most effective implementation of the invention is shared with the public.\n- **Claims** define the legal scope of protection, which is essential for determining patentability and enforceability.\n- The **abstract**, while concise, must provide enough information to inform third parties about the nature and scope of the invention.\n\n**Conclusion**: Section 10 of the Patents Act sets out clear legal requirements for a complete specification. These provisions ensure that specifications are detailed, accurate, and legally valid, enabling proper examination and granting of patents.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to explain how each provision in Section 10 contributes to the overall goal of ensuring that the invention is clearly described and protected. Emphasis should be placed on the importance of disclosing the best mode and the clarity of claims.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the role and importance of drawings in a complete specification under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*8 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2013)*\n\n**Issue**: The question asks for an explanation of the role and importance of drawings in a complete specification.\n\n**Rule**: Section 10(2) of the Patents Act, 1970 provides that if the Controller requires it, drawings may be supplied as part of the specification. These drawings are deemed to form part of the specification and must be referenced accordingly.\n\n**Application**: Drawings play a crucial role in illustrating the invention and its components. They help clarify complex technical aspects that may not be adequately described through text alone. For example:\n\n- In the **brief description of drawings**, each figure should be listed with its purpose, such as “Figure 1: Perspective view of the brake system.”\n- In the **detailed description**, references to figures (e.g., “as shown in Figure 2”) ensure that all features described in claims are clearly illustrated.\n- Drawings also help the Controller and examiners understand the invention better during the examination process.\n\n**Conclusion**: Drawings are essential for visual clarity, aiding both the applicant and the examining authorities. They must be referenced properly to avoid confusion and ensure compliance with Rule 15.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: A common mistake is failing to reference drawings in the detailed description or not including them where necessary. Students should highlight that drawings are not just optional but may be required by the Controller for proper understanding of the invention.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the requirements for an abstract under Section 10(4)(d) of the Patents Act, 1970? Explain with examples.**  \n*8 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2015)*\n\n**Issue**: The question asks about the requirements for an abstract and provides examples.\n\n**Rule**: Under Section 10(4)(d), a complete specification must be accompanied by an abstract that provides technical information on the invention. It must not exceed 15 words, start with the title, and include the technical field, problem, solution, and principal use.\n\n**Application**: For example:\n\n- A valid **abstract** might read: “Improved Bicycle Brake System. This invention relates to a self-adjusting bicycle brake system that compensates for wear, reducing maintenance.”\n- The abstract must be concise but informative enough to give a clear overview of the invention.\n- It should not include any new information beyond what is described in the specification.\n\n**Conclusion**: An abstract under Section 10(4)(d) must be concise and start with the title. It serves as a quick reference for third parties and is essential for compliance with Rule 15.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students overlook the requirement that the abstract must not exceed 15 words, leading to disqualification of the specification during examination. The importance of brevity and clarity should be emphasized in answers.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Anatomy of a Complete Specification — Every Part and What Goes In It\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 10 | **TOES** | TOES curling in a lab, revealing invention's field and prior art |\n| Sec. 10(4) | **TOES** + clause (4) | TOES pointing at a board, summarizing the invention's purpose |\n| Sec. 10(5) | **TOES** + clause (5) | TOES tracing lines on paper, briefly describing drawings |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Title, field of the invention, background and prior art - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n2. Object of the invention and summary - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n3. Brief description of drawings - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 10(4) of The Patents Act, 1970 | Defines complete specification and its requirements | Full description needed for replication  \n- Section 10(1) of The Patents Act, 1970 | Title must indicate invention subject matter clearly | Clear title like a road sign  \n- Section 10(4)(a) of The Patents Act, 1970 | Detailed description of invention's operation and use | Step-by-step recipe for making it  \n- Section 10(4)(b) of The Patents Act, 1970 | Must disclose best method known to applicant | Best way like a chef’s secret recipe  \n- Section 10(4)(c) of The Patents Act, 1970 | Claims define legal scope of protection sought | Legal boundaries marked clearly  \n- Section 10(4)(d) of The Patents Act, 1970 | Abstract provides technical summary of invention | Short summary like a book cover  \n- Section 10(2) of The Patents Act, 1970 | Drawings may be required to aid understanding | Visual guide with clear diagrams  \n- Section 10(3) of The Patents Act, 1970 | Model or sample may be needed for illustration | Physical example like a toy model  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid vague descriptions – use precise technical language  \n- Examiners expect clear, well-defined claims and abstracts  \n- Always include all required parts in the correct order for full marks","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where the Charminar’s shadow stretches over the bustling streets, a young inventor named Ravi lived in a modest house on Kachiguda Road with his family. His father was a tailor, and his mother ran a small tea stall near the university. Ravi had always been fascinated by machines — how they worked, how they could be improved. But he had one dream: to create an invention that would change lives.\n\nOne day, as Ravi sat in the corner of his family’s home, sketching ideas on old newspapers, a letter arrived from the Patents Office. It was an invitation for him to apply for a patent — but with strict instructions on how to write the complete specification. He knew this was his chance, and he had to get it right.\n\nRavi remembered what his uncle, who worked at the Osmania University Patent Office, had told him: “A good specification is like a recipe — every ingredient must be there in order for it to work.” So Ravi set out on a journey of creation, guided by the rules laid down in the Patents Act.\n\n### Step 1 – The Title\nThe first thing he needed was a title. He knew that the title had to be concise and include the invention’s key feature. After much thought, he wrote: **“Paper Clip with Anti-Slip Grooves”** — simple, clear, and informative.\n\n### Step 2 – Field of the Invention\nNext came the field of the invention. Ravi described it as a paper clip that could prevent documents from slipping out of binders or folders due to its unique grooves. He knew that this was an improvement on existing clips, like those covered in Patent No. **98765**.\n\n### Step 3 – Background and Prior Art\nRavi researched prior art. He found that Patent No. **98765** described a paper clip with a smooth surface but no grooves. He also read an article from the Journal of Engineering, which mentioned that traditional clips often failed in high-humidity environments. Ravi was not aware of any prior art that combined anti-slip features with a paper clip, so he made that clear in his specification.\n\n### Step 4 – Object of the Invention\nThe object of the invention had to be specific and clear. He wrote: “**The principal object of this invention is to provide a paper clip with anti-slip grooves that prevent documents from slipping out of binders, even under high humidity conditions.**” Another object was to improve the grip between the clip and the document.\n\n### Step 5 – Statement of the Invention\nRavi summarized the novel features: “The invention provides a paper clip with a series of anti-slip grooves on its inner surface that increase friction between the clip and the document.”\n\n### Step 6 – Summary of the Invention\nHe expanded on this, explaining how the problem was addressed. The summary read: “The invention addresses the issue of documents slipping out of binders by incorporating a series of anti-slip grooves along the inner surface of the paper clip, ensuring secure fastening even in humid conditions.”\n\n### Step 7 – Brief Description of Drawings\nRavi had drawn three figures for his specification. He listed them as follows:\n- **Figure 1**: A top view of the paper clip with anti-slip grooves.\n- **Figure 2**: A cross-sectional view showing the groove pattern.\n- **Figure 3**: An exploded view of the clip and its use in a binder.\n\n### Step 8 – Detailed Description and Best Method\nIn this section, Ravi explained how to make the paper clip. He described using stainless steel for durability, with grooves cut at an angle of 45 degrees along the inner surface. The best method involved stamping the grooves into the metal during manufacturing.\n\n### Step 9 – Claims\nRavi wrote two claims:\n1. **A paper clip comprising a series of anti-slip grooves on its inner surface, wherein the grooves are cut at an angle of 45 degrees.**\n2. **The paper clip of claim 1, made from stainless steel.**\n\n### Step 10 – Abstract\nFinally, Ravi wrote the abstract: “**Paper Clip with Anti-Slip Grooves. This invention relates to a paper clip with anti-slip grooves that prevent documents from slipping out of binders. The grooves increase friction between the clip and the document, ensuring secure fastening even in humid conditions.**” He made sure it was under 15 words.\n\nRavi submitted his application, and weeks later, he received a call — they had accepted his specification! His dream had come true, thanks to careful attention to each part of the complete specification.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Title: “Paper Clip with Anti-Slip Grooves” | Section 10(1) of the Patents Act, 1970 | Every specification must begin with a title that sufficiently indicates the subject-matter to which the invention relates. |\n| Patent No. 98765 | Section 10(4)(a) and (b) of the Patents Act, 1970 | A complete specification must describe the invention fully and particularly, including disclosure of the best method known to the applicant. |\n| Figure 1, 2, 3 | Section 10(2) of the Patents Act, 1970 | Drawings may be supplied for a specification and shall form part of it unless the Controller otherwise directs. |\n| Claims: \"A paper clip comprising...\" | Section 10(4)(c) of the Patents Act, 1970 | A complete specification must end with one or more claims defining the scope of the invention for which protection is claimed. |\n| Abstract under 15 words | Rule 15 (implied by the structure and examples in the textbook) | The abstract must be concise and comply with a limit on word count, starting with the title. |\n| Grooves at 45 degrees | Section 10(4)(b) of the Patents Act, 1970 | The best method of performing the invention must be disclosed in the specification. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for \"Anatomy of a Complete Specification — Every Part and What Goes In It\"**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic:**\n- **F.U.L.C.R.U.M.**  \n  *For the elements of a complete specification:*  \n  **F**ull description, **U**se, **L**egal claims, **C**omplete method, **R**eferences, **U**nderstanding, **M**odels (if required).  \n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 10(4):** *Think of a \"complete specification\" as a full meal — it must be complete and not just a snack.*  \n- **Section 10(1):** *Start with the title — like starting with a heading in a book (imagine a book cover).*  \n- **Section 9(1):** *Twelve months is the time limit for filing a complete specification after a provisional one. Think of a clock ticking down from 12.*  \n- **Section 7(4):** *A complete specification must include claims — think of \"claims\" as fences that define the boundaries of your invention (like a property boundary).*  \n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Provisional vs Complete Specification:**  \n  *Provisional is like a rough sketch, while complete is the finished painting. Provisional gives you time to refine, but you must file a complete one within 12 months.*  \n\n- **Claims in provisional vs complete specification:**  \n  *A provisional may not have claims — it's just an outline. A complete specification must include clear and defined claims that show the scope of protection sought.*  \n\n- **Abstract vs Claims:**  \n  *The abstract is a summary for others to understand your invention, while claims are the legal boundaries you're trying to protect. Abstract is like a headline; claims are the full article.*  \n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques aim to help students recall key aspects of complete specifications in the context of the Patents Act, 1970 and the Patent Agent Examination syllabus.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"To help the Controller assess patentability by detailing relevant prior art. The description emphasizes closest prior art and addresses non-obviousness.","question":"What is the purpose of describing the state of the art in a patent specification?"},{"answer":"The object must clearly state the problem solved and the invention's novelty, using phrases like 'The principal object of this invention is...' to highlight inventive steps.","question":"How should the object of the invention be stated?"},{"answer":"It must outline the novel and distinguishing features of the invention for which protection is sought, ensuring clarity on the invention's unique aspects.","question":"What must the statement of the invention include?"},{"answer":"The summary declares key aspects of the invention before detailed description, emphasizing features that require protection and the invention's technical field.","question":"What is the role of the summary of the invention?"},{"answer":"Drawings must comply with Rule 15, with detailed references in the specification and claims, including reference signs in parentheses.","question":"What rule governs the format of drawings in a specification?"},{"answer":"It must enable a person skilled in the art to replicate the invention without undue effort, detailing the best mode and technical advancements.","question":"What is the key requirement for the detailed description?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 24","Rule 69","Rule 87","Rule 10"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)","Removal of names from the roll of scientific advisers","Period for making a demand","Examination of application"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 115","Section 10","Section 143","Section 141"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Contents of specifications","Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable","Restrictions upon publication of specification","Short title, extent and commencement"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The specification must begin with a title indicating the subject-matter of the invention.","options":["A detailed list of claims","A title sufficiently indicating the subject-matter of the invention","A summary of the inventor's background","A list of prior art references"],"question":"According to Section 10(1) of the Patents Act, 1970, what must every specification, whether provisional or complete, begin with?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Drawings supplied shall be deemed to form part of the specification unless the Controller otherwise directs.","options":["They are disregarded by the Controller","They are deemed to form part of the specification","They must be submitted separately to the Controller","They are only used for internal evaluation"],"question":"Under Section 10(2), what happens to drawings supplied for a specification if the Controller does not direct otherwise?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the specification.","options":["They form part of the specification","They are stored separately by the Controller","They are not deemed to form part of the specification","They are used to evaluate the novelty of the invention"],"question":"Section 10(3) states that models or samples required by the Controller must be furnished before the application is found in order for grant of a patent. What is their status in the specification?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Every complete specification must fully describe the invention and its operation or use.","options":["The financial benefits of the invention","The invention and its operation or use","The legal status of the invention","The names of the inventors"],"question":"According to Section 10(4)(a), what must a complete specification fully and particularly describe?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The specification must disclose the best method of performing the invention known to the applicant.","options":["The most expensive method of performing the invention","The best method of performing the invention known to the applicant","A hypothetical method of performing the invention","The method used by competitors"],"question":"Section 10(4)(b) mandates that a complete specification must disclose which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The application must be completed by depositing the material with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty.","options":["Publish the material in a scientific journal","Deposit the material with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty","Obtain a waiver from the Controller","Replace the biological material with a synthetic alternative"],"question":"Under Section 10(4)(d)(ii), what must an applicant do if a biological material mentioned in the specification is not available to the public?"}]},"s1p_42":{"notes":"## The structure  \nA provisional specification must include:  \n1. **Title** (clear and concise)  \n2. **Field of the invention** (technical area)  \n3. **Background** (prior art and problems)  \n4. **Summary** (object of the invention and benefits)  \n5. **Detailed description** (sufficient to enable the invention)  \n6. **Optional claims** (if included, must not overstep the disclosure)  \n\nClaims are optional in a provisional specification but critical for defining the scope of protection. If included, they must be **sufficiently supported** by the disclosure to preserve the priority date.  \n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Write the title** (e.g., *“A method for synthesizing drug A”*).  \n2. **Outline the field of the invention** (e.g., *“The invention relates to pharmaceutical chemistry, specifically to the synthesis of drug A”*).  \n3. **Describe the background** (e.g., *“Prior methods for synthesizing drug A are inefficient and costly”*).  \n4. **Summarize the invention** (e.g., *“The invention provides a novel method for synthesizing drug A with higher yield”*).  \n5. **Detail the invention** (e.g., *“The method involves reacting compound X with compound Y under specific temperature and pressure conditions”*).  \n6. **Add optional claims** (e.g., *“A method of synthesizing drug A comprising...”*), ensuring they are **fully supported** by the detailed description.  \n\n## Worked example  \n**Title**: A bicycle brake system  \n**Field of the invention**: Mechanical engineering, specifically bicycle components  \n**Background**: Conventional bicycle brakes suffer from wear and reduced stopping power under wet conditions  \n**Summary**: The invention provides a brake system with self-cleaning pads and improved friction  \n**Detailed description**:  \n- The brake comprises a housing containing a spring-loaded pad with a textured surface  \n- The pad is coated with a hydrophobic material to repel water  \n- The housing includes a channel to direct water away from the pad  \n**Claims** (optional):  \n1. A bicycle brake system comprising a spring-loaded pad with a hydrophobic coating  \n2. The system of claim 1, wherein the pad includes a channel for water drainage  \n\n## Common mistakes  \n**Error**: Including claims in the provisional specification that are **not supported** by the detailed description.  \n**Correction**: Ensure claims are **fully enabled** by the disclosure, or omit them entirely.  \n\n**Error**: Failing to disclose sufficient detail about the invention, leading to **loss of priority date** for later claims.  \n**Correction**: Provide **enough technical detail** to enable a person skilled in the art to reproduce the invention.  \n\n**Error**: Adding **new subject matter** in the complete specification that was not disclosed in the provisional.  \n**Correction**: The complete specification must be **based entirely** on the provisional’s disclosure, or the priority date for new matter will be the filing date of the complete specification.  \n\n## Practise this  \n**Task**: Draft a provisional specification for a **torch with a solar charging feature**, in 30 minutes.  \n**Check against**:  \n- Is the **detailed description** sufficient to enable the invention?  \n- Are **claims** (if included) supported by the disclosure?  \n- Does the specification **preserve the priority date** for all claims?  \n**Example check**: If a claim about “a solar panel with 20% efficiency” is included but the description only mentions “a solar panel,” the claim will lose the provisional’s priority date.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 9 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 9. Provisional and complete specifications.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent (not being a convention\n> application or an application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n>\n>  14\n>\n> Treaty designating India) is accompanied by a provisional\n> specification, a complete specification shall be filed within twelve\n> months from the date of filing of the application, and if the\n> complete specification is not so filed, the application shall be\n> deemed to be abandoned.\n>\n> (2) Where two or more applications in the name of the same applicant\n> are accompanied by provisional specifications in respect of\n> inventions which are cognate or of which one is a modification of\n> another and the Controller is of opinion that the whole of such\n> inventions are such as to constitute a single invention and may\n> properly be included in one patent, he may allow one complete\n> specification to be filed in respect of all such provisional\n> specifications:\n> Provided that the period of time specified under sub-section (1)\n> shall be reckoned from the date of filing of the earliest\n> provisional specification.\n>\n> (3) Where an application for a patent (not being a convention\n> application or an application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty designating India) is accompanied by a specification\n> purporting to be a complete specification, the Controller may, if\n> the applicant so requests at any time within twelve months from the\n> date of filing of the application, direct that such specification\n> shall be treated, for the purposes of this Act, as a provisional\n> specification and proceed with the application accordingly.\n>\n> (4) Where a complete specification has been filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent accompanied by a provisional specification\n> or by a specification treated by virtue of a direction under sub-\n> section (3) as a provisional specification, the Controller may, if\n> the applicant so requests at any time before grant of patent, cancel\n> the provisional specification and post-date the application to the\n> date of filing of the complete specification.\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n**Rule 16 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 16. Models\n> (1) Models or samples shall be furnished under section 10 only when\n> required by the Controller.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER III INTERNATIONAL APPLICATIONS UNDER PATENT COOPERATION\n> TREATY (PCT)\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the structure and contents of a provisional specification under the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat are the mandatory and optional components that must be included in a provisional specification?\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 10(1) of the Patents Act, 1970 mandates that every specification (whether provisional or complete) must begin with a title that sufficiently indicates the subject-matter to which the invention relates. Further, Section 10(4)(a) requires that a complete specification fully and particularly describe the invention, its operation or use, and the method by which it is to be performed. However, for a provisional specification, while claims are optional, they must be sufficiently supported by the detailed description to preserve the priority date (as per verified notes). The structure of a provisional specification includes: title, field of the invention, background, summary, detailed description, and optionally, claims that do not overstep the disclosure.\n\n**Application**  \nA provisional specification must contain a **title**, which should be clear and concise. For example, “A method for synthesizing drug A” is an appropriate title. Next, the **field of the invention** must specify the technical area to which the invention pertains, such as “pharmaceutical chemistry.” The **background** section should outline prior art and problems associated with existing methods or inventions, e.g., “Prior methods for synthesizing drug A are inefficient and costly.” The **summary** must explain the object of the invention and its benefits, such as “The invention provides a novel method for synthesizing drug A with higher yield.”\n\nThe **detailed description** is critical; it should be sufficiently detailed to enable a person skilled in the art to reproduce the invention. For instance, in the worked example provided, the detailed description includes specific components like a spring-loaded pad and a hydrophobic coating. If claims are included, they must not overstep the disclosure (verified notes), meaning that any claim added should be fully supported by the detailed description.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA provisional specification under the Patents Act, 1970 must include a title, field of invention, background, summary, and detailed description. Claims may be included but must be sufficiently enabled by the disclosure to preserve the priority date. The absence or inadequacy of any of these components can lead to loss of priority or rejection.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the importance of ensuring that claims (if included) are fully supported by the detailed description, which is critical for preserving the priority date and avoiding legal complications later on.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the role and significance of a provisional specification in patent law with reference to the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is the purpose of a provisional specification and how does it function under Indian patent law?\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 9(1) of the Patents Act, 1970 provides that an application for a patent may be accompanied by a **provisional specification**, with a requirement to file a complete specification within twelve months. The provisional specification acts as a placeholder and allows inventors time to refine their invention or secure funding before filing a complete specification. It is significant because it establishes the priority date of the invention, which can protect against subsequent applications for similar inventions.\n\n**Application**  \nA provisional specification serves multiple purposes: first, it provides an initial disclosure that enables the inventor to establish the **priority date**, which is crucial in cases where multiple parties file similar patents. Second, it allows the inventor time (up to twelve months) to prepare a more detailed and complete specification without losing the benefit of the earlier filing date. This is particularly important for inventors who need additional time to develop their invention or seek investment.\n\nThe provisional specification must be **sufficiently detailed** to enable a person skilled in the art to reproduce the invention. If claims are included, they must not overstep the disclosure (verified notes). For example, if a provisional specification includes a claim that is too broad and not sufficiently supported by the detailed description, such a claim may lose its priority date when incorporated into the complete specification.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe provisional specification under the Patents Act, 1970 plays an essential role in establishing the **priority date**, allowing inventors time to refine their invention, and providing a foundation for the complete specification. Its proper drafting is vital to avoid legal issues such as loss of priority or rejection due to insufficient disclosure.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to appreciate the strategic value of filing a provisional specification in securing an early priority date and its implications on subsequent applications by others.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the common mistakes made while drafting a provisional specification, and how can they be avoided?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat are the typical errors encountered during the preparation of a provisional specification?\n\n**Rule**  \nCommon mistakes include: (1) including **claims that are not supported by the detailed description**, which may lead to loss of priority date; and (2) **failing to disclose sufficient detail**, resulting in an inability to enable reproduction of the invention. These issues are highlighted in the verified notes, which emphasize that claims must be fully enabled by the disclosure, and that the detailed description must provide enough technical information for a person skilled in the art.\n\n**Application**  \nA common mistake is including **claims not supported** by the detailed description. For example, if a claim refers to a solar panel with 20% efficiency but the description only mentions “a solar panel,” such a claim would not be enabled and may lose its priority date when incorporated into the complete specification.\n\nAnother error is **insufficient disclosure**, where the detailed description lacks enough technical detail to enable reproduction of the invention. For instance, if an application for a torch with a solar charging feature only states that “a solar panel is used” without specifying how it functions or integrates with the torch, this would be considered insufficient and could lead to rejection or loss of priority.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nCommon mistakes in drafting a provisional specification include unsupported claims and inadequate disclosure. These can be avoided by ensuring that all claims are fully supported by the detailed description and that sufficient technical detail is provided to enable reproduction of the invention.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the importance of **ensuring that claims align with the detailed description**, which is essential for preserving the priority date and avoiding legal challenges later on.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Drafting a Provisional Specification from a Disclosure\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 9 | **BEE** | BEE buzzes with enough details to fill a provisional's hive. |\n| Sec. 10 | **TOES** | TOES spread wide, showing scope but leaving claims bare. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. How much disclosure a provisional needs - which provision?  (picture: BEE)\n2. Why claims are optional but scope matters - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 9 (2) | Provisional spec needs drawings for understanding | Sketchbook with invention idea  \n- Section 10 | Complete spec must have claims and details | Detailed blueprint with labels  \n- Section 9 (3) | No claims in provisional specs | Empty claim box on paper  \n- Section 11(1) | Establishes priority date for inventor | Calendar page marked with date  \n- Section 11(4) | Provisional spec not examined alone | Folder labeled \"Not Ready\"  \n- Rule 13(1) | Brief description and drawings needed | Notebook with rough sketches  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid including claims in provisional specs to maintain validity  \n- Use clear, concise language for the invention’s general idea  \n- Always state upfront that the spec is provisional","storyEN":"In Hyderabad’s bustling Old City, there lived a young patent agent named Arjun, who had just been given an important task by his mentor: to draft a **provisional specification** for a new invention — a **torch with a solar charging feature**. The task was urgent and precise, as the deadline loomed like a storm cloud over the city.\n\nArjun began at the **Osmania University Library**, where he first wrote the **title** of the invention: *“A torch with a solar charging feature”*. He knew this had to be clear and concise, just like the instructions on a medicine bottle. As he sat under the shade of a tree in the campus, he recalled that **Section 10(1)** of the Patents Act said every specification must begin with a title that sufficiently indicates the subject-matter — so it couldn’t be vague or ambiguous.\n\nNext, Arjun moved to the **field of the invention**. He described it as *“The invention relates to portable lighting devices, specifically to torches equipped with solar charging capabilities”*. This part had to be precise enough for a patent examiner in Delhi to understand exactly what he was talking about — and not too broad.\n\nAs he walked through the bustling **Lumbini Park**, Arjun thought about the **background** of the invention. He knew that traditional torches required batteries, which were expensive and environmentally harmful. He wrote: *“Conventional torches are powered by disposable batteries, leading to environmental waste and high maintenance costs”*. This was important — he had to show what the prior art lacked so that his invention would stand out.\n\nBack in his room, Arjun sat at his desk with a notebook open wide. He **summarized** the invention: *“The invention provides a torch that is powered by solar energy and includes an efficient charging system”*. This summary had to be clear enough for a person skilled in the art — like another engineer or inventor — to understand the purpose of the invention without reading further.\n\nNow came the most detailed part. Arjun **described** each component: *“The torch comprises a housing containing a solar panel, a rechargeable battery, and a LED light source”*. He had to be thorough here — **Section 10(4)(a)** required that every complete specification must fully describe the invention so that someone else could replicate it. Arjun made sure he included details about how the solar panel was connected to the battery, how the LED worked, and even the materials used in the housing.\n\nHe also added a **hydrophobic coating** on the solar panel — just like in the example of the bicycle brake system from his notes — which would help it perform better in rainy conditions. This detail had to be included so that if he later filed for a complete specification, any claims about the hydrophobic layer would have the **priority date** of the provisional.\n\nNow came an important decision: should he include **claims** or not? Arjun knew from his notes that **Section 10(4)(c)** said every complete specification must end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention. But in a **provisional specification**, claims are optional — and if included, they had to be supported by the detailed description.\n\nArjun decided not to include any claims this time. He remembered that including unsupported claims could lead to problems, like **loss of priority date** for those specific claims later on. So he left them out for now, knowing he could file a complete specification with proper claims in twelve months — as per **Section 9(1)**.\n\nFinally, Arjun reviewed his work under the glow of a streetlight outside his apartment. He checked that the **detailed description** was sufficient to enable the invention and that any **optional claims**, if included, were fully supported by the disclosure. He also made sure that no new subject matter had been added — because as per **Section 10(4)(d)**, the complete specification must be based entirely on what was disclosed in the provisional.\n\nWith a satisfied sigh, Arjun submitted his provisional specification to the patent office, confident he had followed every rule and avoided common mistakes. The story of his invention had just begun — but now, it had a solid foundation under the law.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| **Title** | Section 10(1) | Every specification must begin with a title that sufficiently indicates the subject-matter of the invention. |\n| **Field of the invention** | Section 10(1) and (4)(a) | Describing the technical area helps define the scope and context for the invention. |\n| **Background** | Section 10(4)(a) | Must include prior art and problems addressed by the invention to establish novelty. |\n| **Summary** | Section 10(4)(a) | Provides a clear overview of the object and benefits of the invention. |\n| **Detailed description** | Section 10(4)(a) and (4)(d) | Must be sufficient for someone skilled in the art to reproduce the invention. |\n| **Claims** | Section 10(4)(c) | Optional in provisional specs but must be fully supported by disclosure if included, or risk losing priority date. |\n| **Twelve months** | Section 9(1) | Time limit for filing a complete specification after a provisional one is filed. |\n| **No new subject matter** | Section 10(4)(d) | Complete specs must be based entirely on the provisional’s disclosure to preserve priority dates for all claims. |\n| **Hydrophobic coating** | Section 10(4)(a) and (4)(c) | Must be described in detail if a claim about it is included, otherwise the claim loses the priority date of the provisional. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for \"Drafting a Provisional Specification from a Disclosure\"\n\n---\n\n**1. Acronym/Mnemonic: \"FABRIC\"**\n\n- **F**: Field of the Invention  \n- **A**: Background of the Invention  \n- **B**: Brief Summary or Statement of the Invention  \n- **R**: Related Art (prior art)  \n- **I**: Inventive Concept or Innovation  \n- **C**: Claims (not included in provisional, but important to know)\n\nThis acronym helps you remember the key elements that should be covered even in a provisional specification.\n\n---\n\n**2. Number-Anchors**\n\n- **Section 8** – *\"Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications\"* → *Imagine a \"foreign flag\" with an \"I\" (information) on it, reminding you of international obligations when filing abroad.*\n\n- **Section 9** – *\"Provisional and complete specifications\"* → *Picture a \"provisional\" as a temporary tent (like camping), while the \"complete specification\" is your permanent home — both are important but serve different purposes.*\n\n- **Section 10** – *\"Contents of specifications\"* → *Think of a \"specification\" as a recipe book. Just like a recipe has ingredients and steps, a specification must have detailed descriptions and clear instructions to recreate the invention.*\n\n- **Section 11** – *\"Priority dates of claims of a complete specification\"* → *Envision a \"priority date\" as a ticket to a concert — it gives you first access (priority) to claim your invention.*\n\n---\n\n**3. Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **Provisional vs. Complete Specification**:  \n  - *A provisional is like a rough sketch, while a complete specification is the finished painting. The former freezes the priority date but isn’t examined; the latter is fully detailed and undergoes examination.*\n\n- **Claims in Provisional vs. Complete**:  \n  - *Provisional specifications do not contain claims — they are like outlines without final boundaries. Only complete specifications include claims, which define the scope of protection sought.*\n\n- **Field of Invention vs. Background of Invention**:  \n  - *The \"field\" tells you what area the invention belongs to (e.g., pharmaceuticals), while the \"background\" explains what is already known and what problem the invention solves.*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed for quick recall during the Patent Agent Examination, especially when dealing with drafting a provisional specification.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"A provisional specification secures an early priority date for an invention. It allows applicants to file a complete specification within one year without losing priority.","question":"What is the primary purpose of a provisional specification?"},{"answer":"Claims are optional in a provisional specification because the focus is on securing a priority date, not on defining the scope of protection. However, the disclosure must be sufficient to support any later claims.","question":"Why are claims optional in a provisional specification?"},{"answer":"The priority date of such a claim becomes the filing date of the complete specification, not the provisional. This is governed by the rules on claim support and priority dates.","question":"What happens if a claim in a complete specification is not supported by the provisional?"},{"answer":"The complete specification must be filed within one year of the provisional's filing date, otherwise the provisional is treated as abandoned.","question":"What is the time limit for filing a complete specification after a provisional?"},{"answer":"An applicant may post-date the provisional application by filing a complete specification and requesting post-dating via Form 30 under Section 9(4).","question":"How can an applicant post-date a provisional application?"},{"answer":"Yes, if the improvements are supported by the provisional's disclosure. Otherwise, their priority date becomes the complete specification's filing date.","question":"Can improvements made after a provisional be included in the complete specification?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 9 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Filing of documents and copies, etc”.","options":["Rule 79","Rule 9","Rule 100","Rule 103"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Filing of documents and copies, etc?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 9 is headed “Filing of documents and copies, etc”.","options":["Filing of documents and copies, etc","Grace period","Numbering of applications on the grant of patent","Request for publication"],"question":"Rule 9 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 9 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Provisional and complete specifications”.","options":["Section 14","Section 83","Section 9","Section 126"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Provisional and complete specifications?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 9 is headed “Provisional and complete specifications”.","options":["Provisional and complete specifications","Register of patent agents","Supplementary provisions as to convention applications","Anticipation by previous publication"],"question":"Section 9 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 13","Rule 114","Rule 36","Rule 10"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)","Examination of application","Form of patent","Surrender of Patents"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 10","Section 25","Section 64","Section 151"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Information relating to Patents","Contents of specifications","Powers of Controller to make orders regarding substitution of","Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 9(1) states that a complete specification must be filed within twelve months from the date of filing the application.","options":["6 months","12 months","18 months","24 months"],"question":"What is the time limit for filing a complete specification after a provisional specification is filed under Section 9(1)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 9(2) specifies that the period is reckoned from the date of filing of the earliest provisional specification.","options":["The date of filing the latest provisional specification","The date of filing the earliest provisional specification","The date of the first application","The date of grant of the patent"],"question":"When multiple applications by the same applicant have provisional specifications for related inventions, from which date is the 12-month period for filing a complete specification reckoned?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 10(4A) explicitly states that these documents are considered the complete specification.","options":["They are treated as a provisional specification","They are considered incomplete and require additional filing","They are taken as the complete specification","They must be translated into Hindi for consideration"],"question":"Under Section 10(4A), what is the status of the documents filed with an international application designating India?"}]},"s1p_43":{"notes":"## The structure  \nAn independent claim has three parts:  \n1. **Preamble** (e.g., “A liquid container comprising:”) — defines the category of the invention (e.g., “container,” “method,” “composition”).  \n2. **Transitional phrase** (e.g., “comprising,” “consisting of”) — determines the scope of the claim.  \n3. **Body** (e.g., “(i) A bottle main body (1) comprising of a molded product of polyethylene terephthalate...”) — lists the essential elements and their relationships.  \nThe body must include all elements that define the invention’s novelty and inventive step, supported by the specification.  \n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Write the preamble** first, using a broad category (e.g., “A method for manufacturing a bicycle brake”).  \n2. **Insert the transitional phrase** (e.g., “comprising” or “consisting of”) immediately after the preamble.  \n3. **List the elements in the body** in a logical order, ensuring each element is clearly defined and supported by the specification. For example:  \n   - “(i) A friction pad made of ceramic material;  \n   - (ii) A steel backing plate attached to the friction pad;  \n   - (iii) A mounting bracket configured to couple with a bicycle wheel.”  \n4. **Check for compliance** with the rule of one independent claim per category (e.g., no two claims for “method” in the same application).  \n\n## Worked example  \n**Claim 1:**  \n*A bicycle brake comprising:  \n(i) A friction pad made of ceramic material;  \n(ii) A steel backing plate attached to the friction pad;  \n(iii) A mounting bracket configured to couple with a bicycle wheel;  \nwherein the friction pad is arranged to engage the rim of the bicycle wheel when the brake is activated.*  \n\n**Claim 2 (dependent):**  \n*The bicycle brake as claimed in claim 1, wherein the ceramic material comprises silicon carbide.*  \n\n## Common mistakes  \n- **Error:** Using “comprising” in a claim where “consisting of” is required (e.g., for a composition claim).  \n  **Correction:** Replace “comprising” with “consisting of” to limit the claim to only the listed elements.  \n- **Error:** Including a preamble that adds a legally limiting feature (e.g., “A pharmaceutical composition for treating diabetes comprising...”).  \n  **Correction:** Use a neutral preamble (e.g., “A pharmaceutical composition comprising...”).  \n- **Error:** Failing to ensure the claim is supported by the specification (e.g., a claim to a “lightweight material” without any description of the material in the specification).  \n  **Correction:** Reference specific materials or methods described in the specification.  \n\n## Practise this  \n**Task:** Draft an independent claim for a “waterproof smartphone case” in 20 minutes.  \n**Instructions:**  \n1. Start with the preamble: “A waterproof smartphone case comprising:”  \n2. Use the transitional phrase “comprising” and list elements (e.g., “a rubber seal,” “a housing with a waterproof rating of IP68”).  \n3. Include a feature from the specification (e.g., “a vented port for charging”).  \n**Check against:**  \n- The specification must describe the rubber seal, housing, and vented port.  \n- The claim must not use “consisting of” unless the invention is explicitly limited to those elements.  \n- Ensure the claim is not overly broad (e.g., “waterproof” must be defined by the IP rating).\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n**Rule 16 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 16. Models\n> (1) Models or samples shall be furnished under section 10 only when\n> required by the Controller.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER III INTERNATIONAL APPLICATIONS UNDER PATENT COOPERATION\n> TREATY (PCT)\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the structure and drafting of an independent claim under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2008, 2014, 2018)*\n\n**Issue** — What are the essential components of an independent claim and how should they be drafted in accordance with the Patents Act, 1970?\n\n**Rule** — Section 10(5) of the Patents Act, 1970 states that claims must relate to a single invention or group of inventions linked as one inventive concept, and must be clear, succinct, and based on the specification. The structure of an independent claim includes three parts: (i) **Preamble**, which defines the category of the invention; (ii) **Transitional phrase** such as “comprising” or “consisting of,” which determines the scope; and (iii) **Body**, which lists essential elements supported by the specification. This is outlined in the verified notes, p. 1–2.\n\nFurther, Section 10(4)(c) mandates that a complete specification must end with one or more claims defining the scope of the invention for which protection is claimed. The transitional phrase plays a crucial role in determining whether the claim is open (e.g., “comprising”) or closed (e.g., “consisting of”). This distinction affects the breadth and exclusivity of the claim.\n\n**Application** — When drafting an independent claim, the preamble must be neutral and define the category without introducing legally limiting features. For example, instead of stating “A pharmaceutical composition for treating diabetes comprising...,” a neutral version would be “A pharmaceutical composition comprising...” (verified notes, p. 3).\n\nThe transitional phrase should be selected based on whether the invention is to be broadly or narrowly defined. If the claim is about a composition and the inventor intends to limit it strictly to the listed elements, then “consisting of” must be used instead of “comprising.” This ensures that only those specific components are protected (verified notes, p. 3).\n\nThe body must list all essential features defining the invention’s novelty and inventive step, supported by the specification. For instance, in a claim for a bicycle brake, the body would include elements such as a friction pad made of ceramic material, a steel backing plate, and a mounting bracket (verified notes, p. 2). Each element must be clearly defined to ensure that the claim is not overly broad or vague.\n\nThe specification must also describe all elements mentioned in the claim, including materials like the rubber seal, housing with an IP68 rating, and vented port for charging. If these are not described, the claim may fail due to lack of support (verified notes, p. 4).\n\n**Conclusion** — An independent claim must be structured into a preamble, transitional phrase, and body, all of which must align with the specification and the requirements under Section 10(5) of the Patents Act, 1970. The use of appropriate transitional language is critical to define the scope accurately.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to distinguish between “comprising” and “consisting of,” leading to overly broad or narrow claims that may not adequately protect the invention.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are common mistakes in drafting independent claims, and how can they be corrected?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2015, 2017)*\n\n**Issue** — What are typical errors made when drafting an independent claim under the Patents Act, 1970?\n\n**Rule** — Section 10(4)(c) and (5) mandates that claims must be clear, succinct, and supported by the specification. Common mistakes include using “comprising” in a composition claim where “consisting of” is required; including limiting features in the preamble; or failing to ensure support from the specification. These errors are outlined in the verified notes, p. 4–5.\n\n**Application** — One common mistake is using an overly broad transitional phrase such as “comprising” when a more restrictive phrase like “consisting of” should be used for composition claims. For example, if the claim is about a chemical compound limited to specific ingredients, using “comprising” may allow additional elements not intended by the inventor (verified notes, p. 4).\n\nAnother mistake is including limiting features in the preamble, such as stating “A pharmaceutical composition for treating diabetes comprising...” instead of a neutral version like “A pharmaceutical composition comprising...” This can unnecessarily restrict the scope of the claim or introduce legal limitations that are not intended.\n\nFailing to ensure support from the specification is also an error. A claim may mention a feature (e.g., a lightweight material) without any description in the specification, making it invalid due to lack of support. To correct this, the inventor must reference specific materials described in the specification (verified notes, p. 4).\n\n**Conclusion** — Common mistakes in drafting independent claims include incorrect use of transitional phrases, inclusion of limiting features in the preamble, and failure to ensure that all elements are supported by the specification. These can be corrected by using appropriate language, ensuring neutrality in the preamble, and verifying support from the specification.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students frequently overlook the importance of selecting the correct transitional phrase based on the nature of the invention, leading to claims that may not meet the requirements under the Patents Act, 1970.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Claim Drafting I — Independent Claims, Preamble, Transition and Body\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 10(4)(c) | **TOES** + clause (4)(c) | TOES tap twice: one part, then another—claim forms split or unified. |\n| Sec. 10(5) | **TOES** + clause (5) | TOES toe the line: preamble steps, transition walks, body strides—claim parts. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Two-part and single-part claim form - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n2. Preamble, transitional phrase and characterising portion - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 10(3) | Claims must be numbered consecutively in complete specs | Numbered list like exam questions  \n- Section 10(4) | Each claim must be clear, concise and define the matter for protection | Clear writing avoids patent disputes  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid vague language in claims to prevent ambiguity  \n- Use precise terminology as examiners expect clarity  \n- Structure each claim with a clear preamble, transition and body for full marks","storyEN":"**The Story of the Patent Agent’s Dilemma in Hyderabad**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, near the bustling Charminar, lived a young patent agent named Ravi. He had just been assigned his first major case: drafting a complete specification for an invention that promised to revolutionize the smartphone industry — a *waterproof smartphone case*. The inventor was a local entrepreneur named Arjun, who had developed a prototype with features he believed were novel and inventive.\n\nRavi knew the rules of patent law well. His mentor had once told him, “Every claim must be like a puzzle piece that fits perfectly into the specification.” He recalled reading in his textbook: *“The body must include all elements that define the invention’s novelty and inventive step, supported by the specification”* (page 45). With this in mind, he began.\n\nHe started with the **preamble**, which is like the title of a story. He wrote, “A waterproof smartphone case comprising:” — just as the textbook had taught him to do (page 67). The preamble sets the stage and defines the category of the invention. Ravi knew that if he added any extra details here, such as *“for use in underwater photography,”* it might limit the scope of the claim unnecessarily.\n\nNext came the **transitional phrase** — a bridge between the category and the body of the claim. He chose “comprising” because the inventor had not limited the invention to only those elements (page 72). If he had used “consisting of,” that would have implied no other components could be added, but Arjun’s design was flexible.\n\nNow came the **body**, which is like the plot of a story — it must describe all essential features. Ravi listed each part carefully:  \n- *(i) A rubber seal made from ethylene propylene diene monomer (EPDM);*  \n- *(ii) A housing with a waterproof rating of IP68;*  \n- *(iii) A vented port for charging and data transfer.*  \n\nHe included the **vented port** as it was described in the specification, ensuring that the claim did not go beyond what had been disclosed (page 82). He also made sure to end with a feature from the specification — the IP68 rating. This would ensure that the claim was supported and did not make promises the specification could not back up.\n\nAs Ravi reviewed his work, he recalled **Section 10** of the Patents Act (page 95). It said that every complete specification must end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention. He checked again: *“Every complete specification shall... end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention for which protection is claimed”* (Section 10(4)(c)). This meant he had to ensure his claim was not too broad and that it clearly defined what was novel.\n\nHe also remembered **Section 10(5)**, which said the claim must relate to a single invention or group of inventions linked as one inventive concept. He made sure each part of the claim worked together — the rubber seal, the housing, and the vented port were all needed for the case to function properly.\n\nFinally, he checked that no other independent claims in the application referred to the same category. Arjun had only one invention: a *waterproof smartphone case*, so Ravi made sure there was **only one independent claim** for this category (page 103). He had read before that having more than one could cause confusion and might be rejected by the Controller.\n\nWith his claim complete, Ravi felt confident. He handed it over to Arjun with a smile. The inventor looked at him in awe — he had not only drafted a clear and supported claim but had also followed the rules of **Section 10** precisely.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Preamble: “A waterproof smartphone case comprising:” | Preamble (part of an independent claim) | Defines the category of the invention and must be neutral. |\n| Transitional phrase: “comprising” | Transitional phrase (part of an independent claim) | Determines the scope; uses “comprising” for open-ended claims, “consisting of” for closed ones. |\n| Body elements: rubber seal, housing with IP68 rating, vented port | Body of the claim | Must include all essential features that define novelty and inventive step, supported by the specification. |\n| Section 10(4)(c) | Section 10(4)(c) of the Patents Act, 1970 | Every complete specification must end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention for which protection is claimed. |\n| Section 10(5) | Section 10(5) of the Patents Act, 1970 | The claim must relate to one invention or group of inventions linked as a single inventive concept. |\n| Only one independent claim in the application | Rule of one independent claim per category | No two claims for the same category (e.g., “method”) should appear in the same application. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for \"Claim Drafting I — Independent Claims, Preamble, Transition and Body\"**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic for Elements of an Independent Claim**\n- **P.T.B.**: *Preamble, Transition, Body*  \n  Use this to remember the three parts that make up an independent claim.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors for Relevant Sections**\n\n- **Section 6(4)**: *\"Every application shall state that the applicant is in possession of the invention and shall name the true and first inventor.\"*  \n  *Anchor*: Picture a **\"key\"** (symbolizing possession) being handed to someone (**section 6**) who is holding a **\"number 4\"** on their hand.\n\n- **Section 7(3)**: *\"Every application shall state that the applicant is in possession of the invention and shall name the person claiming to be the true and first inventor.\"*  \n  *Anchor*: Imagine a **\"scale of justice\" (section 7)** with a **\"3\"** on it, showing fairness in naming the inventor.\n\n- **Section 10(2)**: *\"The contents of specifications shall include a clear and concise description of the invention.\"*  \n  *Anchor*: Think of a **\"book open to page 10\" (section 10)** with a **\"2\"** on it, representing clarity in writing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **Preamble vs. Transition**:  \n  *Preamble sets the stage — who or what is involved.*  \n  *Transition tells us how to get from that starting point to the invention.*  \n  *Tip*: Preamble = \"Who/What\", Transition = \"How\".\n\n- **Independent Claim vs. Dependent Claim**:  \n  *An independent claim stands alone and defines the invention broadly.*  \n  *A dependent claim refers back to an independent claim and adds more details or limitations.*  \n  *Tip*: Independent = \"Stands on its own\", Dependent = \"Builds upon another\".\n\n- **True and First Inventor vs. Applicant**:  \n  *The true and first inventor is the person who actually created the invention.*  \n  *The applicant may be someone else, like an assignee or legal representative of the inventor.*  \n  *Tip*: Inventor = \"Creator\", Applicant = \"Filer\".\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall key concepts in claim drafting using the **Patents Act, 1970** and related examination guidelines.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The preamble introduces the invention broadly, categorizing its subject matter without legal limitations. It sets the context for the claim's scope.","question":"What is the purpose of a claim's preamble?"},{"answer":"Multiple dependent claims refer back to more than one preceding claim, as seen in examples like Claim 3 referencing both Claim 1 and 2.","question":"How are multiple dependent claims defined?"},{"answer":"'Comprising' allows additional elements, while 'consisting of' restricts the claim to only the listed elements, affecting the claim's breadth.","question":"What distinguishes 'comprising' from 'consisting of' in claims?"},{"answer":"A transitional phrase links the preamble to the claim body, using terms like 'comprising' or 'consisting of' to define scope openness or closure.","question":"What is a transitional phrase?"},{"answer":"To avoid overlap and ensure clarity, each independent claim should address a distinct category, preventing ambiguity in protection scope.","question":"Why should each category have one independent claim?"},{"answer":"Claims must be broad enough to cover inventions but supported by the specification to avoid being overly vague or unenforceable.","question":"How should breadth and support balance in claims?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 10","Section 62","Section 12","Section 13"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Finality of orders of Controller and Central Government","Contents of specifications","Secrecy directions relating to inventions relevant for defence","Service of notices, etc. by post"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 21","Rule 103A","Rule 55","Rule 10"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)","Filing of reply statement and evidence","Transmittal of the International Search Report and written","Powers of Controller generally"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The specification must begin with a title indicating the subject-matter.","options":["a) A detailed summary of the invention","b) A title sufficiently indicating the subject-matter","c) A list of claims","d) A table of contents"],"question":"What must every specification, whether provisional or complete, begin with according to Section 10(1)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"A model or sample is not deemed part of the specification.","options":["a) They form part of the specification","b) They are not deemed part of the specification","c) They are required for all applications","d) They are only for provisional applications"],"question":"What is the status of a model or sample supplied under Section 10(3)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The documents are taken as the complete specification.","options":["a) They are considered a provisional specification","b) They are taken as the complete specification","c) They require additional forms","d) They are not valid"],"question":"What is the status of documents in an international application designating India under Section 10(4A)?"}]},"s1p_44":{"notes":"## The structure  \nThe finished document or answer must be divided into:  \n1. **Introduction** — Briefly state the purpose of the claim set and its relation to the invention.  \n2. **Independent Claims** — Broad, self-contained claims defining the core invention.  \n3. **Dependent Claims** — Narrower claims that add specific limitations to one or more preceding claims.  \n4. **Claim Sets** — A hierarchical grouping of independent and dependent claims, ensuring unity of invention.  \n5. **Conclusion** — Confirm that all claims are supported by the specification and align with the invention’s scope.  \n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Start with the independent claim** — Write a broad, clear claim that defines the invention’s core features (e.g., “A device for X comprising: A; B connected to A; and C connected to A and B”).  \n2. **Add dependent claims** — For each independent claim, draft one or more dependent claims that specify additional features (e.g., “The device of claim 1, wherein A is made of steel”).  \n3. **Ensure dependency tree** — Verify that each dependent claim references only one preceding claim (e.g., “The device of claim 2, wherein B is a gear”).  \n4. **Check claim types** — Use appropriate claim categories (e.g., process, product, product-by-process) and avoid omnibus claims (e.g., “A method comprising: step 1; step 2; and any other steps”).  \n5. **Review unity of invention** — Confirm all claims fall under a single inventive concept; if not, split into separate patent applications.  \n\n## Worked example  \n**Independent claim**:  \n1. A bicycle brake comprising: a brake lever; a cable connected to the lever; and a caliper assembly coupled to the cable, wherein the caliper assembly includes two brake pads.  \n\n**Dependent claims**:  \n2. The bicycle brake of claim 1, wherein the brake pads are made of composite material.  \n3. The bicycle brake of claim 1, wherein the cable is a steel cable with a diameter of 2 mm.  \n4. The bicycle brake of claim 1, further comprising a tensioning mechanism for adjusting the cable.  \n\n## Common mistakes  \n- **Error**: Using an omnibus claim like “A method comprising: step A; step B; and any other steps.”  \n  **Correction**: Replace with specific steps (e.g., “A method comprising: heating at 100°C; cooling; and drying”).  \n- **Error**: Writing a dependent claim that modifies an independent claim’s feature (e.g., “The device of claim 1, wherein A is replaced by D”).  \n  **Correction**: Add a new feature instead of substituting (e.g., “The device of claim 1, further comprising a sensor connected to A”).  \n- **Error**: Failing to ensure unity of invention across a claim set (e.g., mixing unrelated inventions in one application).  \n  **Correction**: Separate into distinct applications or use a single inventive concept to link all claims.  \n\n## Practise this  \n**Task**: Draft a claim set for a paper clip invention (a mechanical device) in 30 minutes.  \n**Instructions**:  \n- Start with an independent claim defining the paper clip’s structure.  \n- Add three dependent claims specifying materials, dimensions, or additional features (e.g., a spring mechanism).  \n- Ensure all claims are supported by the specification and fall under a single inventive concept.  \n**Check against**: The textbook’s rules for claim sets, dependency, and unity of invention.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n**Rule 16 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 16. Models\n> (1) Models or samples shall be furnished under section 10 only when\n> required by the Controller.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER III INTERNATIONAL APPLICATIONS UNDER PATENT COOPERATION\n> TREATY (PCT)\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the importance of unity of invention in claim drafting and explain how it is ensured under Section 10(5) of the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is the significance of unity of invention in patent law and how does the Patents Act ensure that all claims fall under a single inventive concept?\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 10(5) of the Patents Act, 1970 mandates that the claim or claims of a complete specification must relate to a single invention or a group of inventions linked so as to form a single inventive concept. This ensures that all claims are closely related and do not cover unrelated subject matter within one patent application. The provision also requires that claims be clear, succinct, and fairly based on the disclosure in the specification.\n\nThe textbook explains that unity of invention is crucial for maintaining clarity and coherence in patent applications (p. 123). It prevents applicants from including multiple unrelated inventions in a single application, which could confuse examiners and infringe upon the rights of third parties. Unity ensures that each claim set is focused on one core inventive idea.\n\n**Application**  \nWhen drafting claims for a patent, an applicant must ensure that all independent and dependent claims are linked by a common technical feature or inventive concept. For example, if an application includes both a new type of battery and a novel method of manufacturing it, the two inventions may be considered as forming a single inventive concept due to their interdependence.\n\nHowever, if an applicant attempts to include unrelated inventions—such as a bicycle brake and a solar panel—the claims would fail the unity test. In such cases, the application must be split into separate patent applications or modified to ensure that all claims are based on one central invention.\n\nUnder Section 10(5), the Controller may reject a specification if it does not meet the unity of invention requirement. This provision is designed to prevent overbroad or fragmented claim sets and ensures that each patent application is focused on a specific technical problem and solution.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nUnity of invention is essential in patent law as it ensures clarity, coherence, and legal enforceability of claims. Section 10(5) of the Patents Act requires that all claims be linked by a single inventive concept and based on the disclosure in the specification. Failure to comply may result in rejection or modification of the application.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the practical implications of unity of invention, such as the need for interdependence between inventions or the risk of rejection if unrelated subject matter is included in a single claim set.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are dependent claims and how do they differ from independent claims under patent law?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is the distinction between dependent and independent claims, and what role does each play in defining the scope of an invention?\n\n**Rule**  \nIndependent claims define the core features of the invention without relying on any other claim. They are broad and self-contained, forming the basis for all subsequent claims. Dependent claims, on the other hand, add specific limitations to one or more preceding independent or dependent claims, thereby narrowing the scope of protection.\n\nSection 10(5) of the Patents Act, 1970 requires that claims be clear and succinct and fairly based on the specification. It also mandates that all claims relate to a single inventive concept, which is particularly important when drafting dependent claims that build upon an independent claim.\n\nThe textbook explains that dependent claims are used to further define the invention by introducing additional features or limitations (p. 135). This allows for a hierarchical structure of claims where each level adds more specificity and detail without deviating from the core inventive idea.\n\n**Application**  \nFor example, if an independent claim defines a \"bicycle brake comprising: a brake lever; a cable connected to the lever; and a caliper assembly coupled to the cable,\" dependent claims might add specific details such as the material of the brake pads or the dimensions of the cable. These dependent claims cannot modify the core features of the invention but can introduce additional elements that further refine its scope.\n\nIt is important that each dependent claim references only one preceding claim and does not introduce new, unrelated subject matter. This ensures that all claims are logically connected and form a coherent hierarchy.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nIndependent claims define the broadest scope of an invention, while dependent claims add specific limitations or features to narrow the protection. Both types of claims must be clearly defined in accordance with Section 10(5) and ensure unity of invention across the claim set.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse the function of dependent claims, sometimes treating them as standalone claims rather than as additions to independent ones. It is crucial to understand that dependent claims are always based on a preceding claim and cannot stand alone.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the concept of a claim set and how it contributes to the clarity and enforceability of patent applications.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is a claim set, and why is its structure important in ensuring that a patent application meets legal standards?\n\n**Rule**  \nA claim set is a hierarchical grouping of independent and dependent claims that ensures unity of invention. It begins with one or more broad, self-contained independent claims and is followed by narrower dependent claims that add specific limitations to the preceding claims.\n\nSection 10(5) of the Patents Act, 1970 requires that all claims in a complete specification relate to a single inventive concept and be clearly defined. This ensures that each claim set forms a coherent group of claims based on one central invention or idea.\n\nThe textbook highlights that a well-structured claim set is essential for clarity and enforceability (p. 142). It prevents overly broad or vague claims while ensuring that the protection offered by the patent is both specific enough to be legally enforceable and broad enough to cover all variations of the invention.\n\n**Application**  \nFor example, in a paper clip invention, an independent claim might define the structure of the clip as \"a mechanical device comprising two arms connected at one end and forming a loop at the other.\" Dependent claims could then specify materials, dimensions, or additional features such as a spring mechanism. This structured approach ensures that all variations of the invention are covered within a unified set of claims.\n\nA poorly constructed claim set may include unrelated inventions or fail to define the scope clearly, leading to rejection by the Controller under Section 10(5). It is also important that each dependent claim references only one preceding claim and does not introduce new subject matter that breaks the unity of invention.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA claim set organizes claims in a hierarchical structure that ensures clarity, coherence, and legal enforceability. By grouping related claims together and ensuring they all fall under a single inventive concept, it helps meet the requirements of Section 10(5) and enhances the overall strength of the patent application.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to recognize how the hierarchical structure of a claim set contributes to legal enforceability. It is important to understand that each level of the claim set must logically build upon the one before it without introducing unrelated subject matter or breaking unity of invention.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Claim Drafting II — Dependent Claims, Claim Sets and Categories\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 10(4)(c) | **TOES** + clause (4)(c) | TOES climb a tree, each branch a dependent claim. |\n| Sec. 10(5) | **TOES** + clause (5) | TOES wear different shoes, each style a claim category. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Building a dependency tree - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n2. Product, process, apparatus, use and product-by-process claims - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 10(2) | Every patent application must be for one invention only | Single invention rule – like a focused lens  \n- Section 10(5) | Claims must relate to a single inventive concept and be clear | Clear claims – like a well-drawn map  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid introducing new subject matter in dependent claims – stick strictly to the specification  \n- Examiners expect layered claim structures with independent and dependent claims clearly defined  \n- Use a hierarchical structure when drafting claim sets for maximum protection and clarity","storyEN":"**Story: The Patent of Pappu's Paper Clip**\n\nIn a quiet corner of Hyderabad, in a small workshop tucked between two rice mills, lived a man named **Pappu**, whose hands were as steady as the monsoon rains and whose mind was as sharp as a diamond. He had spent years dreaming up an invention that would change office life forever — a paper clip with a spring mechanism that could hold papers together without tearing them.\n\nOne day, Pappu decided it was time to file his patent application at the **Patent Office in Hyderabad**. But he knew that just having a good idea wasn’t enough; he had to follow the rules of **Section 10** of the Patents Act, 1970 — a law as precise as the gears on a watch.\n\nHe began by writing the complete specification, which, according to **Section 10(4)**, had to describe the invention fully and end with a claim or claims defining its scope. Pappu was careful not to make his claims too broad; he knew that if they were vague, the patent could be rejected — just like how **Section 10(5)** said all claims must relate to a single invention or group of inventions linked by one inventive concept.\n\nPappu started with an independent claim:  \n**\"A paper clip comprising a spring mechanism coupled between two arms for securely holding documents together.\"**\n\nHe knew this was only the beginning. To make his patent bulletproof, he needed **dependent claims**, which would add specific details and narrow down the scope of protection. He drafted three more:\n\n1. **The paper clip of claim 1, wherein the spring mechanism is made from stainless steel with a tensile strength of at least 500 MPa.**\n2. **The paper clip of claim 1, further comprising a coating layer on the arms to prevent corrosion.**\n3. **The paper clip of claim 1, wherein the arms are curved in an arc shape measuring thirty degrees from straight.**\n\nPappu checked each dependent claim carefully — they all referenced only one preceding claim and added new features without modifying existing ones. He made sure not to make any omnibus claims, like \"any other steps\" or \"any material,\" because that would violate the rules set out in **Section 10(5)**.\n\nAs he worked on his patent, Pappu remembered a story from a textbook about someone who had failed because their claim set didn’t have unity of invention. He vowed not to make the same mistake. His paper clip was one invention — no more, no less. He grouped all his claims into a **claim set**, ensuring that they formed a single inventive concept.\n\nAfter weeks of work and revisions, Pappu submitted his complete specification with an abstract and all the required details. The Controller reviewed it and made a small amendment to the abstract for clarity, just as **Section 10(4)(i)** allowed.\n\nWhen the patent was finally granted, Pappu’s paper clip became a hit in offices across Hyderabad. But he never forgot the rules that had guided him — from **Section 10** to the structure of claim sets and dependent claims.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 10(4) | Every complete specification must end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention. | A complete patent application must include clear, specific claims that define what is protected. |\n| Section 10(5) | All claims must relate to a single invention or group of inventions linked by one inventive concept. | Unity of invention requires all claims in a patent to be tied together under one central idea. |\n| Dependent claim 1 (stainless steel with tensile strength 500 MPa) | Specific materials and properties add detail to an independent claim. | A dependent claim enhances the scope of protection by specifying additional features. |\n| Dependent claim 2 (corrosion-resistant coating) | Additional features must be added, not substituted. | A dependent claim should not replace a feature but enhance it with new details. |\n| Dependent claim 3 (thirty-degree arc shape) | Dimensions and design specifics are essential for clarity. | Specific dimensions or shapes can define the invention more precisely in dependent claims. |\n| Claim set | Grouping of independent and dependent claims under one inventive concept. | A claim set must ensure that all claims form a unified group tied to one central idea. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"Claim Drafting II — Dependent Claims, Claim Sets and Categories\"**\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic: DEPENDENT CLAIMS**\n- **D**ependent claims are **E**xplaining the **P**rincipal claim with more **A**dditional details.\n- **N**eed to reference a **T**rademark or **E**arlier claim by number.\n- **E**ach dependent must be **N**arrower and not stand alone.\n- **N**ever introduce new matter.\n- **T**hey are like \"children\" of the main claim.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-Anchors for Sections**\n\n- **Section 6**: *Persons Entitled to Apply* → Think of a **group of people (6)** standing in line, all eligible to apply for a patent.\n  \n- **Section 7(1)**: *One Invention Only* → Picture the number **7** as a **single stick**, representing only one invention per application.\n\n- **Section 8**: *Foreign Applications* → Think of an **octopus (8 arms)**, reaching out to different countries with patent applications.\n\n- **Section 10**: *Contents of Specifications* → Imagine a **ten-story building (10)**, each floor containing a part of the specification.\n\n- **Section 11A**: *Publication of Applications* → Visualize an **elephant (11)** standing on one leg and shouting out its application to everyone.\n\n- **Section 25**: *Opposition to Patent* → Picture a **crowd of 25 people** protesting against the patent grant.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Confusion Pairs**\n\n- **Dependent vs. Independent Claims**  \n  ➤ *Independent claims stand alone and define the invention broadly, while dependent claims rely on an independent claim and add more specifics.*\n\n- **Provisional vs. Complete Specifications**  \n  ➤ *A provisional specification is like a rough draft (not complete), whereas a complete specification provides full details needed for examination.*\n\n- **Claim Sets vs. Claim Categories**  \n  ➤ *Claim sets are groups of related claims, while claim categories classify claims based on their type (e.g., method, product).*\n\n- **Priority Date vs. Filing Date**  \n  ➤ *The priority date is the earliest date that gives precedence to a claim, whereas the filing date is when the application was officially submitted.*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall key concepts in **Claim Drafting II**, using only the sections and rules provided from the **Patents Act, 1970**.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"A dependent claim adds limitations to a preceding claim, which may be independent or dependent. It narrows the scope of the broader claim.","question":"What is a dependent claim?"},{"answer":"Use 'A device for... comprising...' with parts and their connections. This format clearly defines the product's components and their relationships.","question":"How should a product claim be structured?"},{"answer":"They lack specificity and fail to define distinct features. This makes them vague and unsearchable.","question":"Why are omnibus claims objected to?"},{"answer":"It claims a product based on the process used to make it. This focuses on the manufacturing method rather than the final product's structure.","question":"What defines a product-by-process claim?"},{"answer":"Unity of invention requires all claims in a set to be based on a single inventive concept. This ensures coherence and avoids overbroad applications.","question":"What is unity of invention?"},{"answer":"They list multiple alternatives, making claims unsearchable. This violates rules requiring clear, specific limitations.","question":"Why are Markush claims problematic?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 69","Section 10","Section 83","Section 103"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Contents of specifications","Power of High Courts to make rules","Time for putting application in order for grant","General principles applicable to working of patented inventions"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 19H","Rule 80","Rule 10","Rule 24A"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming","Removal of a name from the register of patent agents","Supply of certified copies and certificates under sections","Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10(1) explicitly states that every specification must begin with a title indicating the subject-matter of the invention.","options":["a) A detailed list of claims","b) A title sufficiently indicating the subject-matter of the invention","c) A summary of prior art","d) A table of contents"],"question":"What must every specification, whether provisional or complete, begin with according to Section 10(1) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10(4)(d) states the abstract provides technical information on the invention.","options":["a) It replaces the claims","b) It provides technical information on the invention","c) It is used to determine patentability","d) It is not required for international applications"],"question":"What is the role of the abstract in a complete specification under Section 10(4)(d)?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 10(4)(d)(i) allows the Controller to amend the abstract for better information to third parties.","options":["a) If the applicant requests it","b) If the abstract contains errors","c) To provide better information to third parties","d) If the invention involves biological materials"],"question":"Under what condition may the Controller amend the abstract of a specification?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10(4)(d)(ii)(A) specifies the deposit must be made not later than the date of filing the patent application in India.","options":["a) Within 30 days of filing the patent application","b) Not later than the date of filing the patent application in India","c) Within 6 months of the priority date","d) At the time of grant of the patent"],"question":"What is the deadline for depositing biological material under Section 10(4)(d)(ii)(A)?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10(4)(d)(ii)(C) does not specify that the deposit must be made in India.","options":["a) Access to the material is available after the patent application date","b) The deposit must be made in India","c) If priority is claimed, access is available after the priority date","d) The material must be deposited with an international depository authority"],"question":"Which of the following is NOT a condition for depositing biological material under Section 10(4)(d)(ii)(C)?"}]},"s1p_45":{"notes":"## The structure  \nThe answer must be divided into five sections:  \n1. **Introduction**: Briefly state the invention and its purpose.  \n2. **Claim Analysis**: Break down each claim element by element, referencing the specification.  \n3. **Infringement Analysis**: Compare the accused product to the claim, using a claim chart.  \n4. **Prosecution History Considerations**: Address amendments or disclaimers that narrow the scope of claims.  \n5. **Conclusion**: Summarize whether infringement occurs, citing legal principles (e.g., pith and marrow, doctrine of equivalents).  \n\n---\n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Draft the claim chart** first, aligning each element of the claim with corresponding features of the accused product. Use a table with columns: *Claim Element*, *Accused Product Feature*, *Match?*, *Comments*.  \n2. **Analyze literal infringement** by checking if each claim element is directly replicated in the accused product. Highlight any discrepancies.  \n3. **Assess non-literal infringement** using purposive construction: determine if the accused product performs the same function in the same way to achieve the same result, even if features differ.  \n4. **Review prosecution history** for narrowing amendments or disclaimers (e.g., if a feature was removed during examination, it cannot be reclaimed via equivalents).  \n5. **Conclude** with a reasoned opinion: state whether infringement occurs (literal or under equivalents), and reference relevant case law (e.g., *Raj Parkash v. Mangat Ram*).  \n\n---\n\n## Worked example  \n**Claim 1**:  \n*A device for mincing, said device comprising:  \na) a first elongated member supporting a receptacle, wherein the receptacle comprises an open top, sides, and a perforated bottom;  \nb) a second elongated member supporting a forcing member to force a removable plunger towards the bottom of the receptacle, wherein the removable plunger fits within the receptacle and opens to lie at 180° relative to the receptacle;  \nc) a rotating means to enable the rotation of the plunger within the receptacle;  \nd) a handle attached to a shaft passing through a hole in the forcing member, wherein the handle enables the plunger to rotate of the forcing means.*  \n\n**Claim 2**:  \n*The device for mincing as claimed in claim 1, wherein the device components are formed by molding of suitable plastics materials or casting of suitable metals.*  \n\n**Claim 3**:  \n*The device for mincing as claimed in claim 1, wherein the receptacle is preferably in the shape of a truncated cone with inwardly sloping sides.*  \n\n**Claim 4**:  \n*A method of mincing comprising:  \ni) placing a clove of mincing element in the receptacle;  \nii) squeezing the handle to bring the plunger into the receptacle;  \niii) rotating the plunger while applying pressure;  \niv) allowing juice to pass through the perforated bottom.*  \n\n**Claim 5**:  \n*The method as claimed in claim 4, wherein the clove is placed for crushing and/or mincing.*  \n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes  \n- **Error**: Failing to identify essential elements of the claim (e.g., omitting the \"perforated bottom\" in the receptacle).  \n  **Correction**: Revisit the specification and ensure all claim elements are explicitly mapped.  \n- **Error**: Assuming the doctrine of equivalents applies to disclaimed features (e.g., PRV in the textbook example).  \n  **Correction**: Cite prosecution history estoppel to exclude surrendered territory.  \n- **Error**: Confusing literal infringement with purposive construction (e.g., matching the patented article to the infringing article instead of claim elements).  \n  **Correction**: Apply the pith and marrow rule: focus on the invention’s \"substance\" (as per *TVS Motor v. Bajaj Auto*).  \n\n---\n\n## Practise this  \n**Task**: Draft claims for a *wall tie* (as described in the textbook) in 30 minutes.  \n**Instructions**:  \n- Use the structure: independent claim (covering the shank, material, and function), dependent claims (material specifics, shape, etc.).  \n- Reference the specification’s details (e.g., \"hard-drawn stainless steel,\" \"U-shaped bends for water collection\").  \n**Check against**:  \n- The textbook’s example of the mincing device (ensure claims are clear, specific, and cover all functional aspects).  \n- Legal principles: purposive construction, prosecution history estoppel, and the pith and marrow rule.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 48 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 48. Rights of patentees.\n> Subject to the other provisions contained in this Act and the\n> conditions specified in section 47, a patent granted under this Act\n>\n>  43\n>\n> shall confer upon the patentee\n> (a) where the subject matter of the patent is a product, the\n> exclusive right to prevent third parties, who do not have his\n> consent, from the act of making, using, offering for sale, selling\n> or importing for those purposes that product in India;\n> (b) where the subject matter of the patent is a process, the\n> exclusive right to prevent third parties, who do not have his\n> consent, from the act of using that process, and from the act of\n> using, offering for sale, selling or importing for those purposes\n> the product obtained directly by that process in India.\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n**Rule 16 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 16. Models\n> (1) Models or samples shall be furnished under section 10 only when\n> required by the Controller.\n>\n>  19\n>\n> CHAPTER III INTERNATIONAL APPLICATIONS UNDER PATENT COOPERATION\n> TREATY (PCT)\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the doctrine of equivalents and its relevance in infringement analysis under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** Whether the doctrine of equivalents is applicable in determining patent infringement and how it should be interpreted under the Patents Act.\n\n**Rule:** Under Section 48 of the Patents Act, 1970, a patentee has exclusive rights to prevent third parties from using or importing the patented product or process. The doctrine of equivalents allows for non-literal infringement where an accused product performs substantially the same function in the same way to achieve the same result as the claimed invention (textbook page 84). This is also referred to as purposive construction, which focuses on the \"pith and marrow\" of the claim rather than a literal interpretation. The doctrine was discussed in *Raj Parkash v. Mangat Ram*, where it was held that minor differences in structure or function do not necessarily negate infringement if they are equivalent in effect.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a patentee holds a patent for a device with a \"perforated bottom\" (as seen in the worked example). If an accused product uses a different type of opening, such as slits instead of perforations, but performs the same function—allowing liquid to pass through—the doctrine of equivalents may apply. However, if during the prosecution history, the patentee amended the claim to exclude certain features (such as a specific material or shape), then those features are considered \"surrendered territory\" and cannot be reclaimed via equivalents under *prosecution history estoppel*.\n\n**Conclusion:** The doctrine of equivalents is relevant in infringement analysis when literal infringement does not occur, but the accused product performs substantially the same function. However, it must be applied cautiously, taking into account the prosecution history to avoid overreaching the scope of the patent claims.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to distinguish between literal and non-literal infringement and do not consider the impact of prosecution history on the application of the doctrine of equivalents.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Explain the structure and purpose of a claim chart in patent infringement analysis.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** What is a claim chart, and how does it assist in determining whether an accused product infringes a patent?\n\n**Rule:** A claim chart is a tool used to align each element of a patent claim with corresponding features of the accused product. It typically includes columns such as \"Claim Element,\" \"Accused Product Feature,\" \"Match?,\" and \"Comments.\" This structured approach helps in identifying whether all elements of the claim are present in the accused product, either literally or under the doctrine of equivalents (textbook page 62).\n\n**Application:** Suppose a patent claims a device with a \"perforated bottom\" and a \"rotating plunger.\" If an accused product has a similar structure but uses a different material for the perforations and lacks a rotating mechanism, the claim chart would show that not all elements match. However, if the accused product has a slotted bottom (equivalent to perforations) and a motor-driven plunger (equivalent to manual rotation), then the doctrine of equivalents may apply.\n\n**Conclusion:** A claim chart is essential in patent infringement analysis as it systematically maps each element of the claim against the accused product. It helps determine whether literal or non-literal infringement occurs by identifying discrepancies and equivalences between features.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to construct a detailed claim chart, leading to superficial comparisons that do not fully address all claim elements.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What is the significance of prosecution history in interpreting patent claims? Provide examples.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2017, 2023)*\n\n**Issue:** How does the prosecution history affect the interpretation and scope of a patent claim?\n\n**Rule:** The prosecution history includes any amendments or disclaimers made during the examination process. These can narrow the scope of the claims and may be used to invoke *prosecution history estoppel*—a principle that prevents the patentee from later claiming features that were surrendered during the examination (textbook page 90). This is supported by the pith and marrow rule, which focuses on the essential nature of the invention rather than its literal wording.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a patent applicant originally claimed a \"perforated bottom\" but amended it to \"slotted opening\" during prosecution. If an accused product uses a perforated bottom, the doctrine of equivalents may not apply because that feature was disclaimed in the prosecution history. This principle was emphasized in *TVS Motor v. Bajaj Auto*, where the court held that amendments made during examination are binding on the patentee.\n\n**Conclusion:** The prosecution history is significant in interpreting patent claims as it can limit the scope of the claims through amendments or disclaimers. It also plays a key role in determining whether the doctrine of equivalents applies, especially under *prosecution history estoppel*.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the importance of the prosecution history and fail to apply *prosecution history estoppel* when analyzing non-literal infringement.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What is the difference between literal infringement and infringement under the doctrine of equivalents? Discuss with examples.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** How do literal infringement and infringement under the doctrine of equivalents differ in patent law?\n\n**Rule:** Literal infringement occurs when all elements of a claim are directly replicated in the accused product. In contrast, infringement under the doctrine of equivalents arises when the accused product performs the same function in the same way to achieve the same result, even if features differ (textbook page 85). This is also referred to as purposive construction and is guided by the pith and marrow rule.\n\n**Application:** If a patent claims a \"rotating plunger\" used for mincing, and an accused product uses a motor-driven plunger that performs the same function, this would be considered infringement under the doctrine of equivalents. However, if the accused product has no rotating mechanism at all, it does not infringe literally or under the doctrine.\n\n**Conclusion:** Literal infringement requires exact replication of claim elements, while infringement under the doctrine of equivalents allows for functional equivalence even with structural differences. The latter is a broader form of infringement but must be applied carefully in light of prosecution history.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse literal and non-literal infringement and fail to apply the doctrine of equivalents correctly without considering whether the accused product performs the same function.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Write short notes on: \"Pith and Marrow Rule\" in patent law.**  \n*5 marks · Osmania University · asked 1× (2021)*\n\n**Issue:** What is the pith and marrow rule, and how is it applied in interpreting patent claims?\n\n**Rule:** The pith and marrow rule is a principle of purposive construction that focuses on the essential nature or substance of an invention rather than its literal wording. It allows for flexibility in claim interpretation to ensure that the true intent of the inventor is protected (textbook page 89). This rule was emphasized in *TVS Motor v. Bajaj Auto*.\n\n**Application:** If a patent claims a \"perforated bottom\" but the accused product has slits instead, the pith and marrow rule may be applied to determine whether these features are functionally equivalent. However, if the prosecution history shows that the inventor disclaimed perforations in favor of slits, then the doctrine of equivalents would not apply.\n\n**Conclusion:** The pith and marrow rule is crucial for interpreting patent claims by focusing on the essence of the invention rather than its literal wording. It supports both literal and non-literal infringement analyses but must be used with caution regarding prosecution history.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to explain how the pith and marrow rule interacts with the doctrine of equivalents and the prosecution history.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Claim Interpretation and Infringement Analysis in the Exam\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 48 | **ROOF** | ROOF inspector checks each shingle for hidden flaws. |\n| Sec. 10(4)(c) | **TOES** + clause (4)(c) | TOES align with each claim element when comparing the product. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **43-53** - grant, rights, term of patent\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Reading a claim element by element - which provision?  (picture: ROOF)\n2. Claim charting an accused product against the claim - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 47 | Grants exclusive rights to prevent use/sale/import of invention | \"Blocked door with patent key\"\n- Section 48 | Exclusive right for product/process and derived products | \"Locked factory gate and its output\"\n- Section 10 | Requirements for specifications, including claims and abstracts | \"Blueprint with claim lines\"\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing literal infringement with non-literal (equivalents) without clear distinction\n- Use precise language from the Patent Act, especially Section 47 and 48, to define rights\n- Always structure answers by first defining claim interpretation, then applying it to infringement analysis","storyEN":"### The Story of the Patents in Hyderabad\n\nIn a small but bustling neighborhood of Hyderabad, there was an inventor named Arjun who had created a revolutionary *wall tie* — a construction device used to connect two walls and prevent them from spreading apart under pressure. He believed his invention could change the way buildings were constructed across India.\n\nArjun decided to file for a patent at the Indian Patent Office. The process began with drafting a complete specification, as required by **Section 10(4)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*. This meant that he had to describe his invention in full — from its structure and function to the best method of performing it. He also needed to include a claim or claims defining the scope of his invention and an abstract summarizing the technical details.\n\nArjun’s first claim was broad but clear: **Claim 1** described a wall tie with a shank made of *hard-drawn stainless steel*, U-shaped bends for water collection, and a specific function in construction. He then added several dependent claims to cover variations — such as the exact shape of the shank or the use of different materials.\n\nOne day, while Arjun was working on his patent application, he received news that another inventor from Nalgonda had created a similar wall tie and was planning to sell it across the country. This raised concerns about **infringement**, which is covered under **Section 48** of *The Patents Act, 1970*. That section gives patentees exclusive rights to prevent others from making, using, or selling their patented product without consent.\n\nTo determine if infringement had occurred, Arjun’s lawyer prepared a **claim chart**, as outlined in the notes. Each element of his claim was matched with features of the accused wall tie. For example, the *shank* and its material were compared against the new device's components. The chart helped identify whether each element of the original claim was directly replicated — which would be **literal infringement**.\n\nHowever, things weren't so clear-cut. Arjun’s lawyer also had to consider **non-literal infringement**, where even if features differed slightly, the accused product might still perform the same function in the same way to achieve the same result. This is known as the **doctrine of equivalents** — a principle that allows for flexibility in interpreting claims.\n\nBut there was one catch: during the prosecution of Arjun’s patent application, he had made certain amendments to his original claim. These changes were necessary to overcome objections from the examiner and could have limited the scope of the claim. This meant that **prosecution history estoppel** might apply — a rule that prevents inventors from reclaiming surrendered territory through equivalents.\n\nTo ensure clarity in interpretation, Arjun’s lawyer referred to the **pith and marrow rule**, which focuses on the *substance* of an invention rather than its exact wording. This was especially important because if the accused wall tie had the same core function — even with minor differences — it could still be considered infringing.\n\nAs things unfolded, Arjun's case reminded everyone in Hyderabad that **claim interpretation** is not just about words on paper but about understanding the true *purpose* and *function* of an invention. It also emphasized the importance of being precise when drafting claims and being mindful of any amendments made during prosecution.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| **Section 10(4)** | Every complete specification must describe the invention fully, include a claim, and an abstract. | A patent application must contain all necessary details to define the scope of the invention clearly. |\n| **Section 48** | Gives the patentee exclusive rights to prevent others from using or selling the patented product without consent. | The patent holder has legal monopoly over their invention within India. |\n| **Claim chart** | A table used to compare each element of a claim with features in an accused product. | This is essential for analyzing whether infringement occurs literally or through equivalents. |\n| **Doctrine of equivalents** | Allows for non-literal infringement if the accused product performs the same function, in the same way, to achieve the same result. | Even minor differences can still lead to infringement if the core functionality matches. |\n| **Prosecution history estoppel** | Prevents inventors from reclaiming features that were surrendered during amendment or disclaimer. | Changes made during patent examination narrow the scope of claims permanently. |\n| **Pith and marrow rule** | Focuses on the substance or essence of an invention, not just its wording. | This principle helps determine whether a product is infringing based on its true purpose. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for “Claim Interpretation and Infringement Analysis in the Exam” (Patent Agent Examination – Papers I, II and Viva)**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronyms/Mnemonics**\n\n- **LITERAL vs. EQUIVALENTS**:  \n   Use **\"LEE\"** for Literal Enforcement of Exact wording.  \n   Use **\"E.E.\"** for Equivalents' Expansion.\n\n- **CLAIM CONSTRUCTION**:  \n   Remember **“C.C.” = “Crafting Claims”**, as in crafting a claim is central to interpretation and infringement analysis.\n\n- **INFRINGEMENT TYPES**:  \n   Use **“L & N”** for Literal and Non-literal (Non-literal being based on equivalents).\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors**\n\n- **Section 84**: Picture a **\"compulsory licence\"** being issued by the Controller, like a **\"key\"** unlocking access to a patented invention.\n\n- **Section 34**: Recall that this section says **“no anticipation”** if circumstances are only as described in other sections. Use the image of a **“stop sign”** (like a traffic light) saying “No Go” for anticipation.\n\n- **Section 107A**: Think of **\"acts not being infringement\"**, like a **\"shield\"** protecting certain acts from being considered infringement.\n\n- **Section 82**: Visualize the term **“patented articles”** as a **\"box\"** containing items that are covered by a patent.\n\n- **Article 104A**: Imagine **\"burden of proof\"** in court, like a **\"weight\"** being carried on one’s shoulders during litigation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **Literal Infringement vs. Non-literal (Equivalents) Infringement**  \n   *Tell them apart*: Literal infringement requires an **exact match**, while non-literal infringement allows for **functional similarity** even if wording differs.\n\n- **Claim Construction vs. Specification Drafting**  \n   *Tell them apart*: Claim construction is about the **legal meaning of claims**, whereas specification drafting is about the **technical description** of the invention.\n\n- **Patent of Addition vs. Main Patent**  \n   *Tell them apart*: A patent of addition is a **supplemental protection** for improvements, while a main patent covers the **core invention**.\n\n- **Compulsory Licence vs. Voluntary Licence**  \n   *Tell them apart*: Compulsory licence is issued by the Controller **without consent**, whereas voluntary licence requires **mutual agreement** between parties.\n\n- **Infringement vs. Non-infringement Declaration**  \n   *Tell them apart*: Infringement involves **unauthorized use**, while a non-infringement declaration is a **court ruling** that the use is lawful.\n\n---\n\nThese techniques are designed to help you **anchor** key points in your memory, **avoid confusion**, and **recall** important legal concepts quickly during the Patent Agent Examination.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The pith and marrow rule focuses on the essential elements of the claim, not the detailed specifications. It matches the core invention's substance with the accused product to determine infringement.","question":"What is the pith and marrow rule of claim construction?"},{"answer":"Claim charting aligns each element of the patent claim with corresponding features in the accused product, ensuring all essential elements are met for literal infringement.","question":"How is claim charting performed during infringement analysis?"},{"answer":"Prosecution history estoppel bars claiming surrendered territory, as seen in CTR's case where disclaiming PRV in amendments limited later equivalent claims.","question":"Why does prosecution history estoppel prevent invoking the doctrine of equivalents?"},{"answer":"Purposive construction extends claim scope beyond literal language to capture the invention's substance, as Indian courts apply in non-literal infringement cases.","question":"What is purposive construction in claim interpretation?"},{"answer":"A patent applicant must detail all possible examples and features in the specification to avoid ambiguous claim interpretation and ensure robust protection.","question":"What should a patent applicant ensure when drafting claims?"},{"answer":"Non-literal infringement is assessed via the doctrine of equivalents, where the accused product's features perform the same function in the same way, even if not identical.","question":"How is non-literal infringement determined?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 10","Section 56","Section 66","Section 16"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["No anticipation if circumstances are only as described in","Rules to be placed before Parliament","Consequences of secrecy directions","Contents of specifications"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 48 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Rights of patentees”.","options":["Section 136","Section 157A","Section 48","Section 30"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Rights of patentees?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 48 is headed “Rights of patentees”.","options":["Secrecy directions to be periodically reviewed","Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Rights of patentees","Rights of co-owners of patents"],"question":"Section 48 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 96","Rule 10","Rule 24B","Rule 66"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Form of making a claim under section 28(3)","Appropriate office","Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)","Further evidence to be left with the leave of the"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"}]},"s1p_46":{"notes":"## The structure  \nA written reply to a First Examination Report (FER) addressing novelty and inventive step objections must include:  \n1. **Claim amendments** (e.g., narrowing the scope of claims to distinguish from prior art using specific numerical ranges or technical effects).  \n2. **Distinguishing arguments** (e.g., explaining why the prior art does not anticipate or render obvious the claimed invention, citing technical differences like unexpected results or synergistic effects).  \n3. **Technical advance explanation** (e.g., detailing how the invention overcomes prior art limitations, such as improved efficacy or novel application).  \n4. **Response to Section 3(d) objections** (if applicable, e.g., arguing that the invention is not a mere derivative of known compounds but offers a new therapeutic effect).  \n\n---\n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Read the FER carefully** to identify the specific objections (e.g., novelty, inventive step, Section 3(d)).  \n2. **Analyze the cited prior art** (e.g., D1, D2) to map their features against the claims. Highlight where the prior art fails to disclose or enable the claimed invention.  \n3. **Amend the claims** (e.g., using a Jepson-style claim to narrow the scope, such as specifying a 25–30 wt% range of component A).  \n4. **Argue inventive step** by explaining the non-obviousness of the claimed invention (e.g., the prior art does not teach the synergistic effect of A and B in that ratio).  \n5. **Address Section 3(d)** by demonstrating that the invention is not a mere derivative but provides a new therapeutic benefit (e.g., improved flowering rate in the pesticide example).  \n\n---\n\n## Worked example  \n**Response to FER:**  \n**Claim Amendment to Overcome Novelty Issue:**  \n1. The pesticide composition of A and B, characterized in that the said composition comprises **25–30 wt% A** and **75–70 wt% B**, wherein the composition mitigates earworm infestation in tomato fields and improves flowering rate.  \n\n**Inventive Step Argument:**  \nThe prior art (e.g., D1) discloses a broad range of A and B (e.g., 5–55 wt% A). However, the present invention’s specific ratio (25–30 wt% A) achieves a synergistic effect not disclosed in D1, including:  \n- **Unexpected result:** A 30% increase in flowering rate compared to D1’s composition.  \n- **Technical advance:** Overcoming the prior art’s failure to control earworm infestation effectively.  \n\n**Section 3(d) Response:**  \nThe claimed compounds are not mere derivatives of known compounds (e.g., D1’s compounds lack anti-HCV activity). The present invention’s specific ratio and application (pesticide for tomato fields) are novel and non-obvious.  \n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes  \n**Error 1:** Failing to narrow claims sufficiently (e.g., leaving the claim as “A pesticide composition comprising A and B”).  \n**Correction:** Use specific numerical ranges or technical features (e.g., “25–30 wt% A and 75–70 wt% B”).  \n\n**Error 2:** Not linking the inventive step to technical effects (e.g., stating “the invention is better” without evidence).  \n**Correction:** Provide data (e.g., “30% increase in flowering rate” or “synergistic pest control”).  \n\n**Error 3:** Overlooking Section 3(d) objections in the response.  \n**Correction:** Explicitly address whether the invention is a mere derivative or offers a new therapeutic effect.  \n\n---\n\n## Practise this  \n**Task:** Draft a response to a FER objection for a patent application on a new type of solar panel with a 20% efficiency improvement.  \n**Time:** 30 minutes.  \n**Check against:**  \n- Amended claims with specific numerical ranges (e.g., “a photovoltaic layer comprising 15–20% graphene”).  \n- Distinguishing arguments (e.g., prior art does not teach the graphene layer’s role in efficiency).  \n- Technical advance (e.g., “unexpected 20% efficiency increase” with experimental data).  \n- Section 3(d) response (if applicable, e.g., “not a derivative of known solar panels”).\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 13 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 13. Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior\n> claim.\n> (1) The examiner to whom an application for a patent is referred\n> under section 12 shall make investigation for the purpose of\n> ascertaining whether the invention so far as claimed in any claim of\n> the complete specification\n> (a) has been anticipated by publication before the date of filing of\n> the applicant’s complete specification in any specification filed in\n> pursuance of an application for a patent made in India and dated on\n> or after the 1st day of January, 1912;\n> (b) is claimed in any claim of any other complete specification\n> published on or after the date of filing of the applicant’s complete\n> specification, being a specification filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent made in India and dated before or claiming\n> the priority date earlier than that date.\n>\n> (2) The examiner shall, in addition, make such investigation for the\n> purpose of ascertaining whether the invention, so far as claimed in\n> any claim of the complete specification, has been anticipated by\n> publication in India or elsewhere in any document other than those\n>\n>  22\n>\n> mentioned in sub-section (1) before the date of filing of the\n> applicant’s complete specification.\n>\n> (3) Where a complete specification is amended under the provisions\n> of this Act before the grant of a patent, the amended specification\n> shall be examined and investigated in like manner as the original\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) The examination and investigations required under section 12 and\n> this section shall not be deemed in any way to warrant the validity\n> of any patent, and no liability shall be incurred by the Central\n> Government or any officer thereof by reason of, or in connection\n> with, any such examination or investigation or any report or other\n> proceedings consequent thereon.\n\n**Section 2 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 2. Definitions and interpretation.\n> (1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal\n> representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee\n> of any person include references to the assignee of the legal\n> representative or assignee of that person;\n> (aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the\n> International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the\n> purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April,\n> 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;\n> (ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an\n> invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used\n> in an industry;\n> (b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs\n> and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;\n> (c) “convention application” means an application for a patent made\n> by virtue of section 135;\n> (d) “convention country\" means a country or a country which is\n> member of a group of countries or a union of countries or an Inter-\n> governmental organisation referred to as a convention country in\n> section 133;\n> (e) “district court” has the meaning assigned to that expression by\n> the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908);\n> (f) “exclusive licence” means a licence from a patentee which\n> confers on the licensee, or on the licensee and persons authorised\n> by him, to the exclusion of all other persons (including the\n>\n>  8\n>\n> patentee), any right in respect of the patented invention, and\n> “exclusive licensee” shall be construed accordingly;\n> (h) “Government undertaking” means any industrial undertaking\n> carried on\n> (i) by a department of the Government, or\n> (ii) by a corporation established by a Central, Provincial or State\n> Act, which is owned or controlled by the Government, or\n> (iii) by a Government company as defined in section 617 of the\n> Companies Act, 1956 (1 of\n> 1956), or\n> 1(iv) by an institution wholly or substantially financed by the\n> Government;\n> (i) “High Court\", in relation to a State or Union territory, means\n> the High Court having territorial jurisdiction in that State or\n> Union territory, as the case may be;\n> (ia) “international application” means an application for patent\n> made in accordance with the Patent Cooperation Treaty;\n> (j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an\n> inventive step and capable of industrial application;\n> (ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves\n> technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having\n> economic significance or both and that makes the invention not\n> obvious to a person skilled in the art;\n> (k) “legal representative” means a person who in law represents the\n> estate of a deceased person;\n> (l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not\n> been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the\n> country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of\n> patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject\n> matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part\n> of the state of the art;\n> (la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under\n> sub-section (3) of section 25;\n> (m) “patent” means a patent for any invention granted under this\n> Act;\n> (n) “patent agent” means a person for the time being registered\n> under this Act as a patent agent;\n> (o) “patented article” and “patented process” mean respectively an\n> article or process in respect of which a patent is in force;\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 15 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 15. Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,\n> etc., in certain cases.\n> Where the Controller is satisfied that the application or any\n> specification or any other document filed in pursuance thereof does\n> not comply with the requirements of this Act or of any rules made\n> thereunder, the Controller may refuse the application or may require\n> the application, specification or the other documents, as the case\n> may be, to be amended to his satisfaction before he proceeds with\n> the application and refuse the application on failure to do so.\n\n**Section 59 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 59. Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or\n> specification.\n> (1) No amendment of an application for a patent or a complete\n> specification or any document relating thereto shall be made except\n> by way of disclaimer, correction or Explanation, and no amendment\n> thereof shall be allowed, except for the purpose of incorporation of\n> actual fact, and no amendment of a complete specification shall be\n> allowed, the effect of which would be that the specification as\n> amended would claim or describe matter not in substance disclosed or\n> shown in the specification before the amendment, or that any claim\n> of the specification as amended would not fall wholly within the\n> scope of a claim of the specification before the amendment.\n>\n> (2) Where after the date of grant of patent any amendment of the\n> specification or any other documents related thereto is allowed by\n> the Controller or by the High Court, as the case may be,\n> (a) the amendment shall for all purposes be deemed to form part of\n>\n>  51\n>\n> the specification along with other documents related thereto;\n> (b) the fact that the specification or any other documents related\n> thereto has been amended shall be published as expeditiously as\n> possible; and\n> (c) the right of the applicant or patentee to make amendment shall\n> not be called in question except on the ground of fraud.\n>\n> (3) In construing the specification as amended, reference may be\n> made to the specification as originally accepted.\n>\n>  52\n>\n> CHAPTER XI RESTORATION OF LAPSED PATENTS\n\n**Rule 29 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 29. Procedure in case of anticipation by prior claiming\n> (1) When it is found that the invention as far as claimed in any\n>\n>  38\n>\n> claim of the complete specification, is claimed in any claim of any\n> other specification falling within clause (b) of sub-section (1) of\n> section 13, the applicant shall be so informed and shall be afforded\n> an opportunity to amend his specification.\n>\n> (2) If the applicant’s specification is otherwise in order for grant\n> and an objection under clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 13\n> is outstanding, the Controller may postpone the grant of patent and\n> allow a period of two months for removing the objection.\n\n**Rule 28 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 28. Procedure in case of anticipation by prior publication\n> (1) If the Controller is satisfied after investigation under section\n> 13 that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the complete\n> specification has been published in any specification or other\n> document referred to in clause (a) of sub-section (1) or sub-section\n> (2) of the said section, the Controller shall communicate the gist\n> of specific objections and the basis thereof to the applicant and\n> the applicant shall be afforded an opportunity to amend his\n> specification.\n>\n> (2) If the applicant contests any of the objections communicated to\n> him by the Controller under sub-rule (1), or if he refiles his\n> specification along with his observations as to whether or not the\n> specification is to be amended, he shall be given an opportunity to\n> be heard on the matter if he so requests:\n> Provided that such a request shall be made on a date earlier than\n> ten days of the final date of the period preferred to under sub-\n> section (1) of section 21:\n> Provided further that a request for hearing may be allowed to be\n> filed within such a shorter period as the Controller may deem fit in\n> the circumstances of the case.\n>\n>  37\n>\n> (3) If the applicant requests for a hearing under sub-rule (2)\n> within a period of one month from the date of communication of the\n> gist of objections, or, the Controller, considers it desirable to do\n> so, whether or not the applicant has refiled his application, he\n> shall forthwith fix a date and time for hearing having regard to the\n> period remaining for putting the application in order or to the\n> other circumstances of the case.\n>\n> (4) The applicant shall be given ten days’ notice of any such\n> hearing or such shorter notice as appears to the Controller to be\n> reasonable in the circumstances of the case and the applicant shall,\n> as soon as possible, notify the Controller whether he will attend\n> the hearing.\n>\n> (5) After hearing the applicant, or without hearing if the applicant\n> has not attended or has notified that he does not desire to be\n> heard, the Controller may specify or permit such amendment of the\n> specification as he thinks fit to be made and may refuse to grant\n> the patent unless the amendment so specified or permitted is made\n> within such period as may be fixed.\n>\n> (6) The hearing may also be held through video-conferencing or\n> audio-visual communication devices: Provided that such hearing shall\n> be deemed to have taken place at the appropriate office.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purposes of this rule, the expression\n> communication device shall have the same meaning as assigned to it\n> in clause (ha) of sub-section (1) of section 2 of the Information\n> Technology Act, 2000 (21 of 2000).\n>\n> (7) In all cases of hearing, written submissions and the relevant\n> documents, if any, shall be filed within fifteen days from the date\n> of hearing.\n\n**Rule 31 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 31. Form of reference to another specification\n> When in pursuance of rule 30, the Controller directs that a\n> reference to another specification shall be inserted in the\n> applicant’s complete specification, such reference shall be inserted\n> after the claims and shall be in the following form, namely:\n> \"Reference has been directed, in pursuance of section 18(2) of the\n> Patents Act, 1970, to the specification filed in pursuance of\n> application No......\"\n>\n>  39\n>\n> Rule 32. Procedure in case of potential infringement\n> If in consequence of an investigation made under section 13, it\n> appears to the Controller that the applicant’s invention cannot be\n> performed without substantial risk of infringement of a claim of\n> another patent, the applicant shall be so informed and the procedure\n> provided in rule 29 shall, as far as may be necessary, be\n> applicable.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the procedure for responding to a First Examination Report (FER) when novelty and inventive step objections are raised under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue** — What is the proper procedure for responding to a First Examination Report when objections to novelty and inventive step are raised?\n\n**Rule** — Under **Section 13** of the Patents Act, 1970, the examiner must investigate whether an invention has been anticipated by prior art or claimed in another specification. Additionally, **Section 59** allows for amendments to a patent application only through disclaimers, corrections, or explanations and not to claim new matter. For novelty, the applicant must demonstrate that the invention is \"new\" under **Section 2(l)** of the Patents Act, meaning it has not been anticipated by prior publication or use. Inventive step, as defined in **Section 2(ja)**, requires a feature that involves technical advance or economic significance and is non-obvious to a person skilled in the art.\n\nA response to an FER should include **claim amendments** (e.g., narrowing numerical ranges), **distinguishing arguments**, **technical advances**, and addressing **Section 3(d)** if applicable. This aligns with the structure outlined in the verified notes, which emphasize that responses must narrow claims, highlight technical differences from prior art, and demonstrate non-obviousness through unexpected results or synergistic effects.\n\n**Application** — When an FER raises objections to novelty and inventive step based on prior art (e.g., D1, D2), the applicant should first **analyze the cited documents**, identify where they fail to disclose the invention’s features. For example, if a claim is broad and includes a range of component A from 5–55 wt%, the applicant may amend it to specify a narrower range like 25–30 wt% that achieves unexpected results not found in prior art.\n\nIf the objection pertains to **Section 3(d)**, which prohibits granting patents for mere derivatives of known substances unless they offer new therapeutic effects, the applicant must argue that their invention is not merely a derivative but provides a unique application or effect. For instance, if the claimed compound improves flowering rate or has anti-HCV activity not present in prior art, this can be highlighted as evidence of novelty and inventive step.\n\n**Conclusion** — A response to an FER under the Patents Act must include claim amendments that narrow scope, distinguishing arguments based on technical differences from prior art, a clear explanation of the invention’s technical advance, and if applicable, a response to **Section 3(d)** objections by emphasizing new therapeutic or functional benefits.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to properly link inventive step arguments with specific experimental data or unexpected results. A strong FER response must not only argue that prior art is different but also provide evidence of non-obviousness and technical advancement.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the key components of a claim amendment in response to novelty objections raised under Section 13 of the Patents Act, 1970?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue** — What are the essential elements that must be included in a claim amendment to address novelty objections?\n\n**Rule** — Under **Section 13**, an invention is considered novel if it has not been anticipated by prior art. Claim amendments must focus on narrowing the scope of claims so as to distinguish from prior art, using specific numerical ranges or technical features. As per verified notes, **claim amendments** should include specifics like “25–30 wt% A” rather than leaving the claim broad.\n\nThe Patents Act allows for **amendments by way of disclaimer or correction**, but not to introduce new matter under **Section 59(1)**. Therefore, any amendment must be based on features already disclosed in the original specification and not extend beyond it.\n\n**Application** — For example, if a prior art document (D1) discloses a broad range for component A from 5–55 wt%, an applicant may amend the claim to specify a narrower range such as “25–30 wt% A” that is not disclosed in D1. This amendment must be supported by technical arguments, such as evidence of unexpected results or synergistic effects achieved within this specific range.\n\n**Conclusion** — A successful claim amendment must narrow the scope using precise numerical ranges or technical features already disclosed, ensuring it distinguishes from prior art and meets the requirements under **Section 59(1)** for valid amendments.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the requirement that amendments cannot introduce new matter. It is crucial to ensure that any narrowing of claims is based on information originally disclosed in the specification.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Explain the difference between novelty and inventive step, and how they are addressed in a response to an FER.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2017, 2023)*\n\n**Issue** — What is the distinction between novelty and inventive step under the Patents Act, 1970, and how are they addressed in a response to an FER?\n\n**Rule** — **Novelty**, as defined in **Section 2(l)**, requires that the invention has not been anticipated by prior publication or use. On the other hand, **inventive step**, under **Section 2(ja)**, refers to a feature of the invention involving technical advance or economic significance and is non-obvious to a person skilled in the art.\n\nIn response to an FER, novelty objections are addressed through **claim amendments** that distinguish from prior art. Inventive step objections require arguments showing that the claimed invention is not obvious based on prior art, often using evidence of unexpected results or synergistic effects.\n\n**Application** — For example, if a prior art document (D1) discloses a broad range for component A, novelty can be preserved by narrowing the claim to a specific sub-range. To address inventive step, it must be argued that the invention’s specific ratio achieves an effect not found in D1, such as a 30% increase in flowering rate or synergistic pest control.\n\n**Conclusion** — While **novelty** is about whether the invention has been previously disclosed, **inventive step** focuses on whether it would have been obvious to a person skilled in the art. In an FER response, both are addressed by amending claims and providing technical arguments that distinguish from prior art and demonstrate non-obviousness.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often confuse novelty with inventive step. It is important to note that even if an invention is novel, it may still fail the test for inventive step unless it demonstrates a non-obvious technical advance or economic benefit.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. How should an applicant respond to objections under Section 3(d) of the Patents Act when responding to an FER?**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2022)*\n\n**Issue** — What is the correct approach for addressing objections raised under **Section 3(d)** in a response to an FER?\n\n**Rule** — **Section 3(d)** prohibits granting patents for mere derivatives of known substances unless they offer new therapeutic effects. In response, the applicant must argue that the invention is not merely a derivative but provides a novel and non-obvious application or effect.\n\nThis can be done by highlighting data such as improved efficacy, unexpected results, or synergistic interactions not found in prior art. As per verified notes, the response should emphasize that the invention offers a new therapeutic benefit or application, rather than being a mere modification of known compounds.\n\n**Application** — For instance, if an applicant is claiming a pesticide composition with components A and B, they must argue that their specific ratio leads to improved flowering rates in tomato fields compared to prior art. This demonstrates that the invention provides a new effect beyond what was previously known.\n\n**Conclusion** — In response to **Section 3(d)** objections, applicants should clearly demonstrate that their invention is not merely a derivative but offers a novel and non-obvious therapeutic or functional benefit.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to address **Section 3(d)** in their FER responses, even when it is applicable. It is crucial to explicitly argue that the claimed invention provides new effects rather than being a mere modification of known substances.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Responding to a First Examination Report — Novelty and Inventive Step Objections\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 13 | **TOMB** | TOMB stones etched with prior art claims. |\n| Sec. 2(1)(ja) | **HEN** + clause (1)(ja) | _make your own picture for HEN_ |\n| Sec. 15 | **TOWEL** | TOWEL absorbing excess claim scope. |\n| Sec. 59 | **LIP** | LIP curling at unexpected technical advance. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **2** - definitions\n- **12-24** - examination, objections, time for putting in order\n- **57-59** - amendment of application and specification\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Reading the cited prior art and mapping it to the claims - which provision?  (picture: TOMB)\n2. Distinguishing D1 on a claimed feature - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n3. Amending claims without widening scope - which provision?  (picture: TOWEL)\n4. Technical advance and unexpected results argument - which provision?  (picture: LIP)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 2(1)(j) | Defines invention as new and involving inventive step | New idea with technical twist  \n- Section 14 | Provides for examination of patent applications | Examiner checks the application  \n- Rule 24B(3) | Controller must forward FER within one month | Report sent to applicant quickly  \n- Rule 24C | Deals with expedited examination procedures | Faster process if requested  \n- Section 12(2) | Timeframe for examiner’s report submission | Examiner has limited time  \n- Rule 24C(8) | Controller issues objections within 15 days | Objections given promptly  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Don’t forget to mention the timeline for responding to FER  \n- Use clear language when explaining novelty and inventive step  \n- Always reference the relevant sections and rules in your answers","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, in a bustling neighborhood where the scent of biryani mingles with the sounds of rickshaws and honking cars, lived Ravi, a young patent agent who had recently passed his qualifying exams. His new office was on the third floor of an old building that once housed a textile mill. The walls were lined with shelves filled with books, blueprints, and case files — each one representing a challenge he had to solve for clients.\n\nOne morning, Ravi received a letter from the Patent Office. It was a **First Examination Report (FER)** on a patent application submitted by his client, a startup that had developed a new type of solar panel with a 20% efficiency improvement over existing models. The FER raised objections under **Section 13** and **Section 59**, questioning the novelty and inventive step of the invention.\n\nRavi sat at his desk, surrounded by twenty-one lamps — each one symbolizing a different article or section in the Patents Act that he had studied for this very moment. He opened the FER carefully, reading it like a map to find hidden treasure. The objections were clear: **Section 13** said the invention was not novel because prior art (labeled as D1 and D2) had already disclosed similar solar panels. **Section 59** raised concerns about whether the claims could be amended without going beyond what was originally disclosed.\n\nRavi knew from his training that he needed to address each objection with precision. He started by reading through the FER once more, mapping out how the prior art compared to the invention. He noted that D1 described a solar panel using graphene but not in the specific concentration used by Ravi’s client — **15–20%** of the photovoltaic layer.\n\nHe then turned his attention to **Section 15**, which gave him the power to require amendments if the application didn’t meet the requirements. He realized that he needed to narrow the claims to distinguish from D1 and D2, just like in the worked example from his notes — a process known as **claim amendment**.\n\nHe drafted an amended claim: “A photovoltaic layer comprising 15–20% graphene.” This was not just a number; it was a Jepson-style claim that narrowed the scope of the invention and made it distinct from prior art. He also prepared arguments for **inventive step**, explaining how the specific concentration of graphene in his client’s panel led to an **unexpected 20% efficiency increase** — a result not mentioned in D1 or D2.\n\nBut Ravi wasn’t done yet. The FER had also raised concerns under **Section 3(d)**, which deals with whether the invention was merely a derivative of known compounds. Ravi knew that this required him to argue that the new application was not just a tweak but an entirely novel and non-obvious improvement.\n\nHe recalled the example from his notes where they had addressed Section 3(d) by showing that the composition offered **new therapeutic effects** — in this case, a significant increase in efficiency. He made sure to highlight how the invention overcame prior art limitations, such as the inefficiency of existing solar panels, and provided a **technical advance**.\n\nFinally, he reviewed everything one more time, ensuring that his response included all four elements: claim amendments, distinguishing arguments, technical advance explanation, and a clear address of Section 3(d) objections. He was ready to submit it — not just as a response, but as a defense of the innovation itself.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Sections and articles from the Patents Act | Every number in the notes must be integrated into the story as a meaningful detail, not just mentioned. |\n| Section 13 | Search for anticipation by previous publication and prior claim | The examiner must investigate whether the invention has been anticipated by prior art or publications before filing. |\n| Section 15 | Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications | If an application does not meet legal requirements, it may be refused unless amendments are made to satisfy the Controller. |\n| Section 59 | Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or specification | Amendments can only be made by way of disclaimer, correction, or explanation and must not introduce new matter not originally disclosed. |\n| Section 3(d) | Provisions regarding non-obviousness for pharmaceutical inventions | The invention cannot be a mere derivative but must offer a new therapeutic effect or benefit. |\n| 15–20% graphene | Specific numerical range in claim amendment | Narrowing claims using specific ranges helps distinguish from prior art and supports inventive step arguments. |\n| Unexpected 20% efficiency increase | Technical advance explanation | Providing evidence of unexpected results strengthens the argument that the invention is non-obvious and offers a real improvement over existing technology. |","memory":"### Memory Techniques for **Responding to a First Examination Report — Novelty and Inventive Step Objections**\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Acronym/Mnemonic: NOVINT**  \n- **N**ovelty – Check if the invention is new (not disclosed before).  \n- **O**ld Art – Compare with prior art documents.  \n- **V**erification – Ensure all claims are supported by the specification.  \n- **I**nventive Step – Demonstrate non-obviousness over prior art.  \n- **N**ot Obvious – Show that the invention is not an obvious improvement.  \n- **T**imeliness – Respond within the allowed period (e.g., 6 months under Rule 24B(5)).\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Number-Anchors for Sections:**\n\n- **Rule 24B(3)** → *A*pplication and specification are sent to applicant within one month.  \n  *(Visualize a calendar with the number \"24\" and an arrow pointing to a \"3\" on a form being mailed.)*\n\n- **Rule 24B(5)** → *6* months for putting application in order for grant.  \n  *(Imagine a clock showing six o’clock, with the word “grant” written on it.)*\n\n- **Section 12** → *E*xaminer’s report is issued within one month (but not exceeding two).  \n  *(Picture a \"12\" on an hourglass, with sand flowing from top to bottom in one month.)*\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Novelty vs. Inventive Step**  \n  *Novelty is about whether the invention was already known; inventive step is about whether it would have been obvious to a person skilled in the art.*\n\n- **Prior Art vs. Prior Publication**  \n  *Prior art includes all information that could affect the patentability of an invention, while prior publication refers specifically to documents published before the filing date.*\n\n- **First Statement of Objections vs. Reply to Objections**  \n  *The first statement is issued by the Controller; the reply is submitted by the applicant in response.*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Amend claims using Jepson-style amendments to add specific features. This narrows the scope while maintaining novelty, as shown in the pesticide composition example.","question":"How should claims be amended to overcome novelty objections without widening scope?"},{"answer":"Include amended claims and arguments addressing novelty, inventive step, and technical advances. The model answer shows this with claim amendments and synergistic effect explanations.","question":"What is the structure of a written reply to a First Examination Report?"},{"answer":"It highlights that prior art lacks teaching for the specific substitution pattern in claims, proving non-obviousness as the Controller noted in the Gilead case.","question":"Why is distinguishing D1 on a claimed feature critical for inventive step?"},{"answer":"Explain that the specific ratio of components achieves unexpected results, like enhanced flowering, which prior art does not disclose or suggest.","question":"How to argue inventive step based on synergistic effects?"},{"answer":"Demonstrating the invention overcomes prior art disadvantages through specific improvements, such as higher efficacy or novel properties, as in the pesticide example.","question":"What constitutes a technical advance argument in FER responses?"},{"answer":"Prior art must not be interpreted with the invention's knowledge; the Controller emphasized this by stating arbitrary selection without hindsight was impossible.","question":"Why is hindsight unacceptable in novelty assessments?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 15 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,”.","options":["Section 15","Section 51","Section 21","Section 68"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 15 is headed “Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,”.","options":["Register of patents and particulars to be entered therein","Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Amendment of patent granted to deceased applicant","Time for putting application in order for grant"],"question":"Section 15 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 59 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Filing of reply evidence by opponent”.","options":["Rule 74","Rule 87","Rule 104","Rule 59"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Filing of reply evidence by opponent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 59 is headed “Filing of reply evidence by opponent”.","options":["Request under section 52(2)","Manner of publication of the revocation order","Scale of costs","Filing of reply evidence by opponent"],"question":"Rule 59 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Section 72","Section 110","Section 83","Section 2"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Definitions and interpretation?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 2 is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Provisional and complete specifications","Definitions and interpretation","Date of patent","Reliefs in suits for infringement"],"question":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 13 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior”.","options":["Section 141","Section 129","Section 71","Section 13"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 13 is headed “Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior”.","options":["Right of exclusive licensee to take proceedings against","Time for putting application in order for grant","Transmission of orders of courts to Controller","Search for anticipation by previous publication and by prior"],"question":"Section 13 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 13 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Specifications”.","options":["Rule 13","Rule 4","Rule 121","Rule 25"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Specifications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 13 is headed “Specifications”.","options":["Presentation of assignment, etc., of patent to Controller","Specifications","Manner of application under section 51(2)","Surrender of Patents"],"question":"Rule 13 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 59 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or”.","options":["Section 35","Section 3","Section 145","Section 59"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 59 is headed “Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or”.","options":["Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or","Compulsory licences","Savings in respect of other persons authorised to act as","Consideration of report of examiner by Controller"],"question":"Section 59 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 13(1)(b) specifies that the examiner must check claims in other specifications published after the applicant's filing date, but (a) refers to publications in India on or after 1st January 1912, not before. Option 2 incorrectly states 'before 1st January 1912', which is not required.","options":["Anticipation by publication in India or elsewhere before the filing date","Anticipation by prior claims in specifications filed in India before 1st January 1912","Anticipation by other complete specifications published after the applicant's filing date","Anticipation by publications in specifications filed in India on or after 1st January 1912"],"question":"Under Section 13(1) of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is NOT a requirement for the examiner to investigate?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 2(ja) defines 'inventive step' as a feature involving technical advance, economic significance, or both, making it non-obvious. Option 1 contradicts this definition.","options":["A feature that is obvious to a person skilled in the art","A feature with technical advance or economic significance, or both","A feature that is not capable of industrial application","A feature that has been previously published in any document"],"question":"According to Section 2(ja) of the Patents Act, 1970, what constitutes an 'inventive step'?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 15 explicitly states the Controller may refuse the application or require amendments. Options 2-4 are not mentioned in the text.","options":["Refuse the application or require amendments","Grant the patent immediately","Publish the application without examination","Transfer the application to a court"],"question":"Under Section 15 of the Patents Act, 1970, what power does the Controller have if an application does not comply with the Act?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 59(1) allows amendments by 'disclaimer, correction or Explanation', but not adding new matter. Options 0, 2, and 3 violate this rule.","options":["Adding new claims not disclosed in the original specification","Correction of errors in the specification","Expanding the scope of claims beyond the original disclosure","Introducing new technical features not previously described"],"question":"Which of the following is a permissible type of amendment under Section 59(1) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 59(2)(b) mandates expedited publication of amendments. Other options are not mentioned in the text.","options":["The amendment must be kept confidential for 12 months","The fact of amendment must be published as expeditiously as possible","The patent must be revoked","The applicant must pay additional fees"],"question":"According to Section 59(2)(b) of the Patents Act, 1970, what must happen after an amendment is allowed post-grant?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 13(4) explicitly states that examination does not warrant patent validity. Options 0 and 3 contradict this, while option 1 is not mentioned.","options":["It guarantees the validity of the patent","It may be used as evidence in court","It does not warrant the validity of the patent","It creates liability for the Central Government"],"question":"Under Section 13(4) of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the legal effect of the examination conducted by the examiner?"}]},"s1p_47":{"notes":"## The structure  \nThe answer must address each objection type (3(d), 3(i), 3(j), 3(k)) with distinct sections. Each section should include:  \n1. **Objection clause**: Reference to the relevant section (e.g., 3(d)).  \n2. **Key requirement**: The legal threshold (e.g., “enhanced efficacy” for 3(d)).  \n3. **Model paragraph**: A template response (e.g., evidence of therapeutic efficacy).  \n4. **Technical argument**: For 3(k), explain the technical effect.  \n5. **Reframing strategy**: For 3(i), convert a method claim into a device claim.  \n6. **Biological process distinction**: For 3(j), clarify that the process is not “essentially biological.”  \n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Identify the objection type**: Underline the relevant clause (e.g., 3(d)) in the objection letter.  \n2. **Gather evidence**: For 3(d), collect data on enhanced efficacy (e.g., clinical trial results). For 3(i), reframe method claims into device claims (e.g., “a drug delivery device” instead of “a method of treating X”).  \n3. **Draft model paragraphs**: Use textbook examples (e.g., “The new formulation shows 30% higher bioavailability compared to the prior art”).  \n4. **Address technical effect**: For 3(k), argue that the software integrates with hardware (e.g., “the algorithm optimizes energy consumption in a wearable device”).  \n5. **Differentiate biological processes**: For 3(j), state that the process involves genetic engineering (e.g., “CRISPR-Cas9 editing of plant genes” is not “essentially biological” as it uses synthetic tools).  \n\n## Worked example  \n**Objection**: 3(d) – “The new salt form of drug X is not novel.”  \n**Response**:  \n> “The compound is a novel salt form of drug X, which demonstrates a 40% increase in solubility and 25% faster onset of action compared to the known free base. These properties are not merely a result of the salt form but are directly linked to enhanced therapeutic efficacy, as evidenced by in vitro and in vivo studies published in *Journal of Medicinal Chemistry* (2023).”  \n\n**Objection**: 3(i) – “The claim is a method of treatment.”  \n**Response**:  \n> “The invention is not a method of treatment but a novel implantable device comprising a biocompatible mesh coated with a drug-eluting polymer. The device is designed to release therapeutic agents locally at the site of a wound, thereby reducing systemic toxicity. This is a tangible apparatus, not a method, and falls within the scope of patentable subject matter under Section 3(i).”  \n\n**Objection**: 3(k) – “The algorithm is a computer program per se.”  \n**Response**:  \n> “The algorithm is not a standalone computer program but is integrated with a hardware system to control the precise delivery of targeted radiation in a cancer therapy device. The technical effect is the real-time adjustment of radiation dose based on tumor movement, which is not achievable by conventional software alone.”  \n\n**Objection**: 3(j) – “The process is an essentially biological process.”  \n**Response**:  \n> “The process involves the use of a synthetic gene-editing tool (CRISPR-Cas9) to modify plant genes, which is not an essentially biological process. The invention includes specific synthetic vectors and in vitro editing steps, distinguishing it from natural biological processes.”  \n\n## Common mistakes  \n- **Error**: For 3(d), stating “the salt form is novel” without evidence of enhanced efficacy.  \n  **Correction**: Include data (e.g., solubility, bioavailability) and reference to the explanation in Section 3(d).  \n- **Error**: For 3(i), leaving a method claim unchanged.  \n  **Correction**: Reframe as a device (e.g., “a drug delivery system” instead of “a method of administering”).  \n- **Error**: For 3(k), failing to link the algorithm to a technical effect.  \n  **Correction**: Describe how the software interacts with hardware (e.g., “controls a robotic arm with sub-millimeter precision”).  \n- **Error**: For 3(j), not distinguishing synthetic elements in a biological process.  \n  **Correction**: Mention synthetic tools (e.g., “CRISPR-Cas9, synthetic vectors”) to show non-biological intervention.  \n\n## Practise this  \n**Task**: Draft a response to a 3(d) objection for a new formulation of an antihypertensive drug.  \n**Time**: 30 minutes.  \n**Check against**:  \n- The textbook’s explanation of “enhanced efficacy” (e.g., improved bioavailability, reduced side effects).  \n- Model paragraphs from the chapter (e.g., “The new formulation shows 50% higher plasma concentration at 2 hours post-dose”).  \n- Evidence requirements (clinical trial data, in vitro studies).\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 3 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 3. What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within\n> the meaning of this Act,\n> (a) an invention which is frivolous or which claims anything\n> obviously contrary to well established natural laws;\n> (b) an invention the primary or intended use or commercial\n> exploitation of which would be contrary to public order or morality\n> or which causes serious prejudice to human, animal or plant life or\n> health or to the environment;\n> (c) the mere discovery of a scientific principle or the formulation\n> of an abstract theory or discovery of any living thing or non-living\n> substance occurring in nature;\n> (d) the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does\n> not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that\n> substance or the mere discovery of any new property or new use for a\n> known substance or of the mere use of a known process, machine or\n> apparatus unless such known process results in a new product or\n> employs at least one new reactant.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, salts, esters, ethers,\n> polymorphs, metabolites, pure form, particle size, isomers, mixtures\n> of isomers, complexes, combinations and other derivatives of known\n> substance shall be considered to be the same substance, unless they\n> differ significantly in properties with regard to efficacy;\n>\n> (e) a substance obtained by a mere admixture resulting only in the\n> aggregation of the properties of the components thereof or a process\n> for producing such substance;\n> (f) the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known\n> devices each functioning independently of one another in a known\n> way;\n> (h) a method of agriculture or horticulture;\n> (i) any process for the medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic\n> diagnostic, therapeutic or other treatment of human beings or any\n> process for a similar treatment of animals to render them free of\n> disease or to increase their economic value or that of their\n> products.\n> (j) plants and animals in whole or any part thereof other than\n> micro-organisms but including seeds, varieties and species and\n> essentially biological processes for production or propagation of\n>\n>  11\n>\n> plants and animals;\n> (k) a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se\n> or algorithms;\n> (l) a literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work or any other\n> aesthetic creation whatsoever including cinematographic works and\n> television productions;\n> (m) a mere scheme or rule or method of performing mental act or\n> method of playing game;\n> (n) a presentation of information;\n> (o) topography of integrated circuits;\n> (p) an invention which, in effect, is traditional knowledge or which\n> is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of\n> traditionally known component or components.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the requirements for overcoming an objection under Section 3(d) of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2008, 2014, 2020)*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat must be demonstrated to overcome a Section 3(d) objection regarding a new form of a known substance?\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 3(d) of the Patents Act, 1970 states that the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance is not an invention unless it results in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that substance. The explanation to Section 3(d) clarifies that derivatives such as salts, esters, polymorphs, and other forms are considered the same substance unless they differ significantly in properties with regard to efficacy.\n\nIn *Novartis AG v. Union of India*, (2013) 6 SCC 1, it was held that \"enhanced efficacy\" under Section 3(d) refers specifically to therapeutic efficacy, not improved physical or chemical properties alone. This means that the new form must demonstrate a significant improvement in the treatment outcome, such as increased bioavailability, reduced side effects, or faster onset of action.\n\n**Application**  \nSuppose an applicant is seeking a patent for a new salt form of an antihypertensive drug. To overcome Section 3(d) objections, they must provide evidence that this new form results in enhanced therapeutic efficacy compared to the known substance. This can be demonstrated through clinical trial data or in vitro studies showing improved bioavailability, reduced toxicity, or more effective disease management.\n\nFor example, if the new salt form shows a 50% increase in plasma concentration at two hours post-dose compared to the prior art and this leads to better control of blood pressure with fewer side effects, it would satisfy the requirement under Section 3(d). The applicant must also ensure that the evidence is not merely based on physical properties like solubility or melting point but directly ties these improvements to therapeutic outcomes.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nTo overcome a Section 3(d) objection, an applicant must demonstrate that the new form of a known substance results in enhanced therapeutic efficacy. This requires concrete data from clinical trials or studies showing improved treatment outcomes, not merely physical or chemical changes.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse \"enhanced efficacy\" with physical properties like solubility or stability. The key is to show that the new form leads to better therapeutic results in patients, as emphasized by *Novartis AG v. Union of India*.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. How can a method of treatment claim be rephrased to avoid an objection under Section 3(i) of the Patents Act, 1970?**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2016, 2018)*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is the correct approach to rephrase a method of treatment claim into a patentable subject matter under Section 3(i)?\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 3(i) of the Patents Act, 1970 excludes from patentability any process for the medicinal or therapeutic treatment of human beings. However, a method of treatment can be rephrased as a device or apparatus claim to fall within the scope of patentable subject matter.\n\nFor instance, instead of claiming \"a method of treating diabetes by administering drug X,\" the claim can be reframed as \"a drug delivery device comprising an insulin pump with microchip-controlled dosage.\" This transforms the abstract method into a tangible apparatus, thereby avoiding Section 3(i) objections.\n\n**Application**  \nConsider a scenario where an inventor has developed a novel technique for delivering chemotherapy drugs directly to cancerous tissue. If they claim this as a \"method of treating cancer,\" it would be excluded under Section 3(i). However, reframing the claim as \"a drug delivery device comprising a micro-needle array that administers chemotherapy agents locally at the tumor site\" would make it patentable.\n\nThis rephrasing ensures that the invention is considered an apparatus rather than a method of treatment. The focus shifts to the physical structure and function of the device, making the claim eligible for patent protection under Section 3(i).\n\n**Conclusion**  \nTo avoid objections under Section 3(i), a method of treatment should be reframed as a device or apparatus claim that includes tangible components and functions. This transformation makes the invention eligible for patentability.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to rephrase method claims properly, leaving them unchanged and thus falling foul of Section 3(i). The key is to focus on physical devices rather than abstract methods.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Explain how a biological process can be distinguished from an \"essentially biological\" process under Section 3(j) of the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2012, 2017)*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat distinguishes a biological process that is patentable from one that falls within the exclusion under Section 3(j)?\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 3(j) of the Patents Act, 1970 excludes \"essentially biological processes\" for the production or propagation of plants and animals. However, if the process involves non-biological intervention such as genetic engineering or synthetic tools, it may be patentable.\n\nFor example, a process that uses CRISPR-Cas9 to edit plant genes would not be considered an essentially biological process because it involves synthetic vectors and in vitro editing steps. This distinction was emphasized in *Ferid Allani v. Union of India*, 2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867, where the court noted that processes involving non-biological elements can be patentable.\n\n**Application**  \nSuppose an inventor develops a process for modifying rice plants to increase yield by introducing a synthetic gene using CRISPR-Cas9. If this process is claimed as \"a method of increasing crop yield through natural breeding,\" it would fall under Section 3(j). However, if the claim includes specific steps such as the use of synthetic vectors or in vitro editing techniques, it can be considered patentable.\n\nThis distinction hinges on whether the process relies solely on biological mechanisms or incorporates non-biological elements. The inclusion of synthetic tools or genetic modifications is critical to avoid exclusion under Section 3(j).\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA biological process that involves non-biological intervention, such as genetic engineering or the use of synthetic vectors, can be distinguished from an \"essentially biological\" process and may be patentable.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook the importance of including specific non-biological steps in their claims. The presence of synthetic tools is essential to distinguish a claim from being excluded under Section 3(j).\n\n---\n\n### **Q. How can a computer program be distinguished from a \"computer programme per se\" under Section 3(k) of the Patents Act, 1970?**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2015, 2019)*\n\n**Issue**  \nWhat is required to distinguish a computer program from a \"computer programme per se\" for patentability under Section 3(k)?\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 3(k) of the Patents Act, 1970 excludes mathematical or business methods and computer programs per se. However, if a software invention has a technical effect that integrates with hardware, it may be considered patentable.\n\nIn *Ferid Allani v. Union of India*, 2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867, the court held that an algorithm must interact with hardware in a way that produces a specific technical effect to avoid being excluded under Section 3(k). For example, if the software controls a robotic arm or optimizes energy consumption in a device, it is not merely a program per se.\n\n**Application**  \nConsider an invention involving an algorithm for optimizing energy use in a wearable health monitoring device. If the claim is made as \"a computer program that calculates energy usage,\" it would be excluded under Section 3(k). However, if the claim states that the algorithm \"controls the power distribution of sensors in real-time to extend battery life,\" it demonstrates a technical effect and may be patentable.\n\nThe key is to show how the software interacts with hardware to achieve a tangible outcome. This integration distinguishes the invention from a mere computer program per se.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA computer program can be distinguished from a \"computer programme per se\" by showing that it has a specific technical effect, such as controlling hardware or improving device performance, which makes it patentable under Section 3(k).\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to link the software to a tangible technical effect. The integration with hardware and description of the resulting benefit are essential for overcoming objections under Section 3(k).","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Responding to Section 3 Objections — 3(d), 3(i), 3(j) and 3(k)\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 3(d) | **HAM** + clause (d) | HAMmers bang on a pill, showing better healing |\n| Sec. 3(i) | **HAM** + clause (i) | HAMsters gnaw through method claims, reshaping them |\n| Sec. 3(j) | **HAM** + clause (j) | HAMmers fix a broken computer, proving tech effect |\n| Sec. 3(k) | **HAM** + clause (k) | HAMsters in a lab, not making a drug |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### Novartis AG v. Union of India\n_(2013) 6 SCC 1_\n\n- **Held** - Efficacy under Section 3(d) means therapeutic efficacy; improved physical properties alone do not suffice.\n\n### Ferid Allani v. Union of India\n_2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867_\n\n- **Held** - A computer-related invention showing a technical effect is not barred by Section 3(k).\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Enhanced therapeutic efficacy evidence for a 3(d) objection - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n2. Reframing a method of treatment claim - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n3. Technical effect argument against a 3(k) objection - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n4. Essentially biological process objections - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n5. Which case decided: Efficacy under Section 3(d) means therapeutic efficacy; improved physical properties alone do not suffice.\n6. Which case decided: A computer-related invention showing a technical effect is not barred by Section 3(k).\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 3(d) | Inventions are non-patentable if they are aggregation or duplication of known properties | Mere mixing of old ingredients  \n- Section 3(i) | No patent for inventions related to atomic energy | Nuclear bomb blueprint not patented  \n- Section 3(j) | Plants and animals (except microorganisms) not patentable | Patenting a new rose variety blocked  \n- Section 3(k) | Mathematical or business methods, computer programs per se not patentable | Code alone can't get a patent  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013) | Efficacy under 3(d) means therapeutic effect | Medicine must cure, not just look better  \n- Ferid Allani v. Union of India (2019) | Computer invention with technical effect is allowed | Code that does real work passes test  \n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusing \"efficacy\" with physical properties in Section 3(d)  \n- Use textbook definitions, not assumptions, when explaining non-patentable subject matter  \n- Always cite the relevant section number and explain its application clearly","storyEN":"**Story:**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, under a canopy of ancient banyan trees near the Charminar, there lived a young patent agent named **Ravi**, who had just received an urgent letter from his client, **Novartis AG**. The letter was about a new salt form of an antihypertensive drug that was being rejected by the Controller under **Section 3(d)** of the Patents Act, 1970 — a clause Ravi knew all too well.\n\nRavi had to act fast. He recalled the advice from his mentor, who once said: “**Twenty-one lamps must burn in your mind for every number you meet.**” And so, he lit twenty-one lamps on his desk and began to think.\n\nThe first lamp reminded him of **Section 3(d)** — a clause that bars mere new forms of known substances unless they show **enhanced efficacy**. His client had argued the salt form was novel, but without evidence of improved therapeutic effect, their case was weak. He remembered the **Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013) 6 SCC 1**, where the Supreme Court ruled that a new salt form must demonstrate **therapeutic efficacy** — not just better solubility or faster onset.\n\nRavi gathered data from clinical trials: the new formulation showed **50% higher plasma concentration at two hours post-dose** and **reduced side effects by 40%**, as per in vitro studies. He knew this was enough to satisfy Section 3(d), but he had to frame it correctly — not just “novel salt form,” but **enhanced therapeutic efficacy**.\n\nThe second lamp burned bright with the memory of **Section 3(i)**, which bars methods of treatment. His client’s next objection was about a method for delivering drugs through implants. Ravi realized they needed to reframe this as a **device**, not a method. He recalled how his mentor had once said: “**A method is a process; a device is an object.**” So, he suggested changing the claim from “a method of treating hypertension using implantable devices” to “**a drug delivery system comprising a biocompatible mesh coated with a polymer**,” which would fall under patentable subject matter.\n\nThe third lamp reminded him of **Section 3(j)** — a clause that bars essentially biological processes. Ravi had another objection: the client was trying to patent a process involving **CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing in plants**, but the Controller said it was “essentially biological.” Ravi knew from his textbook that the key was to show synthetic intervention. He recalled the case of **Ferid Allani v. Union of India, 2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867** — a case where the court recognized that even computer programs could be patentable if they had a **technical effect**.\n\nRavi reworded the claim to emphasize the use of synthetic vectors and in vitro editing steps, distinguishing it from natural biological processes. He explained: “The process involves **CRISPR-Cas9 gene-editing tools**, which are not found in nature — this is a **synthetic intervention**, not an essentially biological one.”\n\nFinally, the fourth lamp brought to mind **Section 3(k)**, which bars mathematical or business methods and computer programs per se. Ravi’s client had developed an algorithm for optimizing radiation doses during cancer treatment. The Controller said it was just software — but Ravi knew from Ferid Allani that a program could be patentable if it had a tangible technical effect.\n\nHe framed the response: “The algorithm is not standalone; it controls a **wearable device** that adjusts radiation in real-time based on tumor movement, which conventional software cannot do.” This satisfied Section 3(k), as the program was integrated with hardware and had a clear **technical effect**.\n\nWith all four objections addressed — 3(d), 3(i), 3(j), and 3(k) — Ravi submitted his response. The Controller reviewed it carefully, then sent back an approval letter. His client was overjoyed.\n\nRavi smiled as he put out the twenty-one lamps. He had not only saved a patent but also remembered every number, clause, and case perfectly — because they were now part of his story.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Section 3(d), 3(i), 3(j), and 3(k) | Every number must be clearly understood and applied in patent responses. |\n| Section 3(d) | Clause (d) of Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970 | A new form of a known substance is not patentable unless it shows enhanced therapeutic efficacy. |\n| Enhanced efficacy | Explanation under Section 3(d) | Therapeutic improvement must be proven through clinical or in vitro data, not just physical properties. |\n| Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013) 6 SCC 1 | Case law on Section 3(d) | A new salt form is patentable only if it improves therapeutic efficacy; mere solubility or onset does not suffice. |\n| Section 3(i) | Clause (i) of Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Methods of treatment are not patentable unless reframed as devices or apparatuses. |\n| Drug delivery system | Reframing a method into a device claim under Section 3(i) | Converting a process into a tangible object makes it eligible for patent protection. |\n| Ferid Allani v. Union of India, 2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867 | Case law on Section 3(k) | A computer program is not barred if it has a technical effect when integrated with hardware. |\n| CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing | Example under Section 3(j) | Processes involving synthetic tools (like CRISPR) are not “essentially biological” and may be patentable. |\n| Section 3(k) | Clause (k) of Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Mathematical or business methods, and computer programs per se, are not inventions unless they have a technical effect. |\n| Wearable device with algorithm | Technical integration under Section 3(k) | Software must be linked to hardware with a tangible, non-trivial effect to qualify for patent protection. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for Responding to Section 3 Objections — 3(d), 3(i), 3(j) and 3(k)**\n\n---\n\n#### **1. Section 3(d): Efficacy Requirement**\n\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **\"E-T-E\"** – *Enhanced Therapeutic Effect*  \n  (Remember: Only improved therapeutic efficacy qualifies, not just physical properties.)\n\n- **Number-anchor for Section 3(d)**: **\"3(d)\" → \"Therapy Denial\"**  \n  Think of a doctor denying treatment — this ties to the idea that mere enhancement in physical form is not enough; there must be *therapeutic* improvement.\n\n- **Confusion Pair**: *\"Improved physical properties vs. therapeutic efficacy\"*  \n  **Differentiator**: *Therapeutic efficacy refers to medical benefit, while improved physical properties are just cosmetic or structural changes.*\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Section 3(i): Inventions Relating to Atomic Energy**\n\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **\"A-N-E\"** – *Atomic Not Eligible*  \n  (Remember: No patents for atomic energy inventions.)\n\n- **Number-anchor for Section 3(i)**: **\"3(i)\" → \"Nuclear Incident\"**  \n  Visualize a nuclear reactor accident — this connects to the exclusion of atomic energy under the law.\n\n- **Confusion Pair**: *\"Atomic Energy vs. Nuclear Energy\"*  \n  **Differentiator**: *They are essentially the same in legal terms, but “atomic” is used in the Act; both relate to radioactive processes.*\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Section 3(j): Aggregation or Duplication of Known Properties**\n\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **\"K-P-D\"** – *Known Properties Duplicated*  \n  (Remember: If it’s just a mix of known things with no new effect, it doesn’t qualify.)\n\n- **Number-anchor for Section 3(j)**: **\"3(j)\" → \"Junk Recipe\"**  \n  Imagine a recipe that combines common ingredients but produces nothing new — this reflects the idea of duplication or aggregation.\n\n- **Confusion Pair**: *\"Aggregation vs. Combination\"*  \n  **Differentiator**: *Aggregation is just putting known things together without any novel effect, while combination may lead to something new.*\n\n---\n\n#### **4. Section 3(k): Computer Programs**\n\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **\"C-P-B\"** – *Computer Program Barred* (unless it has a technical effect)  \n  (Remember: Just software is not patentable unless it solves a technical problem.)\n\n- **Number-anchor for Section 3(k)**: **\"3(k)\" → \"Keyboard Key\"**  \n  Think of a keyboard — this connects to computers, but only if there's a *technical* application.\n\n- **Confusion Pair**: *\"Computer program vs. software with technical effect\"*  \n  **Differentiator**: *A pure computer program is not patentable, but one that has a tangible, technical impact (like controlling machines) can be patented.*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall the key points of each section quickly and accurately during an exam or practical situation.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Provide data showing enhanced therapeutic efficacy compared to the known substance, such as clinical trial results. Section 3(d) requires proof of improved efficacy beyond mere discovery of a new form.","question":"What evidence is needed to overcome a Section 3(d) objection?"},{"answer":"Reframe to focus on a novel process or apparatus, not the treatment itself, emphasizing technical aspects. Section 3(i) excludes medical treatments but allows patentable processes.","question":"How can a method of treatment claim under Section 3(i) be reframed?"},{"answer":"Demonstrate a technical effect, like improving a machine's performance, not just an abstract algorithm. Section 3(k) excludes standalone computer programs without technical application.","question":"What counters a Section 3(k) objection for a computer program?"},{"answer":"Processes involving genetic modification or cell culture, not natural biological processes. Section 3(j) excludes natural processes but allows patented biological methods.","question":"What qualifies as an essentially biological process under Section 3(j)?"},{"answer":"Cite specific examples, like a new salt form with significantly improved bioavailability. The explanation to Section 3(d) defines acceptable derivatives.","question":"What model paragraph addresses Section 3(d) objections?"},{"answer":"Integrate the algorithm with a technical application, such as optimizing industrial machinery. Section 3(k) excludes pure mathematical methods without technical effect.","question":"How to respond to a Section 3(k) objection for a mathematical method?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Rule 70A","Rule 1","Rule 119","Rule 3"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Prescribed particulars?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 3 is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Manner in which a claim under section 20(1) shall be made","Request for publication","Grace period","Prescribed particulars"],"question":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Section 3","Section 4","Section 124A","Section 156"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3 is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Savings respecting disclosure to Government","What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within","Protection of security of India","Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement"],"question":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 3(d) excludes the mere discovery of a new property or new use for a known substance.","options":["A new process that results in a new product using a known reactant","A new form of a known substance that enhances its known efficacy","A mere discovery of a new property of a known substance","A process that employs at least one new reactant"],"question":"Which of the following is explicitly excluded from being considered an invention under Section 3(d) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The explanation states that such derivatives are considered the same substance unless they differ significantly in efficacy-related properties.","options":["When they are chemically identical to the original substance","When they differ significantly in properties regarding efficacy","When they are used in a new therapeutic application","When they are derived from traditional knowledge"],"question":"According to the explanation in Section 3(d), when are salts, esters, and polymorphs of a known substance considered different for patent purposes?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3(i) excludes processes for medicinal, surgical, or therapeutic treatments of humans or animals.","options":["A method of agricultural cultivation","A surgical treatment for humans","A new chemical compound for drug delivery","A process for improving animal breeding"],"question":"Which of the following is explicitly excluded from patentability under Section 3(i) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3(k) explicitly excludes mathematical or business methods and computer programs per se.","options":["A new method of data encryption","A computer program per se","A biotechnological process for gene editing","A novel pharmaceutical formulation"],"question":"Which category of subject matter is excluded from patentability under Section 3(k) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3(p) excludes inventions that are traditional knowledge or aggregations of known properties of traditional components.","options":["A new application of a known traditional remedy","A novel chemical compound with therapeutic properties","A process involving a new industrial manufacturing technique","A combination of known substances with synergistic effects"],"question":"Under Section 3(p) of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following would be excluded from patentability?"}]},"s1p_48":{"notes":"## The structure  \nA complete pre-grant or post-grant representation (or revocation petition) includes:  \n1. **Title and form reference** (e.g., Form 7A for pre-grant opposition, Form 27 for revocation petitions).  \n2. **Introduction**: Name of the applicant, patent number, and a brief statement of the purpose (e.g., \"This is a pre-grant opposition to the grant of Patent No. XYZ under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970\").  \n3. **Grounds of opposition**: Clear, numbered points citing specific provisions of the Patents Act (e.g., Sections 3(d), 3(e), 45, or 54) and explaining how the patent fails to meet them.  \n4. **Evidence**: Supporting documents (e.g., prior art references, expert affidavits, market data) linked to each ground.  \n5. **Request for hearing**: A statement requesting a hearing if the opposition is not resolved by the Controller.  \n6. **Conclusion**: Summary of the case and a call to action (e.g., \"The Controller is respectfully requested to revoke the patent in question\").  \n\nFor revocation petitions under Section 64, the structure must also include:  \n- A **statement of the grounds** (e.g., lack of novelty, obviousness, or non-patentable subject matter).  \n- **Evidence** (e.g., prior art, expert opinions, or market data).  \n- **Form 27** completed with the petitioner’s details, patent number, and a list of annexures.  \n\n---\n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Fill the form header**: Write the form number (e.g., Form 7A) and the patent number at the top.  \n2. **State the purpose**: Begin with a clear statement of the type of representation (e.g., \"Pre-grant opposition to Patent No. 123456\").  \n3. **List grounds of opposition**: Use numbered points, citing specific sections of the Patents Act (e.g., \"Section 3(d) – Patentability of new forms of known substances\").  \n4. **Attach evidence**: For each ground, reference documents (e.g., \"Prior art reference 1: Journal of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Vol. 50, 2020\").  \n5. **Request a hearing**: Include a paragraph requesting a hearing if the opposition is not resolved by the Controller.  \n6. **Complete Form 27** (for revocation): Ensure all fields (petitioner’s name, address, patent number, annexures) are filled.  \n7. **Proofread**: Check for compliance with rules (e.g., Rule 55(2) for pre-grant opposition requiring a Request for Examination (RFE)).  \n\n---\n\n## Worked example  \n**Pre-grant opposition to Patent No. 123456 (Peginterferon alfa-2a)**  \n**Form 7A**  \n1. **Introduction**:  \n   \"This is a pre-grant opposition to the grant of Patent No. 123456 under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970. The opposition is filed by [Name], a public health NGO, on the grounds that the invention fails to meet the requirements of Sections 3(d) and 3(e).\"  \n\n2. **Grounds of opposition**:  \n   - **Ground 1 (Section 3(d))**: The patent claims a new form of a known substance (peginterferon alfa-2a), which is not patentable under Section 3(d) as it lacks enhanced efficacy.  \n   - **Ground 2 (Section 3(e))**: The invention is a mere combination of two known substances (interferon alfa-2a and polyethylene glycol), which is not patentable under Section 3(e).  \n\n3. **Evidence**:  \n   - **Annexure 1**: Prior art reference (Journal of Hepatology, 2019) showing interferon alfa-2a is a known substance.  \n   - **Annexure 2**: Expert affidavit stating that peginterferon alfa-2a does not demonstrate enhanced efficacy over existing treatments.  \n\n4. **Request for hearing**:  \n   \"The Controller is respectfully requested to schedule a hearing to examine the evidence and determine the validity of this opposition.\"  \n\n5. **Conclusion**:  \n   \"The patent should not be granted as it fails to meet the statutory requirements of the Patents Act, 1970.\"  \n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes  \n**Error**: Failing to cite specific sections of the Patents Act (e.g., writing \"the invention is not novel\" without referencing Section 2(1)(a)).  \n**Correction**: Always explicitly reference the relevant section (e.g., \"The invention lacks novelty under Section 2(1)(a) because...\").  \n\n**Error**: Including incomplete or irrelevant evidence (e.g., a generic market report without linking it to a specific ground).  \n**Correction**: Ensure each piece of evidence directly supports a numbered ground (e.g., \"Annexure 1: Prior art showing the substance was known in 2018, supporting Ground 1 under Section 3(d)\").  \n\n**Error**: Not requesting a hearing in pre-grant oppositions.  \n**Correction**: Always include a request for hearing, even if the opposition is based on clear evidence.  \n\n**Error**: Incorrectly using Form 27 for pre-grant oppositions.  \n**Correction**: Use Form 7A for pre-grant oppositions and Form 27 only for revocation petitions.  \n\n---\n\n## Practise this  \n**Task**: Draft a revocation petition under Section 64 for a patent claiming a \"method of manufacturing a solar panel using graphene\".  \n**Time**: 30 minutes.  \n**Check against**:  \n- **Form 27**: Ensure all fields (petitioner’s details, patent number, annexures) are completed.  \n- **Grounds**: Reference Sections 3(d), 45, or 54 (e.g., \"The invention lacks novelty under Section 2(1)(a) as graphene-based solar panels were disclosed in Journal of Renewable Energy, 2021\").  \n- **Evidence**: Include annexures with prior art, expert affidavits, and market data.  \n- **Request for hearing**: Explicitly state the request.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 25 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 25. Opposition to the patent.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent has been published but a\n> patent has not been granted, any person may, in writing, represent\n> by way of opposition to the Controller against the grant of patent\n> on the ground\n> (a) that the applicant for the patent or the person under or through\n> whom he claims, wrongfully obtained the invention or any part\n> thereof from him or from a person under or through whom he claims;\n> (b) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification has been published before the priority date\n> of the claim\n> (i) in any specification filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent made in India on or after the 1st day of January, 1912; or\n> (ii) in India or elsewhere, in any other document:\n> Provided that the ground specified in sub-clause (ii) shall not be\n> available where such publication does not constitute an anticipation\n> of the invention by virtue of sub-section (2) or sub-section (3) of\n> section 29;\n> (c) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is claimed in a claim of a complete\n> specification published on or after the priority date of the\n> applicant’s claim and filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent in India, being a claim of which the priority date is earlier\n> than that of the applicant’s claim;\n> (d) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was publicly known or publicly used in India\n> before the priority date of that claim.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, an invention relating\n> to a process for which a patent is claimed shall be deemed to have\n> been publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority\n> date of the claim if a product made by that process had already been\n> imported into India before that date except where such importation\n> has been for the purpose of reasonable trial or experiment only;\n>\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious and clearly does not involve any\n> inventive step, having regard to the matter published as mentioned\n> in clause (b) or having regard to what was used in India before the\n>\n>  29\n>\n> priority date of the applicant’s claim;\n> (f) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act, or is not\n> patentable under this Act;\n> (g) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and\n> clearly describe the invention or the method by which it is to be\n> performed;\n> (h) that the applicant has failed to disclose to the Controller the\n> information required by section 8 or has furnished the information\n> which in any material particular was false to his knowledge;\n> (i) that in the case of a convention application, the application\n> was not made within twelve months from the date of the first\n> application for protection for the invention made in a convention\n> country by the applicant or a person from whom he derives title;\n> (j) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n> mentions the source or geographical origin of biological material\n> used for the invention;\n> (k) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is anticipated having regard to the\n> knowledge, oral or otherwise, available within any local or\n> indigenous community in India or elsewhere,\n> but on no other ground and the Controller shall, if requested by\n> such person for being heard, hear him and dispose of such\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 64 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 64. Revocation of Power patents.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions contained in this Act, a patent\n> whether granted before or after the commencement of this Act, may,\n> be revoked on a petition of any person interested or of the Central\n> Government or on a counter-claim in a suit for infringement of the\n> patent by the High Court on any of the following grounds, that is to\n> say\n> (a) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification, was claimed in a valid claim of earlier\n> priority date contained in the complete specification of another\n> patent granted in India;\n> (b) that the patent was granted on the application of a person not\n> entitled under the provisions of this Act to apply therefor:\n> (c) that the patent was obtained wrongfully in contravention of the\n> rights of the petitioner or any person under or through whom he\n> claims;\n> (d) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act;\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is not new, having regard to what was\n>\n>  55\n>\n> publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority date of\n> the claim or to what was published in India or elsewhere in any of\n> the documents referred to in section 13:\n> (f) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious or does not involve any inventive\n> step, having regard to what was publicly known or publicly used in\n> India or what was published in India or elsewhere before the\n> priority date of the claim:\n> (g) that the invention, so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification, is not useful;\n> (h) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and fairly\n> describe the invention and the method by which it is to be\n> performed, that is to say, that the description of the method or the\n> instructions for the working of the invention as contained in the\n> complete specification are not by themselves sufficient to enable a\n> person in India possessing average skill in, and average knowledge\n> of, the art to which the invention relates, to work the invention,\n> or that it does not disclose the best method of performing it which\n> was known to the applicant for the patent and for which he was\n> entitled to claim, protection;\n> (i) that the scope of any claim of the complete specification is not\n> sufficiently and clearly defined or that any claim of the complete\n> specification is not fairly based on the matter disclosed in the\n> specification;\n> (j) that the patent was obtained on a false suggestion or\n> representation;\n> (k) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not patentable under this Act;\n> (l) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was secretly used in India, otherwise than as\n> mentioned in sub-section (3), before the priority date of the claim;\n> (m) that the applicant for the patent has failed to disclose to the\n> Controller the information required by section 8 or has furnished\n> information which in any material particular was false to his\n> knowledge;\n> (n) that the applicant contravened any direction for secrecy passed\n> under section 35 or made or caused to be made an application for the\n> grant of a patent outside India in contravention of section 39;\n> (o) that leave to amend the complete specification under section 57\n> or section 58 was obtained by fraud;\n> (p) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n>\n>  56\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 146 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 146. Power of Controller to call for information from patentees.\n> (1) The Controller may, at any time during the continuance of the\n> patent, by notice in writing, require a patentee or a licensee,\n> exclusive or otherwise, to furnish to him within two months from the\n> date of such notice or within such further time as the Controller\n> may allow, such information or such periodical statements as to the\n> extent to which the patented invention has been commercially worked\n> in India as may be specified in the notice.\n>\n>  107\n>\n> (2) Without prejudice to the provisions of sub-section (1), every\n> patentee and every licensee (whether exclusive or otherwise) shall\n> furnish in such manner and form and at such intervals (not being\n> less than six months) as may be prescribed statements as to the\n> extent to which the patented invention has been worked on a\n> commercial scale in India.\n>\n> (3) The Controller may publish the information received by him under\n> sub-section (1) or sub-section (2) in such manner as may be\n> prescribed.\n\n**Rule 131 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 131. Form and manner in which statements required under section\n> 146(2) to be furnished\n> (1) The statements shall be furnished by every patentee and every\n> licensee under sub-section (2) of section 146 in Form 27 which shall\n> be duly verified by the patentee or the licencee or his authorised\n> agent.\n>\n> (2) The statements referred to in sub-rule (1) shall be furnished\n> once in respect of every period of three financial years starting\n> from the financial year commencing immediately after the financial\n> year in which the patent was granted, and shall be furnished within\n> six months from the expiry of such period. Provided that the\n> Controller may condone the delay or extend the time in filing of\n> such statement for a period up to three months upon a request made\n> in Form 4.\n>\n> (3) The Controller may publish the information received by him under\n> sub-section (1) or sub-section (2) of section 146.\n\n**Rule 55A — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55A. Filing of notice of opposition\n> The notice of opposition to be given under sub-section (2) of\n> section 25 shall be made in Form 7 and sent to the Controller in\n> duplicate at the appropriate office.\n\n**Rule 55 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55. Opposition to the patent\n> (1) Representation for opposition under sub-section (1) of section\n> 25 shall be filed in Form 7(A) at the appropriate office with a copy\n> to the applicant, and shall include a statement and evidence, if\n> any, in support of the representation and a request for hearing, if\n> so desired.\n>\n> (1A) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-rule (1), no patent\n> shall be granted before the expiry of a period of six months from\n> the date of publication of the application under section 11A.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall consider such a representation only when a\n> request for examination of the application has been filed.\n>\n> (3) On consideration of the representation if the Controller is\n> satisfied with that,\n> (a) no prima facie case is made out in the representation, he shall\n> notify the opponent accordingly, and\n> (i) unless the opponent requests to be heard in the matter, the\n> Controller shall, within one month from the date of such\n> notification, pass an order recording the grounds for refusal of the\n> representation;\n> (ii) if opponent requests for a hearing, the Controller shall, after\n> giving the opponent an opportunity of being heard, pass an order\n> within one month from the date of hearing, recording his reasons for\n> refusal or prima facie acceptance of the representation and the\n> applicant shall be notified accordingly.\n> (b) a prima facie case is made out in the representation, the\n> Controller shall, within one month of receiving the representation,\n> pass an order recording his reasons and notify the applicant\n> accordingly.\n>\n> (4) On receiving the notice under sub-rule (3), the applicant shall,\n> if he so desires, file his statement and evidence, if any, in\n> support of his application within two months from the date of the\n> notice, with a copy to the opponent.\n>\n> (5) On consideration of the statement and evidence filed by the\n> applicant, the representation including the statement and evidence\n>\n>  42\n>\n> filed by the opponent, submissions made by the parties, and after\n> hearing the parties, if so requested, the Controller may either\n> reject the representation or require the complete specification and\n> other documents to be amended to his satisfaction before the patent\n> is granted or refuse to grant a patent on the application, by\n> passing a speaking order to simultaneously decide on the application\n> and the representation ordinarily within one month from the\n> completion of above proceedings.\n>\n> (5A) The procedure specified in sub-rules (2) to (4) of rule 62\n> shall, as far as may be, apply to the procedure for hearing under\n> this rule.\n>\n> (5B) An application for a patent, in which a representation for\n> opposition has been filed and notice has been issued by the\n> Controller under rule 3, shall be examined in accordance with rule\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the structure of a pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970.**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The question requires an explanation of how to properly draft and structure a pre-grant opposition petition under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule**: Under Section 25(1), any person may file a written representation by way of opposition against the grant of a patent on various grounds, including lack of novelty, obviousness, or non-patentable subject matter. The structure must include specific elements such as form reference (e.g., Form 7A), introduction with applicant and patent details, numbered grounds citing relevant sections (such as Sections 3(d) and 3(e)), evidence supporting each ground, a request for hearing, and a conclusion.\n\n**Application**: A pre-grant opposition must begin by clearly stating the form number (e.g., Form 7A) and the patent number at the top. The introduction should identify the applicant, provide the patent number, and state the purpose of the opposition, such as \"This is a pre-grant opposition to Patent No. XYZ under Section 25(1).\" Grounds for opposition must be clearly listed in numbered points with specific references to sections of the Patents Act (e.g., \"Section 3(d) – Patentability of new forms of known substances\"). Supporting evidence, such as prior art or expert affidavits, should be attached and referenced under each ground. A request for a hearing must also be included if the opposition is not resolved by the Controller. The conclusion should summarize the case and call upon the Controller to take action.\n\n**Conclusion**: A properly structured pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1) includes form reference, introduction with patent details, numbered grounds citing relevant sections of the Act, supporting evidence, a request for hearing, and a clear conclusion. This ensures clarity and compliance with procedural requirements set by the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often fail to clearly cite specific provisions of the Patents Act in their oppositions or omit essential components such as form numbers, evidence annexures, or requests for hearing, leading to procedural errors.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is the difference between a pre-grant and post-grant opposition?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The question seeks clarification on the distinction between pre-grant and post-grant oppositions under the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule**: A pre-grant opposition is filed before the grant of a patent (under Section 25(1)) and typically uses Form 7A. It can be based on grounds such as lack of novelty, obviousness, or non-patentable subject matter. A post-grant opposition, though not explicitly provided for in the Bare Act block, is generally filed after the grant of a patent and may involve revocation petitions under Section 64. Post-grant oppositions use Form 27 and include additional requirements such as detailed annexures listing evidence.\n\n**Application**: Pre-grant oppositions are submitted before the patent is granted and must adhere to specific procedural rules, including Rule 55(2) which requires a Request for Examination (RFE). These petitions typically focus on grounds such as Sections 3(d), 3(e), or 45 of the Patents Act. Post-grant oppositions are filed after the patent has been granted and may be based on similar grounds but require more detailed evidence, such as expert affidavits and market data, to support claims of invalidity under Section 64.\n\n**Conclusion**: Pre-grant oppositions occur before a patent is granted and use Form 7A, while post-grant oppositions (or revocation petitions) are filed after the grant and use Form 27. Both require detailed evidence but differ in procedural requirements and timing.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students conflate pre-grant and post-grant oppositions without understanding their distinct procedural rules and forms, leading to confusion in practical drafting scenarios.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the common mistakes made while drafting a revocation petition under Section 64 of the Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The question asks for an analysis of typical errors that occur when preparing a revocation petition under Section 64.\n\n**Rule**: Revocation petitions must be filed using Form 27 and include specific details such as the petitioner’s name, patent number, and annexures. Grounds must reference relevant sections (e.g., Sections 3(d), 45, or 54) with supporting evidence attached to each ground. A clear request for hearing is also required.\n\n**Application**: Common mistakes in drafting revocation petitions include failing to cite specific provisions of the Patents Act (e.g., not referencing Section 2(1)(a) when discussing novelty), including irrelevant or incomplete evidence, and omitting a request for hearing. Another frequent error is using Form 7A instead of Form 27 for revocation petitions. Additionally, some petitioners may fail to provide sufficient annexures with prior art references or expert affidavits that directly support each ground of opposition.\n\n**Conclusion**: Common mistakes in drafting revocation petitions include incorrect form usage, failure to cite relevant sections of the Patents Act, inclusion of irrelevant evidence, and omission of a hearing request. These errors can lead to rejection or dismissal of the petition by the Controller.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often neglect the importance of using the correct form (Form 27) for revocation petitions and may fail to provide clear, specific references to relevant sections of the Patents Act in their grounds of opposition.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Draft a revocation petition under Section 64 for a patent claiming a \"method of manufacturing a solar panel using graphene\".**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The question requires students to draft a revocation petition under Section 64 for a specific patent claim.\n\n**Rule**: A revocation petition must be filed on Form 27 and include the petitioner’s details, patent number, grounds of opposition citing relevant sections (e.g., Sections 3(d), 45, or 54), evidence in support of each ground, and a request for hearing. Each ground must be clearly numbered and linked to specific provisions of the Patents Act.\n\n**Application**: The revocation petition should begin with Form 27 completed with the petitioner’s name, address, patent number (e.g., Patent No. XYZ), and a list of annexures. The introduction would state that the petition is filed under Section 64 for the revocation of Patent No. XYZ due to lack of novelty or obviousness. Grounds may include: (1) Lack of novelty under Section 2(1)(a) because graphene-based solar panels were disclosed in a prior publication; (2) Obviousness under Section 3(e), as the method is a mere combination of known techniques; and (3) Non-patentable subject matter under Section 3(k). Supporting evidence such as prior art references, expert affidavits, or market data should be attached to each ground. A request for hearing must also be included.\n\n**Conclusion**: The revocation petition must be drafted on Form 27 with clear grounds citing relevant sections of the Patents Act and supported by appropriate evidence. It should include a request for hearing and be structured in accordance with procedural requirements under Section 64.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to use Form 27 correctly or omit specific references to sections like Sections 3(d) or 54, which are critical for the validity of the petition.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is the role of a working statement under Section 146 of the Patents Act, 1970?**\n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: The question asks about the purpose and legal implications of a working statement under Section 146 of the Patents Act.\n\n**Rule**: Under Section 146(2), every patentee or licensee must submit statements at regular intervals (not less than six months) regarding how the patented invention is being commercially worked in India. The Controller may also require such information upon notice and may publish it as per prescribed rules.\n\n**Application**: A working statement provides an update on the commercial use of a patented invention within India. It ensures that the patentee is complying with the requirement to work the invention, which is essential for maintaining the patent’s validity. Failure to submit these statements can lead to legal consequences such as revocation or fines under Section 64.\n\n**Conclusion**: A working statement under Section 146 ensures transparency in the commercial use of a patented invention and helps enforce the obligation on the part of the patentee or licensee to work the invention within India. It is a crucial procedural requirement for maintaining the patent’s legal standing.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often overlook the importance of working statements, leading to potential issues with patent maintenance and compliance under Section 146.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Drafting Oppositions, Revocation Petitions and Working Statements\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 25(1) | **NAIL** + clause (1) | NAILs pierce paper, forming a pre-grant rep's skeleton. |\n| Sec. 25(2) | **NAIL** + clause (2) | NAILs fasten grounds to the post-grant opposition's wall. |\n| Sec. 64 | **CHAIR** | CHAIR creaks as revocation petition's weight bears down. |\n| Sec. 146 | **TIE-ROACH** | _make your own picture for TIE-ROACH_ |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **25-28** - opposition (pre-grant and post-grant), mention of inventor\n- **64-66** - revocation\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Structure of a pre-grant representation - which provision?  (picture: NAIL)\n2. Grounds pleading and evidence in a post-grant opposition - which provision?  (picture: NAIL)\n3. A revocation petition under Section 64 - which provision?  (picture: CHAIR)\n4. Completing Form 27 correctly - which provision?  (picture: TIE-ROACH)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 25(1) | Pre-grant opposition grounds and who can file | Legal challenge before patent grant  \n- Section 25(2) | Post-grant opposition within one year of grant | Challenge after patent is issued  \n- Section 64 | Revocation petition grounds for invalid patents | Canceling a patent due to invalidity  \n- Section 83 | Working statement requirement for patent use in India | Proof of commercial working in India  \n- Section 146 | Controller can request info on patent usage | Mandatory reporting on invention use  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid listing vague grounds; stick to statutory provisions like novelty, obviousness, or lack of disclosure  \n- Use the exact form numbers (e.g., Form 7A) and procedural timelines (e.g., two months for reply) as examiners expect precision  \n- Structure working statements with clear evidence of commercial use in India to score full marks","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, in a quiet neighborhood near Charminar, lived a young patent agent named Ravi. He had just been assigned a new client: a public health NGO that wanted to challenge a patent for a drug called **Peginterferon alfa-2a**. The NGO believed the patent was unjust and did not meet the legal requirements of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\nRavi sat at his desk, surrounded by twenty-one lamps — each lamp representing one of the key provisions from Section 25(1) that he had to consider in drafting the opposition. The first lamp glowed red: **Section 25(1)(d)**, which dealt with inventions publicly known or used before the priority date. He knew this would be crucial for his argument.\n\nHe began by filling out **Form 7A**, the correct form for a pre-grant opposition. On it, he wrote the patent number and clearly stated that the NGO was opposing the grant of Patent No. 123456 under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\nNext came the **grounds of opposition** — numbered points with clear references to the law. He listed two main grounds: one for **Section 3(d)** and another for **Section 3(e)**. For each ground, he attached evidence. Annexure 1 was a prior art reference from *Journal of Hepatology*, showing that interferon alfa-2a had been known long before the patent application. Annexure 2 was an expert affidavit stating that peginterferon alfa-2a did not show enhanced efficacy over existing treatments.\n\nRavi knew he needed to request a hearing, so he included that in his submission. He concluded by asking the Controller to revoke the patent because it failed to meet the statutory requirements of the Act.\n\nBut Ravi wasn’t done yet — the NGO also wanted to challenge an older patent for **a method of manufacturing solar panels using graphene**. This time, they were filing a **revocation petition under Section 64**, which required **Form 27**. He filled out all the fields carefully: petitioner’s name, address, patent number, and a detailed list of annexures.\n\nThe grounds for revocation included a claim that the invention lacked novelty under **Section 2(1)(a)** because graphene-based solar panels had been disclosed in *Journal of Renewable Energy* (2021). Ravi attached prior art references and an expert affidavit to support this. He also mentioned **Section 3(d)**, which deals with the patentability of new forms of known substances.\n\nAs he worked on these documents, Ravi remembered a story from his training — a case where someone had failed to cite specific sections of the Patents Act and ended up losing their argument. He made sure not to repeat that mistake. Every ground was clearly tied to a section of the law.\n\nFinally, after proofreading everything for compliance with **Rule 55(2)** (which requires a Request for Examination in pre-grant oppositions), Ravi submitted the documents to the Controller. He felt confident because he had followed every step carefully — from choosing the right form, citing the correct sections, attaching evidence properly, and requesting a hearing.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twenty-one lamps | Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970 | Opposition to a patent can be filed on various grounds under this section. |\n| Form 7A | Pre-grant opposition form under the Patents Act, 1970 | This is the correct form to use when filing an opposition before a patent is granted. |\n| Section 3(d) | Patentability of new forms of known substances | A new form of a known substance must show enhanced efficacy to be patentable. |\n| Section 3(e) | Mere combinations of known substances are not patentable | If the invention is just a combination of existing substances, it fails under this section. |\n| Annexure 1 and 2 | Supporting evidence for grounds of opposition | Each piece of evidence must directly support a numbered ground in the petition. |\n| Request for hearing | A requirement in pre-grant oppositions | Even if evidence seems clear, a request for a hearing is mandatory. |\n| Form 27 | Revocation petition form under Section 64 | This form is used when challenging an already granted patent. |\n| Journal of Renewable Energy (2021) | Prior art reference for revocation petition | Evidence must be linked to specific grounds, such as lack of novelty. |\n| Rule 55(2) | Requirement for a Request for Examination in pre-grant oppositions | This rule ensures that the Controller has all necessary information before granting a patent. |\n| Section 64 | Grounds for revocation of patents | A patent may be revoked if it fails to meet statutory requirements, such as lack of novelty or obviousness. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for Drafting Oppositions, Revocation Petitions and Working Statements**\n\n---\n\n### **1. Acronyms/Mnemonics:**\n\n- **Opposition Elements (Section 25(1) & Rules 55–60):**\n  - **F.A.R.E.S.**  \n    *Form*, *Arguments*, *Rebuttals*, *Evidence*, *Submission* — these are the key components of a pre-grant opposition.\n\n- **Revocation Petition (Section 64 and Rules 57–60):**\n  - **P.I.N.K.**  \n    *Petition*, *Interest*, *Notice*, *Kinds of grounds* — essential parts of a revocation petition.\n\n- **Working Statements (Sections 82–94):**\n  - **W.A.S.H.**  \n    *Working*, *Availability*, *Statement*, *Hearing* — components involved in compulsory licensing and working of patents.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Number-Anchors:**\n\n- **Section 25(1)** – *“Opposition before grant”*: Imagine a **\"1\" shaped like a stop sign** — it stops the patent from being granted, hence pre-grant opposition.\n\n- **Rule 55**: *“Filing of opposition”*: Picture a **\"55\" as a pair of glasses** — you need to see clearly when filing an opposition.\n\n- **Section 64**: *“Revocation of patents”*: Think of the number **\"64\" as a key** — it unlocks the possibility of revoking a patent anytime during its life.\n\n- **Rule 62(1)**: *“Notice for hearing”*: Visualize **\"62\" as a clock face**, with the hands pointing to **\"10 days' notice\"**.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Confusion Pair Notes:**\n\n- **Pre-grant vs. Post-grant Opposition (Section 25(1) & 25(2)):**\n  - *Tip:* Remember that **pre-grant is filed before grant**, and **post-grant is filed after the patent has been granted**.\n\n- **Revocation under Section 64 vs. Post-grant Opposition under Section 25(2):**\n  - *Tip:* **Section 64 revocation** can be filed at any time, while **Section 25(2)** opposition must be filed within **one year of grant**.\n\n- **Opposition Representation (Form 7A) vs. Revocation Petition (Form 14):**\n  - *Tip:* Use **\"7A\" for opposition**, and **\"14\" for revocation** — the numbers match the forms used in each process.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall key points about drafting legal documents under the Patents Act, using only the sections and rules provided.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Any person, as defined under Section 25(1), can file a pre-grant opposition. This includes individuals, NGOs, or entities not directly involved in the invention's field.","question":"Who can file a pre-grant opposition in India?"},{"answer":"Post-grant opposition must be filed by a 'person interested' as per Section 2(1)(t). This refers to those engaged in or promoting research in the invention's field.","question":"What is the requirement for post-grant opposition under Section 25(2)?"},{"answer":"A pre-grant opposition in Form 7A must include grounds for opposition and supporting evidence, as outlined in Rule 55(2).","question":"What documents must accompany a pre-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"A revocation petition is filed under Section 64 of the Patents Act. It challenges the validity of an already granted patent.","question":"Under which section is a revocation petition filed?"},{"answer":"To ensure oppositions are relevant and credible, post-grant oppositions are limited to those with research interests in the invention's field, as per Section 2(1)(t).","question":"Why does post-grant opposition require a 'person interested'?"},{"answer":"A request for examination (RFE) must be filed in Form 18, as prescribed under Rule 24B(1)(i), along with the required fee.","question":"What is the correct form for requesting examination during opposition?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 64 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Revocation of Power patents”.","options":["Section 128","Section 118","Section 19","Section 64"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Revocation of Power patents?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 64 is headed “Revocation of Power patents”.","options":["Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court","Other provisions of Act to apply to convention applications","No anticipation if circumstances are only as described in","Revocation of Power patents"],"question":"Section 64 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Section 25","Section 57","Section 81","Section 34"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Opposition to the patent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 25 is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Compulsory licences","Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Officers and employees not to furnish information, etc","Opposition to the patent"],"question":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 146 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of Controller to call for information from patentees”.","options":["Section 146","Section 89","Section 160","Section 108"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of Controller to call for information from patentees?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 146 is headed “Power of Controller to call for information from patentees”.","options":["Power of Controller in cases of anticipation","Supplementary provisions as to amendment of application or","Anticipation by public display, etc","Power of Controller to call for information from patentees"],"question":"Section 146 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 25 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Identification of published applications”.","options":["Rule 34","Rule 61","Rule 25","Rule 133"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Identification of published applications?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 25 is headed “Identification of published applications”.","options":["Register of patents under section 67","Inspection and supply of published documents","Identification of published applications","Examination of application"],"question":"Rule 25 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The application must be made within twelve months from the date of the first application for protection in a convention country.","options":["Within six months from the date of the first application","Within twelve months from the date of the first application","Within eighteen months from the date of the first application","Within twenty-four months from the date of the first application"],"question":"Under Section 25(1)(i) of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the time limit for filing an application for a convention patent?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Publication of the invention before the priority date in any specification filed in India or elsewhere is a valid ground for opposition.","options":["The invention was disclosed in a prior patent application filed in a foreign country","The applicant failed to disclose information required by Section 8","The invention is not useful","The complete specification does not describe the invention sufficiently"],"question":"Which of the following is a valid ground for opposition under Section 25(1)(b) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Importation of a product made by the process before the priority date (except for reasonable trial or experiment) constitutes public use.","options":["If a product made by the process was imported into India for commercial sale","If a product made by the process was imported into India for reasonable trial or experiment","If a product made by the process was imported into India before the priority date","If a product made by the process was exported from India after the priority date"],"question":"Under Section 25(1)(d) of the Patents Act, 1970, when is an invention relating to a process deemed to have been publicly used in India?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"A patent may be revoked if the complete specification does not sufficiently describe the invention.","options":["The invention is not useful","The complete specification does not describe the invention sufficiently","The invention was obtained wrongfully","The patent was granted to an ineligible applicant"],"question":"Which of the following is a ground for revocation under Section 64(g) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Anticipation by knowledge of a local or indigenous community is a valid ground for opposition.","options":["The invention was disclosed in a prior patent","The invention is anticipated by knowledge of a local or indigenous community","The applicant failed to pay the patent fees","The invention is not useful for industrial application"],"question":"Under Section 25(1)(k) of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following can be a ground for opposition?"}]},"s1p_49":{"notes":"# Timelines and Deadlines — The Complete Calendar  \n*(Based on \"Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\" and related study material)*  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Definition of Timelines and Deadlines in Patent Law  \nIn patent law, **timelines** refer to the specific periods within which certain actions must be taken by applicants or other parties involved in a patent process. These timelines are crucial for ensuring fairness, efficiency, and legal compliance throughout the granting and enforcement of patents.  \n\n**Key concepts include:**  \n- **Filing deadlines**: The period within which an application must be filed after an invention is made.  \n- **Examination deadlines**: Time limits for submitting requests for examination or responding to office actions.  \n- **Opposition deadlines**: Periods during which third parties can oppose the grant of a patent.  \n- **Revocation and litigation timelines**: Deadlines related to challenging patents through legal means.  \n\nThese timelines are defined and regulated under various sections of *The Patents Act, 1970*, as amended on August 1, 2024.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Key Timelines Under the Patents Act, 1970  \n\n### a) Filing Deadlines  \n- **Provisional Application**: An applicant may file a provisional specification at any time before filing a complete specification (Section 9).  \n- **Complete Specification**: Must be filed within 12 months of the date of filing the provisional application (Section 11). If not filed, the patent is deemed abandoned.  \n\n> *Textbook Explanation*: The requirement to file a complete specification within 12 months ensures that the applicant does not delay indefinitely in providing sufficient details about the invention, thereby promoting timely examination and granting of patents (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 171).  \n\n### b) Publication of Applications  \n- Under **Section 11A**, applications are published after a certain period following their filing. The exact timeline is not specified in the Bare Act, but it generally occurs within a few months of filing.  \n\n> *Textbook Explanation*: Publication allows for public awareness and helps prevent multiple patents being granted for similar inventions (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 139).  \n\n### c) Request for Examination  \n- A request for examination must be made within **six months from the date of filing** an application in India or **within six months from the priority date**, if applicable (Section 11B).  \n\n> *Textbook Explanation*: This provision ensures that applications are not left unexamined indefinitely, thus maintaining a balance between the rights of inventors and the public interest (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 139).  \n\n### d) Examination Periods  \n- The **examiner** must submit their report within **one month to three months** from the date of reference of the application (Section 12).  \n- The **Controller** must dispose of the examiner’s report within **one month** from the date of receiving it (Section 15).  \n\n> *Textbook Explanation*: These time limits are essential to ensure that the patent process is not delayed unduly, which could lead to unnecessary legal uncertainty or loss of rights for the applicant (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 187).  \n\n### e) Opposition Deadlines  \n- **Section 25** states that any person may file an opposition against a patent within **one year from the date of grant**.  \n\n> *Textbook Explanation*: The one-year period allows sufficient time for interested parties to challenge potentially invalid patents without unduly prolonging the process (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 527).  \n\n### f) Compulsory Licence Applications  \n- Under **Section 84**, an application for a compulsory licence must be filed within **six months** of the publication of the patent or within **one year from the date of grant**, whichever is later.  \n\n> *Textbook Explanation*: This timeline ensures that compulsory licences are not granted too early or too late, thus balancing the rights of the patentee and the public interest (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 527).  \n\n### g) Restoration of Lapsed Patents  \n- Under **Section 60**, an application for restoration must be filed within **one year from the date of lapse**.  \n\n> *Textbook Explanation*: This one-year period allows for a reasonable opportunity to restore a patent that has lapsed due to non-payment or other administrative errors (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 527).  \n\n---\n\n## 3. Importance of Adhering to Deadlines  \n- **Legal Consequences**: Missing deadlines can result in the abandonment of an application or the invalidation of a patent.  \n- **Cost Implications**: Filing late may lead to additional fees and procedural delays.  \n- **Public Interest**: Timelines ensure that the public is not deprived of access to inventions for extended periods due to administrative delays.  \n\n> *Textbook Explanation*: These timelines are designed to maintain fairness, transparency, and efficiency in the patent system (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 527).  \n\n---\n\n## 4. Cases Involving Timelines and Deadlines  \n- **(verify this case with a case-law database)**: No verified or candidate cases are available for this topic in the provided materials.  \n\n> *Note*: While no specific cases were found to support these timelines, it is recommended that students consult legal databases such as IndianKanoon for relevant precedents and interpretations of these provisions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary Table of Key Timelines  \n| Event | Section/Article | Timeline |\n|------|------------------|----------|\n| Filing Complete Specification after Provisional | Section 11 | Within 12 months from the date of filing provisional application |\n| Request for Examination | Section 11B | Within six months of filing or within six months of priority date |\n| Examiner's Report | Section 12 | One month to three months from reference |\n| Controller’s Disposal of Report | Section 15 | One month from receipt of report |\n| Opposition to Patent | Section 25 | Within one year from the grant |\n| Compulsory Licence Application | Section 84 | Within six months of publication or within one year of grant |\n| Restoration of Lapsed Patent | Section 60 | Within one year from date of lapse |\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion  \nUnderstanding and adhering to timelines and deadlines in patent law is critical for both applicants and legal practitioners. These provisions are designed to ensure a fair, efficient, and transparent process that balances the rights of inventors with the public interest. Students preparing for the Patent Agent Examination should pay close attention to these timelines as they often form the basis of practical questions and case scenarios.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 9 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 9. Provisional and complete specifications.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent (not being a convention\n> application or an application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n>\n>  14\n>\n> Treaty designating India) is accompanied by a provisional\n> specification, a complete specification shall be filed within twelve\n> months from the date of filing of the application, and if the\n> complete specification is not so filed, the application shall be\n> deemed to be abandoned.\n>\n> (2) Where two or more applications in the name of the same applicant\n> are accompanied by provisional specifications in respect of\n> inventions which are cognate or of which one is a modification of\n> another and the Controller is of opinion that the whole of such\n> inventions are such as to constitute a single invention and may\n> properly be included in one patent, he may allow one complete\n> specification to be filed in respect of all such provisional\n> specifications:\n> Provided that the period of time specified under sub-section (1)\n> shall be reckoned from the date of filing of the earliest\n> provisional specification.\n>\n> (3) Where an application for a patent (not being a convention\n> application or an application filed under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty designating India) is accompanied by a specification\n> purporting to be a complete specification, the Controller may, if\n> the applicant so requests at any time within twelve months from the\n> date of filing of the application, direct that such specification\n> shall be treated, for the purposes of this Act, as a provisional\n> specification and proceed with the application accordingly.\n>\n> (4) Where a complete specification has been filed in pursuance of an\n> application for a patent accompanied by a provisional specification\n> or by a specification treated by virtue of a direction under sub-\n> section (3) as a provisional specification, the Controller may, if\n> the applicant so requests at any time before grant of patent, cancel\n> the provisional specification and post-date the application to the\n> date of filing of the complete specification.\n\n**Section 11A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11A. Publication of applications.\n> (1) Save as otherwise provided, no application for patent shall\n> ordinarily be opened to the public for such period as may be\n> prescribed.\n>\n> (2) The applicant may, in the prescribed manner, request the\n> Controller to publish his application at any time before the expiry\n> of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) and subject to the\n> provisions of sub-section (3), the Controller shall publish such\n> application as soon as possible.\n>\n> (3) Every application for a patent shall, on the expiry of the\n> period specified under sub-section (1), be published, except in\n> cases where the application\n> (a) in which secrecy direction is imposed under section 35; or\n> (b) has been abandoned under sub-section (1) of section 9; or\n> (c) has been withdrawn three months prior to the period specified\n> under sub-section (1).\n>\n> (4) In case a secrecy direction has been given in respect of an\n> application under section 35, then, it shall be published after the\n> expiry of the period prescribed under sub-section (1) or when the\n> secrecy direction has ceased to operate, whichever is later.\n>\n> (5) The publication of every application under this section shall\n> include the particulars of the date of application, number of\n> application, name and address of the applicant identifying the\n> application and an abstract.\n>\n> (6) Upon publication of an application for a patent under this\n> section\n> (a) the depository institution shall make the biological material\n> mentioned in the specification available to the public;\n> (b) the patent office may, on payment of such fee as may be\n> prescribed, make the specification and drawings, if any, of such\n> application available to the public.\n>\n> (7) On and from the date of publication of the application for\n> patent and until the date of grant of a patent in respect of such\n>\n>  20\n>\n> application, the applicant shall have the like privileges and rights\n> as if a patent for the invention had been granted on the date of\n> publication of the application:\n> Provided that the applicant shall not be entitled to institute any\n> proceedings for infringement until the patent has been granted:\n> Provided further that the rights of a patentee in respect of\n> applications made under sub-section (2) of section 5 before the 1st\n> day of January, 2005 shall accrue from the date of grant of the\n> patent:\n> Provided also that after a patent is granted in respect of\n> applications made under sub-section (2) of section 5, the patent-\n> holder shall only be entitled to receive reasonable royalty from\n> such enterprises which have made significant investment and were\n> producing and marketing the concerned product prior to the 1st day\n> of January, 2005 and which continue to manufacture the product\n> covered by the patent on the date of grant of the patent and no\n> infringement proceedings shall be instituted against such\n> enterprises.\n\n**Section 11B — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 11B. Request for examination.\n> (1) No application for a patent shall be examined unless the\n> applicant or any other interested person makes a request in the\n> prescribed manner for such examination within the prescribed period.\n>\n> (3) In case of an application in respect of a claim for a patent\n> filed under sub-section (2) of section 5 before the 1st day of\n> January, 2005 a request for its examination shall be made in the\n> prescribed manner and within the prescribed period by the applicant\n> or any other interested person.\n>\n> (4) In case the applicant or any other interested person does not\n> make a request for examination of the application for a patent\n> within the period as specified under sub-section (1) or sub-section\n> (3), the application shall be treated as withdrawn by the applicant:\n> Provided that\n> (i) the applicant may, at any time after filing the application but\n> before the grant of a patent, withdraw the application by making a\n> request in the prescribed manner; and\n> (ii) in a case where secrecy direction has been issued under section\n> 35, the request for examination may be made within the prescribed\n> period from the date of revocation of the secrecy direction.\n>\n>  21\n>\n> 12. Examination of application.\n> (1) When a request for examination has been made in respect of an\n> application for a patent in the prescribed manner under sub-section\n> (1) or sub-section (3) of section 11B, the application and\n> specification and other documents related thereto shall be referred\n> at the earliest by the Controller to an examiner for making a report\n> to him in respect of the following matters, namely:\n> (a) whether the application and the specification and other\n> documents relating thereto are in accordance with the requirements\n> of this Act and of any rules made thereunder;\n> (b) whether there is any lawful ground of objection to the grant of\n> the patent under this Act in pursuance of the application;\n> (c) the result of investigations made under section 13; and (d) any\n> other matter which may be prescribed.\n>\n> (2) The examiner to whom the application and the specification and\n> other documents relating thereto are referred under sub-section (1)\n> shall ordinarily make the report to the Controller within such\n> period as may be prescribed.\n\n**Section 21 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 21. Time for putting application in order for grant.\n>\n> CHAPTER V OPPOSITION PROCEEDINGS TO GRANT OF PATENTS\n\n**Section 25 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 25. Opposition to the patent.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent has been published but a\n> patent has not been granted, any person may, in writing, represent\n> by way of opposition to the Controller against the grant of patent\n> on the ground\n> (a) that the applicant for the patent or the person under or through\n> whom he claims, wrongfully obtained the invention or any part\n> thereof from him or from a person under or through whom he claims;\n> (b) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification has been published before the priority date\n> of the claim\n> (i) in any specification filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent made in India on or after the 1st day of January, 1912; or\n> (ii) in India or elsewhere, in any other document:\n> Provided that the ground specified in sub-clause (ii) shall not be\n> available where such publication does not constitute an anticipation\n> of the invention by virtue of sub-section (2) or sub-section (3) of\n> section 29;\n> (c) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is claimed in a claim of a complete\n> specification published on or after the priority date of the\n> applicant’s claim and filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent in India, being a claim of which the priority date is earlier\n> than that of the applicant’s claim;\n> (d) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was publicly known or publicly used in India\n> before the priority date of that claim.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, an invention relating\n> to a process for which a patent is claimed shall be deemed to have\n> been publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority\n> date of the claim if a product made by that process had already been\n> imported into India before that date except where such importation\n> has been for the purpose of reasonable trial or experiment only;\n>\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious and clearly does not involve any\n> inventive step, having regard to the matter published as mentioned\n> in clause (b) or having regard to what was used in India before the\n>\n>  29\n>\n> priority date of the applicant’s claim;\n> (f) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act, or is not\n> patentable under this Act;\n> (g) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and\n> clearly describe the invention or the method by which it is to be\n> performed;\n> (h) that the applicant has failed to disclose to the Controller the\n> information required by section 8 or has furnished the information\n> which in any material particular was false to his knowledge;\n> (i) that in the case of a convention application, the application\n> was not made within twelve months from the date of the first\n> application for protection for the invention made in a convention\n> country by the applicant or a person from whom he derives title;\n> (j) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n> mentions the source or geographical origin of biological material\n> used for the invention;\n> (k) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is anticipated having regard to the\n> knowledge, oral or otherwise, available within any local or\n> indigenous community in India or elsewhere,\n> but on no other ground and the Controller shall, if requested by\n> such person for being heard, hear him and dispose of such\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 53 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 53. Term of patent.\n> (1) Subject to the provisions of this Act, the term of every patent\n> granted, after the commencement of the Patents (Amendment) Act,\n> 2002, and the term of every patent which has not expired and has not\n> ceased to have effect, on the date of such commencement, under this\n> Act, shall be twenty years from the date of filing of the\n> application for the patent.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this sub-section, the term of\n> patent in case of International applications filed under the Patent\n> Cooperation Treaty designating India, shall be twenty years from the\n> international filing date accorded under the Patent Cooperation\n> Treaty.\n>\n> (2) A patent shall cease to have effect notwithstanding anything\n> therein or in this Act on the expiration of the period prescribed\n> for the payment of any renewal fee, if that fee is not paid within\n> the prescribed period or within such extended period as may be\n> prescribed.\n>\n> (4) Notwithstanding anything contained in any other law for the time\n> being in force, on cessation of the patent right due to non-payment\n> of renewal fee or on the expiry of the term of patent, the subject\n> matter covered by the said patent shall not be entitled to any\n> protection.\n>\n>  47\n>\n> CHAPTER IX PATENTS OF ADDITION\n\n**Section 84 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 84. Compulsory licences.\n> (1) At any time after the expiration of three years from the date of\n> the grant of patent, any person interested may make an application\n>\n>  68\n>\n> to the Controller for grant of compulsory licence on patent on any\n> of the following grounds, namely:\n> (a) that the reasonable requirements of the public with respect to\n> the patented invention have not been satisfied, or\n> (b) that the patented invention is not available to the public at a\n> reasonably affordable price, or (c) that the patented invention is\n> not worked in the territory of India.\n>\n> (2) An application under this section may be made by any person\n> notwithstanding that he is already the holder of a licence under the\n> patent and no person shall be estopped from alleging that the\n> reasonable requirements of the public with respect to the patented\n> invention are not satisfied or that the patented invention is not\n> worked in the territory of India or that the patented invention is\n> not available to the public at a reasonably affordable price by\n> reason of any admission made by him, whether in such a licence or\n> otherwise or by reason of his having accepted such a licence.\n>\n> (3) Every application under sub-section (1) shall contain a\n> statement setting out the nature of the applicant’s interest\n> together with such particulars as may be prescribed and the facts\n> upon which the application is based.\n>\n> (4) The Controller, if satisfied that the reasonable requirements of\n> the public with respect to the patented invention have not been\n> satisfied or that the patented invention is not worked in the\n> territory of India or that the patented invention is not available\n> to the public at a reasonably affordable price, may grant a licence\n> upon such terms as he may deem fit.\n>\n> (5) Where the Controller directs the patentee to grant a licence he\n> may, as incidental thereto, exercise the powers set out in section\n\n**Section 139 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 139. Other provisions of Act to apply to convention applications.\n> Save as otherwise provided in this Chapter, all the provisions of\n> this Act shall apply in relation to a convention application and a\n> patent granted in pursuance thereof as they apply in relation to an\n> ordinary application and a patent granted in pursuance thereof.\n>\n>  104\n>\n> CHAPTER XXIII MISCELLANEOUS\n\n**Rule 55 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55. Opposition to the patent\n> (1) Representation for opposition under sub-section (1) of section\n> 25 shall be filed in Form 7(A) at the appropriate office with a copy\n> to the applicant, and shall include a statement and evidence, if\n> any, in support of the representation and a request for hearing, if\n> so desired.\n>\n> (1A) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-rule (1), no patent\n> shall be granted before the expiry of a period of six months from\n> the date of publication of the application under section 11A.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall consider such a representation only when a\n> request for examination of the application has been filed.\n>\n> (3) On consideration of the representation if the Controller is\n> satisfied with that,\n> (a) no prima facie case is made out in the representation, he shall\n> notify the opponent accordingly, and\n> (i) unless the opponent requests to be heard in the matter, the\n> Controller shall, within one month from the date of such\n> notification, pass an order recording the grounds for refusal of the\n> representation;\n> (ii) if opponent requests for a hearing, the Controller shall, after\n> giving the opponent an opportunity of being heard, pass an order\n> within one month from the date of hearing, recording his reasons for\n> refusal or prima facie acceptance of the representation and the\n> applicant shall be notified accordingly.\n> (b) a prima facie case is made out in the representation, the\n> Controller shall, within one month of receiving the representation,\n> pass an order recording his reasons and notify the applicant\n> accordingly.\n>\n> (4) On receiving the notice under sub-rule (3), the applicant shall,\n> if he so desires, file his statement and evidence, if any, in\n> support of his application within two months from the date of the\n> notice, with a copy to the opponent.\n>\n> (5) On consideration of the statement and evidence filed by the\n> applicant, the representation including the statement and evidence\n>\n>  42\n>\n> filed by the opponent, submissions made by the parties, and after\n> hearing the parties, if so requested, the Controller may either\n> reject the representation or require the complete specification and\n> other documents to be amended to his satisfaction before the patent\n> is granted or refuse to grant a patent on the application, by\n> passing a speaking order to simultaneously decide on the application\n> and the representation ordinarily within one month from the\n> completion of above proceedings.\n>\n> (5A) The procedure specified in sub-rules (2) to (4) of rule 62\n> shall, as far as may be, apply to the procedure for hearing under\n> this rule.\n>\n> (5B) An application for a patent, in which a representation for\n> opposition has been filed and notice has been issued by the\n> Controller under rule 3, shall be examined in accordance with rule\n\n**Rule 134 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 134. Request for information under section 153\n> (1) A request for information in respect of the following matters\n> relating to any patent or application for patent shall be\n> admissible, namely:\n> (a) as to when a complete specification following a provisional\n> specification has been filed or an application for patent has been\n> deemed to have been abandoned;\n> (aa) as to when the information under section 8 has been filed.\n> (b) as to when publication of application has been made under\n> section 11A;\n> (c) as to when an application has been withdrawn under section 11B;\n> (d) as to when a request for examination has been made under section\n> 11B;\n> (e) as to when the examination report has been issued under section\n> 12;\n> (f) as to when an application for patent has been refused;\n> (g) as to when a patent has been granted;\n> (h) as to when a renewal fee has been paid;\n> (i) as to when the term of a patent has expired or shall expire;\n> (j) as to when an entry has been made in the register or application\n> has been made for the making of such entry; or\n> (k) as to when any application is made or action taken involving an\n>\n>  73\n>\n> entry in the register, publication in the Official Journal or\n> otherwise, if the nature of the application or action is specified\n> in the request.\n>\n> (2) Separate request shall be made in respect of each item of\n> information required.\n>\n> (3) The fee payable on a request to be made under section 153 shall\n> be as set out in the First Schedule.\n\n**Rule 2 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 2. Definitions\n> In these rules, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (a) \"Act\" means the Patents Act, 1970 (39 of 1970);\n> (b) \"appropriate office\" means the appropriate office of the patent\n> office as specified in rule 4;\n> (c) \"article\" includes any substance or material, and any plant,\n> machinery or apparatus, whether affixed to land or not;\n> (ca)\"educational institution\" means a university established or\n> incorporated by or under Central Act, a Provincial Act, or a State\n> Act, and includes any other educational institution as recognised by\n> an authority designated by the Central Government or the State\n> Government or the Union territories in this regard;\n> (d) \"Form\" means a Form specified in the Second Schedule;\n> (da) \"person other than a natural person\" shall include a \"small\n> entity\";\n> (db) \"Request for examination\" means a request for examination,\n> including expedited examination, made under section 11B in respect\n> of rule 24B or rule 24C;\n> (e) \"Schedule\" means Schedule to these rules;\n> (f) \"section\" means a section of the Act;\n> (fa) \"small entity\" means,\n> (i) in case of an enterprise engaged in the manufacture or\n> production of goods, an enterprise where the investment in plant and\n> machinery does not exceed the limit specified for a medium\n> enterprise under clause (a) of sub-section (1) of section 7 of the\n> Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises Development Act, 2006; and\n> (ii)in case of an enterprise engaged in providing or rendering of\n> services, an enterprise where the investment in equipment is not\n> more than the limit specified for medium enterprises under clause\n> (b) of sub-section (I) of Section 7 of the Micro, Small and Medium\n> Enterprises Development Act, 2006.\n>\n>  7\n>\n> Explanation 1: For the purpose of this clause, \"enterprise\" means an\n> industrial undertaking or a business concern or any other\n> establishment, by whatever name called, engaged in the manufacture\n> or production of goods, in any manner, pertaining to any industry\n> specified in the First Schedule to the Industries (Development and\n> Regulation) Act, 1951 (65 of 1951) or engaged in providing or\n> rendering of any service or services in such an industry.\n> Explanation 2: In calculating the investment in plant and machinery,\n> the cost of pollution control, research and development, industrial\n> safety devices and such other things as may be specified by\n> notification under the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises\n> Development Act, 2006; shall be excluded.\n> Explanation 3: The reference rates of foreign currency of the\n> Reserve Bank of India shall prevail.\n>\n> (fb) (i) \"Startup\" means\n> (a) an entity in India recognised as a startup by the competent\n> authority under Startup India initiative.\n> (b) In case of a foreign entity, an entity fulfilling the criteria\n> for turnover and period of incorporation/ registration as per\n> Startup India Initiative and submitting declaration to that effect.\n>\n> Explanation: In calculating the turnover, reference rates of foreign\n> currency of Reserve Bank of India shall prevail.\n> (g) words and expressions used, but not defined in these rules,\n> shall have the meanings respectively assigned to them in the Act.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the importance of timelines and deadlines under The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: This question seeks an analysis of why timelines and deadlines are crucial in the patent process under *The Patents Act, 1970*.\n\n**Rule**: Under *Section 9*, a complete specification must be filed within 12 months from the date of filing a provisional application; otherwise, the application is deemed abandoned. Similarly, *Section 11B* mandates that a request for examination be made within six months of filing or within six months of the priority date. *Section 25* provides that opposition to a patent must be filed within one year from the grant. These timelines are designed to ensure fairness, efficiency, and legal compliance in the granting and enforcement of patents (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 139). Additionally, *Section 60* allows for restoration of a lapsed patent within one year from the date of lapse.\n\n**Application**: The importance of these timelines lies in their role in preventing indefinite delays and ensuring that patents are granted and enforced in a timely manner. For example, if an applicant fails to file a complete specification within 12 months under *Section 9*, the application is automatically abandoned, which can result in loss of rights. Similarly, failure to request examination under *Section 11B* may lead to the application being treated as withdrawn. These provisions ensure that the patent process remains efficient and that third parties are not left in legal uncertainty for extended periods.\n\n**Conclusion**: Timelines and deadlines under *The Patents Act, 1970* are essential to maintain a balance between the rights of inventors and public interest, ensuring timely examination, granting, and enforcement of patents. They prevent indefinite delays, promote transparency, and uphold the integrity of the patent system.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students may overlook how these timelines affect both the applicant’s rights and third-party interests, failing to explain their broader impact on the legal process and public access to innovations.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Explain the timeline for filing a complete specification under *Section 11* of The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: This question asks for an explanation of the timeline prescribed by *Section 11* for filing a complete specification after a provisional application.\n\n**Rule**: Under *Section 11*, a complete specification must be filed within 12 months from the date of filing the provisional application. If not filed, the patent is deemed abandoned. This rule ensures that applicants do not delay indefinitely in providing sufficient details about their invention, thereby promoting timely examination and granting of patents (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 171).\n\n**Application**: Suppose an inventor files a provisional specification on January 1, 2025. Under *Section 11*, the complete specification must be filed by December 31, 2025. If the applicant fails to file it by this date, the application is deemed abandoned and cannot be revived unless the Controller allows restoration under *Section 60* within one year of the lapse.\n\n**Conclusion**: The timeline in *Section 11* ensures that inventors provide complete details about their invention promptly, thereby facilitating timely examination and granting of patents. It prevents indefinite delays and maintains efficiency in the patent process.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often confuse the timelines for provisional and complete specifications or fail to note the consequence of non-compliance under *Section 11*.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. What are the consequences of missing the deadline for requesting examination under *Section 11B*?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: This question asks about the legal consequences if an applicant misses the deadline to request examination under *Section 11B*.\n\n**Rule**: Under *Section 11B*, a request for examination must be made within six months from the date of filing or within six months from the priority date. If this deadline is missed, the application may be treated as withdrawn by the applicant (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 139).\n\n**Application**: Suppose an applicant files a patent application on January 1, 2025, but does not request examination until July 1, 2025. Since the deadline is six months from filing, this would be beyond the prescribed period and may lead to the application being treated as withdrawn unless the applicant seeks restoration under *Section 60*.\n\n**Conclusion**: Missing the deadline for requesting examination under *Section 11B* can result in the application being treated as withdrawn. This has serious consequences for the applicant, including potential loss of rights and the need to refile the application.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often fail to mention that restoration may be possible under *Section 60*, which is crucial in explaining the full legal consequences.\n\n---\n\n### **Q. Discuss the timeline for opposition to a patent under *Section 25*.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: This question seeks an explanation of the period within which opposition can be filed against a granted patent under *Section 25*.\n\n**Rule**: Under *Section 25*, any person may file an opposition to a patent within one year from the date of grant. The purpose of this provision is to allow sufficient time for interested parties to challenge potentially invalid patents without unduly prolonging the process (The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 527).\n\n**Application**: Suppose a patent is granted on January 1, 2025. Any person who believes that the grant should not have occurred may file an opposition by December 31, 2025. If they fail to do so within this period, they lose the right to challenge the patent under *Section 25*.\n\n**Conclusion**: The one-year timeline for opposition under *Section 25* ensures that interested parties have a reasonable opportunity to challenge a granted patent while preventing unnecessary delays in the legal process.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students fail to explain why the one-year period is significant or how it balances the rights of the patentee with public interest.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Timelines and Deadlines — The Complete Calendar\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 9 | **BEE** | BEE buzzes for twelve months, securing Paris priority. |\n| Sec. 11-A | **TOTE** + the letter A | TOTE bag carries eighteen months to publication, thirty-one to national phase. |\n| Sec. 11-B | **TOTE** + the letter B | TOTE bag holds exam request and FER reply. |\n| Sec. 21 | **NET** | NET catches application in order for grant. |\n| Sec. 25(2) | **NAIL** + clause (2) | NAIL holds one year opposition, three years licence. |\n| Sec. 53 | **LIME** | LIME glows at third-year renewal, extension available. |\n| Sec. 84 | **FIRE** | FIRE burns through timelines and deadlines calendar. |\n| Sec. 139 | **TIE-MAP** | TIE-MAP charts timelines and deadlines calendar. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **12-24** - examination, objections, time for putting in order\n- **25-28** - opposition (pre-grant and post-grant), mention of inventor\n- **43-53** - grant, rights, term of patent\n- **82-94** - working of patents, compulsory licences\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Twelve months provisional to complete and Paris priority - which provision?  (picture: BEE)\n2. Eighteen months to publication and thirty-one months to national phase - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n3. Request for examination and reply to the FER - which provision?  (picture: TOTE)\n4. Time to put the application in order for grant - which provision?  (picture: NET)\n5. One year for post-grant opposition, three years before a compulsory licence - which provision?  (picture: NAIL)\n6. Renewal from the third year and the extension available - which provision?  (picture: LIME)\n7. Timelines and Deadlines — The Complete Calendar - which provision?  (picture: FIRE)\n8. Timelines and Deadlines — The Complete Calendar - which provision?  (picture: TIE-MAP)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 9 | Filing complete spec within 12 months of provisional | 12-month clock ticking\n- Section 11A | Publication after expiry of prescribed period | Public notice board\n- Section 11B | Request for examination within 6 months | Exam deadline bell\n- Section 12 | Examiner's report in 1–3 months | Report on a table\n- Section 15 | Controller disposes of report within 1 month | One-month countdown\n- Section 25 | Opposition within 1 year from grant | Year-long challenge\n- Section 84 | Compulsory licence application within 6 months or 1 year | Licence window opens\n- Section 60 | Restoration within 1 year of lapse | Lapse recovery time\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Missing deadlines can lead to abandonment or invalidation – always note the exact timeline.\n- Examiners expect precise section numbers and clear understanding of each deadline.\n- Use bullet points and timelines in answers for full marks.","storyEN":"**Story: The Clockwork of Invention in Hyderabad**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, where innovation hums through the air like a well-tuned engine, lived a young inventor named Ravi. He had just perfected a new kind of solar-powered water purifier and was determined to secure a patent for it.\n\nRavi’s first challenge came when he filed his **provisional application** under **Section 9** of *The Patents Act, 1970*. He knew that within **twelve months**, he had to submit a complete specification. If not, the application would be deemed abandoned — like a lamp left unlit in a dark room, never to shine again.\n\nHe set his calendar and marked off each day with care. On the twelfth month’s last day, Ravi submitted the complete specification just in time. He had narrowly avoided abandonment thanks to the **12-month rule** under Section 11 — a deadline as strict as a clockwork mechanism.\n\nNext came **Section 11B**, which required him to file a request for examination within six months of filing his application. Ravi made sure he submitted this by the due date, and soon after, an examiner was assigned to review his work. Under **Section 12**, the examiner had between one month and three months to complete their report — a window as brief as a passing train.\n\nWhen the report came back with questions about certain parts of the specification, Ravi responded promptly. The Controller then had just **one month** under **Section 15** to dispose of the matter. If he hadn’t acted quickly, the process could have stalled like a broken gear in a machine.\n\nOne year after the patent was granted, Ravi received a letter from a rival inventor named Arjun, who claimed that parts of Ravi’s invention were already known. This led to an **opposition** under **Section 25**, and Ravi had to defend his rights before the Controller — much like standing up in court for one's honor.\n\nLater, when Ravi’s patent was nearly expired, he considered applying for a **compulsory licence** under **Section 84**. He knew that such an application could only be made within six months of publication or one year from the grant date — a narrow window like a door opening just long enough to pass through.\n\nIf Ravi had missed any of these deadlines, his patent might have been revoked or abandoned. But thanks to careful planning and strict adherence to the timelines in *The Patents Act*, he secured his rights and ensured that his invention would be protected for **twenty years**, as outlined in **Section 53** — a long and steady journey like a river flowing from mountain to sea.\n\nIn Hyderabad, where dreams are built with precision and patience, Ravi became a symbol of how the law shapes innovation. He had learned that every number mattered — not just on paper, but in life itself.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Twelve months | Section 11 | A complete specification must be filed within twelve months of a provisional application; otherwise, it is deemed abandoned. |\n| Six months | Section 11B | A request for examination must be made within six months from the date of filing or within six months of the priority date. |\n| One month to three months | Section 12 | The examiner must submit their report within one month to three months after the application is referred. |\n| One month | Section 15 | The Controller must dispose of the examiner’s report within one month from receiving it. |\n| One year | Section 25 | Any person may oppose a patent within one year from the date of grant. |\n| Six months or one year | Section 84 | A compulsory licence application must be filed within six months of publication or one year from the grant, whichever is later. |\n| Twenty years | Section 53 | The term of a patent is twenty years from the date of filing the application. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for \"Timelines and Deadlines — The Complete Calendar\" from Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Mnemonics for Key Sections and Concepts**\n\n#### a) **Section 11A – Publication of Applications**\n- **Acronym:** *“PA” = “Publishing Always”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Imagine \"11\" as two hands raising a flag (signaling publication).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Publication vs. Examination*  \n  → *Publication happens before examination; it’s the first public step, while examination is a later review process.*\n\n#### b) **Section 25 – Opposition to the Patent**\n- **Acronym:** *“OP” = “Opposition Period”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Visualize \"25\" as two hands pushing against each other (opposition).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Opposition vs. Appeal*  \n  → *Opposition occurs before grant; appeal is a post-grant legal challenge.*\n\n#### c) **Section 84 – Compulsory Licences**\n- **Acronym:** *“CL” = “Compulsory Licence”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Picture \"84\" as a key (licensing is unlocking access).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Compulsory Licence vs. Voluntary Licence*  \n  → *Compulsory licences are mandated by law; voluntary ones are negotiated between parties.*\n\n#### d) **Section 12 – Examination of Application**\n- **Acronym:** *“EX” = “Examination X-ray”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Think of \"12\" as a clock (time for examination).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Examination vs. Search*  \n  → *Examination is the review process; search identifies prior art that may affect novelty.*\n\n#### e) **Section 30 – Anticipation by Communication to Government**\n- **Acronym:** *“AC” = “Anticipated Communication”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Visualize \"30\" as a letter (communication).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Communication vs. Publication*  \n  → *Communication is private; publication is public and affects novelty.*\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Mnemonics for Deadlines and Timelines**\n\n#### a) **Priority Date – Section 11B**\n- **Acronym:** *“PD” = “Prior Date”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Think of \"11\" as two arrows pointing to the past (priority).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Priority date vs. Filing date*  \n  → *Priority is based on a provisional application; filing date is when the main application was submitted.*\n\n#### b) **Request for Examination – Section 11B**\n- **Acronym:** *“REQ” = “Request Exam”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Picture \"11\" as a form being filled out (request).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Request for examination vs. Publication*  \n  → *Request is internal; publication is public.*\n\n#### c) **Time to Put Application in Order – Section 21**\n- **Acronym:** *“TPO” = “Time to Prepare Order”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Imagine \"21\" as a clock with an hourglass (time).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Time for order vs. Time for examination*  \n  → *Ordering is about preparing the application; examination is about reviewing it.*\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Mnemonics for Fees and Costs**\n\n#### a) **Fees under Fourth Schedule – Section 21**\n- **Acronym:** *“FES” = “Fee Entries Schedule”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Think of \"4\" as a diamond (schedule).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Startup fees vs. Natural person fees*  \n  → *Startups and small entities pay lower fees than natural persons or companies.*\n\n#### b) **Notice of Opposition – Section 25**\n- **Acronym:** *“NOP” = “Notice of Opposition”*\n- **Number-anchor:** *Visualize \"25\" as a red flag (opposition).*\n- **Confusion pair:** *Notice vs. Statement*  \n  → *Notice is the initial communication; statement is a formal response.*\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you quickly recall key timelines, deadlines, and procedures under the Patents Act, 1970, using visual and linguistic anchors based on the content from the Bare Act and textbook.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Twelve months from filing; Paris priority allows claiming priority from an earlier application.","question":"What is the timeline for a provisional application to become complete and how does it relate to Paris priority?"},{"answer":"Ordinarily one month; Rule 13(3) of The Patents Rules, 2003.","question":"What is the period for the Controller to dispose of the report of the examiner?"},{"answer":"Six months from the date of issuance; Rule 13(4) of The Patents Rules, 2003.","question":"How long does the applicant have to reply to the first statement of objections?"},{"answer":"Thirty-one months from the priority date; applicable to India and Europe.","question":"What is the deadline for entering the national phase in India under PCT?"},{"answer":"Ensures global disclosure; PCT Article 21 mandates publication within 18 months of priority.","question":"Why is the 18-month publication period important for international applications?"},{"answer":"One year from the grant date; Section 21 of The Patents Act, 1970.","question":"What is the timeframe for post-grant opposition in India?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 139 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Other provisions of Act to apply to convention applications”.","options":["Section 26","Section 139","Section 54","Section 135"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Other provisions of Act to apply to convention applications?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 139 is headed “Other provisions of Act to apply to convention applications”.","options":["Term of patents of addition","Time for putting application in order for grant","Other provisions of Act to apply to convention applications","Appeals to High Court"],"question":"Section 139 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Section 47","Section 139","Section 158","Section 25"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Opposition to the patent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 25 is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Practice by non-registered patent agents","Power of Controller to call for information from patentees","General principles applicable to working of patented inventions","Opposition to the patent"],"question":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 139 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Hearing before the Controller to be in public in certain”.","options":["Rule 91","Rule 63A","Rule 19N","Rule 139"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Hearing before the Controller to be in public in certain?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 139 is headed “Hearing before the Controller to be in public in certain”.","options":["Registration of patent agents","Transmittal of the International Preliminary Examination","Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)","Hearing before the Controller to be in public in certain"],"question":"Rule 139 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 84 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Compulsory licences”.","options":["Section 84","Section 143","Section 64","Section 93"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Compulsory licences?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 84 is headed “Compulsory licences”.","options":["Reports of Controller to be placed before Parliament","Compulsory licences","No anticipation if circumstances are only as described in","Appeals to High Court"],"question":"Section 84 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 84 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Restoration of patents”.","options":["Rule 84","Rule 138","Rule 105","Rule 81"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Restoration of patents?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 84 is headed “Restoration of patents”.","options":["Restoration of patents","Form of application to be made under section 28(7)","On form of 26 No fee No fee No fee No fee","Filing of reply evidence by opponent"],"question":"Rule 84 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 53 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Term of patent”.","options":["Section 49","Section 143","Section 72","Section 53"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Term of patent?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 53 is headed “Term of patent”.","options":["Appeals to High Court","Mention of inventor as such in patent","Term of patent","Surrender of patents"],"question":"Section 53 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"The text states that a complete specification must be filed within twelve months from the date of filing the application.","options":["a) Six months","b) Nine months","c) Twelve months","d) Eighteen months"],"question":"What is the time limit for filing a complete specification after a provisional specification under Section 9(1) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 11A explicitly addresses the publication of applications and the conditions under which they are published.","options":["a) Section 9","b) Section 11A","c) Section 11B","d) Section 25"],"question":"Under which section of the Patents Act, 1970 is the publication of patent applications governed?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 25(1) allows opposition if the invention was publicly known or used in India before the priority date.","options":["a) The invention was publicly used in India after the priority date","b) The invention was published in a foreign document before the priority date","c) The applicant has a history of patent infringements","d) The invention is not useful for industrial application"],"question":"Under Section 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is a valid ground for opposing a patent?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"The text states that such applications are published after the expiry of the period under Section 11A(1) or when the secrecy direction ceases, whichever is later.","options":["a) Immediately upon filing","b) After the expiry of the period under Section 11A(1)","c) When the secrecy direction ceases to operate","d) Both (b) and (c)"],"question":"According to Section 11A(3), when is a patent application published if a secrecy direction is imposed under Section 35?"}]},"s1p_50":{"notes":"# Numbers, Fees and Forms — Rapid Recall Sheet  \n*(For Patent Agent Examination: Papers I, II and Viva)*  \n\n---\n\n## 1. **Fees**  \n### Definition:  \nFees are the monetary charges payable to the Controller of Patents under the *Patents Rules, 2003*, for various procedural actions in a patent application process.\n\n### Key Concepts:\n- Fees must be paid in accordance with the *First Schedule* to the *Patents Rules, 2003*.  \n- **Rule 7(4)** of the Patents Rules, 2003 states that fees once paid are generally non-refundable unless certain conditions apply (e.g., if a fee was mistakenly paid more than once for the same proceeding).  \n- **Rule 7(4A)** allows for refund of examination fees in cases where an application is withdrawn before the first statement of objection.  \n\n### Textbook Explanation:\nAccording to *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 113)*, the payment of fees is a critical part of the patent process. The rules are designed to ensure that all applicants comply with procedural requirements and pay for services rendered by the Controller. This also helps in maintaining administrative efficiency within the Patent Office.\n\n### Cases:\n- **(No verified case on record for this topic - check IndianKanoon before citing one)**\n\n---\n\n## 2. **Forms**  \n### Definition:  \nForms are standardized documents required to be used by applicants and patent agents under the *Patents Rules, 2003* in order to perform specific actions or submit information.\n\n### Key Concepts:\n- Forms are specified in the *Second Schedule* of the *Patents Rules, 2003*.  \n- **Rule 8(1)** mandates that forms must be used as set forth in the Second Schedule. If no form is specified for a purpose, **Form 30** may be used (per Rule 8(2)).  \n\n### Textbook Explanation:\nAs per\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 142 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 142. Fees.\n> (1) There shall be paid in respect of the grant of patents and\n> applications therefor, and in respect of other matters in relation\n> to the grant of patents under this Act, such fees as may be\n> prescribed by the Central Government.\n>\n> (2) Where a fee is payable in respect of the doing of an act by the\n> Controller, the Controller shall not do that act until the fee has\n> been paid.\n>\n> (3) Where a fee is payable in respect of the filing of a document at\n> the patent office, the fee shall be paid along with the document or\n>\n>  106\n>\n> within the prescribed time and the document shall be deemed not to\n> have been filed at the office if the fee has not been paid within\n> such time.\n>\n> (4) Where a principal patent is granted later than two years from\n> the date of filing of the application, the fees which have become\n> due in the meantime may be paid within a term of three months from\n> the date of the recording of the patent in the register or within\n> the extended period not later than nine months from the date of\n> recording.\n\n**Rule 7 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 7. Fees\n> (1) The fees payable under section 142 in respect of the grant of\n> patents and applications therefor, and in respect of other matters\n> for which fees are required to be payable under the Act shall be as\n> specified in the First Schedule.\n>\n>  11\n>\n> Provided that ten per cent additional fee shall be payable when the\n> applications for patent and other documents are filed through\n> physical mode, namely, in hard copy format:\n> Provided further that in the case of a small entity, or startup, or\n> educational institution, every document for which a fee has been\n> specified shall be accompanied by Form 28.\n>\n> (2) (a) (a) The fees payable under the Act or these rules may be\n> paid at the appropriate office either in cash or through electronic\n> means or may be sent by bank draft or banker’s cheque payable to the\n> Controller of Patents and drawn on a scheduled bank at the place\n> where the appropriate office is situated and if the draft or\n> banker’s cheque is sent by post, the fees shall be deemed to have\n> been paid on the date on which the draft or banker’s cheque has\n> actually reached the Controller.\n> (b) omitted\n> (c) Where a fee is payable in respect of a document, the entire fee\n> shall accompany the document.\n>\n> (3) In case an application processed by a natural person, startup,\n> small entity or educational institution is fully or partly\n> transferred to a person other than a natural person, startup, small\n> entity or educational institution, the difference, if any, in the\n> scale of fees between the fees charged from the natural person,\n> startup, small entity or educational institution and the fees\n> chargeable from the person other than a natural person, startup,\n> small entity or educational institution, shall be paid by the new\n> applicant along with the request for transfer.\n>\n> Explanation: Where a startup or small entity, having filed an\n> application for a patent, ceases to be a startup or small entity due\n> to the lapse of the period during which it is recognized by the\n> competent authority, or its turnover subsequently crosses the\n> financial threshold limit as notified by the competent authority, no\n> such difference in the scale of fees shall be payable.]\n>\n> (3A) shall be omitted;\n>\n> (3B) shall be omitted;\n>\n> (4) Fees once paid in respect of any proceeding shall not ordinarily\n>\n>  12\n>\n> be refunded irrespective of whether the proceeding has taken place\n> or not:\n> Provided that, if the Controller is satisfied that during the online\n> filing process, the fee was paid more than once for the same\n> proceeding, the excess fee shall be refunded.\n>\n> (4A) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-rule (4), upon the\n> withdrawal of an application in respect of which a request for\n> examination has been filed, but before issuance of first statement\n> of objection, the fee may be refunded to the extent prescribed in\n> the First Schedule on a request made by the applicant in Form 29.\n>\n> (5) (i) Subject to the approval of the Controller, any person may\n> deposit money in advance and request the Controller to realise any\n> fee payable by him from the said deposit and in such case the date\n> of the receipt of the request to realise the fee or the date on\n> which the request to realise the fee is deemed to have been\n> received, whichever is earlier, shall be taken as the date of\n> payment of the fee:\n> Provided that the requisite amount of money is available at the\n> credit of the person making such request.\n> (ii) Subject to the approval of the Controller, any person may\n> discontinue the deposit of money in advance and in such case the\n> … [truncated]\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provisions of the Patents Rules, 2003 relating to the payment of fees by applicants under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the provisions in the *Patents Rules, 2003* concerning the payment of fees by applicants under the *Patents Act, 1970*, and what are the implications of non-payment or refund?\n\n**Rule:**  \nUnder **Section 142(1)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, the Central Government is empowered to prescribe fees for various acts related to patents. The specific procedural requirements regarding payment of fees are detailed in the *Patents Rules, 2003*. **Rule 7(4)** states that once a fee has been paid, it is generally non-refundable unless certain conditions apply, such as when a fee was mistakenly paid more than once for the same proceeding. Furthermore, **Rule 7(4A)** provides an exception allowing a refund of examination fees if the application is withdrawn before the first statement of objection.\n\nThese rules are supported by the textbook explanation in *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 113)*, which emphasizes that the payment of fees is essential to ensure compliance with procedural requirements and administrative efficiency within the Patent Office.\n\n**Application:**  \nIf an applicant pays a fee for a particular proceeding (e.g., filing a patent application), they must be aware that such payments are generally non-refundable. For instance, if an applicant mistakenly pays the examination fee twice for the same application, the Controller of Patents may not refund the excess amount unless there is clear evidence of an error in processing.\n\nHowever, **Rule 7(4A)** provides a specific exception: if an application is withdrawn before the first statement of objection, the applicant may be eligible to receive a refund of the examination fee. This provision recognizes that in such cases, the applicant has not yet incurred significant costs or administrative burden on the part of the Patent Office.\n\nThe rules also require that fees be paid along with documents filed at the patent office (as per **Section 142(3)**), and failure to do so may result in the document being deemed not filed. For example, if an applicant files a response to an objection but does not include the required fee, the Controller may consider the response as incomplete or non-compliant.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe *Patents Rules, 2003* provide clear guidelines on the payment of fees under the *Patents Act, 1970*. While most fees are non-refundable upon payment, exceptions exist in specific circumstances such as withdrawal before objection. These provisions ensure procedural compliance and administrative efficiency within the Patent Office.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often forget to mention **Rule 7(4A)** and the exception for refunding examination fees when an application is withdrawn before the first statement of objection. This point is critical in demonstrating a nuanced understanding of the rules.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the role and significance of forms under the Patents Rules, 2003.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** What is the role and significance of standardized forms required under the *Patents Rules, 2003*, and how are they governed?\n\n**Rule:**  \nUnder **Rule 8(1)** of the *Patents Rules, 2003*, all applicants and patent agents must use forms as specified in the *Second Schedule* of the rules. If no form is provided for a particular purpose, **Form 30** may be used (as per **Rule 8(2)**). These forms are standardized to ensure uniformity and clarity in communication with the Controller of Patents.\n\nThe textbook *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 113)* explains that these forms help maintain procedural consistency and reduce ambiguity in patent applications and related proceedings.\n\n**Application:**  \nFor example, if an applicant wishes to file a request for expedited examination of their patent application, they must use the form specified in the *Second Schedule* of the *Patents Rules, 2003*. If no such form exists, **Form 30**, which is a general-purpose form, may be used. This ensures that the Controller can process the request efficiently without confusion or delays.\n\nSimilarly, when an applicant needs to submit an objection or respond to an office action, they must use the appropriate form from the *Second Schedule*. Using incorrect forms could lead to rejection of documents by the Patent Office or delay in processing.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nStandardized forms under the *Patents Rules, 2003* play a crucial role in maintaining procedural uniformity and ensuring that all communications with the Controller are clear and consistent. They help prevent misunderstandings and streamline the patent application process.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students overlook the provision of **Form 30** as an alternative when no specific form is available, which is essential to understanding the full scope of the rules.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the implications of non-payment of fees under the Patents Act, 1970? Discuss with reference to relevant provisions.**  \n*10 marks · Osmania University · asked once (2023)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the legal consequences for an applicant who fails to pay required fees under the *Patents Act, 1970*, and what provisions govern these implications?\n\n**Rule:**  \nUnder **Section 142(3)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*, a fee must be paid along with any document filed at the patent office. If the fee is not paid within the prescribed time, the document is deemed not to have been filed. This provision ensures that all procedural actions are accompanied by the necessary financial obligations.\n\nThe textbook *(The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 113)* notes that non-payment of fees can result in significant delays or even rejection of applications if not corrected promptly.\n\n**Application:**  \nFor instance, suppose an applicant files a response to an office action but fails to pay the required fee. According to **Section 142(3)**, the Controller may consider the response as not having been filed at all, leading to possible adverse decisions against the application. If the applicant does not rectify this within the prescribed time, they may lose their right to proceed with the application.\n\nSimilarly, if an applicant delays payment of fees for a patent grant beyond two years from the date of filing (as per **Section 142(4)**), any overdue fees must be paid within three months from the recording of the patent in the register or within nine months at most. Failure to do so could result in the application being deemed abandoned.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nNon-payment of fees under the *Patents Act, 1970* has serious legal implications for applicants. It may lead to documents being treated as not filed and applications being considered abandoned if overdue fees are not paid within the prescribed time limits.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students fail to consider **Section 142(4)**, which allows an extended period of up to nine months for payment of delayed fees in certain circumstances. This provision is key to understanding the flexibility provided by the law.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Numbers, Fees and Forms — Rapid Recall Sheet\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 142 | **TIE-RHINO** | A tied rhino charges through form stacks, horns clashing with act titles. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Which form for which act - which provision?  (picture: TIE-RHINO)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Rule 7(4) Patents Rules, 2003 | Fees are generally non-refundable unless specific conditions apply | Money back only in special cases  \n- Rule 7(4A) Patents Rules, 2003 | Refund of examination fee if application is withdrawn early | Withdraw before objections, get refund  \n- Rule 8(1) Patents Rules, 2003 | Forms must be used as specified in the Second Schedule | Use only official forms for submissions  \n- Rule 8(2) Patents Rules, 2003 | Form 30 may be used if no specific form is available | Fallback form for missing documents  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid assuming refund eligibility – refer strictly to Rule 7(4) and Rule 7(4A)  \n- Examiners expect precise references to Rules and Schedules in answers  \n- Always mention the specific rule or schedule when discussing fees or forms for full marks","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where the old city meets the modern, a young patent agent named Arjun was preparing for his upcoming examination. He had been tasked with managing the case of Ravi, an inventor who had developed a unique water filtration system and needed to file a patent application.\n\nOne evening, as he sat in his small apartment overlooking the Hussain Sagar lake, he reviewed the *Rapid Recall Sheet* on **Numbers, Fees and Forms**. The numbers were not just abstract figures; they were keys that could unlock or block Ravi's path to securing a patent.\n\nThe first challenge came with **Rule 7(4)** of the *Patents Rules, 2003*. Arjun had to remember that once fees were paid, they were generally non-refundable. He imagined a scene where Ravi mistakenly sent two identical cheques for the same fee, and the Controller refused to refund the extra one. This was not just an oddity; it was the rule — **Rule 7(4)** clearly stated that unless specific conditions applied, such as when a fee was paid more than once by mistake, refunds would be denied.\n\nThen came **Rule 7(4A)**, which allowed for the refund of examination fees if Ravi decided to withdraw his application before the first statement of objection. Arjun saw this as a lifeline — like a door that could open just in time if Ravi changed his mind.\n\nAs he moved on to forms, he remembered **Rule 8(1)**, which required all applicants and agents to use forms specified in the *Second Schedule* of the *Patents Rules, 2003*. Arjun imagined a scene where Ravi tried to submit an application using a form that wasn’t listed, only for it to be rejected. But there was always **Form 30**, as per **Rule 8(2)**, which could serve as a last resort if no other form applied.\n\nArjun also recalled the importance of the *First Schedule* to the *Patents Rules, 2003*, where all fees were outlined. He pictured himself sitting at the patent office with a stack of documents and cheques, each one corresponding to a specific fee in that schedule. One slip — like paying too late or using the wrong form — could delay Ravi’s application for months.\n\nThe story came full circle when Arjun imagined **Section 142(3)** of the *Patents Act, 1970*. He saw it as a strict gatekeeper: if Ravi didn’t pay his fees along with the documents or within the prescribed time, the patent office would consider those documents never filed. It was like a clock ticking down — and failure to meet its deadline meant losing everything.\n\nAs he closed his notes that night, Arjun felt confident. The numbers were no longer abstract — they had become part of Ravi’s journey, guiding him through the labyrinth of patent law with precision and care.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Rule 7(4) | Fees once paid are generally non-refundable unless specific conditions apply. | Once fees are paid, they are typically non-refundable unless certain conditions like duplicate payments are met. |\n| Rule 7(4A) | Refund of examination fees is allowed if an application is withdrawn before the first statement of objection. | Examination fees may be refunded if the application is withdrawn prior to the first statement of objection. |\n| Rule 8(1) | Forms must be used as set forth in the Second Schedule of the Patents Rules, 2003. | Only forms specified in the Second Schedule can be used for patent-related actions. |\n| Rule 8(2) | If no form is specified for a purpose, Form 30 may be used. | In the absence of a specific form, **Form 30** serves as an acceptable alternative. |\n| First Schedule to Patents Rules, 2003 | Fees must be paid in accordance with this schedule. | All fees payable under the Patent Act are outlined in the *First Schedule* of the *Patents Rules, 2003*. |\n| Section 142(3) | Fees must be paid along with documents or within a prescribed time; otherwise, documents are deemed not filed. | If a fee is not paid with a document or within the specified period, that document is considered as never having been filed. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for \"Numbers, Fees and Forms — Rapid Recall Sheet\" from Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Fees and Refunds (Rule 7(4A))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **FRR** – *“Fee Refund Rule”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Rule 7** – Imagine a **$7 bill** being returned to you, symbolizing the refund of fees.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Refunds before and after first statement of objection*  \n  → Use this: **Before** the first statement of objection, **refunds are allowed**; **after**, they are not.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Form 3 – Statement and Undertaking (Rule 12(1))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **SUF** – *“Statement and Undertaking Form”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Form 3** – Picture a **triangle (3 sides)** with the word “Undertaking” on it.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Form 3 vs. Form 28*  \n  → Use this: **Form 3** is for **undertaking**, **Form 28** is for **MSME/Startup applications**.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Forms and Specifications (Rule 13(1))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **SPEC** – *“Specifications in Form 2”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Form 2** – Imagine a **spider (8 legs)** with the number **2** on its back.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Provisional vs. Complete Specification*  \n  → Use this: A **provisional spec** is like a **draft**, while a **complete spec** is the **final version**.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. **Form 29 – Refund Request (Rule 7(4A))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **RRF** – *“Refund Request Form”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Form 29** – Picture a **door with number 29**, and you’re asking to go back.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Form 29 vs. Form 3*  \n  → Use this: **Form 29** is for **refunds**, **Form 3** is for **undertaking**.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. **Qualifying Examination (Rule 110)**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **QEPV** – *“Qualifying Exam Paper and Viva”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Paper I & II** – Think of a **book with two pages**, one for **Patents Act**, the other for **Drafting Specs**.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Viva Voce vs. Written Test*  \n  → Use this: The **written test** is **paper-based**, while **viva voce** is an **oral exam**.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. **Form 22 – Application for Registration as Patent Agent (Rule 125(1))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **APAR** – *“Application for Patent Agent Registration”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Form 22** – Imagine a **pair of shoes (2 + 2)** being filled out.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Form 22 vs. Form 23*  \n  → Use this: **Form 22** is for **registration**, **Form 23** was used earlier but has been **replaced**.\n\n---\n\n#### 7. **Prior Art Search (Part A Question, Page 542)**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **PAS** – *“Prior Art Search”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Page 542** – Picture a **library with shelves numbered 542**, filled with **old books**.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Prior art vs. Patent specification*  \n  → Use this: **Prior art** is what’s already known, while the **specification** is your **new invention**.\n\n---\n\n#### 8. **Form 6 – Change in Applicant Name (Page 113)**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **CNA** – *“Change of Name Application”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Form 6** – Picture a **six-sided die**, symbolizing the change.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Form 6 vs. Form 13*  \n  → Use this: **Form 6** is for **name changes**, **Form 13** is for **amendments**.\n\n---\n\n#### 9. **NBA Permission (Page 113)**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **NBP** – *“National Biodiversity Permission”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **NBA Form III** – Picture a **tree with the number 3**, showing permission.\n- **Confusion pair**: *NBA vs. Patent Office*  \n  → Use this: **NBA** deals with **biological resources**, while the **Patent Office** handles **patents**.\n\n---\n\n#### 10. **Form 9 – Early Publication Request (Rule 24(A))**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **EPR** – *“Early Publication Request”*\n- **Number-anchor**: **Form 9** – Imagine a **clock with hands at 9**, showing early time.\n- **Confusion pair**: *Form 9 vs. Form 3*  \n  → Use this: **Form 9** is for **early publication**, **Form 3** is for **undertaking**.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to be concise, vivid and help in quick recall during the Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva).","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Form 2 is used for requesting an extension of time. It is specified in the FIRST SCHEDULE—FORMS under The Patents Rules 2003.","question":"Which form is used to apply for an extension of time?"},{"answer":"The delay condoned must not exceed the period between the expected and actual receipt dates of the document. This is outlined in rule 12(5) of The Patents Rules 2003.","question":"What is the time limit for condoning delay under sub-rule (5) of rule 12?"},{"answer":"A 10% additional fee is charged for physical filings as per the First Schedule, to encourage electronic filing and reduce administrative costs.","question":"Why is a 10% additional fee charged for physical filing?"},{"answer":"Form 3 is used for condoning delay in filing an appeal under Section 117A. It is specified in the FIRST SCHEDULE—FORMS under The Patents (Appeals and Applications) Rules 2011.","question":"Which form is used to condone delay in filing an appeal under Section 117A?"},{"answer":"The delay condonable under sub-rule (6) of rule 24B cannot exceed the national emergency period or six months from its expiry, whichever is earlier.","question":"What is the maximum delay condonable under sub-rule (6) of rule 24B?"},{"answer":"A statement of circumstances is required to justify the delay and demonstrate that the party acted as soon as reasonably possible, as per rule 12(5).","question":"Why is a statement of circumstances required for condoning delay?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 18 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Power of Controller in cases of anticipation”.","options":["Section 54","Section 18","Section 78","Section 149"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Power of Controller in cases of anticipation?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 18 is headed “Power of Controller in cases of anticipation”.","options":["Rights of patent agents","Power of Controller in cases of anticipation","Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats","Form of application"],"question":"Section 18 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 117 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Restoration of name of persons removed from the register”.","options":["Rule 101","Rule 117","Rule 129","Rule 111"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Restoration of name of persons removed from the register?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 117 is headed “Restoration of name of persons removed from the register”.","options":["Leaving and serving documents","Constitution of Opposition Board and its proceeding","Restoration of name of persons removed from the register","Manner in which a claim under section 20(1) shall be made"],"question":"Rule 117 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 137 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Powers of Controller generally”.","options":["Rule 19K","Rule 10","Rule 100","Rule 137"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Powers of Controller generally?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 137 is headed “Powers of Controller generally”.","options":["Form of application for the issue of a duplicate patent","Disqualifications for inclusion in the roll of scientific","Permission for making patent application outside India","Powers of Controller generally"],"question":"Rule 137 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 25 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Identification of published applications”.","options":["Rule 60","Rule 133","Rule 4","Rule 25"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Identification of published applications?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 25 is headed “Identification of published applications”.","options":["Identification of published applications","Manner in which a claim under section 20(1) shall be made","Publication of application","Particulars of the qualifying examination for patent"],"question":"Rule 25 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 94 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Termination of compulsory licence”.","options":["Section 94","Section 92A","Section 106","Section 153"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Termination of compulsory licence?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 94 is headed “Termination of compulsory licence”.","options":["Powers of Controller in case of potential infringement","Termination of compulsory licence","Disposal by Controller of applications for extension of time","Rights of patentees of lapsed patents which have been restored"],"question":"Section 94 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 93 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Order for licence to operate as a deed between parties”.","options":["Section 144","Section 118","Section 93","Section 154"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Order for licence to operate as a deed between parties?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 93 is headed “Order for licence to operate as a deed between parties”.","options":["Subscription and verification of certain documents by patent","Powers of Controller to correct clerical errors, etc","Order for licence to operate as a deed between parties","Disposal by Controller of applications for extension of time"],"question":"Section 93 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 142(1) states that fees are prescribed by the Central Government.","options":["a) The Controller","b) The Patent Office","c) The Central Government","d) The State Government"],"question":"Who prescribes the fees related to the grant of patents under the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 142(2) explicitly states that the Controller cannot perform the act until the fee is paid.","options":["a) The Controller may proceed with the act after 15 days","b) The Controller must perform the act immediately","c) The Controller shall not do that act until the fee has been paid","d) The Controller may waive the fee"],"question":"What is the consequence if a fee is not paid before the Controller performs an act?"}]},"s1p_51":{"notes":"# Landmark Indian Patent Decisions a Candidate Must Know  \n*(Based on the \"Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)\" syllabus)*  \n\n---\n\n## 1. **Definition of Patent Agent**  \nA patent agent is a professional who is authorized to draft and file patent applications on behalf of an applicant. Their name must be registered in the register of patent agents maintained by the Patent Office.\n\n### Key Concepts:  \n- **Eligibility**: A person without a law degree can also become a patent agent.  \n- **Role**: They may prepare documents, transact business before the Controller, and give advice on matters other than scientific or technical ones regarding the validity of patents or their infringement (Section 127 and Section 129).  \n\n### Explanation from Textbook:  \nAccording to *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45*, a patent agent plays an essential role in the complex process of filing and prosecuting patents. They are increasingly important due to the growing complexity of patent applications and the need for professional efficiency.\n\n---\n\n## 2. **Publication of Patent Applications**  \nA patent application is automatically published after 18 months from the date of priority or first filing, whichever is earlier (Section 11A).  \n\n### Key Concepts:  \n- **Purpose**: To allow public scrutiny before a patent is granted.  \n- **Request for Early Publication**: An applicant may request early publication by submitting Form 9 under Rule 24A of the Patents Act.\n\n[Note: output was cut short here — the model began repeating itself. Regenerate this chapter.]\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 3 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 3. What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within\n> the meaning of this Act,\n> (a) an invention which is frivolous or which claims anything\n> obviously contrary to well established natural laws;\n> (b) an invention the primary or intended use or commercial\n> exploitation of which would be contrary to public order or morality\n> or which causes serious prejudice to human, animal or plant life or\n> health or to the environment;\n> (c) the mere discovery of a scientific principle or the formulation\n> of an abstract theory or discovery of any living thing or non-living\n> substance occurring in nature;\n> (d) the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does\n> not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that\n> substance or the mere discovery of any new property or new use for a\n> known substance or of the mere use of a known process, machine or\n> apparatus unless such known process results in a new product or\n> employs at least one new reactant.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, salts, esters, ethers,\n> polymorphs, metabolites, pure form, particle size, isomers, mixtures\n> of isomers, complexes, combinations and other derivatives of known\n> substance shall be considered to be the same substance, unless they\n> differ significantly in properties with regard to efficacy;\n>\n> (e) a substance obtained by a mere admixture resulting only in the\n> aggregation of the properties of the components thereof or a process\n> for producing such substance;\n> (f) the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known\n> devices each functioning independently of one another in a known\n> way;\n> (h) a method of agriculture or horticulture;\n> (i) any process for the medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic\n> diagnostic, therapeutic or other treatment of human beings or any\n> process for a similar treatment of animals to render them free of\n> disease or to increase their economic value or that of their\n> products.\n> (j) plants and animals in whole or any part thereof other than\n> micro-organisms but including seeds, varieties and species and\n> essentially biological processes for production or propagation of\n>\n>  11\n>\n> plants and animals;\n> (k) a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se\n> or algorithms;\n> (l) a literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work or any other\n> aesthetic creation whatsoever including cinematographic works and\n> television productions;\n> (m) a mere scheme or rule or method of performing mental act or\n> method of playing game;\n> (n) a presentation of information;\n> (o) topography of integrated circuits;\n> (p) an invention which, in effect, is traditional knowledge or which\n> is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of\n> traditionally known component or components.\n\n**Section 2 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 2. Definitions and interpretation.\n> (1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal\n> representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee\n> of any person include references to the assignee of the legal\n> representative or assignee of that person;\n> (aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the\n> International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the\n> purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April,\n> 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;\n> (ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an\n> invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used\n> in an industry;\n> (b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs\n> and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;\n> (c) “convention application” means an application for a patent made\n> by virtue of section 135;\n> (d) “convention country\" means a country or a country which is\n> member of a group of countries or a union of countries or an Inter-\n> governmental organisation referred to as a convention country in\n> section 133;\n> (e) “district court” has the meaning assigned to that expression by\n> the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908);\n> (f) “exclusive licence” means a licence from a patentee which\n> confers on the licensee, or on the licensee and persons authorised\n> by him, to the exclusion of all other persons (including the\n>\n>  8\n>\n> patentee), any right in respect of the patented invention, and\n> “exclusive licensee” shall be construed accordingly;\n> (h) “Government undertaking” means any industrial undertaking\n> carried on\n> (i) by a department of the Government, or\n> (ii) by a corporation established by a Central, Provincial or State\n> Act, which is owned or controlled by the Government, or\n> (iii) by a Government company as defined in section 617 of the\n> Companies Act, 1956 (1 of\n> 1956), or\n> 1(iv) by an institution wholly or substantially financed by the\n> Government;\n> (i) “High Court\", in relation to a State or Union territory, means\n> the High Court having territorial jurisdiction in that State or\n> Union territory, as the case may be;\n> (ia) “international application” means an application for patent\n> made in accordance with the Patent Cooperation Treaty;\n> (j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an\n> inventive step and capable of industrial application;\n> (ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves\n> technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having\n> economic significance or both and that makes the invention not\n> obvious to a person skilled in the art;\n> (k) “legal representative” means a person who in law represents the\n> estate of a deceased person;\n> (l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not\n> been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the\n> country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of\n> patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject\n> matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part\n> of the state of the art;\n> (la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under\n> sub-section (3) of section 25;\n> (m) “patent” means a patent for any invention granted under this\n> Act;\n> (n) “patent agent” means a person for the time being registered\n> under this Act as a patent agent;\n> (o) “patented article” and “patented process” mean respectively an\n> article or process in respect of which a patent is in force;\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 84 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 84. Compulsory licences.\n> (1) At any time after the expiration of three years from the date of\n> the grant of patent, any person interested may make an application\n>\n>  68\n>\n> to the Controller for grant of compulsory licence on patent on any\n> of the following grounds, namely:\n> (a) that the reasonable requirements of the public with respect to\n> the patented invention have not been satisfied, or\n> (b) that the patented invention is not available to the public at a\n> reasonably affordable price, or (c) that the patented invention is\n> not worked in the territory of India.\n>\n> (2) An application under this section may be made by any person\n> notwithstanding that he is already the holder of a licence under the\n> patent and no person shall be estopped from alleging that the\n> reasonable requirements of the public with respect to the patented\n> invention are not satisfied or that the patented invention is not\n> worked in the territory of India or that the patented invention is\n> not available to the public at a reasonably affordable price by\n> reason of any admission made by him, whether in such a licence or\n> otherwise or by reason of his having accepted such a licence.\n>\n> (3) Every application under sub-section (1) shall contain a\n> statement setting out the nature of the applicant’s interest\n> together with such particulars as may be prescribed and the facts\n> upon which the application is based.\n>\n> (4) The Controller, if satisfied that the reasonable requirements of\n> the public with respect to the patented invention have not been\n> satisfied or that the patented invention is not worked in the\n> territory of India or that the patented invention is not available\n> to the public at a reasonably affordable price, may grant a licence\n> upon such terms as he may deem fit.\n>\n> (5) Where the Controller directs the patentee to grant a licence he\n> may, as incidental thereto, exercise the powers set out in section\n\n**Rule 96 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 96. Application for compulsory licence etc.\n> An application to the Controller for an order under section 84,\n> section 85, section 91 or section 92 or section 92A shall be in Form\n> 17, or Form 19, as the case may be. Except in the case of an\n> application made by the Central Government, the application shall\n> set out the nature of the applicant’s interest and terms and\n> conditions of the licence the applicant is willing to accept.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Define and explain the role of a patent agent under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 3× (2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** What is the definition and scope of duties of a patent agent as per Indian law?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 127** and **Section 129** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, a patent agent is authorized to draft and file patent applications on behalf of an applicant. Their role includes preparing documents, transacting business before the Controller, and giving advice on matters other than scientific or technical ones regarding the validity of patents or their infringement. A patent agent must be registered in the register maintained by the Patent Office, as per **Section 127**.\n\nIn *The Patents Act 1970 (Commercial's) and Dr. Sheetal Chopra, p. 45*, it is emphasized that a patent agent plays an essential role due to the growing complexity of patent applications and the need for professional efficiency. The eligibility criteria do not require a law degree, which makes this profession accessible to a broader range of professionals.\n\n**Application:** A patent agent acts as a representative of the applicant before the Controller of Patents. They are responsible for ensuring that all procedural requirements under the Act are met, including drafting and filing applications, responding to office actions, and managing oppositions or revocation proceedings where necessary. However, they are not permitted to provide technical advice on the invention itself but may offer legal and procedural guidance.\n\n**Conclusion:** A patent agent is a registered professional who assists applicants in navigating the complexities of the patent application process under *The Patents Act, 1970*. Their role includes drafting, filing, and managing patent applications before the Controller, as well as providing non-technical legal advice on matters related to patents.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Many students confuse the roles of a patent agent with that of an attorney or a technical expert. It is crucial to emphasize that while they can provide legal and procedural assistance, they are not permitted to offer scientific or technical opinions regarding the invention itself.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Discuss the provisions relating to the publication of patent applications under the Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*12 marks · Osmania University · asked 2× (2019, 2021)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the legal provisions governing the publication of patent applications in India?\n\n**Rule:** Under **Section 11A** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, a patent application is automatically published after 18 months from the date of priority or first filing, whichever is earlier. The purpose of this provision is to allow public scrutiny before a patent is granted, ensuring transparency and enabling prior art searches.\n\nAn applicant may request early publication by submitting **Form 9** under **Rule 24A** of *The Patents Act*. This rule provides the procedural mechanism for such requests, allowing applicants who wish to disclose their invention earlier than the standard 18-month period to do so.\n\n**Application:** Suppose an inventor files a patent application on January 1, 2023. The application will be published automatically on July 1, 2024 (18 months later). However, if the applicant wishes to publish it earlier, say on April 1, 2023, they can submit **Form 9** under **Rule 24A**, which would trigger early publication.\n\n**Conclusion:** The automatic publication of patent applications after 18 months is a key feature of *The Patents Act, 1970*, aimed at promoting transparency and public scrutiny. Early publication may be requested by the applicant using **Form 9** under **Rule 24A**, which offers flexibility in managing disclosure timelines.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often overlook the provision for early publication under **Rule 24A**. It is important to highlight both the automatic and optional mechanisms of publication as per the Act.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the provisions relating to non-patentable inventions under Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*15 marks · Osmania University · asked 4× (2016, 2018, 2020, 2022)*\n\n**Issue:** What are the categories of inventions that cannot be patented under *The Patents Act, 1970*?\n\n**Rule:** **Section 3** of *The Patents Act, 1970* explicitly lists what is not considered an invention for patent purposes. These include:\n\n- (a) Inventions that are frivolous or contrary to natural laws (**Section 3(a)**).\n- (b) Inventions whose use would be against public order or morality, or cause serious harm to life, health, or the environment (**Section 3(b)**).\n- (c) Mere scientific principles, abstract theories, or discoveries of naturally occurring substances (**Section 3(c)**).\n- (d) New forms of known substances that do not enhance efficacy (**Section 3(d)**).\n- (e) Inventions resulting from mere admixture of components (**Section 3(e)**).\n- (f) Mere arrangement or duplication of devices (**Section 3(f)**).\n- (h) Methods of agriculture or horticulture (**Section 3(h)**).\n- (i) Medical treatments for humans and animals (**Section 3(i)**).\n- (j) Plants, animals, and biological processes for their production (**Section 3(j)**).\n- (k) Mathematical methods or computer programs per se (**Section 3(k)**).\n- (l) Literary or artistic works (**Section 3(l)**).\n- (m) Schemes, rules, or mental acts (**Section 3(m)**).\n- (n) Presentation of information (**Section 3(n)**).\n- (o) Topography of integrated circuits (**Section 3(o)**).\n- (p) Inventions that are traditional knowledge or merely aggregate known properties (**Section 3(p)**).\n\nIn *Novartis AG v. Union of India, (2013) 6 SCC 1*, the Supreme Court affirmed that **Section 3(d)** requires an enhancement in efficacy for new forms of known substances to be patentable.\n\n**Application:** Suppose a pharmaceutical company files a patent application for a new salt form of an existing drug that does not improve its therapeutic effect. Under **Section 3(d)**, this would not qualify as a patentable invention unless it demonstrates enhanced efficacy.\n\n**Conclusion:** *The Patents Act, 1970* explicitly excludes various categories from being patented under **Section 3**, including scientific principles, medical treatments, and computer programs. These exclusions are aimed at ensuring that only truly innovative inventions receive protection.\n\n**Examiner's eye:** Students often fail to distinguish between the different sub-clauses of **Section 3**. It is important to clearly explain each category with examples from cases like *Novartis AG v. Union of India* and *Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries, (1979) 2 SCC 511*.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Landmark Indian Patent Decisions a Candidate Must Know\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 3(d) | **HAM** + clause (d) | HAMmers clash over drug patents, testing therapeutic efficacy |\n| Sec. 2(1)(ja) | **HEN** + clause (1)(ja) | HENs peck at obviousness, scratching patent tests |\n| Sec. 84 | **FIRE** | FIREs blaze under compulsory licenses, burning patent barriers |\n| Sec. 3(k) | **HAM** + clause (k) | HAM smacks a keyboard, coding a patent |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **2** - definitions\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n- **82-94** - working of patents, compulsory licences\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## 3. Case cards\n\n### Novartis AG v. Union of India\n_(2013) 6 SCC 1_\n\n- **Held** - Therapeutic efficacy is the test under Section 3(d).\n\n### Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries\n_(1979) 2 SCC 511_\n\n- **Hook** - Bishwanath's HEN pecks at obviousness, novelty test\n- **Held** - The obviousness and novelty standard in Indian patent law\n- **Use in** - Novelty and obviousness assessments\n\n### Bayer Corporation v. Union of India\n_2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963_\n\n- **Held** - The first Indian compulsory licence upheld under Section 84.\n\n### Ferid Allani v. Union of India\n_2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867_\n\n- **Held** - Technical effect saves a computer-related invention from Section 3(k).\n\n### Dimminaco A.G. v. Controller of Patents and Designs\n_2002 SCC OnLine Cal 133_\n\n- **Hook** - Dimminaco's process births living products, patentable\n- **Held** - A process yielding a living end product is patentable\n- **Use in** - Patentable processes in biotechnology\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Section 3(d) and pharmaceutical patenting - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n2. The obviousness standard - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n3. Compulsory licensing in practice - which provision?  (picture: FIRE)\n4. Computer-related inventions - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n5. Which case decided: Therapeutic efficacy is the test under Section 3(d).\n6. Which case decided: The obviousness and novelty standard in Indian patent law\n7. Which case decided: The first Indian compulsory licence upheld under Section 84.\n8. Which case decided: Technical effect saves a computer-related invention from Section 3(k).\n9. Which case decided: A process yielding a living end product is patentable\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 3 | Lists what is not an invention, like traditional knowledge or frivolous ideas | \"No patent for old ideas\"\n- Section 11A | Publishes patent applications after 18 months | \"Wait 18 months for public view\"\n- Section 2(j) | Defines invention as new and industrially applicable | \"New idea with use\"\n- Section 84 | Allows compulsory licences under certain conditions | \"Public needs override patents\"\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013) | Therapeutic efficacy is key for patentability | \"Drug must work to win patent\"\n- Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries (1979) | Obviousness and novelty are central | \"New idea, not obvious\"\n- Bayer Corporation v. Union of India (2014) | First compulsory licence under Section 84 | \"Patent forced for public good\"\n- Ferid Allani v. Union of India (2019) | Technical effect saves computer inventions | \"Code with real impact\"\n- Dimminaco A.G. v. Controller of Patents and Designs (2002) | Process creating living product is patentable | \"Living result from process\"\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid confusion between novelty and inventive step — they are different.\n- Use exact section numbers when discussing legal provisions.\n- Always tie case law to the relevant sections of the Patents Act.","storyEN":"**Story: The Patent of Hyderabad**\n\nIn the heart of Hyderabad, where the old city meets the new, lived a young man named **Ravi**, who had dreams as vast as the Golconda Fort. He was not an inventor himself but a **patent agent**, and he had just secured his place in the register maintained by the **Patent Office** under **Section 127** — a title that came with great responsibility.\n\nOne day, Ravi received a call from **Dimminaco A.G.**, a name that echoed like a distant bell. He was asked to help file a patent for a process that yielded a living end product. The case had already reached the **Delhi High Court** and was known as **Ferid Allani v. Union of India, 2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867**, where it had been decided that if a computer-related invention has a **technical effect**, it is not excluded by **Section 3(k)**. Ravi knew this well and worked with the team to ensure clarity in the application.\n\nMeanwhile, in another corner of the city, an old man named **Bishwanath** was preparing for his final days. He had once been involved in a case that shaped Indian patent law — **Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries, (1979) 2 SCC 511**, where the court laid down the standard of **obviousness and novelty**. This was not just a memory; it was a legacy he wanted to pass on.\n\nAs Ravi worked late into the night, he received news that **Novartis AG v. Union of India, (2013) 6 SCC 1**, had been brought up again in the Supreme Court. The case had tested the limits of **Section 3(d)** — a provision that says an invention must show **therapeutic efficacy** to be patentable. Ravi knew this well and was reminded once more how crucial it was for patent agents like him to understand these nuances.\n\nThen came the call from **Bayer Corporation**, which had recently been granted its first compulsory licence under **Section 84** of the Patents Act, in a case known as **Bayer Corporation v. Union of India, 2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963**. The court had ruled that if the public's needs were not met or the price was unreasonable, the Controller could grant such a licence. Ravi felt the weight of this decision — it reminded him why he had chosen his path.\n\nOne day, as Ravi walked through the old city, he passed by **Section 2**, where in the textbook on page 45, it said that patent agents are vital for the complex process of filing patents. He paused and thought about how far he had come — from a boy with no law degree to a professional who could advise on matters other than scientific or technical ones, as stated under **Section 129**.\n\nAs the sun set over Hyderabad, Ravi looked at his work and felt proud. He knew that every number in the Patents Act was not just a rule but a story waiting to be told — from **Section 3**, which lists what is not an invention, to **Section 84**, where compulsory licences are granted for the public good.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Ravi's registration under Section 127 | Section 127 of the Patents Act, 1970 | A patent agent must be registered in the register maintained by the Patent Office. |\n| Dimminaco A.G.'s case | **Ferid Allani v. Union of India, 2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867** | A computer-related invention is not excluded if it has a technical effect under Section 3(k). |\n| Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam's legacy | **Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries, (1979) 2 SCC 511** | The standard of obviousness and novelty in Indian patent law is clear and strict. |\n| Novartis AG's case | **Novartis AG v. Union of India, (2013) 6 SCC 1** | Therapeutic efficacy is the test for granting a patent under Section 3(d). |\n| Bayer Corporation’s compulsory licence | **Bayer Corporation v. Union of India, 2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963** | A compulsory licence may be granted if public needs are not met or prices are unreasonable under Section 84. |\n| Ravi's work on Section 2 | Section 2 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Patent agents play an essential role in filing and prosecuting patents due to their professional efficiency. |\n| Ravi’s understanding of Section 3(d) | Section 3(d) of the Patents Act, 1970 | An invention must show therapeutic efficacy to be patentable. |\n| The legacy of Section 84 | Section 84 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Compulsory licences may be granted after three years from the grant of a patent if public needs are not met or prices are unreasonable. |\n| Ravi’s knowledge under Section 129 | Section 129 of the Patents Act, 1970 | Patent agents can give advice on matters other than scientific or technical ones regarding the validity of patents or their infringement. |","memory":"### **Landmark Indian Patent Decisions – Memory Techniques**\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013) 6 SCC 1**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **T**est for **E**fficacy under **S**ection **3(d)** → **TESD**\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 3(d)** – Think of a **\"drug\"** being tested in a **lab (3)** with a **dose (d)**.\n- **Confusion Pair**: *Therapeutic efficacy vs. chemical efficacy*  \n  > Use the acronym **TESD** to remember that **therapeutic**, not just **chemical**, efficacy is required under Section 3(d).\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Bishwanath Prasad Radhey Shyam v. Hindustan Metal Industries (1979) 2 SCC 511**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **O**bviousness and **N**ovelty standard → **ON**\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 2(1)(j)** – Picture a **\"new\" (novelty)** invention being **\"obvious\"** to an **expert (2)** in the field.\n- **Confusion Pair**: *Novelty vs. non-obviousness*  \n  > Remember: **Novelty is about newness**, while **non-obviousness is about not being obvious** to a skilled person.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. **Bayer Corporation v. Union of India (2014 SCC OnLine Bom 963)**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **F**irst **C**ompulsory **L**icence → **FCL**\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 84(1)** – Think of a **\"first\" (8)** **\"licence\" (4)** being issued under **compulsory conditions**.\n- **Confusion Pair**: *Compulsory licence vs. voluntary licence*  \n  > A **compulsory licence** is **granted by the Controller**, while a **voluntary licence** is **agreed upon by parties**.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. **Ferid Allani v. Union of India (2019 SCC OnLine Del 11867)**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **T**echnical **E**ffect saves **C**omputer invention → **TEC**\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 3(k)** – Picture a **\"technical effect\" (k)** being the key to saving a **computer-related (3)** invention.\n- **Confusion Pair**: *Computer programs vs. technical processes*  \n  > A **program** is not patentable unless it has a **technical effect**, which makes it different from pure software.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. **Dimminaco A.G. v. Controller of Patents and Designs (2002 SCC OnLine Cal 133)**\n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: **P**rocess with **L**iving end product → **PL**\n- **Number-anchor**: **Section 3(a)** – Imagine a **\"process\" (3)** producing a **\"living\" (a)** result.\n- **Confusion Pair**: *Processes vs. methods of treatment*  \n  > A **process** that yields a **living** end product is patentable, but a **method of treatment** may not be under certain conditions.\n\n---\n\nThese memory techniques are designed to help you recall the key points of each landmark case and their relevance in Indian patent law with ease and accuracy.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Section 3(d) requires proof of enhanced therapeutic efficacy, not just improved bioavailability. The Supreme Court in Novartis AG v. Union of India emphasized that physico-chemical properties alone are insufficient.","question":"What does Section 3(d) require for pharmaceutical inventions?"},{"answer":"The obviousness standard requires that an invention is not obvious to a person skilled in the art. This is assessed using the 'problem-solution' approach, as clarified in the Ericsson vs. Intex case.","question":"What is the standard for obviousness in Indian patent law?"},{"answer":"A compulsory license may be granted if the patented invention is not worked in India or if it is not available at a reasonable price. This is outlined in Section 84 of the Patents Act, 1970.","question":"Under what conditions can a compulsory license be granted?"},{"answer":"Computer-related inventions must demonstrate a technical contribution or effect beyond mere software. The Delhi High Court in Ericsson vs. Intex clarified that 'per se' excludes standalone programs.","question":"What criteria must computer-related inventions meet to be patentable?"},{"answer":"Cite the case with the name of the parties, year, and court. For example: 'Ericsson vs. Intex (2015), Delhi High Court.' This format ensures clarity and proper reference.","question":"How should a case be cited in a descriptive patent answer?"},{"answer":"A compulsory license application must be filed within 12 months of the patent grant. This is specified under Section 84(1) of the Patents Act, 1970.","question":"What is the time limit for filing a compulsory license application?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Rule 121","Rule 138","Rule 3","Rule 124"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Prescribed particulars?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 3 is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Manner in which a request may be made under section 20(4)","Manner of application under section 51(2)","Prescribed particulars","On form of 26 No fee No fee No fee No fee"],"question":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 84 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Compulsory licences”.","options":["Section 45","Section 35","Section 84","Section 6"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Compulsory licences?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 84 is headed “Compulsory licences”.","options":["Amendment of specification before Appellate Board or High Court","Exercise of discretionary powers by Controller","Persons entitled to apply for patents","Compulsory licences"],"question":"Section 84 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 84 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Restoration of patents”.","options":["Rule 23","Rule 114","Rule 37","Rule 84"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Restoration of patents?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 84 is headed “Restoration of patents”.","options":["Restoration of patents","Inclusion of the name of any other person in the roll of","Further evidence to be left with the leave of the","Manner of publication of the revocation order"],"question":"Rule 84 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Section 94","Section 3","Section 52","Section 91"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 3 is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Falsification of entries in register, etc","Anticipation by public display, etc","Anticipation by previous publication","What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within"],"question":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Section 19","Section 11","Section 2","Section 153"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Definitions and interpretation?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 2 is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Reference to High Court of disputes as to use for purposes of","Definitions and interpretation","Inventions relating to atomic energy not patentable","Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical"],"question":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 3(h) explicitly excludes methods of agriculture or horticulture from being considered inventions.","options":["A method of agriculture or horticulture","A new chemical compound with enhanced efficacy","A process for manufacturing a patented drug","A computer program with novel user interface features"],"question":"Which of the following is explicitly excluded from being considered an invention under Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 3(d) excludes the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance that does not result in enhanced efficacy.","options":["A new form of a known substance that enhances its efficacy","A new use for a known substance","A new property of a known substance","A mere discovery of a new form of a known substance without efficacy enhancement"],"question":"Which of the following is not considered an invention under Section 3(d) of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 84(1) allows compulsory licences if the invention is not available to the public at a reasonably affordable price.","options":["The invention is not available at a reasonably affordable price","The patentee has filed an application for international patent","The invention is a computer program per se","The invention involves traditional knowledge"],"question":"What is the primary ground for granting a compulsory licence under Section 84(1) of the Patents Act, 1970?"}]},"s1p_52":{"notes":"## The structure  \nA patent specification must be divided into clear, numbered sections:  \n1. **Title**: A concise, technical name for the invention.  \n2. **Field of the Invention**: A brief description of the technical area.  \n3. **Background of the Invention**:  \n   - **Prior Art**: Summary of existing technologies, including prior art references (e.g., \"US 2015/0342567 A1\" or \"WO 2018/123456 A1\").  \n   - **Problems in the Art**: Specific issues with existing solutions.  \n4. **Summary of the Invention**: A high-level overview of the invention’s purpose and advantages.  \n5. **Detailed Description**:  \n   - **Embodiments**: Step-by-step explanations of how to implement the invention.  \n   - **Drawings**: References to figures (e.g., \"Figure 1 shows...\").  \n6. **Claims**: Numbered, hierarchical claims defining the scope of protection.  \n7. **Abstract**: A concise summary of the invention’s core features.  \n\n---\n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Title**: Write a clear, technical title (e.g., \"Biodegradable Fish Feed Pellet with Coconut Oil for Extended Disintegration Time\").  \n2. **Field of the Invention**: State the technical domain (e.g., \"The present invention relates to fish feed formulations, particularly to improved disintegration properties in aquatic environments\").  \n3. **Background of the Invention**:  \n   - List prior art references (e.g., \"US 2015/0342567 A1 discloses fish feed with oil additives\").  \n   - Identify problems (e.g., \"Conventional formulations disintegrate too quickly, reducing nutrient absorption\").  \n4. **Summary of the Invention**: Explain the invention’s purpose (e.g., \"The invention introduces coconut oil to extend disintegration time by 20%\").  \n5. **Detailed Description**:  \n   - Describe embodiments (e.g., \"10% coconut oil by weight is added to the pellet matrix\").  \n   - Reference drawings (e.g., \"Figure 1 illustrates the disintegration curve of the invention vs. prior art\").  \n6. **Claims**: Write hierarchical claims (e.g., \"Claim 1: A fish feed pellet comprising 5–15% coconut oil by weight\").  \n7. **Abstract**: Summarize the invention (e.g., \"A fish feed formulation with coconut oil for prolonged disintegration in water\").  \n\n---\n\n## Worked example  \n**Title**: Biodegradable Fish Feed Pellet with Coconut Oil for Extended Disintegration Time  \n**Field of the Invention**: The present invention relates to fish feed formulations, particularly to improved disintegration properties in aquatic environments.  \n**Background of the Invention**:  \n- **Prior Art**: US 2015/0342567 A1 discloses fish feed with oil additives.  \n- **Problems in the Art**: Conventional formulations disintegrate too quickly, reducing nutrient absorption.  \n**Summary of the Invention**: The invention introduces coconut oil to extend disintegration time by 20%.  \n**Detailed Description**:  \n- **Embodiments**: 10% coconut oil by weight is added to the pellet matrix.  \n- **Drawings**: Figure 1 illustrates the disintegration curve of the invention vs. prior art.  \n**Claims**:  \n1. A fish feed pellet comprising 5–15% coconut oil by weight.  \n2. The pellet of claim 1, wherein the coconut oil is derived from Cocos nucifera.  \n**Abstract**: A fish feed formulation with coconut oil for prolonged disintegration in water.  \n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes  \n- **Error**: Omitting prior art references in the specification.  \n  **Correction**: Include relevant prior art (e.g., \"US 2015/0342567 A1\") to demonstrate awareness of the field.  \n- **Error**: Vague claims (e.g., \"a better fish feed\").  \n  **Correction**: Use specific, measurable terms (e.g., \"a fish feed pellet comprising 10% coconut oil by weight\").  \n- **Error**: Not aligning drawings with the detailed description.  \n  **Correction**: Reference figures explicitly (e.g., \"Figure 1 shows the disintegration curve\").  \n\n---\n\n## Practise this  \n**Task**: Draft a patent specification for a \"Biodegradable Fish Feed Pellet with Coconut Oil for Extended Disintegration Time\" in 30 minutes.  \n**Check against**:  \n- **Structure**: Ensure all sections (title, field, background, summary, detailed description, claims, abstract) are present.  \n- **Prior Art**: Include at least two prior art references.  \n- **Claims**: Write three hierarchical claims.  \n- **Drawings**: Reference a figure (e.g., \"Figure 1 illustrates...\").  \n- **Clarity**: Use technical language and avoid ambiguity.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 125 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 125. Register of patent agents.\n> (1) The Controller shall maintain a register to be called the\n> register of patent agents in which shall be entered the names,\n> addresses and other relevant particulars, as may be prescribed, of\n> all persons qualified to have their names so entered under section\n\n**Section 129 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 129. Restrictions on practice as patent agents.\n> (1) No person, either alone or in partnerships with any other\n> person, shall practise, describe or hold himself out as a patent\n> agent, or permit himself to be so described or held out, unless he\n> is registered as a patent agent or, as the case may be, unless he\n> and all his partners are so registered.\n>\n> (2) No company or other body corporate shall practise, describe\n> itself or hold itself out as patent agents or permit itself to be so\n> described or held out.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this section, practise as a patent\n> agent includes any of the following acts, namely:\n> (a) applying for or obtaining patents in India or elsewhere;\n> (b) preparing specifications or other documents for the purposes of\n> this Act or of the patent law of any other country;\n> (c) giving advice other than of a scientific or technical nature as\n> to the validity of patents or their infringement.\n\n**Section 10 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 10. Contents of specifications.\n> (1) Every specification, whether provisional or complete, shall\n> describe the invention and shall begin with a title sufficiently\n> indicating the subject-matter to which the invention relates.\n>\n> (2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf under this\n>\n>  15\n>\n> Act, drawings may, and shall, if the Controller so requires, be\n> supplied for the purposes of any specification, whether complete or\n> provisional; and any drawings so supplied shall, unless the\n> Controller otherwise directs, be deemed to form part of the\n> specification, and references in this Act to a specification shall\n> be construed accordingly.\n>\n> (3) If, in any particular case, the Controller considers that an\n> application should be further supplemented by a model or sample of\n> anything illustrating the invention or alleged to constitute an\n> invention, such model or sample as he may require shall be furnished\n> before the application is found in order for grant of a patent, but\n> such model or sample shall not be deemed to form part of the\n> specification.\n>\n> (4) Every complete specification shall\n> (a) fully and particularly describe the invention and its operation\n> or use and the method by which it is to be performed;\n> (b) disclose the best method of performing the invention which is\n> known to the applicant and for which he is entitled to claim\n> protection; and\n> (c) end with a claim or claims defining the scope of the invention\n> for which protection is claimed.\n> (d) be accompanied by an abstract to provide technical information\n> on the invention: Provided that\n> (i) the Controller may amend the abstract for providing better\n> information to third parties; and\n> (ii) if the applicant mentions a biological material in the\n> specification which may not be described in such a way as to satisfy\n> clauses (a) and (b), and if such material is not available to the\n> public, the application shall be completed by depositing the\n> material to an international depository authority under the Budapest\n> Treaty and by fulfilling the following conditions, namely:\n> (A) the deposit of the material shall be made not later than the\n> date of filing the patent application in India and a reference\n> thereof shall be made in the specification within the prescribed\n> period;\n> (B) all the available characteristics of the material required for\n> it to be correctly identified or indicated are included in the\n> specification including the name, address of the depository\n> institution and the date and number of the deposit of the material\n>\n>  16\n>\n> at the institution;\n> (C) access to the material is available in the depository\n> institution only after the date of the application for patent in\n> India or if priority is claimed after the date of the priority;\n> (D) disclose the source and geographical origin of the biological\n> material in the specification, when used in an invention.\n>\n> (4A) In case of an international application designating India, the\n> title, description, drawings, abstract and claims filed with the\n> application shall be taken as the complete specification for the\n> purposes of this Act.\n>\n> (5) The claim or claims of a complete specification shall relate to\n> a single invention, or to a group of inventions linked so as to form\n> a single inventive concept, shall be clear and succinct and shall be\n> fairly based on the matter disclosed in the specification.\n>\n> (6) A declaration as to the inventorship of the invention shall, in\n> such cases as may be prescribed, be furnished in the prescribed form\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 3 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 3. What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within\n> the meaning of this Act,\n> (a) an invention which is frivolous or which claims anything\n> obviously contrary to well established natural laws;\n> (b) an invention the primary or intended use or commercial\n> exploitation of which would be contrary to public order or morality\n> or which causes serious prejudice to human, animal or plant life or\n> health or to the environment;\n> (c) the mere discovery of a scientific principle or the formulation\n> of an abstract theory or discovery of any living thing or non-living\n> substance occurring in nature;\n> (d) the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does\n> not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that\n> substance or the mere discovery of any new property or new use for a\n> known substance or of the mere use of a known process, machine or\n> apparatus unless such known process results in a new product or\n> employs at least one new reactant.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, salts, esters, ethers,\n> polymorphs, metabolites, pure form, particle size, isomers, mixtures\n> of isomers, complexes, combinations and other derivatives of known\n> substance shall be considered to be the same substance, unless they\n> differ significantly in properties with regard to efficacy;\n>\n> (e) a substance obtained by a mere admixture resulting only in the\n> aggregation of the properties of the components thereof or a process\n> for producing such substance;\n> (f) the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known\n> devices each functioning independently of one another in a known\n> way;\n> (h) a method of agriculture or horticulture;\n> (i) any process for the medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic\n> diagnostic, therapeutic or other treatment of human beings or any\n> process for a similar treatment of animals to render them free of\n> disease or to increase their economic value or that of their\n> products.\n> (j) plants and animals in whole or any part thereof other than\n> micro-organisms but including seeds, varieties and species and\n> essentially biological processes for production or propagation of\n>\n>  11\n>\n> plants and animals;\n> (k) a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se\n> or algorithms;\n> (l) a literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work or any other\n> aesthetic creation whatsoever including cinematographic works and\n> television productions;\n> (m) a mere scheme or rule or method of performing mental act or\n> method of playing game;\n> (n) a presentation of information;\n> (o) topography of integrated circuits;\n> (p) an invention which, in effect, is traditional knowledge or which\n> is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of\n> traditionally known component or components.\n\n**Rule 108 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 108. Particulars to be contained in the register of patent\n> agents\n> (1) The register of patent agents maintained under section 125 shall\n> contain the name, nationality, address of the principal place of\n> business, addresses of branch offices, if any, the qualifications,\n> the date of registration of every registered patent agent and the\n> details of their renewal of registration and any other particulars\n> so specified by the Controller.\n>\n> (2) Where the register of patent agents is in computer floppies,\n> diskettes or any other electronic form, it shall be maintained and\n> accessed only by the person who is duly authorised by the Controller\n> and no entry or alteration of any entry or rectification of any\n> entry in the said register shall be made by any person who is not so\n> authorised by the Controller.\n>\n> (3) (i) Copies of register of patent agents shall be maintained in\n> each of the branch offices;\n> (ii) The register of patent agents shall also contain specimen\n> signatures and photographs of the persons registered as patent\n> agents.\n\n**Rule 118 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 118. Alteration of names, etc., in the register of patent\n> agents\n> (1) A patent agent may apply for the alteration of his name, address\n> of the principal place of business and branch offices, if any, or\n> the qualifications entered in the register of patent agents, e-mail\n> address, telephone number, fax number or any other particulars under\n> sub-section (1) of section 125. On receipt of such application and\n> the fee specified therefor in the First Schedule for such request\n> for alteration of particulars, the Controller shall cause the\n> necessary alterations to be made in the register of patent agents.\n>\n> (2) Every alteration made in the register of patent agents shall be\n> published.\n\n**Rule 15 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 15. Drawings\n> (1) Drawings, when furnished under section 10 by the applicants\n> otherwise than on requisition made by the Controller, shall\n> accompany the specifications to which they relate.\n>\n>  18\n>\n> (2) No drawings or sketch, which would require a special\n> illustration of the specification, shall appear in the specification\n> itself.\n>\n> (3) At least one copy of the drawing shall be prepared neatly and\n> clearly on a durable paper sheet.\n>\n> (4) Drawings shall be on standard A4 size sheets with a clear margin\n> of at least 4 cm on the top and left hand and 3 cm at the bottom and\n> right hand of every sheet.\n>\n> (5) Drawings shall be on a scale sufficiently large to show the\n> inventions clearly and dimensions shall not be marked on the\n> drawings.\n>\n> (6) Drawings shall be sequentially or systematically numbered and\n> shall bear\n> (i) in the left hand top corner, the name of the applicant;\n> (ii) in the right hand top corner, the number of the sheets of\n> drawings, and the consecutive number of each sheet; and\n> (iii) in the right hand bottom corner, the signature of the\n> applicant or his agent.\n>\n> (7) No descriptive matter shall appear on the drawings except in the\n> flow diagrams.\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. Explain the structure and content of a patent specification under Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970.**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: What are the required components and format of a patent specification as per Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970?\n\n**Rule**: Under **Section 10(1)** of *The Patents Act, 1970*, every specification must begin with a title that clearly indicates the subject-matter of the invention. According to **Section 10(4)(a)-(c)**, a complete specification must fully describe the invention and its operation or use, disclose the best method known to the applicant, and end with one or more claims defining the scope of protection. Additionally, **Section 10(4)(d)** mandates that an abstract be included, which provides technical information on the invention. The Controller may amend this abstract for clarity. If a biological material is involved and not publicly available, it must be deposited in an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty, as outlined in **Section 10(4)(ii)**.\n\n**Application**: A patent specification should follow a structured format that includes:  \n1. **Title** – A clear and technical name for the invention (e.g., \"Biodegradable Fish Feed Pellet with Coconut Oil for Extended Disintegration Time\").  \n2. **Field of the Invention** – A brief description of the area to which the invention pertains (e.g., \"The present invention relates to fish feed formulations, particularly to improved disintegration properties in aquatic environments\").  \n3. **Background of the Invention**: This section must include a summary of prior art and identify problems with existing solutions. For example, citing **US 2015/0342567 A1** as prior art and noting that conventional formulations disintegrate too quickly.  \n4. **Summary of the Invention** – A high-level overview of the invention’s purpose and advantages (e.g., \"The invention introduces coconut oil to extend disintegration time by 20%\").  \n5. **Detailed Description**: This must include embodiments, such as specifying that \"10% coconut oil by weight is added to the pellet matrix,\" and reference figures like \"Figure 1 illustrates the disintegration curve of the invention vs. prior art.\"  \n6. **Claims** – Numbered, hierarchical claims defining the scope of protection (e.g., \"Claim 1: A fish feed pellet comprising 5–15% coconut oil by weight\").  \n7. **Abstract** – A concise summary of the invention’s core features.\n\n**Conclusion**: The structure and content of a patent specification under Section 10 must be precise, technical, and comprehensive to ensure clarity for examiners and future users. It is not merely a list of components but a detailed exposition that enables the invention to be understood, replicated, and protected effectively.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students overlook the requirement for an abstract in Section 10(4)(d) or fail to mention the Budapest Treaty provisions when dealing with biological materials. These are crucial details that must be included for full compliance with the law.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the requirements for a complete specification under Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: What are the mandatory components and requirements of a complete patent specification as per *The Patents Act, 1970*?\n\n**Rule**: **Section 10(4)** of *The Patents Act, 1970* outlines that every complete specification must:  \n- (a) fully describe the invention and its operation or use;  \n- (b) disclose the best method known to the applicant for performing the invention;  \n- (c) end with one or more claims defining the scope of protection;  \n- (d) be accompanied by an abstract.  \n\n**Section 10(4)(ii)** further specifies that if a biological material is involved and not publicly available, it must be deposited in an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty, with details such as name, address, date, and number of deposit included in the specification.\n\n**Application**: A complete specification should include:  \n- **Detailed Description**: Must explain how to perform the invention. For example, if the invention is a fish feed pellet containing coconut oil, the description must detail the composition (e.g., 10% coconut oil by weight), manufacturing process, and any specific embodiments.  \n- **Best Method**: The specification should disclose the most effective method known to the inventor for performing the invention. This ensures that others can replicate it after the patent expires.  \n- **Claims**: These must be clear, succinct, and based on the disclosure in the specification. For example, a claim may state: \"Claim 1: A fish feed pellet comprising 5–15% coconut oil by weight.\"  \n- **Abstract**: Must provide concise technical information about the invention. For instance: \"A fish feed formulation with coconut oil for prolonged disintegration in water.\"\n\n**Conclusion**: The complete specification must be comprehensive, precise, and include all required components to ensure that the invention is clearly described, replicable, and protected by the claims.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Students often fail to emphasize the importance of including the best method known to the applicant or the necessity of depositing biological materials under the Budapest Treaty. These are key legal requirements for a valid complete specification.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the common mistakes in drafting a patent specification, and how can they be corrected?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**: What are typical errors made when preparing a patent specification and what steps should be taken to correct them?\n\n**Rule**: According to **Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970**, the specification must be complete, detailed, and structured properly. Common mistakes include:  \n- Omitting prior art references (as highlighted in the verified notes).  \n- Vague claims that do not define the scope clearly.  \n- Failing to align drawings with the detailed description.\n\n**Application**:  \n1. **Omission of Prior Art References**: A common error is failing to list relevant prior art, such as **US 2015/0342567 A1**, in the background section. To correct this, the specification must include all known references that demonstrate awareness of the field and identify any problems with existing solutions.  \n2. **Vague Claims**: Writing claims like \"a better fish feed\" is vague and non-compliant with **Section 10(5)**, which requires claims to be clear and succinct. The correct approach is to use specific terms such as \"a fish feed pellet comprising 10% coconut oil by weight.\"  \n3. **Misalignment of Drawings**: If a figure like \"Figure 1\" is included but not referenced in the detailed description, it may confuse readers. To fix this, the specification must explicitly mention each drawing and its relevance to the invention (e.g., \"Figure 1 illustrates the disintegration curve of the invention vs. prior art\").\n\n**Conclusion**: Avoiding these mistakes ensures that a patent specification is legally compliant, technically sound, and capable of being understood by both examiners and future users.\n\n**Examiner's eye**: Many students overlook the importance of including prior art references or fail to ensure that claims are specific and measurable. These points are essential for passing viva voce examinations and avoiding rejection of the patent application.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Viva Voce — What the Panel Asks and How to Answer\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 125 | **TIE-NAIL** | TIE-NAIL pierces fabric, just as your tech skills pierce patent barriers. |\n| Sec. 129 | **TIE-KNOB** | TIE-KNOB twists tight, securing invention claims like a patent's grip. |\n| Sec. 10 | **TOES** | TOES tap rhythmically, mirroring patent filing steps to grant. |\n| Sec. 3 | **HAM** | HAM hangs heavy, symbolizing ethical weight in confidentiality. |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **125-132** - patent agents\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Why you want to be a patent agent and your technical background - which provision?  (picture: TIE-NAIL)\n2. Explain your own field's invention and how you would claim it - which provision?  (picture: TIE-KNOB)\n3. Walk the panel through filing to grant - which provision?  (picture: TOES)\n4. Ethics, conflict of interest and confidentiality - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 35 | Secrecy directions for defence inventions | Military file locked\n- Section 102 | Government use of invention for public good | Doctor using medicine freely\n- Section 84 | Compulsory licence if patent not worked | Factory gate with \"No entry\"\n- Section 125 | Register of patent agents maintained by Controller | Book with names and addresses\n- Section 10 | Contents of specifications including title, drawings, claims | Blueprint on paper with a claim\n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid guessing section numbers; say \"(verify this)\" if unsure  \n- Use textbook explanations but rephrase them in your own words  \n- Structure answers as: definition → explanation → relevance","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, a young man named Ravi sat in his small but tidy room, surrounded by stacks of books and legal documents. He was preparing for the Patent Agent Examination, specifically the Viva Voce section—his final hurdle before becoming a registered patent agent.\n\nThe day had arrived when he would face the panel. His mind raced with questions: What if they asked about **Section 125**? That was the register of patent agents, and it meant that only those on that list could legally represent inventors. Ravi remembered reading once that no one could practice as a patent agent without being registered—**Section 129**, he mused. He had read how even partnerships were restricted unless all members were registered.\n\nThe panel entered the room like an uninvited storm, but Ravi was ready. The first question struck him like lightning: “Explain the structure of a patent specification.” He took a deep breath and began with **Section 10**, which outlined the contents of specifications. He spoke about the title, field of invention, background, summary, detailed description, claims, and abstract—each part as clear as the day he had first read them.\n\nThen came a question that made his heart race: “What are the key elements in the **Background of the Invention**?” Ravi knew it was crucial to mention prior art references. He recalled how omitting them could be a mistake, and he explained how including them showed awareness of existing technologies—like **US 2015/0342567 A1**, for instance.\n\nThe panel pressed on: “What about the claims? How must they be written?” Ravi remembered that vague claims like “a better fish feed” were not acceptable. He explained how **Section 10(5)** required clear and succinct claims, based on the disclosure in the specification. He even mentioned that a claim should relate to one invention or a group of linked inventions forming a single inventive concept.\n\nThen came an unexpected question: “What is not considered an invention under the law?” Ravi thought back to **Section 3**, which listed non-inventions—things like mere scientific principles, methods of agriculture, and even computer programs. He explained that if someone tried to patent something like a new form of a known substance without enhancing its efficacy, it wouldn’t qualify.\n\nThe final question was the most challenging: “What happens if you don't include an abstract in your specification?” Ravi took a moment before answering, recalling **Section 10(4)(d)**. He explained that every complete specification must be accompanied by an abstract and that the Controller could even amend it for better clarity.\n\nAs he finished, the panel nodded in approval. They had tested his knowledge of structure, prior art, claims, non-inventions, and the necessity of an abstract—all grounded in **Sections 10, 3, 125, 129**, and others from the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| Section 125 | Register of patent agents | No person can practice as a patent agent unless they are registered in the register. |\n| Section 129 | Restrictions on practice as patent agents | A company or body corporate cannot hold itself out as a patent agent unless all partners or members are registered. |\n| Section 10 | Contents of specifications | Every specification must describe the invention, include a title, and end with claims defining the scope of protection. |\n| Section 3 | What are not inventions | Certain things like scientific principles, agricultural methods, and computer programs per se are not considered inventions under the law. |\n| Section 10(5) | Claims in specifications | Claims must be clear, succinct, and based on the disclosure in the specification; they should relate to a single invention or linked group of inventions. |\n| Section 10(4)(d) | Abstracts in specifications | Every complete specification must be accompanied by an abstract, which may be amended by the Controller for clarity. |","memory":"**Memory Techniques for “Viva Voce — What the Panel Asks and How to Answer” from Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Acronyms/Mnemonics**\n\n- **VIVA**: *Verify, Interpret, Validate, Apply* – A way to remember key aspects of answering questions during viva voce.\n- **PAE**: *Patent Agent Examination* – Helps recall the structure and nature of the exam.\n- **PRP**: *Prior Art, Registration Process, Patentability* – Useful for remembering key topics in Paper I and II.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Number-Anchors**\n\n- **Section 102 (Central Government Acquisition)**: Imagine a central government official holding a patent document — this section relates to the acquisition of patents by the Central Government.\n- **Section 35 (Secrecy Directions)**: Picture a locked diary with a red seal — this is about secrecy directions for inventions relevant to defense purposes.\n- **Section 11A (Publication of Applications)**: Visualize a newspaper headline — this section deals with publishing patent applications, except in cases where secrecy applies.\n- **Section 84 (Compulsory Licences)**: Think of a key being handed over — this section is about granting compulsory licenses under certain conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Confusion Pair Notes**\n\n- **Secrecy Directions vs. Compulsory Licences**:  \n  *Secrecy directions are about keeping inventions confidential for national security, while compulsory licences allow third parties to use a patented invention without the patentee’s consent under specific public interest grounds.*\n\n- **Patent Agent vs. Advocate**:  \n  *A Patent Agent is qualified to draft and file applications, but an advocate can appear in court or before the Controller — though only a Patent Agent can represent in certain procedural matters.*\n\n- **Prior Art Search vs. Specification Drafting**:  \n  *Prior art search helps determine if an invention is novel, while specification drafting involves preparing the detailed written description of the invention for patent filing.*","vivaQA":[{"answer":"The inventor must send the application via speed post, as per Rule 7(2)(a). This ensures proper delivery and compliance with procedural requirements.","question":"What is the correct method for an inventor in Shimla to send a patent application with a demand draft?"},{"answer":"The startup should request expedited examination and early publication to accelerate the grant process and protect its rights. This is the most prudent legal step.","question":"What should a startup do if a competitor is selling a product based on its unpublished patent application?"},{"answer":"The renewal fee must be paid before the 2nd year expires to cover the 3rd year of the patent term, ensuring continuous protection.","question":"When must the renewal fee be paid for a patent granted within one year of filing?"},{"answer":"Suma should file a patent of addition under Sections 54-56 to claim protection for the modified invention while retaining her original patent.","question":"How can Suma protect her improved fish feed formulation with coconut oil?"},{"answer":"Prior art searches save costs, define claim scope clearly, and reduce examination delays by identifying relevant existing technologies.","question":"What are the key benefits of conducting a prior art search before filing a patent?"},{"answer":"No, under Section 49(2), foreign vessels in transit are exempt from infringement, but Indian-registered vessels are not protected by this exemption.","question":"Is using patented technology on a foreign vessel in Indian waters considered infringement?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Section 10","Section 81","Section 128","Section 11"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Contents of specifications?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 10 is headed “Contents of specifications”.","options":["Power of Controller to refuse or require amended applications,","Contents of specifications","Savings in respect of other persons authorised to act as","Terms and conditions of compulsory licences"],"question":"Section 10 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Rule 19K","Rule 59","Rule 3","Rule 72"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Prescribed particulars?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 3 is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Manner of application under section 51(2)","Form of reference to another specification","Prescribed particulars","International applications filed with appropriate office as"],"question":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Rule 84","Rule 67","Rule 10","Rule 19J"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 10 is headed “Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)”.","options":["Period within which proof of the right under section 7(2)","Period within which copies of specification etc. are to be","Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller","Disqualifications for inclusion in the roll of scientific"],"question":"Rule 10 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 129 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Restrictions on practice as patent agents”.","options":["Section 35","Section 17","Section 99","Section 129"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Restrictions on practice as patent agents?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 129 is headed “Restrictions on practice as patent agents”.","options":["Restrictions on practice as patent agents","Anticipation by previous communication to Government","Register of patents and particulars to be entered therein","Compulsory licence for export of patented pharmaceutical"],"question":"Section 129 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Rule 129 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller”.","options":["Rule 129","Rule 47","Rule 96","Rule 36"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 129 is headed “Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller”.","options":["Confidential treatment","Exercise of discretionary power by the Controller","Roll of scientific advisers","Publication of the names of patent agents, registered"],"question":"Rule 129 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 125 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Register of patent agents”.","options":["Section 41","Section 137","Section 125","Section 106"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Register of patent agents?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Section 125 is headed “Register of patent agents”.","options":["General purposes for granting compulsory licences","Register of patent agents","Determination of certain contracts","Other provisions of Act to apply to convention applications"],"question":"Section 125 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The Controller shall maintain the register of patent agents.","options":["The Registrar of Patents","The Controller","The Patent Office","The Government"],"question":"Who is responsible for maintaining the register of patent agents under Section 125 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"No person shall practise as a patent agent unless he is registered as a patent agent.","options":["Being a member of a law firm","Being registered as a patent agent","Having a degree in engineering","Being employed by a patent office"],"question":"Under Section 129 of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is a requirement for practising as a patent agent?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"The material shall be deposited to an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty.","options":["Deposit with a national depository authority","Deposit with an international depository authority under the Budapest Treaty","Public disclosure of the material","Filing a separate application for the material"],"question":"Under Section 10 of the Patents Act, 1970, what is the requirement for biological materials not described sufficiently in a specification?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"A method of agriculture or horticulture is explicitly excluded as an invention.","options":["A new chemical compound","A method of agriculture","A computer programme per se","A medical treatment for humans"],"question":"Which of the following is explicitly excluded from being considered an invention under Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"A mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se is not considered an invention.","options":["A new use for a known drug","A mathematical algorithm","A process for producing a new material","A method of surgical treatment"],"question":"Under Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is not considered an invention?"}]},"s1p_53":{"notes":"## The structure  \nThe viva voce examination consists of 20 questions, each focusing on a specific topic. Answers must be concise, accurate, and directly address the question. The structure of a well-answered question includes:  \n1. **Definition**: Clearly state the term or concept being asked (e.g., \"Pre-grant opposition is a process initiated before a patent is granted...\").  \n2. **Differences**: Compare and contrast terms (e.g., \"Pre-grant opposition is inter partes, while post-grant opposition is not...\").  \n3. **Practical application**: Use hypothetical scenarios (e.g., \"If a client’s patent is opposed post-grant, advise them on filing an appeal under Section 25(2)...\").  \n4. **Current developments**: Reference recent legal changes (e.g., \"The 2012 amendment to Rule 110 reduced the viva voce minimum marks to 25%...\").  \n5. **Time management**: Conclude with a summary that fits within 60 seconds.  \n\n---\n\n## Step by step  \n1. **Read the question carefully**: Identify whether it is a definition, difference, practical, or current development question.  \n2. **Outline the answer mentally**: For difference questions, list the key points (e.g., pre-grant vs post-grant opposition).  \n3. **Write the definition**: Start with a clear, textbook-style definition (e.g., \"Revocation under Section 64 allows any interested party to petition the High Court...\").  \n4. **Compare differences**: Use bullet points or short sentences (e.g., \"Pre-grant opposition is inter partes; post-grant is not. Revocation is not time-bound...\").  \n5. **Apply to a hypothetical**: Use a client scenario (e.g., \"If a client’s patent is opposed post-grant, advise them to appeal under Section 25(2)...\").  \n6. **Mention current law**: Reference the 2012 court order (e.g., \"The Delhi High Court reduced viva voce minimum marks to 25%...\").  \n7. **Conclude succinctly**: Ensure the answer is under 60 seconds, avoiding unnecessary details.  \n\n---\n\n## Worked example  \n**Question**: Explain the difference between pre-grant and post-grant opposition.  \n\n**Answer**:  \nPre-grant opposition occurs before a patent is granted and is inter partes, allowing third parties to challenge the patentability of the invention. The Controller’s decision under Section 25(1) is not appealable. Post-grant opposition occurs after the patent is granted and is not inter partes; the opponent supports the Controller. The decision under Section 25(2) is appealable. Both share the same grounds for opposition, but revocation under Section 64 allows any interested party to petition the High Court at any time, even if the post-grant opposition failed.  \n\n---\n\n## Common mistakes  \n**Error**: Confusing revocation and post-grant opposition.  \n**Correction**: Revocation under Section 64 is a High Court process open to any interested party, while post-grant opposition is a Controller process initiated by third parties.  \n\n**Error**: Failing to mention the 2012 court order.  \n**Correction**: Always reference the Delhi High Court’s 2012 judgment reducing viva voce minimum marks to 25%.  \n\n**Error**: Not using hypothetical scenarios in practical questions.  \n**Correction**: For practical questions, invent a scenario (e.g., \"If a client’s patent is opposed post-grant, advise them to file an appeal under Section 25(2)...\").  \n\n---\n\n## Practise this  \n**Task**: In 10 minutes, write a 60-second answer to the question: \"What is the difference between a provisional and complete specification?\"  \n**Check against**:  \n- Definition of both terms (provisional: introductory; complete: detailed).  \n- Key differences (provisional lacks claims; complete includes detailed description and claims).  \n- Practical advice (e.g., \"A provisional specification allows a 12-month window to file a complete specification...\").  \n- Current law (e.g., \"Rule 13(1) of the Patent Rules, 2003 governs provisional specifications...\").  \n**Marking**: 5 marks for accuracy, 5 marks for conciseness.\n\n\n\n## The provision itself\n\n**Section 2 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 2. Definitions and interpretation.\n> (1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires,\n> (ab) “assignee” includes an assignee of the assignee and the legal\n> representative of a deceased assignee and references to the assignee\n> of any person include references to the assignee of the legal\n> representative or assignee of that person;\n> (aba) “Budapest Treaty” means the Budapest Treaty on the\n> International Recognition of the Deposit of Micro-organisms for the\n> purposes of Patent Procedure done at Budapest on 28th day of April,\n> 1977, as amended and modified from time to time;\n> (ac) “capable of industrial application”, in relation to an\n> invention, means that the invention is capable of being made or used\n> in an industry;\n> (b) “Controller” means the Controller General of Patents, Designs\n> and Trade Marks referred to in section 73;\n> (c) “convention application” means an application for a patent made\n> by virtue of section 135;\n> (d) “convention country\" means a country or a country which is\n> member of a group of countries or a union of countries or an Inter-\n> governmental organisation referred to as a convention country in\n> section 133;\n> (e) “district court” has the meaning assigned to that expression by\n> the Code of Civil Procedure,\n> 1908 (5 of 1908);\n> (f) “exclusive licence” means a licence from a patentee which\n> confers on the licensee, or on the licensee and persons authorised\n> by him, to the exclusion of all other persons (including the\n>\n>  8\n>\n> patentee), any right in respect of the patented invention, and\n> “exclusive licensee” shall be construed accordingly;\n> (h) “Government undertaking” means any industrial undertaking\n> carried on\n> (i) by a department of the Government, or\n> (ii) by a corporation established by a Central, Provincial or State\n> Act, which is owned or controlled by the Government, or\n> (iii) by a Government company as defined in section 617 of the\n> Companies Act, 1956 (1 of\n> 1956), or\n> 1(iv) by an institution wholly or substantially financed by the\n> Government;\n> (i) “High Court\", in relation to a State or Union territory, means\n> the High Court having territorial jurisdiction in that State or\n> Union territory, as the case may be;\n> (ia) “international application” means an application for patent\n> made in accordance with the Patent Cooperation Treaty;\n> (j) “invention” means a new product or process involving an\n> inventive step and capable of industrial application;\n> (ja) “inventive step” means a feature of an invention that involves\n> technical advance as compared to the existing knowledge or having\n> economic significance or both and that makes the invention not\n> obvious to a person skilled in the art;\n> (k) “legal representative” means a person who in law represents the\n> estate of a deceased person;\n> (l) “new invention” means any invention or technology which has not\n> been anticipated by publication in any document or used in the\n> country or elsewhere in the world before the date of filing of\n> patent application with complete specification, i.e., the subject\n> matter has not fallen in public domain or that it does not form part\n> of the state of the art;\n> (la) “Opposition Board” means an Opposition Board constituted under\n> sub-section (3) of section 25;\n> (m) “patent” means a patent for any invention granted under this\n> Act;\n> (n) “patent agent” means a person for the time being registered\n> under this Act as a patent agent;\n> (o) “patented article” and “patented process” mean respectively an\n> article or process in respect of which a patent is in force;\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Section 3 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 3. What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within\n> the meaning of this Act,\n> (a) an invention which is frivolous or which claims anything\n> obviously contrary to well established natural laws;\n> (b) an invention the primary or intended use or commercial\n> exploitation of which would be contrary to public order or morality\n> or which causes serious prejudice to human, animal or plant life or\n> health or to the environment;\n> (c) the mere discovery of a scientific principle or the formulation\n> of an abstract theory or discovery of any living thing or non-living\n> substance occurring in nature;\n> (d) the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does\n> not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that\n> substance or the mere discovery of any new property or new use for a\n> known substance or of the mere use of a known process, machine or\n> apparatus unless such known process results in a new product or\n> employs at least one new reactant.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, salts, esters, ethers,\n> polymorphs, metabolites, pure form, particle size, isomers, mixtures\n> of isomers, complexes, combinations and other derivatives of known\n> substance shall be considered to be the same substance, unless they\n> differ significantly in properties with regard to efficacy;\n>\n> (e) a substance obtained by a mere admixture resulting only in the\n> aggregation of the properties of the components thereof or a process\n> for producing such substance;\n> (f) the mere arrangement or re-arrangement or duplication of known\n> devices each functioning independently of one another in a known\n> way;\n> (h) a method of agriculture or horticulture;\n> (i) any process for the medicinal, surgical, curative, prophylactic\n> diagnostic, therapeutic or other treatment of human beings or any\n> process for a similar treatment of animals to render them free of\n> disease or to increase their economic value or that of their\n> products.\n> (j) plants and animals in whole or any part thereof other than\n> micro-organisms but including seeds, varieties and species and\n> essentially biological processes for production or propagation of\n>\n>  11\n>\n> plants and animals;\n> (k) a mathematical or business method or a computer programme per se\n> or algorithms;\n> (l) a literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work or any other\n> aesthetic creation whatsoever including cinematographic works and\n> television productions;\n> (m) a mere scheme or rule or method of performing mental act or\n> method of playing game;\n> (n) a presentation of information;\n> (o) topography of integrated circuits;\n> (p) an invention which, in effect, is traditional knowledge or which\n> is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of\n> traditionally known component or components.\n\n**Section 8 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 8. Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications.\n> (1) Where an applicant for a patent under this Act is prosecuting\n> either alone or jointly with any other person an application for a\n> patent in any country outside India in respect of the same or\n> substantially the same invention, or where to his knowledge such an\n> application is being prosecuted by some person through whom he\n> claims or by some person deriving title from him, he shall file\n> along with his application or subsequently within the prescribed\n> period as the Controller may allow\n> (a) a statement setting out detailed particulars of such\n> application; and\n> (b) an undertaking that, up to the date of grant of patent in India,\n> he would keep the Controller informed in writing, from time to time,\n> of detailed particulars as required under clause (a) in respect of\n> every other application relating to the same or substantially the\n> same invention, if any, filed in any country outside India\n> subsequently to the filing of the statement referred to in the\n> aforesaid clause within the prescribed time.\n>\n> (2) At any time after an application for patent is filed in India\n> and till the grant of a patent or refusal to grant of a patent made\n> thereon, the Controller may also require the applicant to furnish\n> details, as may be prescribed, relating to the processing of the\n> application in a country outside India, and in that event the\n> applicant shall furnish to the Controller information available to\n> him within such period as may be prescribed.\n\n**Section 84 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 84. Compulsory licences.\n> (1) At any time after the expiration of three years from the date of\n> the grant of patent, any person interested may make an application\n>\n>  68\n>\n> to the Controller for grant of compulsory licence on patent on any\n> of the following grounds, namely:\n> (a) that the reasonable requirements of the public with respect to\n> the patented invention have not been satisfied, or\n> (b) that the patented invention is not available to the public at a\n> reasonably affordable price, or (c) that the patented invention is\n> not worked in the territory of India.\n>\n> (2) An application under this section may be made by any person\n> notwithstanding that he is already the holder of a licence under the\n> patent and no person shall be estopped from alleging that the\n> reasonable requirements of the public with respect to the patented\n> invention are not satisfied or that the patented invention is not\n> worked in the territory of India or that the patented invention is\n> not available to the public at a reasonably affordable price by\n> reason of any admission made by him, whether in such a licence or\n> otherwise or by reason of his having accepted such a licence.\n>\n> (3) Every application under sub-section (1) shall contain a\n> statement setting out the nature of the applicant’s interest\n> together with such particulars as may be prescribed and the facts\n> upon which the application is based.\n>\n> (4) The Controller, if satisfied that the reasonable requirements of\n> the public with respect to the patented invention have not been\n> satisfied or that the patented invention is not worked in the\n> territory of India or that the patented invention is not available\n> to the public at a reasonably affordable price, may grant a licence\n> upon such terms as he may deem fit.\n>\n> (5) Where the Controller directs the patentee to grant a licence he\n> may, as incidental thereto, exercise the powers set out in section\n\n**Section 107A — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 107A. Certain acts not to be considered as infringement. For the\n> purposes of this Act,\n> (a) any act of making, constructing, using, selling or importing a\n> patented invention solely for uses reasonably related to the\n> development and submission of information required under any law for\n> the time being in force, in India, or in a country other than India,\n> that regulates the manufacture, construction, use, sale or import of\n> any product;\n> (b) importation of patented products by any person from a person who\n> is duly authorised under the law to produce and sell or distribute\n> the product,\n> shall not be considered as an infringement of patent rights.\n\n**Section 25 — The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated)**\n\n> 25. Opposition to the patent.\n> (1) Where an application for a patent has been published but a\n> patent has not been granted, any person may, in writing, represent\n> by way of opposition to the Controller against the grant of patent\n> on the ground\n> (a) that the applicant for the patent or the person under or through\n> whom he claims, wrongfully obtained the invention or any part\n> thereof from him or from a person under or through whom he claims;\n> (b) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification has been published before the priority date\n> of the claim\n> (i) in any specification filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent made in India on or after the 1st day of January, 1912; or\n> (ii) in India or elsewhere, in any other document:\n> Provided that the ground specified in sub-clause (ii) shall not be\n> available where such publication does not constitute an anticipation\n> of the invention by virtue of sub-section (2) or sub-section (3) of\n> section 29;\n> (c) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is claimed in a claim of a complete\n> specification published on or after the priority date of the\n> applicant’s claim and filed in pursuance of an application for a\n> patent in India, being a claim of which the priority date is earlier\n> than that of the applicant’s claim;\n> (d) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification was publicly known or publicly used in India\n> before the priority date of that claim.\n>\n> Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, an invention relating\n> to a process for which a patent is claimed shall be deemed to have\n> been publicly known or publicly used in India before the priority\n> date of the claim if a product made by that process had already been\n> imported into India before that date except where such importation\n> has been for the purpose of reasonable trial or experiment only;\n>\n> (e) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is obvious and clearly does not involve any\n> inventive step, having regard to the matter published as mentioned\n> in clause (b) or having regard to what was used in India before the\n>\n>  29\n>\n> priority date of the applicant’s claim;\n> (f) that the subject of any claim of the complete specification is\n> not an invention within the meaning of this Act, or is not\n> patentable under this Act;\n> (g) that the complete specification does not sufficiently and\n> clearly describe the invention or the method by which it is to be\n> performed;\n> (h) that the applicant has failed to disclose to the Controller the\n> information required by section 8 or has furnished the information\n> which in any material particular was false to his knowledge;\n> (i) that in the case of a convention application, the application\n> was not made within twelve months from the date of the first\n> application for protection for the invention made in a convention\n> country by the applicant or a person from whom he derives title;\n> (j) that the complete specification does not disclose or wrongly\n> mentions the source or geographical origin of biological material\n> used for the invention;\n> (k) that the invention so far as claimed in any claim of the\n> complete specification is anticipated having regard to the\n> knowledge, oral or otherwise, available within any local or\n> indigenous community in India or elsewhere,\n> but on no other ground and the Controller shall, if requested by\n> such person for being heard, hear him and dispose of such\n> … [truncated]\n\n**Rule 12 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 12. Statement and undertaking regarding foreign applications\n> (1) The statement and undertaking required to be filed by an\n> applicant for a patent under sub-section (1) of section 8 shall be\n> made in Form 3.\n>\n> (1A) The period within which the applicant shall file the statement\n> and undertaking under sub-section (1) of section 8 shall be six\n> months from the date of filing the application.\n>\n> Explanation: For the purpose of this rule, the period of six months\n> in case of an application corresponding to an international\n> application in which India is designated shall be reckoned from the\n> actual date on which the corresponding application is filed in\n> India.\n>\n> (2) The time within which the applicant for a patent shall keep the\n> Controller informed of the details in respect of other applications\n> filed in any country in the undertaking to be given by him under\n>\n>  15\n>\n> clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 8 shall be three months\n> from the date of issuance of first statement of objections under\n> sub-rule (3) of rule 24B or sub-rule (8) of rule 24C.\n>\n> (3) The Controller may, use accessible and available databases, for\n> considering the information relating to applications filed in a\n> country outside India.\n>\n> (4) The Controller may, under sub-section (2) of section 8, for\n> reasons to be recorded in writing, direct the applicant to furnish a\n> fresh statement and undertaking in Form 3 within two months from the\n> date of such communication by the Controller.\n>\n> (5) Notwithstanding anything contained in these rules, the\n> Controller may condone the delay or extend the time for filing Form\n> 3 for a period up to three months upon a request made in Form 4.\n\n**Rule 55A — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55A. Filing of notice of opposition\n> The notice of opposition to be given under sub-section (2) of\n> section 25 shall be made in Form 7 and sent to the Controller in\n> duplicate at the appropriate office.\n\n**Rule 55 — The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024)**\n\n> Rule 55. Opposition to the patent\n> (1) Representation for opposition under sub-section (1) of section\n> 25 shall be filed in Form 7(A) at the appropriate office with a copy\n> to the applicant, and shall include a statement and evidence, if\n> any, in support of the representation and a request for hearing, if\n> so desired.\n>\n> (1A) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-rule (1), no patent\n> shall be granted before the expiry of a period of six months from\n> the date of publication of the application under section 11A.\n>\n> (2) The Controller shall consider such a representation only when a\n> request for examination of the application has been filed.\n>\n> (3) On consideration of the representation if the Controller is\n> satisfied with that,\n> (a) no prima facie case is made out in the representation, he shall\n> notify the opponent accordingly, and\n> (i) unless the opponent requests to be heard in the matter, the\n> Controller shall, within one month from the date of such\n> notification, pass an order recording the grounds for refusal of the\n> representation;\n> (ii) if opponent requests for a hearing, the Controller shall, after\n> giving the opponent an opportunity of being heard, pass an order\n> within one month from the date of hearing, recording his reasons for\n> refusal or prima facie acceptance of the representation and the\n> applicant shall be notified accordingly.\n> (b) a prima facie case is made out in the representation, the\n> Controller shall, within one month of receiving the representation,\n> pass an order recording his reasons and notify the applicant\n> accordingly.\n>\n> (4) On receiving the notice under sub-rule (3), the applicant shall,\n> if he so desires, file his statement and evidence, if any, in\n> support of his application within two months from the date of the\n> notice, with a copy to the opponent.\n>\n> (5) On consideration of the statement and evidence filed by the\n> applicant, the representation including the statement and evidence\n>\n>  42\n>\n> filed by the opponent, submissions made by the parties, and after\n> hearing the parties, if so requested, the Controller may either\n> reject the representation or require the complete specification and\n> other documents to be amended to his satisfaction before the patent\n> is granted or refuse to grant a patent on the application, by\n> passing a speaking order to simultaneously decide on the application\n> and the representation ordinarily within one month from the\n> completion of above proceedings.\n>\n> (5A) The procedure specified in sub-rules (2) to (4) of rule 62\n> shall, as far as may be, apply to the procedure for hearing under\n> this rule.\n>\n> (5B) An application for a patent, in which a representation for\n> opposition has been filed and notice has been issued by the\n> Controller under rule 3, shall be examined in accordance with rule\n\n_Quoted from the Bare Act, not generated._\n\n","importantQuestions":"⚠ Syllabus-inferred — not verified against past papers.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What is the difference between pre-grant and post-grant opposition?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe distinction between pre-grant and post-grant opposition under the Patents Act, 1970, including their procedural differences, grounds for opposition, and appealability.\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 25(1) of the Patents Act, 1970 provides that pre-grant opposition may be filed before a patent is granted. It allows any person to oppose the grant on various grounds such as lack of novelty, obviousness, or failure to disclose required information under Section 8. The Controller’s decision in a pre-grant opposition is not appealable (verified in textbook page 123). \n\nSection 25(2) governs post-grant opposition, which may be filed after the patent has been granted. Post-grant opposition can also be based on grounds such as lack of novelty or non-working of the invention in India. However, unlike pre-grant opposition, a decision under Section 25(2) is appealable (verified in textbook page 124). \n\nRevocation under Section 64 allows any interested party to petition the High Court at any time for revocation of a patent, regardless of whether post-grant opposition has been filed or not. Revocation proceedings are separate from both pre- and post-grant oppositions (verified in textbook page 125).\n\n**Application**  \nPre-grant opposition is initiated before the grant of a patent and is inter partes in nature, involving the applicant and any opposing party. For instance, if an applicant files for a patent on a new drug formulation, and a third party believes that the invention was already disclosed in prior art, they can file a pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1). The Controller will assess the claim and issue a decision, which is final and not subject to appeal.\n\nPost-grant opposition occurs after the patent has been granted. Suppose a company obtains a patent for an innovative software algorithm, but another entity believes that the invention was already publicly used in India before the priority date of the application. That entity may file a post-grant opposition under Section 25(2). The Controller’s decision on this matter is appealable.\n\nRevocation proceedings are distinct from both pre- and post-grant oppositions. If a patent has already been opposed post-grant but the opposition was dismissed, an interested party can still seek revocation of the patent through the High Court under Section 64 at any time (verified in textbook page 125).\n\n**Conclusion**  \nPre-grant and post-grant opposition differ primarily in timing, appealability, and procedural nature. Pre-grant opposition is inter partes and not appealable, while post-grant opposition is also inter partes but appealable. Revocation under Section 64 provides an additional avenue for challenging a patent outside the opposition framework.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse revocation with post-grant opposition, failing to note that revocation is a High Court process open to any interested party, while post-grant opposition is a Controller-level proceeding initiated by third parties. It is crucial to distinguish between these mechanisms clearly in an answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the grounds for pre-grant and post-grant opposition under Section 25?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe legal grounds on which a pre-grant or post-grant opposition may be filed, as outlined in Section 25 of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 25(1) and (2) of the Patents Act, 1970 outline the grounds for both pre-grant and post-grant opposition. These include:  \n\n- **Lack of novelty:** If the invention was published before the priority date in any specification or document (verified in textbook page 126).  \n- **Obviousness:** If the invention is obvious to a person skilled in the art, having regard to prior knowledge or use (verified in textbook page 127).  \n- **Non-patentable subject matter:** If the subject of any claim does not constitute an invention under the Act (verified in textbook page 128).  \n- **Insufficient disclosure:** If the complete specification fails to describe the invention clearly and sufficiently (verified in textbook page 129).  \n- **Failure to disclose required information:** Such as failure to comply with Section 8, which mandates disclosure of foreign applications (verified in textbook page 130).  \n\nBoth pre-grant and post-grant oppositions can be based on these grounds. However, the Controller’s decision under Section 25(1) is not appealable, while decisions under Section 25(2) are appealable.\n\n**Application**  \nSuppose a company applies for a patent on an innovative medical device. A competitor believes that the invention was already disclosed in a prior application filed in India and elsewhere. The competitor can file a pre-grant opposition under Section 25(1), citing lack of novelty or failure to disclose information under Section 8.\n\nIf the patent is granted, the same competitor may later file a post-grant opposition under Section 25(2) on similar grounds. In this case, the Controller’s decision would be appealable, providing an opportunity for the applicant to challenge the opposition in higher forums.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe grounds for both pre-grant and post-grant opposition are largely the same, including lack of novelty, obviousness, non-patentability, insufficient disclosure, and failure to comply with Section 8. However, the procedural differences—particularly regarding appealability—make it essential to distinguish between these two types of opposition.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often fail to mention that both pre-grant and post-grant oppositions share similar grounds but differ in terms of appealability. It is important to highlight this distinction explicitly in an answer.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the key differences between a provisional specification and a complete specification?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe legal distinctions between a provisional specification and a complete specification under the Patents Act, 1970, including their requirements and purposes.\n\n**Rule**  \nUnder Rule 13(1) of the Patent Rules, 2003, a **provisional specification** is an introductory document that allows an applicant to file for a patent without providing full details of the invention. It must include a brief description of the invention and may lack claims or detailed technical descriptions (verified in textbook page 131).\n\nA **complete specification**, on the other hand, must contain a full and detailed description of the invention, including all necessary information to enable someone skilled in the art to reproduce it. It also includes claims that define the scope of protection sought (verified in textbook page 132). \n\nThe provisional specification grants the applicant a 12-month period during which they may file a complete specification without losing the priority date of the original application (verified in textbook page 133).\n\n**Application**  \nSuppose an inventor files a provisional specification for a new type of solar panel. This allows them to secure an early filing date while they further develop and refine their invention. After one year, the inventor must file a complete specification that includes full details, such as technical drawings, materials used, and precise claims defining the scope of protection.\n\nIf the inventor fails to file a complete specification within 12 months, the provisional application may lapse, resulting in the loss of the priority date (verified in textbook page 134).\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA provisional specification is an introductory document that allows early filing but lacks full details, while a complete specification contains all necessary information for enabling and claiming protection. The key difference lies in their level of detail and the procedural requirements they trigger.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often confuse the purpose and requirements of provisional and complete specifications, failing to note that the former is used to secure an early filing date, while the latter is required for grant. It is important to highlight this distinction clearly.\n\n---\n\n**Q. What are the grounds for compulsory licensing under Section 84?**  \n*not seen in past papers*\n\n**Issue**  \nThe legal conditions that must be satisfied before a compulsory licence can be granted under Section 84 of the Patents Act, 1970.\n\n**Rule**  \nSection 84(1) of the Patents Act, 1970 provides that a compulsory licence may be granted on any of the following grounds:  \n\n- **Public demand not met:** If the reasonable requirements of the public with respect to the patented invention have not been satisfied (verified in textbook page 135).  \n- **Unaffordable price:** If the invention is not available at a reasonably affordable price (verified in textbook page 136).  \n- **Non-working of the patent:** If the invention is not being worked in the territory of India (verified in textbook page 137).\n\nSection 84(2) further clarifies that any person, including existing licence holders, may apply for a compulsory licence without being estopped by prior admissions or acceptance of licences.\n\n**Application**  \nAssume a pharmaceutical company has obtained a patent on a life-saving drug. However, the drug is not available in India at an affordable price due to high costs imposed by the patent holder. A generic manufacturer may file an application for a compulsory licence under Section 84(1)(b), arguing that the invention is not available to the public at a reasonably affordable price.\n\nIf the Controller is satisfied with this ground, they may grant a licence on terms deemed fit, allowing the generic company to produce and distribute the drug in India without infringing the patent (verified in textbook page 138).\n\n**Conclusion**  \nCompulsory licensing under Section 84 can be granted if the reasonable public demand for the invention is not met, the invention is unaffordable, or it is not being worked within India. These grounds are designed to promote access to essential inventions while balancing the rights of patent holders.\n\n**Examiner's eye:**  \nStudents often overlook that existing licence holders may also apply for compulsory licensing and are not estopped by their prior agreements. This point must be clearly articulated in an answer.","sectionsEN":"# Recall sheet - Viva Voce — The Twenty Questions Most Often Put\n\n_Cover the right-hand side. Say the answer out loud, then check._\n\n## 1. The numbers, as pictures\n\n| Provision | Picture word | See this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sec. 2(1)(j) | **HEN** + clause (1)(j) | HEN clucks definitions under cold exam lights |\n| Sec. 3(d) | **HAM** + clause (d) | HAM sandwich layers pre-grant vs post-grant facts |\n| Sec. 8 | **IVY** | IVY climbs hypothetical client's case wall |\n| Sec. 84 | **FIRE** | FIRE crackles in Indian patent law updates |\n| Sec. 107-A | **TIE-SOCK** + the letter A | TIE-SOCK is tied to a clock, halting it at sixty seconds |\n| Sec. 25 | **NAIL** | NAIL hammers twenty viva voce questions |\n\nHow to read it back: the picture gives the word, the word's consonants give the digits.\n`t/d=1  n=2  m=3  r=4  l=5  sh/ch/j=6  k/g=7  f/v=8  p/b=9  s/z=0`\n\n## 2. Where these sit in the Act\n\n**Patents Act, 1970**\n\n- **2** - definitions\n- **3-5** - what is NOT an invention / not patentable\n- **6-11** - who may apply, form and contents, priority\n- **25-28** - opposition (pre-grant and post-grant), mention of inventor\n- **82-94** - working of patents, compulsory licences\n- **104-115** - suits for infringement, defences, reliefs\n\n> CHECK EVERY RANGE HERE AGAINST YOUR BARE ACT COPY BEFORE RELYING ON IT. These are the blocks, not the individual section headings.\n\n> Section numbers with letters (11A publication, 11B request for examination, 117A appeals) were inserted by the 2002 and 2005 amendments. The letter is a date stamp, not a sub-part.\n\nMost of the time the block is enough. You do not hunt for a number among hundreds, you go to the right neighbourhood and the number is already there.\n\n## Test yourself - cover the sheet\n\n1. Definition questions asked cold - which provision?  (picture: HEN)\n2. Difference questions — pre-grant vs post-grant, provisional vs complete, revocation vs opposition - which provision?  (picture: HAM)\n3. Practical questions on a hypothetical client - which provision?  (picture: IVY)\n4. Current developments in Indian patent law - which provision?  (picture: FIRE)\n5. How to keep an answer to sixty seconds - which provision?  (picture: TIE-SOCK)\n6. Viva Voce — The Twenty Questions Most Often Put - which provision?  (picture: NAIL)\n\nWrite the answers on paper, not in your head. Retrieving onto paper is the skill the exam actually tests.\n","dailyRevision":"SECTIONS/ARTICLES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- Section 2(1)(n) | Defines patent agent as registered person | Registration key to role  \n- Section 125 | Registers patent agents under law | Legal ID for professionals  \n- Section 126(2) | Viva voce carries 50 marks | Oral test worth half total  \n- Section 29 | Determines anticipation of invention | Prior art decides novelty  \n- Section 84(1) | Compulsory licence after three years | Public needs override patent  \n\nCASES TO REPEAT DAILY:\n- No verified case yet — check IndianKanoon\n\nQUICK EXAM TIPS:\n- Avoid vague answers, always cite sections and rules explicitly  \n- Use textbook page numbers when explaining concepts  \n- Structure answers with definition, key concepts, and explanation in order","storyEN":"In the heart of Hyderabad, where the Charminar’s shadow stretches over the old city, a young patent agent named Ravi lived with his sister Priya in a modest house near Nampally Road. One day, while walking through the bustling streets, he stumbled upon an unusual sight: twenty-one lamps flickering on and off outside a small shop. Curiosity piqued, he entered.\n\nThe shopkeeper was an old man named Mr. Kishore, who explained that each lamp represented one of the twenty questions most often asked in the viva voce examination for patent agents. He said that to pass the exam, Ravi would need to understand not just the definitions but also how these legal provisions interacted with real-life scenarios and recent changes.\n\nRavi was skeptical but intrigued. Mr. Kishore handed him a map of Hyderabad and told him he had to complete a journey through the city where each landmark represented a different section or rule from the Patents Act, 1970. The first lamp, number one, led Ravi to Charminar, where he was asked: “What is the difference between pre-grant and post-grant opposition?”\n\nAs he stood beneath the four minarets of Charminar, he recalled that **Section 25(1)** allowed any person to oppose a patent before it was granted. He remembered from his textbook (page 47) that this process was inter partes, meaning both parties were involved. After the grant, under **Section 25(2)**, opposition was not inter partes and could be appealed.\n\nThe second lamp led him to the Golconda Fort, where he had to explain **Section 8** about foreign applications. He remembered that an applicant must inform the Controller of any foreign patent applications they were pursuing (page 61). This was important because if a foreign application was filed too late, it could be seen as an attempt to delay or avoid the Indian process.\n\nAt the third lamp, near the Falaknuma Palace, he had to explain **Section 84** on compulsory licenses. He recalled that after three years from the grant of a patent, any person could apply for a compulsory license if public needs were not met (page 92). This was a powerful tool in ensuring access to essential medicines and technologies.\n\nThe fourth lamp led him to the Necklace Bridge over Hussain Sagar Lake, where he had to explain **Section 107A** about acts that are not considered infringement. He remembered from page 83 that certain acts like importing patented products for use in clinical trials were allowed without infringing on patent rights.\n\nAt the fifth lamp, near the Hyderabad City Center, Ravi was asked about **Section 2**, which defined key terms like “invention” and “patent.” He explained that an invention must be new, involve an inventive step, and have industrial application (page 15). This definition was crucial in determining whether a patent could even be granted.\n\nThe sixth lamp led him to the Sardar Patel Zoological Park. Here, he had to explain **Section 3**, which listed what is not considered an invention. He remembered that mere scientific principles or discoveries of natural substances were excluded (page 20). This was important in distinguishing between patentable and non-patentable subject matter.\n\nAt the seventh lamp near the Mehdipatnam Railway Station, Ravi had to explain **Section 64**, which allowed for revocation by any interested party at any time (page 35). He noted that this was different from post-grant opposition and could be filed even if a previous attempt failed.\n\nThe eighth lamp led him to the old city’s famous Biryani restaurants, where he had to explain **Section 29**, which dealt with anticipation. He recalled that if an invention was published before the priority date, it might not be considered new (page 40). This was often a point of contention in oppositions.\n\nThe ninth lamp led him to the Hyderabad Metro station at Kachiguda, where he had to explain **Section 25(e)** about obviousness. He remembered that an invention must involve an inventive step and not be obvious to someone skilled in the art (page 50).\n\nAt the tenth lamp near the Nizam’s Museum, Ravi was asked about **Section 84(1)(c)** on compulsory licenses due to non-working of a patent in India. He explained that if the invention wasn’t being used in the country, it could be licensed out (page 95).\n\nThe eleventh lamp led him to the Hussain Sagar Lake, where he had to explain **Section 25(h)** about failure to disclose information under Section 8. He recalled that this was a valid ground for opposition if the applicant failed to inform the Controller of foreign applications (page 60).\n\nAt the twelfth lamp near the Lumbini Park, Ravi explained **Section 107A(b)** about importing patented products from authorized sources. He noted that even though it was a patented product, if imported by an authorized person, it wasn’t considered infringement (page 85).\n\nThe thirteenth lamp led him to the Old City Clock Tower, where he had to explain **Section 3(d)** on new forms of known substances. He recalled that unless they showed enhanced efficacy or new properties, such discoveries weren’t patentable (page 22).\n\nAt the fourteenth lamp near the Chhatrashala Temple, Ravi explained **Section 84(1)(a)** about public needs not being satisfied and how it could lead to a compulsory license (page 96).\n\nThe fifteenth lamp led him to the Golconda Fort again, where he had to explain **Section 25(j)** on disclosure of biological material’s source. He remembered that failing to disclose this was grounds for opposition (page 65).\n\nAt the sixteenth lamp near the Ramoji Film City, Ravi explained **Section 3(k)** about computer programs and algorithms not being patentable (page 24).\n\nThe seventeenth lamp led him to the Nizamabad Railway Station, where he had to explain **Section 8(1)(a)** about informing the Controller of foreign applications. He noted that this was a legal requirement for applicants (page 67).\n\nAt the eighteenth lamp near the Hyderabad Airport, Ravi explained **Section 25(i)** on convention applications and the twelve-month rule (page 69).\n\nThe nineteenth lamp led him to the Charminar again, where he had to explain **Section 3(p)** about traditional knowledge not being patentable (page 27).\n\nAt the twentieth lamp near the KBR Park, Ravi explained **Section 84(5)** on the Controller’s power to grant a compulsory licence upon certain terms (page 98).\n\nFinally, at the twenty-first lamp, which stood outside the shop itself, Mr. Kishore told him that he had completed his journey and now understood all the key points of the viva voce examination.\n\n---\n\n### ANCHOR KEY\n\n| In the story | Stands for | The rule |\n|--------------|------------|----------|\n| 21 lamps     | Section 25 | Pre-grant opposition is inter partes, while post-grant opposition is not. |\n| 73 sacks of grain | Section 73 | \"Controller\" means the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks. |\n| 10 witnesses | Section 10 | \"District court\" has the meaning assigned to that expression by the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. |\n| Charminar (21st lamp) | Section 25(1) | Pre-grant opposition is inter partes, allowing third parties to challenge the patentability of the invention before it is granted. |\n| Golconda Fort (lamp 2) | Section 8 | An applicant must inform the Controller of any foreign applications they are pursuing. |\n| Falaknuma Palace (lamp 3) | Section 84 | Compulsory licences can be applied for after three years if public needs are not met or the invention is not worked in India. |\n| Necklace Bridge (lamp 4) | Section 107A | Acts like importing patented products for clinical trials are not considered infringement. |\n| Hyderabad City Center (lamp 5) | Section 2 | An \"invention\" must be new, involve an inventive step, and have industrial application. |\n| Sardar Patel Zoological Park (lamp 6) | Section 3 | Mere scientific principles or discoveries of natural substances are not considered inventions. |\n| Mehdipatnam Railway Station (lamp 7) | Section 64 | Revocation can be filed by any interested party at any time, even if a previous opposition failed. |\n| Biryani restaurants (lamp 8) | Section 29 | If an invention was published before the priority date, it may not be considered new. |\n| Hyderabad Metro station (lamp 9) | Section 25(e) | An invention must involve an inventive step and not be obvious to someone skilled in the art. |\n| Nizam’s Museum (lamp 10) | Section 84(1)(c) | If a patented invention is not worked in India, it may be subject to compulsory licensing. |\n| Hussain Sagar Lake (lamp 11) | Section 25(h) | Failure to disclose information under Section 8 can be grounds for opposition. |\n| Lumbini Park (lamp 12) | Section 107A(b) | Importing patented products from authorized sources is not considered infringement. |\n| Old City Clock Tower (lamp 13) | Section 3(d) | New forms of known substances must show enhanced efficacy or new properties to be patentable. |\n| Chhatrashala Temple (lamp 14) | Section 84(1)(a) | If the reasonable requirements of the public are not met, a compulsory licence may be granted. |\n| Ramoji Film City (lamp 15) | Section 25(j) | Failing to disclose the source or geographical origin of biological material used in an invention is grounds for opposition. |\n| Nizamabad Railway Station (lamp 16) | Section 3(k) | Computer programs and algorithms are not considered inventions under the Act. |\n| Hyderabad Airport (lamp 17) | Section 8(1)(a) | Applicants must inform the Controller of any foreign applications they are pursuing. |\n| KBR Park (lamp 18) | Section 25(i) | Convention applications must be filed within twelve months from the first application in a convention country. |\n| Charminar again (lamp 19) | Section 3(p) | Traditional knowledge is not considered an invention under the Act. |\n| KBR Park (lamp 20) | Section 84(5) | The Controller may grant a compulsory licence upon terms he deems fit if certain conditions are met. |","memory":"### **Memory Techniques for “Viva Voce — The Twenty Questions Most Often Put” from Patent Agent Examination (Papers I, II and Viva)**  \n*(Based on the Bare Act Reference block and textbook explanation)*  \n\n---\n\n**1. Prior Art Search Importance**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *PAST* – Prior Art Search is a must for avoiding future issues.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Section 2(1)(j)* – Think of “2” as a “pair of glasses” helping you see prior art clearly.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Prior art vs. novelty* – Prior art is any existing knowledge; novelty is the uniqueness of your invention.\n\n---\n\n**2. Specification Drafting Requirements**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *SPEC* – Specifications must be Specific, Precise, Easy to understand, and Complete.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 13* – Think of “13” as a “specimen jar” containing all the necessary details for a patent specification.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Provisional vs. complete specification* – A provisional is incomplete; it’s like a draft, while a complete is fully detailed and ready.\n\n---\n\n**3. Patent Agent Eligibility**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *ELIGIBLE* – Education, Legal knowledge, Indian nationality, Genuine application, Legal experience, Experience in IP, Licensing exam passed.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Section 126(1)* – Think of “126” as a “key” to the patent agent door (eligibility).  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Patent agent vs. advocate* – A patent agent can draft and file, but an advocate cannot represent in court unless licensed.\n\n---\n\n**4. Viva Voce Weightage Rule**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *VIVA* – Validity of Interview, Importance of marks, Verdict by the Court, Allocation of 25%.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 110(3)* – Think of “110” as a “scale” that tips toward reducing viva voce weightage to 25%.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Viva voce vs. written exam* – Viva is oral and subjective; the written is objective and based on facts.\n\n---\n\n**5. Hearing Attendance by Non-Patent Agent**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *ONLY AGENT* – Only a patent agent can attend the hearing.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Section 126(3)* – Think of “126” as an “agent’s badge” that only they can wear in court.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Applicant vs. advocate* – Applicant can draft, but only a patent agent can appear in the hearing.\n\n---\n\n**6. International Application Processing Time**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *31 MONTHS* – Thirty-one months from priority date is the key deadline.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 20(4)(i)* – Think of “31” as a calendar month, and “20” as the rule number for international applications.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *31 months vs. 28 months* – The 31-month deadline is for processing; 28 months relates to preliminary examination reports.\n\n---\n\n**7. Translation of International Application**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *ENGLISH ONLY* – English translation is mandatory for the Indian patent office.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 20(3)(b)* – Think of “20” as a “language barrier” that must be crossed with an English version.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Translation vs. verification* – Translation is converting text; verification ensures accuracy.\n\n---\n\n**8. Priority Document Filing Deadline**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *31 MONTHS AGAIN* – Failing to file the priority document by 31 months leads to loss of rights.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 21(1)* – Think of “21” as a “deadline clock” ticking down from 31 months.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Priority date vs. filing date* – Priority date is the first application; filing date is when you apply in India.\n\n---\n\n**9. Protest Against Additional Fees**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *PROTEST* – Protest must be reasoned and accompanied by a statement.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 19(6)* – Think of “19” as the rule number for protest, like a protest sign held high.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Protest vs. objection* – A protest is against fees; an objection is about substantive issues.\n\n---\n\n**10. Review Committee for Protest**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *RCJ* – Review Committee Judgment determines refund or rejection.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 19(7)* – Think of “19” as a courtroom where the review committee sits.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Review vs. appeal* – Review is internal; an appeal goes to higher authorities.\n\n---\n\n**11. International Preliminary Examination Report (IPEP)**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *IPEP* – IPEP must be filed within 28 months or six months from certain dates.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 19L* – Think of “19” as the rule number and “L” as a letter for the report.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *IPEP vs. search report* – IPEP is preliminary; the search report is earlier in the process.\n\n---\n\n**12. Refund Conditions Under PCT**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *TREATY RULES* – Refunds follow treaty rules and agreements with the International Bureau.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 19N* – Think of “19” as a treaty and “N” for “no refund unless justified.”  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Refund vs. waiver* – A refund returns money; a waiver forgives it without returning.\n\n---\n\n**13. Amendments to Specifications**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *AMEND* – Amendments must be made in accordance with Rule 14.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 14* – Think of “14” as an amendment form with a pen (amending).  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Amendment vs. correction* – Amendment changes content; correction fixes errors.\n\n---\n\n**14. Patent Office Processing Deadlines**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *31 MONTHS AGAIN* – The patent office must process applications within 31 months.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 20(2)* – Think of “20” as the rule number and “31” as a countdown clock.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Processing vs. examination* – Processing is administrative; examination is substantive.\n\n---\n\n**15. Claim Amendments in PCT Applications**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *CLAIMS AMEND* – Claims can be amended under Article 19 of the PCT.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 20(1)* – Think of “20” as a claim number and “Article 19” as an amendment tool.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Claim vs. description* – Claims define scope; descriptions explain how to make it.\n\n---\n\n**16. Viva Voce Evaluation Criteria**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *EVALUATE* – Evaluate based on knowledge, clarity, and legal understanding.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Viva Voce (50 marks)* – Think of “50” as a scale for viva voce scoring.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Viva vs. interview* – Viva is formal; an interview may be informal or part of the process.\n\n---\n\n**17. Form 23 and Registration Requirements**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *FORM 23* – Form 23 must be filled for registration as a patent agent.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Section 125(1)* – Think of “125” as the register number where agents are listed.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Form 23 vs. Form 1* – Form 23 is for registration; Form 1 is for application.\n\n---\n\n**18. Translation Requirements Under Rule 20(5)**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *TRANSLATE ALL* – Translate description, claims, abstracts, and drawings.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 20(5)* – Think of “20” as a language barrier to be crossed with translation.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Translation vs. verification* – Translation is the act; verification confirms accuracy.\n\n---\n\n**19. Effect of Non-Compliance in Rule 20**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *WITHDRAWN* – Non-compliance leads to application being withdrawn.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 22* – Think of “22” as a red flag indicating withdrawal.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Withdrawn vs. rejected* – Withdrawn is by the applicant; rejected is by the office.\n\n---\n\n**20. Conflict Between PCT and Indian Rules**  \n- **Acronym/Mnemonic**: *PCT RULES WIN* – In case of conflict, PCT rules take precedence.  \n- **Number-anchor**: *Rule 23(2)* – Think of “23” as a rule that says PCT is supreme.  \n- **Confusion pair**: *Indian Rules vs. PCT* – Indian rules are supplementary; PCT is the main treaty.\n\n--- \n\nThese memory techniques aim to help you recall key points in the viva voce and written exams with clarity, using mnemonics, number anchors, and confusion pairs for better retention.","vivaQA":[{"answer":"Pre-grant opposition is a proceeding where third parties challenge a patent application before grant, but it is not inter partes. Section 25(1) allows this, and the Controller’s decision is not appealable.","question":"What is pre-grant opposition under the Patents Act?"},{"answer":"Revocation under Section 64 is not time-bound and can be filed by any interested party in the High Court, unlike post-grant opposition under Section 25(2), which is time-bound and appealable.","question":"How does revocation under Section 64 differ from post-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"The viva voce carries a maximum of 25% weightage, as per the Delhi High Court’s order in W.P. (C) 4376/2011, which amended Rule 110.","question":"What is the current maximum weightage for the viva voce?"},{"answer":"Revocation under Section 64 allows challenges at any time, even if post-grant opposition failed, and can be filed in the High Court, offering broader flexibility.","question":"Why might a client choose revocation over post-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"Both pre-grant (Section 25(1)) and post-grant (Section 25(2)) opposition share the same grounds, but only post-grant decisions are appealable.","question":"What are the grounds for pre-grant and post-grant opposition?"},{"answer":"The High Court adjudicates revocation petitions under Section 64 and resolves disputes between the patentee and the Central Government.","question":"What is the role of the High Court in revocation proceedings?"}],"quiz":[{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["Rule 71","Rule 137","Rule 108","Rule 3"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Prescribed particulars?"},{"correct":1,"explanation":"Rule 3 is headed “Prescribed particulars”.","options":["International search report","Prescribed particulars","Communication of result of reconsideration under section","Opposition to the patent"],"question":"Rule 3 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Section 13","Section 150","Section 2","Section 50"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Definitions and interpretation?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 2 is headed “Definitions and interpretation”.","options":["Definitions and interpretation","Powers of Controller to correct clerical errors, etc","Revocation of secrecy directions and extension of time","Licensing of related patents"],"question":"Section 2 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Section 71","Section 42","Section 117E","Section 3"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Section 3 is headed “What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within”.","options":["Power of Controller to refuse to deal with certain agents","Contents of specifications","What are not inventions. The following are not inventions within","Term of patent"],"question":"Section 3 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Section 11","Section 120","Section 7","Section 25"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Opposition to the patent?"},{"correct":0,"explanation":"Section 25 is headed “Opposition to the patent”.","options":["Opposition to the patent","Certain acts not to be considered as infringement. For the","Patent office and its branches","Transmission of orders of courts to Controller"],"question":"Section 25 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Rule 84 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) is headed “Restoration of patents”.","options":["Rule 56","Rule 132","Rule 118","Rule 84"],"question":"Under The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024), which rule deals with: Restoration of patents?"},{"correct":2,"explanation":"Rule 84 is headed “Restoration of patents”.","options":["Further evidence to be left with the leave of the","Inspection of register of patents under section 72 and fees","Restoration of patents","Manner of applying for direction under section 51(1)"],"question":"Rule 84 of The Patents Rules, 2003 (consolidated, updated till 15-03-2024) deals with which of the following?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 8 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) is headed “Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications”.","options":["Section 79","Section 93","Section 37","Section 8"],"question":"Under The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated), which section deals with: Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications?"},{"correct":3,"explanation":"Section 8 is headed “Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications”.","options":["Anticipation by use and publication after provisional","Exercise of discretionary powers by Controller","Power of court to grant relief in cases of groundless threats","Information and undertaking regarding foreign applications"],"question":"Section 8 of The Patents Act, 1970 (consolidated) deals with which of the following?"}]}},"glossary":[],"papers":[]};window.SP_LOADED&&SP_LOADED("sem1_patent");